// The Coach. Lexicon, meeting contributions and speeches.
window.COACH={"lexicon": [{"field": "Argument, reasoning and evidence", "intro": "This is the register of the seminar, the appeals panel and the evaluation report, where terms are used in their strict senses and loose usage is noticed. Sound native by naming the exact move in dispute, the premise, the warrant or the inference, and by applying the same tests to your own case first.", "entries": [{"id": "argument-1", "field": "Argument, reasoning and evidence", "term": "premise", "gloss": "A proposition offered as a reason for a conclusion; distinct from the conclusion itself and from the evidence that supports the premise.", "say": "Before we argue about the conclusion, I'd like to test the *premise*: that enrolments fall when the audition is harder.", "note": "Lands when you name the specific premise you dispute; backfires if used as a general sneer at someone's 'premises'.", "level": 1}, {"id": "argument-2", "field": "Argument, reasoning and evidence", "term": "inference", "gloss": "The step from premises to a conclusion; a good inference can still yield a false conclusion if a premise is false.", "say": "The data are fine. It's the *inference* from one cohort's results to the whole program that I can't follow.", "note": "Separating the data from the inference lets you disagree without accusing anyone of getting the numbers wrong.", "level": 1}, {"id": "argument-3", "field": "Argument, reasoning and evidence", "term": "warrant", "gloss": "In Toulmin's model, the usually unstated principle that licenses the move from data to claim; challenging the warrant is different from challenging the data.", "say": "I accept the survey results. What's the *warrant* for treating parent satisfaction as a measure of musical standard?", "note": "Precise and respected in academic company; in a parent forum say 'what links those two things' instead.", "level": 2}, {"id": "argument-4", "field": "Argument, reasoning and evidence", "term": "burden of proof", "gloss": "The obligation on a party to establish a claim; it normally rests on whoever proposes to change the status quo or asserts the positive.", "say": "The *burden of proof* sits with the proposal to cut the ensemble, not with the people asking to keep it.", "note": "Strong when you place it calmly and early; it sounds defensive if you invoke it only after losing the exchange.", "level": 1}, {"id": "argument-5", "field": "Argument, reasoning and evidence", "term": "begging the question", "gloss": "Strictly, petitio principii: an argument that assumes in its premises the very conclusion it sets out to prove; it does not mean 'raising the question'.", "say": "Saying the new rubric is valid because it measures what we value *begs the question*: whether it measures that is exactly what's in dispute.", "note": "In a room of academics the strict use marks you as precise; if others use the loose sense, say 'raises the question' yourself and let it pass.", "level": 2}, {"id": "argument-6", "field": "Argument, reasoning and evidence", "term": "non sequitur", "gloss": "A conclusion that does not follow from the premises offered, whatever its truth on other grounds.", "say": "With respect, the budget shortfall is real but the conclusion that we drop instrumental tuition is a *non sequitur*.", "note": "Use 'with respect' or similar before it; on its own it can sound like a put-down.", "level": 1}, {"id": "argument-7", "field": "Argument, reasoning and evidence", "term": "conflation", "gloss": "Treating two distinct things as one, usually so that evidence about one is used for the other.", "say": "I think we're *conflating* participation with achievement; the report has numbers for the first and none for the second.", "note": "More collegial than 'you're confused'; name both things you think are being merged.", "level": 1}, {"id": "argument-8", "field": "Argument, reasoning and evidence", "term": "equivocation", "gloss": "Shifting between two senses of one word within an argument so that the reasoning only works if the senses are run together.", "say": "There's an *equivocation* on 'excellence' here: the first paragraph means selective entry, the recommendation means broad access.", "note": "Point to the exact word and the two senses; an unspecific charge of equivocation sounds like a debating trick.", "level": 2}, {"id": "argument-9", "field": "Argument, reasoning and evidence", "term": "false dichotomy", "gloss": "Presenting two options as exhaustive when others exist, or as exclusive when both are possible.", "say": "It isn't a choice between rigour and inclusion; that's a *false dichotomy*, and our Year 7 intake shows both can coexist.", "note": "Most persuasive when you immediately name the third option; otherwise it reads as evasion.", "level": 1}, {"id": "argument-10", "field": "Argument, reasoning and evidence", "term": "motte and bailey", "gloss": "A tactic, named by the philosopher Nicholas Shackel, of advancing a bold contentious claim (the bailey) and retreating to a modest defensible one (the motte) when challenged, then returning to the bold claim.", "say": "We've moved between 'the arts should be cut' and 'the arts should be reviewed' three times; I'd like to settle which one is on the table, because the *motte and bailey* is making this hard to vote on.", "note": "Explain it in a clause the first time; unexplained, it sounds like insider jargon from online argument.", "level": 3}, {"id": "argument-11", "field": "Argument, reasoning and evidence", "term": "survivorship bias", "gloss": "Drawing conclusions from cases that passed a selection filter while ignoring those that did not, so the filter's effect is mistaken for a cause.", "say": "Our alumni who became professionals all did the full program, but that's *survivorship bias* unless we know about the students who did it and left music.", "note": "Lands well with funding panels; give the missing group explicitly.", "level": 1}, {"id": "argument-12", "field": "Argument, reasoning and evidence", "term": "confounding", "gloss": "When a third variable influences both the supposed cause and the effect, producing an association that is not causal.", "say": "Specialist music students outperform on NAPLAN, but selection and family background are obvious *confounders*.", "note": "'Confounder' is the noun; avoid using it as a general word for 'complication'.", "level": 1}, {"id": "argument-13", "field": "Argument, reasoning and evidence", "term": "base-rate neglect", "gloss": "Judging the probability of something from case-specific information while ignoring how common it is in the population.", "say": "Three complaints sounds alarming, but against six hundred families a term that's below the *base rate* we've had for a decade.", "note": "Useful in risk and complaints discussions; supply the actual base rate or the point evaporates.", "level": 2}, {"id": "argument-14", "field": "Argument, reasoning and evidence", "term": "correlation and causation", "gloss": "Association between two variables does not establish that one causes the other; causation also needs direction, mechanism and the exclusion of confounding.", "say": "I'm not disputing the *correlation*; I'm saying nothing here yet shows *causation*.", "note": "The bare slogan 'correlation isn't causation' is tired in academic rooms; say what further evidence would establish causation.", "level": 1}, {"id": "argument-15", "field": "Argument, reasoning and evidence", "term": "null hypothesis", "gloss": "Informally, the default assumption that there is no effect or difference; failing to reject it is not proof that it is true.", "say": "Before we credit the new timetable, the *null hypothesis* is that results would have moved this much anyway.", "note": "Informal use is fine in committees; with statisticians present, do not claim a null has been 'proved'.", "level": 2}, {"id": "argument-16", "field": "Argument, reasoning and evidence", "term": "falsifiable", "gloss": "Capable in principle of being shown false by some possible observation; Popper's criterion for a scientific claim, distinct from being false.", "say": "If no outcome could count against 'the program builds character', the claim isn't *falsifiable*, and it shouldn't anchor a funding case.", "note": "Strong against vague goals in strategic plans; avoid aiming it at personal values, where it sounds pedantic.", "level": 2}, {"id": "argument-17", "field": "Argument, reasoning and evidence", "term": "parsimony", "gloss": "Preferring the explanation that requires fewer assumptions when explanations account equally well for the evidence; Ockham's razor.", "say": "On *parsimony*, a single timetable clash explains the drop in Year 10 better than a decline in student interest.", "note": "Parsimony breaks ties; it is not evidence, and an expert will say so if you lean on it too hard.", "level": 2}, {"id": "argument-18", "field": "Argument, reasoning and evidence", "term": "steel-man", "gloss": "To state an opponent's position in its strongest form before answering it; the opposite of a straw man.", "say": "Let me *steel-man* the case for centralising the instrumental service, because I think it's better than it's been put today.", "note": "Disarming when sincere; announcing a steel-man and then delivering a weak version damages trust.", "level": 1}, {"id": "argument-19", "field": "Argument, reasoning and evidence", "term": "the counterfactual", "gloss": "What would have happened in the absence of the intervention; causal claims are claims about the difference from this.", "say": "The question isn't whether enrolments rose after the festival; it's what *the counterfactual* looks like without it.", "note": "Ask for it as 'compared with what' in less technical rooms.", "level": 2}, {"id": "argument-20", "field": "Argument, reasoning and evidence", "term": "a priori", "gloss": "Knowable or argued independently of experience, from concepts or principles; distinct from a posteriori claims that depend on evidence.", "say": "We can't settle *a priori* whether a combined ensemble suits Year 8s; that's an empirical question we can pilot.", "note": "Commonly misused to mean 'presumed in advance'; use it only for reasoning from principle. Pronounced ah pree-OR-ee or ay pry-OR-eye; both are accepted.", "level": 2}, {"id": "argument-21", "field": "Argument, reasoning and evidence", "term": "a posteriori", "gloss": "Known or justified through experience and evidence rather than by reasoning alone.", "say": "The principle sounds right, but its value here is an *a posteriori* matter: let's see two years of data.", "note": "Best paired with 'a priori' in a single contrast; on its own it can sound like ornament.", "level": 3}, {"id": "argument-22", "field": "Argument, reasoning and evidence", "term": "necessary condition", "gloss": "A condition without which the outcome cannot occur, though its presence does not guarantee the outcome.", "say": "Adequate rehearsal space is a *necessary condition* for a third concert band; it isn't enough on its own.", "note": "Use when someone treats a precondition as if it guaranteed success.", "level": 1}, {"id": "argument-23", "field": "Argument, reasoning and evidence", "term": "sufficient condition", "gloss": "A condition whose presence guarantees the outcome, though the outcome may occur by other routes as well.", "say": "A high ATAR score is *sufficient* for entry but not necessary; there are portfolio pathways.", "note": "The necessary and sufficient pairing clarifies policy wording quickly; keep the two terms distinct under pressure.", "level": 1}, {"id": "argument-24", "field": "Argument, reasoning and evidence", "term": "slippery slope, used fairly", "gloss": "A legitimate argument that one step makes further steps likely, when a causal mechanism and evidence for the progression are supplied; fallacious only when the progression is merely asserted.", "say": "I'm making a *slippery slope* argument and I'll own it: once one ensemble is optional, the precedent and the timetable pressure both point to the next.", "note": "Name the mechanism at each step; owning the form openly pre-empts the 'that's a slippery slope' dismissal.", "level": 2}, {"id": "argument-25", "field": "Argument, reasoning and evidence", "term": "valid versus sound", "gloss": "An argument is valid if its conclusion follows from its premises; sound only if it is valid and its premises are true.", "say": "The argument is *valid*, I'll grant that. It isn't *sound*, because the second premise is wrong about the funding formula.", "note": "Granting validity first signals fairness and makes the attack on the premise land harder.", "level": 2}, {"id": "argument-26", "field": "Argument, reasoning and evidence", "term": "enthymeme", "gloss": "In Aristotle, a rhetorical syllogism drawn from probabilities; in modern use, an argument with a premise left unstated for the audience to supply.", "say": "There's an *enthymeme* in the proposal: it assumes, without saying so, that every student will have a private teacher.", "note": "Rare outside philosophy and rhetoric; in most rooms 'unstated premise' does the same work.", "level": 3}, {"id": "argument-27", "field": "Argument, reasoning and evidence", "term": "load-bearing assumption", "gloss": "The assumption on which an argument or plan depends, such that if it fails the whole case fails.", "say": "The *load-bearing assumption* in this budget is that hire income holds at last year's level.", "note": "A clear, modern phrase that carries well in boards; identify one, not five.", "level": 1}, {"id": "argument-28", "field": "Argument, reasoning and evidence", "term": "weak man", "gloss": "Answering the weakest version of an opposing view that someone actually holds, then implying the view as a whole is refuted; subtler than a straw man because the version is real.", "say": "Some parents did say that, but answering the angriest email is a *weak man*; the stronger version of their concern is about fairness in auditions.", "note": "Not widely known; gloss it on first use or it will be heard as a slip for 'straw man'.", "level": 3}, {"id": "argument-29", "field": "Argument, reasoning and evidence", "term": "ad hominem, strictly", "gloss": "Rejecting an argument because of who made it rather than on its merits; legitimately questioning a witness's interest or competence is not the fallacy.", "say": "His interest in the outcome is relevant to how we weigh his testimony, but dismissing the argument on that basis would be *ad hominem*.", "note": "Knowing when a challenge to credibility is legitimate is the insider distinction; do not cry 'ad hominem' at every criticism.", "level": 2}, {"id": "argument-30", "field": "Argument, reasoning and evidence", "term": "tu quoque", "gloss": "Deflecting a criticism by pointing out that the critic is guilty of the same thing; it may be true and still not answer the criticism.", "say": "Other departments overspend too, but that's a *tu quoque*; it doesn't answer the question about ours.", "note": "Pronounced too KWOH-kway. Best used against your own side's arguments, which shows integrity.", "level": 3}, {"id": "argument-31", "field": "Argument, reasoning and evidence", "term": "argument from authority", "gloss": "Relying on an expert's view as evidence; legitimate when the expert speaks within their field and experts broadly agree, fallacious otherwise.", "say": "A conductor's opinion on assessment design is an *argument from authority* outside its field.", "note": "Distinguish appropriate deference from fallacy; academics will expect you to.", "level": 1}, {"id": "argument-32", "field": "Argument, reasoning and evidence", "term": "genetic fallacy", "gloss": "Judging a claim by its origin or history rather than by its present evidence or merit.", "say": "That the idea came from a consultant isn't a reason to reject it; that would be the *genetic fallacy*.", "note": "Useful for defusing tribal reactions to where a proposal originated.", "level": 2}, {"id": "argument-33", "field": "Argument, reasoning and evidence", "term": "fallacy of composition", "gloss": "Inferring that what is true of the parts is true of the whole; division is the converse.", "say": "Each ensemble can justify its rehearsal time, but it's the *fallacy of composition* to assume the timetable can carry all of them.", "note": "Name 'division' as well if the error runs the other way, from institution to individual.", "level": 2}, {"id": "argument-34", "field": "Argument, reasoning and evidence", "term": "ecological fallacy", "gloss": "Inferring characteristics of individuals from aggregate data about the group to which they belong.", "say": "The school's median ATAR tells us nothing about this applicant; reading it that way is the *ecological fallacy*.", "note": "Well known in social science and epidemiology; outside those rooms explain it in a clause.", "level": 3}, {"id": "argument-35", "field": "Argument, reasoning and evidence", "term": "regression to the mean", "gloss": "The tendency of unusually extreme measurements to be followed by measurements closer to the average, which can make interventions applied after an extreme result look effective.", "say": "We introduced the support program after our worst exam year; some of the recovery will be *regression to the mean*.", "note": "Particularly powerful when the intervention was triggered by the bad result; say so explicitly.", "level": 2}, {"id": "argument-36", "field": "Argument, reasoning and evidence", "term": "selection bias", "gloss": "Distortion that arises when the cases studied differ systematically from the population about which conclusions are drawn.", "say": "The feedback came from families who chose to attend the forum, so there's obvious *selection bias*.", "note": "Pair it with what a less biased sample would look like.", "level": 1}, {"id": "argument-37", "field": "Argument, reasoning and evidence", "term": "small n", "gloss": "A sample too small for differences to be distinguished reliably from chance.", "say": "With an *n* of eleven in the ATAR class, a two-point shift in the average isn't telling us much.", "note": "Say 'small n' or 'small sample'; quoting a precise threshold without basis invites correction.", "level": 1}, {"id": "argument-38", "field": "Argument, reasoning and evidence", "term": "effect size", "gloss": "The magnitude of a difference or relationship, as distinct from its statistical significance, which mainly reflects whether it is distinguishable from chance given the sample.", "say": "The result is statistically significant, but with a sample this large the *effect size* is trivial in practical terms.", "note": "Asking for effect size, not just significance, marks a reader of research; avoid quoting conventional cut-offs as if they were rules.", "level": 2}, {"id": "argument-39", "field": "Argument, reasoning and evidence", "term": "confidence interval", "gloss": "A range produced by a method that would capture the true value in a stated proportion of repeated samples; it is not a probability that this particular range contains the value.", "say": "The *confidence interval* on that satisfaction figure overlaps last year's, so I wouldn't call it a decline.", "note": "The common misreading is the probability claim; avoid it in front of statisticians.", "level": 2}, {"id": "argument-40", "field": "Argument, reasoning and evidence", "term": "reverse causation", "gloss": "When the supposed effect is actually the cause, or the influence runs both ways.", "say": "Perhaps motivated students choose more ensembles, rather than ensembles creating motivation; *reverse causation* is at least as plausible.", "note": "Offer it as a live alternative, not a gotcha.", "level": 1}, {"id": "argument-41", "field": "Argument, reasoning and evidence", "term": "operationalise", "gloss": "To define an abstract concept in terms of specific, observable measures so that claims about it can be tested.", "say": "If 'musical independence' is a goal in the plan, we need to *operationalise* it, or we'll never know whether we met it.", "note": "Natural in academic and policy rooms; with parents, say 'how we'd see it in practice'.", "level": 2}, {"id": "argument-42", "field": "Argument, reasoning and evidence", "term": "external validity", "gloss": "The extent to which a finding holds beyond the setting, population and conditions of the study; distinct from internal validity, whether the study's own causal conclusion is sound.", "say": "It's a well-run study, but its *external validity* for a public school in Perth is limited: it was a single private conservatorium.", "note": "Pairing it with internal validity shows you know the distinction rather than borrowing the word.", "level": 2}, {"id": "argument-43", "field": "Argument, reasoning and evidence", "term": "hasty generalisation", "gloss": "Drawing a general conclusion from too few or unrepresentative cases.", "say": "Two difficult rehearsals with a relief teacher is a *hasty generalisation* about the whole casual pool.", "note": "Australian spelling 'generalisation'; use it about arguments, not people.", "level": 1}, {"id": "argument-44", "field": "Argument, reasoning and evidence", "term": "cherry-picking", "gloss": "Selecting the evidence that supports a position while omitting comparable evidence that does not.", "say": "I'm not suggesting anyone's *cherry-picked*, but we've seen the three best years; can we see all ten?", "note": "Framing it as a request for the full set avoids an accusation of bad faith.", "level": 1}, {"id": "argument-45", "field": "Argument, reasoning and evidence", "term": "Goodhart's law", "gloss": "The principle, after the economist Charles Goodhart, often put as: when a measure becomes a target, it ceases to be a good measure.", "say": "If we tie funding to the number of performances, *Goodhart's law* says we'll get more performances and not better music.", "note": "Widely recognised in policy rooms; the popular wording is a later paraphrase, so don't present it as Goodhart's own words.", "level": 2}, {"id": "argument-46", "field": "Argument, reasoning and evidence", "term": "Campbell's law", "gloss": "Donald Campbell's observation that the more a quantitative social indicator is used for decision-making, the more it is subject to corruption pressures and the more it distorts the process it monitors.", "say": "*Campbell's law* is the better reference here than Goodhart, because it's specifically about social indicators like school results.", "note": "Rarer than Goodhart and more apt for education; use it with people who know both.", "level": 3}, {"id": "argument-47", "field": "Argument, reasoning and evidence", "term": "inference to the best explanation", "gloss": "Abductive reasoning: accepting the hypothesis that would, if true, best explain the evidence, judged by scope, simplicity and fit.", "say": "No single piece of evidence proves it, but the timetable clash is the *inference to the best explanation* for all four drops at once.", "note": "Respected in academic company; be ready to say what makes your explanation 'best'.", "level": 3}, {"id": "argument-48", "field": "Argument, reasoning and evidence", "term": "inductive and deductive", "gloss": "Deductive reasoning guarantees its conclusion if the premises are true; inductive reasoning makes its conclusion probable and remains open to revision.", "say": "That's an *inductive* case, and a good one, but it isn't a proof, so let's build in a review point.", "note": "Labelling your own argument as inductive is a quiet show of rigour.", "level": 1}, {"id": "argument-49", "field": "Argument, reasoning and evidence", "term": "defeasible", "gloss": "Of reasoning or a rule: holding by default but liable to be overturned by further information.", "say": "The three-year commitment is a strong presumption, but it's *defeasible*; documented hardship should defeat it.", "note": "Pronounced dih-FEE-zuh-bul. Common in law and philosophy; elsewhere 'open to exception' is safer.", "level": 3}, {"id": "argument-50", "field": "Argument, reasoning and evidence", "term": "modus tollens", "gloss": "The valid form: if P then Q; not Q; therefore not P.", "say": "If the change were working, enrolments in Year 10 would have held; they didn't; that's *modus tollens*, and the burden shifts back to the proposal.", "note": "Almost always better to run the argument than to name the form; naming it in a parent forum is affected.", "level": 3}, {"id": "argument-51", "field": "Argument, reasoning and evidence", "term": "affirming the consequent", "gloss": "The invalid form: if P then Q; Q; therefore P.", "say": "If the program were failing, we'd lose students; we have lost students; concluding that the program is failing is *affirming the consequent*.", "note": "Show the structure briefly so the room can see the gap for itself.", "level": 2}, {"id": "argument-52", "field": "Argument, reasoning and evidence", "term": "denying the antecedent", "gloss": "The invalid form: if P then Q; not P; therefore not Q.", "say": "If we had a bigger budget we could tour; we don't, so we can't: that's *denying the antecedent*, because sponsorship is another route.", "note": "Rarely named outside logic; give the counterexample that exposes it instead.", "level": 3}, {"id": "argument-53", "field": "Argument, reasoning and evidence", "term": "reductio ad absurdum", "gloss": "Refuting a claim by showing that its consequences, followed through, are absurd or contradictory.", "say": "Taken as a *reductio*: if every elective must have forty students, we'd also lose Physics.", "note": "Lands if the absurd consequence genuinely follows; if it doesn't, it reads as caricature.", "level": 2}, {"id": "argument-54", "field": "Argument, reasoning and evidence", "term": "prima facie", "gloss": "Sufficient on first appearance to establish a point unless rebutted; it describes the stage of the argument, not its final strength.", "say": "There's a *prima facie* case that the policy disadvantages students without private lessons; I'd want the department to answer it.", "note": "Pronounced PRY-muh FAY-shee or FAY-shuh. Useful for asking for a response without claiming you've won.", "level": 2}, {"id": "argument-55", "field": "Argument, reasoning and evidence", "term": "principle of charity", "gloss": "Interpreting another's statements in their most reasonable sense before evaluating them; it governs interpretation, where steel-manning governs reconstruction.", "say": "On the *principle of charity*, I take the email to be about workload, not about the students.", "note": "Invoking it openly can sound patronising toward the person interpreted; often better to just practise it.", "level": 2}, {"id": "argument-56", "field": "Argument, reasoning and evidence", "term": "moving the goalposts", "gloss": "Changing the criteria for success after the evidence has met the original ones.", "say": "We were asked for fifteen enrolments to run the class and we have seventeen; asking now for twenty is *moving the goalposts*.", "note": "Keep the original criterion in writing; the phrase lands only if you can quote it.", "level": 1}, {"id": "argument-57", "field": "Argument, reasoning and evidence", "term": "no true Scotsman", "gloss": "Protecting a generalisation from counterexamples by redefining the category to exclude them; named by Antony Flew.", "say": "Saying no real jazz program would include a ukulele group is a *no true Scotsman*.", "note": "Light, recognisable and good for defusing purity arguments; don't overuse it.", "level": 2}, {"id": "argument-58", "field": "Argument, reasoning and evidence", "term": "loaded question", "gloss": "A question that presupposes a contested claim, so that any direct answer concedes it; also called a complex question.", "say": "That's a *loaded question*; I can't answer when we stopped favouring private-school students because we didn't start.", "note": "Name it and then answer the honest version of the question; refusing outright looks evasive.", "level": 2}, {"id": "argument-59", "field": "Argument, reasoning and evidence", "term": "category error", "gloss": "Ascribing to something a property that belongs only to things of a different logical type; Ryle's 'category mistake'.", "say": "Asking which ensemble 'is' the music program is a *category error*; the program is the relationship between them.", "note": "Powerful but easily overused for ordinary confusion; keep it for genuine type mistakes.", "level": 2}, {"id": "argument-60", "field": "Argument, reasoning and evidence", "term": "the is-ought gap", "gloss": "Hume's point that no conclusion about what ought to be done follows from purely factual premises without an evaluative premise.", "say": "That most schools have dropped the course is a fact; that we should is an ought, and the *is-ought gap* needs a value premise to bridge it.", "note": "Sounds philosophical; use when a descriptive trend is being passed off as a mandate.", "level": 3}, {"id": "argument-61", "field": "Argument, reasoning and evidence", "term": "naturalistic fallacy", "gloss": "Strictly, G. E. Moore's charge against defining 'good' in terms of a natural property; loosely, inferring that something is good because it is natural. Distinct from the is-ought gap, though often conflated with it.", "say": "I'd avoid calling it the *naturalistic fallacy*, which has a narrower meaning; the point is simply that common practice isn't a justification.", "note": "Most people use it loosely; using it correctly in front of a philosopher earns credit, using it loosely costs it.", "level": 3}, {"id": "argument-62", "field": "Argument, reasoning and evidence", "term": "persuasive definition", "gloss": "A definition, in Charles Stevenson's term, that keeps a word's favourable or unfavourable emotional force while quietly changing its descriptive content.", "say": "Defining 'equity' as identical outcomes in every elective is a *persuasive definition*; it keeps the word's approval and changes what it means.", "note": "Precise and rarely heard; best in policy drafting where definitions carry votes.", "level": 3}, {"id": "argument-63", "field": "Argument, reasoning and evidence", "term": "standard of proof", "gloss": "The degree of certainty a decision-maker must reach; distinct from the burden of proof, which concerns who must reach it.", "say": "Before we hear the complaint, we should agree the *standard of proof*: are we deciding on the balance of probabilities?", "note": "Separating the burden from the standard is an insider move in disciplinary and appeals settings.", "level": 2}, {"id": "argument-64", "field": "Argument, reasoning and evidence", "term": "the Briginshaw principle", "gloss": "From Briginshaw v Briginshaw (1938) 60 CLR 336: in Australian civil and administrative decisions on the balance of probabilities, the seriousness of an allegation and its consequences affect how readily the decision-maker should be satisfied.", "say": "Given what's alleged, *Briginshaw* applies; we'll need clear evidence, not inference, before the council finds against her.", "note": "Familiar to lawyers and senior public servants in Australia; it does not create a third standard, so don't describe it as one.", "level": 3}, {"id": "argument-65", "field": "Argument, reasoning and evidence", "term": "dispositive", "gloss": "Decisive of the matter; settling the question rather than merely bearing on it.", "say": "The legal advice is relevant, but it isn't *dispositive*; the council still has to decide what's right for the school.", "note": "Legal register; sounds affected outside boards, councils and committees with lawyers present.", "level": 3}, {"id": "argument-66", "field": "Argument, reasoning and evidence", "term": "probative value", "gloss": "The degree to which evidence tends to prove or disprove a fact in issue, weighed against its capacity to mislead or prejudice.", "say": "A single anonymous review has low *probative value* about a teacher's practice.", "note": "Legal in origin; works on panels and in complaints processes, less well in staff meetings.", "level": 3}, {"id": "argument-67", "field": "Argument, reasoning and evidence", "term": "triangulate", "gloss": "To corroborate a finding by drawing on independent sources or methods whose errors are unlikely to coincide.", "say": "Let's *triangulate* the survey against attendance and retention before we report it to council.", "note": "Standard in research and evaluation; the point is independence of the sources, not just their number.", "level": 1}, {"id": "argument-68", "field": "Argument, reasoning and evidence", "term": "disconfirming evidence", "gloss": "Evidence that would count against a hypothesis; seeking it deliberately is the remedy for confirmation bias.", "say": "What would be *disconfirming evidence* here? If we can't name any, we haven't really tested the plan.", "note": "Asking for it about your own proposal first sets a tone that makes others reciprocate.", "level": 2}, {"id": "argument-69", "field": "Argument, reasoning and evidence", "term": "motivated reasoning", "gloss": "Reasoning shaped by the conclusion one wants to reach, through selective scrutiny of evidence and arguments.", "say": "I'm aware of the risk of *motivated reasoning* on my part; I run the program, so test my numbers harder than theirs.", "note": "Strongest when applied to yourself; applied to an opponent it becomes an insinuation.", "level": 2}, {"id": "argument-70", "field": "Argument, reasoning and evidence", "term": "post hoc ergo propter hoc", "gloss": "Inferring that because B followed A, A caused B.", "say": "Retention improved the year we changed uniforms, but I'd resist *post hoc ergo propter hoc*.", "note": "Pronounced post hok ER-goh PROP-ter hok. The full Latin can sound showy; 'post hoc reasoning' is often enough.", "level": 2}, {"id": "argument-71", "field": "Argument, reasoning and evidence", "term": "the comparator", "gloss": "The baseline or alternative against which a result is judged; without one a figure has no meaning.", "say": "Eighty per cent satisfaction sounds fine, but what's *the comparator*: last year, other schools, or other departments?", "note": "'Compared with what?' is the plain version and is often better in the room.", "level": 1}, {"id": "argument-72", "field": "Argument, reasoning and evidence", "term": "Chesterton's fence", "gloss": "The principle, from G. K. Chesterton, that one should not remove a rule or structure until one understands why it was put there.", "say": "Before we abolish the second audition round, *Chesterton's fence*: does anyone know why it was introduced?", "note": "Recognised in policy and governance; it is a call for inquiry, not an argument against change.", "level": 2}, {"id": "argument-73", "field": "Argument, reasoning and evidence", "term": "epistemic", "gloss": "Concerning knowledge and justification, as distinct from what is true or what we value.", "say": "My concern is *epistemic*: I'm not saying the policy is wrong, I'm saying we don't yet know enough to know.", "note": "Useful for separating 'I disagree' from 'we can't tell'; overused, it sounds academic.", "level": 2}, {"id": "argument-74", "field": "Argument, reasoning and evidence", "term": "the sorites", "gloss": "The paradox of the heap: a vague predicate admits no sharp boundary, yet small steps accumulate into a clear difference; the honest answer to line-drawing problems is a principled but conventional line.", "say": "Yes, any cut-off score is arbitrary at the margin; that's *the sorites*, and it argues for a reviewed line, not for no line.", "note": "Pronounced so-RYE-teez. Keep it for real line-drawing arguments; most rooms need the point more than the name.", "level": 3}, {"id": "argument-75", "field": "Argument, reasoning and evidence", "term": "proxy", "gloss": "A measurable stand-in for something not directly observable; its value depends on how closely it tracks the real target.", "say": "Exam scores are a reasonable *proxy* for theory knowledge, and a poor one for musicianship.", "note": "Name both the proxy and the thing it stands for.", "level": 1}, {"id": "argument-76", "field": "Argument, reasoning and evidence", "term": "absence of evidence", "gloss": "The distinction between not having evidence of an effect and having evidence that there is no effect; the first licenses only suspended judgement.", "say": "We have no complaints about the new schedule, but that's *absence of evidence*, not evidence of absence; we haven't asked.", "note": "The full contrast is familiar; it lands when you add what would turn one into the other.", "level": 2}, {"id": "argument-77", "field": "Argument, reasoning and evidence", "term": "ceteris paribus", "gloss": "Other things being equal; the qualification that a claimed relationship holds only if other relevant factors are held constant.", "say": "*Ceteris paribus*, smaller ensembles mean more individual attention, but other things are rarely equal in our timetable.", "note": "Pronounced KAY-ter-is PA-ri-bus or SET-er-is. Economists' register; elsewhere 'all else being equal'.", "level": 3}, {"id": "argument-78", "field": "Argument, reasoning and evidence", "term": "priors", "gloss": "In Bayesian terms, the degree of belief held before new evidence, which the evidence should then revise in proportion to its strength.", "say": "My *priors* were against the merger, but the pilot data have moved me some of the way.", "note": "Naturally used by people from quantitative fields; in other rooms it can sound like borrowed jargon, so pair it with plain words.", "level": 2}, {"id": "argument-79", "field": "Argument, reasoning and evidence", "term": "Simpson's paradox", "gloss": "A trend that appears in several groups reverses or disappears when the groups are combined, usually because group sizes differ.", "say": "Pass rates fell overall but rose in every class; that's *Simpson's paradox*, because the intake mix shifted.", "note": "Spectacular when it genuinely applies; check the numbers yourself before invoking it.", "level": 3}, {"id": "argument-80", "field": "Argument, reasoning and evidence", "term": "distinction without a difference", "gloss": "A verbal distinction that changes nothing in practice or substance.", "say": "Calling it a 'restructure' instead of a cut is a *distinction without a difference* for the students affected.", "note": "Effective when the practical sameness is plain; if there is a real difference, the phrase rebounds.", "level": 2}]}, {"field": "Rhetoric and persuasion", "intro": "Rhetoric is the craft vocabulary of persuasion, and in educated company the classical terms signal that you understand persuasion as a discipline, not a trick. Sound native by using the terms when planning, reviewing or coaching a speech, and by letting the figures do their work unnamed in the room itself.", "entries": [{"id": "rhetoric-1", "field": "Rhetoric and persuasion", "term": "ethos", "gloss": "Persuasion through the speaker's perceived character, competence and goodwill, as established in and by the speech, not merely by reputation.", "say": "Our *ethos* with the panel rests on the fact that we've reported our failures as well as our concerts.", "note": "In Aristotle ethos is built within the speech; using it to mean 'brand' or 'culture' sounds loose to rhetoricians.", "level": 1}, {"id": "rhetoric-2", "field": "Rhetoric and persuasion", "term": "pathos", "gloss": "Persuasion through putting the audience into an emotional state that disposes them to judge in a certain way.", "say": "The video of the Year 7s does the *pathos* for us; my job is to give them the numbers that make the feeling defensible.", "note": "Do not use it pejoratively; in the classical sense it is a legitimate proof, not manipulation.", "level": 1}, {"id": "rhetoric-3", "field": "Rhetoric and persuasion", "term": "logos", "gloss": "Persuasion through the argument itself, the reasoning and evidence presented; one of Aristotle's three artistic proofs.", "say": "The *logos* is solid; what the submission lacks is any reason for this panel to care.", "note": "Pronounced LOG-oss or LOH-goss. Avoid treating it as simply 'facts'; it is about demonstration.", "level": 1}, {"id": "rhetoric-4", "field": "Rhetoric and persuasion", "term": "kairos", "gloss": "The opportune moment and the fitness of an argument to it; what can be said, and how, depends on when.", "say": "The *kairos* is now, a week before the budget, not in February when the allocations are locked.", "note": "Pronounced KYE-ross. Lands with people who know the term; otherwise 'the moment' carries the meaning.", "level": 2}, {"id": "rhetoric-5", "field": "Rhetoric and persuasion", "term": "the five canons", "gloss": "The classical divisions of rhetoric: invention, arrangement, style, memory and delivery.", "say": "We've done invention and arrangement; what's left in the *canons* is style and delivery, and that's where we'll lose the room if we aren't careful.", "note": "A useful frame for coaching or reviewing a speech; listing them in a meeting is pedantry.", "level": 2}, {"id": "rhetoric-6", "field": "Rhetoric and persuasion", "term": "inventio", "gloss": "The canon concerned with discovering the available arguments and material for a case.", "say": "Most of the work on this submission is *inventio*: finding the three arguments this panel will actually weigh.", "note": "Pronounced in-VEN-tee-oh. The English 'invention' is usually clearer and less affected.", "level": 3}, {"id": "rhetoric-7", "field": "Rhetoric and persuasion", "term": "dispositio", "gloss": "The canon of arrangement: ordering the parts of a speech and the sequence of arguments for effect.", "say": "The content is right but the *dispositio* is wrong; lead with the student outcomes, not the budget.", "note": "Latin terms like this suit a rhetoric seminar or a speechwriting conversation; elsewhere say 'structure'.", "level": 3}, {"id": "rhetoric-8", "field": "Rhetoric and persuasion", "term": "actio", "gloss": "The canon of delivery: voice, gesture, pace and bearing; also called pronuntiatio.", "say": "Demosthenes is said to have ranked *actio* first, second and third, and on a night like this I believe him.", "note": "The Demosthenes anecdote is traditional and reported through Cicero and Quintilian; present it as 'is said to'.", "level": 3}, {"id": "rhetoric-9", "field": "Rhetoric and persuasion", "term": "exordium", "gloss": "The opening of a speech, whose classical task is to make the audience well disposed, attentive and receptive.", "say": "Keep the *exordium* short: thank the panel, state the stake, and get to the case.", "note": "Pronounced ex-OR-dee-um. Useful when planning; in the room itself, it is the effect, not the term, that matters.", "level": 2}, {"id": "rhetoric-10", "field": "Rhetoric and persuasion", "term": "partitio", "gloss": "The part of a speech that announces the divisions of the argument to follow.", "say": "A clear *partitio* at the start, three points and in this order, will keep a committee with us.", "note": "The concept matters more than the word; most people call it 'the roadmap'.", "level": 3}, {"id": "rhetoric-11", "field": "Rhetoric and persuasion", "term": "refutatio", "gloss": "The part of a classical speech that answers opposing arguments, usually placed after one's own proofs.", "say": "Put the *refutatio* after our case, not before; answering objections first makes them the agenda.", "note": "Placement is the insider point; the Latin is for planning conversations.", "level": 3}, {"id": "rhetoric-12", "field": "Rhetoric and persuasion", "term": "peroration", "gloss": "The conclusion of a speech, which classically recapitulates, amplifies and moves the audience to the judgement sought.", "say": "The *peroration* should come back to the students in the first image, not to the budget line.", "note": "A recognised English word among educated speakers; in a speechwriting context it is not affected.", "level": 2}, {"id": "rhetoric-13", "field": "Rhetoric and persuasion", "term": "captatio benevolentiae", "gloss": "The capture of goodwill, typically in the opening, through modesty, courtesy or shared interest.", "say": "A line of *captatio benevolentiae* is wise here: acknowledge how much reading the panel has done before we add to it.", "note": "Pronounced kap-TAH-tee-oh ben-eh-vo-LEN-tee-eye. Overdone, it becomes flattery and costs ethos.", "level": 2}, {"id": "rhetoric-14", "field": "Rhetoric and persuasion", "term": "anaphora", "gloss": "Repetition of a word or phrase at the beginning of successive clauses or sentences.", "say": "The speech turns on *anaphora*: 'Because of you, they rehearsed. Because of you, they performed. Because of you, they stayed.'", "note": "Powerful at ceremonies; in a committee it can sound like an oration where a submission was expected.", "level": 2}, {"id": "rhetoric-15", "field": "Rhetoric and persuasion", "term": "epistrophe", "gloss": "Repetition of a word or phrase at the end of successive clauses or sentences.", "say": "End each paragraph with the same phrase, a sort of *epistrophe*: 'and that is why the program matters'.", "note": "Pronounced eh-PIS-truh-fee. Rarer and more insistent than anaphora; use once per speech.", "level": 2}, {"id": "rhetoric-16", "field": "Rhetoric and persuasion", "term": "antithesis", "gloss": "The juxtaposition of contrasting ideas in balanced, parallel phrasing.", "say": "The line works by *antithesis*: 'We are not asking for more money; we are asking for the money to stay.'", "note": "Strong for the sentence you want quoted; a speech made of antitheses sounds glib.", "level": 1}, {"id": "rhetoric-17", "field": "Rhetoric and persuasion", "term": "chiasmus", "gloss": "A reversal of grammatical structure or ideas in the second half of a parallel construction (ABBA), not necessarily with the same words.", "say": "The close is a *chiasmus*: 'The band was built for the students, and the students made the band.'", "note": "Pronounced kye-AZ-mus. Many use it loosely for any reversal; see antimetabole for the strict word-for-word form.", "level": 2}, {"id": "rhetoric-18", "field": "Rhetoric and persuasion", "term": "antimetabole", "gloss": "Repetition of the same words in reverse order; a strict form of chiasmus.", "say": "Strictly that's *antimetabole*, not just chiasmus: the same words come back reversed, as in 'we teach music, and music teaches us'.", "note": "Pronounced an-tee-meh-TAB-oh-lee. The distinction impresses rhetoricians and nobody else; don't correct people in public.", "level": 2}, {"id": "rhetoric-19", "field": "Rhetoric and persuasion", "term": "tricolon", "gloss": "A series of three parallel elements, words, phrases or clauses.", "say": "The ask is a *tricolon*: keep the staffing, keep the room, keep the timetable.", "note": "Use it for the point you most want remembered; three tricolons in a minute is a tic.", "level": 2}, {"id": "rhetoric-20", "field": "Rhetoric and persuasion", "term": "tricolon crescens", "gloss": "A tricolon whose members grow in length or weight toward the third.", "say": "Build it as *tricolon crescens*: 'They came. They practised every week. And they stayed with the program through three years of change.'", "note": "Pronounced KRESS-enz. The rising third member naturally lands at a pause; let it.", "level": 2}, {"id": "rhetoric-21", "field": "Rhetoric and persuasion", "term": "praeteritio", "gloss": "Drawing attention to something by claiming to pass over it; also called paralipsis or occupatio.", "say": "I won't dwell on the three years of deferred maintenance in that building; I'll simply note that the room floods. That *praeteritio* gets it on the record.", "note": "Pronounced pree-teh-RIT-ee-oh. Transparent and slightly sly; use it for a fact, never for a personal attack.", "level": 2}, {"id": "rhetoric-22", "field": "Rhetoric and persuasion", "term": "litotes", "gloss": "Understatement by negating the contrary, as in 'not inconsiderable'.", "say": "'It was not a small achievement' is *litotes*, and in an Australian room it's often more persuasive than praise.", "note": "Pronounced LYE-tuh-teez. Suits understated Australian register; strings of it read as evasive.", "level": 2}, {"id": "rhetoric-23", "field": "Rhetoric and persuasion", "term": "hypophora", "gloss": "Raising a question and answering it oneself.", "say": "Use *hypophora* to frame the objection: 'Why not run the band after school? Because that's when our students work.'", "note": "Distinct from a rhetorical question, which expects no answer; hypophora controls which question is asked.", "level": 2}, {"id": "rhetoric-24", "field": "Rhetoric and persuasion", "term": "prolepsis", "gloss": "Anticipating an objection and answering it before it is raised; also procatalepsis.", "say": "A bit of *prolepsis* early on helps: 'Some will say this favours students with private lessons. It doesn't, and here's why.'", "note": "Pronounced proh-LEP-sis. Answer only objections the room will actually have, or you seed new ones.", "level": 2}, {"id": "rhetoric-25", "field": "Rhetoric and persuasion", "term": "aposiopesis", "gloss": "Breaking off a sentence before its end, leaving the audience to complete it.", "say": "If we lose the Year 7 intake this year, then by 2029 there will be no ATAR class to ... well. That's *aposiopesis*, and it lets the room finish the thought.", "note": "Pronounced ap-oh-sye-oh-PEE-sis. Deliberate only; an unplanned trailing off reads as losing the thread.", "level": 2}, {"id": "rhetoric-26", "field": "Rhetoric and persuasion", "term": "concessio", "gloss": "Conceding a point to an opponent in order to strengthen one's own position or credibility.", "say": "Start with *concessio*: 'The department is right that the costs have risen.' Then show why the case still holds.", "note": "The concession must be real and specific; a token concession is spotted at once.", "level": 2}, {"id": "rhetoric-27", "field": "Rhetoric and persuasion", "term": "correctio", "gloss": "Correcting or sharpening one's own word in the act of speaking, for emphasis; also epanorthosis.", "say": "'It was a good year; no, a remarkable year' is *correctio*, and it lets you raise the claim without seeming to boast.", "note": "Planned correctio sounds controlled; frequent unplanned self-correction sounds like hedging.", "level": 2}, {"id": "rhetoric-28", "field": "Rhetoric and persuasion", "term": "periodic sentence", "gloss": "A sentence whose main clause, and so its sense, is withheld until the end, creating suspense.", "say": "Write the closing line as a *periodic sentence*: 'After four years, three principals and one flood, the program is still here.'", "note": "Excellent for a climax; too many in a row tire a listening audience.", "level": 2}, {"id": "rhetoric-29", "field": "Rhetoric and persuasion", "term": "loose sentence", "gloss": "A cumulative sentence that states its main clause first and adds modifying elements after.", "say": "Most of the speech should be *loose sentences*; the ear needs the main point first.", "note": "The default for speech; its virtue is that the listener is never kept waiting for the sense.", "level": 2}, {"id": "rhetoric-30", "field": "Rhetoric and persuasion", "term": "decorum", "gloss": "The fitness of style, argument and manner to subject, audience and occasion; Cicero's decorum, the Greek prepon.", "say": "A joke there would breach *decorum*; it's a memorial, not a concert.", "note": "The classical sense is broader than etiquette; use it to discuss fit, not just politeness.", "level": 2}, {"id": "rhetoric-31", "field": "Rhetoric and persuasion", "term": "enargeia", "gloss": "Vivid description that brings a scene before the audience's eyes; distinct from energeia, vigour or actuality.", "say": "Thirty seconds of *enargeia*, a Year 7 in the front row of her first festival, will do more than a page of retention data.", "note": "Pronounced en-AR-gay-uh. Easily confused with energeia; if unsure, say 'vivid description'.", "level": 3}, {"id": "rhetoric-32", "field": "Rhetoric and persuasion", "term": "rhetorical question", "gloss": "A question asked for effect, with no answer expected; distinct from hypophora, where the speaker answers.", "say": "Don't end on a *rhetorical question*; in a funding panel they'll answer it, and not in our favour.", "note": "In adversarial rooms rhetorical questions invite hostile answers; prefer statements.", "level": 1}, {"id": "rhetoric-33", "field": "Rhetoric and persuasion", "term": "anadiplosis", "gloss": "Repetition of the last word of one clause at the beginning of the next.", "say": "The line builds through *anadiplosis*: 'Practice builds skill; skill builds confidence; confidence builds musicians.'", "note": "Pronounced an-uh-dih-PLOH-sis. A strong chain for a ceremony; in a meeting it sounds rehearsed.", "level": 3}, {"id": "rhetoric-34", "field": "Rhetoric and persuasion", "term": "climax", "gloss": "In its rhetorical sense, an arrangement of words, clauses or ideas in ascending order of importance; also gradatio.", "say": "Order the outcomes for *climax*: participation, then achievement, then the students who became teachers.", "note": "In the rhetorical sense it names the ascending arrangement, not just the high point.", "level": 2}, {"id": "rhetoric-35", "field": "Rhetoric and persuasion", "term": "asyndeton", "gloss": "Omitting conjunctions between coordinate elements for speed and force.", "say": "*Asyndeton* suits the summary: 'Three bands, two orchestras, one festival, no extra staff.'", "note": "Pronounced a-SIN-dih-ton. Sharp and businesslike; overused, it sounds like a slogan.", "level": 2}, {"id": "rhetoric-36", "field": "Rhetoric and persuasion", "term": "polysyndeton", "gloss": "Using more conjunctions than necessary, to slow the line and accumulate weight.", "say": "In the tribute, *polysyndeton* gives the weight: 'and the rehearsals and the tours and the early mornings and the late returns'.", "note": "Suits ceremony and gravity; in a report it reads as padding.", "level": 2}, {"id": "rhetoric-37", "field": "Rhetoric and persuasion", "term": "isocolon", "gloss": "A sequence of clauses of equal length and parallel structure.", "say": "The balance is *isocolon*: 'We hear them weekly; we judge them yearly; we remember them always.'", "note": "Pronounced eye-soh-KOH-lon. Its effect is felt more than noticed; naming it in public is unnecessary.", "level": 3}, {"id": "rhetoric-38", "field": "Rhetoric and persuasion", "term": "parallelism", "gloss": "Using similar grammatical structures for related ideas, so that sense and rhythm reinforce each other.", "say": "The recommendations need *parallelism*; at the moment two are verbs, one is a noun and one is a question.", "note": "The plain workhorse behind most figures; in written submissions it also signals care.", "level": 1}, {"id": "rhetoric-39", "field": "Rhetoric and persuasion", "term": "epizeuxis", "gloss": "Immediate repetition of a word for emphasis.", "say": "One *epizeuxis* is enough for the close: 'This matters. It matters, it matters for every child in this room.'", "note": "Pronounced ep-ih-ZOOK-sis. Easy to overdo; a single instance at the climax is the limit.", "level": 3}, {"id": "rhetoric-40", "field": "Rhetoric and persuasion", "term": "metonymy", "gloss": "Referring to something by a closely associated thing, as in 'the Crown' for the state.", "say": "When we say 'the department wants', that's *metonymy*; there's a person with a name making the decision.", "note": "Pronounced meh-TON-ih-mee. Useful for exposing how institutional language hides agency.", "level": 2}, {"id": "rhetoric-41", "field": "Rhetoric and persuasion", "term": "synecdoche", "gloss": "Using a part for the whole or the whole for a part, as in 'hands' for workers.", "say": "'Every chair in the band room' is *synecdoche* for every student; it lets the image carry the number.", "note": "Pronounced sin-EK-duh-kee. Often confused with metonymy; the part-whole relation is the test.", "level": 2}, {"id": "rhetoric-42", "field": "Rhetoric and persuasion", "term": "meiosis", "gloss": "Understatement that presents something as smaller or less important than it is; litotes is understatement by negation.", "say": "'A slight inconvenience' for a flooded band room is *meiosis*, and the panel will feel the irony.", "note": "Pronounced mye-OH-sis. Keep the distinction from litotes for precise company; the effect is the same family.", "level": 3}, {"id": "rhetoric-43", "field": "Rhetoric and persuasion", "term": "amplification", "gloss": "Expanding and dwelling on a point, through detail, repetition or comparison, to heighten its importance; amplificatio.", "say": "The figure needs *amplification*: three hundred students is a number; three hundred students who rehearse before school is a commitment.", "note": "Amplify one point; amplifying everything flattens the speech.", "level": 2}, {"id": "rhetoric-44", "field": "Rhetoric and persuasion", "term": "the rhetorical situation", "gloss": "Lloyd Bitzer's account of rhetoric as a response to a situation made of an exigence, an audience able to act on it, and constraints.", "say": "Read the *rhetorical situation* first: who in that room can actually change the allocation?", "note": "Well known in rhetoric and communication studies; elsewhere it is just good analysis without the label.", "level": 2}, {"id": "rhetoric-45", "field": "Rhetoric and persuasion", "term": "exigence", "gloss": "In Bitzer's sense, an imperfection marked by urgency that discourse can help to modify.", "say": "The *exigence* isn't the budget in general; it's the decision on Thursday.", "note": "Pronounced EK-sih-jens. Distinct from 'exigency' in ordinary use; rarely needed outside rhetoric.", "level": 3}, {"id": "rhetoric-46", "field": "Rhetoric and persuasion", "term": "stasis theory", "gloss": "The classical method, associated with Hermagoras, of locating the point at issue: conjecture (did it happen), definition (what is it), quality (was it justified) and jurisdiction or procedure.", "say": "By *stasis theory* we're arguing about definition, whether this is a cut, when the real disagreement is about quality, whether it's justified.", "note": "Superb for diagnosing why a committee is going round in circles; the plain version is 'what exactly are we disagreeing about?'", "level": 3}, {"id": "rhetoric-47", "field": "Rhetoric and persuasion", "term": "topoi", "gloss": "The 'places' of argument: general lines of reasoning or commonplaces from which arguments can be drawn, such as cause and effect, more and less, or precedent.", "say": "The obvious *topoi* here are precedent and consequence; nobody has tried definition.", "note": "Pronounced TOP-oy. Singular topos. Useful in planning; in debate, just use the argument.", "level": 2}, {"id": "rhetoric-48", "field": "Rhetoric and persuasion", "term": "deliberative rhetoric", "gloss": "Aristotle's genre concerned with future action and what is advantageous or harmful; the rhetoric of councils and committees.", "say": "This is *deliberative*, not forensic: the question isn't who was at fault, it's what we do next year.", "note": "Re-classifying a blame-laden discussion as deliberative is a strong chairing move.", "level": 2}, {"id": "rhetoric-49", "field": "Rhetoric and persuasion", "term": "forensic rhetoric", "gloss": "Aristotle's judicial genre, concerned with past acts and what is just or unjust; also called judicial.", "say": "The meeting has drifted into *forensic* mode, reconstructing what happened at the festival, when we came to decide policy.", "note": "Not confined to courts; it describes any argument about past conduct.", "level": 2}, {"id": "rhetoric-50", "field": "Rhetoric and persuasion", "term": "epideictic", "gloss": "Aristotle's ceremonial genre of praise and blame, concerned with present values and the honourable; the rhetoric of tributes, awards and occasions.", "say": "Speech night is *epideictic*: we're affirming what the community values, not arguing for funding.", "note": "Pronounced ep-ih-DYKE-tik. Recognising that ceremonies also reinforce values helps you write them with purpose.", "level": 2}, {"id": "rhetoric-51", "field": "Rhetoric and persuasion", "term": "framing", "gloss": "Selecting and emphasising aspects of an issue so that a particular interpretation, cause or remedy becomes salient.", "say": "The *framing* matters: 'consolidation' invites agreement, 'closure' invites resistance, and they're the same proposal.", "note": "Naming the other side's frame explicitly often neutralises it.", "level": 1}, {"id": "rhetoric-52", "field": "Rhetoric and persuasion", "term": "the ask", "gloss": "The specific action or decision requested of the audience.", "say": "Put *the ask* in the first paragraph and the last; the panel shouldn't have to find it.", "note": "Plain and current; vagueness about the ask is the commonest failure in submissions.", "level": 1}, {"id": "rhetoric-53", "field": "Rhetoric and persuasion", "term": "signposting", "gloss": "Verbal markers that tell the audience where they are in an argument and where it is going.", "say": "A bit more *signposting* would help: 'That's the cost. Now the benefit.'", "note": "Indispensable for listeners; overdone, it becomes a table of contents read aloud.", "level": 1}, {"id": "rhetoric-54", "field": "Rhetoric and persuasion", "term": "through-line", "gloss": "The single idea that runs through a speech or document and organises everything in it.", "say": "The *through-line* of the submission is continuity: every section should show what's lost if it breaks.", "note": "If you can't state the through-line in a sentence, the speech doesn't have one yet.", "level": 1}, {"id": "rhetoric-55", "field": "Rhetoric and persuasion", "term": "register", "gloss": "The variety of language suited to a context, set by formality, vocabulary and syntax.", "say": "The draft is in the wrong *register*; it reads like a newsletter, and this is going to the Minister's office.", "note": "Shifting register within a speech can be powerful; failing to notice it is the risk.", "level": 1}, {"id": "rhetoric-56", "field": "Rhetoric and persuasion", "term": "cadence", "gloss": "The rise and fall of a sentence, especially its close, in rhythm and pitch.", "say": "Let the *cadence* fall at the end of the sentence; you're lifting every ending into a question.", "note": "Relevant to anyone who speaks with an upward inflection under pressure.", "level": 1}, {"id": "rhetoric-57", "field": "Rhetoric and persuasion", "term": "the strategic pause", "gloss": "A deliberate silence before or after a key line, which marks importance and gives the audience time to absorb it.", "say": "Leave a *pause* after the number; let the room do the arithmetic.", "note": "The single most underused tool of authority; a pause feels longer to the speaker than to the audience.", "level": 1}, {"id": "rhetoric-58", "field": "Rhetoric and persuasion", "term": "identification", "gloss": "Kenneth Burke's term for the process by which a speaker persuades by establishing shared substance, interests or identity with the audience.", "say": "The opening works through *identification* in Burke's sense: we're all people who were once the kid in the back row of a band.", "note": "Rare outside rhetoric; elsewhere say 'common ground', which is narrower but understood.", "level": 3}, {"id": "rhetoric-59", "field": "Rhetoric and persuasion", "term": "the plain style", "gloss": "The lowest of the three classical styles (plain, middle, grand): clear, unornamented and suited to teaching and explanation.", "say": "For the committee, the *plain style*; save the grand style for the gala.", "note": "In expert rooms the plain style usually signals the most authority.", "level": 2}, {"id": "rhetoric-60", "field": "Rhetoric and persuasion", "term": "sententia", "gloss": "A pithy maxim or general truth, often placed to close a passage.", "say": "End the section on a *sententia*: 'A program is only as strong as its weakest year.'", "note": "Pronounced sen-TEN-shee-uh. One per speech; more and it starts to sound like a fridge magnet.", "level": 3}, {"id": "rhetoric-61", "field": "Rhetoric and persuasion", "term": "exemplum", "gloss": "An illustrative example or short story offered as evidence or model for a general point.", "say": "Use one *exemplum*, the student who joined in Year 7 and now teaches at the school, rather than six statistics.", "note": "Choose an exemplum the audience cannot dismiss as an outlier.", "level": 2}, {"id": "rhetoric-62", "field": "Rhetoric and persuasion", "term": "in utramque partem", "gloss": "Arguing on both sides of a question, the Ciceronian practice that trains judgement and reveals a case's weaknesses.", "say": "Before the meeting, argue it *in utramque partem*; if the other side's case is stronger than ours, better to know now.", "note": "Pronounced in oo-TRAM-kweh PAR-tem. A planning practice; you'll rarely say the phrase aloud in the room.", "level": 3}, {"id": "rhetoric-63", "field": "Rhetoric and persuasion", "term": "recapitulation", "gloss": "A brief restatement of the main points, usually at the close.", "say": "A one-sentence *recapitulation* before the ask: three points, one decision.", "note": "Short is everything; a recapitulation longer than the points loses the room.", "level": 1}, {"id": "rhetoric-64", "field": "Rhetoric and persuasion", "term": "audience analysis", "gloss": "Systematic consideration of who the audience is, what they know, value and fear, and what they can decide.", "say": "The *audience analysis* says the Deputy Director General cares about equity data, not concerts.", "note": "Practical and uncontroversial; the insider move is to analyse the decision-maker, not the room.", "level": 1}, {"id": "rhetoric-65", "field": "Rhetoric and persuasion", "term": "derived ethos", "gloss": "Credibility the speaker earns during the speech itself, as distinct from initial ethos brought in and terminal ethos left behind.", "say": "We have little initial ethos with that panel, so the *derived ethos* has to come from precision in the first minute.", "note": "The three-stage distinction is used in speech communication; it helps plan for a hostile or unfamiliar room.", "level": 2}, {"id": "rhetoric-66", "field": "Rhetoric and persuasion", "term": "apostrophe", "gloss": "Turning from the audience to address an absent person, an abstraction or the dead directly.", "say": "There's an *apostrophe* in the eulogy, where she speaks to him directly; it's the emotional centre.", "note": "Belongs to ceremony and elegy; in a meeting it would be theatrical.", "level": 3}, {"id": "rhetoric-67", "field": "Rhetoric and persuasion", "term": "dysphemism", "gloss": "Substituting a harsher or more offensive term for a neutral one; the opposite of euphemism.", "say": "Calling the ensemble 'a babysitting service' is a *dysphemism*, and it tells us more about the argument than about the ensemble.", "note": "Naming the move calmly is often enough to strip it of force.", "level": 2}, {"id": "rhetoric-68", "field": "Rhetoric and persuasion", "term": "parrhesia", "gloss": "Frank speech; as a figure, claiming licence to speak candidly, often at risk to oneself, before someone with power.", "say": "I'll exercise a little *parrhesia*, Principal: the plan as written will lose us the program.", "note": "Pronounced pa-REE-zee-uh. Saying the word is rarely wise; doing the thing, sparingly and respectfully, is what counts.", "level": 3}, {"id": "rhetoric-69", "field": "Rhetoric and persuasion", "term": "soundbite", "gloss": "A short, self-contained line designed to be quoted out of context and still carry the argument.", "say": "We need one *soundbite* for the newsletter and the media: 'Every student who starts, finishes.'", "note": "Write it deliberately and check that it survives being quoted alone.", "level": 1}, {"id": "rhetoric-70", "field": "Rhetoric and persuasion", "term": "bridging", "gloss": "Acknowledging a question briefly and moving to the message one wants to deliver.", "say": "Answer the question about cost directly, then *bridge*: 'And what that cost buys is continuity.'", "note": "Legitimate after a real answer; bridging without answering is visible to an experienced room.", "level": 1}, {"id": "rhetoric-71", "field": "Rhetoric and persuasion", "term": "bottom line up front", "gloss": "Placing the conclusion or recommendation first, then the reasoning; often abbreviated BLUF.", "say": "In the briefing, *bottom line up front*: we recommend no change to the audition process.", "note": "From military and civil-service writing; it suits busy decision-makers and frustrates audiences who need to be brought along first.", "level": 1}, {"id": "rhetoric-72", "field": "Rhetoric and persuasion", "term": "controlling metaphor", "gloss": "A single metaphor sustained across a speech or document to organise and unify it.", "say": "The *controlling metaphor* is the ecosystem; don't switch to a pipeline halfway through.", "note": "Mixed metaphors are the risk; check the vocabulary of every section against the controlling one.", "level": 1}, {"id": "rhetoric-73", "field": "Rhetoric and persuasion", "term": "the turn", "gloss": "The point at which a speech or passage pivots from one movement to another, from problem to solution or concession to argument; the volta in poetics.", "say": "The *turn* comes too late; we're two-thirds through before we say what we want.", "note": "Make the turn audible with a pause or a 'but'; listeners miss quiet turns.", "level": 1}, {"id": "rhetoric-74", "field": "Rhetoric and persuasion", "term": "sprezzatura", "gloss": "Castiglione's term for studied nonchalance: art that conceals the effort behind it.", "say": "The ideal for the speech is *sprezzatura*: rehearsed to the word and delivered as if it had just occurred to you.", "note": "Pronounced spret-sah-TOO-rah. A musician's natural concept; say it among people who'll enjoy it.", "level": 3}, {"id": "rhetoric-75", "field": "Rhetoric and persuasion", "term": "presence", "gloss": "In Perelman and Olbrechts-Tyteca's New Rhetoric, the effect of selecting and dwelling on certain elements so that they occupy the foreground of the audience's mind.", "say": "We need to give the students *presence* in the submission; at the moment they're a line item.", "note": "A technical term in argumentation theory; outside it, 'keep them in front of the panel' says the same.", "level": 3}, {"id": "rhetoric-76", "field": "Rhetoric and persuasion", "term": "rhetor", "gloss": "The speaker or author as the agent of persuasion, considered in relation to audience and situation.", "say": "The *rhetor* matters here; a parent saying this carries more weight than I do.", "note": "Pronounced REE-tor. Common in rhetoric scholarship; elsewhere 'the speaker' or 'the messenger'.", "level": 2}, {"id": "rhetoric-77", "field": "Rhetoric and persuasion", "term": "hedge", "gloss": "A qualifier that softens a claim's force, such as 'perhaps' or 'I think'; useful for calibrated claims and corrosive when habitual.", "say": "Cut the *hedges* from the first paragraph; we're certain about the numbers, so sound like it.", "note": "Calibrated hedging signals rigour; reflexive hedging under pressure reads as doubt about your own case.", "level": 1}, {"id": "rhetoric-78", "field": "Rhetoric and persuasion", "term": "set piece", "gloss": "A rehearsed passage delivered with polish, often the planned high point of a speech.", "say": "The *set piece* is the student's story; everything else should be plain so that it stands out.", "note": "Effective when surrounded by plainer material; a speech of set pieces feels performed.", "level": 1}, {"id": "rhetoric-79", "field": "Rhetoric and persuasion", "term": "pacing", "gloss": "Managing the speed and variation of delivery and the distribution of material across the time available.", "say": "Watch the *pacing* in the first minute; nerves will push you to rush exactly where the panel decides about you.", "note": "Variation matters more than speed; slow down for numbers and for the ask.", "level": 1}, {"id": "rhetoric-80", "field": "Rhetoric and persuasion", "term": "callback", "gloss": "Returning near the end to an image, phrase or story from the opening, to close the frame.", "say": "End with a *callback* to the empty band room from the opening, now full.", "note": "Creates a sense of design; it fails if the opening image wasn't memorable enough to recall.", "level": 1}]}, {"field": "Arts, music and cultural policy", "intro": "Arts policy speaks two languages at once: the economist's and evaluator's vocabulary of value, outcomes and market failure, and the practitioner's vocabulary of ecology, protocol and practice. Sounding native means moving between them without apology, conceding the limits of your evidence before a panel does, and naming the frameworks you rely on precisely.", "entries": [{"id": "arts-policy-1", "field": "Arts, music and cultural policy", "term": "intrinsic value", "gloss": "The worth of an artistic experience in and for itself, as distinct from what it produces elsewhere; the term names a kind of value, not a claim that it cannot be evidenced.", "say": "Before we reach for attendance and wellbeing data, I want the panel to hear the *intrinsic value* case plainly, because that is what the students actually came for.", "note": "Lands when you state it calmly and then move on; backfires if it becomes a refusal to be accountable, which is how funders hear it when it is repeated.", "level": 1}, {"id": "arts-policy-2", "field": "Arts, music and cultural policy", "term": "instrumental value", "gloss": "Value the arts generate for ends outside themselves, such as health, attainment, social cohesion or economic return.", "say": "The *instrumental value* argument will get us into the Minister's office, but it should not be the only thing we say once we are there.", "note": "Use it as a neutral descriptor; said with a sneer it signals you have not read the policy literature, which treats the two kinds of value as complementary.", "level": 1}, {"id": "arts-policy-3", "field": "Arts, music and cultural policy", "term": "institutional value", "gloss": "John Holden's third category (Demos, 2006): the value an organisation creates through how it behaves towards the public, such as trust, fairness and conviviality, beyond its programs.", "say": "The festival's *institutional value* is that every school, however small its band, is treated with the same courtesy at the desk and on the stage.", "note": "Lands with policy people who know Holden's intrinsic, instrumental and institutional triad; elsewhere you will need a sentence of explanation, so give it without the citation.", "level": 3}, {"id": "arts-policy-4", "field": "Arts, music and cultural policy", "term": "cultural value", "gloss": "The umbrella question of how the worth of arts and culture is understood and evidenced, associated in the literature with the AHRC Cultural Value Project (Crossick and Kaszynska, 2016).", "say": "If the committee wants a *cultural value* framework, I would rather we adapt an existing one than invent our own rubric over morning tea.", "note": "It is a field of inquiry, not a single number; treating it as a metric you can report sounds naive to anyone from the research side.", "level": 2}, {"id": "arts-policy-5", "field": "Arts, music and cultural policy", "term": "arts ecology", "gloss": "The interdependent system of artists, organisations, venues, educators, funders and audiences, where damage to one part is felt elsewhere.", "say": "School music is the root system of the state's *arts ecology*; cut it and the effect on the professional sector arrives a decade later.", "note": "Lands when you name the specific dependencies; used loosely it is a familiar sector cliché and panels have heard it many times.", "level": 1}, {"id": "arts-policy-6", "field": "Arts, music and cultural policy", "term": "pathway", "gloss": "A designed, sequenced route of progression for a learner or artist, with defined points of entry, transition and exit.", "say": "Our job on this committee is to make the *pathway* from Year 7 to tertiary audition legible to a family who has never met a conservatoire.", "note": "Stronger when you specify the transitions; a pathway that is only a diagram of stages without support at each step will be called out.", "level": 1}, {"id": "arts-policy-7", "field": "Arts, music and cultural policy", "term": "pipeline", "gloss": "A supply-side metaphor for talent flowing toward elite or professional outcomes; unlike a pathway, it implies a single direction and invites talk of leakage.", "say": "I am wary of *pipeline* language because it treats every student who stops at Year 10 as a leak rather than a musician for life.", "note": "Useful when you want to critique a narrow elite focus; using it uncritically yourself can make a participation program sound like a talent factory.", "level": 2}, {"id": "arts-policy-8", "field": "Arts, music and cultural policy", "term": "participation versus excellence", "gloss": "The persistent policy tension between broad engagement in the arts and investment in the highest artistic standards, often treated as a false binary.", "say": "I do not accept the *participation versus excellence* framing for this program: the senior big band exists because two hundred juniors played first.", "note": "Name the tension and then dissolve it with evidence from your own program; simply asserting that it is a false binary sounds like a slogan.", "level": 2}, {"id": "arts-policy-9", "field": "Arts, music and cultural policy", "term": "access", "gloss": "The removal of barriers, whether financial, geographic, physical or cultural, to engaging with the arts; distinct from participation, which is actual engagement.", "say": "We have solved *access* for the audition, since it is free, but participation still skews to families who already own an instrument.", "note": "Precise when you separate it from participation and equity; used as a catch-all it blurs the argument.", "level": 1}, {"id": "arts-policy-10", "field": "Arts, music and cultural policy", "term": "regional reach", "gloss": "The extent to which a program, organisation or funding stream delivers outside metropolitan centres, a standing criterion in Australian arts funding.", "say": "If we want state support for the festival, our *regional reach* has to be more than one school from Bunbury every second year.", "note": "Lands when quantified honestly; overstated reach is easy for a panel to check against the participant list.", "level": 1}, {"id": "arts-policy-11", "field": "Arts, music and cultural policy", "term": "peak body", "gloss": "A representative organisation that speaks for a whole sector or profession to government and the public, rather than delivering programs itself.", "say": "This should go to the relevant *peak body* first, so the submission carries the weight of the sector rather than one school.", "note": "Standard Australian usage; overseas colleagues may say umbrella or membership organisation instead.", "level": 1}, {"id": "arts-policy-12", "field": "Arts, music and cultural policy", "term": "auspicing", "gloss": "An arrangement in which an incorporated organisation receives and administers a grant on behalf of an unincorporated group or individual, and carries legal and financial responsibility for it.", "say": "The parent ensemble is not incorporated, so AJEA could consider *auspicing* the grant, provided the board understands it then answers for the acquittal.", "note": "Pronounced AW-spis-ing. Lands when you make clear that the auspice carries real liability; treating it as a mere signature is a governance error.", "level": 2}, {"id": "arts-policy-13", "field": "Arts, music and cultural policy", "term": "acquittal", "gloss": "The formal report accounting for how grant funds were spent and what was delivered, measured against the funding agreement.", "say": "Before we apply again, I want last year's *acquittal* closed out, because a late one is the quickest way to lose a funder's confidence.", "note": "Standard Australian funding term; in other jurisdictions the equivalent is often a final or grant report.", "level": 1}, {"id": "arts-policy-14", "field": "Arts, music and cultural policy", "term": "in-kind support", "gloss": "Non-cash contributions such as venue hire, staff time or equipment, valued and declared as part of a project's budget.", "say": "The school's contribution is substantial *in-kind support*: the hall, the risers, the staff time and the piano tuning.", "note": "Panels expect it to be costed at a realistic rate; inflated in-kind figures are noticed and damage credibility.", "level": 1}, {"id": "arts-policy-15", "field": "Arts, music and cultural policy", "term": "arm's length principle", "gloss": "The convention that arts funding decisions are made by expert peers or an independent body at a distance from ministers, to protect artistic freedom from political interference.", "say": "The *arm's length principle* protects us as much as it constrains us: no one in government gets to choose which charts we play.", "note": "Lands in governance and funding discussions; invoking it to avoid all accountability to government sounds precious.", "level": 2}, {"id": "arts-policy-16", "field": "Arts, music and cultural policy", "term": "peer assessment", "gloss": "Evaluation of funding applications by practitioners and experts from the relevant art form rather than by administrators alone.", "say": "I have sat on *peer assessment* panels, and the applications that succeed are the ones a stranger can understand in four minutes.", "note": "Neutral descriptor; useful to signal you know how decisions are actually made inside a funding body.", "level": 1}, {"id": "arts-policy-17", "field": "Arts, music and cultural policy", "term": "multi-year organisational funding", "gloss": "Recurrent funding committed to an organisation over several years, as distinct from project funding for a single activity.", "say": "AJEA will never be stable on project grants alone; *multi-year organisational funding* is the conversation we should start now, even if it takes three cycles.", "note": "Signals sector literacy; be careful with specific program names, which change between funding rounds.", "level": 2}, {"id": "arts-policy-18", "field": "Arts, music and cultural policy", "term": "Revive", "gloss": "Australia's National Cultural Policy, released in January 2023 as Revive: a place for every story, a story for every place, organised around five pillars.", "say": "Our submission should map directly to *Revive*, particularly the pillars on First Nations First and engaging the audience.", "note": "Lands when you show you know the pillars rather than just the title; quoting it as though it carried funding of its own overstates what a policy document does.", "level": 2}, {"id": "arts-policy-19", "field": "Arts, music and cultural policy", "term": "First Nations First", "gloss": "The first pillar of Revive, recognising and respecting the crucial place of First Nations stories at the centre of Australian arts and culture.", "say": "If we claim *First Nations First* in the application, it has to show in who leads the work, not only in the acknowledgement on page one.", "note": "Use it as a commitment you can evidence; used decoratively it reads as tokenism to First Nations panel members.", "level": 2}, {"id": "arts-policy-20", "field": "Arts, music and cultural policy", "term": "the centrality of the artist", "gloss": "A Revive pillar asserting that artists are workers whose pay, conditions and careers are a policy concern, not simply a means to programs.", "say": "Paying the guest musicians properly is not generosity; it is *the centrality of the artist* in practice.", "note": "Particularly effective at a festival or board table when fees are being trimmed; it reframes the cost as a policy obligation.", "level": 2}, {"id": "arts-policy-21", "field": "Arts, music and cultural policy", "term": "Creative Australia", "gloss": "The Australian Government's principal arts investment and advisory body, established under the Creative Australia Act 2023 as the successor to the Australia Council for the Arts.", "say": "*Creative Australia* is unlikely to fund a school program directly, but it may well fund the professional artists who come into it.", "note": "Use the current name; saying the Australia Council marks you as out of date, though older colleagues will still use it.", "level": 1}, {"id": "arts-policy-22", "field": "Arts, music and cultural policy", "term": "Music Australia", "gloss": "The dedicated music body established within Creative Australia to support the contemporary music industry and its development.", "say": "*Music Australia* is focused on the contemporary industry, so we should frame the jazz education case in terms of the industry's future workforce.", "note": "Signals current knowledge of the Commonwealth architecture; check its current program priorities before naming them, as they move.", "level": 2}, {"id": "arts-policy-23", "field": "Arts, music and cultural policy", "term": "cultural infrastructure", "gloss": "The physical and organisational foundations that make cultural activity possible: venues, rehearsal spaces, collections, and the organisations and skills that sustain them.", "say": "A school performing arts centre is *cultural infrastructure* for the suburb, and it is idle most evenings.", "note": "Lands when you include the soft infrastructure of people and organisations; treating it as buildings only invites a capital-works conversation you may not want.", "level": 2}, {"id": "arts-policy-24", "field": "Arts, music and cultural policy", "term": "creative industries versus cultural industries", "gloss": "Cultural industries descends from Adorno and Horkheimer's critical term 'culture industry' and centres symbolic production; creative industries, popularised by the UK's 1998 mapping document, extends to IP-generating sectors such as software and design.", "say": "Under a *creative industries* lens, our students become a workforce; under a *cultural industries* lens, they are makers of meaning, and the policy follows from which one we choose.", "note": "Lands with academics and policy officers; used with people who treat the terms as synonyms it can sound like hair-splitting unless you show why it changes the funding logic.", "level": 3}, {"id": "arts-policy-25", "field": "Arts, music and cultural policy", "term": "cultural rights", "gloss": "The human right to take part in cultural life, recognised in Article 27 of the Universal Declaration of Human Rights and Article 15 of the ICESCR.", "say": "Framed as *cultural rights*, music education is not an enrichment extra; it is part of what a public school owes every child.", "note": "Powerful in advocacy when used sparingly; leaning on it in an operational budget meeting can sound grandiose.", "level": 2}, {"id": "arts-policy-26", "field": "Arts, music and cultural policy", "term": "cultural democracy versus the democratisation of culture", "gloss": "Democratisation of culture extends access to an established canon; cultural democracy supports people to make and value their own culture on their own terms.", "say": "The feeder band is the *democratisation of culture*; the student-led songwriting collective is closer to *cultural democracy*, and we should fund both knowingly.", "note": "A distinction that marks real reading in cultural policy; lands best when you attach a concrete example, otherwise it sounds abstract.", "level": 3}, {"id": "arts-policy-27", "field": "Arts, music and cultural policy", "term": "Indigenous Cultural and Intellectual Property (ICIP)", "gloss": "The rights First Nations peoples hold in their cultural heritage, knowledge and expressions, which extend beyond what copyright protects; strongly associated in Australia with the work of Terri Janke.", "say": "Before the ensemble performs that arrangement, we need to be satisfied on *ICIP*: who holds the song, and have they agreed to this use.", "note": "Say I-C-I-P letter by letter. Lands when it leads to an action; raising it without a process for consent can stall projects and frustrate First Nations collaborators.", "level": 2}, {"id": "arts-policy-28", "field": "Arts, music and cultural policy", "term": "cultural protocols", "gloss": "Agreed principles for respectful engagement with First Nations cultural material and communities, covering consent, attribution, benefit sharing and cultural integrity.", "say": "We follow *cultural protocols* because they are how the relationship works, not because the funding agreement requires a box ticked.", "note": "Lands when framed as relationship and process; framed as compliance it suggests you have missed the point.", "level": 2}, {"id": "arts-policy-29", "field": "Arts, music and cultural policy", "term": "free, prior and informed consent", "gloss": "Consent given voluntarily, before an activity begins, with full information, by those entitled to give it; a standard articulated in the UN Declaration on the Rights of Indigenous Peoples.", "say": "*Free, prior and informed consent* means we ask before the arrangement is written, not after the concert is programmed.", "note": "Each of the three words carries weight; consent sought after decisions are made is not prior, and a panel member who knows the standard will notice.", "level": 2}, {"id": "arts-policy-30", "field": "Arts, music and cultural policy", "term": "Welcome to Country versus Acknowledgement of Country", "gloss": "A Welcome to Country is given only by a Traditional Owner or Custodian of that land; an Acknowledgement of Country may be given by anyone, including non-Indigenous people.", "say": "We have booked a *Welcome to Country* from a Whadjuk Noongar Elder for the opening night, and I will give an *Acknowledgement of Country* at the awards.", "note": "Confusing the two is a common error that First Nations colleagues notice immediately; never say you will 'do the Welcome' yourself.", "level": 1}, {"id": "arts-policy-31", "field": "Arts, music and cultural policy", "term": "Traditional Custodians", "gloss": "The First Nations people with continuing cultural connection to and responsibility for a particular area of land and waters.", "say": "The school stands on Whadjuk Noongar boodja, and the *Traditional Custodians* should be part of planning, not only part of the ceremony.", "note": "Use the specific nation where you know it; a generic reference where a specific one is known can sound perfunctory.", "level": 1}, {"id": "arts-policy-32", "field": "Arts, music and cultural policy", "term": "self-determination", "gloss": "The right of First Nations peoples to make decisions about matters affecting them, including how their culture is represented and taught.", "say": "The principle of *self-determination* means the community decides what this project is, and we decide whether we can support it.", "note": "Lands when you show where decision-making power actually sits; claiming it for a project that is school-led throughout invites a fair challenge.", "level": 2}, {"id": "arts-policy-33", "field": "Arts, music and cultural policy", "term": "community music", "gloss": "Intentional, participatory music-making facilitated outside or alongside formal institutions, valuing inclusion and the group's own goals over a set curriculum or standard.", "say": "The Saturday ensemble is closer to *community music* than to a school program, so its success measures should be different.", "note": "Lands when you respect it as a distinct practice with its own scholarship; using it to mean 'less serious' offends practitioners.", "level": 1}, {"id": "arts-policy-34", "field": "Arts, music and cultural policy", "term": "socially engaged practice", "gloss": "Art-making in which collaboration with communities and social process are central to the work, not merely its context.", "say": "The aged-care residency was *socially engaged practice*: the residents shaped the repertoire, and the students learned to listen differently.", "note": "Common in visual arts and contemporary policy; in music circles you may need to give an example to land it.", "level": 2}, {"id": "arts-policy-35", "field": "Arts, music and cultural policy", "term": "conservatoire model", "gloss": "The tradition of specialist, performance-centred training built on audition entry, intensive one-to-one tuition and progression by demonstrated mastery.", "say": "Our selective program borrows the *conservatoire model* for its seniors, but we would be foolish to impose it on twelve-year-olds.", "note": "Lands in tertiary and SCSA committees; criticising it wholesale sounds uninformed, since much of the profession is trained within it.", "level": 2}, {"id": "arts-policy-36", "field": "Arts, music and cultural policy", "term": "master-apprentice tradition", "gloss": "Transmission of musical knowledge through sustained close study with one expert, emphasising embodied, tacit knowledge over codified curriculum.", "say": "Jazz is still largely a *master-apprentice tradition*, so a syllabus that only assesses what can be written down misses most of what is learned.", "note": "Lands well in jazz and tertiary contexts; acknowledge its risks too, such as dependence and abuse of power, or it sounds nostalgic.", "level": 2}, {"id": "arts-policy-37", "field": "Arts, music and cultural policy", "term": "sequential, developmental music education", "gloss": "Music learning that builds cumulatively over years under specialist teaching, rather than episodic exposure; a staple phrase of music education advocacy since the 2005 National Review of School Music Education.", "say": "What the primary schools lack is not enthusiasm; it is *sequential, developmental music education* with a specialist in the room every week.", "note": "Expected language in advocacy; be ready to say what sequence actually means in your program, or it becomes a slogan.", "level": 2}, {"id": "arts-policy-38", "field": "Arts, music and cultural policy", "term": "far transfer", "gloss": "The claim that music learning improves unrelated abilities such as mathematics or general intelligence; meta-analytic support for far transfer is weak.", "say": "I would keep *far transfer* claims out of the submission; the evidence is thin and a sceptical reader will discount everything else we say.", "note": "Shows real command of the research; the claim that music makes children better at maths backfires with academics, so leave it to others.", "level": 3}, {"id": "arts-policy-39", "field": "Arts, music and cultural policy", "term": "advocacy versus lobbying", "gloss": "Advocacy makes a public case for a cause; lobbying is direct representation to decision-makers to influence a specific decision, and may attract regulation when done by third parties for clients.", "say": "As a school we can do *advocacy* in the open; *lobbying* a particular Minister is a job for the association, with the board's authority.", "note": "Lands in board discussions about who may speak for whom; public servants in particular are careful with this line.", "level": 2}, {"id": "arts-policy-40", "field": "Arts, music and cultural policy", "term": "evidence base", "gloss": "The body of research and data supporting a claim or policy, with attention to its quality and fit to context.", "say": "Our *evidence base* is strong on retention and weak on outcomes after Year 12; let us say so before the panel does.", "note": "Admitting the limits of your own evidence base raises credibility; overstating it lowers it.", "level": 1}, {"id": "arts-policy-41", "field": "Arts, music and cultural policy", "term": "theory of change", "gloss": "An explicit account of the causal logic linking a program's activities to its intended outcomes, including the assumptions in between.", "say": "Our *theory of change* assumes that a free instrument removes the main barrier; the attrition data suggests it is actually transport.", "note": "Lands with evaluators and funders; as decoration on an application without testable assumptions it is easily seen through.", "level": 2}, {"id": "arts-policy-42", "field": "Arts, music and cultural policy", "term": "outputs, outcomes and impact", "gloss": "Outputs are what a program delivers (concerts, lessons); outcomes are the changes it produces in participants; impact is the longer-term, broader change attributable to it.", "say": "Forty concerts is an *output*; students still playing at twenty-five would be an *outcome*; we should be modest about claiming *impact*.", "note": "Funders expect the distinction; reporting outputs as outcomes is the commonest weakness in arts acquittals.", "level": 1}, {"id": "arts-policy-43", "field": "Arts, music and cultural policy", "term": "attribution versus contribution", "gloss": "Attribution claims a program caused an outcome; contribution claims a plausible, evidenced share in an outcome with several causes.", "say": "We cannot claim *attribution* for students who later study music at university, but we can make a sound *contribution* case.", "note": "Signals evaluation literacy; it is the honest framing for most arts outcomes and senior public servants respect it.", "level": 3}, {"id": "arts-policy-44", "field": "Arts, music and cultural policy", "term": "deadweight", "gloss": "In evaluation, the share of an outcome that would have occurred without the intervention.", "say": "Some of these students would have taken lessons anyway; the panel will ask about *deadweight*, so we should estimate it ourselves.", "note": "Lands with economists and Treasury-minded officials; in arts company it can sound cold unless you explain it.", "level": 3}, {"id": "arts-policy-45", "field": "Arts, music and cultural policy", "term": "counterfactual", "gloss": "What would have happened in the absence of the program or decision, the comparison against which effect is judged.", "say": "The *counterfactual* here is not silence; it is students learning privately, which only some families can afford.", "note": "Strong in any evaluation argument; avoid using it for simple hypotheticals, where it sounds inflated.", "level": 2}, {"id": "arts-policy-46", "field": "Arts, music and cultural policy", "term": "public value", "gloss": "Mark Moore's concept (Creating Public Value, 1995) of the value public organisations create for citizens, judged by what the public values and authorises, not by market measures.", "say": "A school concert series creates *public value* that no ticket price captures, and that is precisely why it sits in a public school.", "note": "Lands with senior public servants, many of whom were trained in Moore's framework; in arts rooms it may need unpacking.", "level": 3}, {"id": "arts-policy-47", "field": "Arts, music and cultural policy", "term": "market failure", "gloss": "A situation in which markets alone under-supply a good relative to its social value, the standard economic justification for public subsidy of the arts.", "say": "If live jazz for under-18s were profitable, someone would already be doing it; that is *market failure*, and it is why the festival needs support.", "note": "Persuasive with Treasury and economic audiences; use precisely, as it does not mean simply that something is underfunded.", "level": 2}, {"id": "arts-policy-48", "field": "Arts, music and cultural policy", "term": "merit good", "gloss": "A good that society judges people should have more of than they would choose to buy unaided, a concept associated with the economist Richard Musgrave.", "say": "Instrumental tuition is treated as a *merit good* in this state; the question is whether we fund it as one.", "note": "Precise and rare; lands with economists but can sound paternalistic if you do not frame it carefully.", "level": 3}, {"id": "arts-policy-49", "field": "Arts, music and cultural policy", "term": "Baumol's cost disease", "gloss": "Baumol and Bowen's observation (1966) that labour-intensive performing arts cannot raise productivity as other sectors do, so their relative costs rise over time.", "say": "A big band still needs seventeen players, as it did in 1940; *Baumol's cost disease* is why its cost per student keeps rising faster than the budget.", "note": "An excellent answer to 'why does it keep getting more expensive'; with non-economists, give the string quartet example rather than the label alone.", "level": 3}, {"id": "arts-policy-50", "field": "Arts, music and cultural policy", "term": "non-use value", "gloss": "Value people place on a cultural asset they do not use themselves: option value (keeping the possibility), existence value (knowing it exists) and bequest value (passing it on).", "say": "Plenty of ratepayers will never attend, but the *non-use value* of a thriving youth jazz scene still shows up when you ask them.", "note": "Lands with economists and evaluators; elsewhere, lead with the plain example before the term.", "level": 3}, {"id": "arts-policy-51", "field": "Arts, music and cultural policy", "term": "contingent valuation", "gloss": "A survey method estimating what people would be willing to pay for, or accept the loss of, a non-market good such as a cultural service.", "say": "If the council wants a dollar figure, *contingent valuation* is the defensible method, but it is expensive and the panel should know its limits.", "note": "Signals real economic literacy; claiming results without a properly designed study will be picked apart.", "level": 3}, {"id": "arts-policy-52", "field": "Arts, music and cultural policy", "term": "crowding out", "gloss": "The possibility that public funding displaces private giving or earned income rather than adding to it; the empirical evidence is mixed.", "say": "Some on the board worry a government grant will cause *crowding out* of our sponsors; I think the reverse is likelier, since grants signal credibility.", "note": "Lands in funding strategy discussions; be clear the evidence is contested rather than asserting either direction.", "level": 3}, {"id": "arts-policy-53", "field": "Arts, music and cultural policy", "term": "instrumentalisation", "gloss": "The critique that justifying the arts primarily by non-artistic outcomes distorts what is funded and how it is judged.", "say": "My worry about this framework is *instrumentalisation*: within three years we would be choosing repertoire for its wellbeing score.", "note": "A familiar critique among cultural policy scholars; lands when you also concede the political need for instrumental arguments.", "level": 3}, {"id": "arts-policy-54", "field": "Arts, music and cultural policy", "term": "Bildung", "gloss": "The German humanist ideal, associated with Humboldt, of self-formation through education and culture, underpinning the classical university and conservatoire ethos.", "say": "There is still a *Bildung* argument for music in a public school: it forms the person, not only the player.", "note": "Pronounced BIL-doong. Lands with academics; in a parent meeting it sounds affected, so translate it.", "level": 3}, {"id": "arts-policy-55", "field": "Arts, music and cultural policy", "term": "artistic merit", "gloss": "A funding and assessment criterion judging the quality, ambition and integrity of the proposed work itself.", "say": "The application is strong on reach and weak on *artistic merit*; we need the repertoire and the guest artists named.", "note": "Funders weigh it alongside other criteria; check the specific criteria of each round, as wording varies.", "level": 1}, {"id": "arts-policy-56", "field": "Arts, music and cultural policy", "term": "thin markets", "gloss": "Markets with too few buyers or sellers to sustain commercial supply, common in regional and specialist arts provision.", "say": "Regional touring for youth jazz is a *thin market*; it will not exist unless someone subsidises the first years.", "note": "Term borrowed from economics and service policy; lands with policy officers, can sound technical to artists.", "level": 3}, {"id": "arts-policy-57", "field": "Arts, music and cultural policy", "term": "agent of change principle", "gloss": "A planning principle placing responsibility for noise mitigation on whoever introduces a change, so a new residential development near an existing venue must manage noise rather than the venue.", "say": "If the Perth venues are to survive infill, the *agent of change principle* has to be in the planning scheme, not left to individual complaints.", "note": "Adopted in Victoria and in the UK planning framework, among others; check the local planning position before claiming it applies in a given jurisdiction.", "level": 2}, {"id": "arts-policy-58", "field": "Arts, music and cultural policy", "term": "all-ages", "gloss": "Live music events open to under-18s, which in licensed venues require specific approvals and conditions under liquor licensing.", "say": "There is no point training young jazz players if there are no *all-ages* gigs where they can hear and play the music live.", "note": "Lands in live music policy discussions; be specific about the licensing obstacle rather than blaming venues in general.", "level": 1}, {"id": "arts-policy-59", "field": "Arts, music and cultural policy", "term": "night-time economy", "gloss": "The economic and cultural activity of a city after dark, including live music, hospitality and transport, increasingly treated as a policy domain in its own right.", "say": "Live jazz is a small but telling part of the *night-time economy*, and its health depends on transport after eleven as much as on venues.", "note": "Lands with local government and planning people; artists may find it reduces music to economics, so pair it with cultural argument.", "level": 2}, {"id": "arts-policy-60", "field": "Arts, music and cultural policy", "term": "presenter versus producer", "gloss": "A presenter programs and hosts work made by others; a producer initiates, finances and assembles the work and carries its risk.", "say": "At the festival we are the *presenter* for school bands but the *producer* of the gala, and the risk sits very differently.", "note": "Signals industry literacy; the distinction matters for contracts, insurance and who owns the work.", "level": 2}, {"id": "arts-policy-61", "field": "Arts, music and cultural policy", "term": "small-to-medium sector", "gloss": "The Australian term for arts organisations outside the major performing arts companies, typically leaner and more dependent on project and short-term funding.", "say": "AJEA belongs in the *small-to-medium sector*, and our case should be read alongside theirs rather than against the majors.", "note": "Standard Australian funding language; it describes position and scale, not quality.", "level": 2}, {"id": "arts-policy-62", "field": "Arts, music and cultural policy", "term": "precarity", "gloss": "Structural insecurity of work and income, characteristic of artistic careers, as documented in David Throsby's long-running studies of Australian artists.", "say": "We should be honest with Year 12s about *precarity* without making it the only thing they hear about a life in music.", "note": "Lands in policy and academic company; avoid quoting income figures unless you have the current survey in front of you.", "level": 2}, {"id": "arts-policy-63", "field": "Arts, music and cultural policy", "term": "Status of the Artist", "gloss": "The UNESCO Recommendation concerning the Status of the Artist (1980), affirming artists' rights as workers, including to fair remuneration and social protection.", "say": "If the festival is going to cite the *Status of the Artist* recommendation, our own guest fees have to meet it.", "note": "Rare and authoritative; lands at policy forums, sounds excessive in a routine fee negotiation.", "level": 3}, {"id": "arts-policy-64", "field": "Arts, music and cultural policy", "term": "soft power", "gloss": "Joseph Nye's term for influence through attraction, culture and values rather than coercion; cultural diplomacy is its deliberate use by governments.", "say": "A student big band touring Asia is *soft power* on a very small scale, which is exactly how DFAT tends to think about it.", "note": "Lands with government and international audiences; overclaiming a school tour's diplomatic effect will be heard as inflation.", "level": 2}, {"id": "arts-policy-65", "field": "Arts, music and cultural policy", "term": "decolonising the canon", "gloss": "Examining and changing whose music counts as core repertoire and knowledge, and on what authority, rather than only adding diverse works at the margins.", "say": "*Decolonising the canon* is not removing Bach; it is asking why Bach is the default and others are electives.", "note": "Lands when made concrete; used as a slogan it divides a committee before the argument has started.", "level": 2}, {"id": "arts-policy-66", "field": "Arts, music and cultural policy", "term": "intangible cultural heritage", "gloss": "Living practices, knowledge and expressions, including music and oral traditions, as defined in UNESCO's 2003 Convention for the Safeguarding of the Intangible Cultural Heritage.", "say": "Jazz in this city is *intangible cultural heritage*; it lives in people who are in their seventies, and nobody is recording how they teach.", "note": "Lands at conferences and with heritage bodies; check the Convention's status in any jurisdiction before relying on it formally.", "level": 3}, {"id": "arts-policy-67", "field": "Arts, music and cultural policy", "term": "open round versus targeted program", "gloss": "An open round accepts applications for any eligible activity; a targeted program funds specific priorities, cohorts or outcomes set by the funder.", "say": "This is a *targeted program* for regional youth, so a metropolitan festival application would need a genuine regional component to be competitive.", "note": "Reading which kind of round you are in is basic grant literacy; applying to a targeted program with a generic application wastes everyone's time.", "level": 1}, {"id": "arts-policy-68", "field": "Arts, music and cultural policy", "term": "variation request", "gloss": "A formal request to a funder to change the approved activity, timeline or budget of a funded project.", "say": "The venue has changed, so we should lodge a *variation request* now rather than explain it in the acquittal.", "note": "Funders prefer early variations to late surprises; changing scope without one can make funds repayable.", "level": 1}, {"id": "arts-policy-69", "field": "Arts, music and cultural policy", "term": "milestone payment", "gloss": "Grant funds released in instalments upon meeting specified deliverables or reporting points.", "say": "The second *milestone payment* depends on the interim report, so the treasurer needs the attendance figures by the end of term.", "note": "Matters for cash-flow planning; small organisations often fail to budget for the gap between milestones.", "level": 1}, {"id": "arts-policy-70", "field": "Arts, music and cultural policy", "term": "talent identification versus talent development", "gloss": "Identification selects students judged to have potential; development builds capability over time. Programs that over-invest in selection risk mistaking early advantage for aptitude.", "say": "Our audition is *talent identification*; whether the program works depends on *talent development*, and that is where the resources should go.", "note": "Lands in selective-program debates; it lets you defend selection while acknowledging its known biases.", "level": 2}, {"id": "arts-policy-71", "field": "Arts, music and cultural policy", "term": "audience development", "gloss": "Planned activity to broaden, deepen or diversify an organisation's audiences over time, as distinct from marketing a single event.", "say": "Free entry for primary families is *audience development*; it is the parents of next year's Year 7s in those seats.", "note": "Familiar in the sector; 'audience engagement' is now often preferred where the emphasis is on relationship rather than numbers.", "level": 1}, {"id": "arts-policy-72", "field": "Arts, music and cultural policy", "term": "receptive versus creative participation", "gloss": "Receptive participation is attending or listening; creative participation is making, performing or composing. Policy data often conflate the two.", "say": "The festival numbers count *receptive participation*; what we care about is *creative participation*, and we should report that separately.", "note": "Lands with researchers and statistically minded policy staff; useful when headline attendance figures are being overvalued.", "level": 3}, {"id": "arts-policy-73", "field": "Arts, music and cultural policy", "term": "amateur", "gloss": "In its strict sense, one who practises an art for love (from Latin amator) rather than payment; the distinction from professional concerns remuneration, not standard.", "say": "Some of the finest players in the community band are *amateurs* in the true sense, and the students should hear that word as a compliment.", "note": "Lands when you make the etymological point lightly; in everyday speech the word implies lower quality, so signal which sense you mean.", "level": 3}, {"id": "arts-policy-74", "field": "Arts, music and cultural policy", "term": "vernacular music and informal learning", "gloss": "Music made and transmitted outside formal institutions, and the self-directed, peer-based, by-ear learning through which it travels; Lucy Green's work on popular musicians is the standard reference.", "say": "Half our contemporary students arrive through *informal learning*; our job is to build on it, not to correct it.", "note": "Lands with music educators and SCSA committees; acknowledge the formal strengths too, or it sounds anti-institutional.", "level": 2}, {"id": "arts-policy-75", "field": "Arts, music and cultural policy", "term": "musicking", "gloss": "Christopher Small's term (1998) treating music as an activity, including listening, rehearsing and organising, rather than as a thing or a work.", "say": "Setting up the stage, selling tickets and running the sound desk is *musicking* too, and those students deserve recognition at the awards.", "note": "Lands with academics and reflective educators; to a general board it may sound like jargon, so gloss it once.", "level": 3}, {"id": "arts-policy-76", "field": "Arts, music and cultural policy", "term": "case for support", "gloss": "The concise, evidence-based statement of why a funder or donor should invest, written for the reader's priorities rather than the applicant's.", "say": "Our *case for support* talks about our needs; it should talk about what the funder's money will change.", "note": "Standard in fundraising; the commonest failure is writing it for the organisation's own board.", "level": 1}, {"id": "arts-policy-77", "field": "Arts, music and cultural policy", "term": "philanthropy versus sponsorship", "gloss": "Philanthropy is a gift given without commercial return; sponsorship is a commercial exchange in which the sponsor receives benefits such as branding.", "say": "If they want naming rights on the program, it is *sponsorship*, and we need a contract, not a thank-you letter.", "note": "The distinction affects tax treatment, contracts and the board's obligations; blurring it causes trouble later.", "level": 1}, {"id": "arts-policy-78", "field": "Arts, music and cultural policy", "term": "social licence", "gloss": "The informal acceptance by the community and stakeholders that an organisation's activities, partners or sponsors are legitimate.", "say": "Before we take that sponsor, the board should ask whether it affects our *social licence* with parents and schools.", "note": "Lands in sponsorship and reputational discussions; overused, it can sound like corporate risk-speak.", "level": 2}, {"id": "arts-policy-79", "field": "Arts, music and cultural policy", "term": "Deductible Gift Recipient status", "gloss": "Endorsement allowing an organisation to receive gifts that are tax-deductible for donors, central to private giving to Australian arts organisations.", "say": "Without *Deductible Gift Recipient status*, our donors are giving after tax, and the larger ones will notice.", "note": "Usually shortened to DGR status, said letter by letter; check current eligibility rules, which have been reformed over time.", "level": 2}, {"id": "arts-policy-80", "field": "Arts, music and cultural policy", "term": "parachute programming", "gloss": "Delivering a program into a community briefly from outside, without local partnership or continuing presence, in contrast to embedded, community-led work.", "say": "One workshop day in the Wheatbelt is *parachute programming*; a partnership with the local teacher over a year is reach.", "note": "Lands as a self-critique; directed at others' programs it can sound sanctimonious.", "level": 2}]}, {"field": "Law, ethics and employment in education", "intro": "This register rewards exactness and restraint: the right term for the right stage of a process, the right standard for the right question, and no more law than the room needs. Sound native by naming the process and the standard plainly, keeping findings and decisions apart, and leaving doctrine you are unsure of to counsel.", "entries": [{"id": "law-ethics-1", "field": "Law, ethics and employment in education", "term": "procedural fairness", "gloss": "The common law obligation on decision-makers to act fairly in making decisions that affect a person's rights, interests or legitimate expectations, chiefly through the hearing rule and the bias rule; its content varies with the context.", "say": "Whatever we decide about the ensemble placement, *procedural fairness* means the family sees the reasons and has a chance to respond first.", "note": "The preferred Australian term since Kioa v West (1985); it is flexible in content, so asserting it requires a full hearing in every case overstates it.", "level": 1}, {"id": "law-ethics-2", "field": "Law, ethics and employment in education", "term": "natural justice", "gloss": "The older, largely synonymous term for procedural fairness, comprising the hearing rule and the rule against bias.", "say": "*Natural justice* is not a formal trial; it is the right to know the case against you and to have it decided by someone without a stake.", "note": "Interchangeable with procedural fairness in most rooms; lawyers in Australia now usually prefer the latter.", "level": 1}, {"id": "law-ethics-3", "field": "Law, ethics and employment in education", "term": "hearing rule", "gloss": "The limb of procedural fairness requiring that a person be told the substance of the case against them and given a genuine opportunity to respond before an adverse decision.", "say": "The *hearing rule* is satisfied by a letter, time to respond and genuine consideration of the response; it does not require a panel.", "note": "Lands when you match the process to the stakes; insisting on maximal process for minor matters is its own unfairness to others.", "level": 2}, {"id": "law-ethics-4", "field": "Law, ethics and employment in education", "term": "audi alteram partem", "gloss": "Latin for 'hear the other side', the traditional name of the hearing rule.", "say": "The principle is as old as *audi alteram partem*: before we act on the complaint, we hear from the teacher.", "note": "Pronounced OW-dee AL-ter-am PAR-tem. Lands with lawyers and academics; in a staff meeting it sounds showy, so use the English.", "level": 3}, {"id": "law-ethics-5", "field": "Law, ethics and employment in education", "term": "nemo iudex in causa sua", "gloss": "Latin for 'no one should be a judge in their own cause', the traditional name of the bias rule.", "say": "I taught the student privately for three years, so *nemo iudex in causa sua*: I will step out of this audition decision.", "note": "Pronounced NAY-mo YOO-dex in COW-sa SOO-a; sometimes written judex. Use once, then use plain English; repeated Latin sounds affected.", "level": 3}, {"id": "law-ethics-6", "field": "Law, ethics and employment in education", "term": "apprehended bias", "gloss": "Bias established where a fair-minded lay observer might reasonably apprehend that the decision-maker might not bring an impartial mind to the question; actual bias need not be shown.", "say": "No one doubts your integrity; the problem is *apprehended bias*, because your daughter is in the section being reviewed.", "note": "The test is the observer's reasonable apprehension, not the decision-maker's actual state of mind; saying 'but I am not biased' misses the point.", "level": 2}, {"id": "law-ethics-7", "field": "Law, ethics and employment in education", "term": "prejudgement", "gloss": "A decision-maker having so fixed a view in advance that they are not open to persuasion by the evidence or submissions.", "say": "If the email already says the student will be removed, any later meeting looks like *prejudgement*, not a hearing.", "note": "The Australian spelling keeps the middle e; the risk usually arises from careless correspondence before a decision.", "level": 2}, {"id": "law-ethics-8", "field": "Law, ethics and employment in education", "term": "an open mind, not a blank mind", "gloss": "The principle that a decision-maker may hold prior views and experience provided they remain genuinely open to persuasion; discussed by the High Court in Minister for Immigration v Jia Legeng (2001).", "say": "Having a view on the band program does not disqualify me; I need *an open mind, not a blank mind*, and I can show I have changed position before.", "note": "Lands when someone argues that any prior opinion is disqualifying; it is a defence of judgement, not of predetermination.", "level": 3}, {"id": "law-ethics-9", "field": "Law, ethics and employment in education", "term": "legitimate expectation", "gloss": "Historically, an expectation of a benefit or procedure that grounded a right to be heard; in Australia the High Court has moved away from it as the foundation of procedural fairness.", "say": "I would not rest the argument on *legitimate expectation*; the stronger point is that the decision plainly affects the student's interests.", "note": "Use with care in Australia; it remains prominent in English administrative law, and importing it uncritically can mislead a committee.", "level": 3}, {"id": "law-ethics-10", "field": "Law, ethics and employment in education", "term": "credible, relevant and significant", "gloss": "The formulation from Kioa v West (1985) identifying adverse information that a decision-maker must generally put to the affected person for comment.", "say": "The neighbour's complaint is *credible, relevant and significant* to the decision, so the teacher must see the substance of it before we decide anything.", "note": "A precise test that lands with lawyers and senior public servants; it is a guide to what must be disclosed, not a requirement to disclose everything.", "level": 3}, {"id": "law-ethics-11", "field": "Law, ethics and employment in education", "term": "duty of care", "gloss": "The legal obligation to take reasonable care to avoid foreseeable harm to those one could reasonably expect to be affected; schools owe it to students in their charge.", "say": "Our *duty of care* on the tour does not end at the concert; it covers the hotel and the travel between.", "note": "The duty is to take reasonable care, not to prevent all harm; framing it as a guarantee drives unnecessary risk aversion.", "level": 1}, {"id": "law-ethics-12", "field": "Law, ethics and employment in education", "term": "non-delegable duty", "gloss": "A duty that cannot be discharged simply by appointing a competent person; the school authority must ensure reasonable care is taken, as established for schools in Commonwealth v Introvigne (1982).", "say": "Hiring a tour company does not transfer our responsibility, because the school's *non-delegable duty* remains with us.", "note": "Lands when contracts or volunteers are being treated as a transfer of responsibility; it does not make the authority liable for all harm.", "level": 2}, {"id": "law-ethics-13", "field": "Law, ethics and employment in education", "term": "reasonable foreseeability", "gloss": "The threshold for duty and breach that a risk was not far-fetched or fanciful, as stated in Wyong Shire Council v Shirt (1980); foreseeability alone does not establish breach.", "say": "Heat stress at an outdoor gig in February meets the test of *reasonable foreseeability*; the question is what reasonable precautions look like.", "note": "A low threshold; the real argument is usually about the reasonable response, which civil liability legislation now frames.", "level": 2}, {"id": "law-ethics-14", "field": "Law, ethics and employment in education", "term": "standard of care", "gloss": "What a reasonable person in the defendant's position would have done in response to a foreseeable risk, considering probability, seriousness, burden of precautions and social utility.", "say": "The *standard of care* for a Year 7 camp is not the same as for a Year 12 tour, because the students' capacity differs.", "note": "Distinguish it from the existence of a duty; most school disputes are about the standard, not whether a duty existed.", "level": 2}, {"id": "law-ethics-15", "field": "Law, ethics and employment in education", "term": "vicarious liability", "gloss": "Liability of an employer for wrongs committed by an employee in the course of employment, without fault on the employer's part; distinct from the employer's direct liability for its own negligence.", "say": "The Department's exposure here is *vicarious liability* for the teacher's conduct on duty, separate from any failure in its own supervision.", "note": "Whether intentional wrongdoing falls within the course of employment is a difficult area of law; do not offer a view on a live case.", "level": 1}, {"id": "law-ethics-16", "field": "Law, ethics and employment in education", "term": "reasonable adjustments", "gloss": "Measures taken to enable a person with disability to access and participate in education or employment on the same basis as others, required unless they would impose unjustifiable hardship.", "say": "Extra time and a separate room for the aural exam are *reasonable adjustments*; changing what the exam assesses is a different question.", "note": "The Disability Standards for Education 2005 frame the obligation; adjustments should preserve the inherent requirements of the assessment.", "level": 1}, {"id": "law-ethics-17", "field": "Law, ethics and employment in education", "term": "unjustifiable hardship", "gloss": "The statutory limit on the obligation to make adjustments under the Disability Discrimination Act 1992 (Cth), assessed against all relevant circumstances including cost and benefit.", "say": "Cost alone rarely establishes *unjustifiable hardship*; we would need to show we weighed the benefit to the student as well.", "note": "A high bar; organisations that assert it casually tend to lose credibility with tribunals and families.", "level": 2}, {"id": "law-ethics-18", "field": "Law, ethics and employment in education", "term": "Disability Standards for Education 2005", "gloss": "Standards made under the Disability Discrimination Act 1992 (Cth) setting out how education providers must enable students with disability to participate on the same basis as others, including a duty to consult.", "say": "Under the *Disability Standards for Education 2005*, consultation with the student and family comes before the adjustment, not after a complaint.", "note": "'On the same basis' is the key phrase; it does not mean identical treatment.", "level": 2}, {"id": "law-ethics-19", "field": "Law, ethics and employment in education", "term": "direct versus indirect discrimination", "gloss": "Direct discrimination is less favourable treatment because of a protected attribute; indirect discrimination is a condition applied to all that disadvantages people with an attribute and is not reasonable.", "say": "Requiring every student to own an instrument before audition looks neutral, but it may be *indirect discrimination* if it is not reasonable.", "note": "Indirect discrimination does not require intent; that is the point most often missed in staff discussions.", "level": 1}, {"id": "law-ethics-20", "field": "Law, ethics and employment in education", "term": "protected attribute", "gloss": "A characteristic on which discrimination is prohibited by statute, such as sex, race, disability or age, with the list varying between Commonwealth and state law.", "say": "Hearing loss is a *protected attribute*, so the audition process has to be reviewed with that in mind.", "note": "In WA, the Equal Opportunity Act 1984 (WA) operates alongside the Commonwealth Acts; the lists are not identical.", "level": 1}, {"id": "law-ethics-21", "field": "Law, ethics and employment in education", "term": "victimisation", "gloss": "In discrimination law, subjecting a person to detriment because they have made, or intend to make, a complaint or have assisted in one.", "say": "Whatever we think of the complaint, the student's seat in the ensemble cannot change because of it, or we risk *victimisation*.", "note": "A separate wrong from the underlying discrimination; it can succeed even if the original complaint fails.", "level": 2}, {"id": "law-ethics-22", "field": "Law, ethics and employment in education", "term": "positive duty", "gloss": "The obligation, introduced into the Sex Discrimination Act 1984 (Cth) in 2022, for employers to take reasonable and proportionate measures to eliminate sexual harassment and related conduct, enforceable by the Australian Human Rights Commission.", "say": "Under the *positive duty*, waiting for a complaint is no longer enough; the association has to show what it has done to prevent harassment at the festival.", "note": "Lands at boards; it shifts the question from response to prevention, which many organisations have not absorbed.", "level": 3}, {"id": "law-ethics-23", "field": "Law, ethics and employment in education", "term": "inherent requirements", "gloss": "The essential, non-peripheral demands of a job or course; in disability discrimination law, inability to meet them even with adjustments can be a defence.", "say": "Sight-reading may be an *inherent requirement* of the ATAR performance exam; it is not obviously one for entry into Year 7.", "note": "The defence is narrow and assessed after considering adjustments; using it to protect preferences rather than essentials fails.", "level": 2}, {"id": "law-ethics-24", "field": "Law, ethics and employment in education", "term": "special measures", "gloss": "Measures taken to achieve substantive equality for a disadvantaged group, which discrimination statutes recognise as not themselves discriminatory.", "say": "Reserving workshop places for First Nations students could be a *special measure*, but we should check its basis before announcing it.", "note": "A technical term with statutory conditions; confirm with legal advice before describing a program this way.", "level": 3}, {"id": "law-ethics-25", "field": "Law, ethics and employment in education", "term": "conciliation", "gloss": "A confidential, assisted negotiation conducted by a statutory body such as the Equal Opportunity Commission of WA or the Australian Human Rights Commission, usually the stage before a complaint can proceed to a tribunal.", "say": "If the family's complaint goes to *conciliation*, our aim should be a workable arrangement for the student, not a win on the record.", "note": "The conciliator does not decide the complaint; treating it as a hearing to be won tends to harden positions and lose the chance of settlement.", "level": 2}, {"id": "law-ethics-26", "field": "Law, ethics and employment in education", "term": "confidentiality versus privacy", "gloss": "Confidentiality is an obligation not to disclose information received in confidence; privacy concerns how personal information is collected, used and disclosed under law and policy.", "say": "This is a *confidentiality* issue because the parent told us in confidence, and a *privacy* issue because it is the student's health information.", "note": "Keeping the two apart clarifies what may be shared and with whom; they overlap but are not the same obligation.", "level": 1}, {"id": "law-ethics-27", "field": "Law, ethics and employment in education", "term": "need to know", "gloss": "The principle that sensitive information is shared only with those who require it to perform their role in the matter.", "say": "The ensemble directors need to know an adjustment exists; they do not *need to know* the diagnosis.", "note": "Lands when you apply it narrowly and explain why; applied too strictly it can leave staff unable to discharge their duty of care.", "level": 1}, {"id": "law-ethics-28", "field": "Law, ethics and employment in education", "term": "duty of confidence", "gloss": "The equitable obligation arising where information with the necessary quality of confidence is imparted in circumstances importing an obligation of confidence, and unauthorised use is threatened; the elements are classically stated in Coco v A N Clark (Engineers) (1969).", "say": "The draft syllabus was shared under a *duty of confidence*, so it cannot go to the department meeting until SCSA releases it.", "note": "Rare and precise; useful on committees where embargoed material circulates, but most rooms will be served by 'in confidence'.", "level": 3}, {"id": "law-ethics-29", "field": "Law, ethics and employment in education", "term": "without prejudice", "gloss": "A label for communications made in a genuine attempt to settle a dispute, which are generally inadmissible as evidence of admissions; the label alone does not create the protection where there is no dispute.", "say": "Marking a routine email *without prejudice* does nothing if there is no dispute being settled, and it reads as defensive.", "note": "Often misused by non-lawyers to mean confidential; it is about settlement communications in a dispute.", "level": 1}, {"id": "law-ethics-30", "field": "Law, ethics and employment in education", "term": "without prejudice save as to costs", "gloss": "An offer made on terms that it cannot be shown to the court on the merits but may be relied upon on the question of costs; known as a Calderbank offer after Calderbank v Calderbank (1975).", "say": "If it reaches that stage, the lawyers may make an offer *without prejudice save as to costs*; that is their call, not the board's.", "note": "Litigation language; use only to show you understand what the lawyers are doing, not to deploy it yourself.", "level": 3}, {"id": "law-ethics-31", "field": "Law, ethics and employment in education", "term": "without admission of liability", "gloss": "Wording that accompanies a payment, apology or concession to make clear it is not an acceptance of legal fault; distinct from without prejudice.", "say": "The festival can refund the entry fee *without admission of liability* while the complaint is being considered.", "note": "Useful in correspondence; it does not guarantee the statement will be inadmissible, so get advice in serious matters.", "level": 2}, {"id": "law-ethics-32", "field": "Law, ethics and employment in education", "term": "mandatory reporting", "gloss": "A statutory obligation on specified professionals to report certain beliefs about child abuse to the designated authority; in WA, teachers must report a belief on reasonable grounds of child sexual abuse formed in the course of their work.", "say": "*Mandatory reporting* is a personal obligation; telling the principal does not discharge it on my behalf.", "note": "Keep the scope accurate: categories of reportable abuse and the authority receiving reports differ between jurisdictions.", "level": 1}, {"id": "law-ethics-33", "field": "Law, ethics and employment in education", "term": "belief versus suspicion", "gloss": "Belief is an inclination of the mind toward accepting a proposition; suspicion is a lesser state of apprehension that it may be so. The High Court drew the distinction in George v Rockett (1990).", "say": "The threshold is a *belief* on reasonable grounds, which is more than *suspicion*, but it does not require certainty or investigation.", "note": "Lands in any discussion of reporting thresholds; never use it to argue a teacher should investigate before reporting.", "level": 2}, {"id": "law-ethics-34", "field": "Law, ethics and employment in education", "term": "actual, potential and perceived conflicts", "gloss": "An actual conflict is a present clash between private interest and public duty; a potential conflict could arise; a perceived conflict is one a reasonable person might believe exists, regardless of fact.", "say": "My son plays in the band applying for the grant, so this is at least a *perceived conflict*, and I will declare it and step out.", "note": "The perceived category matters most in public bodies; dismissing it with 'but I am not influenced' is a common error.", "level": 1}, {"id": "law-ethics-35", "field": "Law, ethics and employment in education", "term": "recusal", "gloss": "Withdrawal by a decision-maker from participating in a matter because of a conflict or apprehended bias.", "say": "Declaring the interest is not enough on this item; *recusal* means I leave the room for the discussion as well as the vote.", "note": "Pronounced ri-KYOO-zl. Lands when you specify what it involves; a recusal from the vote only, after influencing the discussion, is not one.", "level": 2}, {"id": "law-ethics-36", "field": "Law, ethics and employment in education", "term": "conflict of duty and duty", "gloss": "A conflict arising where a person owes duties to two bodies whose interests diverge, as distinct from a conflict between personal interest and duty.", "say": "As chair of the council and Teacher in Charge I face a *conflict of duty and duty* on this budget item, and the council should decide it without me.", "note": "Precise and underused; it explains why someone with no personal interest can still be conflicted, which disarms indignant objections.", "level": 3}, {"id": "law-ethics-37", "field": "Law, ethics and employment in education", "term": "material personal interest", "gloss": "The statutory phrase, used in the Corporations Act and in WA associations legislation, for an interest of a director or committee member in a matter that must be disclosed.", "say": "Before the board approves the venue contract, anyone with a *material personal interest* in the venue needs to say so, and the minutes should record it.", "note": "Use the phrase accurately; materiality is a judgement, so when in doubt, declare.", "level": 2}, {"id": "law-ethics-38", "field": "Law, ethics and employment in education", "term": "fiduciary duty", "gloss": "An obligation of loyalty owed by a person in a position of trust, such as a committee member or treasurer, to act in the interests of the body and not for their own advantage.", "say": "As treasurer I owe a *fiduciary duty* to the association, which means its money is never a convenience for my own band's cash flow.", "note": "Pronounced fi-DYOO-sha-ree. Lands in governance training; overstating it to cover every disagreement dilutes it.", "level": 2}, {"id": "law-ethics-39", "field": "Law, ethics and employment in education", "term": "no conflict rule and no profit rule", "gloss": "The two core fiduciary prohibitions: a fiduciary must not place themselves where interest and duty conflict, and must not profit from their position without informed consent.", "say": "Booking my own trio for the gala breaches the *no profit rule* unless the board knows and approves in advance.", "note": "Lands with lawyers and experienced directors; frame consent as the remedy, so it does not sound like an accusation.", "level": 3}, {"id": "law-ethics-40", "field": "Law, ethics and employment in education", "term": "probity", "gloss": "Integrity, fairness and transparency in a decision process, particularly procurement and grant allocation, so that it can withstand scrutiny.", "say": "The *probity* of the audition panel matters as much as its expertise; families must be able to see it is fair.", "note": "Lands when attached to concrete controls such as declarations and documented criteria; used vaguely it sounds bureaucratic.", "level": 1}, {"id": "law-ethics-41", "field": "Law, ethics and employment in education", "term": "gifts and benefits register", "gloss": "A record of gifts, hospitality and benefits offered to or accepted by staff or office-holders, kept to manage conflicts and maintain public confidence.", "say": "The instrument supplier's concert tickets go on the *gifts and benefits register*, or better, back to the supplier.", "note": "Routine in the public sector; the reputational risk lies in small, frequent benefits from suppliers rather than large gifts.", "level": 1}, {"id": "law-ethics-42", "field": "Law, ethics and employment in education", "term": "misconduct versus performance", "gloss": "Misconduct concerns breaches of conduct standards, typically culpable; performance concerns capability or standard of work. They call for different processes and should not be blended.", "say": "This is a *performance* matter, not *misconduct*; running it as discipline would be unfair and would almost certainly be overturned.", "note": "Choosing the wrong track is one of the commonest errors in workplace management; name the track before acting.", "level": 1}, {"id": "law-ethics-43", "field": "Law, ethics and employment in education", "term": "minor misconduct versus serious misconduct", "gloss": "In WA, under the Corruption, Crime and Misconduct Act 2003, serious misconduct falls to the Corruption and Crime Commission and minor misconduct to the Public Sector Commissioner.", "say": "If there is any suggestion of *serious misconduct*, it is not ours to investigate; the principal notifies and we preserve the records.", "note": "Accuracy on who receives which matter carries weight with senior public servants; never decide the classification informally yourself.", "level": 2}, {"id": "law-ethics-44", "field": "Law, ethics and employment in education", "term": "right of reply", "gloss": "The opportunity for a person to respond to adverse allegations or material before a decision or publication.", "say": "Before the report goes to the council, the staff member named in it is entitled to a *right of reply*, and it should be attached.", "note": "Lands when you give a real timeframe and consider the reply genuinely; a reply invited after the decision is not one.", "level": 1}, {"id": "law-ethics-45", "field": "Law, ethics and employment in education", "term": "particularised allegations", "gloss": "Allegations stated with enough specificity, including what, when, where and who, for the person to understand and answer them.", "say": "'Unprofessional behaviour' is not an allegation; we need *particularised allegations* or there is nothing for the teacher to answer.", "note": "A vague allegation is itself a procedural fairness failure; senior HR staff will expect you to know this.", "level": 2}, {"id": "law-ethics-46", "field": "Law, ethics and employment in education", "term": "show cause", "gloss": "A notice inviting a person to explain why a proposed adverse action, such as a sanction, should not be taken.", "say": "The *show cause* letter should state the proposed outcome and why, so the response can address it squarely.", "note": "Strictly a step after findings and before a final decision; using it as the opening of an investigation confuses the stages.", "level": 2}, {"id": "law-ethics-47", "field": "Law, ethics and employment in education", "term": "grievance", "gloss": "A formal concern raised by an employee about a workplace decision, action or behaviour affecting them, dealt with under a defined grievance process.", "say": "If she wants it on the record, she can lodge a *grievance*, and I will make sure it reaches someone who has no part in the decision.", "note": "Distinguish a grievance (about one's own treatment) from a complaint about misconduct, which may trigger a different process.", "level": 1}, {"id": "law-ethics-48", "field": "Law, ethics and employment in education", "term": "informal resolution", "gloss": "Resolving a concern by discussion, facilitated conversation or agreed actions without a formal finding, where the matter and the parties suit it.", "say": "*Informal resolution* is right for a clash about rehearsal times; it is wrong for anything that touches safety or alleged misconduct.", "note": "Lands when matched to the matter; pressing a reluctant complainant into it can itself become a grievance.", "level": 1}, {"id": "law-ethics-49", "field": "Law, ethics and employment in education", "term": "workplace investigation", "gloss": "A structured, impartial fact-finding process into allegations, producing findings on the evidence against a stated standard of proof.", "say": "A *workplace investigation* finds facts; it does not decide the sanction, and we should not let it drift into doing both.", "note": "Lands when you keep its scope to the terms of reference; investigations that wander invite procedural challenge.", "level": 1}, {"id": "law-ethics-50", "field": "Law, ethics and employment in education", "term": "separation of investigator and decision-maker", "gloss": "The practice of having one person establish the facts and another decide the outcome, protecting against prejudgement and apprehended bias.", "say": "Given my involvement with the ensemble, the *separation of investigator and decision-maker* matters: I can give evidence, but I cannot do either job.", "note": "Not always legally required, but expected as good practice in serious matters; small organisations should plan for it in advance.", "level": 2}, {"id": "law-ethics-51", "field": "Law, ethics and employment in education", "term": "terms of reference", "gloss": "The written scope of an investigation, review or committee, setting out the questions, powers, timeframe and reporting line.", "say": "The *terms of reference* should name the allegations precisely, or we will find ourselves investigating the whole department culture.", "note": "Well-drafted terms prevent scope creep; vague ones are the source of most later disputes.", "level": 1}, {"id": "law-ethics-52", "field": "Law, ethics and employment in education", "term": "findings of fact versus recommendations", "gloss": "Findings state what the investigator concludes happened on the evidence; recommendations propose what the decision-maker might do. They should be clearly separated in a report.", "say": "The report should give *findings of fact* first and keep any *recommendations* separate, so the decision-maker can reject one without disturbing the other.", "note": "Blurring the two invites challenge to the whole report; senior readers look for the separation.", "level": 2}, {"id": "law-ethics-53", "field": "Law, ethics and employment in education", "term": "substantiated and not substantiated", "gloss": "Outcomes indicating whether an allegation has been established to the required standard; 'not substantiated' means not proven, not that the allegation was false.", "say": "*Not substantiated* is not the same as exonerated, and we should be careful how that is communicated to the complainant.", "note": "Precision here protects both parties; describing an unsubstantiated complaint as false or vexatious without evidence is a serious error.", "level": 2}, {"id": "law-ethics-54", "field": "Law, ethics and employment in education", "term": "balance of probabilities", "gloss": "The civil standard of proof: a fact is established if it is more probable than not, as distinct from the criminal standard of beyond reasonable doubt.", "say": "The investigation applies the *balance of probabilities*; nobody needs to be satisfied beyond reasonable doubt, and saying so in the report would be wrong.", "note": "Lands when you correct the common slide into criminal standards in workplace matters.", "level": 1}, {"id": "law-ethics-55", "field": "Law, ethics and employment in education", "term": "Briginshaw", "gloss": "The principle from Briginshaw v Briginshaw (1938), particularly Dixon J, that the seriousness of an allegation, its inherent unlikelihood and the gravity of the consequences affect whether a tribunal is reasonably satisfied; it is not a third standard of proof.", "say": "Given the gravity of the allegation, *Briginshaw* applies: reasonable satisfaction should not be produced by inexact proofs, indefinite testimony or indirect inferences.", "note": "Pronounced BRIG-in-shaw. Lands strongly with lawyers and investigators; describing it as a higher standard is a well-known error.", "level": 3}, {"id": "law-ethics-56", "field": "Law, ethics and employment in education", "term": "onus versus standard", "gloss": "The onus (burden) of proof is who must establish a fact; the standard of proof is the degree of satisfaction required.", "say": "The *onus* is on the investigation to establish the allegation, not on the teacher to disprove it, and the *standard* is the balance of probabilities.", "note": "Pronounced OH-nus. Conflating the two is common in staff rooms; separating them cleanly signals training.", "level": 2}, {"id": "law-ethics-57", "field": "Law, ethics and employment in education", "term": "hearsay", "gloss": "Evidence of a statement made out of court offered as proof of its contents; generally admissible in workplace investigations, but usually given less weight than direct evidence.", "say": "The parent's account of what another student said is *hearsay*; it can be considered, but it cannot carry the finding on its own.", "note": "Lands when you speak of weight rather than exclusion; workplace processes are not bound by the rules of evidence.", "level": 2}, {"id": "law-ethics-58", "field": "Law, ethics and employment in education", "term": "weight versus admissibility", "gloss": "Admissibility is whether evidence may be considered at all; weight is how much it counts once considered.", "say": "Nothing in the folder is inadmissible in this process; the question is *weight*, and the anonymous note has very little.", "note": "Lands in investigations and appeals; non-lawyers often argue admissibility when the real issue is weight.", "level": 2}, {"id": "law-ethics-59", "field": "Law, ethics and employment in education", "term": "credibility versus reliability", "gloss": "Credibility concerns whether a witness is honest; reliability concerns whether an honest witness's account is accurate.", "say": "I do not doubt the student's *credibility*; I have some doubt about *reliability*, because she was across the room at the time.", "note": "A valuable distinction for findings and feedback; it lets you discount evidence without accusing anyone of lying.", "level": 2}, {"id": "law-ethics-60", "field": "Law, ethics and employment in education", "term": "contemporaneous notes", "gloss": "Records made at or very close to the time of the events they describe, generally given greater weight than later recollection.", "say": "Please make *contemporaneous notes* of the conversation tonight, dated and signed, and do not edit them later.", "note": "Notes amended after the event lose most of their value; add a dated addendum instead.", "level": 1}, {"id": "law-ethics-61", "field": "Law, ethics and employment in education", "term": "Jones v Dunkel inference", "gloss": "The principle from Jones v Dunkel (1959) that an unexplained failure by a party to call a witness or give evidence may permit an inference that it would not have helped that party's case.", "say": "In court a *Jones v Dunkel inference* might be drawn from that silence; in our process we should simply note that he chose not to respond.", "note": "Strictly a litigation principle; invoking it in a workplace or school process sounds like over-lawyering and should be left to counsel.", "level": 3}, {"id": "law-ethics-62", "field": "Law, ethics and employment in education", "term": "Browne v Dunn", "gloss": "The rule from Browne v Dunn (1893) that a party intending to contradict a witness must put that contrary case to the witness so they can respond; its spirit informs fair investigations.", "say": "In the spirit of *Browne v Dunn*, the teacher should be told of the contrary account before the investigator prefers it.", "note": "Pronounced brown v dun. A litigation rule; cite it only as an analogy in workplace settings, or it will sound like borrowed authority.", "level": 3}, {"id": "law-ethics-63", "field": "Law, ethics and employment in education", "term": "support person", "gloss": "A person who accompanies a party to an interview or meeting for support, without acting as an advocate or answering on their behalf.", "say": "You are welcome to bring a *support person*; they can take notes and ask for a break, but the answers need to be yours.", "note": "State the role clearly in advance; confusion between a support person and a representative derails interviews.", "level": 1}, {"id": "law-ethics-64", "field": "Law, ethics and employment in education", "term": "administrative suspension", "gloss": "Removal of a staff member from duty pending investigation, ordinarily on pay, as a protective measure rather than a finding or penalty.", "say": "The *administrative suspension* protects the investigation and the students; it is not a judgement and should never be described to staff as one.", "note": "Lands when you insist on neutral communication; leaked or loaded descriptions of a suspension can create defamation and fairness problems.", "level": 2}, {"id": "law-ethics-65", "field": "Law, ethics and employment in education", "term": "proportionality", "gloss": "The requirement that a sanction or response be commensurate with the seriousness of the conduct, taking into account mitigating and aggravating circumstances.", "say": "*Proportionality* matters: a first lapse in record-keeping does not warrant removal from the tour.", "note": "Lands in sanction discussions; it is not leniency, and should be argued on facts rather than sympathy.", "level": 2}, {"id": "law-ethics-66", "field": "Law, ethics and employment in education", "term": "harsh, unjust or unreasonable", "gloss": "The statutory test for unfair dismissal under the Fair Work Act 2009 (Cth); WA public sector teachers generally fall within the state industrial system, where the governing formulation differs.", "say": "Before anyone threatens termination, remember any dismissal will be tested on whether it was *harsh, unjust or unreasonable*, or the state equivalent.", "note": "Know which jurisdiction applies before quoting the test; leave the classification to industrial relations specialists.", "level": 3}, {"id": "law-ethics-67", "field": "Law, ethics and employment in education", "term": "reasonable management action", "gloss": "Management action carried out in a reasonable manner, which the federal workplace bullying definition expressly excludes from bullying.", "say": "Setting expectations about rehearsal punctuality is *reasonable management action*, provided it is done in a reasonable manner.", "note": "Both limbs matter: the action and the manner. A legitimate direction delivered abusively is not protected.", "level": 2}, {"id": "law-ethics-68", "field": "Law, ethics and employment in education", "term": "psychosocial hazard", "gloss": "A workplace hazard arising from work design, management or interactions that may cause psychological harm, now expressly within WA work health and safety duties under the Work Health and Safety Act 2020 (WA) and its regulations.", "say": "An unmanaged festival workload for volunteers is a *psychosocial hazard*, and the board has to treat it as a safety issue, not a morale issue.", "note": "Lands at boards and with leadership; it reframes workload and conflict as matters of legal duty.", "level": 2}, {"id": "law-ethics-69", "field": "Law, ethics and employment in education", "term": "person conducting a business or undertaking", "gloss": "The primary duty holder under harmonised work health and safety law, usually shortened to PCBU, covering organisations and individuals regardless of the employment relationship.", "say": "For the festival, AJEA is the *person conducting a business or undertaking*, and its duty extends to volunteers and visiting school staff.", "note": "Say P-C-B-U letter by letter. Lands in governance and safety discussions; the breadth of the definition surprises many boards.", "level": 2}, {"id": "law-ethics-70", "field": "Law, ethics and employment in education", "term": "legal professional privilege", "gloss": "Protection from compelled disclosure of confidential communications made for the dominant purpose of giving or obtaining legal advice or for use in litigation; Esso Australia Resources v FCT (1999) settled the dominant purpose test at common law.", "say": "Forwarding the legal advice to the whole committee could waive *legal professional privilege*, so the chair should summarise it instead.", "note": "Privilege can be waived by careless circulation; that practical point is what lands in a board room.", "level": 2}, {"id": "law-ethics-71", "field": "Law, ethics and employment in education", "term": "public interest disclosure", "gloss": "A protected disclosure of wrongdoing in the public sector made to a proper authority under the Public Interest Disclosure Act 2003 (WA), carrying statutory protections for the discloser.", "say": "If she is making a *public interest disclosure*, it must go to the proper authority, and her identity must be protected from that moment.", "note": "Distinguish it from an ordinary complaint; mishandling the discloser's confidentiality is a serious breach.", "level": 2}, {"id": "law-ethics-72", "field": "Law, ethics and employment in education", "term": "ultra vires", "gloss": "Beyond power: an act or decision made without legal authority under the relevant statute, constitution or delegation.", "say": "The committee cannot approve the fee change itself; under the rules of association it would be *ultra vires* without a general meeting.", "note": "Pronounced UL-tra VEE-reez or VY-reez. Lands in governance; check the constitution before asserting it, as confidence without the text backfires.", "level": 3}, {"id": "law-ethics-73", "field": "Law, ethics and employment in education", "term": "delegate", "gloss": "A person exercising a power delegated by an authorised body or officer; the decision remains the delegate's own and must be made within the terms of the delegation.", "say": "As the principal's *delegate* for ensemble placements, I have to make the decision myself, within the policy, not simply endorse what a colleague prefers.", "note": "Lands when roles are muddled; a delegate who rubber-stamps another's decision exposes it to challenge.", "level": 2}, {"id": "law-ethics-74", "field": "Law, ethics and employment in education", "term": "fettering discretion", "gloss": "Applying a policy so rigidly that a decision-maker fails to consider the merits of an individual case where the power calls for discretion.", "say": "The three-year commitment policy is sound, but refusing even to consider this family's circumstances would be *fettering discretion*.", "note": "Lands with administrative lawyers and senior public servants; it allows you to defend a policy while insisting on individual consideration.", "level": 3}, {"id": "law-ethics-75", "field": "Law, ethics and employment in education", "term": "relevant and irrelevant considerations", "gloss": "Grounds of review where a decision-maker fails to take into account a matter it was bound to consider, or takes into account a matter it was bound to ignore.", "say": "A parent's seniority on the P&C is an *irrelevant consideration* in the audition result, and the record should show it played no part.", "note": "Lands in public decision-making; the relevant matters are usually found in the governing statute or policy, so name them.", "level": 2}, {"id": "law-ethics-76", "field": "Law, ethics and employment in education", "term": "Wednesbury unreasonableness", "gloss": "From Associated Provincial Picture Houses v Wednesbury Corporation (1948): a decision so unreasonable that no reasonable authority could have made it; Australian law developed the concept further in Minister for Immigration v Li (2013).", "say": "I do not think the decision reaches *Wednesbury unreasonableness*; it is simply one I would not have made.", "note": "Pronounced WENZ-bree. A high threshold; using it for decisions you merely disagree with signals you do not know how high.", "level": 3}, {"id": "law-ethics-77", "field": "Law, ethics and employment in education", "term": "reasons for decision", "gloss": "A statement of the findings, evidence and reasoning on which a decision rests; at common law there is no general duty to give reasons, as held in Public Service Board of NSW v Osmond (1986), though statute or policy may require them.", "say": "Whether or not we are obliged to, I would give the family written *reasons for decision*; it is the best protection we have.", "note": "Lands when you present reasons as good practice rather than a legal compulsion you cannot point to.", "level": 2}, {"id": "law-ethics-78", "field": "Law, ethics and employment in education", "term": "qualified privilege", "gloss": "A defence in defamation for communications made in the performance of a duty or interest to someone with a corresponding duty or interest, which can be defeated by malice.", "say": "A frank reference to the principal is likely covered by *qualified privilege*; the same remark in the staff room is not.", "note": "Lands as a reason to confine sensitive information to proper channels; do not offer it as advice on a live dispute.", "level": 3}, {"id": "law-ethics-79", "field": "Law, ethics and employment in education", "term": "Freedom of Information", "gloss": "The statutory right to access documents held by government agencies, in WA under the Freedom of Information Act 1992, subject to exemptions.", "say": "Write every file note as though it will be released under *Freedom of Information*, because some day one will be.", "note": "Usually shortened to FOI. Lands as a discipline on tone in records; exemptions exist, but do not rely on them when writing.", "level": 1}, {"id": "law-ethics-80", "field": "Law, ethics and employment in education", "term": "in camera", "gloss": "In private: a meeting or part of a meeting closed to non-members or the public, typically for confidential personnel or commercial matters.", "say": "We will take the staffing item *in camera*, and the minute will record only the resolution.", "note": "Pronounced in KAM-er-a. Standard in board and council practice; overuse for routine business erodes the transparency a council owes.", "level": 3}]}, {"field": "Finance, budgets and audit", "intro": "Finance is spoken in a register of calm exactness: the native speaker names the basis of accounting, the period and the restriction before giving a number, and never lets a surplus, a reserve or a variance stand without saying what kind it is. Authority comes from distinctions (cash against accrual, restricted against designated, timing against permanent) delivered without drama, and from asking the question the auditor would ask before the auditor asks it.", "entries": [{"id": "finance-1", "field": "Finance, budgets and audit", "term": "accrual basis", "gloss": "Accounting that recognises income when earned and expenses when incurred, regardless of when cash moves, as distinct from the cash basis.", "say": "On an *accrual basis* the Festival made a small loss; the bank balance looks healthy only because next year's sponsorship arrived in June.", "note": "Lands whenever a cash balance is being mistaken for a result. Backfires if used to dismiss a genuine cash problem: accruals do not pay invoices.", "level": 1}, {"id": "finance-2", "field": "Finance, budgets and audit", "term": "cash basis", "gloss": "Accounting that records transactions only when money is received or paid, simple and transparent but blind to obligations and entitlements not yet settled.", "say": "The school's tour account is effectively kept on a *cash basis*, so it tells us nothing about the deposits we have already committed to.", "note": "Useful for naming why a small account misleads. Avoid implying it is improper; many small bodies legitimately run on it.", "level": 1}, {"id": "finance-3", "field": "Finance, budgets and audit", "term": "restricted funds", "gloss": "Money that may be spent only for a purpose specified by the donor or funder, as distinct from funds the board has merely chosen to set aside.", "say": "That grant is *restricted funds* for the regional workshops; we cannot use it to cover the venue shortfall, however tempting.", "note": "Lands as a firm boundary in board discussion. Say who imposed the restriction; otherwise people assume it is a board preference that can be lifted.", "level": 1}, {"id": "finance-4", "field": "Finance, budgets and audit", "term": "unrestricted reserves", "gloss": "Accumulated funds the organisation may apply to any purpose within its objects, the true measure of its financial resilience.", "say": "Once you strip out the grants, our *unrestricted reserves* would carry us for about one bad year, not three.", "note": "The phrase that cuts through a flattering balance sheet. Pair it with a period of cover so it is not merely a number.", "level": 1}, {"id": "finance-5", "field": "Finance, budgets and audit", "term": "contingency", "gloss": "A budgeted allowance for identifiable but uncertain costs within a specific project or year, as distinct from a reserve, which sits on the balance sheet across years.", "say": "I have built a ten per cent *contingency* into the tour budget, and I would ask that it is not treated as discretionary spending money.", "note": "Lands when you say what it is for and who may release it. Backfires if it quietly becomes padding; reviewers spot an unexplained round number.", "level": 1}, {"id": "finance-6", "field": "Finance, budgets and audit", "term": "variance", "gloss": "The difference between budgeted and actual figures for a line or period, which demands an explanation rather than merely a report.", "say": "The *variance* on instrument repairs is mostly the two sousaphones; it is not a trend.", "note": "Always follow the figure with its cause. A variance report without commentary signals a treasurer who reads numbers but not the business.", "level": 1}, {"id": "finance-7", "field": "Finance, budgets and audit", "term": "reforecast", "gloss": "A revised projection of the year-end position made part-way through the year, leaving the original approved budget intact as the benchmark.", "say": "Rather than amend the budget, I will bring a *reforecast* to the August meeting so we can see the year-end position honestly.", "note": "Signals discipline: you keep the budget as the yardstick and update the expectation. Do not call a reforecast a new budget, or you lose the comparison.", "level": 1}, {"id": "finance-8", "field": "Finance, budgets and audit", "term": "operating surplus", "gloss": "The excess of recurrent income over recurrent expenditure for the period, excluding capital items and one-off gains.", "say": "The headline surplus is flattered by the bequest; the underlying *operating surplus* is close to nil.", "note": "Lands when separating the core business from windfalls. Specify whether depreciation is included, because people will ask.", "level": 1}, {"id": "finance-9", "field": "Finance, budgets and audit", "term": "depreciation", "gloss": "The systematic allocation of an asset's cost over its useful life, a non-cash expense that reflects the using-up of the asset.", "say": "We are not losing money on the timpani; the deficit is mostly *depreciation*, which is exactly why we should be saving for their replacement.", "note": "Powerful when explaining an accounting deficit with healthy cash. Backfires if offered as reassurance, since it signals a replacement cost that is coming.", "level": 1}, {"id": "finance-10", "field": "Finance, budgets and audit", "term": "recurrent versus capital", "gloss": "The distinction between ongoing operating expenditure consumed within the year and expenditure on assets that deliver benefit over several years.", "say": "This is a *recurrent versus capital* question: the piano is capital, but the tuning contract it brings with it is recurrent forever.", "note": "Lands when a one-off purchase hides a permanent cost. Funders and principals both respect someone who sees the recurrent tail of a capital request.", "level": 1}, {"id": "finance-12", "field": "Finance, budgets and audit", "term": "in-kind contribution", "gloss": "A non-cash contribution of goods, services, space or labour, valued at a fair estimate and usually reported to funders separately from cash.", "say": "The school's *in-kind contribution* of the hall, staff time and equipment is worth more than the cash grant, and the application should say so.", "note": "Lands in funding applications that ask for co-contribution. Value it defensibly; inflated in-kind figures are easily recognised by experienced panels.", "level": 1}, {"id": "finance-13", "field": "Finance, budgets and audit", "term": "cost recovery", "gloss": "Setting a charge to recover the cost of providing a service, without aiming at surplus, which raises the question of which costs are counted.", "say": "The camp levy is on a *cost recovery* basis, so if the bus quote rises, the levy rises; there is no margin to absorb it.", "note": "Clear and defensible with parents. Be ready to say whether overheads are included, because 'cost' is where the argument lives.", "level": 1}, {"id": "finance-14", "field": "Finance, budgets and audit", "term": "audit versus review", "gloss": "An audit gives reasonable assurance and a positive opinion on the financial statements; a review gives limited assurance and a negative-form conclusion that nothing has come to the reviewer's attention.", "say": "Our revenue tier only requires a *review*, but given the grants I would argue for a full *audit*; it is a different level of assurance, not a more expensive version of the same thing.", "note": "Shows you know what the board is actually buying. Under the Associations Incorporation Act 2015 (WA) the association's revenue tier determines which is required, so check the tier before asserting either.", "level": 1}, {"id": "finance-15", "field": "Finance, budgets and audit", "term": "going concern", "gloss": "The assumption that an entity will continue operating for the foreseeable future, on which the normal basis of preparing financial statements depends.", "say": "If the major sponsor walks, the question is not whether we have a bad year but whether we are still a *going concern*.", "note": "A serious phrase; use it only when the question is genuinely live, or you will alarm a board unnecessarily.", "level": 1}, {"id": "finance-16", "field": "Finance, budgets and audit", "term": "solvency", "gloss": "The ability to pay debts as and when they fall due, the legal and practical test that matters more than a positive balance sheet.", "say": "We are asset-rich and cash-poor in June; *solvency* is the test, and it is the June cash position I am watching.", "note": "Lands precisely because it is about timing of payment, not net worth. Officers who incur debts they cannot pay take on real exposure, so the word carries weight.", "level": 1}, {"id": "finance-17", "field": "Finance, budgets and audit", "term": "cash flow forecast", "gloss": "A month-by-month projection of cash receipts and payments that reveals timing gaps a balanced annual budget conceals.", "say": "The budget balances for the year, but the *cash flow forecast* shows we are short in May before ticket income lands.", "note": "The tool that separates treasurers from bookkeepers. Bring one whenever a board assumes a balanced budget means no cash risk.", "level": 1}, {"id": "finance-18", "field": "Finance, budgets and audit", "term": "financial delegation", "gloss": "The authority, formally conferred and usually limited by amount and purpose, for a person to commit or approve expenditure on the organisation's behalf.", "say": "I am happy to sign this, but it is above my *financial delegation*, so it needs the principal's approval first.", "note": "Lands as a clean, impersonal reason to decline or escalate. Know your own limit exactly; vagueness about delegation reads as carelessness.", "level": 1}, {"id": "finance-19", "field": "Finance, budgets and audit", "term": "segregation of duties", "gloss": "Dividing responsibilities so that no one person can initiate, approve, record and reconcile the same transaction.", "say": "I should not be both approving and paying the invoices; *segregation of duties* protects whoever holds this role, including me.", "note": "Frame it as protection for the person, not suspicion of them; that is how it lands well in small organisations where people feel trusted.", "level": 1}, {"id": "finance-20", "field": "Finance, budgets and audit", "term": "bank reconciliation", "gloss": "The regular matching of the accounting records to the bank statement, explaining every difference, as the foundation control over cash.", "say": "Before we discuss the forecast, can I confirm the *bank reconciliation* is complete to the end of last month?", "note": "A quiet question that signals competence. Asking whether it was independently reviewed signals more.", "level": 1}, {"id": "finance-21", "field": "Finance, budgets and audit", "term": "audit trail", "gloss": "The documented chain from source document through approval to ledger entry that lets any transaction be traced and verified.", "say": "The money was spent properly, but there is no *audit trail*: no quote, no approval, no receipt matched to the payment.", "note": "Lands when distinguishing proper conduct from provable conduct. Useful in a gentle conversation with a colleague who 'just paid it'.", "level": 1}, {"id": "finance-22", "field": "Finance, budgets and audit", "term": "cost centre", "gloss": "A unit of the organisation to which costs are assigned for budgeting and accountability, without necessarily generating its own income.", "say": "Strings, bands and classroom music should be separate *cost centres*; at the moment one line hides three very different programs.", "note": "Useful when arguing for visibility of a program's real cost. Too many cost centres create administrative noise, so justify each one.", "level": 1}, {"id": "finance-23", "field": "Finance, budgets and audit", "term": "carry forward", "gloss": "Unspent funds or budget authority approved to move into the next financial year rather than lapsing.", "say": "We need a formal *carry forward* of the uniform money, or it will be swept back into the general pool at year end.", "note": "Lands when you request it before the year end, with a stated purpose. Asked for after the event, it sounds like rescue.", "level": 1}, {"id": "finance-24", "field": "Finance, budgets and audit", "term": "value for money", "gloss": "The best combination of cost, quality, fitness for purpose and whole-of-life outcome, which is not the same as the lowest price.", "say": "The cheapest quote is not *value for money* if we are re-hiring the PA every year because it fails.", "note": "Standard public-sector language and widely trusted. Back it with whole-of-life reasoning, or it sounds like a justification for preference.", "level": 1}, {"id": "finance-25", "field": "Finance, budgets and audit", "term": "designated reserves", "gloss": "Unrestricted funds the board has chosen to earmark for a purpose, which the board can undesignate, as distinct from restricted funds, which only the funder can release.", "say": "The instrument replacement fund is a *designated reserve*, not a restricted one; this board created it and this board could release it.", "note": "The distinction many boards blur, and naming it shows command. Be careful not to sound as though you are inviting a raid on the reserve.", "level": 2}, {"id": "finance-26", "field": "Finance, budgets and audit", "term": "reserves policy", "gloss": "A board-approved statement of the level of unrestricted reserves the organisation aims to hold, why, and what happens if it falls above or below that range.", "say": "Without a *reserves policy*, every surplus becomes an argument about spending it; I would like us to adopt one before we discuss the surplus.", "note": "Positions you as raising the governance level of the discussion. Bring a proposed range and a rationale rather than an abstract principle.", "level": 2}, {"id": "finance-27", "field": "Finance, budgets and audit", "term": "months of operating cover", "gloss": "Unrestricted reserves expressed as the number of months of normal expenditure they could fund if income stopped.", "say": "Our reserves look large in dollars, but they are about four *months of operating cover*, which is modest for a body with one dominant sponsor.", "note": "Translates a balance into risk. Define operating expenditure for the calculation, since festival-month costs distort the average.", "level": 2}, {"id": "finance-28", "field": "Finance, budgets and audit", "term": "favourable and adverse variance", "gloss": "A favourable variance improves the result against budget and an adverse variance worsens it, regardless of whether the line is income or expenditure.", "say": "The underspend on catering is a *favourable variance*, but it exists because we cancelled an event, so it is not good news.", "note": "Precise and slightly more senior than 'over' and 'under'. Lands best when you add the cause, as here.", "level": 2}, {"id": "finance-29", "field": "Finance, budgets and audit", "term": "timing variance versus permanent variance", "gloss": "A timing variance will reverse within the year because money arrives or leaves later than phased; a permanent variance will persist to year end.", "say": "Most of this is a *timing variance*: the grant arrived in July rather than June. The only *permanent variance* is the insurance increase.", "note": "One of the most useful distinctions in a budget meeting; it calms the room about the right things and focuses it on the rest.", "level": 2}, {"id": "finance-30", "field": "Finance, budgets and audit", "term": "budget phasing", "gloss": "Spreading an annual budget across months to match the expected pattern of income and spending, so that monthly comparisons are meaningful.", "say": "The report looks alarming because the budget was divided by twelve; with proper *budget phasing* the festival costs sit in August where they belong.", "note": "Lands when explaining a misleading monthly report. Suggesting it signals that you understand how a budget is used, not only set.", "level": 2}, {"id": "finance-31", "field": "Finance, budgets and audit", "term": "income in advance", "gloss": "Cash received for goods or services not yet delivered, held as a liability until earned; also called deferred income.", "say": "The 2027 registration fees are *income in advance*; they are in the bank, but we owe the schools a festival for them.", "note": "Essential when a board feels rich in the months before an event. Auditors will expect it to sit on the balance sheet, not in this year's income.", "level": 2}, {"id": "finance-32", "field": "Finance, budgets and audit", "term": "accrued expense", "gloss": "A cost incurred in the period but not yet invoiced or paid, recognised so that the period carries its true cost.", "say": "The adjudicators' fees for December need to go in as an *accrued expense*, or this year looks better than it was and next year worse.", "note": "Lands at year end and in handovers between treasurers. Without accruals, two consecutive years are both misstated.", "level": 2}, {"id": "finance-33", "field": "Finance, budgets and audit", "term": "prepayment", "gloss": "Cash paid in one period for a benefit received in a later period, held as an asset until the benefit is consumed.", "say": "The venue deposit is a *prepayment* against next year's festival, not a cost of this one.", "note": "Pairs naturally with income in advance; using both correctly signals a clear grasp of period.", "level": 2}, {"id": "finance-34", "field": "Finance, budgets and audit", "term": "capitalisation threshold", "gloss": "The minimum cost at which an item is recorded as an asset and depreciated rather than expensed immediately.", "say": "Under our *capitalisation threshold* the music stands are simply expensed; the marimba, though, should be on the asset register.", "note": "Lands when a large purchase distorts a year's result. Check the policy figure before you cite it; organisations set their own.", "level": 2}, {"id": "finance-35", "field": "Finance, budgets and audit", "term": "useful life", "gloss": "The period over which an asset is expected to provide benefit, which determines its depreciation and signals when replacement must be funded.", "say": "If the *useful life* of the student instruments is ten years, then a tenth of the fleet should be replaced every year, and we are nowhere near that.", "note": "Converts depreciation into a replacement plan, which principals and councils find persuasive. Do not overstate precision; useful lives are estimates.", "level": 2}, {"id": "finance-36", "field": "Finance, budgets and audit", "term": "non-cash expense", "gloss": "An expense recognised in the accounts that involves no cash payment in the period, such as depreciation or a provision.", "say": "The deficit is almost entirely *non-cash expense*; the question for the board is whether we are funding the replacement those numbers imply.", "note": "Lands when reconciling a loss with a healthy bank. The trap is to treat 'non-cash' as 'not real'.", "level": 2}, {"id": "finance-37", "field": "Finance, budgets and audit", "term": "full cost versus marginal cost", "gloss": "Full cost includes a fair share of overheads and fixed costs; marginal cost is only the extra cost of doing one more unit or activity.", "say": "On *marginal cost* the extra concert is almost free; on *full cost*, including staff time and venue wear, it is not, and we should price it honestly.", "note": "The heart of most arguments about pricing and 'just one more' activity. Name which basis you are using before anyone gives a figure.", "level": 2}, {"id": "finance-38", "field": "Finance, budgets and audit", "term": "overheads", "gloss": "Indirect costs that support the organisation as a whole and are not attributable to a single program, such as insurance, audit fees and administration.", "say": "Funders increasingly accept a share of *overheads*; we should stop pretending the festival runs without an accountant and an insurer.", "note": "Lands in grant budgets where people under-claim. Call them indirect costs in funder language if the guidelines do.", "level": 2}, {"id": "finance-39", "field": "Finance, budgets and audit", "term": "cross-subsidy", "gloss": "Using surplus from one activity or group to fund another, a legitimate policy when explicit and a governance problem when hidden.", "say": "The senior tour levy is a *cross-subsidy* of the junior ensembles; I am comfortable with that, but parents should know it is happening.", "note": "Lands when you make the subsidy visible and defend it on purpose. Uttered as an accusation, it can sour a parent meeting fast.", "level": 2}, {"id": "finance-40", "field": "Finance, budgets and audit", "term": "break-even", "gloss": "The level of activity, such as ticket sales or enrolments, at which total income equals total costs.", "say": "At this ticket price *break-even* is about four hundred seats; the hall holds five hundred, so there is little margin for a wet night.", "note": "Converts budgets into something a committee can test against reality. Always state the assumptions behind the number.", "level": 2}, {"id": "finance-41", "field": "Finance, budgets and audit", "term": "fixed versus variable costs", "gloss": "Fixed costs remain constant regardless of activity level within a range; variable costs rise and fall with activity.", "say": "The venue and the sound crew are *fixed costs*; only catering and medals are variable, so cutting numbers saves less than people think.", "note": "Lands when a committee assumes a smaller event is proportionally cheaper. Note that many fixed costs are fixed only within a range.", "level": 2}, {"id": "finance-42", "field": "Finance, budgets and audit", "term": "management letter", "gloss": "The auditor's letter to those charged with governance setting out control weaknesses and recommendations found during the audit, separate from the audit opinion.", "say": "The opinion was clean, but the *management letter* raised the petty cash and the dual signatories, and I would like an action against each point.", "note": "Shows you know the opinion is not the whole story. A board that tracks management letter points from year to year is visibly well governed.", "level": 2}, {"id": "finance-43", "field": "Finance, budgets and audit", "term": "materiality", "gloss": "The threshold above which a misstatement or omission could reasonably influence the decisions of users of the financial statements, judged by size and nature.", "say": "Forty dollars is not *material* to the accounts, but a forty-dollar payment to a committee member's business is material by its nature.", "note": "Lands when you show that materiality is qualitative as well as quantitative. Avoid using it to wave away small irregularities.", "level": 2}, {"id": "finance-44", "field": "Finance, budgets and audit", "term": "unmodified opinion", "gloss": "The auditor's conclusion that the financial statements are presented fairly in all material respects, the technical term for what people call a clean audit.", "say": "We have an *unmodified opinion* again this year, with one emphasis of matter paragraph I will explain.", "note": "The correct term in audit reports; 'clean' is fine in conversation. Using the right word to an accountant on a board earns quiet credit.", "level": 2}, {"id": "finance-45", "field": "Finance, budgets and audit", "term": "qualified opinion", "gloss": "An audit opinion that the statements are fairly presented except for a specified matter, either a material misstatement or an inability to obtain sufficient evidence.", "say": "Cash takings at the door are where small associations get a *qualified opinion*; the auditor simply cannot verify them.", "note": "Know that it is 'except for' and not a failure; adverse opinions and disclaimers are the graver forms. Raising it pre-emptively shows foresight.", "level": 2}, {"id": "finance-46", "field": "Finance, budgets and audit", "term": "liquidity", "gloss": "The ability to meet short-term obligations from cash or assets readily convertible to cash, a timing measure distinct from overall solvency or net worth.", "say": "Our problem is *liquidity*, not solvency: the money is in a term deposit that matures a month after the invoices fall due.", "note": "Lands when separating a short-term squeeze from a structural problem. Do not use it as a softer synonym for insolvency.", "level": 2}, {"id": "finance-47", "field": "Finance, budgets and audit", "term": "working capital", "gloss": "Current assets less current liabilities, the operating cushion available to fund the gap between paying costs and receiving income.", "say": "We need enough *working capital* to pay the venue and the buses six weeks before school registrations clear.", "note": "Precise in a board setting. In a parent forum, 'the cash to bridge the gap' says the same thing without the jargon.", "level": 2}, {"id": "finance-48", "field": "Finance, budgets and audit", "term": "cash runway", "gloss": "The length of time an organisation can continue operating at its current rate of spending before cash runs out, absent new income.", "say": "If the sponsorship is not renewed, our *cash runway* is about seven months, which tells us when the board must decide, not merely that it must.", "note": "Borrowed from start-up language but useful because it converts reserves into a decision deadline. Sounds glib if used for a healthy organisation.", "level": 2}, {"id": "finance-49", "field": "Finance, budgets and audit", "term": "delegation register", "gloss": "The formal record of who holds which financial and decision-making authorities, to what limit, and from whom they were delegated.", "say": "Could we confirm the *delegation register* reflects the new committee, because the bank mandate still lists last year's officers?", "note": "Lands at handover and after elections. It signals governance housekeeping without implying anyone has done wrong.", "level": 2}, {"id": "finance-50", "field": "Finance, budgets and audit", "term": "related party transaction", "gloss": "A transaction between the organisation and a person or entity connected to someone with influence over it, which requires disclosure and arm's-length terms.", "say": "The printing quote from my brother-in-law's firm is a *related party transaction*; I will declare it, leave the room, and the board can decide on the merits.", "note": "Lands when you name your own conflict first; it makes the standard easy to apply to others. Treat it as disclosure, not as an accusation.", "level": 2}, {"id": "finance-51", "field": "Finance, budgets and audit", "term": "provision", "gloss": "A liability of uncertain timing or amount recognised because a present obligation exists and the outflow can be reliably estimated, such as employee leave.", "say": "If we employ a festival coordinator, we must carry a *provision* for their leave; it is a real liability from the first month.", "note": "Lands with boards moving from volunteers to staff. Do not use 'provision' loosely for any money put aside; that is a designated reserve.", "level": 2}, {"id": "finance-52", "field": "Finance, budgets and audit", "term": "contingent liability", "gloss": "A possible obligation dependent on an uncertain future event, or a present obligation that cannot yet be measured reliably, disclosed rather than recognised.", "say": "The dispute with the hire company is a *contingent liability*; it belongs in the notes until we know whether we owe anything.", "note": "Shows you know the difference between a liability and a risk. Raise it early with the auditor rather than at sign-off.", "level": 2}, {"id": "finance-53", "field": "Finance, budgets and audit", "term": "commitments", "gloss": "Amounts the organisation has contractually or formally undertaken to spend, not yet incurred, which reduce the funds genuinely available.", "say": "There is forty thousand in the account, but twenty-eight of it is *commitments*; what is truly available is twelve.", "note": "Lands in every conversation where someone sees a balance and wants to spend it. In school finance the same idea is often called encumbered funds.", "level": 2}, {"id": "finance-54", "field": "Finance, budgets and audit", "term": "matched funding", "gloss": "A funding arrangement where the funder contributes only in proportion to money raised or committed by the applicant or other sources.", "say": "The foundation offers *matched funding* dollar for dollar, so our P&C contribution effectively doubles; that is the case to put to them.", "note": "Lands as leverage in fundraising. Check whether in-kind counts towards the match; guidelines differ and panels notice.", "level": 2}, {"id": "finance-55", "field": "Finance, budgets and audit", "term": "zero-based budgeting", "gloss": "Building each budget line from nil and justifying every amount afresh, as opposed to incremental budgeting, which adjusts last year's figures.", "say": "I do not want full *zero-based budgeting* every year, but once a cycle it is worth asking what each line would cost if we were starting today.", "note": "Lands as a periodic discipline. Proposed annually it sounds like an exhausting purge and invites resistance.", "level": 2}, {"id": "finance-56", "field": "Finance, budgets and audit", "term": "one-line budget", "gloss": "The WA public school funding arrangement under which the school receives its resources as a single allocation, giving the principal discretion over how it is distributed.", "say": "Because the school runs on a *one-line budget*, the specialist program is a choice the school makes each year, which is why we have to argue its value internally.", "note": "Lands with principals and councils because it names where the discretion really sits. Pair it with the Student-Centred Funding Model only when you are sure of the detail.", "level": 2}, {"id": "finance-57", "field": "Finance, budgets and audit", "term": "internal controls", "gloss": "The policies and procedures that safeguard assets, ensure reliable records and prevent or detect error and fraud.", "say": "This is not a question of trust; our *internal controls* should be designed so that nobody has to be trusted alone.", "note": "The standard umbrella term. Become specific quickly (approvals, reconciliations, signatories), or it sounds like compliance theatre.", "level": 2}, {"id": "finance-58", "field": "Finance, budgets and audit", "term": "structural deficit", "gloss": "A deficit that persists because recurrent costs exceed recurrent income under normal conditions, as distinct from a one-off deficit caused by a particular event.", "say": "Last year's loss was a one-off; what worries me is that, without the bequest, we would be running a *structural deficit* of about eight thousand a year.", "note": "The phrase that moves a board from 'bad luck' to 'we must change something'. Make sure the numbers support it before you say it.", "level": 2}, {"id": "finance-59", "field": "Finance, budgets and audit", "term": "ring-fenced", "gloss": "Formally protected for a specific purpose and shielded from reallocation, whether by external restriction or by internal decision.", "say": "I would like the instrument fund *ring-fenced* in the minutes, so it survives a change of principal or committee.", "note": "Lands when you say who did the fencing and how. Overused in public-sector speech; one use per meeting is plenty.", "level": 2}, {"id": "finance-60", "field": "Finance, budgets and audit", "term": "drawdown on reserves", "gloss": "The deliberate use of accumulated reserves to fund a deficit or project, which should be approved and time-limited.", "say": "I am comfortable with a planned *drawdown on reserves* for the anniversary year, provided we agree the floor we will not go below.", "note": "Lands when framed as a decision with a limit. An unplanned drawdown is simply a loss; calling it a drawdown afterwards fools nobody.", "level": 2}, {"id": "finance-61", "field": "Finance, budgets and audit", "term": "virement", "gloss": "The authorised transfer of budget provision from one line or head to another within an approved total.", "say": "Rather than seek new money, I propose a *virement* from the unused travel line to accompanist fees, within the approved total.", "note": "Precise and Commonwealth public-finance in flavour; lands with accountants and senior public servants. Elsewhere 'reallocation' is safer; pronunciation varies between anglicised and French forms, and either passes.", "level": 3}, {"id": "finance-62", "field": "Finance, budgets and audit", "term": "hypothecation", "gloss": "Earmarking a particular revenue stream to fund a specific expenditure, so that the spending rises and falls with that source.", "say": "I would resist *hypothecation* of the bar takings to the scholarship; a good scholarship should not depend on how thirsty the audience is.", "note": "High register, from public finance; lands in a sophisticated board. Avoid it in parent forums, where it sounds like obfuscation.", "level": 3}, {"id": "finance-63", "field": "Finance, budgets and audit", "term": "fungibility", "gloss": "The quality of money being interchangeable, so that restricting one source may simply free other money for the purpose the restriction meant to prevent.", "say": "Because money is *fungible*, a grant for instruments still lets the school move its own instrument money elsewhere; the funder knows this, and so should we.", "note": "Lands when explaining why funders ask for additionality. Use sparingly; it can sound like a lecture in economics.", "level": 3}, {"id": "finance-64", "field": "Finance, budgets and audit", "term": "emphasis of matter", "gloss": "A paragraph in an audit report drawing attention to a matter properly disclosed in the statements that is fundamental to users' understanding, without modifying the opinion.", "say": "The *emphasis of matter* about our reliance on one sponsor is not a criticism; it is the auditor making sure no one can say they were not told.", "note": "Lands when calming a board that has misread an audit report. Make clear it is not a qualification.", "level": 3}, {"id": "finance-65", "field": "Finance, budgets and audit", "term": "cut-off", "gloss": "The principle and audit test that transactions are recorded in the correct period, especially around the year end.", "say": "The auditor will test *cut-off*, so the December invoices dated in January need to be accrued properly this time.", "note": "An auditor's word; using it shows you know where year-end errors live. Unknown to most lay board members, so explain it once.", "level": 3}, {"id": "finance-66", "field": "Finance, budgets and audit", "term": "sinking fund", "gloss": "A fund built by regular contributions to meet a known future expenditure, such as asset replacement, as it falls due.", "say": "Every hire of the school's instruments should contribute to a *sinking fund*; otherwise the replacement bill arrives as a crisis every decade.", "note": "Slightly old-fashioned, which gives it authority with older board members and accountants. 'Replacement fund' works for everyone else.", "level": 3}, {"id": "finance-67", "field": "Finance, budgets and audit", "term": "headroom", "gloss": "The margin between a current position and a limit, such as available cash above a minimum reserve or budget left before a cap.", "say": "We have some *headroom* above the reserve floor, but not enough to underwrite a second venue.", "note": "Common in Treasury and banking speech; precise and unshowy. Specify what limit the headroom is measured against.", "level": 3}, {"id": "finance-68", "field": "Finance, budgets and audit", "term": "contribution margin", "gloss": "Income from an activity less its variable costs, showing what the activity contributes towards fixed costs and surplus.", "say": "The workshops lose money on full cost, but their *contribution margin* is positive, so cancelling them would make the overall result worse.", "note": "Wins arguments about cutting apparently loss-making activities. Requires a clean fixed and variable split, or the number is easily challenged.", "level": 3}, {"id": "finance-69", "field": "Finance, budgets and audit", "term": "substance over form", "gloss": "The principle that transactions are accounted for according to their economic reality rather than their legal or documentary form.", "say": "On paper it is a donation, but in *substance* it is a sponsorship with obligations attached; we should treat it as such.", "note": "Lands with accountants and lawyers, and in disputes over how a deal is described. Can sound pedantic if the distinction changes nothing.", "level": 3}, {"id": "finance-70", "field": "Finance, budgets and audit", "term": "prudence", "gloss": "In accounting, the exercise of caution under uncertainty so that assets and income are not overstated and liabilities and expenses are not understated.", "say": "I have budgeted ticket sales at last year's level as a matter of *prudence*; if they grow, that is a pleasant surprise rather than a hole.", "note": "The technical sense of an ordinary word; signals training. Do not let it justify deliberately understated budgets, which is its own distortion.", "level": 3}, {"id": "finance-71", "field": "Finance, budgets and audit", "term": "matching principle", "gloss": "The principle that expenses are recognised in the same period as the income they help to generate.", "say": "Under the *matching principle*, the festival's costs and its fees belong in the same year, even though the cash spans two.", "note": "Precise and somewhat textbook; lands with a treasurer handing over to a successor. In the boardroom, 'same year' usually suffices.", "level": 3}, {"id": "finance-72", "field": "Finance, budgets and audit", "term": "nugatory expenditure", "gloss": "Spending that yields no benefit because the purpose for which it was incurred has fallen away, such as fees for a cancelled booking.", "say": "The deposit on the cancelled tour is *nugatory expenditure*; we should record why it happened, not just write it off.", "note": "A Whitehall term, still current in UK public finance; sounds distinctly mandarin. Lands with senior public servants; elsewhere say 'wasted' or 'sunk'.", "level": 3}, {"id": "finance-73", "field": "Finance, budgets and audit", "term": "imprest", "gloss": "A system in which a fixed float is held and periodically replenished to exactly that amount on presentation of receipts.", "say": "Petty cash should run on an *imprest* of two hundred dollars: receipts plus cash should always equal two hundred.", "note": "Old and exact; accountants recognise it instantly. To others explain it once, then use it.", "level": 3}, {"id": "finance-74", "field": "Finance, budgets and audit", "term": "material uncertainty related to going concern", "gloss": "The auditing-standards language for a significant doubt about an entity's ability to continue, which must be disclosed in the statements and highlighted in the audit report.", "say": "If we sign the venue contract without the sponsorship confirmed, the auditor may well report a *material uncertainty related to going concern*.", "note": "The exact phrase from audit reports; using it shows you know what follows if the board takes a risk. Reserve it for when that consequence is genuinely possible.", "level": 3}, {"id": "finance-75", "field": "Finance, budgets and audit", "term": "management override", "gloss": "The ability of those in authority to bypass controls, which auditors treat as a fraud risk present in every entity regardless of other controls.", "say": "Our controls are fine for volunteers; the risk is *management override*, which is why even the president's reimbursements need a second signature.", "note": "Lands as an impersonal reason for applying controls to senior people. Phrase it as design, not suspicion.", "level": 3}, {"id": "finance-76", "field": "Finance, budgets and audit", "term": "reasonable assurance versus limited assurance", "gloss": "Reasonable assurance, given in an audit, is a high but not absolute level of confidence; limited assurance, given in a review, is meaningfully lower and expressed negatively.", "say": "A funder asking for *reasonable assurance* is asking for an audit, not a review; we should budget for the difference.", "note": "Precise language that accountants and funders respect. Do not claim an audit guarantees the absence of fraud; 'reasonable' is the point.", "level": 3}, {"id": "finance-77", "field": "Finance, budgets and audit", "term": "below the line", "gloss": "Items presented after the operating result, such as one-off, capital or extraordinary items, so that the core result is shown separately.", "say": "I would present the bequest *below the line*, so the board can see the operating result without it.", "note": "Lands in discussing how a report is presented. Usage varies between organisations, so say which line you mean.", "level": 3}, {"id": "finance-79", "field": "Finance, budgets and audit", "term": "tone at the top", "gloss": "The ethical climate set by leaders' behaviour towards controls, which auditors and regulators treat as the foundation of the control environment.", "say": "If I bypass the purchase order process, I cannot expect anyone else to follow it; the *tone at the top* is the control.", "note": "Lands when applied to yourself first. Applied to others, it can sound like a pointed rebuke.", "level": 3}, {"id": "finance-80", "field": "Finance, budgets and audit", "term": "run-rate", "gloss": "The annualised level of income or expenditure implied by current performance, used to project the year end.", "say": "On the current *run-rate*, accompanist costs will exceed budget by about a third, so this is the moment to adjust, not November.", "note": "Corporate and slightly brisk; precise in a finance committee. Misleads when income or spending is seasonal, so qualify it in a festival body.", "level": 3}]}, {"field": "Strategy, leadership and change", "intro": "Strategy and leadership talk is easily captured by management jargon, so the native register is the opposite: plain nouns, named trade-offs, and concepts used with their source in mind rather than as slogans. The senior speaker sounds native by diagnosing before prescribing, by saying what will not be done, and by knowing where each borrowed idea (Heifetz, Schein, Rumelt, Kotter) stops being useful.", "entries": [{"id": "strategy-1", "field": "Strategy, leadership and change", "term": "strategic intent", "gloss": "A concise statement of the position an organisation is committed to reaching over the long term, which sets direction without specifying every step.", "say": "Our *strategic intent* is that every student who auditions for the program finishes Year 12 still playing; everything in this plan should be tested against that.", "note": "Lands when it is a single, testable sentence. Backfires when it is a list of values, which is a mission statement wearing a suit.", "level": 1}, {"id": "strategy-3", "field": "Strategy, leadership and change", "term": "capability", "gloss": "The skills, knowledge and systems an organisation or person possesses to do a kind of work well, as distinct from capacity, which is the volume of work they can absorb.", "say": "We have the *capability* to run a jazz workshop series; what we do not have is the capacity this term.", "note": "Precise when kept apart from capacity. 'Capability uplift' is public-service jargon and grates outside government.", "level": 1}, {"id": "strategy-4", "field": "Strategy, leadership and change", "term": "capacity", "gloss": "The amount of work, time or resource available to be applied, regardless of how skilled the people are.", "say": "This is not a question of willingness; the department is at *capacity*, and adding a fifth ensemble means removing something else.", "note": "The cleanest way to decline without seeming unwilling. Back it with a concrete account of current load.", "level": 1}, {"id": "strategy-5", "field": "Strategy, leadership and change", "term": "sequencing", "gloss": "Deciding the order in which changes are introduced so that each builds the conditions the next requires.", "say": "I agree with all three proposals; my concern is *sequencing*. The timetable change has to land before the new ensembles can work.", "note": "Lets you support an idea while slowing it down, which is often the real point. Lands when you can say why the order matters.", "level": 1}, {"id": "strategy-6", "field": "Strategy, leadership and change", "term": "trade-off", "gloss": "A choice in which gaining more of one desired thing necessarily means accepting less of another.", "say": "The honest *trade-off* is breadth against depth: we can offer more ensembles or rehearse fewer of them properly.", "note": "Naming the trade-off openly is a mark of senior judgement. Backfires when presented as if the room had not already seen it.", "level": 1}, {"id": "strategy-7", "field": "Strategy, leadership and change", "term": "opportunity cost", "gloss": "The value of the best alternative forgone when a resource is committed to one use.", "say": "The *opportunity cost* of my running the tour is a term of curriculum work I will not do.", "note": "Lands when the forgone alternative is named specifically. In the abstract it sounds like an economics tutorial.", "level": 1}, {"id": "strategy-8", "field": "Strategy, leadership and change", "term": "program sustainability", "gloss": "The capacity of a program to continue at its intended quality without depending on extraordinary effort, a single person or one-off funding.", "say": "The jazz program is thriving, but it is not *sustainable* if it depends on me giving up every Thursday night.", "note": "Lands when it means durability of quality, not just survival. Be ready to show what makes it unsustainable, or it sounds like a complaint.", "level": 1}, {"id": "strategy-9", "field": "Strategy, leadership and change", "term": "succession planning", "gloss": "Deliberately preparing people and systems so that key roles can be filled without loss of continuity when incumbents leave.", "say": "If I were hit by a bus tomorrow, there is no *succession planning* for the Festival's finances; that is a governance risk, not a personal one.", "note": "Lands when framed around the organisation, not the incumbent. Raised by the incumbent, it signals security and maturity.", "level": 1}, {"id": "strategy-10", "field": "Strategy, leadership and change", "term": "distributed leadership", "gloss": "Leadership understood as practice spread across many people and their interactions with the situation, not as the actions of one positional leader.", "say": "*Distributed leadership* is not delegation with a nicer name; the string and guitar programs need genuine authority over their own decisions.", "note": "Associated with James Spillane's work in schools. Backfires when used to dress up offloading work without authority.", "level": 1}, {"id": "strategy-11", "field": "Strategy, leadership and change", "term": "adaptive versus technical problem", "gloss": "Ronald Heifetz's distinction between problems solvable with existing expertise and problems that require people to change their values, habits or loyalties.", "say": "Rehearsal attendance is not a *technical problem* we can fix with a new roll system; it is an *adaptive* one about what students believe the ensemble is for.", "note": "The single most useful diagnostic in the field. It backfires if used to label every disagreement adaptive, which can sound like avoiding a straightforward fix.", "level": 1}, {"id": "strategy-12", "field": "Strategy, leadership and change", "term": "change fatigue", "gloss": "The exhaustion and cynicism that build when people face continual or poorly integrated change, reducing their willingness to engage with the next one.", "say": "After the syllabus change and the new reporting system, there is real *change fatigue* in this department; I would rather do one thing well this year.", "note": "Lands when used to argue for sequencing, not to resist all change. Leaders who invoke it about others but never about themselves are noticed.", "level": 1}, {"id": "strategy-13", "field": "Strategy, leadership and change", "term": "burning platform", "gloss": "A crisis framing used to convince people that staying put is more dangerous than changing, after Daryl Conner's image drawn from the Piper Alpha oil platform disaster.", "say": "I do not want to sell this as a *burning platform*; nothing is on fire, and people will feel manipulated if we pretend otherwise.", "note": "Its limit is the point: fear produces compliance and short bursts, not sustained commitment, and a manufactured crisis spends trust you will need later.", "level": 1}, {"id": "strategy-14", "field": "Strategy, leadership and change", "term": "organisational culture", "gloss": "The shared assumptions, norms and habits that shape how people in an organisation actually behave, especially when no one is watching.", "say": "The policy says students may leave ensembles freely; the *organisational culture* says they will be judged if they do.", "note": "Lands when tied to specific behaviour. The line 'culture eats strategy for breakfast' is widely attributed to Peter Drucker without a clear source, so do not cite him for it.", "level": 1}, {"id": "strategy-15", "field": "Strategy, leadership and change", "term": "stakeholder mapping", "gloss": "Identifying the people and groups affected by or able to affect a decision, and analysing their interest and influence.", "say": "Before the council paper goes up, I have done some *stakeholder mapping*: the feeder primary principals matter more here than the P&C.", "note": "A useful private discipline. Announcing it to stakeholders can make them feel managed rather than consulted.", "level": 1}, {"id": "strategy-16", "field": "Strategy, leadership and change", "term": "quick win", "gloss": "An early, visible success that builds credibility and momentum for a larger change.", "say": "The new rehearsal schedule is a *quick win*; it costs nothing and parents will notice it in a week.", "note": "Lands when it genuinely serves the larger change. A series of quick wins without a strategy is simply activity.", "level": 1}, {"id": "strategy-17", "field": "Strategy, leadership and change", "term": "pilot", "gloss": "A limited, bounded trial of a change, designed in advance to answer specific questions before any decision to scale.", "say": "Let us *pilot* the combined ensemble with Year 8 only, and agree now what result would make us extend or stop it.", "note": "Lands when success and failure criteria are set before it starts. A pilot with no criteria is a launch without the courage to say so.", "level": 1}, {"id": "strategy-18", "field": "Strategy, leadership and change", "term": "scaling", "gloss": "Extending a practice beyond its original setting while preserving what made it work.", "say": "The feeder band works because three principals trust each other; *scaling* it to ten schools is a different program, not a bigger one.", "note": "Lands when you identify what may not survive scaling. Assuming success transfers unchanged is the common error.", "level": 1}, {"id": "strategy-19", "field": "Strategy, leadership and change", "term": "key-person risk", "gloss": "The exposure created when an organisation's capability or knowledge is concentrated in one individual.", "say": "The festival's sponsor relationships are a *key-person risk*; they live in one person's phone.", "note": "Precise and impersonal; lands well in a board. Applied to a colleague in their presence, frame it as a compliment to their importance.", "level": 1}, {"id": "strategy-20", "field": "Strategy, leadership and change", "term": "institutional memory", "gloss": "The accumulated knowledge of an organisation's history, decisions and reasons, whether held in records or in long-serving people.", "say": "We tried a Saturday festival in 2014 and it failed for reasons nobody wrote down; our *institutional memory* is a set of anecdotes.", "note": "Lands when arguing for documentation. Leaning on it too heavily ('we tried that') can sound like a reason never to try again.", "level": 1}, {"id": "strategy-21", "field": "Strategy, leadership and change", "term": "implementation dip", "gloss": "Michael Fullan's term for the temporary decline in performance and confidence that typically follows the introduction of a new practice before benefits appear.", "say": "Expect an *implementation dip* in the first term of the new assessment model; if results fall in Term 1, that is not evidence it has failed.", "note": "Lands when predicted in advance, which protects the change. Invoked only after results fall, it sounds like an excuse.", "level": 1}, {"id": "strategy-22", "field": "Strategy, leadership and change", "term": "lead and lag indicators", "gloss": "Lag indicators measure outcomes after the fact; lead indicators measure earlier behaviours that predict those outcomes and can still be influenced.", "say": "ATAR results are a *lag indicator*; Year 10 retention into the elective is the *lead indicator* we can actually act on.", "note": "Lands in planning conversations obsessed with outcome data. Make sure the lead indicator genuinely predicts the outcome.", "level": 1}, {"id": "strategy-23", "field": "Strategy, leadership and change", "term": "logic model", "gloss": "A structured chain from inputs and activities to outputs, outcomes and impact, used to plan and evaluate a program.", "say": "The funder wants a *logic model*, so let us be clear that the number of workshops is an output, not an outcome.", "note": "Expected in funding applications. The common error, confusing outputs with outcomes, is exactly what panels look for.", "level": 1}, {"id": "strategy-24", "field": "Strategy, leadership and change", "term": "buy-in versus ownership", "gloss": "Buy-in is agreement to someone else's plan; ownership is the commitment that comes from having shaped it.", "say": "I do not want *buy-in* from the instrumental staff; I want *ownership*, which means they help write the plan rather than receive it.", "note": "Lands as a stance on consultation. Promising ownership and then delivering a finished plan does lasting damage.", "level": 1}, {"id": "strategy-25", "field": "Strategy, leadership and change", "term": "deliberate versus emergent strategy", "gloss": "Henry Mintzberg's distinction between strategy realised as intended and strategy that forms from patterns of action in the absence of, or despite, intentions.", "say": "The jazz program was never in a plan; it is an *emergent strategy* that the school should now decide whether to make *deliberate*.", "note": "Lands when legitimising what has grown organically, and when asking a board to own it. Avoid implying that planning is pointless.", "level": 2}, {"id": "strategy-26", "field": "Strategy, leadership and change", "term": "the kernel of strategy", "gloss": "Richard Rumelt's three-part core of good strategy: a diagnosis of the challenge, a guiding policy for dealing with it, and coherent actions that carry it out.", "say": "Our plan has goals but no *kernel*: no diagnosis of why students leave after Year 9, and no guiding policy for responding.", "note": "Devastatingly effective in reviewing a plan that is all aspiration. Rumelt also names 'mistaking goals for strategy' as bad strategy, which is often the exact problem.", "level": 2}, {"id": "strategy-27", "field": "Strategy, leadership and change", "term": "guiding policy", "gloss": "In Rumelt's terms, the overall approach chosen to deal with the diagnosed challenge, which rules some actions in and others out.", "say": "The *guiding policy* is depth before breadth: no new ensembles until existing ones are properly staffed.", "note": "Lands when it excludes something; a guiding policy that rules out nothing is not one.", "level": 2}, {"id": "strategy-28", "field": "Strategy, leadership and change", "term": "initiative overload", "gloss": "The condition in which too many concurrent initiatives fragment attention and resources so that none is properly implemented; colloquially, initiativitis.", "say": "Our problem is *initiative overload*: seven priorities, each reasonable, together impossible.", "note": "'Initiativitis' raises a smile and lands well in a staff meeting; in a formal paper, 'initiative overload' reads better.", "level": 2}, {"id": "strategy-29", "field": "Strategy, leadership and change", "term": "absorptive capacity", "gloss": "An organisation's ability to recognise, assimilate and apply new knowledge, which depends on what it already knows; from Wesley Cohen and Daniel Levinthal.", "say": "The new syllabus resources are excellent, but small schools lack the *absorptive capacity* to use them without professional learning.", "note": "Lands in SCSA and system-level discussions about rollout. In a department meeting, 'room to take it in' says the same.", "level": 2}, {"id": "strategy-30", "field": "Strategy, leadership and change", "term": "critical mass", "gloss": "The minimum scale at which an activity becomes self-sustaining or viable in quality.", "say": "Two bassoons is not *critical mass*; we either recruit a section or arrange the parts elsewhere.", "note": "Lands when the threshold is defined. Used vaguely, it becomes a reason for growth for its own sake.", "level": 2}, {"id": "strategy-31", "field": "Strategy, leadership and change", "term": "bench strength", "gloss": "The depth of capable people ready to step into key roles, an indicator of succession readiness.", "say": "We have strong leaders in the Festival committee, but very little *bench strength*; most of us will retire from it together.", "note": "Sporting metaphor used in corporate and board settings. Lands well; slightly brisk for parent bodies.", "level": 2}, {"id": "strategy-32", "field": "Strategy, leadership and change", "term": "single point of failure", "gloss": "A component, role or person whose failure alone would halt the whole system.", "say": "The booking system is a *single point of failure*: one login, one person, no backup.", "note": "Borrowed from engineering; precise and unemotional. More neutral than 'key-person risk' when the issue is a system rather than a person.", "level": 2}, {"id": "strategy-33", "field": "Strategy, leadership and change", "term": "sunk cost", "gloss": "Cost already incurred and unrecoverable, which rationally should not influence decisions about the future.", "say": "The two years we spent on the old model are a *sunk cost*; the question is only which option is better from here.", "note": "Lands as a release for people anchored to past effort. Be gentle; the people who did the work may hear it as dismissal.", "level": 2}, {"id": "strategy-34", "field": "Strategy, leadership and change", "term": "path dependence", "gloss": "The way earlier decisions constrain later options, so that history, not current merit, explains the present arrangement.", "say": "Our ensemble structure is *path dependent*: it reflects the staff we had in 2010, not the students we have now.", "note": "Lands when explaining why an odd arrangement persists without blaming anyone. Do not use it to imply that change is impossible.", "level": 2}, {"id": "strategy-35", "field": "Strategy, leadership and change", "term": "mission drift", "gloss": "The gradual movement of an organisation away from its founding purpose, usually through accumulated pragmatic decisions or funding pressures.", "say": "Every sponsorship ask pushes us towards gala events; I worry about *mission drift* away from the student festival we exist to run.", "note": "Lands in boards of purpose-driven bodies. Be specific about the drift, or it sounds nostalgic.", "level": 2}, {"id": "strategy-36", "field": "Strategy, leadership and change", "term": "strategic drift", "gloss": "Gerry Johnson's term for the gradual divergence between an organisation's strategy and its changing environment, often unnoticed because change is incremental.", "say": "We have not done anything wrong; we have experienced *strategic drift*. The schools changed and we kept running the 2015 festival.", "note": "Lands as a non-blaming account of decline. Distinguish it from mission drift: one is losing the environment, the other losing the purpose.", "level": 2}, {"id": "strategy-37", "field": "Strategy, leadership and change", "term": "loose-tight", "gloss": "Holding a few things non-negotiable while leaving everything else to local discretion, after Tom Peters and Robert Waterman's 'simultaneous loose-tight properties'.", "say": "We should be *loose-tight*: tight on the assessment standards and the three-year commitment, loose on how each teacher runs their ensemble.", "note": "Lands as a principled answer to autonomy debates. Only works if the tight items are genuinely few.", "level": 2}, {"id": "strategy-38", "field": "Strategy, leadership and change", "term": "subsidiarity", "gloss": "The principle that decisions should be taken at the lowest level competent to make them, with higher levels acting only where they add value.", "say": "On *subsidiarity* grounds, ensemble repertoire belongs with the director, not with me or with council.", "note": "Originally from Catholic social teaching and later European Union law; lands with policy and governance people. Sounds grand in a department meeting.", "level": 2}, {"id": "strategy-39", "field": "Strategy, leadership and change", "term": "guiding coalition", "gloss": "John Kotter's term for a group with enough credibility, expertise and authority to lead a change, assembled before the change is announced.", "say": "Before we take the proposal to the whole staff, I want a *guiding coalition*: the HOLA, one senior instrumental teacher and a parent with standing.", "note": "Lands when it is genuinely a coalition and not a group of allies. Kotter's eight-step model is often treated as a recipe, which he did not intend everyone to follow mechanically.", "level": 2}, {"id": "strategy-40", "field": "Strategy, leadership and change", "term": "sense of urgency", "gloss": "In Kotter's model, the shared recognition that change is needed now, as the first condition for any significant change.", "say": "There is no *sense of urgency* because enrolments are fine this year; the risk is three years out.", "note": "Lands as a diagnosis of why nothing moves. Manufacturing urgency is the burning platform's problem in milder form.", "level": 2}, {"id": "strategy-41", "field": "Strategy, leadership and change", "term": "getting on the balcony", "gloss": "Heifetz and Linsky's image for stepping back from the action to observe patterns, including one's own part in them, before re-entering.", "say": "Let me *get on the balcony* for a moment: we have had this same argument about rehearsal times three meetings in a row.", "note": "Lands when you use it to reframe a stuck discussion. Saying it too often makes it a mannerism.", "level": 2}, {"id": "strategy-42", "field": "Strategy, leadership and change", "term": "critical path", "gloss": "The sequence of dependent tasks that determines the earliest possible completion date, where any delay delays the whole.", "say": "The venue contract is on the *critical path*; nothing else in the festival plan matters until that is signed.", "note": "From project management; precise and useful for committees that treat all tasks as equally urgent.", "level": 2}, {"id": "strategy-43", "field": "Strategy, leadership and change", "term": "change readiness", "gloss": "The degree to which people and systems are prepared, in belief, capacity and capability, to adopt a particular change.", "say": "The idea is sound but the *change readiness* is not there; half the staff do not yet see the problem it solves.", "note": "Lands as a reason to prepare rather than abandon. Can sound like a polite way of blaming staff for reluctance.", "level": 2}, {"id": "strategy-44", "field": "Strategy, leadership and change", "term": "institutionalise", "gloss": "To embed a practice in structures, roles, budgets and routines so that it persists beyond the people who introduced it.", "say": "The feeder band only works because I run it; until we *institutionalise* it in the budget and a role description, it is a personal project.", "note": "Lands when connected to succession and sustainability. Avoid in emotional settings; it sounds bureaucratic.", "level": 2}, {"id": "strategy-45", "field": "Strategy, leadership and change", "term": "load-bearing", "gloss": "Describes a program, role or practice that other parts of the organisation depend on, so that removing it causes unexpected collapse.", "say": "The Year 7 band camp looks like a luxury, but it is *load-bearing*: it is where the three-year commitment is formed.", "note": "A vivid architectural metaphor that lands with principals deciding what to cut. Use once; it loses force with repetition.", "level": 2}, {"id": "strategy-46", "field": "Strategy, leadership and change", "term": "early adopters", "gloss": "In Everett Rogers's diffusion of innovations, the respected minority who take up a change early and influence the majority who follow.", "say": "I would start with the *early adopters* in the instrumental team and let the rest see it working before we ask them.", "note": "Lands as a practical rollout strategy. Avoid casting the remainder as 'laggards', Rogers's term, in their hearing.", "level": 2}, {"id": "strategy-47", "field": "Strategy, leadership and change", "term": "espoused values versus basic assumptions", "gloss": "Edgar Schein's distinction between the values an organisation professes and the unspoken assumptions that actually drive behaviour, with visible artefacts as the third, surface level.", "say": "Our *espoused value* is inclusion; our *basic assumption*, judging by who gets solos, is that talent is fixed by Year 7.", "note": "The sharpest tool in Schein's model. Lands best as self-diagnosis; directed at another department it is an accusation.", "level": 2}, {"id": "strategy-48", "field": "Strategy, leadership and change", "term": "espoused theory versus theory-in-use", "gloss": "Chris Argyris and Donald Schön's distinction between how people say they act and the theory that can be inferred from how they actually act.", "say": "Our *espoused theory* is that ensembles are for everyone; our *theory-in-use* is that the strongest players get the best rehearsal time.", "note": "Lands in reflective leadership settings and with academics. Like Schein's version, it is safest applied to your own group first.", "level": 2}, {"id": "strategy-49", "field": "Strategy, leadership and change", "term": "double-loop learning", "gloss": "Argyris's term for learning that questions the underlying goals and assumptions behind an action, rather than only adjusting the action (single-loop learning).", "say": "Changing the audition date is single-loop; asking whether we should audition at all is *double-loop learning*.", "note": "Lands in reviews where adjustments keep failing. With the uninitiated, explain single-loop in the same breath.", "level": 2}, {"id": "strategy-50", "field": "Strategy, leadership and change", "term": "psychological safety", "gloss": "Amy Edmondson's term for a shared belief that a team is safe for interpersonal risk-taking, such as admitting mistakes or raising concerns.", "say": "If no one in this department ever disagrees with me, that is not harmony; it may be a lack of *psychological safety*.", "note": "Lands when the leader turns it on themselves. Widely misused to mean comfort or the absence of challenge; Edmondson's sense includes high standards.", "level": 2}, {"id": "strategy-51", "field": "Strategy, leadership and change", "term": "small wins", "gloss": "Karl Weick's argument that large problems are better tackled as a series of concrete, completed outcomes of modest importance that change perceptions and build momentum.", "say": "This is a *small wins* strategy: fix the storeroom, then the loan system, then the repair budget, and the bigger argument makes itself.", "note": "Lands with people overwhelmed by a large problem. It is a considered strategy, not the same as a quick win.", "level": 2}, {"id": "strategy-52", "field": "Strategy, leadership and change", "term": "premortem", "gloss": "Gary Klein's technique of imagining that a plan has already failed and asking the team to explain why, surfacing risks that optimism suppresses.", "say": "Before council approves the tour, can we do a *premortem*: it is next December and the tour was a disaster; what went wrong?", "note": "Lands as a constructive way to voice doubts without seeming obstructive. Run it briskly; a long premortem becomes a wake.", "level": 2}, {"id": "strategy-53", "field": "Strategy, leadership and change", "term": "wicked problem", "gloss": "Horst Rittel and Melvin Webber's term for a problem with no definitive formulation, no stopping rule and no right answer, only better or worse responses.", "say": "Equity of access to instrumental music is a *wicked problem*; any solution changes the problem, so we should judge options, not seek the answer.", "note": "Lands in policy and academic rooms. Overused for problems that are merely hard; reserve it for genuinely contested ones.", "level": 2}, {"id": "strategy-54", "field": "Strategy, leadership and change", "term": "transition versus change", "gloss": "William Bridges's distinction between the external event of change and the internal psychological process of transition people go through to accept it.", "say": "The timetable change happens on day one; the *transition* for the staff who lose their Friday rehearsal will take a year.", "note": "Lands when leaders confuse announcing a change with completing it. Bridges's point is that transition starts with an ending, not a beginning.", "level": 2}, {"id": "strategy-55", "field": "Strategy, leadership and change", "term": "neutral zone", "gloss": "Bridges's term for the uncomfortable in-between phase after the old way has ended and before the new way is established.", "say": "We are in the *neutral zone*: the old ensemble structure is gone and the new one does not yet feel like ours.", "note": "Lands by normalising discomfort rather than treating it as failure. Saying it too early can seem to excuse poor planning.", "level": 2}, {"id": "strategy-56", "field": "Strategy, leadership and change", "term": "fidelity versus adaptation", "gloss": "The tension in implementation between delivering a program exactly as designed and adapting it to local context.", "say": "With the new syllabus resources, the question is *fidelity versus adaptation*: which elements are core, and which can schools reshape?", "note": "Lands in SCSA and system discussions. The useful move is to name the core components that require fidelity.", "level": 2}, {"id": "strategy-57", "field": "Strategy, leadership and change", "term": "stop-doing list", "gloss": "A deliberate list of activities the organisation will cease, popularised by Jim Collins, so that new priorities are resourced from existing capacity.", "say": "If this plan adds three things, I would like a *stop-doing list* with three things on it as well.", "note": "Lands well because it is concrete and fair. The difficulty is political, so propose your own items first.", "level": 2}, {"id": "strategy-58", "field": "Strategy, leadership and change", "term": "decision rights", "gloss": "The explicit allocation of who has the authority to make which decisions, as distinct from who is consulted or informed.", "say": "Most of our conflict is about *decision rights*: nobody is sure whether repertoire is my call, the director's or the principal's.", "note": "Lands as a structural diagnosis that depersonalises conflict. Particularly useful in role-definition discussions.", "level": 2}, {"id": "strategy-59", "field": "Strategy, leadership and change", "term": "change saturation", "gloss": "The point at which the total volume of concurrent change exceeds people's capacity to absorb it, regardless of the merit of any single change.", "say": "Each change is reasonable; together they exceed the department's capacity. We have reached *change saturation*, not resistance.", "note": "More precise than change fatigue because it is about volume, not mood. Lands when you can list the concurrent changes.", "level": 2}, {"id": "strategy-60", "field": "Strategy, leadership and change", "term": "holding environment", "gloss": "Heifetz's term, borrowed from the psychoanalyst Donald Winnicott, for the bonds, structures and trust that contain the stress of adaptive work so people can stay engaged.", "say": "The department meeting has to be a *holding environment* for this argument, not a place where it is settled by whoever speaks loudest.", "note": "Lands with people who know the literature. To others, explain it as keeping people in the room while it is hard.", "level": 3}, {"id": "strategy-61", "field": "Strategy, leadership and change", "term": "productive zone of disequilibrium", "gloss": "Heifetz's band of stress high enough to motivate adaptive work but not so high that people become overwhelmed or retreat.", "say": "Our job as leaders is to keep the staff in the *productive zone of disequilibrium*: uncomfortable enough to change, not so stressed they shut down.", "note": "High register and recognisable to leadership scholars. 'Regulating the heat' is Heifetz's plainer phrase and often lands better.", "level": 3}, {"id": "strategy-62", "field": "Strategy, leadership and change", "term": "work avoidance", "gloss": "Heifetz's term for the ways groups evade adaptive challenges, such as scapegoating, denial, a premature technical fix or diverting attention.", "say": "Commissioning another review is *work avoidance*; we already know the diagnosis.", "note": "Sharp and accurate; lands in a confident group. Directed at a superior's proposal, it can sound insolent.", "level": 3}, {"id": "strategy-63", "field": "Strategy, leadership and change", "term": "giving the work back", "gloss": "Heifetz's principle that leaders should return adaptive problems to the people who must change, rather than solving them on their behalf.", "say": "I could write the ensemble policy myself, but this is a case for *giving the work back* to the directors; they are the ones who have to live with it.", "note": "Lands as a deliberate leadership choice. If the group lacks the capacity, it looks like abdication.", "level": 3}, {"id": "strategy-64", "field": "Strategy, leadership and change", "term": "isomorphism", "gloss": "Paul DiMaggio and Walter Powell's term for the tendency of organisations in a field to become alike through coercive, mimetic and normative pressures.", "say": "Every specialist program in the state now looks the same; that is *isomorphism*, and it is not the same thing as best practice.", "note": "Lands in academic and policy rooms; signals sociology training. Explain the three pressures if you use it with a lay board.", "level": 3}, {"id": "strategy-65", "field": "Strategy, leadership and change", "term": "ambidexterity", "gloss": "An organisation's ability to exploit existing strengths efficiently while also exploring new possibilities, after James March's distinction between exploitation and exploration.", "say": "The Festival needs *ambidexterity*: run the core event flawlessly and reserve some capacity to experiment with the regional format.", "note": "Lands in strategy discussions with educated boards. Ambitious organisations often stop exploring once exploitation works.", "level": 3}, {"id": "strategy-66", "field": "Strategy, leadership and change", "term": "muddling through", "gloss": "Charles Lindblom's account of policy-making as successive limited comparisons, incremental steps adjusted by feedback, rather than comprehensive rational planning.", "say": "In defence of *muddling through*: we do not know enough to design the perfect intake model, so let us change one variable a year and watch.", "note": "A respectable position when cited knowingly; Lindblom's 1959 paper is a classic. Said without the reference, it sounds like an admission of drift.", "level": 3}, {"id": "strategy-67", "field": "Strategy, leadership and change", "term": "bounded rationality", "gloss": "Herbert Simon's concept that decision-makers are limited by information, time and cognitive capacity, so they cannot optimise and instead seek acceptable solutions.", "say": "Given *bounded rationality*, the committee will decide on what is in the paper; so the paper must carry the real trade-offs, not an appendix.", "note": "Lands when designing decision processes. As an excuse for a poor decision, it sounds evasive.", "level": 3}, {"id": "strategy-68", "field": "Strategy, leadership and change", "term": "satisficing", "gloss": "Simon's term for choosing the first option that meets an acceptable threshold rather than searching for the optimal one.", "say": "For the bus contract, *satisficing* is the right approach; three acceptable quotes, pick the best value, move on.", "note": "Lands with precision when deployed as a deliberate method. Without context, people hear a typo for 'satisfying'.", "level": 3}, {"id": "strategy-69", "field": "Strategy, leadership and change", "term": "requisite variety", "gloss": "W. Ross Ashby's law that a system can control its environment only if it has at least as much variety in its responses as the environment presents.", "say": "A single audition format lacks *requisite variety*; the students arriving are too different for one test to read them all fairly.", "note": "Cybernetic and genuinely rare; lands with systems thinkers and some academics. Elsewhere it sounds abstruse.", "level": 3}, {"id": "strategy-70", "field": "Strategy, leadership and change", "term": "loosely coupled system", "gloss": "Karl Weick's description of educational organisations as made of units that are responsive to each other yet retain their own identity and separateness, so change at one point travels weakly.", "say": "Schools are *loosely coupled systems*; a directive from the head of department does not reach the rehearsal room unchanged.", "note": "Lands with educational researchers and senior system leaders. Weick saw loose coupling as having advantages too, so do not present it only as a fault.", "level": 3}, {"id": "strategy-71", "field": "Strategy, leadership and change", "term": "decoupling", "gloss": "John Meyer and Brian Rowan's observation that organisations often separate formal structures, adopted for legitimacy, from the actual work, which proceeds differently.", "say": "The policy satisfies the review panel; what happens in the practice room is *decoupled* from it entirely.", "note": "Precise in institutional theory and with academic committees. Can sound like an accusation of hypocrisy if not framed as a structural observation.", "level": 3}, {"id": "strategy-72", "field": "Strategy, leadership and change", "term": "garbage can model", "gloss": "Michael Cohen, James March and Johan Olsen's account of decision-making in 'organised anarchies' as the chance collision of problems, solutions, participants and choice opportunities.", "say": "This committee follows the *garbage can model*: the solution walked in looking for a problem, and the agenda supplied one.", "note": "Witty and accurate with academics, who often know it; the name alone can offend a lay committee. Use with a light touch.", "level": 3}, {"id": "strategy-73", "field": "Strategy, leadership and change", "term": "second-order change", "gloss": "From Paul Watzlawick and colleagues: change that alters the system's rules or assumptions, as opposed to first-order change, which adjusts behaviour within the existing system.", "say": "Moving rehearsals is first-order; asking whether the program is ensemble-led or curriculum-led is *second-order change*.", "note": "Lands in reflective leadership settings; overlaps with double-loop learning. Do not claim second-order change for a reorganisation of the same assumptions.", "level": 3}, {"id": "strategy-74", "field": "Strategy, leadership and change", "term": "no-regret moves", "gloss": "Actions worth taking under any plausible future scenario, so that they can proceed before strategic uncertainty is resolved.", "say": "Whatever council decides about the specialist program, documenting the instrument inventory is a *no-regret move*.", "note": "From scenario planning; lands when a group is paralysed by uncertainty. Make sure the move is genuinely good in every scenario.", "level": 3}, {"id": "strategy-75", "field": "Strategy, leadership and change", "term": "optionality", "gloss": "The value of keeping future choices open, especially when a decision is costly or impossible to reverse.", "say": "Signing a three-year venue contract destroys *optionality*; a one-year contract with a right of renewal keeps it.", "note": "Lands when distinguishing reversible from irreversible decisions. Overused in finance-adjacent speech; avoid using it for trivial choices.", "level": 3}, {"id": "strategy-76", "field": "Strategy, leadership and change", "term": "policy window", "gloss": "John Kingdon's term for the brief moment when problem, policy and political streams converge, making change possible that was impossible before or after.", "say": "The syllabus review is a *policy window*; if we do not put the ensemble assessment case now, it will not reopen for years.", "note": "Lands with policy people and senior public servants, who know the multiple streams framework. Name what has converged, or it sounds like opportunism.", "level": 3}, {"id": "strategy-77", "field": "Strategy, leadership and change", "term": "theory of action", "gloss": "The working logic connecting a leader's actions to intended results, often stated as 'if we do this, then that will follow', and testable in practice.", "say": "My *theory of action* is simple: if Year 7s have a good first term in ensembles, retention into Year 10 rises.", "note": "Associated with Argyris and Schön and taken into school leadership by Richard Elmore and colleagues. It is closer to the leader's own reasoning than a program's theory of change.", "level": 3}, {"id": "strategy-78", "field": "Strategy, leadership and change", "term": "exit, voice and loyalty", "gloss": "Albert Hirschman's framework for how members respond to decline: leaving, speaking up, or staying out of loyalty, with loyalty affecting the balance between the other two.", "say": "When families withdraw silently, that is *exit*; I would rather build channels for *voice* before Year 9, when loyalty is still strong.", "note": "Lands in governance and parent-body discussions with an educated audience. The insight is that suppressing voice increases exit.", "level": 3}]}, {"field": "Education policy and research", "intro": "The register is that of the research seminar and the cabinet submission at once: cautious about causal claims, exact about what evidence can and cannot bear, and quick to ask for the mechanism. Native speakers concede limits early, name designs and effects precisely, and treat measures as instruments with side effects rather than as facts.", "entries": [{"id": "education-policy-and-research-1", "field": "Education policy and research", "term": "evidence hierarchy", "gloss": "A ranking of research designs by their capacity to support causal claims, usually with systematic reviews and randomised trials at the top; it ranks designs for one question type, not the worth of all knowledge.", "say": "Before we rank these submissions, can we agree which *evidence hierarchy* we are applying, because a case study and a trial are answering different questions.", "note": "Lands when it disciplines a loose discussion of what counts. Backfires if wielded to dismiss practitioner or qualitative evidence outright, which policy people now regard as naive.", "level": 1}, {"id": "education-policy-and-research-2", "field": "Education policy and research", "term": "evidence-informed versus evidence-based", "gloss": "Evidence-based implies practice derived from and licensed by research; evidence-informed concedes that research is one input weighed alongside context, professional judgement and values.", "say": "I'd prefer the policy say *evidence-informed*, because the research on ensemble pedagogy is thin and we should not claim more than it can carry.", "note": "Choosing the weaker phrase deliberately signals that you know the difference. Used by habit with no reason given, it reads as hedging.", "level": 1}, {"id": "education-policy-and-research-4", "field": "Education policy and research", "term": "standardised mean difference", "gloss": "The difference between two group means divided by a pooled or control standard deviation, of which Cohen's d and Hedges' g are the common forms, g correcting for small-sample bias.", "say": "Is that a *standardised mean difference* against a business-as-usual control, or against no instruction at all? The number means something quite different in each case.", "note": "Precise and senior in a research committee. In a parent forum it is jargon; say how big the difference was instead.", "level": 2}, {"id": "education-policy-and-research-5", "field": "Education policy and research", "term": "the hinge point", "gloss": "Hattie's benchmark of d = 0.40 in Visible Learning, offered as the average effect of a year of schooling across his syntheses, above which an influence is said to be worth attention.", "say": "I'd be cautious about the *hinge point*; it averages across outcome measures and age groups that aren't comparable, so it isn't a threshold any single program should be held to.", "note": "Knowing its origin and its critiques signals real reading. Treating 0.40 as a pass mark sounds like a professional learning slide.", "level": 2}, {"id": "education-policy-and-research-6", "field": "Education policy and research", "term": "systematic review versus meta-analysis", "gloss": "A systematic review is a protocol-driven search and appraisal of all relevant studies; a meta-analysis is the statistical pooling of their effects, which a systematic review may or may not include.", "say": "It's a *systematic review*, not a meta-analysis; they found the studies too heterogeneous to pool, which is itself a finding.", "note": "The distinction is routinely blurred, so holding it lands well with academics. Easy to over-labour; one clause is enough.", "level": 1}, {"id": "education-policy-and-research-7", "field": "Education policy and research", "term": "synthesis of meta-analyses", "gloss": "A second-order aggregation that combines the results of many meta-analyses, as in Visible Learning, compounding any differences in the populations, designs and outcome measures beneath them.", "say": "A *synthesis of meta-analyses* is a very long way from any classroom; I'd want to see the primary studies on the specific practice before we fund it.", "note": "Lands as a quiet challenge to league-table use of evidence. Sounds pedantic if no decision actually turns on it.", "level": 2}, {"id": "education-policy-and-research-8", "field": "Education policy and research", "term": "outcome measure sensitivity", "gloss": "The tendency for researcher-designed, narrowly aligned tests to produce much larger effect sizes than broad standardised assessments, so effects measured on different instruments are not directly comparable.", "say": "Half of those large effects come from tests the researchers wrote themselves; that's *outcome measure sensitivity*, not a better intervention.", "note": "A high-register point that unsettles a weak evidence claim. Make it once and briefly, or it sounds like you are dismissing research wholesale.", "level": 3}, {"id": "education-policy-and-research-9", "field": "Education policy and research", "term": "internal versus external validity", "gloss": "Internal validity is whether a study's design supports its causal claim within the sample; external validity is whether that claim generalises to other people, settings and times.", "say": "The trial has strong *internal validity*; my question is external validity, because nothing in that sample looks like a selective music program.", "note": "Standard in any research committee and safe to use. Keep the two halves distinct; collapsing them into 'is it valid' loses the point.", "level": 1}, {"id": "education-policy-and-research-11", "field": "Education policy and research", "term": "randomised controlled trial", "gloss": "A design in which participants or units are randomly allocated to conditions so that, in expectation, the only systematic difference between groups is the intervention.", "say": "A *randomised controlled trial* would tell us whether the model works on average; it won't tell us why, or under what staffing conditions.", "note": "Credit its strength before naming its limits. Calling it 'the gold standard' unqualified marks you as either naive or provocative.", "level": 1}, {"id": "education-policy-and-research-12", "field": "Education policy and research", "term": "quasi-experimental design", "gloss": "A study comparing treated and untreated groups without random allocation, relying on matching, statistical controls or natural variation to approximate a counterfactual.", "say": "Short of randomising schools, a *quasi-experimental design* with matched comparison sites is the most defensible thing we could do.", "note": "Signals methodological literacy in a university or departmental setting. Pronounce quasi as KWAY-zye or KWAH-zee; both are accepted.", "level": 2}, {"id": "education-policy-and-research-13", "field": "Education policy and research", "term": "regression discontinuity", "gloss": "A quasi-experimental method that compares units just either side of an eligibility cut-off, treating the cut-off as locally random to estimate a causal effect.", "say": "The audition cut score would give us a natural *regression discontinuity*: students who just made it against those who just missed out.", "note": "Impressive and exactly right where a sharp threshold exists. Out of place anywhere else; reserve it for a research audience.", "level": 3}, {"id": "education-policy-and-research-14", "field": "Education policy and research", "term": "intention to treat", "gloss": "Analysing participants in the groups to which they were assigned, regardless of whether they completed the intervention, to avoid inflating effects by excluding drop-outs.", "say": "If we only count the students who stayed for three years, we flatter the program; an *intention to treat* view counts everyone who started.", "note": "Very precise and persuasive with academics and senior public servants. Explain it in a clause for anyone else.", "level": 3}, {"id": "education-policy-and-research-15", "field": "Education policy and research", "term": "publication bias", "gloss": "The systematic over-representation of positive or significant findings in the published literature, which inflates pooled effect estimates.", "say": "Allowing for *publication bias*, the true effect is probably smaller than that review suggests.", "note": "Standard in research settings. Used reflexively against any finding you dislike, it becomes a cheap shot.", "level": 2}, {"id": "education-policy-and-research-16", "field": "Education policy and research", "term": "file-drawer problem", "gloss": "Rosenthal's name for null results that remain unpublished in researchers' drawers, the mechanism behind publication bias.", "say": "Every school that tried this and saw nothing simply didn't write it up; it's the *file-drawer problem* at the scale of a sector.", "note": "Vivid and well understood by academics. A touch informal for a written submission, where publication bias is cleaner.", "level": 2}, {"id": "education-policy-and-research-17", "field": "Education policy and research", "term": "heterogeneity", "gloss": "Variation in effect sizes across studies beyond what sampling error would predict, signalling that the effect depends on context, population or implementation.", "say": "The average looks healthy, but the *heterogeneity* is enormous; it works brilliantly somewhere and not at all elsewhere.", "note": "A senior point that moves the conversation from whether to where and how. Pronounce het-er-oh-jeh-NEE-ih-tee.", "level": 2}, {"id": "education-policy-and-research-18", "field": "Education policy and research", "term": "moderator versus mediator", "gloss": "A moderator changes the strength or direction of an effect (for whom, when); a mediator is the mechanism through which the effect operates (how, why).", "say": "Prior instrumental experience looks like a *moderator* here, while practice time is the likely mediator.", "note": "The distinction is frequently confused even by researchers, so holding it lands. Labour it and you sound like a methods lecture.", "level": 3}, {"id": "education-policy-and-research-19", "field": "Education policy and research", "term": "implementation fidelity", "gloss": "The degree to which a program is delivered as designed, covering adherence, dosage, quality of delivery and participant responsiveness.", "say": "Before we conclude the model failed, I'd want to know about *implementation fidelity*; two of the four sites never ran the sectionals.", "note": "Essential in any evaluation discussion. Treated as rigid compliance, it alienates teachers who adapt for good reasons.", "level": 1}, {"id": "education-policy-and-research-20", "field": "Education policy and research", "term": "lethal mutation", "gloss": "An adaptation of a research-based practice that preserves its surface form while destroying the mechanism that made it work, a term from design research popularised in schools by Dylan Wiliam.", "say": "Peer feedback without criteria is a *lethal mutation*; it keeps the ritual and loses the learning.", "note": "Memorable and precise in a curriculum or professional learning setting. Sounds theatrical if used about minor variations.", "level": 3}, {"id": "education-policy-and-research-21", "field": "Education policy and research", "term": "dosage", "gloss": "The amount, frequency and duration of an intervention actually received, as distinct from what was planned.", "say": "The planned *dosage* was two sessions a week; with excursions and assemblies most students got closer to one.", "note": "Clean and borrowed from health research, which gives it weight. Avoid it with parents, where it sounds clinical.", "level": 2}, {"id": "education-policy-and-research-22", "field": "Education policy and research", "term": "active ingredients", "gloss": "The components of an intervention that carry its effect and must be preserved under adaptation, as distinct from its incidental features.", "say": "Sites can change the timetable and the repertoire; the *active ingredients* are weekly small-group playing and specific feedback.", "note": "The best way to reconcile fidelity with local adaptation. Ask what the evidence says the ingredients are before asserting them.", "level": 2}, {"id": "education-policy-and-research-24", "field": "Education policy and research", "term": "depth of scale", "gloss": "Coburn's argument that genuine scale requires depth of change in practice, sustainability and a shift in ownership, not merely spread to more sites.", "say": "Twenty schools have the badge, but there's no *depth of scale*; in most of them nothing in the rehearsal room has changed.", "note": "A precise rebuttal to scaling by numbers. Name Coburn only to a research audience; elsewhere the idea stands without the citation.", "level": 3}, {"id": "education-policy-and-research-25", "field": "Education policy and research", "term": "Hawthorne effect", "gloss": "Improvement in performance caused by participants' awareness of being studied or given attention rather than by the intervention itself.", "say": "Any new program gets a lift from novelty and attention; the *Hawthorne effect* is why I'd want three years of data.", "note": "Widely known, so safe. Some scholars dispute the original studies, so use it as a label for the phenomenon, not as settled history.", "level": 1}, {"id": "education-policy-and-research-29", "field": "Education policy and research", "term": "backward mapping", "gloss": "Elmore's approach to implementation that begins with the behaviour required at the point of delivery and works back up through the system to determine what policy must enable.", "say": "Let's do some *backward mapping*: start with what a Year 11 teacher needs in the room in week three, then ask what the syllabus document has to say.", "note": "A senior move on a curriculum committee. Not to be confused with backward design in curriculum planning, though the logic is cousin to it.", "level": 3}, {"id": "education-policy-and-research-30", "field": "Education policy and research", "term": "unintended consequences", "gloss": "Effects of a policy that were not part of its purpose, which may be beneficial, harmful or perverse, and which a mature policy process tries to anticipate.", "say": "What are the likely *unintended consequences* if the ensemble requirement becomes a graduation condition?", "note": "Expected in any serious deliberation. Name a specific one; the generic phrase alone sounds like obstruction.", "level": 1}, {"id": "education-policy-and-research-31", "field": "Education policy and research", "term": "perverse incentive", "gloss": "An incentive that rewards behaviour contrary to the policy's own purpose.", "say": "Funding by enrolment numbers creates a *perverse incentive* to keep students in a course that no longer suits them.", "note": "Sharp and widely understood. Accusatory if it implies colleagues are responding cynically, so frame it as structural.", "level": 1}, {"id": "education-policy-and-research-34", "field": "Education policy and research", "term": "gaming", "gloss": "Strategic behaviour that improves measured performance without improving the underlying outcome, such as reclassifying students or timing entries.", "say": "I'm not suggesting anyone is cheating; I'm saying the metric invites *gaming*, and good people respond to incentives.", "note": "The neutral technical term. Always detach it from individual motive or it lands as an allegation.", "level": 2}, {"id": "education-policy-and-research-35", "field": "Education policy and research", "term": "curriculum narrowing", "gloss": "The reduction of taught content and time to what is assessed under high-stakes regimes, typically at the expense of the arts, humanities and unassessed capabilities.", "say": "If the reporting regime only sees literacy and numeracy, music will be the first casualty of *curriculum narrowing*.", "note": "Well understood across the sector. Avoid presenting it as a uniquely Australian problem; it is documented internationally.", "level": 1}, {"id": "education-policy-and-research-36", "field": "Education policy and research", "term": "educational triage", "gloss": "Booher-Jennings' term for concentrating resources on students near a proficiency threshold, the so-called bubble students, at the expense of those well above or below it.", "say": "A rule that only counts students reaching a grade cut-off encourages *educational triage*; the strongest and the weakest stop being anyone's priority.", "note": "Precise and sobering in an accountability debate. Use the term, not 'bubble kids', in formal settings.", "level": 3}, {"id": "education-policy-and-research-37", "field": "Education policy and research", "term": "high-stakes accountability", "gloss": "Accountability arrangements in which measured results carry significant consequences for schools, staff or students, such as funding, reputation, sanction or selection.", "say": "My School made NAPLAN a form of *high-stakes accountability* for schools even though it was designed as a diagnostic.", "note": "Standard term. Stakes are relative and perceived; acknowledge that before treating a test as inherently high-stakes.", "level": 1}, {"id": "education-policy-and-research-38", "field": "Education policy and research", "term": "intelligent accountability", "gloss": "Onora O'Neill's term, from her 2002 Reith Lectures, for accountability that relies on informed professional judgement and trust rather than proliferating performance indicators.", "say": "What I'm proposing is *intelligent accountability*: fewer measures, external moderation and honest reporting of what we can't yet quantify.", "note": "Carries weight with principals and public servants. Can sound like a plea to escape scrutiny unless paired with a concrete alternative.", "level": 2}, {"id": "education-policy-and-research-39", "field": "Education policy and research", "term": "lateral accountability", "gloss": "Accountability exercised among peers and professional communities, such as moderation and collegial review, as distinct from vertical accountability upward to authorities.", "say": "Consensus moderation across the four schools is a form of *lateral accountability*, and it changes practice more than any audit does.", "note": "A precise senior term. Make sure the peer process is real before claiming it; reviewers know the difference.", "level": 2}, {"id": "education-policy-and-research-40", "field": "Education policy and research", "term": "performativity", "gloss": "Stephen Ball's term for a regime in which teachers and institutions are governed through judgements, comparisons and displays of performance that come to reshape professional identity.", "say": "Some of what we call quality assurance is really *performativity*: evidence produced to be seen, not to improve anything.", "note": "High register and sociological; lands in a university or conference setting. In a school leadership meeting it can sound like theory used to avoid work.", "level": 3}, {"id": "education-policy-and-research-41", "field": "Education policy and research", "term": "value-added measure", "gloss": "An estimate of a school's or teacher's contribution to student progress after adjusting for prior attainment and sometimes background characteristics.", "say": "Our ATAR results look modest in raw terms, but on any *value-added measure* the music cohort is doing well above expectation.", "note": "Persuasive when you have the data. Value-added models are statistically noisy at small cohort sizes, so do not over-claim for a single class.", "level": 2}, {"id": "education-policy-and-research-43", "field": "Education policy and research", "term": "selection effect", "gloss": "A difference in outcomes attributable to who enters a group rather than to what the group experiences, the central problem in evaluating selective programs.", "say": "Our students outperform, but that's partly a *selection effect*; they were auditioned in. The honest claim is about growth, not raw results.", "note": "Conceding it first, especially about your own selective program, earns enormous credibility. Avoid the cruder synonym 'creaming' in formal settings.", "level": 2}, {"id": "education-policy-and-research-44", "field": "Education policy and research", "term": "compositional effect", "gloss": "The effect of the social or academic composition of a school or class on an individual's outcomes, over and above that individual's own characteristics.", "say": "Some of the advantage of a specialist program is *compositional*: students learn from the standard set by the people around them.", "note": "Precise and senior. Distinguish it from peer effects narrowly defined if speaking to economists.", "level": 2}, {"id": "education-policy-and-research-45", "field": "Education policy and research", "term": "residualisation", "gloss": "The process by which public schools come to serve disproportionately disadvantaged students as more advantaged families exit to selective or non-government schools.", "say": "Selective programs can slow the *residualisation* of a public school, but they can also accelerate it in the schools nearby.", "note": "A recognised term in Australian education debate. Use it with care in public forums, where it can sound like a judgement on a school's community.", "level": 2}, {"id": "education-policy-and-research-46", "field": "Education policy and research", "term": "vertical versus horizontal equity", "gloss": "Horizontal equity treats like cases alike; vertical equity treats unlike cases differently in proportion to their need.", "say": "Equal instrumental hours per student is horizontal equity; *vertical equity* would weight the allocation towards schools with no private tuition base.", "note": "Clarifies a muddled fairness argument instantly. Lands best when you then give the concrete allocation.", "level": 2}, {"id": "education-policy-and-research-47", "field": "Education policy and research", "term": "proportionate universalism", "gloss": "Marmot's principle that actions should be universal but delivered with a scale and intensity proportionate to the level of disadvantage.", "say": "I'd argue for *proportionate universalism*: every Year 7 gets an instrument, and the schools with the steepest gradient get extra tuition time.", "note": "Respected among policy people, from public health. Can sound borrowed if you cannot say how the proportion would be set.", "level": 3}, {"id": "education-policy-and-research-48", "field": "Education policy and research", "term": "needs-based funding", "gloss": "Funding that allocates resources according to measured student need, in the Australian context through the Schooling Resource Standard and its loadings following the Gonski review.", "say": "The instrumental program sits outside *needs-based funding* entirely, which is why access tracks postcode so closely.", "note": "Standard national vocabulary. Be accurate about which loadings apply before arguing from them.", "level": 1}, {"id": "education-policy-and-research-49", "field": "Education policy and research", "term": "excellence and equity", "gloss": "The paired goal at the head of the Alice Springs (Mparntwe) Education Declaration, treated in policy as complementary rather than as a trade-off.", "say": "The Declaration puts *excellence and equity* side by side; a specialist program has to show it serves both, not one at the expense of the other.", "note": "Expected in any national policy discussion. Treating the pair as a slogan rather than a tension to be managed sounds hollow.", "level": 1}, {"id": "education-policy-and-research-50", "field": "Education policy and research", "term": "social gradient", "gloss": "The stepwise relationship between socio-economic position and outcomes across the whole distribution, not only at the extremes.", "say": "Participation in instrumental music follows a steep *social gradient*, and it's visible at every step, not just at the bottom.", "note": "More precise than 'disadvantage', which implies a line between groups. Commonly used in public health and in Australian education research.", "level": 2}, {"id": "education-policy-and-research-51", "field": "Education policy and research", "term": "policy borrowing", "gloss": "The adoption of a policy or practice from another system, typically justified by that system's apparent success, often without regard to the conditions that made it work there.", "say": "Finland's results are a weak basis for *policy borrowing* unless we are also borrowing its teacher education, salaries and social policy.", "note": "A core term in comparative education. The Finland example is now a cliche; choose a less familiar case if you can.", "level": 1}, {"id": "education-policy-and-research-52", "field": "Education policy and research", "term": "travelling versus embedded policy", "gloss": "The distinction between globally circulating policy ideas and the local histories, institutions and politics into which they land and by which they are transformed.", "say": "That model is *travelling policy*; what we need to understand is how it becomes embedded in a WA public school with our industrial agreement.", "note": "High register, from comparative education sociology. Lands at a conference; overly theoretical in a principal's office.", "level": 3}, {"id": "education-policy-and-research-53", "field": "Education policy and research", "term": "PISA shock", "gloss": "A sudden national policy reaction to disappointing international assessment results, the archetype being Germany after the first PISA results in 2001.", "say": "I'm wary of a *PISA shock* response that cuts the arts to fund remedial literacy; the evidence for that trade is poor.", "note": "Widely recognised among policy people. Avoid implying every reform after a PISA release is a panic.", "level": 2}, {"id": "education-policy-and-research-54", "field": "Education policy and research", "term": "Global Education Reform Movement", "gloss": "Pasi Sahlberg's label, abbreviated GERM, for a cluster of market-oriented reforms: standardisation, core-subject focus, test-based accountability, competition and choice.", "say": "Much of what's being proposed sits squarely in the *Global Education Reform Movement* playbook, and the arts tend to lose in that playbook.", "note": "Recognised but partisan; it signals a position. Use it when you are prepared to own that position.", "level": 2}, {"id": "education-policy-and-research-55", "field": "Education policy and research", "term": "institutional isomorphism", "gloss": "DiMaggio and Powell's account of why organisations in a field grow alike through coercive, mimetic and normative pressures, independently of efficiency.", "say": "Every school now has a music academy brand; that's *institutional isomorphism*, not evidence that the model works.", "note": "Senior and sociological; impressive at a university committee. Pronounce eye-so-MOR-fizm.", "level": 3}, {"id": "education-policy-and-research-56", "field": "Education policy and research", "term": "loose coupling", "gloss": "Weick's description of educational organisations as systems whose parts are connected weakly, so that decisions at one level only loosely determine practice at another.", "say": "Schools are *loosely coupled*; the policy can say whatever it likes, and the rehearsal room on Tuesday afternoon will decide.", "note": "An explanatory concept senior people respect. Can sound like an excuse for non-implementation if not followed by a remedy.", "level": 3}, {"id": "education-policy-and-research-57", "field": "Education policy and research", "term": "street-level bureaucracy", "gloss": "Lipsky's account of front-line public servants, teachers among them, whose discretion under resource pressure effectively makes policy in its delivery.", "say": "Instrumental teachers are *street-level bureaucrats* in Lipsky's sense; their discretion about who gets the extra lesson is the real allocation policy.", "note": "Powerful in a policy or implementation discussion. 'Bureaucrat' can grate with teachers; explain that it is a compliment to their discretion.", "level": 3}, {"id": "education-policy-and-research-58", "field": "Education policy and research", "term": "implementation gap", "gloss": "The distance between a policy's stated intent and what is actually delivered at the point of practice.", "say": "The syllabus is sound; the *implementation gap* is in resourcing, because half the schools don't have a specialist teacher.", "note": "Common and useful. Locate the gap precisely or it becomes a vague complaint.", "level": 1}, {"id": "education-policy-and-research-59", "field": "Education policy and research", "term": "the policy cycle", "gloss": "A stylised sequence of identifying issues, analysis, instruments, consultation, coordination, decision, implementation and evaluation, given its standard Australian form by Bridgman and Davis.", "say": "We've gone straight from issue to decision; on the *policy cycle* we've skipped analysis and consultation, and that's where this will come undone.", "note": "A shared reference for public servants. Acknowledge that real policy rarely follows the cycle; insiders treat it as a heuristic, not a description.", "level": 1}, {"id": "education-policy-and-research-61", "field": "Education policy and research", "term": "incrementalism", "gloss": "Lindblom's account of policy as 'muddling through': successive limited comparisons and small adjustments rather than comprehensive rational planning.", "say": "I'd defend some *incrementalism* here; we don't know enough to redesign the whole program in one stroke.", "note": "Respectable when chosen deliberately. Can be heard as timidity, so give the reason you are choosing small steps.", "level": 2}, {"id": "education-policy-and-research-65", "field": "Education policy and research", "term": "consultation versus co-design", "gloss": "Consultation seeks views on a proposal largely formed by the decision-maker; co-design shares the framing and development of the proposal itself with those affected.", "say": "Let's be honest with parents about whether this is *consultation* or co-design; calling it co-design when the decision is made will cost us trust.", "note": "The honest labelling is what lands. Promising co-design you cannot deliver is a common, costly error.", "level": 1}, {"id": "education-policy-and-research-66", "field": "Education policy and research", "term": "the IAP2 spectrum", "gloss": "The International Association for Public Participation's scale of public participation: inform, consult, involve, collaborate and empower, each carrying a different promise to the public.", "say": "On the *IAP2 spectrum* this is 'consult', not 'involve'; our communication should make that promise and no more.", "note": "Widely used in Australian government engagement. Senior public servants recognise it instantly; teachers may not.", "level": 2}, {"id": "education-policy-and-research-67", "field": "Education policy and research", "term": "tokenism", "gloss": "Participation that gives people a voice without influence, a rung on Arnstein's ladder of citizen participation below genuine partnership and delegated power.", "say": "A single student on the committee with no vote is *tokenism*; either give them a real role or don't pretend.", "note": "Sharp but legitimate. Directed at a colleague's initiative, it stings; frame it as a design problem.", "level": 2}, {"id": "education-policy-and-research-68", "field": "Education policy and research", "term": "consultation fatigue", "gloss": "The disengagement of stakeholders who have been consulted repeatedly without visible effect on decisions.", "say": "The parents have *consultation fatigue*; three surveys in two years and none of them changed anything they could see.", "note": "Common in government. Lands when you then propose closing the loop on earlier consultations.", "level": 1}, {"id": "education-policy-and-research-69", "field": "Education policy and research", "term": "policy entrepreneur", "gloss": "Kingdon's term for an actor who invests time and reputation in coupling a problem to their preferred solution when a policy window opens.", "say": "Every reform like this needs a *policy entrepreneur* inside the Department; without one it will sit in a drawer.", "note": "Strategic and precise. Calling yourself one sounds self-aggrandising; use it of others or of the role.", "level": 3}, {"id": "education-policy-and-research-70", "field": "Education policy and research", "term": "unfunded mandate", "gloss": "A requirement imposed by a higher authority without the resources needed to meet it.", "say": "Requiring every school to run a performance showcase without staffing it is an *unfunded mandate*.", "note": "Instantly understood and often decisive. Use it sparingly or you will be heard as the person who always asks for money.", "level": 1}, {"id": "education-policy-and-research-72", "field": "Education policy and research", "term": "cost-effectiveness", "gloss": "The ratio of outcome achieved to cost incurred, allowing comparison of interventions that pursue the same outcome, as distinct from cost-benefit analysis, which monetises the outcome.", "say": "On *cost-effectiveness*, small-group tuition beats a new auditorium by a wide margin.", "note": "Persuasive with funders and executives. Don't call it cost-benefit unless you are actually monetising benefits.", "level": 2}, {"id": "education-policy-and-research-73", "field": "Education policy and research", "term": "sunset clause", "gloss": "A provision that a policy or arrangement expires on a set date unless actively renewed after review.", "say": "Can we put a *sunset clause* on the new structure? Two years, then it lapses unless the evaluation supports it.", "note": "A constructive way to win support for a trial. Signals governance maturity on a board or council.", "level": 2}, {"id": "education-policy-and-research-75", "field": "Education policy and research", "term": "the instructional core", "gloss": "The relationship between teacher, student and content in the presence of a task, which on Elmore's argument is the only place improvement in learning actually occurs.", "say": "None of these structural changes touches the *instructional core*; unless the rehearsal itself changes, results won't.", "note": "A powerful test for any reform proposal. Lands with principals familiar with instructional rounds.", "level": 2}, {"id": "education-policy-and-research-76", "field": "Education policy and research", "term": "compliance versus commitment", "gloss": "Compliance is behaviour secured by rules and monitoring; commitment is behaviour arising from staff conviction, and reform that relies only on the former tends to decay when monitoring stops.", "say": "We can get *compliance* with the new reporting template by Friday; commitment will take a term of showing staff why it helps.", "note": "A useful leadership distinction. Avoid implying staff who comply are uncommitted; often they are simply overloaded.", "level": 2}, {"id": "education-policy-and-research-77", "field": "Education policy and research", "term": "adaptive versus technical challenge", "gloss": "Heifetz's distinction between problems solvable with existing expertise and authority, and problems requiring people to change values, habits or beliefs.", "say": "Booking the venue is technical; getting staff to share ensemble students across programs is an *adaptive challenge*.", "note": "Diagnoses why a good plan stalls. Sounds like leadership-course language if used too often.", "level": 2}, {"id": "education-policy-and-research-78", "field": "Education policy and research", "term": "realist evaluation", "gloss": "Pawson and Tilley's approach that asks what works, for whom, in what circumstances and how, by identifying context, mechanism and outcome configurations.", "say": "A *realist evaluation* would be more useful to us than a single effect size; we need to know which students it works for and why.", "note": "Highly respected in evaluation circles. Requires you to be able to name a plausible mechanism, or the term is decoration.", "level": 3}, {"id": "education-policy-and-research-80", "field": "Education policy and research", "term": "system coherence", "gloss": "The alignment of goals, curriculum, assessment, resourcing and accountability so that they pull in the same direction rather than sending competing signals.", "say": "The syllabus rewards improvisation and the exam doesn't assess it; that's a *system coherence* problem, not a teacher problem.", "note": "Moves a debate from blame to design. Lands strongly on SCSA committees.", "level": 2}]}, {"field": "Pedagogy and learning science", "intro": "The register is cognitive science spoken with a practitioner's authority: named effects, their boundary conditions, and the difference between performance today and learning that lasts. Native speakers cite findings by their proper names, state the conditions under which they hold, and translate them into the rehearsal room without overclaiming.", "entries": [{"id": "pedagogy-and-learning-science-1", "field": "Pedagogy and learning science", "term": "cognitive load theory", "gloss": "Sweller's instructional theory that learning is constrained by limited working memory and facilitated by schemas in long-term memory, so instruction should manage the load it imposes.", "say": "*Cognitive load theory* would say the problem isn't the piece, it's that we've asked them to read, count and blend in the same rehearsal.", "note": "Widely invoked in schools, often loosely. Precision about its limits and its evidence base is what separates the informed from the converted.", "level": 1}, {"id": "pedagogy-and-learning-science-2", "field": "Pedagogy and learning science", "term": "intrinsic versus extraneous load", "gloss": "Intrinsic load arises from the inherent complexity of the material relative to the learner's expertise; extraneous load arises from the way it is presented and can be reduced by design.", "say": "The chart's harmony is *intrinsic load*, fine at Year 11; the badly copied parts are extraneous load, and that we can fix.", "note": "The distinction gives teachers something to act on. Remember intrinsic load is relative to the learner, not fixed in the material.", "level": 1}, {"id": "pedagogy-and-learning-science-3", "field": "Pedagogy and learning science", "term": "germane load", "gloss": "Originally the load devoted to schema construction, later reconceptualised by Sweller as the working memory resources allocated to intrinsic load rather than a separate category.", "say": "I'd avoid building the argument on *germane load*; even Sweller has largely folded it back into intrinsic load.", "note": "Knowing its revised status marks real currency with the literature. Using it as a fresh third category sounds dated.", "level": 3}, {"id": "pedagogy-and-learning-science-4", "field": "Pedagogy and learning science", "term": "element interactivity", "gloss": "The degree to which elements of material must be processed simultaneously because they interact, the central determinant of intrinsic load.", "say": "Swing phrasing has high *element interactivity*; articulation, placement and subdivision can't be learnt one at a time in any meaningful sense.", "note": "The technically exact concept beneath intrinsic load. Lands with researchers; needs a gloss for most staff.", "level": 3}, {"id": "pedagogy-and-learning-science-5", "field": "Pedagogy and learning science", "term": "limited-capacity working memory", "gloss": "The constraint that working memory can hold and manipulate only a few novel elements at once, while long-term memory has no known practical limit.", "say": "Sight-reading a new chart taxes *limited-capacity working memory*; that's why patterns they've already automated free up the attention.", "note": "Foundational. Avoid quoting 'seven plus or minus two' as settled; current estimates for novel material are lower and the figure is contested.", "level": 1}, {"id": "pedagogy-and-learning-science-6", "field": "Pedagogy and learning science", "term": "schema", "gloss": "An organised structure in long-term memory that integrates many elements into a single unit, allowing experts to treat complex material as one chunk; plural schemas or schemata.", "say": "A rhythm section player with a strong blues *schema* hears twelve bars as one object, not forty-eight beats.", "note": "Standard in learning science. 'Schemata' is correct but can sound affected; 'schemas' is accepted in cognitive load literature.", "level": 1}, {"id": "pedagogy-and-learning-science-7", "field": "Pedagogy and learning science", "term": "automaticity", "gloss": "The capacity to perform a skill with minimal conscious attention, achieved through extensive practice, freeing working memory for higher-order processing.", "say": "Until the scale fingerings have *automaticity*, there's no attention left over for line or time feel.", "note": "Precise and safe. Distinguish it from mastery; something can be automatic and still wrong.", "level": 1}, {"id": "pedagogy-and-learning-science-8", "field": "Pedagogy and learning science", "term": "chunking", "gloss": "The grouping of individual elements into larger meaningful units in memory, the mechanism by which expertise expands effective working memory capacity.", "say": "Good readers aren't reading notes, they're *chunking*: a ii V I in a bar, a turnaround in a glance.", "note": "Long established, from the chess-memory research. Easy to overextend into a general theory of expertise.", "level": 1}, {"id": "pedagogy-and-learning-science-9", "field": "Pedagogy and learning science", "term": "worked example effect", "gloss": "The finding that novices learn more from studying worked solutions than from solving equivalent problems unaided, because problem solving imposes heavy search load.", "say": "For beginners, a transcribed solo with the analysis annotated exploits the *worked example effect*; asking them to improvise cold doesn't.", "note": "Robust for novices in well-structured domains. Pair it with expertise reversal, or you will be heard as anti-inquiry across the board.", "level": 1}, {"id": "pedagogy-and-learning-science-10", "field": "Pedagogy and learning science", "term": "split-attention effect", "gloss": "Reduced learning when learners must mentally integrate physically or temporally separated sources of information that are unintelligible in isolation.", "say": "Putting the chord symbols on a separate sheet from the melody creates a *split-attention effect*; integrate them on the part.", "note": "Practical and well supported. Applies only when sources must be integrated to be understood.", "level": 2}, {"id": "pedagogy-and-learning-science-11", "field": "Pedagogy and learning science", "term": "redundancy effect", "gloss": "Reduced learning when the same information is presented in more than one form, so learners must process both unnecessarily.", "say": "Reading out every word on the slide is the *redundancy effect* in action; the panel can either read or listen.", "note": "Useful about presentations as well as teaching. Not to be confused with the benefits of dual coding, which combine different, complementary information.", "level": 2}, {"id": "pedagogy-and-learning-science-12", "field": "Pedagogy and learning science", "term": "expertise reversal effect", "gloss": "Kalyuga's finding that instructional techniques effective for novices, such as worked examples, become ineffective or harmful as expertise grows, because the guidance becomes redundant.", "say": "The step-by-step guide suits Year 7; for the ATAR group it triggers the *expertise reversal effect*, and they'll learn more from open problems.", "note": "Resolves the false choice between explicit instruction and inquiry. Lands with anyone who has heard the debate simplified.", "level": 2}, {"id": "pedagogy-and-learning-science-13", "field": "Pedagogy and learning science", "term": "guidance fading", "gloss": "The progressive withdrawal of instructional support, often through completion problems, as the learner's schemas develop.", "say": "We use *guidance fading* across the voicing unit: fully written, then guide tones only, then just the symbols.", "note": "Precise term for good practice many teachers do intuitively. Name the steps or it sounds like a label without substance.", "level": 2}, {"id": "pedagogy-and-learning-science-14", "field": "Pedagogy and learning science", "term": "productive failure", "gloss": "Kapur's design in which students attempt complex problems before instruction, generating solutions that fail but prepare them to understand the canonical solution more deeply.", "say": "There's a case for *productive failure* with the senior arrangers; let them voice the chorale badly before we show the rules.", "note": "Supported under specific conditions, notably subsequent explicit instruction. Not a licence for unstructured discovery.", "level": 2}, {"id": "pedagogy-and-learning-science-15", "field": "Pedagogy and learning science", "term": "desirable difficulty", "gloss": "Bjork's term for conditions that slow apparent progress during learning but enhance long-term retention and transfer, such as spacing, interleaving and testing.", "say": "Rotating keys every few minutes feels worse to the students, but it's a *desirable difficulty*; the gains show up a week later.", "note": "Precise when the difficulty is the right kind. Not every difficulty is desirable; if the learner cannot respond successfully, it is simply difficulty.", "level": 2}, {"id": "pedagogy-and-learning-science-16", "field": "Pedagogy and learning science", "term": "storage strength versus retrieval strength", "gloss": "In Bjork's new theory of disuse, storage strength is how well learned something is; retrieval strength is how accessible it is now, and low retrieval strength at practice increases gains in storage strength.", "say": "They nailed it in the lesson, so retrieval strength was high, but *storage strength* was low; that's why it was gone by Thursday.", "note": "Explains the forgetting that frustrates teachers and students. Senior and exact; few people in the room will know it.", "level": 3}, {"id": "pedagogy-and-learning-science-17", "field": "Pedagogy and learning science", "term": "retrieval practice", "gloss": "Learning activity that requires recalling information from memory, which strengthens and reorganises the memory more than restudying it.", "say": "Playing the head from memory at the start of rehearsal is *retrieval practice*; reading it off the part again isn't.", "note": "Widely used and well supported. Do not reduce it to low-stakes quizzing; the principle is effortful recall in any form.", "level": 1}, {"id": "pedagogy-and-learning-science-18", "field": "Pedagogy and learning science", "term": "testing effect", "gloss": "The finding that taking a test on material produces better long-term retention than an equivalent period of restudy, independently of feedback.", "say": "The *testing effect* is about learning, not measurement; the mock is worth doing even if we never mark it.", "note": "Distinguishing assessment as learning event from assessment as measurement lands well. The name misleads parents; explain it.", "level": 1}, {"id": "pedagogy-and-learning-science-19", "field": "Pedagogy and learning science", "term": "spacing effect", "gloss": "The finding that practice distributed over time produces better long-term retention than the same amount massed into one session.", "say": "Twenty minutes a day beats two hours on Sunday; the *spacing effect* is among the most replicated findings we have.", "note": "Safe and robust. Its strength makes it a good anchor for practice advice to parents.", "level": 1}, {"id": "pedagogy-and-learning-science-20", "field": "Pedagogy and learning science", "term": "optimal lag", "gloss": "The interval between study episodes that maximises retention, which research indicates scales with how long the material must be retained.", "say": "If they need the audition excerpt in three months, the *optimal lag* between reviews is weeks, not days.", "note": "Precise and rarely heard outside research. Avoid quoting specific ratios unless you have the study in hand.", "level": 3}, {"id": "pedagogy-and-learning-science-21", "field": "Pedagogy and learning science", "term": "interleaving", "gloss": "Mixing different types of problems or skills within a practice session, which improves discrimination between categories and long-term retention compared with blocked practice.", "say": "*Interleaving* the three modes forces them to decide which sound is which, and that decision is what they need in the exam.", "note": "Well supported for discrimination tasks. Its benefit depends on the categories being confusable; interleaving unrelated material does less.", "level": 1}, {"id": "pedagogy-and-learning-science-22", "field": "Pedagogy and learning science", "term": "contextual interference", "gloss": "The motor learning finding, from Shea and Morgan, that high-interference practice schedules impair performance during practice but improve retention and transfer.", "say": "Changing the excerpt every few repetitions increases *contextual interference*; it'll look messier now and hold up better in the audition.", "note": "The motor-learning ancestor of interleaving research; exact in a tertiary performance setting. Rare elsewhere.", "level": 3}, {"id": "pedagogy-and-learning-science-23", "field": "Pedagogy and learning science", "term": "blocked versus random practice", "gloss": "Blocked practice repeats one task many times before moving on; random practice mixes tasks in unpredictable order, generally yielding weaker practice performance but stronger retention.", "say": "Ten times in a row is *blocked practice*; it gives a feeling of mastery the concert will expose.", "note": "Directly useful in instrumental teaching. Novices may need some blocked practice first; don't overstate the case.", "level": 1}, {"id": "pedagogy-and-learning-science-24", "field": "Pedagogy and learning science", "term": "fluency illusion", "gloss": "The mistaken judgement that material is learned because it feels familiar or easy to process, typically produced by rereading or massed repetition.", "say": "Listening to the recording ten times creates a *fluency illusion*; they recognise it, which isn't the same as being able to play it.", "note": "Lands with students and parents as well as staff. Also called the illusion of competence.", "level": 2}, {"id": "pedagogy-and-learning-science-25", "field": "Pedagogy and learning science", "term": "learning versus performance", "gloss": "The distinction between relatively permanent changes in capability, which must be inferred, and momentary performance during acquisition, which can be a poor index of learning.", "say": "What we see in the rehearsal is *performance*; whether they've learnt it, we'll only know after the holidays.", "note": "The foundational distinction behind desirable difficulties. Precise wording matters; the casual use of the words blurs it.", "level": 2}, {"id": "pedagogy-and-learning-science-26", "field": "Pedagogy and learning science", "term": "generation effect", "gloss": "Better retention of information that learners produce themselves than of the same information presented to them.", "say": "Getting them to work out the voicing themselves, even slowly, uses the *generation effect*; handing it out doesn't.", "note": "Robust in laboratory settings. Generation must succeed often enough, so pair it with support.", "level": 2}, {"id": "pedagogy-and-learning-science-27", "field": "Pedagogy and learning science", "term": "elaborative interrogation", "gloss": "A learning strategy of prompting learners to explain why a fact or relationship is true, connecting it to prior knowledge.", "say": "Ask why the bridge modulates to the fourth: that's *elaborative interrogation*, and it fixes the form far better than a diagram.", "note": "Exact term from the Dunlosky review of learning techniques. Sounds clinical; usually better to describe the move.", "level": 3}, {"id": "pedagogy-and-learning-science-28", "field": "Pedagogy and learning science", "term": "dual coding", "gloss": "Paivio's theory that verbal and non-verbal representations are processed in partly separate systems, so complementary words and images can strengthen encoding.", "say": "A form diagram alongside the recording gives them *dual coding*; the same words on the slide as I'm saying doesn't.", "note": "Often misused to justify decorative images. The images must carry complementary information.", "level": 1}, {"id": "pedagogy-and-learning-science-29", "field": "Pedagogy and learning science", "term": "encoding specificity", "gloss": "Tulving's principle that retrieval is most effective when cues present at retrieval match those present at encoding.", "say": "They can play it in the band room and not on stage; *encoding specificity* says practise in conditions closer to the performance.", "note": "High precision; explains context-dependent forgetting. Rare outside cognitive psychology.", "level": 3}, {"id": "pedagogy-and-learning-science-30", "field": "Pedagogy and learning science", "term": "near versus far transfer", "gloss": "Near transfer applies learning to closely similar contexts; far transfer to contexts that differ substantially, which is much harder to achieve and to demonstrate.", "say": "Improvising over rhythm changes in B-flat to F is near transfer; the claim that music improves maths would be *far transfer*, and the evidence there is weak.", "note": "Using it to temper advocacy claims for music earns trust with researchers. Overstating far transfer loses it quickly.", "level": 1}, {"id": "pedagogy-and-learning-science-31", "field": "Pedagogy and learning science", "term": "low-road versus high-road transfer", "gloss": "Salomon and Perkins' distinction between transfer through extensive, varied practice to automaticity and transfer through deliberate, mindful abstraction of a principle.", "say": "Learning tunes in all keys builds *low-road transfer*; analysing why the voice leading works builds high-road.", "note": "Elegant and exact in a tertiary pedagogy setting. Unfamiliar to most; give the gloss.", "level": 3}, {"id": "pedagogy-and-learning-science-32", "field": "Pedagogy and learning science", "term": "inert knowledge", "gloss": "Whitehead's term for knowledge that can be recalled when asked but is not used spontaneously in situations where it is relevant.", "say": "They can name every mode and can't hear one in a solo; that's *inert knowledge*.", "note": "Sharp diagnosis of rote theory teaching. Lands as a challenge to curriculum design, not to students.", "level": 2}, {"id": "pedagogy-and-learning-science-33", "field": "Pedagogy and learning science", "term": "adaptive versus routine expertise", "gloss": "Hatano and Inagaki's distinction between experts who perform familiar tasks efficiently and experts who can also invent new procedures and understand why procedures work.", "say": "The audition panel rewards routine expertise; jazz needs *adaptive expertise*, and our assessment should look for it.", "note": "Highly relevant to performance and improvisation. Rare register; best in a committee designing assessment.", "level": 3}, {"id": "pedagogy-and-learning-science-34", "field": "Pedagogy and learning science", "term": "deliberate practice", "gloss": "Ericsson's term for effortful, teacher-designed practice targeting specific weaknesses with immediate feedback, in a domain with established training methods, as distinct from mere repetition or play.", "say": "Running the tune top to bottom isn't *deliberate practice*; isolating the bridge at a tempo you can't yet manage, with recorded playback, is.", "note": "Its strict definition is often lost in popular use. Its role in explaining expertise is contested; avoid claiming it accounts for all variance.", "level": 1}, {"id": "pedagogy-and-learning-science-35", "field": "Pedagogy and learning science", "term": "purposeful versus naive practice", "gloss": "Ericsson's later distinction: naive practice is repetition, purposeful practice has specific goals and feedback, and deliberate practice adds expert-designed methods and teaching.", "say": "Most home practice is *naive practice*; getting them to purposeful practice is the realistic goal before Year 10.", "note": "Shows you know the definitions are graded. Calling a student's practice naive in a parent meeting will be heard as an insult.", "level": 2}, {"id": "pedagogy-and-learning-science-36", "field": "Pedagogy and learning science", "term": "mental representation", "gloss": "In Ericsson's account, the domain-specific internal model an expert uses to perceive, plan and monitor performance, refined through practice.", "say": "What distinguishes the strongest students is the *mental representation*: they hear what the phrase should be before they play it.", "note": "Close to audiation in music, and the comparison is worth making. Vague if not tied to observable behaviour.", "level": 2}, {"id": "pedagogy-and-learning-science-37", "field": "Pedagogy and learning science", "term": "long-term working memory", "gloss": "Ericsson and Kintsch's proposal that experts use retrieval structures in long-term memory to extend effective working memory within their domain.", "say": "Professional readers aren't superhuman at holding notes; they're using *long-term working memory* built over years of the idiom.", "note": "Exact and rare; persuasive with cognitive scientists. Not the same as simply having a large working memory.", "level": 3}, {"id": "pedagogy-and-learning-science-38", "field": "Pedagogy and learning science", "term": "biologically primary versus secondary knowledge", "gloss": "Geary's distinction between knowledge humans evolved to acquire readily without instruction, such as speech, and culturally recent knowledge, such as notation, that typically requires explicit teaching.", "say": "Singing a tune back may be close to *biologically primary*; reading notation is secondary, and it needs explicit teaching.", "note": "Influential in cognitive load circles. Contested by some evolutionary psychologists, so present it as a framework rather than settled fact.", "level": 3}, {"id": "pedagogy-and-learning-science-39", "field": "Pedagogy and learning science", "term": "formative assessment", "gloss": "Assessment whose evidence is used to adapt teaching and learning to meet learners' needs, defined by the use made of the information rather than by the instrument.", "say": "The same practice exam is *formative* if we change next week's teaching because of it and summative if we just record the mark.", "note": "Defining it by use, not instrument, is the insider move from Black and Wiliam. Calling every quiz formative sounds uninformed.", "level": 1}, {"id": "pedagogy-and-learning-science-40", "field": "Pedagogy and learning science", "term": "assessment as learning", "gloss": "The use of assessment by students themselves to monitor and direct their own learning, distinct from assessment for learning, which informs the teacher's decisions.", "say": "Self-recording and comparing against the reference recording is *assessment as learning*; the student is both assessor and learner.", "note": "Common in Australian curriculum documents. Some researchers regard the three-way split as more rhetorical than conceptual.", "level": 1}, {"id": "pedagogy-and-learning-science-41", "field": "Pedagogy and learning science", "term": "hinge question", "gloss": "Wiliam's term for a diagnostic question at a pivotal point in a lesson, designed so each wrong answer reveals a specific misconception and the class's responses can be read in seconds.", "say": "Before moving to modulation, a *hinge question* on the relative minor tells me whether half the room is lost.", "note": "Exact and well regarded. Its value lies in the diagnostic design of the distractors, not in pausing to ask any question.", "level": 2}, {"id": "pedagogy-and-learning-science-42", "field": "Pedagogy and learning science", "term": "responsive teaching", "gloss": "Teaching that elicits evidence of learning during instruction and adjusts in the moment, the practical core of formative assessment.", "say": "*Responsive teaching* in a big band rehearsal means stopping for the section that actually needs it, not the one that's loudest.", "note": "Wiliam now prefers it to 'assessment for learning', which many heard as more testing. A nice signal of current reading.", "level": 2}, {"id": "pedagogy-and-learning-science-43", "field": "Pedagogy and learning science", "term": "feed up, feed back, feed forward", "gloss": "Hattie and Timperley's three questions effective feedback answers: where am I going, how am I going, and where to next.", "say": "The comments were all *feed back* with no feed forward; the student knew what went wrong and had no idea what to do next.", "note": "Widely known in Australian schools. Fluent use means applying all three, not reciting them.", "level": 1}, {"id": "pedagogy-and-learning-science-44", "field": "Pedagogy and learning science", "term": "the four levels of feedback", "gloss": "Hattie and Timperley's levels at which feedback operates: the task, the processing of the task, self-regulation, and the self as a person, with effectiveness varying by level.", "say": "Most of our written feedback sits at the task level; the *four levels of feedback* would push us towards process and self-regulation.", "note": "Senior and exact. The levels are not a strict hierarchy; task feedback is often what novices need.", "level": 2}, {"id": "pedagogy-and-learning-science-45", "field": "Pedagogy and learning science", "term": "feedback about the self", "gloss": "Praise or criticism directed at the person rather than the task or process, which Hattie and Timperley identify as least effective for learning.", "say": "'You're so talented' is *feedback about the self*; it feels kind and teaches nothing.", "note": "Lands with staff and parents alike. Not a ban on warmth; the point is that warmth isn't the feedback.", "level": 2}, {"id": "pedagogy-and-learning-science-46", "field": "Pedagogy and learning science", "term": "Sadler's three conditions", "gloss": "Royce Sadler's argument that for feedback to improve performance the learner must hold a concept of the standard, compare their current performance with it, and act to close the gap.", "say": "We give marks, but on *Sadler's three conditions* the students don't have the standard; they've never heard an exemplary submission.", "note": "Foundational in assessment scholarship; impresses at SCSA and university level. Name it only if you can state all three.", "level": 3}, {"id": "pedagogy-and-learning-science-47", "field": "Pedagogy and learning science", "term": "Kluger and DeNisi", "gloss": "Shorthand for Kluger and DeNisi's 1996 meta-analysis showing that feedback interventions on average improved performance but reduced it in a substantial share of cases, especially when attention shifted to the self.", "say": "Feedback isn't automatically good; *Kluger and DeNisi* found a sizeable fraction of feedback interventions made performance worse.", "note": "The authoritative counterweight to 'feedback is the biggest effect'. Do not quote a precise fraction unless you have checked it.", "level": 3}, {"id": "pedagogy-and-learning-science-48", "field": "Pedagogy and learning science", "term": "metacognition", "gloss": "Knowledge about and regulation of one's own cognition: planning, monitoring and evaluating one's learning, distinct from cognition itself.", "say": "Teaching *metacognition* here means getting them to plan a practice session and judge afterwards whether it worked.", "note": "Very common and often used vaguely. Specify which component, knowledge or regulation, you mean.", "level": 1}, {"id": "pedagogy-and-learning-science-49", "field": "Pedagogy and learning science", "term": "self-regulated learning", "gloss": "The cyclical process, in Zimmerman's model, by which learners set goals, select strategies, monitor performance and reflect, through forethought, performance and self-reflection phases.", "say": "The jump from Year 10 to ATAR is really a jump in *self-regulated learning*; the content is the easier part.", "note": "Precise and current. Distinguish it from independence; self-regulated learners still seek help strategically.", "level": 2}, {"id": "pedagogy-and-learning-science-50", "field": "Pedagogy and learning science", "term": "calibration", "gloss": "The accuracy of learners' judgements of their own performance or knowledge relative to actual performance.", "say": "The issue isn't confidence, it's *calibration*; she rates every performance a nine, whatever happened.", "note": "Neutral and exact; better than 'overconfident'. Lands in reporting and feedback discussions.", "level": 2}, {"id": "pedagogy-and-learning-science-51", "field": "Pedagogy and learning science", "term": "judgement of learning", "gloss": "A learner's prediction of how well they will later remember or perform something, studied as a measure of metacognitive monitoring.", "say": "Their *judgement of learning* is inflated straight after rehearsal; asked a day later, it's far more accurate.", "note": "Research vocabulary, rare in schools. Useful when explaining why delayed self-assessment is more honest.", "level": 3}, {"id": "pedagogy-and-learning-science-52", "field": "Pedagogy and learning science", "term": "self-efficacy", "gloss": "Bandura's term for belief in one's capacity to succeed at a specific task, distinct from self-esteem, which is a global evaluation of self-worth.", "say": "His self-esteem is fine; his *self-efficacy* for sight-reading is close to nil, and that's what's driving the avoidance.", "note": "The distinction from self-esteem is what lands. Self-efficacy is task-specific; using it globally blurs the concept.", "level": 1}, {"id": "pedagogy-and-learning-science-53", "field": "Pedagogy and learning science", "term": "self-determination theory", "gloss": "Deci and Ryan's theory that motivation is sustained when three basic psychological needs are met: autonomy, competence and relatedness, along a continuum from controlled to autonomous motivation.", "say": "*Self-determination theory* would explain the retention problem: we've stripped out autonomy in Year 9 just when they most need it.", "note": "Robust and widely respected. Autonomy here means volition, not absence of structure.", "level": 2}, {"id": "pedagogy-and-learning-science-54", "field": "Pedagogy and learning science", "term": "expectancy-value theory", "gloss": "Eccles' model in which achievement choices depend on expectations of success and the subjective value of the task, including intrinsic, attainment and utility value and perceived cost.", "say": "On *expectancy-value theory*, the ATAR course loses students on cost: the time it takes against everything else in Year 11.", "note": "Particularly useful about subject choice. 'Cost' as a value component is the point most people miss.", "level": 2}, {"id": "pedagogy-and-learning-science-55", "field": "Pedagogy and learning science", "term": "mastery versus performance goals", "gloss": "In achievement goal theory, mastery goals aim at developing competence, performance goals at demonstrating it relative to others, with performance-avoidance goals the most consistently harmful.", "say": "A ranked seating audition every term pushes students towards *performance goals*, and the anxious ones towards avoidance.", "note": "Senior and relevant to ensemble culture. Note the approach and avoidance split; performance-approach goals are not uniformly harmful.", "level": 2}, {"id": "pedagogy-and-learning-science-56", "field": "Pedagogy and learning science", "term": "attribution theory", "gloss": "Weiner's account of how people explain success and failure along dimensions of locus, stability and controllability, and how those explanations shape subsequent motivation.", "say": "If they put a bad audition down to talent, that's a stable, uncontrollable cause in *attribution theory* terms, and they'll stop practising.", "note": "Precise and older than mindset research, which draws on it. Rare enough to need a gloss.", "level": 3}, {"id": "pedagogy-and-learning-science-57", "field": "Pedagogy and learning science", "term": "overjustification effect", "gloss": "The decline in intrinsic motivation that can follow the introduction of expected, tangible rewards for an activity people already enjoy.", "say": "Paying practice points for minutes logged risks the *overjustification effect*; we could turn a love of playing into a chore.", "note": "Lands in a debate about incentive schemes. The effect depends on reward type and contingency; don't claim all rewards harm motivation.", "level": 3}, {"id": "pedagogy-and-learning-science-58", "field": "Pedagogy and learning science", "term": "growth mindset", "gloss": "Dweck's construct that beliefs about the malleability of ability affect learning behaviour; large-scale replications show small average effects concentrated in specific groups.", "say": "I'd keep *growth mindset* posters off the wall; the replication effects are modest and it's better embedded in how we give feedback.", "note": "Everyone uses it; knowing its evidence status is the distinguishing move. Don't dismiss it outright either; the effects are small, not nil.", "level": 1}, {"id": "pedagogy-and-learning-science-59", "field": "Pedagogy and learning science", "term": "the meshing hypothesis", "gloss": "The claim, tested and unsupported in Pashler and colleagues' 2008 review, that matching instruction to a learner's preferred style improves learning.", "say": "The issue with learning styles is the *meshing hypothesis*; preferences exist, but matching teaching to them doesn't improve outcomes.", "note": "The precise way to dismiss learning styles without overclaiming. Concede that people do have preferences.", "level": 2}, {"id": "pedagogy-and-learning-science-60", "field": "Pedagogy and learning science", "term": "scaffolding", "gloss": "Temporary, responsive support enabling a learner to accomplish what they cannot yet do alone, introduced by Wood, Bruner and Ross and often linked to, though distinct from, Vygotsky's zone of proximal development.", "say": "*Scaffolding* means it comes down; if the bass line is still written out in Term 4, it's a crutch.", "note": "Widely used and widely diluted. Emphasising contingency and withdrawal restores its meaning.", "level": 1}, {"id": "pedagogy-and-learning-science-61", "field": "Pedagogy and learning science", "term": "cognitive apprenticeship", "gloss": "Collins, Brown and Newman's model that makes expert thinking visible through modelling, coaching, scaffolding, articulation, reflection and exploration.", "say": "Thinking aloud while I transcribe is *cognitive apprenticeship*; the craft is in the reasoning they normally can't see.", "note": "Elegant bridge between the studio tradition and learning science. Sounds academic in a parent forum.", "level": 2}, {"id": "pedagogy-and-learning-science-62", "field": "Pedagogy and learning science", "term": "constructive alignment", "gloss": "Biggs' principle that intended learning outcomes, teaching activities and assessment tasks should be aligned, with students constructing meaning through relevant activity.", "say": "If the outcome is improvisation and the assessment is a written test, there's no *constructive alignment*.", "note": "Standard vocabulary in Australian universities; strong at an academic committee. Less known in schools.", "level": 2}, {"id": "pedagogy-and-learning-science-63", "field": "Pedagogy and learning science", "term": "threshold concept", "gloss": "Meyer and Land's term for a concept that is transformative, irreversible, integrative and often troublesome, opening a previously inaccessible way of thinking in a discipline.", "say": "Hearing harmony functionally is a *threshold concept*; before it chords are labels, after it they're directions.", "note": "Popular in higher education. Not every hard idea is a threshold concept; argue the criteria.", "level": 2}, {"id": "pedagogy-and-learning-science-64", "field": "Pedagogy and learning science", "term": "pedagogical content knowledge", "gloss": "Shulman's term for the specialised knowledge of how to teach particular content: the representations, examples and anticipated misconceptions specific to a subject.", "say": "Being a fine player isn't enough; what we need in the tutor pool is *pedagogical content knowledge* about teaching beginners to swing.", "note": "Highly respected and directly relevant to hiring and tutor training. Abbreviated PCK in research circles.", "level": 2}, {"id": "pedagogy-and-learning-science-65", "field": "Pedagogy and learning science", "term": "signature pedagogy", "gloss": "Shulman's term for the characteristic forms of teaching through which a profession prepares its practitioners, such as the clinical round or, in music, the one-to-one studio lesson.", "say": "The one-to-one lesson is the *signature pedagogy* of the conservatoire; that's why it's so hard to question.", "note": "Lands at a university committee. Using it critically, not just descriptively, shows real command.", "level": 3}, {"id": "pedagogy-and-learning-science-66", "field": "Pedagogy and learning science", "term": "legitimate peripheral participation", "gloss": "Lave and Wenger's account of learning as movement from peripheral to full participation in a community of practice.", "say": "Year 7s sitting in on the big band is *legitimate peripheral participation*; they learn the culture before they can play the charts.", "note": "Exact and high-register. The whole phrase is cumbersome; use it once and then say peripheral participation.", "level": 3}, {"id": "pedagogy-and-learning-science-67", "field": "Pedagogy and learning science", "term": "audiation", "gloss": "Edwin Gordon's term for hearing and comprehending music in the mind when the sound is not physically present, distinct from mere aural imagery in that it involves understanding.", "say": "The students who can *audiate* the line before they play it are the ones whose intonation sorts itself out.", "note": "Specific to music learning theory; signals tertiary music pedagogy literacy. Pronounce aw-dee-AY-shun.", "level": 2}, {"id": "pedagogy-and-learning-science-68", "field": "Pedagogy and learning science", "term": "sound before sign", "gloss": "The principle, central to Kodaly-inspired and Gordon traditions, that musical concepts should be experienced aurally before they are named or notated.", "say": "Teaching the dotted rhythm from the page first ignores *sound before sign*; they should sing it a hundred times before they read it.", "note": "A well-established maxim. Its strength is as a sequencing principle, not as a reason to avoid notation.", "level": 1}, {"id": "pedagogy-and-learning-science-69", "field": "Pedagogy and learning science", "term": "enculturation", "gloss": "The largely implicit acquisition of a musical culture's norms through immersion, as distinct from formal training.", "say": "Swing feel is mostly *enculturation*; the students who've listened for years have it before we teach a thing.", "note": "Precise in music cognition and ethnomusicology. Careful with assumptions about which students lack it.", "level": 2}, {"id": "pedagogy-and-learning-science-70", "field": "Pedagogy and learning science", "term": "informal learning", "gloss": "In Lucy Green's research, the practices by which popular musicians learn: choosing their own music, learning by ear from recordings, learning with peers, and integrating listening, playing and composing.", "say": "The Cont ensembles already run on *informal learning* principles in Green's sense; we should evaluate them on those terms.", "note": "Grounded in her How Popular Musicians Learn. Distinguish informal learning from unstructured teaching.", "level": 2}, {"id": "pedagogy-and-learning-science-71", "field": "Pedagogy and learning science", "term": "master-apprentice model", "gloss": "The traditional one-to-one model of instrumental teaching in which a recognised performer transmits craft, repertoire and values to a student, largely through modelling and correction.", "say": "The *master-apprentice model* is powerful and largely unexamined; we should keep the strengths and question the dependence.", "note": "Common in tertiary music research. Critique it carefully; panel members may be its products and practitioners.", "level": 1}, {"id": "pedagogy-and-learning-science-72", "field": "Pedagogy and learning science", "term": "tacit knowledge", "gloss": "Polanyi's term for knowledge that can be enacted but not fully articulated: 'we can know more than we can tell'.", "say": "Time feel is *tacit knowledge*; I can model it and correct it, but I can't write it down in a rubric.", "note": "Exact and respected. Using it as a reason to resist any explicit criteria sounds defensive.", "level": 2}, {"id": "pedagogy-and-learning-science-73", "field": "Pedagogy and learning science", "term": "external focus of attention", "gloss": "Wulf's finding that directing attention to the effect of a movement rather than the body's movements generally improves motor performance and learning.", "say": "Telling her to relax her wrist is an internal focus; '*external focus*' would be 'let the bow ride the string'.", "note": "Strongly supported in motor learning and directly useful in instrumental teaching. Few music educators know the term.", "level": 2}, {"id": "pedagogy-and-learning-science-74", "field": "Pedagogy and learning science", "term": "explicit monitoring", "gloss": "The account of choking under pressure in which anxiety prompts skilled performers to attend consciously to automated components, disrupting their execution.", "say": "In the audition she started thinking about her fingers; that's *explicit monitoring*, and it's why we rehearse under pressure.", "note": "Precise and rare; credible with performance science researchers. Distinguish it from distraction accounts of choking.", "level": 3}, {"id": "pedagogy-and-learning-science-75", "field": "Pedagogy and learning science", "term": "embodied cognition", "gloss": "The view that cognitive processes are grounded in bodily states, action and perception, not only in abstract representation.", "say": "Dalcroze was doing *embodied cognition* a century before it had a name; moving the phrase is understanding the phrase.", "note": "Fashionable, so use it with substance. Lands in tertiary music education, where Dalcroze provides the obvious bridge.", "level": 2}, {"id": "pedagogy-and-learning-science-76", "field": "Pedagogy and learning science", "term": "reflection-in-action", "gloss": "Schon's term for thinking about practice while engaged in it, distinct from reflection-on-action, which happens afterwards.", "say": "Good improvisers are constantly doing *reflection-in-action*; the debrief after the gig is reflection-on-action.", "note": "From The Reflective Practitioner; well known in tertiary teacher education. The distinction is the point.", "level": 2}, {"id": "pedagogy-and-learning-science-77", "field": "Pedagogy and learning science", "term": "artistic research", "gloss": "Research in which artistic practice is itself the method and a primary output, distinct from research about art and from practice merely accompanied by an exegesis.", "say": "What I'm proposing is *artistic research*; the performances are the inquiry, not an illustration of it.", "note": "Established in European and Australian conservatoire research. Contested in its boundaries; know the debate before claiming it.", "level": 3}, {"id": "pedagogy-and-learning-science-78", "field": "Pedagogy and learning science", "term": "overlearning", "gloss": "Continued practice beyond the point of first correct performance, which improves short-term retention and robustness under pressure, though its long-term benefit is smaller than spacing.", "say": "For the audition excerpt, some *overlearning* is right; for general repertoire, spacing will serve them better.", "note": "Precise when you also state its limits. Unqualified, it sounds like an argument for drilling.", "level": 2}, {"id": "pedagogy-and-learning-science-79", "field": "Pedagogy and learning science", "term": "mastery learning", "gloss": "Bloom's model in which learners must demonstrate mastery of a unit before proceeding, supported by formative assessment and corrective instruction, associated with his 'two sigma problem'.", "say": "Grade exams are a crude form of *mastery learning*; nobody moves to Grade 5 without passing Grade 4.", "note": "Respected and current again. Bloom's two sigma tutoring result is often quoted loosely; avoid precise claims about it.", "level": 2}, {"id": "pedagogy-and-learning-science-80", "field": "Pedagogy and learning science", "term": "SOLO taxonomy", "gloss": "Biggs and Collis' Structure of the Observed Learning Outcome: prestructural, unistructural, multistructural, relational and extended abstract levels of understanding.", "say": "The analysis answers are mostly multistructural on the *SOLO taxonomy*; they list features without relating them.", "note": "Useful for writing marking keys and criteria. Less known than Bloom's taxonomy; give the level names.", "level": 2}]}, {"field": "Meeting procedure and chairing", "intro": "Australian meeting practice speaks in the compact, slightly formal idiom of Westminster: motions are moved, seconded, carried or lost, and the chair rules rather than opines. Sounding native means using the exact form of words once, without commentary, and keeping the procedure invisible until it is needed.", "entries": [{"id": "procedure-1", "field": "Meeting procedure and chairing", "term": "standing orders", "gloss": "The written rules that govern how a body conducts its meetings and debates, subordinate to its constitution and unable to override it.", "say": "Before we admit a late item, can I check what our *standing orders* say about notice?", "note": "Lands in any formally constituted body; in a small committee with no written rules, say 'our usual procedure' or it sounds borrowed from Parliament House.", "level": 1}, {"id": "procedure-2", "field": "Meeting procedure and chairing", "term": "substantive motion", "gloss": "The motion that states the actual proposal before the meeting, as distinct from procedural motions about how the meeting will deal with it.", "say": "Let's dispose of the amendment first and then come back to the *substantive motion*.", "note": "Signals that you can see the structure of the debate; use it when the room has lost track of what is actually being decided.", "level": 2}, {"id": "procedure-3", "field": "Meeting procedure and chairing", "term": "procedural motion", "gloss": "A motion about the conduct of the meeting itself, such as to adjourn, defer or put the question, which usually needs no notice and takes precedence over the business in hand.", "say": "That's a *procedural motion*, so I'll deal with it before we go back to the budget.", "note": "Useful for explaining why a motion jumps the queue; do not use the label to brush off a member whose point is really substantive.", "level": 2}, {"id": "procedure-4", "field": "Meeting procedure and chairing", "term": "the question be now put", "gloss": "The closure motion: if carried, debate ends and the immediate question goes straight to a vote, though the chair may decline to accept it where debate has been inadequate.", "say": "Chair, we have heard every view twice; I move that *the question be now put*.", "note": "This is the Westminster form; 'I call the question' is American and reads oddly in an Australian room. Moving it early looks like a gag and costs goodwill.", "level": 2}, {"id": "procedure-5", "field": "Meeting procedure and chairing", "term": "point of order", "gloss": "An interruption alleging a breach of the rules, which the chair must hear and rule on at once; it is not a vehicle for disagreeing with the substance.", "say": "*Point of order*, Chair: the amendment is a direct negative of the motion.", "note": "Name the rule breached in your first sentence. Using it to make a debating point is the fastest way to lose a room's patience.", "level": 1}, {"id": "procedure-6", "field": "Meeting procedure and chairing", "term": "through the chair", "gloss": "The convention that all remarks are addressed to the chair rather than across the table, which depersonalises debate and keeps the chair in control.", "say": "*Through the chair*, could I ask Professor Lee whether the moderation data supports that?", "note": "Most useful when a two-person exchange is heating up; insisting on it in a relaxed working group can sound stiff.", "level": 1}, {"id": "procedure-7", "field": "Meeting procedure and chairing", "term": "second a motion", "gloss": "To indicate that at least one other member wishes the motion to be debated; seconding does not commit the seconder to vote for it.", "say": "I'll *second* it so we can have the discussion, though I may well vote against.", "note": "Saying explicitly that you second without endorsing is a gracious way to let a colleague's idea be heard.", "level": 1}, {"id": "procedure-8", "field": "Meeting procedure and chairing", "term": "lapses for want of a seconder", "gloss": "The fate of a motion that no other member is willing to second: it is not debated, not put and not recorded as lost.", "say": "With no seconder, the motion *lapses for want of a seconder*, and we move to item six.", "note": "Say it flatly and move on; any commentary on the motion at that point looks like the chair taking sides.", "level": 2}, {"id": "procedure-9", "field": "Meeting procedure and chairing", "term": "amendment", "gloss": "A proposal to alter the words of a motion by omitting, inserting or substituting words, which must be relevant to the motion and must not simply negate it.", "say": "I'd like to move an *amendment*: omit 'from Term 1' and insert 'from Term 3, subject to staffing'.", "note": "Always state the exact words to omit and insert. A vague 'can we amend it to be softer' leaves the chair and secretary to draft it for you.", "level": 1}, {"id": "procedure-10", "field": "Meeting procedure and chairing", "term": "direct negative", "gloss": "An amendment whose only effect is to reverse the motion, ruled out of order because members can achieve the same result by voting the motion down.", "say": "Inserting 'not' is a *direct negative*; if you oppose the motion, vote against it.", "note": "A clean, unanswerable ruling; explain the reason in one clause so the mover does not feel stonewalled.", "level": 3}, {"id": "procedure-11", "field": "Meeting procedure and chairing", "term": "foreshadowed amendment", "gloss": "An amendment announced during debate, to be moved once the amendment currently before the meeting is disposed of, reflecting the Australian practice of taking one amendment at a time.", "say": "I'll *foreshadow an amendment* on the reporting date, once we have dealt with this one.", "note": "Lets members vote on the current amendment knowing the alternative; foreshadowing several at once can feel like holding the meeting hostage.", "level": 3}, {"id": "procedure-12", "field": "Meeting procedure and chairing", "term": "the motion as amended", "gloss": "The text that results once an amendment is carried, which becomes the substantive motion, open to further amendment, and is then put in its new form.", "say": "The amendment is carried; I'll now read *the motion as amended* before we vote.", "note": "Always read the amended text aloud before putting it. Votes taken on wording nobody has heard are the commonest source of disputed minutes.", "level": 2}, {"id": "procedure-14", "field": "Meeting procedure and chairing", "term": "notice of motion", "gloss": "Written advance notice that a member intends to move a specified motion, so that others can prepare, required by most rules for significant or constitutional business.", "say": "This changes the ensemble fee structure, so I'll give *notice of motion* for the March meeting.", "note": "Giving notice for contentious business is both correct and disarming; springing it without notice invites a procedural fight you may lose.", "level": 1}, {"id": "procedure-15", "field": "Meeting procedure and chairing", "term": "take on notice", "gloss": "To accept a question that cannot be answered accurately on the spot and commit to a considered answer later, usually in writing or at the next meeting.", "say": "I'll *take that on notice* and circulate the enrolment figures before the next meeting.", "note": "Senate Estimates usage, now standard in Australian committees; it signals precision, but used repeatedly it reads as evasion.", "level": 2}, {"id": "procedure-16", "field": "Meeting procedure and chairing", "term": "rescission motion", "gloss": "A motion to revoke a resolution previously carried, which usually requires notice and sometimes a higher majority to protect decisions from relitigation.", "say": "If members want to reverse last month's decision, the proper course is a *rescission motion* on notice.", "note": "Pointing to it calmly is the best answer to someone reopening a settled decision under 'business arising'.", "level": 2}, {"id": "procedure-17", "field": "Meeting procedure and chairing", "term": "by leave", "gloss": "With the unanimous consent of the meeting, signified by the absence of objection, permitting a departure from the rules; a single objection denies leave.", "say": "*By leave*, I'll withdraw the motion and bring a revised version next month.", "note": "Precise and economical; remember that leave is not a vote, so the chair asks 'is leave granted?' and one 'no' ends it.", "level": 2}, {"id": "procedure-18", "field": "Meeting procedure and chairing", "term": "friendly amendment", "gloss": "An American parliamentary notion with no standing in Westminster practice, where a mover may alter their motion only by leave of the meeting.", "say": "There's no such thing as a *friendly amendment* under our rules, but if the mover agrees we can alter the wording by leave.", "note": "Correcting it is accurate but can sound pedantic; do it once, lightly, and offer the proper mechanism in the same breath.", "level": 3}, {"id": "procedure-19", "field": "Meeting procedure and chairing", "term": "suspension of standing orders", "gloss": "A resolution, often requiring a special majority, that sets aside particular rules for a defined purpose or period so that business can proceed differently.", "say": "I'd move a *suspension of standing orders* so the guest presenters can speak before the formal business.", "note": "Specify which rule and for how long; a blanket suspension invites disorder.", "level": 2}, {"id": "procedure-20", "field": "Meeting procedure and chairing", "term": "quorum", "gloss": "The minimum number of members, fixed by the constitution or rules, that must be present for business to be validly transacted.", "say": "We have eight apologies, so I'll confirm we have a *quorum* before we begin.", "note": "Pronounced KWOR-um. Check it at the start and whenever someone leaves on a decisive item.", "level": 1}, {"id": "procedure-21", "field": "Meeting procedure and chairing", "term": "inquorate", "gloss": "Lacking a quorum, so that the meeting cannot validly transact business and any decisions purportedly made are invalid.", "say": "Two members have left, which makes us *inquorate*; I'll have to adjourn the meeting.", "note": "Saying it before someone else notices protects the decision; carrying on regardless is a classic ground for later challenge.", "level": 2}, {"id": "procedure-22", "field": "Meeting procedure and chairing", "term": "minutes", "gloss": "The formal record of a meeting: attendance, apologies, declarations, the motions put and their outcomes, and actions, as distinct from a transcript of discussion.", "say": "The *minutes* should record the resolution and who is responsible, not a précis of everyone's views.", "note": "The insider point is brevity: minutes that narrate debate create disputes and discoverable material without adding authority.", "level": 1}, {"id": "procedure-23", "field": "Meeting procedure and chairing", "term": "confirmation of the minutes", "gloss": "The motion that the minutes of the previous meeting be confirmed as a true and accurate record; the meeting confirms the accuracy of the record, not the merit of the decisions.", "say": "On *confirmation of the minutes*, the only question is whether they accurately record what we decided.", "note": "Use it to stop debate being reopened under the guise of correcting the record.", "level": 1}, {"id": "procedure-24", "field": "Meeting procedure and chairing", "term": "business arising", "gloss": "The agenda item that follows up matters recorded in the previous minutes, for reporting on actions rather than reopening decisions.", "say": "Under *business arising*, can the treasurer confirm the grant acquittal was lodged?", "note": "A disciplined chair keeps it to progress reports; anything substantive should be its own agenda item.", "level": 1}, {"id": "procedure-25", "field": "Meeting procedure and chairing", "term": "taken as read", "gloss": "The convention that papers circulated before the meeting are deemed to have been read, so they are not presented in full.", "say": "The reports are *taken as read*; I'll invite questions on each in turn.", "note": "Works only if papers went out in time; declaring a paper tabled that morning 'taken as read' breeds resentment.", "level": 1}, {"id": "procedure-26", "field": "Meeting procedure and chairing", "term": "for noting", "gloss": "The classification of an agenda item presented for information only, on which no decision is sought, as distinct from items for decision or for discussion.", "say": "That report is *for noting*; if anyone wants a decision on it, we need a paper next month.", "note": "Insist the classification is accurate; decisions smuggled in under 'for noting' are a known governance failure.", "level": 1}, {"id": "procedure-27", "field": "Meeting procedure and chairing", "term": "en bloc", "gloss": "Taking several items or recommendations together in a single resolution, rather than one by one.", "say": "Unless anyone wants one starred, I'll take items 4.1 to 4.6 *en bloc*.", "note": "Pronounced 'on BLOCK'. Always offer members the chance to pull an item out first.", "level": 2}, {"id": "procedure-28", "field": "Meeting procedure and chairing", "term": "consent agenda", "gloss": "A block of routine items that are adopted together without discussion unless a member asks for an item to be removed for separate consideration.", "say": "We'll put the routine reports on a *consent agenda* and spend the time on the strategic plan.", "note": "Frees serious time for serious business; its integrity depends on any single member being able to release an item without justification.", "level": 2}, {"id": "procedure-29", "field": "Meeting procedure and chairing", "term": "starred item", "gloss": "An item a member has asked to be removed from the consent agenda or block resolution so that it can be discussed and resolved separately.", "say": "I'd like the facilities report to be a *starred item*; I have a question about the risk rating.", "note": "Common in university and public-sector committees; asking to star an item needs no explanation, which is the point.", "level": 3}, {"id": "procedure-31", "field": "Meeting procedure and chairing", "term": "Chatham House Rule", "gloss": "The rule that participants may use the information received but may not reveal the identity or affiliation of the speaker or of any other participant.", "say": "This session is under the *Chatham House Rule*, so the ideas can travel but not the names.", "note": "It is singular, 'the Rule', not 'Rules', and it does not mean total confidentiality; misstating it is a tell.", "level": 2}, {"id": "procedure-32", "field": "Meeting procedure and chairing", "term": "ruling", "gloss": "The chair's binding determination of a question of procedure or order, which stands unless the meeting carries a motion of dissent.", "say": "My *ruling* is that the amendment is in order, and we'll debate it now.", "note": "Give the ruling and a brief reason, then stop; a chair who argues for a ruling invites dissent.", "level": 1}, {"id": "procedure-33", "field": "Meeting procedure and chairing", "term": "dissent from the chair's ruling", "gloss": "A motion, moved immediately after the ruling, that the meeting disagrees with it; if carried, the ruling is overturned, though the motion is about the ruling and not confidence in the chair.", "say": "With respect, Chair, I move *dissent from your ruling*; the item was listed on the agenda.", "note": "A legitimate tool rather than a personal attack; a chair who receives it graciously gains authority.", "level": 2}, {"id": "procedure-34", "field": "Meeting procedure and chairing", "term": "casting vote", "gloss": "An additional vote used to resolve a tie, held by the chair only where the constitution or rules expressly confer it; at common law the chair has no casting vote.", "say": "Our rules give the chair a *casting vote*, but I'd rather we tried to reach agreement first.", "note": "Check the rules before assuming you have one; exercising a casting vote you do not hold invalidates the decision.", "level": 1}, {"id": "procedure-35", "field": "Meeting procedure and chairing", "term": "deliberative vote", "gloss": "The chair's ordinary vote as a member of the body, distinct from any casting vote; some rules give the chair both, some only one.", "say": "I'll exercise my *deliberative vote* like any other member, and it will be recorded with the rest.", "note": "Knowing the difference matters when the chair has both and a tie results.", "level": 2}, {"id": "procedure-36", "field": "Meeting procedure and chairing", "term": "Denison's rule", "gloss": "The convention, associated with Speaker Denison of the House of Commons, that a casting vote is used to preserve the status quo or allow further discussion rather than to decide on the chair's personal view.", "say": "I'll follow *Denison's rule* and vote against change, so the proposal can come back with better data.", "note": "Impressive in a room that knows it; elsewhere say 'the status quo convention' and explain it in a clause.", "level": 3}, {"id": "procedure-37", "field": "Meeting procedure and chairing", "term": "carried", "gloss": "The formal declaration that a motion has received the required majority, its counterpart being 'lost'.", "say": "The ayes have it; the motion is *carried*.", "note": "'Carried' and 'lost' are the correct pair in Australian practice; 'passed' and 'failed' sound imported.", "level": 1}, {"id": "procedure-38", "field": "Meeting procedure and chairing", "term": "nem con", "gloss": "Short for nemine contradicente, meaning that no one voted against, though some may have abstained; distinct from unanimous, which means everyone voted in favour.", "say": "Carried *nem con*, with two abstentions recorded.", "note": "Pronounced NEM-KON. Only use it when it is precisely true; in a casual room 'carried, no votes against' does the same work.", "level": 3}, {"id": "procedure-39", "field": "Meeting procedure and chairing", "term": "majority of votes cast", "gloss": "The standard threshold under most rules, counting only those who vote for or against, so that abstentions do not count against the motion.", "say": "It's a *majority of votes cast*, so abstaining doesn't help the opponents.", "note": "The distinction from 'majority of those present' decides close votes; know which your rules require.", "level": 2}, {"id": "procedure-40", "field": "Meeting procedure and chairing", "term": "absolute majority", "gloss": "A majority of the total membership of the body, regardless of how many are present or vote, used for decisions that should not turn on attendance.", "say": "Changing the fee policy needs an *absolute majority*, so we need seven votes in favour whoever turns up.", "note": "Often confused with a simple majority; getting it wrong is a reliable way to invalidate a decision.", "level": 2}, {"id": "procedure-41", "field": "Meeting procedure and chairing", "term": "special resolution", "gloss": "A resolution requiring notice and a supermajority, commonly three-quarters of votes cast, reserved by legislation or the constitution for fundamental matters such as amending the rules.", "say": "Amending the objects requires a *special resolution* at a general meeting, with proper notice to members.", "note": "Check the exact threshold and notice period in the governing rules and Act rather than relying on memory.", "level": 2}, {"id": "procedure-42", "field": "Meeting procedure and chairing", "term": "division", "gloss": "A formal counted vote in which members' votes are recorded individually, called for when a voice vote or show of hands is doubted.", "say": "I'd ask for a *division*, Chair; it was too close to call on the voices.", "note": "Parliamentary in flavour; in a school council 'can we have a count, and record it?' achieves the same without the theatre.", "level": 3}, {"id": "procedure-43", "field": "Meeting procedure and chairing", "term": "have one's dissent recorded", "gloss": "To ask that the minutes show one voted against a resolution, which preserves one's position without breaching the body's collective decision.", "say": "I accept the decision, but I'd like *my dissent recorded* in the minutes.", "note": "Dignified and final; it matters most where liability or professional judgement is at stake. Overuse makes it look like posturing.", "level": 2}, {"id": "procedure-44", "field": "Meeting procedure and chairing", "term": "adjourn", "gloss": "To suspend a meeting or a debate to a later time, with business resuming where it left off; adjourning the debate is different from adjourning the meeting.", "say": "I'll *adjourn* the debate on this item until we have the legal advice, and we'll continue with the agenda.", "note": "Be explicit about which you mean and when it resumes; an open-ended adjournment is often a quiet burial.", "level": 1}, {"id": "procedure-45", "field": "Meeting procedure and chairing", "term": "sine die", "gloss": "Adjourned without a fixed date for resumption.", "say": "The working party has finished its task, so I'll adjourn it *sine die*.", "note": "Pronounced SY-nee DY-ee or SEE-nay DEE-ay. Fine in a formal minute; spoken in a small meeting it can sound grand.", "level": 3}, {"id": "procedure-46", "field": "Meeting procedure and chairing", "term": "table a paper", "gloss": "In Australian and British usage, to present a document formally to the meeting; in American usage 'to table' means to postpone, the opposite sense.", "say": "I'll *table* the auditor's report now so it is part of the record.", "note": "With international or American colleagues, say 'present' or 'set aside' to avoid a genuine misunderstanding.", "level": 2}, {"id": "procedure-47", "field": "Meeting procedure and chairing", "term": "proceed to the next business", "gloss": "A procedural motion that, if carried, sets aside the question before the meeting without a vote on its merits.", "say": "Rather than defeat it outright, I move that we *proceed to the next business*.", "note": "A courteous way to let an untimely motion fall without a recorded defeat; using it to dodge a vote that should be taken looks evasive.", "level": 3}, {"id": "procedure-48", "field": "Meeting procedure and chairing", "term": "dilatory motion", "gloss": "A motion whose purpose or effect is to delay business, such as repeated motions to adjourn, which the chair may rule out of order when it is an abuse.", "say": "That's the third adjournment motion tonight; I'm ruling it a *dilatory motion* and out of order.", "note": "Use only when the pattern is clear; ruling a genuine motion dilatory looks like suppression.", "level": 3}, {"id": "procedure-49", "field": "Meeting procedure and chairing", "term": "out of order", "gloss": "Contrary to the rules of debate or outside the meeting's competence, as determined by a ruling of the chair.", "say": "That motion is *out of order*, because it deals with a staffing matter outside this council's functions.", "note": "Rule the motion or the conduct out of order, never the person.", "level": 1}, {"id": "procedure-50", "field": "Meeting procedure and chairing", "term": "impute improper motives", "gloss": "To attribute bad faith or dishonourable intent to another member, which standing orders typically prohibit.", "say": "I'll ask you not to *impute improper motives*; argue the proposal, not the proposer.", "note": "A clean way for the chair to cool a personal attack; members can use it on a point of order.", "level": 3}, {"id": "procedure-51", "field": "Meeting procedure and chairing", "term": "withdraw the remark", "gloss": "The chair's direction that a member retract an offensive or unparliamentary remark, in Westminster practice unconditionally.", "say": "I'll ask you to *withdraw the remark*, without qualification, and then we'll continue.", "note": "The withdrawal should be unconditional; a 'withdrawal, but' is a repetition.", "level": 2}, {"id": "procedure-52", "field": "Meeting procedure and chairing", "term": "order of business", "gloss": "The sequence in which the agenda items are taken, set by the rules or the agenda and varied only by the chair with the meeting's agreement.", "say": "With the meeting's agreement, I'll vary the *order of business* so our guests can present first.", "note": "Seek agreement explicitly; a chair who reorders silently can appear to be steering outcomes.", "level": 1}, {"id": "procedure-53", "field": "Meeting procedure and chairing", "term": "general business", "gloss": "Items raised by members that are not otherwise on the agenda, ideally notified to the chair in advance so they can be dealt with properly.", "say": "Please send *general business* items to the secretary by Friday, so nothing arrives as a surprise.", "note": "Prefer it to 'any other business', which invites ambush; matters needing decisions belong on the agenda with papers.", "level": 1}, {"id": "procedure-54", "field": "Meeting procedure and chairing", "term": "late item", "gloss": "An agenda item not circulated with the agenda papers, admitted at the chair's discretion or by leave, usually only for urgency.", "say": "I'll accept it as a *late item* given the grant deadline, but it can only be for noting tonight.", "note": "Admit late items sparingly; a habit of them signals weak secretariat or worse.", "level": 2}, {"id": "procedure-55", "field": "Meeting procedure and chairing", "term": "flying minute", "gloss": "An Australian term for a resolution made out of session by written circulation and signature, valid only where the constitution expressly permits it and on the required level of agreement.", "say": "Given the timing, we'll deal with the venue contract by *flying minute* and record it at the next meeting.", "note": "Check the rules on whether all members must sign; a flying minute that falls short of the required threshold is no decision at all.", "level": 2}, {"id": "procedure-56", "field": "Meeting procedure and chairing", "term": "out of session", "gloss": "Taking place between formal meetings, by circulation or delegated decision, rather than at a properly constituted meeting.", "say": "That was agreed *out of session*, so I'll ask the meeting to note it formally for the record.", "note": "Out-of-session decisions should always be reported to the next meeting; otherwise they become invisible.", "level": 2}, {"id": "procedure-57", "field": "Meeting procedure and chairing", "term": "ratify", "gloss": "To adopt and confirm, after the event, an action taken without prior authority, giving it the body's endorsement where the law and rules permit.", "say": "I'd ask the board to *ratify* the chair's decision to accept the late entry fee waiver.", "note": "Ratification cures want of authority, not a decision the body could never have made in the first place.", "level": 2}, {"id": "procedure-58", "field": "Meeting procedure and chairing", "term": "chair's action", "gloss": "A decision taken by the chair alone between meetings, under an express delegation or for genuine urgency, and reported to the next meeting.", "say": "That was approved by *chair's action* under the delegation, and it's reported for noting at item seven.", "note": "Standard in British-tradition university committees; used beyond its delegation it erodes trust quickly.", "level": 3}, {"id": "procedure-60", "field": "Meeting procedure and chairing", "term": "ex officio", "gloss": "Holding membership by virtue of an office, such as a principal on a school council; ex officio members usually have full rights, including voting, unless the rules say otherwise.", "say": "The principal is a member *ex officio*, and under our rules that includes a vote.", "note": "Pronounced ex o-FISH-ee-oh. The common misconception that ex officio means non-voting is worth correcting gently.", "level": 2}, {"id": "procedure-62", "field": "Meeting procedure and chairing", "term": "committee of the whole", "gloss": "The full body sitting as a committee, with relaxed rules on speaking, to work through detail before resuming formal session to resolve.", "say": "Let's resolve into *committee of the whole* to go through the draft syllabus, and then resume to adopt it.", "note": "Parliamentary; in most associations an informal workshop session achieves the same thing with less ceremony.", "level": 3}, {"id": "procedure-63", "field": "Meeting procedure and chairing", "term": "seriatim", "gloss": "Taking a document clause by clause or point by point, rather than as a whole.", "say": "Given it's a syllabus revision, I suggest we take the assessment outline *seriatim*.", "note": "Pronounced seer-ee-AY-tim. Elegant in a drafting committee; in a general meeting 'clause by clause' is plainer and better.", "level": 3}, {"id": "procedure-64", "field": "Meeting procedure and chairing", "term": "motion versus resolution", "gloss": "A motion is a proposal before the meeting; once carried it becomes a resolution, the body's decision.", "say": "It's no longer a motion; it's a *resolution* of council, and it binds us until rescinded.", "note": "A precise distinction that quietly signals command of procedure, especially in minutes and correspondence.", "level": 2}, {"id": "procedure-66", "field": "Meeting procedure and chairing", "term": "the floor", "gloss": "The right to speak at a given moment, given by the chair; to 'have the floor' is to be the recognised speaker.", "say": "Professor Lee has *the floor*; I'll come to you next.", "note": "A light, effective way to protect a speaker from interruption without scolding the interrupter.", "level": 1}, {"id": "procedure-67", "field": "Meeting procedure and chairing", "term": "secret ballot", "gloss": "A vote in which each member's choice is not disclosed, used for elections and sensitive matters where the rules require or allow it.", "say": "For the office-bearer elections we'll use a *secret ballot*, with the returning officer counting.", "note": "Agree the method before nominations close; changing it mid-process looks like manipulation.", "level": 1}, {"id": "procedure-68", "field": "Meeting procedure and chairing", "term": "exhaustive ballot", "gloss": "An election method in which repeated rounds of voting are held, eliminating the lowest candidate each time, until one candidate has an absolute majority.", "say": "With four candidates, I suggest an *exhaustive ballot* so the winner has majority support.", "note": "Distinct from preferential voting, which achieves a similar result in a single round of ranked ballots.", "level": 3}, {"id": "procedure-69", "field": "Meeting procedure and chairing", "term": "returning officer", "gloss": "The neutral person appointed to conduct an election, receive nominations, count votes and declare the result.", "say": "The secretary will act as *returning officer*, since she isn't standing.", "note": "Choose someone visibly neutral; an interested returning officer taints even a clean result.", "level": 2}, {"id": "procedure-70", "field": "Meeting procedure and chairing", "term": "chair pro tem", "gloss": "A person presiding temporarily, pro tempore, in the absence or withdrawal of the chair.", "say": "The deputy will be *chair pro tem* while I step out for the AJEA item.", "note": "Pronounced pro TEM. Fine in a minute; spoken, 'acting chair' is warmer.", "level": 3}, {"id": "procedure-71", "field": "Meeting procedure and chairing", "term": "vacate the chair", "gloss": "For the chair to step aside from presiding, for example because of a conflict of interest or to take part in debate, with a deputy or elected member presiding.", "say": "Because I have an interest in this item, I'll *vacate the chair* and the deputy will preside.", "note": "Doing it promptly and visibly is one of the clearest signals of integrity a chair can give.", "level": 2}, {"id": "procedure-72", "field": "Meeting procedure and chairing", "term": "guillotine", "gloss": "A timetable fixing the time allowed for an item or stage of debate, after which the question is put automatically.", "say": "I'll put a *guillotine* on this item at eight o'clock and put the question then.", "note": "Parliamentary jargon with a forceful image; in most rooms 'we'll vote at eight' is the same rule without the threat.", "level": 3}, {"id": "procedure-73", "field": "Meeting procedure and chairing", "term": "refer to committee", "gloss": "A motion sending a matter to a committee or working party to investigate and report back, rather than deciding it now.", "say": "I move that we *refer* the proposal to the finance committee to report at the next meeting.", "note": "Specify the question and the report date; referral without either is a polite burial.", "level": 2}, {"id": "procedure-74", "field": "Meeting procedure and chairing", "term": "frame the question", "gloss": "To state precisely, before debate, the decision the meeting is being asked to make and the options available.", "say": "Let me *frame the question*: we are deciding whether to pilot this in 2027, not whether to adopt it.", "note": "The chair's single most powerful act; a well-framed question shortens debate more than any time limit.", "level": 2}, {"id": "procedure-75", "field": "Meeting procedure and chairing", "term": "put the question", "gloss": "To state the motion formally and take the vote on it.", "say": "If there's no further debate, I'll *put the question*: those in favour?", "note": "Read the exact words first; the vote is on the text, not on a paraphrase of it.", "level": 1}, {"id": "procedure-76", "field": "Meeting procedure and chairing", "term": "sense of the meeting", "gloss": "The chair's articulation of where the meeting has arrived, without a formal vote, used in consensus processes and to guide drafting.", "say": "The *sense of the meeting* is that we support the principle but want the costings; is that fair?", "note": "Always test it with the room; a sense of the meeting the chair has invented is resented once noticed.", "level": 2}, {"id": "procedure-77", "field": "Meeting procedure and chairing", "term": "straw poll", "gloss": "An informal, non-binding show of hands to gauge opinion before formal debate or drafting.", "say": "Before we draft a motion, a quick *straw poll*: who could support a two-year pilot?", "note": "Make clear that it binds no one; a straw poll later cited as a decision damages trust.", "level": 1}, {"id": "procedure-78", "field": "Meeting procedure and chairing", "term": "consensus versus unanimity", "gloss": "Consensus means every participant can accept and will not obstruct the decision; unanimity means every participant positively votes for it.", "say": "I'm not asking for *unanimity*; I'm asking whether anyone can't live with this.", "note": "Naming the distinction frees reluctant members to agree without pretending to enthusiasm.", "level": 2}, {"id": "procedure-79", "field": "Meeting procedure and chairing", "term": "stand aside", "gloss": "In formal consensus decision-making, a participant's recorded disagreement that does not prevent the decision, as distinct from a block, which does.", "say": "I'll *stand aside* on this one; I disagree, but I won't block it.", "note": "Natural in community and co-operative settings; in a Westminster-style board it may need a sentence of explanation.", "level": 3}, {"id": "procedure-80", "field": "Meeting procedure and chairing", "term": "the chair is the servant of the meeting", "gloss": "The principle that the chair's authority derives from the meeting and is exercised for it, which is why the meeting may overrule a ruling by dissent.", "say": "*The chair is the servant of the meeting*, so if you'd rather take the items in a different order, say so.", "note": "Disarming when said sincerely and early; said in the middle of a contested ruling it can sound defensive.", "level": 3}]}, {"field": "Board governance and fiduciary duty", "intro": "Governance talk is precise about roles, duties and evidence: who decides, on what information, with what interest, and how the board knows. The native register is calm and exact, naming the duty or distinction plainly and asking for evidence rather than reassurance.", "entries": [{"id": "board-1", "field": "Board governance and fiduciary duty", "term": "governance versus management", "gloss": "Governance is the board's work of setting direction, approving strategy and policy, overseeing performance and holding management to account; management is the executive work of running the organisation within that frame.", "say": "That's a question of *management*, not governance; our role is to ask whether the principal has the resources and the plan.", "note": "The distinction is about the nature of the role, not the topic: a board may discuss rosters if a roster creates a strategic risk.", "level": 1}, {"id": "board-2", "field": "Board governance and fiduciary duty", "term": "noses in, fingers out", "gloss": "The shorthand that directors should probe and understand operations closely but leave execution to management.", "say": "*Noses in, fingers out*: we can ask how the tour is staffed, but we don't pick the chaperones.", "note": "Useful once, said lightly; repeated it becomes a cliché and can sound like a rebuke to the person asking.", "level": 1}, {"id": "board-3", "field": "Board governance and fiduciary duty", "term": "duty of care and diligence", "gloss": "The duty to exercise the degree of care and diligence a reasonable person would exercise in the director's position and circumstances, an objective standard set for companies by s180(1) of the Corporations Act 2001 (Cth).", "say": "Signing off accounts we haven't read wouldn't meet our *duty of care and diligence*.", "note": "The standard is objective and rises with the director's role and expertise; a treasurer is held to more on the financials than a lay member.", "level": 1}, {"id": "board-4", "field": "Board governance and fiduciary duty", "term": "business judgment rule", "gloss": "The safe harbour in s180(2) of the Corporations Act deeming the care-and-diligence duty met for a business judgment made in good faith, for a proper purpose, without material personal interest, on adequate information and in the rational belief it is in the company's best interests.", "say": "The *business judgment rule* protects a considered decision, not a decision we failed to inform ourselves about.", "note": "The statute spells it 'judgment'. It protects only the duty of care, not good faith, conflicts or insolvent trading; overclaiming its reach is a common error.", "level": 2}, {"id": "board-5", "field": "Board governance and fiduciary duty", "term": "good faith and for a proper purpose", "gloss": "The duty, for companies in s181 of the Corporations Act, to exercise powers in good faith in the best interests of the organisation and for the purpose for which the power was conferred.", "say": "Using the fee waiver to reward a donor's child would fail the *proper purpose* test, however generous it looks.", "note": "Proper purpose asks why a power was used, not whether the outcome was sensible; a well-meant use for the wrong purpose still breaches it.", "level": 2}, {"id": "board-6", "field": "Board governance and fiduciary duty", "term": "improper use of position", "gloss": "The prohibition, for companies in ss182 and 183 of the Corporations Act, on directors and officers using their position or information gained through it to gain an advantage or cause detriment.", "say": "Using the board's festival data to pitch a private workshop would be an *improper use of position*.", "note": "Applies even when no loss results; the wrong is in the use, not the harm.", "level": 2}, {"id": "board-7", "field": "Board governance and fiduciary duty", "term": "fiduciary", "gloss": "Describing a relationship of trust in which one party must act in the interests of another; in Australian law fiduciary duties are proscriptive, forbidding conflict and unauthorised profit, rather than imposing positive obligations.", "say": "As directors we're *fiduciaries*, so the question isn't whether it's a good deal for us but whether we're conflicted at all.", "note": "Pronounced fi-DYOO-shee-uh-ree. The proscriptive point is the mark of real understanding and distinguishes it from the statutory duty of care.", "level": 2}, {"id": "board-8", "field": "Board governance and fiduciary duty", "term": "no-conflict and no-profit rules", "gloss": "The two strands of fiduciary obligation: not to place oneself where interest and duty conflict, and not to profit from the position without informed consent.", "say": "Even if the price is fair, the *no-conflict rule* bites the moment a director stands on both sides.", "note": "The insider point is that fairness of the transaction is not a defence; only proper disclosure and authorisation are.", "level": 3}, {"id": "board-12", "field": "Board governance and fiduciary duty", "term": "register of interests", "gloss": "The standing record of each director's relevant interests, offices and relationships, reviewed regularly and tabled with each meeting's conflict declarations.", "say": "Could we have the *register of interests* in each pack, so declarations aren't left to memory?", "note": "Its value is currency; an annual form nobody revisits gives false comfort.", "level": 1}, {"id": "board-13", "field": "Board governance and fiduciary duty", "term": "standing notice", "gloss": "A general disclosure of an ongoing interest that remains effective for future matters, so the director need not re-declare it each time, though it must be updated if the interest changes.", "say": "I've given *standing notice* of my role with the association, and it's in the register.", "note": "Does not excuse a director from withdrawing when the interest becomes live on a specific item.", "level": 3}, {"id": "board-14", "field": "Board governance and fiduciary duty", "term": "recuse", "gloss": "To withdraw from deliberation and voting on a matter because of a conflict, which is more than abstaining, since an abstainer may still shape the discussion.", "say": "I'll *recuse* myself from this item and leave the room, rather than just abstain.", "note": "Pronounced ri-KYOOZ. Leaving the room is cleaner than staying silent at the table; minute both the departure and the return.", "level": 2}, {"id": "board-15", "field": "Board governance and fiduciary duty", "term": "nominee director", "gloss": "A director appointed by, or to represent, a stakeholder group, who nonetheless owes duties to the organisation as a whole rather than to the appointer.", "say": "As a parent representative you're effectively a *nominee director*: you bring the parent view, but your duty is to the school.", "note": "One of the most important things to explain to new council and board members, kindly and early.", "level": 2}, {"id": "board-18", "field": "Board governance and fiduciary duty", "term": "materiality threshold", "gloss": "A defined level of financial or other impact above which matters must be escalated to the board or reported specifically.", "say": "We should set a *materiality threshold* so the treasurer knows what comes to us and what doesn't.", "note": "Thresholds focus board attention but never displace judgement; sub-threshold matters can still be material.", "level": 2}, {"id": "board-19", "field": "Board governance and fiduciary duty", "term": "risk appetite", "gloss": "The amount and type of risk the board is willing to pursue or accept in pursuit of its objectives, distinct from risk tolerance, the acceptable variation around particular measures, and risk capacity, the maximum the organisation can bear.", "say": "Our *risk appetite* for reputational risk with students is low; for artistic risk in programming it should be much higher.", "note": "Most persuasive when expressed by category; a single appetite statement for everything says nothing.", "level": 2}, {"id": "board-20", "field": "Board governance and fiduciary duty", "term": "risk capacity", "gloss": "The maximum level of risk an organisation can absorb before its objectives or survival are threatened, which sets the outer limit for appetite.", "say": "A second festival venue might fit our appetite, but one bad year would exceed our *risk capacity*.", "note": "Distinguishes the willing from the able; useful in small not-for-profits where a single loss can be existential.", "level": 3}, {"id": "board-21", "field": "Board governance and fiduciary duty", "term": "inherent and residual risk", "gloss": "Inherent risk is the level before controls; residual risk is the level remaining after current controls, which is what the board must judge against appetite.", "say": "The *inherent risk* of an overseas tour is high; the question is whether the *residual risk* is within appetite.", "note": "Boards that read only residual ratings never see what happens if a control fails.", "level": 2}, {"id": "board-22", "field": "Board governance and fiduciary duty", "term": "risk register", "gloss": "The structured record of identified risks, their ratings, controls, owners and treatments, reviewed by the board or its committee.", "say": "Child safeguarding should sit at the top of the *risk register*, with named controls and a named owner.", "note": "A register is a tool for discussion, not evidence that risk is managed.", "level": 1}, {"id": "board-23", "field": "Board governance and fiduciary duty", "term": "risk owner", "gloss": "The person accountable for managing a particular risk and reporting on its controls and treatments.", "say": "Who is the *risk owner* for volunteer screening?", "note": "A risk owned by 'the committee' is owned by nobody.", "level": 1}, {"id": "board-24", "field": "Board governance and fiduciary duty", "term": "key risk indicator", "gloss": "A metric that signals a change in the level of a risk, as distinct from a key performance indicator, which measures progress toward objectives.", "say": "Volunteer numbers are a *key risk indicator* for the festival, not just a performance measure.", "note": "Pairing KPIs with KRIs is a mark of sophisticated board reporting.", "level": 2}, {"id": "board-25", "field": "Board governance and fiduciary duty", "term": "Three Lines Model", "gloss": "The Institute of Internal Auditors' framework, revised in 2020 from the 'three lines of defence', distinguishing management's first-line ownership of risk, second-line risk and compliance functions, and third-line independent assurance from internal audit.", "say": "In *Three Lines* terms, the department head's checks are first line; we need something independent at the third.", "note": "Use the current name; 'three lines of defence' marks you as working from the older version.", "level": 2}, {"id": "board-26", "field": "Board governance and fiduciary duty", "term": "assurance", "gloss": "Objective, evidence-based confidence that controls and processes are working, ideally from a source independent of those who run them, as distinct from reassurance, which is simply being told all is well.", "say": "That's *reassurance*, not assurance; what independent evidence do we have that the controls work?", "note": "One of the most incisive questions a director can ask, provided it is asked courteously.", "level": 1}, {"id": "board-27", "field": "Board governance and fiduciary duty", "term": "assurance map", "gloss": "A matrix showing, for each key risk, which sources of assurance cover it and how independent and recent they are, revealing gaps and duplication.", "say": "An *assurance map* would show that nobody independent has looked at the ticketing system.", "note": "Powerful in audit and risk committees; in a small association a short table does the job without the jargon.", "level": 3}, {"id": "board-28", "field": "Board governance and fiduciary duty", "term": "delegations of authority", "gloss": "The documented framework specifying who may make which decisions and commit which resources, within what limits; the board delegates authority but retains accountability.", "say": "Under our *delegations*, the treasurer can approve up to two thousand dollars; above that it comes to the board.", "note": "The insider point is that delegation does not discharge the board's responsibility to oversee the delegate.", "level": 1}, {"id": "board-29", "field": "Board governance and fiduciary duty", "term": "instrument of delegation", "gloss": "The formal document by which authority is delegated, specifying the delegate, the scope, any limits and conditions, and how the delegation can be revoked.", "say": "Before the coordinator signs venue contracts, we need an *instrument of delegation* that says so.", "note": "Public-sector language; in a small association 'written delegation' is enough, so long as it exists.", "level": 3}, {"id": "board-30", "field": "Board governance and fiduciary duty", "term": "delegatus non potest delegare", "gloss": "The principle that one to whom power is delegated may not delegate it further unless the delegation expressly permits it.", "say": "The committee can't hand that decision on to a subcommittee: *delegatus non potest delegare*, unless the rules say otherwise.", "note": "Pronounced del-eh-GAH-tus non POH-test del-eh-GAH-reh. Say 'a delegate can't sub-delegate' in most rooms; the Latin suits a legal audience.", "level": 3}, {"id": "board-31", "field": "Board governance and fiduciary duty", "term": "reserved powers", "gloss": "The matters a board keeps for its own decision and does not delegate, typically including strategy, budget approval, the chief executive's appointment and major commitments.", "say": "Signing a three-year venue lease is one of our *reserved powers*, not something the coordinator can do.", "note": "A clear schedule of reserved matters prevents most turf disputes before they start.", "level": 2}, {"id": "board-32", "field": "Board governance and fiduciary duty", "term": "board charter", "gloss": "The document setting out the board's role, composition, responsibilities, relationship with management and ways of working.", "say": "The *board charter* says the chair speaks for the association publicly, so I'll handle the media inquiry.", "note": "Useful precisely because it is agreed before a dispute; citing it mid-conflict lowers the temperature.", "level": 1}, {"id": "board-33", "field": "Board governance and fiduciary duty", "term": "board calendar", "gloss": "The annual work plan scheduling when the board deals with recurring business such as strategy, budget, audit, risk review, evaluation and the AGM.", "say": "If we put the risk review on the *board calendar* for May, it won't keep slipping behind festival logistics.", "note": "A well-designed calendar is the cheapest governance improvement a small board can make.", "level": 1}, {"id": "board-34", "field": "Board governance and fiduciary duty", "term": "company secretary", "gloss": "The officer responsible for the board's governance processes, papers, minutes and statutory compliance, and in listed-company practice accountable directly to the board, through the chair, on the board's functioning.", "say": "The *company secretary* should own the minutes and the register, not whoever happens to volunteer.", "note": "In an incorporated association the equivalent is usually the secretary under the rules; the function matters more than the title.", "level": 1}, {"id": "board-35", "field": "Board governance and fiduciary duty", "term": "officer", "gloss": "In the Corporations Act sense, a person who makes or participates in decisions affecting the whole or a substantial part of the business, which extends statutory duties beyond formally appointed directors.", "say": "As festival director she may be an *officer* in the legal sense, with duties of her own.", "note": "A reminder that senior executives carry personal duties, not just their boards.", "level": 2}, {"id": "board-36", "field": "Board governance and fiduciary duty", "term": "de facto and shadow directors", "gloss": "A de facto director acts as a director without valid appointment; a shadow director is one whose instructions the appointed directors are accustomed to follow; both can bear directors' duties.", "say": "If the founder still directs every decision from outside, he risks being a *shadow director*.", "note": "A delicate point to raise; make it about protecting the person, not accusing them.", "level": 3}, {"id": "board-37", "field": "Board governance and fiduciary duty", "term": "accountability versus responsibility", "gloss": "Accountability is answerability for outcomes, which rests with one party and cannot be delegated; responsibility is the obligation to carry out tasks, which can be shared or delegated.", "say": "The coordinator is *responsible* for the bookings, but the board remains *accountable* for the festival.", "note": "Use the pair precisely and the room starts to use it with you.", "level": 1}, {"id": "board-38", "field": "Board governance and fiduciary duty", "term": "independence", "gloss": "Freedom from any interest, position or relationship that might influence, or reasonably be perceived to influence, a director's capacity to bring independent judgement.", "say": "For audit and risk, we want at least one member whose *independence* is beyond question.", "note": "Independence is a judgement made by the board about each director, not a status claimed by the director.", "level": 1}, {"id": "board-39", "field": "Board governance and fiduciary duty", "term": "independent non-executive director", "gloss": "A director who is not part of management and is free of relationships that could compromise independent judgement.", "say": "An *independent non-executive director* with finance expertise would strengthen the board considerably.", "note": "Corporate language; for a school council or association, 'an independent member' carries the same idea.", "level": 1}, {"id": "board-40", "field": "Board governance and fiduciary duty", "term": "independence of mind and in appearance", "gloss": "The auditing distinction between actually exercising objective judgement and being seen by a reasonable observer to be free of influence; both are required.", "say": "I'm confident of my *independence of mind*, but *in appearance* I'm too close to the applicant to sit on this panel.", "note": "Particularly useful on arts funding panels; it lets you withdraw without conceding any actual bias.", "level": 3}, {"id": "board-41", "field": "Board governance and fiduciary duty", "term": "information asymmetry", "gloss": "The structural gap between what management knows and what the board knows, which the board must manage through the design of its information flows.", "say": "There's an *information asymmetry* here; we only hear about the program when something has gone wrong.", "note": "Framing it structurally avoids implying anyone is hiding anything.", "level": 3}, {"id": "board-42", "field": "Board governance and fiduciary duty", "term": "the resolution sought", "gloss": "The exact words a board paper asks the board to adopt, stated at the outset so that directors read the paper against a decision.", "say": "Could papers open with *the resolution sought*? Then we'd know what we're deciding before page six.", "note": "One of the most effective reforms a chair can make to board papers.", "level": 2}, {"id": "board-43", "field": "Board governance and fiduciary duty", "term": "exception reporting", "gloss": "Reporting that highlights only departures from plan, policy or tolerance, so board attention goes to what needs it, a form of management by exception.", "say": "Rather than twelve pages of updates, let's move to *exception reporting* against the plan.", "note": "It works only if the thresholds for an exception are agreed and trusted.", "level": 2}, {"id": "board-44", "field": "Board governance and fiduciary duty", "term": "deep dive", "gloss": "A scheduled, focused examination of a single risk, program or function in more depth than routine reporting allows.", "say": "Could we schedule a *deep dive* into volunteer safeguarding for the August meeting?", "note": "Widely used and slightly worn; 'a focused session on' sounds less like consultancy.", "level": 1}, {"id": "board-45", "field": "Board governance and fiduciary duty", "term": "constructive challenge", "gloss": "Rigorous, respectful questioning of management proposals that tests assumptions and evidence while supporting the executive's capacity to deliver.", "say": "I mean this as *constructive challenge*: what would have to be true for these ticket numbers to hold?", "note": "The adjective matters; challenge without visible support reads as distrust.", "level": 2}, {"id": "board-46", "field": "Board governance and fiduciary duty", "term": "stewardship", "gloss": "The board's role as custodian of the organisation's purpose, assets and reputation for those who come after, beyond any current interests.", "say": "Our *stewardship* question is whether the festival will still exist for the students of 2040.", "note": "Lands in mission-driven bodies; in a purely commercial room it can sound sentimental.", "level": 2}, {"id": "board-48", "field": "Board governance and fiduciary duty", "term": "Carver's Policy Governance", "gloss": "John Carver's governance model in which the board defines ends, the outcomes for whom and at what cost, and sets executive limitations, leaving management free to choose means within them.", "say": "In *Carver* terms, we'd define the ends and set executive limitations, and stop approving individual purchases.", "note": "Influential and sometimes applied dogmatically; cite it as a lens, not a creed.", "level": 3}, {"id": "board-49", "field": "Board governance and fiduciary duty", "term": "executive limitations", "gloss": "In Policy Governance, the board's written statements of what management may not do, within which the executive is free to act.", "say": "Rather than approving every contract, we could set *executive limitations* and monitor against them.", "note": "The concept transfers well even to boards that do not adopt the full model.", "level": 3}, {"id": "board-50", "field": "Board governance and fiduciary duty", "term": "the CEO is the board's only employee", "gloss": "The principle that the board directs and holds to account the chief executive alone, who in turn directs staff, so the board does not instruct staff directly.", "say": "*The CEO is the board's only employee*, so concerns about staff go through the director, not to the staff member.", "note": "Clarifying in associations where volunteers and directors overlap; in schools the principal's statutory role adds complications.", "level": 2}, {"id": "board-51", "field": "Board governance and fiduciary duty", "term": "separation of chair and CEO roles", "gloss": "The principle that the person who leads the board should not be the person the board oversees, to preserve the board's independence from management.", "say": "The *separation of chair and CEO roles* matters even in a small association; the person running the festival shouldn't chair its board.", "note": "Uncontroversial in principle, sensitive in practice where a founder holds both roles.", "level": 2}, {"id": "board-52", "field": "Board governance and fiduciary duty", "term": "directors-only session", "gloss": "A scheduled part of a board meeting held without management present, so directors can discuss performance and concerns freely.", "say": "Let's make a *directors-only session* a standing item, so holding one never signals a problem.", "note": "Making it routine is the whole point; called ad hoc it alarms the executive.", "level": 2}, {"id": "board-53", "field": "Board governance and fiduciary duty", "term": "insolvent trading", "gloss": "Incurring debts when an organisation is insolvent or would thereby become so, which directors of companies have a statutory duty to prevent under s588G of the Corporations Act.", "say": "Before we commit to the venue deposit, I want to be sure we're nowhere near *insolvent trading*.", "note": "Solvency is assessed when each debt is incurred; 'we'll sort it out after the festival' is not a defence.", "level": 2}, {"id": "board-55", "field": "Board governance and fiduciary duty", "term": "true and fair view", "gloss": "The standard that financial statements present the organisation's financial position and performance faithfully, beyond technical compliance with accounting standards.", "say": "These may comply with the standards, but do they give a *true and fair view* of the festival's position?", "note": "The phrase carries legal weight; use it when you mean the substance, not as a flourish.", "level": 2}, {"id": "board-56", "field": "Board governance and fiduciary duty", "term": "ASIC v Healey", "gloss": "The 2011 Federal Court decision in the Centro case holding that each director must read, understand and apply their own mind to the financial statements and cannot rely wholly on management and auditors.", "say": "Since *Healey*, no director can say 'the auditors signed off, so I didn't look'.", "note": "Cite it to a board of directors or lawyers; in a school council, state the principle and leave the case name out.", "level": 3}, {"id": "board-57", "field": "Board governance and fiduciary duty", "term": "reliance on others", "gloss": "The statutory position, for companies in s189 of the Corporations Act, that a director may rely on information or advice from competent others if the reliance is in good faith and after an independent assessment.", "say": "We can rely on the auditor's advice, but *reliance* is reasonable only if we've assessed it ourselves.", "note": "The independent-assessment condition is the part most often forgotten.", "level": 3}, {"id": "board-58", "field": "Board governance and fiduciary duty", "term": "independent professional advice", "gloss": "Expert advice obtained by the board, or a director, separately from management, often provided for in the board charter at the organisation's expense.", "say": "Given the stakes, I'd like *independent professional advice* on the sponsorship contract before we sign.", "note": "Asking for it signals diligence, not distrust, if framed as the board's normal practice.", "level": 2}, {"id": "board-59", "field": "Board governance and fiduciary duty", "term": "collective responsibility", "gloss": "The convention that once the board has decided, all directors support and implement the decision publicly, whatever their view in the room.", "say": "I argued against it, but under *collective responsibility* I'll defend the decision to the members.", "note": "Dissent may be minuted, but it stays inside the boardroom; a director who campaigns against a board decision should resign.", "level": 2}, {"id": "board-60", "field": "Board governance and fiduciary duty", "term": "confidentiality of board deliberations", "gloss": "The obligation not to disclose board papers, discussions or individual positions without authority, which protects candour and the board's collective voice.", "say": "Given the *confidentiality of board deliberations*, I can tell staff the decision but not who argued what.", "note": "Applies to parent and staff representatives as much as anyone, and is worth restating at the first meeting each year.", "level": 1}, {"id": "board-61", "field": "Board governance and fiduciary duty", "term": "board evaluation", "gloss": "A structured periodic review of the board's performance, processes and composition, and often of individual directors and the chair.", "say": "A short *board evaluation* each year would tell us whether these meetings are actually adding value.", "note": "An external facilitator every few years makes it honest; self-assessment alone tends toward comfort.", "level": 1}, {"id": "board-62", "field": "Board governance and fiduciary duty", "term": "skills matrix", "gloss": "A table mapping the skills, experience and attributes the board needs against those its current directors bring, used to guide recruitment.", "say": "Our *skills matrix* shows we have plenty of musicians and no one with legal or audit experience.", "note": "Most useful when it drives a specific recruitment decision rather than sitting in the annual report.", "level": 1}, {"id": "board-63", "field": "Board governance and fiduciary duty", "term": "board renewal", "gloss": "The deliberate refreshing of board membership over time through tenure limits, succession planning and recruitment against identified needs.", "say": "*Board renewal* means planning now for the treasurer's role, before I step down.", "note": "Raising your own succession first gives you standing to raise others'.", "level": 1}, {"id": "board-64", "field": "Board governance and fiduciary duty", "term": "audit and risk committee", "gloss": "The board committee that oversees financial reporting, internal control, risk management and the relationship with the external auditor, reporting to the full board.", "say": "The *audit and risk committee* should meet the auditor without management at least once a year.", "note": "The committee advises; the full board remains accountable for the financial statements.", "level": 1}, {"id": "board-66", "field": "Board governance and fiduciary duty", "term": "control environment", "gloss": "The overall attitude, structures and practices governing internal control, including segregation of duties, authorisation and oversight.", "say": "With one person banking and reconciling the takings, our *control environment* is weaker than we'd like.", "note": "In small organisations segregation of duties is the issue that matters most and is most often missing.", "level": 2}, {"id": "board-68", "field": "Board governance and fiduciary duty", "term": "objects clause", "gloss": "The provision of a constitution or set of rules stating the purposes for which the organisation exists, against which its activities and decisions are tested.", "say": "A commercial recording label might be worthwhile, but is it within our *objects clause*?", "note": "Particularly important for charities, whose registration depends on acting within charitable purposes.", "level": 2}, {"id": "board-69", "field": "Board governance and fiduciary duty", "term": "rules of association", "gloss": "The constitution of an incorporated association, which under the Associations Incorporation Act 2015 (WA) is called its rules and binds the association and its members.", "say": "Under our *rules of association*, the treasurer has to present the financial report at the AGM.", "note": "In a WA association, 'rules' is the correct term; 'constitution' is understood but less precise.", "level": 2}, {"id": "board-70", "field": "Board governance and fiduciary duty", "term": "members in general meeting", "gloss": "The members of an organisation acting collectively at a properly convened general meeting, holding the powers the rules reserve to them, such as amending the rules and electing the committee.", "say": "Changing the membership categories is for *members in general meeting*, not for this committee.", "note": "Respecting the line between board powers and member powers avoids most AGM revolts.", "level": 2}, {"id": "board-71", "field": "Board governance and fiduciary duty", "term": "responsible persons", "gloss": "The ACNC term for the directors or committee members who govern a registered charity, whose duties are set out in ACNC Governance Standard 5.", "say": "As *responsible persons* under the ACNC standards, we have duties regardless of our being volunteers.", "note": "A sobering point for volunteer boards; deliver it as protection, not threat.", "level": 2}, {"id": "board-72", "field": "Board governance and fiduciary duty", "term": "deed of access, indemnity and insurance", "gloss": "An agreement giving a director access to board records after leaving office, an indemnity against certain liabilities, and directors and officers insurance cover.", "say": "Before new members join, we should check our D and O cover and whether we offer a *deed of access*.", "note": "Standard in companies, rarely considered in associations; raising it signals experience on other boards.", "level": 3}, {"id": "board-73", "field": "Board governance and fiduciary duty", "term": "escalation protocol", "gloss": "The agreed rules for when and how matters move from management to the chair or full board, including timing and thresholds.", "say": "We need an *escalation protocol*, so a safeguarding incident reaches the chair within hours, not at the next meeting.", "note": "Good protocols are specific about who, how quickly and by what channel.", "level": 2}, {"id": "board-76", "field": "Board governance and fiduciary duty", "term": "put on inquiry", "gloss": "Placed, by information that raises a concern, under an obligation to investigate further rather than accept assurances at face value.", "say": "Once the auditor flagged the cash discrepancy, we were *put on inquiry*; we can't simply wait for next year.", "note": "A legally flavoured phrase that sharpens a board's sense of obligation; use it when the concern is concrete.", "level": 3}, {"id": "board-77", "field": "Board governance and fiduciary duty", "term": "agency problem", "gloss": "The risk that those managing an organisation pursue interests that differ from those of the principals they serve, which governance mechanisms exist to contain.", "say": "Every *agency problem* we have comes back to the same thing: the people running the festival also set their own fees.", "note": "Economic language that suits a university or policy audience; elsewhere name the specific misalignment.", "level": 3}, {"id": "board-78", "field": "Board governance and fiduciary duty", "term": "capture", "gloss": "The condition in which a board ceases to exercise independent judgement because it has come to identify with management or a dominant stakeholder.", "say": "After ten years with the same executive, I worry about board *capture*.", "note": "A serious charge; raise it as a structural risk for the board to manage, never as an accusation.", "level": 3}, {"id": "board-79", "field": "Board governance and fiduciary duty", "term": "groupthink", "gloss": "The tendency of a cohesive group to converge on a view without critical evaluation, in Irving Janis's account, suppressing dissent in favour of consensus.", "say": "We all agreed very quickly; can someone argue the other side so we're sure this isn't *groupthink*?", "note": "Worn from overuse; it lands better as a request for a devil's advocate than as a diagnosis.", "level": 1}, {"id": "board-80", "field": "Board governance and fiduciary duty", "term": "conformance and performance", "gloss": "The two dimensions of board work: conformance, meaning compliance and accountability, and performance, meaning strategy and value creation, which a board must hold in balance.", "say": "We spend nearly every meeting on *conformance*; when do we talk about *performance*?", "note": "A useful frame for rebalancing agendas, especially for compliance-heavy school and charity boards.", "level": 3}]}, {"field": "High register: the precise word", "intro": "This is the register of the senior common room, the bench and the drafting committee, where one exact word replaces a vague phrase and is noticed for its exactness. Native speakers use these words sparingly and correctly, deploy them on documents and arguments rather than on people, and know which of them must be translated back into plain English for a lay room.", "entries": [{"id": "high-register-1", "field": "High register: the precise word", "term": "adumbrate", "gloss": "To outline in a sketchy, provisional way or to foreshadow, as distinct from 'set out', which implies completeness.", "say": "The discussion paper *adumbrates* a senior pathway in contemporary music but stops well short of a course outline.", "note": "Lands in writing or in a committee of academics when you mean precisely 'sketched but not specified'; spoken in a school council it sounds like showing off, so use 'foreshadows' there. Stress on the first syllable: AD-um-brate.", "level": 3}, {"id": "high-register-2", "field": "High register: the precise word", "term": "gainsay", "gloss": "To deny or contradict a claim, usually used in the negative ('no one can gainsay').", "say": "No one at this table would *gainsay* the quality of the ensemble program; the question is what it costs per student.", "note": "Almost always works best in the negative construction; as a positive verb ('I gainsay that') it sounds archaic. Pronounced gain-SAY.", "level": 3}, {"id": "high-register-3", "field": "High register: the precise word", "term": "vitiate", "gloss": "To impair the quality or validity of something, often fatally, as in a flaw that vitiates a finding or a contract.", "say": "A sample that small *vitiates* the comparison with the state mean, however striking the numbers look.", "note": "Exact and powerful in an assessment or research committee; overkill for a minor weakness, where 'weakens' is truer. Pronounced VISH-ee-ate.", "level": 3}, {"id": "high-register-4", "field": "High register: the precise word", "term": "obviate", "gloss": "To remove the need for something by anticipating it, as distinct from 'avoid' or 'prevent' in general.", "say": "If we publish the audition criteria in advance we *obviate* most of the appeals we had last year.", "note": "Means 'make unnecessary', not 'make obvious'; misusing it is a giveaway. Fine in any formal room.", "level": 1}, {"id": "high-register-5", "field": "High register: the precise word", "term": "elide", "gloss": "To omit or pass over something, often so that a distinction is quietly suppressed, as distinct from simply 'leaving out'.", "say": "The draft *elides* the difference between participation and achievement, and the two need separate measures.", "note": "Carries a faint charge that the omission was convenient, so it doubles as a polite criticism; in a parent meeting, say 'skips over'.", "level": 2}, {"id": "high-register-6", "field": "High register: the precise word", "term": "foreground", "gloss": "To give something prominence in an argument or document, deliberately placing it ahead of other considerations.", "say": "I would *foreground* student retention in the submission and leave the facilities argument for the appendix.", "note": "Standard in academic and policy talk and safe as a verb; overuse in one meeting makes it a tic.", "level": 1}, {"id": "high-register-7", "field": "High register: the precise word", "term": "problematise", "gloss": "To treat something taken for granted as open to question and examine its assumptions, as distinct from simply calling it a problem.", "say": "I want to *problematise* the idea that the audition measures potential rather than prior tuition.", "note": "Native among education academics; among principals, board members or public servants it can sound like jargon for 'question', so translate it there.", "level": 2}, {"id": "high-register-8", "field": "High register: the precise word", "term": "conflate", "gloss": "To merge two distinct ideas so they are treated as one, usually in error.", "say": "We are *conflating* the instrumental program's budget with the classroom music budget, and they come from different lines.", "note": "Precise and widely understood; use it on the idea, not the person ('the paper conflates', not 'you conflate'), to keep it collegial.", "level": 1}, {"id": "high-register-9", "field": "High register: the precise word", "term": "reify", "gloss": "To treat an abstraction as if it were a concrete thing with independent existence.", "say": "We should be careful not to *reify* the achievement band; it is a description of a performance, not a property of the child.", "note": "Exact in a philosophy-of-assessment discussion; elsewhere it puzzles people. Pronounced REE-uh-fy or RAY-uh-fy; both are accepted.", "level": 3}, {"id": "high-register-10", "field": "High register: the precise word", "term": "traduce", "gloss": "To speak badly of someone or misrepresent them in a way that damages their reputation.", "say": "I would not want the minutes to *traduce* the previous coordinator, who built the program from nothing.", "note": "A strong, formal word for defamation-lite; useful precisely because it is sharper than 'criticise'. Too heavy for anything short of real unfairness.", "level": 3}, {"id": "high-register-11", "field": "High register: the precise word", "term": "impugn", "gloss": "To call into question the honesty, motives or validity of someone or something.", "say": "I am not *impugning* anyone's motives; I am questioning whether the data supports the conclusion.", "note": "Most useful in the disclaimer form above, which separates the argument from the person; you impugn integrity or a claim, not a person directly.", "level": 2}, {"id": "high-register-12", "field": "High register: the precise word", "term": "cavil", "gloss": "To raise trivial or petty objections; as a noun, such an objection.", "say": "My only *cavil* is with the timeline, which assumes the venue is free in Week 3.", "note": "Elegant when applied to yourself as a modest reservation; applied to someone else's objection it is a put-down. Pronounced KAV-il.", "level": 3}, {"id": "high-register-13", "field": "High register: the precise word", "term": "inveigh against", "gloss": "To protest or speak out strongly and at length against something.", "say": "I could *inveigh against* the scheduling of exams over the festival, but it is more useful to propose an alternative date.", "note": "Always takes 'against'; used self-deprecatingly it signals you know you are on a hobby horse. Pronounced in-VAY.", "level": 3}, {"id": "high-register-14", "field": "High register: the precise word", "term": "militate against", "gloss": "Of a fact or circumstance, to be a powerful factor working against an outcome; distinct from 'mitigate', which means to lessen severity.", "say": "The timing *militates against* a Term 4 launch; the staffing is not in place until January.", "note": "The subject is a circumstance, not a person. Confusing it with 'mitigate' ('mitigate against') is a common and noticed error.", "level": 2}, {"id": "high-register-15", "field": "High register: the precise word", "term": "mitigate", "gloss": "To make something less severe, serious or painful; it takes a direct object and never 'against'.", "say": "We can *mitigate* the risk by having two staff on every bus, but we cannot remove it.", "note": "Say 'mitigate the risk', never 'mitigate against the risk'; the latter is a conflation with 'militate' that careful listeners notice.", "level": 1}, {"id": "high-register-16", "field": "High register: the precise word", "term": "refute", "gloss": "To prove a claim false with evidence or argument; distinct from 'rebut' or 'deny', which only contest it.", "say": "The enrolment figures *refute* the claim that the specialist program draws students away from the local intake.", "note": "Only say 'refute' if you have actually disproved the point; 'I refute that' meaning 'I deny that' is a common misuse that undercuts you in front of academics.", "level": 1}, {"id": "high-register-17", "field": "High register: the precise word", "term": "rebut", "gloss": "To contest or argue against a claim, offering a counter-case, without implying you have disproved it.", "say": "Let me *rebut* that briefly, and the committee can weigh the two positions.", "note": "The modest, accurate word when you are arguing rather than demonstrating; in law a rebuttal answers a case, which is the sense it carries.", "level": 1}, {"id": "high-register-18", "field": "High register: the precise word", "term": "subsume", "gloss": "To include or absorb something within a larger category or structure, so that it loses its separate status.", "say": "If music technology is *subsumed* under Media, it will lose its specialist staffing within two years.", "note": "Precise for organisational and conceptual absorption; not a synonym for 'consume' or 'replace'.", "level": 2}, {"id": "high-register-19", "field": "High register: the precise word", "term": "countenance", "gloss": "As a verb, to tolerate, approve or permit, usually in the negative.", "say": "The council would not *countenance* a fee that excluded students on the basis of family income.", "note": "Strong and formal, best in the negative; signals a firm line without raising the voice.", "level": 2}, {"id": "high-register-20", "field": "High register: the precise word", "term": "equivocate", "gloss": "To use ambiguous language to avoid committing oneself; distinct from 'hedge', which signals uncertainty rather than evasion.", "say": "I will not *equivocate*: the program cannot run at current staffing.", "note": "Powerful when used of yourself to announce directness; used of someone else it is an accusation of evasion.", "level": 2}, {"id": "high-register-21", "field": "High register: the precise word", "term": "temporise", "gloss": "To delay a decision or avoid committing in order to gain time, often while appearing to engage.", "say": "We can *temporise* until the budget is released, but parents will need an answer before re-enrolment.", "note": "Accurate and slightly critical; acknowledging that you are temporising, and why, makes it honest rather than evasive.", "level": 3}, {"id": "high-register-22", "field": "High register: the precise word", "term": "dissemble", "gloss": "To conceal one's real motives or feelings under a false appearance; distinct from 'disassemble'.", "say": "There is no point *dissembling* about the reason for the change: it is the timetable, not the pedagogy.", "note": "A moral word; aimed at a colleague it is an accusation of deceit, so keep it for yourself or for institutions.", "level": 2}, {"id": "high-register-23", "field": "High register: the precise word", "term": "valorise", "gloss": "To assign high value to something, often implicitly and in a way that privileges it over alternatives.", "say": "The current criteria *valorise* notated performance and give almost nothing for improvisation.", "note": "Common in critical theory and arts education writing; outside academic rooms it can sound like jargon, so 'privilege' or 'rewards' may travel better.", "level": 3}, {"id": "high-register-24", "field": "High register: the precise word", "term": "privilege", "gloss": "As a verb, to treat one thing as more important than another, especially in a framework or criteria.", "say": "Any rubric *privileges* something; the question is whether it privileges what we actually teach.", "note": "Native in academic and policy talk; distinct from its legal and social senses, so keep the context clear.", "level": 2}, {"id": "high-register-25", "field": "High register: the precise word", "term": "belie", "gloss": "To give a false impression of something, or to show something to be false; distinct from 'betray', which reveals the truth.", "say": "The modest budget line *belies* how much of the festival depends on volunteer hours.", "note": "Often misused to mean 'reveal'; the core sense is contradiction between appearance and reality.", "level": 2}, {"id": "high-register-26", "field": "High register: the precise word", "term": "arrogate", "gloss": "To claim or take a power, right or responsibility without justification.", "say": "The subcommittee should not *arrogate* to itself a decision the constitution reserves to the full board.", "note": "Exact for overreach of authority; often confused with 'abrogate', which means to repeal or abolish.", "level": 3}, {"id": "high-register-27", "field": "High register: the precise word", "term": "abrogate", "gloss": "To repeal or formally abolish a law, agreement or right; in common use, to fail to discharge a responsibility.", "say": "Delegating it entirely would *abrogate* the board's responsibility for financial oversight.", "note": "The 'abdicate responsibility' sense is widespread but some purists hold to the formal 'repeal' sense; either way, do not confuse it with 'arrogate'.", "level": 3}, {"id": "high-register-28", "field": "High register: the precise word", "term": "interrogate", "gloss": "In academic usage, to examine an idea or assumption critically and systematically.", "say": "We need to *interrogate* what 'excellence' means before it goes into the mission statement.", "note": "Fluent in a university or curriculum room; among parents and principals it sounds like seminar jargon, and overuse anywhere becomes a tell.", "level": 2}, {"id": "high-register-29", "field": "High register: the precise word", "term": "elucidate", "gloss": "To make something clear by explanation, especially something complex or obscure.", "say": "Could the writer *elucidate* how the moderation sample will be drawn?", "note": "Polite and formal as a request; asking a colleague to 'elucidate' in a casual meeting can sound arch.", "level": 1}, {"id": "high-register-30", "field": "High register: the precise word", "term": "prevaricate", "gloss": "To speak or act evasively so as to avoid the truth; distinct from 'procrastinate', which is to delay.", "say": "The department is not refusing; it is *prevaricating*, which is worse because we cannot plan around it.", "note": "Frequently confused with procrastinate; using it precisely signals care. It is an accusation of evasion, so use it of institutions more readily than of people present.", "level": 2}, {"id": "high-register-31", "field": "High register: the precise word", "term": "tendentious", "gloss": "Promoting a particular cause or point of view, especially a controversial one, under the appearance of neutrality.", "say": "The framing of the survey question is *tendentious*; it assumes the program is a burden on the school.", "note": "A measured but pointed criticism of a document or framing; aimed at a person it reads as an accusation of bad faith.", "level": 2}, {"id": "high-register-32", "field": "High register: the precise word", "term": "specious", "gloss": "Superficially plausible but actually wrong; distinct from 'spurious', which means false or not genuine.", "say": "The comparison with interstate programs is *specious*; they are funded on a completely different basis.", "note": "The key is 'looks right but isn't'; using it for something simply wrong loses the nuance. Pronounced SPEE-shus.", "level": 2}, {"id": "high-register-33", "field": "High register: the precise word", "term": "sanguine", "gloss": "Optimistic, especially in a difficult situation; often used in the negative to signal measured doubt.", "say": "I am less *sanguine* than the report about recruitment in regional areas.", "note": "'I am not sanguine' is an elegant way to express worry without alarm; pronounced SANG-gwin.", "level": 1}, {"id": "high-register-34", "field": "High register: the precise word", "term": "perfunctory", "gloss": "Carried out with minimum effort or reflection, as a routine duty.", "say": "A *perfunctory* consultation will not satisfy the parent body; they need to see their submissions answered.", "note": "Precise and widely understood; it is a criticism of manner, not of motive.", "level": 1}, {"id": "high-register-35", "field": "High register: the precise word", "term": "salutary", "gloss": "Producing a beneficial effect, especially through an unwelcome or unpleasant experience; distinct from 'salubrious', which means healthy.", "say": "Losing the grant was a *salutary* lesson in not relying on a single funding source.", "note": "Best for a useful lesson learnt the hard way; not a synonym for 'good'.", "level": 2}, {"id": "high-register-36", "field": "High register: the precise word", "term": "fulsome", "gloss": "Traditionally, excessive or insincere to the point of being distasteful; in modern usage increasingly 'abundant'.", "say": "I would avoid anything too *fulsome* in the vote of thanks; a short, specific acknowledgement will land better.", "note": "'Fulsome praise' was once a criticism; many careful speakers still hear it that way, so 'generous' is safer if you mean praise you approve of.", "level": 2}, {"id": "high-register-37", "field": "High register: the precise word", "term": "enormity", "gloss": "Traditionally, great wickedness or a moral outrage; in common use it also means immense size.", "say": "Only later did the board grasp the *enormity* of the misappropriation.", "note": "Careful listeners still reserve it for wrongdoing; for scale say 'magnitude' or 'scale', or you risk sounding as if a workload were a crime.", "level": 2}, {"id": "high-register-38", "field": "High register: the precise word", "term": "disinterested", "gloss": "Impartial, having no stake in the outcome; distinct from 'uninterested', which means not interested.", "say": "We need a *disinterested* panel member, someone with no student in the ensemble.", "note": "Using it for 'bored' is a common error that a precise room notices; in governance it is exactly the word for an unconflicted person.", "level": 1}, {"id": "high-register-39", "field": "High register: the precise word", "term": "meretricious", "gloss": "Apparently attractive but having no real value; showily superficial.", "say": "The marketing video is *meretricious*: lovely footage, no evidence of learning.", "note": "Strong and literary; it has nothing to do with merit, and its root refers to prostitution, so it is a sharp insult. Use sparingly.", "level": 3}, {"id": "high-register-40", "field": "High register: the precise word", "term": "otiose", "gloss": "Serving no practical purpose; redundant.", "say": "With the new clause in place, the second paragraph is *otiose* and can be struck.", "note": "Excellent in drafting committees; elsewhere 'redundant' is clearer. Pronounced OH-shee-ohss or OH-tee-ohss.", "level": 3}, {"id": "high-register-41", "field": "High register: the precise word", "term": "anodyne", "gloss": "Not likely to cause offence or disagreement and, as a result, somewhat bland.", "say": "The draft mission statement is so *anodyne* that it could belong to any school in the state.", "note": "A neat critique of safe drafting; it can also be a virtue in a press line, so say which you mean.", "level": 2}, {"id": "high-register-42", "field": "High register: the precise word", "term": "apposite", "gloss": "Apt in the circumstances, well suited; distinct from 'opposite'.", "say": "That example is *apposite*: it is exactly the kind of task the new syllabus is designed to reward.", "note": "A gracious way to credit a colleague's contribution; pronounced AP-uh-zit.", "level": 2}, {"id": "high-register-43", "field": "High register: the precise word", "term": "germane", "gloss": "Relevant to the subject under consideration.", "say": "The history of the dispute is *germane* only insofar as it explains the current constitution.", "note": "Useful for steering a discussion politely; 'not germane' is a courteous way to rule a tangent out.", "level": 1}, {"id": "high-register-44", "field": "High register: the precise word", "term": "cogent", "gloss": "Clear, logical and convincing.", "say": "The submission is *cogent*, but it argues for a resource we do not control.", "note": "A respectful acknowledgement before a 'but'; it praises the argument, not necessarily its conclusion.", "level": 1}, {"id": "high-register-45", "field": "High register: the precise word", "term": "moot", "gloss": "In Australian and British usage, open to debate; in American usage, of no practical significance.", "say": "Whether the festival moves to August is a *moot* point; the board has not yet considered it.", "note": "The two senses point in opposite directions, so make the meaning clear from context; in an Australian room the debatable sense is native.", "level": 2}, {"id": "high-register-46", "field": "High register: the precise word", "term": "egregious", "gloss": "Outstandingly bad; shocking.", "say": "The most *egregious* error is the one in the published timetable, because parents have already acted on it.", "note": "Strong but well understood; save it for real failures so it keeps its force.", "level": 1}, {"id": "high-register-47", "field": "High register: the precise word", "term": "invidious", "gloss": "Likely to arouse resentment or unfairness, especially by making unfair distinctions.", "say": "It would be *invidious* to single out one ensemble for praise in the newsletter.", "note": "Good for declining to rank people or groups; distinct from 'insidious', which means harmful in a gradual, hidden way.", "level": 2}, {"id": "high-register-48", "field": "High register: the precise word", "term": "putative", "gloss": "Generally considered or reputed to be; supposed.", "say": "The *putative* savings from merging the ensembles disappear once you count the extra transport.", "note": "Precise for something assumed but not yet established; avoids saying 'alleged', which implies wrongdoing.", "level": 2}, {"id": "high-register-49", "field": "High register: the precise word", "term": "inchoate", "gloss": "Just begun and not fully formed or developed; distinct from 'incoherent'.", "say": "The idea of a regional outreach program is still *inchoate*; I would not put it in the plan yet.", "note": "Often misused to mean 'confused'; the correct sense is 'undeveloped'. Pronounced in-KOH-ate.", "level": 2}, {"id": "high-register-50", "field": "High register: the precise word", "term": "sui generis", "gloss": "Unique; of its own kind and not comparable to others.", "say": "The jazz festival is *sui generis* in the state calendar; there is no comparable event to benchmark against.", "note": "Latin that lands in a legal or academic room; in a parent body say 'unique'. Pronounced SOO-ee JEN-er-is.", "level": 3}, {"id": "high-register-51", "field": "High register: the precise word", "term": "parlous", "gloss": "Full of danger or uncertainty; precarious.", "say": "The association's reserves are in a *parlous* state after two years of reduced sponsorship.", "note": "A dignified old word that suits a treasurer's report; it signals alarm without hysteria.", "level": 3}, {"id": "high-register-52", "field": "High register: the precise word", "term": "peremptory", "gloss": "Insisting on immediate attention or obedience, brusquely; in law, not open to appeal or challenge.", "say": "The email was rather *peremptory*, but the deadline itself is reasonable.", "note": "Useful for naming tone without overreacting; stress on the second syllable: puh-REMP-tuh-ree.", "level": 2}, {"id": "high-register-53", "field": "High register: the precise word", "term": "ineluctable", "gloss": "Unable to be resisted or avoided; inescapable.", "say": "The *ineluctable* conclusion is that the program needs a second full-time position.", "note": "Grand; it lands once in a speech or report and sounds overwrought if repeated.", "level": 3}, {"id": "high-register-54", "field": "High register: the precise word", "term": "facile", "gloss": "Superficially easy and lacking depth, ignoring the true complexities.", "say": "It would be *facile* to blame the decline on the timetable alone.", "note": "A cutting word for an argument; applied to a person it is a serious insult.", "level": 1}, {"id": "high-register-55", "field": "High register: the precise word", "term": "desideratum", "gloss": "Something needed or wanted; a thing regarded as essential (plural desiderata).", "say": "A dedicated rehearsal space is a *desideratum*, not a luxury, if we want the ensembles to grow.", "note": "Lands in a university or funding submission; spoken in a department meeting it can sound mannered.", "level": 3}, {"id": "high-register-56", "field": "High register: the precise word", "term": "sine qua non", "gloss": "An essential condition; something without which the thing cannot exist.", "say": "Instrumental tuition is the *sine qua non* of a specialist music program.", "note": "Latin that most educated listeners know; overusing Latin tags in one meeting looks performative. Pronounced SIN-ay kwah NON or SY-nee kway NON.", "level": 2}, {"id": "high-register-57", "field": "High register: the precise word", "term": "lacuna", "gloss": "A gap or missing part, especially in a text, argument or body of knowledge (plural lacunae).", "say": "The literature review has an obvious *lacuna*: nothing on instrumental programs in public schools.", "note": "Native in academic and research rooms; in plain talk 'gap' is clearer. Pronounced luh-KYOO-nuh.", "level": 2}, {"id": "high-register-58", "field": "High register: the precise word", "term": "aporia", "gloss": "An irresolvable internal contradiction or logical impasse in a text or argument.", "say": "The policy reaches an *aporia*: it requires selection by merit and open access in the same paragraph.", "note": "A term from philosophy and literary theory; very precise in an academic room, affected anywhere else.", "level": 3}, {"id": "high-register-59", "field": "High register: the precise word", "term": "corollary", "gloss": "A proposition that follows directly from one already established.", "say": "The *corollary* of open enrolment is that we must resource beginners as well as advanced students.", "note": "Precise and widely understood; British and Australian stress on the second syllable: kuh-ROL-uh-ree.", "level": 1}, {"id": "high-register-60", "field": "High register: the precise word", "term": "shibboleth", "gloss": "A custom, phrase or belief that distinguishes a group, often one that is outdated or unexamined.", "say": "The idea that only auditioned students benefit from ensembles is a *shibboleth* worth testing.", "note": "Captures 'an unquestioned insider belief'; strongest when you add that it should be examined, not mocked.", "level": 2}, {"id": "high-register-61", "field": "High register: the precise word", "term": "canard", "gloss": "An unfounded rumour or story, often one repeated so often it is believed.", "say": "The claim that music students underperform in other subjects is a *canard*; the school's own data shows the opposite.", "note": "A strong word for a false claim; be sure of your evidence before using it.", "level": 3}, {"id": "high-register-62", "field": "High register: the precise word", "term": "sophistry", "gloss": "The use of clever but fallacious arguments, especially with the intention of deceiving.", "say": "Calling the cut a 'reallocation' is *sophistry*; the program will have less money.", "note": "Implies bad faith; aimed at a person present it ends the conversation, so apply it to the argument.", "level": 2}, {"id": "high-register-64", "field": "High register: the precise word", "term": "imprimatur", "gloss": "Formal or official approval or sanction.", "say": "The proposal will move faster if it carries the principal's *imprimatur* before it reaches council.", "note": "A crisp, weighty word in governance; pronounced im-pri-MAH-tur.", "level": 2}, {"id": "high-register-65", "field": "High register: the precise word", "term": "gravamen", "gloss": "The essence or most serious part of a complaint or accusation.", "say": "The *gravamen* of the parents' complaint is not the result but the lack of written feedback.", "note": "A legal term that is very exact for complaints handling; to a lay room say 'the heart of the complaint'.", "level": 3}, {"id": "high-register-66", "field": "High register: the precise word", "term": "caveat", "gloss": "A warning or proviso of specific stipulations, conditions or limitations.", "say": "I support the motion, with one *caveat*: the trial needs a review date.", "note": "Standard and safe; one caveat sounds judicious, five sound like hedging.", "level": 1}, {"id": "high-register-68", "field": "High register: the precise word", "term": "purview", "gloss": "The scope of the influence or concerns of a person, body or document.", "say": "Uniform policy is outside the *purview* of this committee.", "note": "A clean way to mark boundaries of authority; more formal than 'remit' and less legal than 'jurisdiction'.", "level": 1}, {"id": "high-register-69", "field": "High register: the precise word", "term": "decimate", "gloss": "Strictly, to kill one in ten; in modern usage, to destroy a large proportion of something.", "say": "Losing the Year 10 elective would *decimate* the senior pipeline within three years.", "note": "Some purists object to the modern sense, but it is standard; the real error is using it for total destruction ('annihilate').", "level": 1}, {"id": "high-register-70", "field": "High register: the precise word", "term": "nonplussed", "gloss": "Surprised and confused so much that one is unsure how to react; not 'unbothered' or 'calm'.", "say": "The panel was *nonplussed* by the request; it had not been raised in any earlier correspondence.", "note": "The 'unfazed' sense is a common error, especially in North America; in a precise room the traditional sense is expected.", "level": 2}, {"id": "high-register-71", "field": "High register: the precise word", "term": "comprise", "gloss": "To consist of or be made up of; the whole comprises the parts.", "say": "The board *comprises* seven elected members and two appointees.", "note": "'Is comprised of' is widely used but criticised by careful writers; 'comprises' or 'is composed of' is safe.", "level": 1}, {"id": "high-register-72", "field": "High register: the precise word", "term": "reticent", "gloss": "Not revealing one's thoughts or feelings readily; reserved. Not 'reluctant'.", "say": "The previous treasurer was *reticent* about the details, so the audit took longer than expected.", "note": "Using it to mean 'unwilling' ('reticent to commit') is a common slip; say 'reluctant' for that.", "level": 2}, {"id": "high-register-73", "field": "High register: the precise word", "term": "bemused", "gloss": "Puzzled or bewildered; not 'mildly amused'.", "say": "I am a little *bemused* that the proposal reached the agenda without coming through the department.", "note": "The 'amused' sense is spreading but is still read as an error by careful listeners; the puzzled sense is a polite way to signal concern.", "level": 2}, {"id": "high-register-74", "field": "High register: the precise word", "term": "fortuitous", "gloss": "Happening by chance, rather than by design; not necessarily 'fortunate'.", "say": "The overlap of the two festivals was *fortuitous*, not planned, but we should plan for it next year.", "note": "Traditionally means 'by chance', whether good or bad; using it for 'lucky' is common but not universally accepted.", "level": 1}, {"id": "high-register-75", "field": "High register: the precise word", "term": "restive", "gloss": "Unable to keep still or silent, especially due to boredom or dissatisfaction; restless in a way that resists control.", "say": "The parent body is growing *restive* about the delay in the instrument hire scheme.", "note": "Not a synonym for 'restful'; it signals mounting dissatisfaction, which makes it valuable in governance.", "level": 2}, {"id": "high-register-76", "field": "High register: the precise word", "term": "proscribe", "gloss": "To forbid, especially by law; distinct from 'prescribe', which means to direct or require.", "say": "The policy *proscribes* the use of school instruments for private engagements.", "note": "Confusing the two inverts the meaning; this is one of the errors precise drafters catch immediately.", "level": 2}, {"id": "high-register-77", "field": "High register: the precise word", "term": "tortuous", "gloss": "Full of twists and turns; excessively complex. Distinct from 'torturous', which means causing torture.", "say": "The approval process for the tour was *tortuous*: six forms and three sign-offs.", "note": "Mixing the two is a common error; 'tortuous' is the correct word for a convoluted process.", "level": 2}, {"id": "high-register-78", "field": "High register: the precise word", "term": "credulous", "gloss": "Having too great a readiness to believe things; distinct from 'credible', which means believable.", "say": "We should not be *credulous* about the vendor's claims on durability.", "note": "Using 'credible' for 'credulous' or vice versa is a familiar slip; get them right and you will be heard as precise.", "level": 2}, {"id": "high-register-79", "field": "High register: the precise word", "term": "flout", "gloss": "To openly disregard a rule or convention; distinct from 'flaunt', which means to display ostentatiously.", "say": "The booking agent *flouted* the agreed timetable and moved our slot without notice.", "note": "'Flaunting the rules' is a well-known error; precise speakers notice it.", "level": 1}, {"id": "high-register-80", "field": "High register: the precise word", "term": "simplistic", "gloss": "Treating complex issues as if they were much simpler than they are; not a synonym for 'simple'.", "say": "A single participation figure is *simplistic*; it hides the drop-off between Years 9 and 10.", "note": "It is always a criticism; using it as a fancier 'simple' is a giveaway.", "level": 1}]}, {"field": "Politics, negotiation and diplomacy", "intro": "This is the register of people who move decisions through institutions: understated, procedural and precise about who decides, who must be told and what has actually been agreed. Fluency shows less in vocabulary than in distinctions, such as the office versus the department, off the record versus not for attribution, and a position versus the interest behind it.", "entries": [{"id": "politics-1", "field": "Politics, negotiation and diplomacy", "term": "stakeholder management", "gloss": "The deliberate identification, prioritisation and engagement of those affected by or able to influence a decision; distinct from consultation, which is one tool within it.", "say": "The proposal is sound; what it lacks is *stakeholder management*, especially with the feeder primaries.", "note": "Standard in public sector rooms; among academics it can sound managerial, so 'who needs to be brought along' may travel better.", "level": 1}, {"id": "politics-2", "field": "Politics, negotiation and diplomacy", "term": "coalition", "gloss": "A temporary alliance of parties with distinct interests who combine to achieve a shared aim.", "say": "We need a *coalition* with the Arts and Languages areas if the timetable change is to get through.", "note": "The core idea is that members keep their own interests; you build a coalition around a specific ask, not a friendship.", "level": 1}, {"id": "politics-4", "field": "Politics, negotiation and diplomacy", "term": "BATNA", "gloss": "Best Alternative To a Negotiated Agreement: what you will do if the negotiation fails, which sets your real leverage (from Fisher and Ury, Getting to Yes).", "say": "Our *BATNA* is to run the festival at the university venue; it costs more, but it means we do not have to accept their dates.", "note": "Lands with anyone trained in negotiation; with others, describe it ('our fallback') rather than using the acronym. Pronounced BAT-nuh.", "level": 2}, {"id": "politics-5", "field": "Politics, negotiation and diplomacy", "term": "anchoring", "gloss": "Setting an initial figure or position that frames all subsequent discussion and pulls the outcome towards it.", "say": "They opened at forty per cent of the hall fee, which was *anchoring*; we need to put our own number on the table before we respond.", "note": "Naming the tactic aloud can neutralise it, but usually it is better to re-anchor quietly with your own credible figure.", "level": 2}, {"id": "politics-6", "field": "Politics, negotiation and diplomacy", "term": "the walk-away point", "gloss": "The least favourable outcome you will accept before ending the negotiation; also called the reservation point.", "say": "Our *walk-away point* is a two-year agreement; anything shorter and we lose the sponsorship cycle.", "note": "Decide it before the meeting and keep it private; revealing it invites the other side to settle at it.", "level": 2}, {"id": "politics-7", "field": "Politics, negotiation and diplomacy", "term": "ZOPA", "gloss": "Zone of possible agreement: the range between the parties' walk-away points within which a deal is possible.", "say": "If their maximum is below our minimum, there is no *ZOPA* and we should stop spending time on this.", "note": "A negotiation-course term; useful with trained negotiators, jargon to everyone else. Pronounced ZOH-puh.", "level": 3}, {"id": "politics-8", "field": "Politics, negotiation and diplomacy", "term": "interests versus positions", "gloss": "The distinction between what a party says it wants (its position) and why it wants it (its interest); central to principled negotiation.", "say": "It is a case of *interests versus positions*: their position is that rehearsals finish at five, their interest is the cleaners' roster, and that we can solve.", "note": "The single most useful analytic move in a stalemate; saying 'what is the interest behind that?' signals sophistication without jargon.", "level": 2}, {"id": "politics-9", "field": "Politics, negotiation and diplomacy", "term": "principled negotiation", "gloss": "Negotiation on the merits, focused on interests and objective criteria rather than positional bargaining (Fisher and Ury's method).", "say": "I would rather approach the venue on the basis of *principled negotiation*: show them the attendance data and agree a fee formula.", "note": "Credible with people who know the literature; elsewhere just do it, without naming it.", "level": 2}, {"id": "politics-10", "field": "Politics, negotiation and diplomacy", "term": "integrative bargaining", "gloss": "Negotiation that looks for trade-offs creating joint gains ('expanding the pie'), distinct from distributive bargaining over a fixed sum.", "say": "This is *integrative bargaining*: they want weekday use of our hall, we want weekend use of their stage.", "note": "A technical term; with a lay board, 'a trade that suits both of us' is the same idea.", "level": 3}, {"id": "politics-11", "field": "Politics, negotiation and diplomacy", "term": "distributive bargaining", "gloss": "Negotiation over a fixed resource in which one party's gain is the other's loss.", "say": "The hall fee is pure *distributive bargaining*; there is nothing to trade except the number.", "note": "Useful for diagnosing when to stop looking for creative solutions and simply haggle.", "level": 3}, {"id": "politics-12", "field": "Politics, negotiation and diplomacy", "term": "quid pro quo", "gloss": "An exchange of one thing for another, a favour or advantage given in return for something.", "say": "There is a reasonable *quid pro quo* available: our students perform at their open day, they waive the venue fee.", "note": "Neutral in negotiation but can imply impropriety in public office; avoid it where it could be read as an inducement.", "level": 1}, {"id": "politics-13", "field": "Politics, negotiation and diplomacy", "term": "horse-trading", "gloss": "Hard, shrewd bargaining in which each side concedes something to get something, often informally.", "say": "There will be some *horse-trading* over the timetable, and I am prepared to give up the Friday slot.", "note": "Candid and a little cynical; fine among peers, unwise in a minute or in front of parents.", "level": 1}, {"id": "politics-14", "field": "Politics, negotiation and diplomacy", "term": "logrolling", "gloss": "The trading of support between parties so each gets something passed: I back your measure, you back mine.", "say": "I suspect some *logrolling* between the two faculties; they have voted together on every item today.", "note": "An Americanism from legislative politics; in an Australian room it may need a gloss, and it implies the trade is self-serving.", "level": 3}, {"id": "politics-15", "field": "Politics, negotiation and diplomacy", "term": "sotto voce", "gloss": "Literally 'under the voice': said quietly or privately, often aside, so that it is not part of the formal record.", "say": "The deputy mentioned *sotto voce* that the budget might be revisited in Term 2.", "note": "A musician will pronounce it correctly (SOT-oh VOH-chay); used once it is elegant, but it can sound precious in an email.", "level": 3}, {"id": "politics-16", "field": "Politics, negotiation and diplomacy", "term": "off the record", "gloss": "Information given on the understanding that it will not be quoted or attributed, and often not used at all.", "say": "*Off the record*, the panel was split; on the record, the decision is unanimous.", "note": "Its meaning varies between people, so agree the terms explicitly before saying anything sensitive; never assume it binds a journalist.", "level": 1}, {"id": "politics-17", "field": "Politics, negotiation and diplomacy", "term": "not for attribution", "gloss": "Information that may be used but not attributed to the source by name; distinct from 'off the record', which means not used at all.", "say": "You can use the figure, but it is *not for attribution*; say it came from the sector.", "note": "The distinction from 'off the record' matters and is often missed; spell the terms out.", "level": 2}, {"id": "politics-19", "field": "Politics, negotiation and diplomacy", "term": "the back channel", "gloss": "An informal, unofficial line of communication used alongside formal ones, often to test positions or resolve deadlock.", "say": "The formal letter will go to the Authority, but I would use *the back channel* through the reference group chair first.", "note": "Valuable and normal; overt reliance on it can look like going around formal process, so use it to prepare, not replace, the official channel.", "level": 2}, {"id": "politics-20", "field": "Politics, negotiation and diplomacy", "term": "in principle", "gloss": "Accepted as to the general idea, but not committed as to the detail, resources or timing.", "say": "The principal supports the tour *in principle*, which means we still need to find the money.", "note": "Often a polite delay; always ask what would make it 'in practice'.", "level": 1}, {"id": "politics-21", "field": "Politics, negotiation and diplomacy", "term": "a holding position", "gloss": "A provisional public stance adopted to buy time while a fuller response is prepared, committing to nothing substantive.", "say": "Our *holding position* is that the matter is under review and families will hear by Friday.", "note": "A legitimate tool in a crisis; held too long, it reads as evasion.", "level": 2}, {"id": "politics-22", "field": "Politics, negotiation and diplomacy", "term": "political capital", "gloss": "The trust, goodwill and influence a person has accumulated, which can be spent to win support for a contested decision.", "say": "I am not going to spend *political capital* on the uniform debate; I need it for the staffing case.", "note": "Thinking in these terms is a sign of seniority; saying it too often can sound calculating.", "level": 1}, {"id": "politics-23", "field": "Politics, negotiation and diplomacy", "term": "optics", "gloss": "How an action or decision will appear to the public or an audience, as distinct from its substance.", "say": "The decision is defensible, but the *optics* of cutting the band in the same week as the new sports oval are poor.", "note": "Ubiquitous in politics and increasingly mocked as a buzzword; 'how it will look' is plainer and sometimes stronger.", "level": 1}, {"id": "politics-24", "field": "Politics, negotiation and diplomacy", "term": "the minister's office", "gloss": "The minister's personal political staff, distinct from the department, whose officers are public servants.", "say": "The department supports the idea, but *the minister's office* will decide the timing.", "note": "Knowing the difference between the office and the department is a basic marker of fluency in government; mixing them up reads as naive.", "level": 1}, {"id": "politics-25", "field": "Politics, negotiation and diplomacy", "term": "briefing up", "gloss": "Informing a superior or more senior body of an issue, its implications and a recommended action, so they are not surprised.", "say": "I will be *briefing up* to the principal before the parents' letter goes out.", "note": "The cardinal rule in public institutions is 'no surprises'; briefing up before an issue breaks is the expected practice.", "level": 2}, {"id": "politics-26", "field": "Politics, negotiation and diplomacy", "term": "no surprises", "gloss": "The convention that seniors and ministers must be told of significant issues before they become public or reach them from elsewhere.", "say": "In the spirit of *no surprises*, I wanted you to know the festival will run at a loss this year.", "note": "A deeply held norm in Westminster systems; breaching it costs more trust than the bad news itself.", "level": 1}, {"id": "politics-27", "field": "Politics, negotiation and diplomacy", "term": "a briefing note", "gloss": "A short formal document setting out an issue, background, options and a recommendation for a decision-maker.", "say": "I will put a one-page *briefing note* to the principal with three options and a recommendation.", "note": "Senior readers want the recommendation and the decision required on the first page; background goes last.", "level": 1}, {"id": "politics-28", "field": "Politics, negotiation and diplomacy", "term": "noting brief versus decision brief", "gloss": "The distinction between a brief that informs a decision-maker and one that asks them to make or approve a decision.", "say": "Let us be clear on *noting brief versus decision brief*: this one informs the board, it does not ask it to decide.", "note": "Labelling it explicitly saves meetings and avoids accidental decisions; mislabelling invites scope creep.", "level": 2}, {"id": "politics-29", "field": "Politics, negotiation and diplomacy", "term": "the long game", "gloss": "A strategy focused on long-term outcomes, accepting short-term losses or delays.", "say": "Losing this vote is fine; we are playing *the long game* on the senior syllabus.", "note": "Persuasive when backed by a visible plan; without one, it sounds like a rationalisation.", "level": 1}, {"id": "politics-30", "field": "Politics, negotiation and diplomacy", "term": "face-saving", "gloss": "Allowing a party to concede without humiliation, often by framing or a token concession.", "say": "We should give them a *face-saving* way out: let them announce the change as part of their review.", "note": "Often the cheapest concession in any negotiation; ignoring face is a common cause of deadlock.", "level": 1}, {"id": "politics-31", "field": "Politics, negotiation and diplomacy", "term": "a golden bridge", "gloss": "An easy, dignified route for an opponent to retreat or concede, offered deliberately.", "say": "Let us build them *a golden bridge*: if they accept the August dates, we will credit them in the program.", "note": "An old military-strategy image; it signals magnanimity, so use it without triumph.", "level": 2}, {"id": "politics-32", "field": "Politics, negotiation and diplomacy", "term": "constructive ambiguity", "gloss": "Deliberately ambiguous wording that lets parties with differing positions each accept a text and move forward.", "say": "The phrase 'where practicable' is *constructive ambiguity*; it lets both the Authority and the schools sign off.", "note": "Powerful for unlocking a stalemate; leave too much ambiguity and the dispute returns at implementation.", "level": 3}, {"id": "politics-33", "field": "Politics, negotiation and diplomacy", "term": "nothing is agreed until everything is agreed", "gloss": "The principle that concessions on individual points are provisional until the whole package is settled.", "say": "We can discuss the dates, but *nothing is agreed until everything is agreed*, including the fee.", "note": "A standard protection against having concessions banked one by one; state it at the outset, not mid-way.", "level": 2}, {"id": "politics-35", "field": "Politics, negotiation and diplomacy", "term": "red lines", "gloss": "Non-negotiable positions that a party will not cross.", "say": "Our *red lines* are student safety and the audition criteria; everything else is negotiable.", "note": "Declare few; too many red lines and none of them are credible.", "level": 1}, {"id": "politics-36", "field": "Politics, negotiation and diplomacy", "term": "linkage", "gloss": "Making progress on one issue conditional on progress on another.", "say": "I would avoid *linkage* between the hall fee and the sponsorship; it gives them leverage over both.", "note": "A diplomatic term of art; useful for analysing why talks stall when unrelated issues are tied together.", "level": 3}, {"id": "politics-37", "field": "Politics, negotiation and diplomacy", "term": "the side payment", "gloss": "A concession or benefit given to a party outside the main deal to secure its agreement.", "say": "The *side payment* to the sports department is the use of the bus on Saturdays.", "note": "Analytic language from negotiation theory; in public it can sound like an improper inducement, so keep it to private planning.", "level": 3}, {"id": "politics-38", "field": "Politics, negotiation and diplomacy", "term": "the nibble", "gloss": "A small additional demand made late in a negotiation, after the main deal seems settled.", "say": "Watch for *the nibble*: they will ask for free tickets once the contract is otherwise agreed.", "note": "Recognising it allows you to trade rather than concede ('we can, if you…'); naming it out loud can feel combative.", "level": 2}, {"id": "politics-39", "field": "Politics, negotiation and diplomacy", "term": "kite-flying", "gloss": "Floating an idea publicly or informally to gauge reaction before committing to it; also called a trial balloon.", "say": "The principal's comment at assembly was *kite-flying*; she wants to see how parents react before proposing it formally.", "note": "Doing it yourself is legitimate; being seen to do it too often looks unprincipled.", "level": 2}, {"id": "politics-40", "field": "Politics, negotiation and diplomacy", "term": "socialising the idea", "gloss": "Introducing a proposal informally to key people over time so it is familiar and less threatening when formally put.", "say": "I have been *socialising the idea* of a jazz elective with heads of department since Term 2.", "note": "Effective and normal; done visibly in some rooms and invisibly in others, so judge the culture.", "level": 2}, {"id": "politics-41", "field": "Politics, negotiation and diplomacy", "term": "taking soundings", "gloss": "Informally consulting people to discover their views before a decision or formal proposal.", "say": "Before I put this to council, I will *take soundings* from the parent representatives.", "note": "A courteous, very Westminster phrase; it suggests openness without committing to follow what you hear.", "level": 2}, {"id": "politics-42", "field": "Politics, negotiation and diplomacy", "term": "counting the numbers", "gloss": "Establishing in advance how many votes a proposal will receive.", "say": "Before we move the motion, let us *count the numbers*; I am not confident of the staff representatives.", "note": "A core Australian political idiom; it implies discipline, not manipulation, but never move without doing it.", "level": 1}, {"id": "politics-43", "field": "Politics, negotiation and diplomacy", "term": "the corridor conversation", "gloss": "An informal discussion outside the formal meeting in which positions are tested and deals often made.", "say": "Most of the real progress was in *the corridor conversation* after the committee broke.", "note": "Normal in every institution; be careful not to let it hollow out formal process or exclude people.", "level": 1}, {"id": "politics-44", "field": "Politics, negotiation and diplomacy", "term": "the gatekeeper", "gloss": "A person who controls access to a decision-maker or to resources, formally or informally.", "say": "The executive assistant is the real *gatekeeper*; if she knows the briefing is urgent, it gets read.", "note": "Respect gatekeepers and they become allies; going around them usually costs more than it saves.", "level": 1}, {"id": "politics-45", "field": "Politics, negotiation and diplomacy", "term": "managing up", "gloss": "Actively shaping the relationship with superiors to secure support, resources and good decisions.", "say": "Part of the role is *managing up*: making sure the principal has the information she needs to back us.", "note": "Widely used; it implies competence rather than manipulation, provided it is done transparently.", "level": 1}, {"id": "politics-46", "field": "Politics, negotiation and diplomacy", "term": "the authorising environment", "gloss": "The set of people and bodies whose support confers legitimacy and resources on a public initiative.", "say": "The idea is sound, but the *authorising environment* is not there yet: the Director General's office has not signalled any interest.", "note": "A public-value term familiar to senior public servants; in a school setting, 'who needs to say yes' is plainer.", "level": 2}, {"id": "politics-47", "field": "Politics, negotiation and diplomacy", "term": "machinery of government", "gloss": "The structures, allocation of responsibilities and administrative arrangements of government departments and agencies.", "say": "After the *machinery of government* changes, arts education may sit with a different department.", "note": "Standard in the public service; using it correctly signals you understand how responsibilities can move.", "level": 2}, {"id": "politics-48", "field": "Politics, negotiation and diplomacy", "term": "caretaker conventions", "gloss": "In Westminster systems, conventions limiting major decisions, appointments and contracts by a government in the period before an election.", "say": "Under the *caretaker conventions* the grant cannot be announced until after the election.", "note": "A well-established Australian convention; misjudging the timing can stall a project for months.", "level": 2}, {"id": "politics-49", "field": "Politics, negotiation and diplomacy", "term": "Cabinet-in-confidence", "gloss": "A security classification for documents relating to Cabinet deliberations, which must not be disclosed.", "say": "I cannot share the paper; it is *Cabinet-in-confidence* until the decision is announced.", "note": "Signals respect for process; never treat it lightly, as breach is a serious matter for public officers.", "level": 2}, {"id": "politics-50", "field": "Politics, negotiation and diplomacy", "term": "frank and fearless advice", "gloss": "The public service ideal of giving honest, impartial advice to ministers, regardless of how welcome it is.", "say": "My job on the reference group is to give *frank and fearless advice*, not to tell the Authority what it wants to hear.", "note": "A recognised Westminster phrase; it carries weight because it invokes a professional duty rather than personal opinion.", "level": 2}, {"id": "politics-51", "field": "Politics, negotiation and diplomacy", "term": "the talking points", "gloss": "A set of agreed key messages for use in conversations, interviews or meetings.", "say": "Let us agree *the talking points* before the parent forum so we are all saying the same thing.", "note": "Useful for consistency; reciting them verbatim sounds scripted and undermines trust.", "level": 1}, {"id": "politics-52", "field": "Politics, negotiation and diplomacy", "term": "the wedge", "gloss": "A tactic of raising an issue that divides an opponent's supporters or allies.", "say": "The question about fees is a *wedge*; it splits the parent body from the staff.", "note": "Australian political usage is common ('to wedge'); naming it analytically is fine, doing it to colleagues erodes trust.", "level": 2}, {"id": "politics-53", "field": "Politics, negotiation and diplomacy", "term": "deniability", "gloss": "The ability to deny knowledge of or responsibility for something, often because one was deliberately not told.", "say": "The principal does not want details, for *deniability*; she wants to know it is handled.", "note": "Analytically accurate; in an accountable public institution, cultivating it deliberately can be seen as poor governance.", "level": 2}, {"id": "politics-54", "field": "Politics, negotiation and diplomacy", "term": "political cover", "gloss": "Protection from blame or criticism provided by a supporting decision, endorsement or process.", "say": "A council resolution gives the principal *political cover* to make the change.", "note": "Legitimate and valuable; seeking it is good practice, demanding it from others can look like blame-shifting.", "level": 2}, {"id": "politics-55", "field": "Politics, negotiation and diplomacy", "term": "the quiet word", "gloss": "A discreet private conversation to raise a concern or seek a change, avoiding formal confrontation.", "say": "A *quiet word* with the head of sport will sort this out faster than an email.", "note": "Very Australian and very effective; it works because it gives the other person room to fix the problem without loss of face.", "level": 1}, {"id": "politics-56", "field": "Politics, negotiation and diplomacy", "term": "going over someone's head", "gloss": "Appealing to a person's superior without their involvement or consent.", "say": "I would rather not *go over his head*, but if the booking is not confirmed by Friday I will speak to the deputy.", "note": "Sometimes necessary, always costly; saying in advance that you will do it is more honourable than doing it quietly.", "level": 1}, {"id": "politics-57", "field": "Politics, negotiation and diplomacy", "term": "being across the detail", "gloss": "Having a thorough working knowledge of the specifics of an issue.", "say": "The business manager is *across the detail* of the levy; I would defer to her on the figures.", "note": "An Australian idiom; it is high praise in public service and a reliable way to signal confidence in a colleague.", "level": 1}, {"id": "politics-58", "field": "Politics, negotiation and diplomacy", "term": "the circuit breaker", "gloss": "An action or proposal designed to break a stalemate or change the dynamic of a dispute.", "say": "A joint meeting with both ensembles might be the *circuit breaker* we need.", "note": "Common in Australian politics; it promises momentum, so follow through quickly.", "level": 1}, {"id": "politics-59", "field": "Politics, negotiation and diplomacy", "term": "the carve-out", "gloss": "An exception excluding certain cases from a general rule or agreement.", "say": "We can accept the uniform policy with a *carve-out* for concert dress.", "note": "A precise drafting tool; too many carve-outs hollow out the rule.", "level": 2}, {"id": "politics-60", "field": "Politics, negotiation and diplomacy", "term": "the sunset clause", "gloss": "A provision that causes a rule or arrangement to expire on a set date unless renewed.", "say": "If we include a *sunset clause*, the board can try the new fee without being locked in.", "note": "A reassuring way to secure agreement for an experiment; it forces a later review.", "level": 2}, {"id": "politics-61", "field": "Politics, negotiation and diplomacy", "term": "the stalking horse", "gloss": "A proposal or candidate put forward to test the waters or disguise someone's real intentions.", "say": "The motion to review all electives was a *stalking horse* for cutting music.", "note": "An accusation of covert motive; be sure of your evidence before alleging it publicly.", "level": 2}, {"id": "politics-62", "field": "Politics, negotiation and diplomacy", "term": "a straw man proposal", "gloss": "In drafting and negotiation, a rough first proposal put forward for criticism and improvement; distinct from the 'straw man' fallacy.", "say": "Let me put up a *straw man proposal* for the rehearsal schedule so we have something to shoot at.", "note": "Make the sense clear; in an academic room 'straw man' usually means misrepresenting an opponent's argument.", "level": 2}, {"id": "politics-63", "field": "Politics, negotiation and diplomacy", "term": "holding the pen", "gloss": "Being responsible for drafting the text that others will negotiate or approve.", "say": "If we *hold the pen* on the draft guidelines, our framing will carry through to the final version.", "note": "In diplomacy and committees, the drafter often shapes the outcome; volunteering to hold the pen is a quiet power move.", "level": 2}, {"id": "politics-64", "field": "Politics, negotiation and diplomacy", "term": "square brackets", "gloss": "In negotiated drafts, the marking of text not yet agreed, signalling disputed language.", "say": "The wording on assessment weighting is still in *square brackets*; we need to resolve it before the final meeting.", "note": "A drafting convention in diplomacy and policy; using it signals that you know how texts are actually negotiated.", "level": 2}, {"id": "politics-65", "field": "Politics, negotiation and diplomacy", "term": "ad referendum", "gloss": "Agreed by a negotiator subject to confirmation by their principal or governing body.", "say": "I can agree to the terms *ad referendum*; the board will confirm at its next meeting.", "note": "A diplomatic term of art; useful to accept provisionally without overstepping authority, but obscure outside formal settings. Pronounced ad ref-er-EN-dum.", "level": 3}, {"id": "politics-66", "field": "Politics, negotiation and diplomacy", "term": "ripeness", "gloss": "In conflict resolution, the moment when parties perceive a stalemate as costly enough to make settlement attractive (associated with I. William Zartman).", "say": "I do not think the dispute has reached *ripeness* yet; neither side is feeling the cost of the stalemate.", "note": "Analytic language from conflict studies; useful for advising patience, though it can sound detached to those in the dispute.", "level": 3}, {"id": "politics-67", "field": "Politics, negotiation and diplomacy", "term": "good offices", "gloss": "The services of a neutral party in facilitating communication between disputing parties, short of mediation.", "say": "Perhaps the principal could offer her *good offices* to bring the two committees together.", "note": "A dignified diplomatic phrase; it implies neutrality, so do not use it for someone with a stake.", "level": 3}, {"id": "politics-68", "field": "Politics, negotiation and diplomacy", "term": "shuttle diplomacy", "gloss": "Mediation conducted by an intermediary moving between parties who do not meet directly.", "say": "It turned into *shuttle diplomacy*: I spent the afternoon between the venue manager and the festival committee.", "note": "Wry in a school or association context; it signals the parties are not yet ready to meet.", "level": 2}, {"id": "politics-69", "field": "Politics, negotiation and diplomacy", "term": "track two", "gloss": "Informal, unofficial dialogue between non-governmental or semi-official actors, alongside official negotiations.", "say": "The teacher networks are a kind of *track two* process; they can test ideas the Authority cannot discuss formally.", "note": "Very specialised diplomatic language; apt with policy people, obscure elsewhere.", "level": 3}, {"id": "politics-70", "field": "Politics, negotiation and diplomacy", "term": "the démarche", "gloss": "A formal diplomatic representation or protest made to another government, or more loosely a formal approach.", "say": "The association will make a formal *démarche* to the department about the festival dates.", "note": "Precise in diplomacy; outside it, in a school or board, it sounds grandiose unless used with a smile. Pronounced DAY-marsh.", "level": 3}, {"id": "politics-71", "field": "Politics, negotiation and diplomacy", "term": "the non-paper", "gloss": "An informal document presented without attribution or official status, to test ideas without commitment.", "say": "I have circulated a *non-paper* to the committee: options for discussion, not a proposal.", "note": "A diplomatic tool that translates well to committees; make sure everyone understands it has no formal standing.", "level": 3}, {"id": "politics-72", "field": "Politics, negotiation and diplomacy", "term": "the aide-mémoire", "gloss": "A written summary of points made in a meeting or discussion, given to the other party as a record.", "say": "I will send an *aide-mémoire* after the meeting so we have a shared record of what was agreed.", "note": "Useful and courteous; too formal for everyday matters. Pronounced ayd-mem-WAH.", "level": 3}, {"id": "politics-73", "field": "Politics, negotiation and diplomacy", "term": "the communiqué", "gloss": "An official statement issued at the end of a meeting or negotiation, setting out agreed outcomes.", "say": "The joint *communiqué* from the festival committee should name both schools as co-hosts.", "note": "Its wording is often negotiated as hard as the substance; what is omitted can matter as much as what is included.", "level": 2}, {"id": "politics-74", "field": "Politics, negotiation and diplomacy", "term": "rapprochement", "gloss": "The re-establishment of cordial relations, especially after estrangement.", "say": "There has been a real *rapprochement* between the two ensembles' parent groups this term.", "note": "Elegant and diplomatic; avoid it for minor disagreements, where it sounds inflated. Pronounced ra-PROSH-mon.", "level": 3}, {"id": "politics-75", "field": "Politics, negotiation and diplomacy", "term": "the modus vivendi", "gloss": "A practical arrangement allowing parties in dispute to coexist, without resolving underlying differences.", "say": "We have reached a *modus vivendi* with the drama department over the hall: we alternate weeks.", "note": "Exact for a truce rather than a resolution; it signals realism about lasting differences.", "level": 3}, {"id": "politics-76", "field": "Politics, negotiation and diplomacy", "term": "the fait accompli", "gloss": "Something already decided or done before those affected are consulted, leaving them no option but to accept it.", "say": "The new timetable was presented as a *fait accompli*; there was no real consultation.", "note": "A pointed criticism of process; using it signals you notice when consultation is hollow. Pronounced fet a-KOM-plee.", "level": 2}, {"id": "politics-77", "field": "Politics, negotiation and diplomacy", "term": "salami tactics", "gloss": "Achieving a large change through a series of small steps, each too minor to provoke resistance.", "say": "The reduction in rehearsal time has been *salami tactics*: ten minutes each year.", "note": "A vivid, slightly cynical term; useful for naming a pattern, but it implies deliberate intent.", "level": 3}, {"id": "politics-78", "field": "Politics, negotiation and diplomacy", "term": "the usual channels", "gloss": "In Westminster parliaments, the informal arrangements between party managers or whips; more loosely, established informal routes of communication.", "say": "I will raise it through *the usual channels* before the next council meeting.", "note": "Very British in flavour; in an Australian school context it sounds dry and knowing, which can be useful.", "level": 3}, {"id": "politics-80", "field": "Politics, negotiation and diplomacy", "term": "primus inter pares", "gloss": "First among equals: a leader with formal equality but greater standing or influence.", "say": "As chair I am *primus inter pares*; the decision belongs to the council, not to me.", "note": "Classic description of a chair or prime minister; it sounds modest when applied to yourself, pompous when used of others. Pronounced PREE-mus IN-ter PAR-ays.", "level": 3}]}, {"field": "Academic discourse and stance", "intro": "This is the register of people who argue for a living: claims are calibrated to the evidence, concession is offered before it is extracted, and disagreement is signalled by form rather than heat. Sounding native means choosing the epistemic verb that fits exactly, stating the limit of your claim before anyone else does, and using Latin only where it does work plain English cannot.", "entries": [{"id": "stance-1", "field": "Academic discourse and stance", "term": "on the evidence", "gloss": "Ties a claim to what has actually been shown, conceding in advance that different evidence would change the view; narrower than 'in my opinion' and stronger than 'I think'.", "say": "*On the evidence* we have from the pilot, the listening task discriminates well at the top band and poorly in the middle.", "note": "Lands when you can name the evidence if asked; backfires if the next question is 'which evidence?' and you have none to hand.", "level": 1}, {"id": "stance-2", "field": "Academic discourse and stance", "term": "the literature", "gloss": "The body of published scholarship on a question, treated as a collective position rather than a list of authors.", "say": "*The literature* on ensemble participation and retention is larger than the policy paper suggests, and less unanimous.", "note": "Safe in academic rooms; in a council or parent body it can sound like gatekeeping, so say 'the research' there.", "level": 1}, {"id": "stance-3", "field": "Academic discourse and stance", "term": "contested", "gloss": "Describes a claim or concept on which informed people genuinely disagree, as distinct from one that is merely unpopular or new.", "say": "The notion of 'musical ability' as a fixed trait is *contested*, and the selection criteria should not rest on it.", "note": "Use it of concepts and claims, not of people; calling everything contested sounds evasive.", "level": 1}, {"id": "stance-4", "field": "Academic discourse and stance", "term": "suggests", "gloss": "The weakest of the epistemic verbs: the evidence points in a direction without ruling out alternatives.", "say": "The retention data *suggests* that Year 9 is where we lose students, though the sample is one cohort.", "note": "Precise when the evidence really is partial; using it of something well proven reads as timidity rather than rigour.", "level": 1}, {"id": "stance-5", "field": "Academic discourse and stance", "term": "indicates", "gloss": "Stronger than 'suggests': the evidence points reasonably clearly, though it stops short of proof or a demonstrated mechanism.", "say": "Three years of moderation data *indicates* that the performance marks drift upward in the final term.", "note": "Sits well in a committee paper; pair it with the source so it does not float.", "level": 1}, {"id": "stance-6", "field": "Academic discourse and stance", "term": "I take the point, but", "gloss": "A concession that registers the force of an objection before qualifying or limiting it, rather than rejecting it outright.", "say": "*I take the point, but* the cost of the extra adjudicator is small against the cost of a challenged result.", "note": "Only works if you then show you did take the point; said reflexively it signals that you did not listen.", "level": 1}, {"id": "stance-7", "field": "Academic discourse and stance", "term": "granted", "gloss": "A one-word concession that accepts a premise for the sake of the argument while moving on to what follows from it.", "say": "*Granted*, the timetable is tight. That is an argument for scheduling the sectionals earlier, not for cutting them.", "note": "Elegant at the head of a sentence; repeated, it starts to sound like a debating trick.", "level": 1}, {"id": "stance-8", "field": "Academic discourse and stance", "term": "with respect", "gloss": "In Westminster and legal usage a formal marker that disagreement follows, not a softener; listeners hear it as 'I disagree'.", "say": "*With respect*, Chair, the motion as worded commits the association to a deficit budget.", "note": "Everyone in the room knows what it means, so do not use it unless you are about to disagree; 'with great respect' is stronger still.", "level": 1}, {"id": "stance-9", "field": "Academic discourse and stance", "term": "I'm not persuaded that", "gloss": "Disagreement framed as the current state of the speaker's judgement, which leaves room for the other side to supply more argument.", "say": "*I'm not persuaded that* a single external examiner improves consistency more than a second internal marker would.", "note": "Cooler and more senior than 'I disagree'; it puts the onus back on the proposer without heat.", "level": 1}, {"id": "stance-10", "field": "Academic discourse and stance", "term": "necessary but not sufficient", "gloss": "A condition that must be present for an outcome but does not on its own produce it.", "say": "Instrument hire is *necessary but not sufficient* for access; the families who drop out cite the timetable, not the cost.", "note": "Exact and widely understood; avoid stacking it with other logical terms in one sentence.", "level": 1}, {"id": "stance-13", "field": "Academic discourse and stance", "term": "provisional", "gloss": "Held as the best current view and explicitly open to revision when further evidence arrives.", "say": "Treat this as a *provisional* recommendation until we have the second round of audition data.", "note": "Sounds rigorous rather than weak because it names the condition for revision.", "level": 1}, {"id": "stance-14", "field": "Academic discourse and stance", "term": "the evidence base is thin", "gloss": "A measured way of saying a claim rests on little or weak evidence, without saying it is false.", "say": "*The evidence base is thin* for that claim; it comes from one small study of private-studio students.", "note": "Disarming in a policy room because it criticises the support, not the person advancing the view.", "level": 1}, {"id": "stance-15", "field": "Academic discourse and stance", "term": "it doesn't follow that", "gloss": "Identifies a gap between a premise and a conclusion while accepting the premise itself.", "say": "The ensemble is oversubscribed. *It doesn't follow that* we should audition harder; we might simply run a second ensemble.", "note": "Clean and unanswerable when the gap is real; then stop, and let the room see it.", "level": 1}, {"id": "stance-16", "field": "Academic discourse and stance", "term": "an empirical question", "gloss": "A question that can only be settled by looking at data, as opposed to one settled by argument, definition or values.", "say": "Whether the change reduces drop-out is *an empirical question*; let's agree what we'd measure before we argue about it.", "note": "Useful for ending a circular debate; misused when the real disagreement is about values.", "level": 1}, {"id": "stance-17", "field": "Academic discourse and stance", "term": "in principle versus in practice", "gloss": "Separates whether something is coherent or permissible from whether it is workable with the actual resources and people.", "say": "I support the model *in principle*; *in practice* it needs a second teacher on Wednesdays we do not have.", "note": "Lets you agree and object in one breath; keep the 'in practice' half concrete.", "level": 1}, {"id": "stance-18", "field": "Academic discourse and stance", "term": "a straw man", "gloss": "A weaker version of an opponent's position, set up to be knocked down in place of the real one.", "say": "That is a bit of *a straw man*: nobody is proposing to abolish the audition, only to change its weighting.", "note": "Soften with 'a bit of'; said flatly it can sound like an accusation of dishonesty.", "level": 1}, {"id": "stance-19", "field": "Academic discourse and stance", "term": "I wonder whether", "gloss": "Frames a contribution as a question, which invites the room to test it rather than defend against it.", "say": "*I wonder whether* the problem is the rubric or the training of the people applying it.", "note": "Senior and disarming when the point is sharp; on a trivial point it can sound coy.", "level": 1}, {"id": "stance-20", "field": "Academic discourse and stance", "term": "a working hypothesis", "gloss": "A claim adopted to guide action or inquiry while it is being tested, not asserted as true.", "say": "My *working hypothesis* is that the Year 8 drop is about the timetable clash with sport.", "note": "Signals rigour and openness together; follow it with what would test it.", "level": 1}, {"id": "stance-21", "field": "Academic discourse and stance", "term": "the evidence is mixed", "gloss": "Studies or data point in different directions, so no confident general claim is yet warranted.", "say": "*The evidence is mixed* on whether early specialisation helps; it seems to depend heavily on the instrument.", "note": "Honest and respected; avoid using it to dodge a view you actually hold.", "level": 1}, {"id": "stance-22", "field": "Academic discourse and stance", "term": "arguably", "gloss": "Marks a claim as defensible but open to dispute; the speaker is prepared to argue for it.", "say": "The festival is *arguably* the most important professional-development event for jazz teachers in the state.", "note": "One per paragraph at most; stacked, it becomes the hedging tic you are trying to drop.", "level": 1}, {"id": "stance-23", "field": "Academic discourse and stance", "term": "on my reading", "gloss": "Offers an interpretation of a text, policy or syllabus as one reading among possible others, owned by the speaker.", "say": "*On my reading* of the syllabus, improvisation is assessable in Unit 3 but not required.", "note": "Better than 'I think it says' because it implies you have read it closely; be ready to cite the clause.", "level": 1}, {"id": "stance-24", "field": "Academic discourse and stance", "term": "implies versus infers", "gloss": "The speaker or text implies; the listener infers. Swapping them is a marker educated listeners notice.", "say": "The report doesn't say the program is failing; I *infer* that from the numbers, and I accept the report *implies* the opposite.", "note": "Get this right reliably and nobody comments; get it wrong once in a university committee and someone will.", "level": 1}, {"id": "stance-27", "field": "Academic discourse and stance", "term": "under-theorised", "gloss": "Lacking an adequate conceptual account, so practice or policy rests on assumptions nobody has examined.", "say": "Ensemble pedagogy in the senior syllabus is badly *under-theorised*; we assess the product and say almost nothing about the learning.", "note": "A legitimate academic criticism; use it of a field or document, not of a colleague's work in front of them.", "level": 2}, {"id": "stance-28", "field": "Academic discourse and stance", "term": "a category error", "gloss": "Treating something as belonging to a kind it does not belong to, so a question asked of it makes no sense.", "say": "Asking for a single 'correct' interpretation of the solo is *a category error*; improvisation is not marked against an answer key.", "note": "Devastating when exact; used loosely for any mistake, it sounds pretentious.", "level": 2}, {"id": "stance-29", "field": "Academic discourse and stance", "term": "establishes", "gloss": "Shows a claim to be settled to an accepted standard; stronger than 'indicates' and weaker in implication than 'proves'.", "say": "The audit *establishes* that the hire fleet is under-insured; it doesn't establish why.", "note": "Reserve it for findings that would survive scrutiny; overclaiming here costs credibility for the rest of the meeting.", "level": 2}, {"id": "stance-30", "field": "Academic discourse and stance", "term": "demonstrates", "gloss": "Shows by evidence or argument that something is the case, typically with the mechanism visible; the strongest of the common epistemic verbs.", "say": "The recording *demonstrates* the problem better than the mark sheet does: the time collapses at bar 33.", "note": "Use it when you can point to the demonstration itself; if you can't, step down to 'indicates'.", "level": 2}, {"id": "stance-31", "field": "Academic discourse and stance", "term": "I'd want to resist", "gloss": "A firm but courteous signal of disagreement with a framing, conclusion or move, short of outright rejection.", "say": "*I'd want to resist* the idea that the elective is a feeder for ATAR; it has its own value.", "note": "Senior and measured; the conditional 'want to' keeps it collegial without weakening it.", "level": 2}, {"id": "stance-32", "field": "Academic discourse and stance", "term": "the burden of proof", "gloss": "Which party must make the case: usually the one proposing change or asserting the positive claim.", "say": "*The burden of proof* sits with the proposal; the current structure is working, so it is for the change to show it improves on it.", "note": "Use it to reallocate the argument, not to avoid making one yourself.", "level": 2}, {"id": "stance-33", "field": "Academic discourse and stance", "term": "a non sequitur", "gloss": "A conclusion that does not follow from what preceded it; a gap in logic, not merely a change of subject.", "say": "With respect, that's *a non sequitur*: the festival's growth doesn't tell us anything about the judging standard.", "note": "Pronounced non SEK-wit-er. Name it once; repeated, it sounds like point-scoring.", "level": 2}, {"id": "stance-34", "field": "Academic discourse and stance", "term": "the strong version and the weak version", "gloss": "Distinguishes the ambitious, harder-to-defend form of a claim from a modest, more defensible form of it.", "say": "*The weak version* of the claim, that ensembles aid retention, I accept. *The strong version*, that they cause higher ATAR results, I don't.", "note": "One of the best tools for agreeing and disagreeing with precision; it also shows you are being fair to the proposer.", "level": 2}, {"id": "stance-35", "field": "Academic discourse and stance", "term": "generalisable", "gloss": "Holds beyond the particular sample or setting in which it was found.", "say": "It's a good result, but I'm not sure it's *generalisable* from a selective school to a comprehensive one.", "note": "Applies to findings, not people; pair it with the specific difference that limits it.", "level": 2}, {"id": "stance-36", "field": "Academic discourse and stance", "term": "a distinction without a difference", "gloss": "A verbal distinction that changes nothing in substance or practice.", "say": "Calling it a 'review' rather than an 'audit' is *a distinction without a difference* if the same people see the same files.", "note": "Crisp in governance rooms; it can sound dismissive if the other side has a real reason for the distinction.", "level": 2}, {"id": "stance-37", "field": "Academic discourse and stance", "term": "begs the question", "gloss": "In the strict sense: assumes the conclusion in the premises. It does not mean 'raises the question'.", "say": "Defining a strong program as one with high ATAR enrolment *begs the question* we're trying to answer.", "note": "Many educated listeners now hear the loose sense; if precision matters, say 'that assumes what it sets out to prove'.", "level": 2}, {"id": "stance-38", "field": "Academic discourse and stance", "term": "stipulate", "gloss": "To fix the meaning of a term for the purpose of a discussion, openly, rather than claim it is the ordinary meaning.", "say": "Let me *stipulate* that by 'specialist' I mean the selective program, not the general elective.", "note": "Clears confusion early; it signals you know definitions can be argued over.", "level": 2}, {"id": "stance-39", "field": "Academic discourse and stance", "term": "normative versus descriptive", "gloss": "A descriptive claim says what is the case; a normative claim says what ought to be. Arguments often slide between them.", "say": "That's a *descriptive* point about current enrolments; the paper's real claim is *normative*, about what the program should be for.", "note": "Very useful for exposing a hidden shift in an argument; explain it in plainer terms in non-academic rooms.", "level": 2}, {"id": "stance-40", "field": "Academic discourse and stance", "term": "an unstated premise", "gloss": "An assumption an argument needs but does not declare, which is where it is often weakest.", "say": "The proposal rests on *an unstated premise*, that every student in the program intends to study music after school.", "note": "Naming it calmly is often enough; the room does the rest.", "level": 2}, {"id": "stance-41", "field": "Academic discourse and stance", "term": "confounded", "gloss": "Of a comparison: another variable varies with the one being studied, so its effect cannot be separated out.", "say": "The comparison is *confounded* by socio-economic status; the program students come disproportionately from higher-fee instrumental studios.", "note": "A research term of art; use it only where you can name the confounding variable.", "level": 2}, {"id": "stance-42", "field": "Academic discourse and stance", "term": "a selection effect", "gloss": "An apparent effect produced by who ends up in the sample rather than by the thing being studied.", "say": "Some of that is *a selection effect*: the students who stay to Year 12 were always the most committed.", "note": "Lands with academics and principals alike; it is one of the most useful corrections in school data.", "level": 2}, {"id": "stance-43", "field": "Academic discourse and stance", "term": "won't bear the weight", "gloss": "The evidence or argument is too slight to support the conclusion being placed on it.", "say": "One cohort of results *won't bear the weight* of a restructure.", "note": "Measured and visual; implies the conclusion might be right while denying it is shown.", "level": 2}, {"id": "stance-44", "field": "Academic discourse and stance", "term": "reductive", "gloss": "Explaining something complex by one of its factors in a way that loses what matters.", "say": "Treating the audition as a test of pitch discrimination alone is *reductive*.", "note": "Accurate criticism in arts rooms; it can sound like a reflex if you don't say what is lost.", "level": 2}, {"id": "stance-45", "field": "Academic discourse and stance", "term": "the received view", "gloss": "The position generally accepted in a field or institution, usually named in order to question it.", "say": "*The received view* is that big band is the entry point to jazz; I'm less sure it should be.", "note": "Elegant way to set up a challenge without attacking anyone in the room who holds it.", "level": 2}, {"id": "stance-47", "field": "Academic discourse and stance", "term": "bracket", "gloss": "To set an issue aside deliberately for the moment, without dismissing it, so that another can be discussed cleanly.", "say": "If we can *bracket* the budget for ten minutes, I'd like to settle what we actually want the program to do.", "note": "A good chair's move; promise to return to it and then do.", "level": 2}, {"id": "stance-49", "field": "Academic discourse and stance", "term": "the question I'd put is", "gloss": "Frames a contribution as a question for the room, which positions the speaker as setting the inquiry rather than defending a view.", "say": "*The question I'd put is* what a pass in this unit should guarantee a student can do.", "note": "A chair-like move that shifts the meeting; use it once, for the question that matters.", "level": 2}, {"id": "stance-50", "field": "Academic discourse and stance", "term": "what would count as evidence that", "gloss": "Asks the other side to specify what would confirm or refute their claim, which tests whether it can be tested.", "say": "*What would count as evidence that* the new structure is working, in two years' time?", "note": "Constructive rather than hostile; it often reveals that a claim has no agreed measure.", "level": 2}, {"id": "stance-51", "field": "Academic discourse and stance", "term": "a proxy", "gloss": "A measurable stand-in for something harder to measure, which can drift away from the thing it stands for.", "say": "Attendance is *a proxy* for engagement, and a poor one in Year 11, when everyone attends.", "note": "Strong in data discussions; follow with why this proxy fails.", "level": 2}, {"id": "stance-52", "field": "Academic discourse and stance", "term": "there is a body of work on", "gloss": "Cites a research area without naming individuals, signalling familiarity without name-dropping.", "say": "*There is a body of work on* informal learning in popular music education that speaks directly to this.", "note": "Lands well if you can name a source when asked; if you can't, don't use it.", "level": 2}, {"id": "stance-53", "field": "Academic discourse and stance", "term": "the standard objection", "gloss": "The objection anyone familiar with the debate would raise, named in order to answer it pre-emptively.", "say": "*The standard objection* is that a portfolio is harder to moderate. That's true, and here's how other jurisdictions handle it.", "note": "Shows command of the debate and takes the wind out of the reply.", "level": 2}, {"id": "stance-55", "field": "Academic discourse and stance", "term": "entails", "gloss": "Strictly: if the first is true, the second must be. Stronger than 'implies' in its loose sense.", "say": "Accepting the new rubric *entails* re-marking last year's sample, or the comparison is meaningless.", "note": "Use it only when the necessity is real; for 'probably leads to', use 'suggests' or 'would likely mean'.", "level": 2}, {"id": "stance-56", "field": "Academic discourse and stance", "term": "the charitable reading", "gloss": "The interpretation that makes an opponent's argument as strong as it can reasonably be, before responding to it.", "say": "*The charitable reading* of the proposal is that it's about workload, not about the program's standing.", "note": "Disarming and fair; avoid saying it with an edge, or it sounds like condescension.", "level": 2}, {"id": "stance-57", "field": "Academic discourse and stance", "term": "I'd stop short of saying", "gloss": "A calibrated hedge that marks the exact limit of the speaker's claim, which sounds rigorous because the limit is stated.", "say": "The data shows a real decline. *I'd stop short of saying* it's caused by the timetable change.", "note": "One of the best replacements for vague hedging: it says how far you go and no further.", "level": 2}, {"id": "stance-58", "field": "Academic discourse and stance", "term": "so far as it goes", "gloss": "Concedes that an argument is valid within its scope while signalling that its scope is too narrow.", "say": "The financial case is sound *so far as it goes*; it doesn't account for the students we'd lose.", "note": "Quietly cutting; the pause before 'so far as it goes' does the work.", "level": 2}, {"id": "stance-59", "field": "Academic discourse and stance", "term": "agnostic", "gloss": "Holding no view either way on a specific question, often deliberately, while taking a view on others.", "say": "I'm *agnostic* on whether the band rehearses before or after school; I care that it rehearses twice.", "note": "Signals focus rather than indecision; useful for narrowing a debate to what matters.", "level": 2}, {"id": "stance-61", "field": "Academic discourse and stance", "term": "mutatis mutandis", "gloss": "With the necessary changes made: an argument or rule applies to a second case once the relevant differences are adjusted for.", "say": "The same moderation process should apply to the composition portfolio, *mutatis mutandis*.", "note": "Pronounced moo-TAH-tis moo-TAN-dis. Natural in legal and governance drafting; in conversation, once per meeting at most.", "level": 3}, {"id": "stance-62", "field": "Academic discourse and stance", "term": "pace X", "gloss": "Placed before a name: 'with due respect to X', acknowledging that the person named holds a contrary view.", "say": "*Pace* the working party, I don't think a second audition round would change the outcome.", "note": "Pronounced PAY-see or PAH-chay, never like 'pace' the walk. Rare in speech and easily misheard; safer in writing.", "level": 3}, {"id": "stance-64", "field": "Academic discourse and stance", "term": "underdetermined", "gloss": "Of a conclusion: the available evidence is consistent with more than one explanation, so it does not settle which is true.", "say": "The decline is *underdetermined* by the data; the timetable, the staffing change and the pandemic cohort all fit.", "note": "A philosophy-of-science term; very precise with academics, opaque to most others.", "level": 3}, {"id": "stance-65", "field": "Academic discourse and stance", "term": "a fortiori", "gloss": "With stronger reason: if a claim holds in a weaker case, it holds all the more in a stronger one.", "say": "If we require a second marker for the elective, then *a fortiori* we should require one for ATAR.", "note": "Pronounced ay for-tee-OR-eye or ah for-tee-OR-ee. Elegant in argument; 'all the more so' does the same work plainly.", "level": 3}, {"id": "stance-66", "field": "Academic discourse and stance", "term": "inter alia", "gloss": "Among other things: signals that the items listed are a selection, not exhaustive.", "say": "The review considered, *inter alia*, the staffing ratio and the instrument fleet.", "note": "Natural in minutes and reports; in speech it can sound like reading aloud.", "level": 3}, {"id": "stance-68", "field": "Academic discourse and stance", "term": "ex ante and ex post", "gloss": "Before the event and after it: distinguishes judgements made with the information then available from those made with hindsight.", "say": "*Ex ante* the decision was reasonable; *ex post* it looks costly, but that isn't the test for the board.", "note": "Native in finance and governance; strong for defending a past decision fairly.", "level": 3}, {"id": "stance-69", "field": "Academic discourse and stance", "term": "heterodox", "gloss": "Departing from the accepted view in a field, usually in a considered and defensible way.", "say": "My view on the audition weighting is somewhat *heterodox*, so let me make the case properly.", "note": "Self-applied, it pre-empts the charge of eccentricity; applied to others, it can sound like a slight.", "level": 3}, {"id": "stance-71", "field": "Academic discourse and stance", "term": "teleological", "gloss": "Explaining or justifying something by its end or purpose, or assuming that development is heading towards a fixed goal.", "say": "The syllabus has a *teleological* view of jazz history, as if every style were heading towards bebop.", "note": "A precise critique in arts and humanities rooms; check the listener knows it before building on it.", "level": 3}, {"id": "stance-72", "field": "Academic discourse and stance", "term": "parsimonious", "gloss": "Of an explanation: the simplest one that accounts for the data, with the fewest assumptions.", "say": "The more *parsimonious* explanation is the timetable clash; we don't need a theory about motivation.", "note": "Native in research methods talk; in ordinary use it means stingy, so context matters.", "level": 3}, {"id": "stance-74", "field": "Academic discourse and stance", "term": "pro tanto", "gloss": "To that extent: a reason that genuinely counts in favour of something, though it may be outweighed by others.", "say": "Parental demand is a *pro tanto* reason to add the ensemble; it isn't a conclusive one.", "note": "Philosophers' Latin; superb in an ethics or academic committee, obscure almost everywhere else.", "level": 3}, {"id": "stance-75", "field": "Academic discourse and stance", "term": "scope conditions", "gloss": "The circumstances under which a finding or theory is claimed to hold, beyond which it makes no claim.", "say": "Before we adopt the model, what are its *scope conditions*? It was developed for private studios, not classes of thirty.", "note": "Precise in research and policy rooms; it sounds rigorous because it limits rather than inflates.", "level": 3}, {"id": "stance-76", "field": "Academic discourse and stance", "term": "obiter", "gloss": "Short for obiter dictum: a remark made in passing that is not part of the reasoning a decision rests on.", "say": "The comment about outsourcing was *obiter*; the report's recommendation doesn't depend on it.", "note": "Pronounced OB-it-er. Lawyers will hear it exactly; others may need the plain gloss.", "level": 3}, {"id": "stance-77", "field": "Academic discourse and stance", "term": "tout court", "gloss": "Without qualification; simply, and in its entirety.", "say": "I'm not arguing against assessment *tout court*, only against this rubric.", "note": "Pronounced too KOOR. Elegant when it sharpens a distinction; affected if dropped in casually.", "level": 3}, {"id": "stance-78", "field": "Academic discourse and stance", "term": "a desideratum", "gloss": "Something desirable or needed, listed among the criteria a solution should meet.", "say": "Portability between schools is a *desideratum*, not a requirement.", "note": "Pronounced dez-id-er-AH-tum; plural desiderata. Useful in design and policy rooms for ranking criteria.", "level": 3}, {"id": "stance-79", "field": "Academic discourse and stance", "term": "a lacuna", "gloss": "A gap, especially in an argument, a text, a policy or a body of knowledge.", "say": "There's a *lacuna* in the policy: it says nothing about students who join mid-year.", "note": "Pronounced la-KOO-na. Precise and quietly scholarly; 'gap' is fine in most rooms.", "level": 3}, {"id": "stance-80", "field": "Academic discourse and stance", "term": "ipso facto", "gloss": "By that very fact: the conclusion follows directly from the fact stated, with no further step.", "say": "Being an accredited adjudicator doesn't *ipso facto* qualify someone to judge improvisation.", "note": "Most often used in the negative, as here, which is where it lands best.", "level": 3}]}, {"field": "Music: the professional's vocabulary", "intro": "Speaking about music to academics, panels and adjudicators means translating what the bandstand knows into terms that hold up under scrutiny, without losing the players' precision. Sounding native means using the working musician's word for the thing, then the scholar's word for the concept, and knowing which room needs which.", "entries": [{"id": "music-1", "field": "Music: the professional's vocabulary", "term": "time feel", "gloss": "The way a player or ensemble inhabits the pulse: placement, density and weight of the beat, as distinct from mere tempo accuracy.", "say": "The tempo was steady, but the *time feel* was rigid; nothing breathed between the beats.", "note": "The word adjudicators reach for when 'good time' is too vague; be ready to say what the feel was.", "level": 1}, {"id": "music-2", "field": "Music: the professional's vocabulary", "term": "pocket", "gloss": "The shared rhythmic placement in which a rhythm section locks, so that the groove feels settled and inevitable.", "say": "Once the bass and drums found the *pocket* in the second chorus, the horns relaxed into it.", "note": "Native among players; to an academic panel, gloss it once as 'a shared rhythmic placement'.", "level": 1}, {"id": "music-3", "field": "Music: the professional's vocabulary", "term": "beat placement", "gloss": "Where a player sits relative to the nominal beat: behind, in the middle or on top, as a stylistic and expressive choice.", "say": "His *beat placement* is slightly behind, in the manner of the Basie rhythm section, and the drummer has to accommodate it.", "note": "Shows you understand time as a choice rather than a correctness; precise in adjudication feedback.", "level": 1}, {"id": "music-4", "field": "Music: the professional's vocabulary", "term": "phrasing", "gloss": "The shaping of a musical line over time, through breath, accent, dynamics, articulation and placement, into units of sense.", "say": "The *phrasing* was idiomatic in the head but became square in the solo, two-bar units with no larger shape.", "note": "Universal, so it needs a specific attachment to sound professional.", "level": 1}, {"id": "music-5", "field": "Music: the professional's vocabulary", "term": "idiomatic articulation", "gloss": "Articulation that belongs to a style, such as the tonguing, ghosting and accent patterns of swing, rather than generic accuracy.", "say": "The section is accurate, but the *articulation* isn't idiomatic yet: the off-beats need weight and the ghosted notes need to disappear.", "note": "The distinction between correct and idiomatic is one panels often lack the language for; give it to them.", "level": 1}, {"id": "music-6", "field": "Music: the professional's vocabulary", "term": "voicing", "gloss": "The vertical arrangement of a chord's notes: which tones, in what register, in which instruments, with what spacing.", "say": "The *voicing* in the trombones sits too low for that tempo; it muddies the bass line.", "note": "Standard among musicians; with non-musicians, say 'how the chord is spread across the band'.", "level": 1}, {"id": "music-7", "field": "Music: the professional's vocabulary", "term": "voice leading", "gloss": "The way individual notes move from one chord to the next, typically by step and with minimal motion, so that harmony is heard as lines.", "say": "His comping is all about *voice leading*: the top note moves by step through the bridge and you hear a melody.", "note": "The term most likely to signal training in both jazz and classical theory; precise with academics.", "level": 1}, {"id": "music-8", "field": "Music: the professional's vocabulary", "term": "reharmonisation", "gloss": "Supplying new chords beneath an existing melody, changing its harmonic colour and implication while keeping the tune.", "say": "The arrangement's *reharmonisation* of the bridge is ambitious, but it obscures the melody the students should be learning.", "note": "Distinguish it from substitution of single chords; reharmonisation is a broader design decision.", "level": 1}, {"id": "music-9", "field": "Music: the professional's vocabulary", "term": "the form", "gloss": "The structural layout of a piece, such as AABA, the twelve-bar blues or through-composed, which every player must hold in mind while improvising.", "say": "The soloist lost *the form* at the bridge, and the rhythm section, to its credit, didn't follow.", "note": "Losing the form is a serious fault in jazz adjudication; say so plainly.", "level": 1}, {"id": "music-10", "field": "Music: the professional's vocabulary", "term": "idiom", "gloss": "The stylistic language of a genre or tradition: its characteristic gestures, rhythms, harmonies and sound.", "say": "It's a fine performance, but not in the *idiom*; it sounds like a concert band playing a jazz chart.", "note": "Works with every audience; 'idiomatic' is the adjective panels quote back.", "level": 1}, {"id": "music-11", "field": "Music: the professional's vocabulary", "term": "stylistic authenticity", "gloss": "Fidelity to the practices, sound and conventions of the style as its practitioners understand them, not merely to the notes.", "say": "The criterion should be *stylistic authenticity*, not accuracy to the page, because in this music the page is a sketch.", "note": "Useful in criteria debates; note that 'authenticity' is itself contested in the scholarship, so expect pushback from musicologists.", "level": 1}, {"id": "music-12", "field": "Music: the professional's vocabulary", "term": "the tradition", "gloss": "In jazz, the living lineage of practice, repertoire and values handed down through players, recordings and the bandstand.", "say": "The students know the chart; what they don't yet know is *the tradition* the chart is drawing on.", "note": "Lands with musicians as a term of seriousness; with academics, be ready for 'whose tradition?'", "level": 1}, {"id": "music-13", "field": "Music: the professional's vocabulary", "term": "repertoire", "gloss": "The body of works a performer, ensemble or program draws on, chosen with intent rather than accumulated.", "say": "The *repertoire* across the four bands needs to be planned as a sequence, not chosen chart by chart.", "note": "Pronounced REP-er-twah. Sounds professional when tied to curation and sequence.", "level": 1}, {"id": "music-14", "field": "Music: the professional's vocabulary", "term": "aural tradition", "gloss": "Transmission of music by listening, imitation and memory, in which the sound is the primary text.", "say": "Jazz is fundamentally an *aural tradition*; the notated chart is a convenience, not the work.", "note": "Distinct from 'oral tradition', which some use for the same idea; be consistent once you choose.", "level": 1}, {"id": "music-15", "field": "Music: the professional's vocabulary", "term": "notated tradition", "gloss": "Transmission and authority grounded in the written score, in which the performer interprets a fixed text.", "say": "Our assessment model was built for the *notated tradition*, and it shows when we try to apply it to improvisation.", "note": "Paired with 'aural tradition', it frames the most important assessment debate in music education.", "level": 1}, {"id": "music-16", "field": "Music: the professional's vocabulary", "term": "improvisation as composition", "gloss": "Treats improvisation as real-time composition, with structure, development and design, rather than as spontaneous decoration.", "say": "If we accept *improvisation as composition*, then motivic development and architecture are legitimate criteria.", "note": "The framing that wins over composers and classical academics; it gives them a familiar standard.", "level": 1}, {"id": "music-17", "field": "Music: the professional's vocabulary", "term": "adjudication criteria", "gloss": "The stated standards against which performances are judged, ideally published, weighted and applied consistently.", "say": "The *adjudication criteria* weight technique at forty per cent and style at ten, which tells bands what we value.", "note": "Point to the criteria rather than to taste when a result is challenged.", "level": 1}, {"id": "music-18", "field": "Music: the professional's vocabulary", "term": "balance and blend", "gloss": "Balance is the relative volume of parts; blend is how well timbres fuse into one sound. They are separate faults.", "say": "The *balance* is right, the melody comes through, but the *blend* isn't there: the altos are brighter than the tenors.", "note": "Separating the two is a mark of a trained ensemble director.", "level": 1}, {"id": "music-19", "field": "Music: the professional's vocabulary", "term": "comping", "gloss": "Accompanying in jazz, chiefly by piano or guitar, with rhythmically placed chords that support and converse with the soloist.", "say": "The *comping* was harmonically correct but rhythmically static; it never answered the soloist.", "note": "Short for accompanying or complementing; both explanations are heard.", "level": 1}, {"id": "music-20", "field": "Music: the professional's vocabulary", "term": "head arrangement", "gloss": "An arrangement worked out by ear and memory in rehearsal, not written down, in the manner of the Kansas City bands.", "say": "The riffs behind the soloist are a *head arrangement*; the students built them in the room.", "note": "Useful for showing panels that aural creation is part of the tradition, not a shortcut.", "level": 1}, {"id": "music-21", "field": "Music: the professional's vocabulary", "term": "interpretive latitude", "gloss": "The degree of freedom a style or score allows the performer in tempo, phrasing, ornamentation and sound.", "say": "The *interpretive latitude* in a Basie chart is much wider than in a band-festival test piece, and the criteria should allow for it.", "note": "Lets you argue that 'deviation' is sometimes the point.", "level": 1}, {"id": "music-22", "field": "Music: the professional's vocabulary", "term": "historically informed performance", "gloss": "Performance that draws on period evidence about instruments, technique and conventions to inform interpretation.", "say": "The jazz equivalent of *historically informed performance* is listening to the original recordings, not just reading the chart.", "note": "Often shortened to HIP; the analogy to jazz lands well with classical panellists.", "level": 1}, {"id": "music-23", "field": "Music: the professional's vocabulary", "term": "vocabulary", "gloss": "In jazz, the stock of phrases, gestures and devices a player has absorbed from the tradition and can deploy fluently.", "say": "She has the *vocabulary*; what she's developing now is the ability to shape it into a solo.", "note": "Pairs with 'language'; useful to distinguish knowledge of the idiom from creative use of it.", "level": 1}, {"id": "music-24", "field": "Music: the professional's vocabulary", "term": "the Great American Songbook", "gloss": "The core repertoire of popular songs from roughly the 1920s to the 1950s that provides much of jazz's standard repertoire.", "say": "If the students don't know *the Great American Songbook* as songs, with lyrics, they phrase the melodies like exercises.", "note": "Widely recognised; a good bridge to academics outside music.", "level": 1}, {"id": "music-25", "field": "Music: the professional's vocabulary", "term": "rubato", "gloss": "Expressive flexibility of tempo within a phrase, traditionally 'stolen' time that is given back, as distinct from simply slowing down.", "say": "The ballad needed *rubato* in the opening, and then strict time once the rhythm section enters.", "note": "Distinguish true rubato from unsteady time when giving feedback; students hear the difference only when you name it.", "level": 2}, {"id": "music-26", "field": "Music: the professional's vocabulary", "term": "swing ratio", "gloss": "The proportion between the long and short notes of a swung pair, which varies with tempo and player rather than sitting at a fixed triplet.", "say": "At that tempo the *swing ratio* should flatten toward even; playing hard triplets makes it sound dated.", "note": "Shows real understanding of swing; it also corrects the widespread classroom simplification.", "level": 2}, {"id": "music-27", "field": "Music: the professional's vocabulary", "term": "hemiola", "gloss": "A rhythmic shift in which groups of three are heard against a duple frame, or vice versa, typically across a cadence.", "say": "The *hemiola* in the Brahms is the same device as the three-over-four in the shout chorus; the students should hear both.", "note": "Pronounced hem-ee-OH-la. A bridge term between classical and jazz rhythm.", "level": 2}, {"id": "music-28", "field": "Music: the professional's vocabulary", "term": "metric modulation", "gloss": "A change of tempo or metre in which a note value in the old tempo becomes a different note value in the new.", "say": "The arrangement uses a *metric modulation* into the double-time section, and the drummer needs to own it.", "note": "Associated with Elliott Carter in art music and common in contemporary jazz; precise with composers.", "level": 2}, {"id": "music-29", "field": "Music: the professional's vocabulary", "term": "the hookup", "gloss": "The rhythmic relationship between bass and drums, especially the lock between walking bass and ride cymbal.", "say": "The *hookup* between bass and drums is the first thing I listen for; if it isn't there, nothing else settles.", "note": "Players' vernacular; gloss it for academic panels.", "level": 2}, {"id": "music-30", "field": "Music: the professional's vocabulary", "term": "tritone substitution", "gloss": "Replacing a dominant chord with the dominant a tritone away, which shares its guide tones and creates chromatic bass motion.", "say": "The *tritone substitution* in bar 8 is fine, but the bass player has to play it or the horns' voicing clashes.", "note": "A standard term; to an academic panel, frame it as an example of functional equivalence.", "level": 2}, {"id": "music-31", "field": "Music: the professional's vocabulary", "term": "modal interchange", "gloss": "Borrowing chords from a parallel mode or key, such as the minor iv in a major key, for colour without changing the tonic.", "say": "The *modal interchange* in the turnaround gives the ending its melancholy.", "note": "Also called modal mixture in classical theory; knowing both names helps in mixed rooms.", "level": 2}, {"id": "music-32", "field": "Music: the professional's vocabulary", "term": "guide tones", "gloss": "The thirds and sevenths of chords, which define their quality and form smooth lines through a progression.", "say": "If the soloist targets the *guide tones*, you hear the changes even with the rhythm section out.", "note": "Practical and precise; it shows pedagogy rooted in the harmony, not in scales.", "level": 2}, {"id": "music-33", "field": "Music: the professional's vocabulary", "term": "contrafact", "gloss": "A new melody written over the chord progression of an existing tune, a central compositional practice of bebop.", "say": "The set list has three *contrafacts* of 'I Got Rhythm'; I'd like more variety in the form.", "note": "Pronounced KON-tra-fakt. A term that signals real jazz-history literacy to academics.", "level": 2}, {"id": "music-34", "field": "Music: the professional's vocabulary", "term": "motivic development", "gloss": "Building a solo or composition from the transformation of a small motif, through repetition, sequence, inversion and variation.", "say": "The second solo had genuine *motivic development*; the first was a chain of licks.", "note": "A criterion that classical adjudicators recognise instantly; it makes improvisation legible to them.", "level": 2}, {"id": "music-35", "field": "Music: the professional's vocabulary", "term": "the architecture of a solo", "gloss": "The large-scale shape of an improvisation: its arc of density, register, intensity and climax across choruses.", "say": "Technically he's ready; what's missing is *the architecture of a solo*, a sense of where it's going.", "note": "Pitches feedback above note-level; panels hear it as compositional thinking.", "level": 2}, {"id": "music-36", "field": "Music: the professional's vocabulary", "term": "tessitura", "gloss": "The range in which a part predominantly lies, as distinct from its full range; it governs comfort, colour and fatigue.", "say": "The lead trumpet part's *tessitura* is too high for Year 9 for a whole chart, even if the top note is within range.", "note": "Pronounced tess-i-TOO-ra. Distinguishing it from range is a mark of experience.", "level": 2}, {"id": "music-37", "field": "Music: the professional's vocabulary", "term": "sound concept", "gloss": "A player's internalised ideal of their own tone, built from listening, which drives technical choices.", "say": "Before we fix the embouchure, he needs a *sound concept*; he hasn't heard enough great tenor players.", "note": "Common among jazz educators; it frames tone as aural and aesthetic, not purely mechanical.", "level": 2}, {"id": "music-38", "field": "Music: the professional's vocabulary", "term": "performance practice", "gloss": "The conventions of execution in a style or period that the notation does not specify but practitioners understand.", "say": "The swing-era *performance practice* includes falls, scoops and section vibrato that the chart never marks.", "note": "Lets you argue that unnotated conventions are part of the work, which helps in criteria debates.", "level": 2}, {"id": "music-39", "field": "Music: the professional's vocabulary", "term": "the canon", "gloss": "The works treated as central and exemplary in a tradition, whose formation is itself a historical and contestable process.", "say": "Whatever we think of *the canon*, students need to know it before they can argue with it.", "note": "Expect academics to question it; acknowledging canon formation pre-empts that.", "level": 2}, {"id": "music-40", "field": "Music: the professional's vocabulary", "term": "transcription as apprenticeship", "gloss": "Learning solos by ear and reproducing them, understood as the traditional apprenticeship in jazz, not a mere technical exercise.", "say": "*Transcription as apprenticeship* is how the tradition has always taught itself; it belongs in the senior course.", "note": "Strong in curriculum debate; it reframes copying as a historically grounded pedagogy.", "level": 2}, {"id": "music-41", "field": "Music: the professional's vocabulary", "term": "lineage", "gloss": "The line of influence from teacher or model to player, through which style and values are transmitted.", "say": "You can hear the *lineage* in her playing, from Ray Brown through Christian McBride.", "note": "A respectful, precise term; lands with musicians and historians alike.", "level": 2}, {"id": "music-42", "field": "Music: the professional's vocabulary", "term": "microtiming", "gloss": "Small, systematic deviations from metronomic time, often in milliseconds, that produce groove and feel.", "say": "The *microtiming* between the ride cymbal and the bass is what makes that recording swing.", "note": "The empirical research term for what players call feel; it gives academics something to hold on to.", "level": 2}, {"id": "music-43", "field": "Music: the professional's vocabulary", "term": "inside and outside", "gloss": "Playing within the prevailing harmony versus deliberately departing from it, with the departure resolved or controlled.", "say": "Going *outside* is legitimate, but only if you can find your way back *inside*; here it sounded like losing the changes.", "note": "The distinction is about control, not quantity; make that explicit when adjudicating.", "level": 2}, {"id": "music-44", "field": "Music: the professional's vocabulary", "term": "enclosure", "gloss": "Approaching a target note from above and below, chromatically or diatonically, a signature bebop device.", "say": "The line is full of *enclosures* around the thirds; it's very good Charlie Parker vocabulary.", "note": "A precise technical term; useful to show the analytic depth of jazz pedagogy.", "level": 2}, {"id": "music-45", "field": "Music: the professional's vocabulary", "term": "harmonic rhythm", "gloss": "The rate at which chords change, which shapes momentum and phrasing independently of tempo.", "say": "The *harmonic rhythm* doubles in the bridge, which is why the soloists are rushing.", "note": "A classical term that maps perfectly onto jazz; it opens doors with theorists.", "level": 2}, {"id": "music-46", "field": "Music: the professional's vocabulary", "term": "planing", "gloss": "Moving a chord shape in parallel motion, so that its quality stays constant as it moves, rather than functionally.", "say": "The *planing* in the saxophone soli is pure Ellington, and very hard to tune.", "note": "Associated with Debussy in art music; the cross-reference often impresses classical panels.", "level": 2}, {"id": "music-47", "field": "Music: the professional's vocabulary", "term": "arrangement versus orchestration", "gloss": "Arrangement shapes the material: form, harmony, sequence. Orchestration assigns it to instruments and colours.", "say": "The *arrangement* is strong; the *orchestration* is the issue, with too much doubling in the middle register.", "note": "Separating the two marks a professional ear; many panels treat them as one.", "level": 2}, {"id": "music-48", "field": "Music: the professional's vocabulary", "term": "inner-voice movement", "gloss": "The motion of the middle parts of a texture, which gives harmony life even when the outer voices are static.", "say": "The *inner-voice movement* in the trombones is what makes this chart sound expensive.", "note": "Useful praise and diagnosis; it directs attention to parts students treat as filler.", "level": 2}, {"id": "music-49", "field": "Music: the professional's vocabulary", "term": "shout chorus", "gloss": "In big-band writing, the climactic ensemble chorus, usually after the solos, in which the full band plays a newly composed tutti line.", "say": "The *shout chorus* needs the lead trumpet to commit to the phrasing, or the section has nothing to follow.", "note": "Native to jazz arranging; to academics, call it the structural climax.", "level": 2}, {"id": "music-50", "field": "Music: the professional's vocabulary", "term": "the lead-player concept", "gloss": "In big-band practice, the lead player of each section sets phrasing, articulation and dynamics, and the section follows.", "say": "We teach the *lead-player concept* from Year 7; the conductor is not the source of phrasing in this music.", "note": "Shows how jazz ensemble pedagogy differs from wind-band pedagogy; strong in curriculum discussions.", "level": 2}, {"id": "music-51", "field": "Music: the professional's vocabulary", "term": "chamber listening", "gloss": "Players listening to each other across the ensemble, as in chamber music, rather than relying on the conductor.", "say": "The band played better with my hands down; that's *chamber listening*, and it's the goal.", "note": "A useful bridge to classical colleagues; it frames jazz ensemble skill in their terms.", "level": 2}, {"id": "music-52", "field": "Music: the professional's vocabulary", "term": "diagnostic rehearsal", "gloss": "Rehearsing by identifying the cause of a problem, such as time, tuning or balance, rather than repeating the passage.", "say": "Running it again won't fix it. *Diagnostic rehearsal* says it's the third trombone's placement, not the ensemble.", "note": "Signals rehearsal craft to panels and colleagues; use it to argue for skilled ensemble staffing.", "level": 2}, {"id": "music-53", "field": "Music: the professional's vocabulary", "term": "sound before symbol", "gloss": "The principle that musical concepts should be experienced aurally before they are named or notated.", "say": "Our junior program follows *sound before symbol*: they play the blues before they see it on paper.", "note": "A long-standing principle in music education; it connects jazz practice to classroom pedagogy.", "level": 2}, {"id": "music-54", "field": "Music: the professional's vocabulary", "term": "criterion-referenced adjudication", "gloss": "Judging each performance against published standards, not ranking performances against one another.", "say": "The festival is moving to *criterion-referenced adjudication*; bands are judged against the descriptors, not the band before them.", "note": "Distinct from norm-referencing; essential when a festival awards ratings rather than places.", "level": 2}, {"id": "music-55", "field": "Music: the professional's vocabulary", "term": "holistic versus analytic judgement", "gloss": "Holistic judgement weighs the performance as a whole; analytic marking scores separate criteria and sums them.", "say": "The panel split on *holistic versus analytic judgement*: the marks summed to silver, but every adjudicator heard a gold performance.", "note": "A central assessment distinction; make the trade-off explicit rather than picking a side.", "level": 2}, {"id": "music-56", "field": "Music: the professional's vocabulary", "term": "moderation", "gloss": "The process of comparing and adjusting judgements across assessors so that standards are applied consistently.", "say": "Before the festival, the adjudicators should do *moderation* on three recordings, so we agree what a gold rating sounds like.", "note": "The Australian term; in other systems it may be called standardisation or calibration.", "level": 2}, {"id": "music-57", "field": "Music: the professional's vocabulary", "term": "technical demand versus musical realisation", "gloss": "Distinguishes how difficult a piece is from how well it is actually performed.", "say": "A hard chart is not a merit in itself; the criteria should reward *musical realisation*, not *technical demand*.", "note": "The single most useful distinction in festival debates; it stops bands choosing music beyond them.", "level": 2}, {"id": "music-58", "field": "Music: the professional's vocabulary", "term": "Urtext", "gloss": "An edition that aims to present the composer's text as faithfully as possible, without later editorial additions.", "say": "The jazz equivalent of an *Urtext* is the original recording, not the published chart.", "note": "Pronounced OOR-text. It shows classical literacy and makes the analogy with jazz sharp.", "level": 2}, {"id": "music-59", "field": "Music: the professional's vocabulary", "term": "the long line", "gloss": "The sense of continuity across a whole phrase or movement, so that local details serve a larger shape.", "say": "The detail is beautiful, but the *long line* is missing; each phrase stops instead of leading on.", "note": "A classical teacher's phrase; using it in jazz feedback shows breadth.", "level": 2}, {"id": "music-60", "field": "Music: the professional's vocabulary", "term": "linear versus vertical thinking", "gloss": "Linear improvisation thinks in melodic lines through the changes; vertical improvisation thinks chord by chord.", "say": "He's still thinking *vertically*, chord by chord; the next step is *linear*, so the line connects the changes.", "note": "A practical diagnostic distinction; it frames the developmental step clearly.", "level": 2}, {"id": "music-61", "field": "Music: the professional's vocabulary", "term": "upper-structure triads", "gloss": "Triads placed above a chord's basic tones to produce the upper extensions and alterations, a common voicing technique in modern jazz.", "say": "The pianist is using *upper-structure triads* over the dominants; the guitar needs to stay out of that register.", "note": "Precise and technical; use it with players, not general panels.", "level": 3}, {"id": "music-62", "field": "Music: the professional's vocabulary", "term": "the score as script, not text", "gloss": "The idea that a score is a set of instructions for social performance, not a fixed text whose correct reading is the goal.", "say": "If we treat *the score as script, not text*, the criteria have to reward what the performers make of it.", "note": "From performance studies; lands with musicologists and can clarify assessment debates. Attribute only if you know the source.", "level": 3}, {"id": "music-63", "field": "Music: the professional's vocabulary", "term": "Werktreue", "gloss": "Fidelity to the musical work as conceived by its composer, an ideal of nineteenth-century and later concert culture.", "say": "Our criteria carry an unexamined *Werktreue* ideal; it doesn't fit music where the performance is the work.", "note": "Pronounced VAIRK-troy-uh. Very strong with musicologists; opaque elsewhere.", "level": 3}, {"id": "music-64", "field": "Music: the professional's vocabulary", "term": "the work-concept", "gloss": "The historically situated idea of a musical work as a fixed, composed object, which shapes how we value performance.", "say": "Jazz sits awkwardly with *the work-concept*; a standard is a vehicle, not a work in that sense.", "note": "Associated with Lydia Goehr's account of the work-concept; use it only if you can sustain the discussion.", "level": 3}, {"id": "music-65", "field": "Music: the professional's vocabulary", "term": "signifyin(g)", "gloss": "An African American rhetorical practice of repetition with critical difference, applied in scholarship to jazz quotation and revision.", "say": "Coltrane's 'My Favorite Things' is the classic case of *signifyin(g)*: the tune is honoured and transformed at once.", "note": "From African American literary theory, then jazz scholarship; use it with care and with knowledge of its lineage.", "level": 3}, {"id": "music-66", "field": "Music: the professional's vocabulary", "term": "participatory discrepancies", "gloss": "Small asynchronies and timbral discrepancies between players that generate groove and draw listeners in.", "say": "The slight push of the ride against the bass is the kind of *participatory discrepancy* that makes the groove.", "note": "An ethnomusicology term from Charles Keil; it gives academic weight to what players call feel.", "level": 3}, {"id": "music-67", "field": "Music: the professional's vocabulary", "term": "entrainment", "gloss": "The process by which players' and listeners' internal rhythms synchronise to an external pulse and to each other.", "say": "Ensemble time isn't imposed by the conductor; it's *entrainment*, and it can be trained.", "note": "A cognitive-science term; lands with researchers and makes time feel a teachable construct.", "level": 3}, {"id": "music-69", "field": "Music: the professional's vocabulary", "term": "tempo giusto", "gloss": "The right tempo, or a strict tempo, implied by the character and style of the music rather than given by a marking.", "say": "The chart says 'medium swing', but the *tempo giusto* is the Basie tempo, and the students need to hear it.", "note": "Pronounced TEM-po JOO-sto. Elegant in classical rooms; affected if used with jazz players.", "level": 3}, {"id": "music-71", "field": "Music: the professional's vocabulary", "term": "heterophony", "gloss": "Simultaneous variation of a single melody by several performers, each ornamenting it differently.", "say": "The front line in early New Orleans style works close to *heterophony*, three versions of one tune at once.", "note": "Pronounced het-er-OFF-o-nee. An ethnomusicological term that shows breadth.", "level": 3}, {"id": "music-72", "field": "Music: the professional's vocabulary", "term": "Klangfarbenmelodie", "gloss": "Melody of tone colours: a line shaped by changing timbres, from Schoenberg's Harmonielehre.", "say": "Ellington's mutes and plungers create a kind of *Klangfarbenmelodie* long before the classical avant-garde made it a doctrine.", "note": "Pronounced KLAHNG-far-ben-meh-lo-DEE. Strong in a classical room, but the comparison needs care.", "level": 3}, {"id": "music-73", "field": "Music: the professional's vocabulary", "term": "agogic accent", "gloss": "Emphasis created by lengthening a note in time rather than by playing it louder.", "say": "The lead alto shapes the phrase with *agogic accent*, a little more time on the top note, not more volume.", "note": "Pronounced a-GOJ-ik. Precise in classical feedback, rarely heard in jazz rooms.", "level": 3}, {"id": "music-74", "field": "Music: the professional's vocabulary", "term": "orality", "gloss": "The character of a culture or tradition grounded in oral transmission, including its modes of memory, variation and authority.", "say": "Jazz has a primary *orality* with a literate overlay; our assessment model has it backwards.", "note": "Borrowed from oral-literary theory; use it in academic rooms where the framing will be followed.", "level": 3}, {"id": "music-75", "field": "Music: the professional's vocabulary", "term": "the recording as primary text", "gloss": "In jazz, the canonical recording rather than the score functions as the authoritative text of a piece.", "say": "In this music we should treat *the recording as primary text*; a band that only reads the chart is reading a summary.", "note": "Clarifies a key difference from the classical tradition; useful when panels ask why scores are insufficient.", "level": 3}, {"id": "music-76", "field": "Music: the professional's vocabulary", "term": "polymeter versus polyrhythm", "gloss": "Polyrhythm sets conflicting rhythmic divisions within a shared metre; polymeter sets different metres with different bar lengths simultaneously.", "say": "Strictly it's *polyrhythm*, three against four within the bar, not *polymeter*; the bar lines still coincide.", "note": "Often confused even by trained musicians; get it exactly right or leave it alone.", "level": 3}, {"id": "music-77", "field": "Music: the professional's vocabulary", "term": "the changing same", "gloss": "Amiri Baraka's phrase for the continuity of Black musical tradition through constant stylistic change.", "say": "The tradition is what Baraka called *the changing same*; teaching it as a museum misses the point.", "note": "Attribute it when you use it; it lands in jazz scholarship and cultural-policy rooms.", "level": 3}, {"id": "music-78", "field": "Music: the professional's vocabulary", "term": "notes inégales", "gloss": "The French Baroque convention of playing written equal notes unequally, an often-cited analogy for swing.", "say": "Swing isn't exotic to the classical tradition; *notes inégales* is a written convention played otherwise, just as in a swing chart.", "note": "Pronounced nawt een-ay-GAHL. Use the analogy loosely; the two practices are not identical.", "level": 3}, {"id": "music-79", "field": "Music: the professional's vocabulary", "term": "intertextuality", "gloss": "The way a work draws meaning from its relationships to other works, through quotation, allusion and revision.", "say": "Quotation in a solo is *intertextuality*, a dialogue with the tradition, not merely a joke.", "note": "A literary-theory term; it can sound inflated unless the example is concrete.", "level": 3}, {"id": "music-80", "field": "Music: the professional's vocabulary", "term": "Klangideal", "gloss": "The ideal sound of an ensemble, instrument or tradition, held as an aesthetic norm by its practitioners.", "say": "Every great band has a *Klangideal*; ours is closer to the Thad Jones and Mel Lewis Orchestra than to the festival sound.", "note": "Pronounced KLAHNG-ee-day-AHL. Rare; use it with classical and organological specialists.", "level": 3}]}, {"field": "University governance and academic life", "intro": "The register is procedural, understated and exact: senior academics win by routing a matter to the right body, naming the test that applies and asking the one question the paper avoids. Sound native by speaking in dispositions, delegations and evidence rather than opinions, and by letting the terms of reference do the arguing.", "entries": [{"id": "university-governance-1", "field": "University governance and academic life", "term": "academic governance", "gloss": "The oversight of academic quality and standards (courses, assessment, research integrity) by academic bodies, as distinct from corporate governance of finance, risk and strategy by the governing body.", "say": "That is a question of *academic governance*, so it belongs at Academic Board before it goes anywhere near Council.", "note": "Lands when you are routing an item to the right body. Backfires if used to wall off a resourcing question that is really corporate.", "level": 1}, {"id": "university-governance-2", "field": "University governance and academic life", "term": "self-accrediting authority", "gloss": "The power of a registered provider, which every Australian university holds, to accredit its own courses rather than having each one accredited by TEQSA.", "say": "Because the university has *self-accrediting authority*, the burden of rigour sits with this committee, not with Canberra.", "note": "Use it to remind a committee that it is the last line of quality assurance. Do not imply TEQSA approves individual university courses.", "level": 2}, {"id": "university-governance-3", "field": "University governance and academic life", "term": "the Threshold Standards", "gloss": "The Higher Education Standards Framework (Threshold Standards), the minimum standards every provider must meet to be registered and to offer accredited courses.", "say": "I would want to be confident this proposal meets the *Threshold Standards* on staffing before we worry about whether it is ambitious.", "note": "Insiders say 'the Threshold Standards' or 'the HESF'. 'Threshold' signals a floor, so do not cite it as a mark of excellence.", "level": 1}, {"id": "university-governance-4", "field": "University governance and academic life", "term": "risk-based and proportionate regulation", "gloss": "TEQSA's statutory approach of regulating according to a provider's risk profile and in proportion to that risk, rather than applying identical scrutiny to all.", "say": "TEQSA's approach is *risk-based and proportionate*, so a long record of compliance buys us latitude, not exemption.", "note": "Accurate and quietly authoritative. Avoid quoting section numbers unless you have them in front of you.", "level": 2}, {"id": "university-governance-5", "field": "University governance and academic life", "term": "remit", "gloss": "The scope of matters a body is empowered to deal with, as set by its terms of reference.", "say": "I am not sure workload falls within this committee's *remit*; I would rather we referred it than resolved it.", "note": "A cleaner word than 'jurisdiction' for committees. Pronounced REE-mit as a noun.", "level": 1}, {"id": "university-governance-6", "field": "University governance and academic life", "term": "delegated authority", "gloss": "Power formally passed from a governing body to a committee or officer, exercisable only within the terms and limits of the instrument of delegation.", "say": "The Dean can approve the change under *delegated authority*, but only if it does not alter the learning outcomes.", "note": "Lands when you can name the instrument. Backfires if asserted vaguely: someone will ask to see the delegations schedule.", "level": 1}, {"id": "university-governance-8", "field": "University governance and academic life", "term": "standing committee versus working party", "gloss": "A standing committee is a permanent body with continuing terms of reference; a working party is time-limited, set up to report on a defined question and then dissolve.", "say": "This needs a *working party* with a reporting date, not another *standing committee* that meets forever.", "note": "Shows you think about the life cycle of a body. Useful for killing scope creep.", "level": 1}, {"id": "university-governance-9", "field": "University governance and academic life", "term": "quorate", "gloss": "Having the minimum number of members present for a meeting's decisions to be valid; its opposite is inquorate.", "say": "Before we vote, Chair, could we confirm we are still *quorate*? Two members left at the break.", "note": "Precise and courteous. Raise it before the vote, not after, or it looks tactical.", "level": 1}, {"id": "university-governance-10", "field": "University governance and academic life", "term": "for noting, for discussion, for decision", "gloss": "The three standard dispositions of an agenda item: information received, matters canvassed without resolution, and matters requiring a resolution.", "say": "Can we be clear whether this paper is *for noting* or *for decision*? The cover sheet says one and the recommendation says the other.", "note": "Asking this early is the mark of an experienced committee member. Overused, it sounds procedural for its own sake.", "level": 2}, {"id": "university-governance-11", "field": "University governance and academic life", "term": "starred items", "gloss": "Agenda items reserved for discussion at a meeting; unstarred items are taken together and adopted without debate.", "say": "I would like to *star* item seven; the recommendation has implications for the conservatorium that the paper does not mention.", "note": "Practice varies by institution, so learn the local convention first. Starring sparingly preserves credibility.", "level": 2}, {"id": "university-governance-13", "field": "University governance and academic life", "term": "to have one's dissent recorded", "gloss": "To ask that the minutes note one's opposition to a resolution by name, which matters where members may later be held responsible for a decision.", "say": "I accept the decision of the committee, but I would ask that my *dissent be recorded*.", "note": "Powerful precisely because it is rare. Use it once a year at most, and say it without heat.", "level": 3}, {"id": "university-governance-16", "field": "University governance and academic life", "term": "actual, perceived and potential conflicts", "gloss": "The standard taxonomy of conflicts of interest: one that exists, one a reasonable observer might think exists, and one that could arise.", "say": "It may only be a *perceived conflict*, but perception is exactly what the minutes need to address.", "note": "The 'perceived' limb is the one novices dismiss and seniors take seriously.", "level": 1}, {"id": "university-governance-19", "field": "University governance and academic life", "term": "the hearing rule and the bias rule", "gloss": "The two limbs of natural justice: the right to be heard before an adverse decision, and the right to an impartial decision-maker.", "say": "We have satisfied the *hearing rule*; my worry is the *bias rule*, given who chairs the panel.", "note": "Splitting the doctrine like this signals legal literacy. Keep it for appeal and grievance settings.", "level": 3}, {"id": "university-governance-20", "field": "University governance and academic life", "term": "academic judgement", "gloss": "An evaluation of the quality of academic work that review bodies generally decline to second-guess, confining appeals to process, procedure and evidence of bias.", "say": "The appeal cannot reopen the *academic judgement* of the examiners; it can only ask whether the process was followed.", "note": "Clarifies the scope of any appeal instantly. Do not hide a procedural defect behind it.", "level": 2}, {"id": "university-governance-21", "field": "University governance and academic life", "term": "collegiality", "gloss": "Governance and decision-making by peers through deliberation and consent, as distinct from line management; also the expectation of professional good faith among colleagues.", "say": "I value *collegiality*, but collegial does not mean unanimous, and it does not mean nobody decides.", "note": "Lands when defending deliberation. Backfires if it sounds like a demand that colleagues stop disagreeing with you.", "level": 1}, {"id": "university-governance-26", "field": "University governance and academic life", "term": "the three lines model", "gloss": "A risk governance framework distinguishing operational management (first line), risk and compliance functions (second) and independent internal audit (third).", "say": "Our moderation process is a *first line* control; nobody in the *second line* has looked at it.", "note": "Lands with audit and risk committees. Means nothing to a faculty board, so translate it there.", "level": 3}, {"id": "university-governance-28", "field": "University governance and academic life", "term": "trustee, not delegate", "gloss": "The Burkean distinction between a representative who exercises independent judgement for the whole and one who carries instructions from those who elected them.", "say": "On Council I sit as a *trustee, not a delegate*, which is why I may vote against what the music staff would prefer.", "note": "Burke's distinction is well known to well-read colleagues. Name him only if the room is that kind of room.", "level": 3}, {"id": "university-governance-31", "field": "University governance and academic life", "term": "teach-out", "gloss": "The arrangement by which students already enrolled in a discontinued course are enabled to complete it, often with protections set out in policy.", "say": "Discontinuing the degree is defensible; the *teach-out* plan for current students is what Academic Board will scrutinise.", "note": "Shows you think about obligations to existing students, which regulators take seriously.", "level": 2}, {"id": "university-governance-32", "field": "University governance and academic life", "term": "disestablishment", "gloss": "The formal closure of a course, unit, position or organisational unit through governance processes.", "say": "This is a *disestablishment* in substance, whatever the paper calls it, and it should come to us as one.", "note": "Sharper than 'closure' and harder to euphemise. Use it when a cut is being disguised as a restructure.", "level": 2}, {"id": "university-governance-33", "field": "University governance and academic life", "term": "AQF level", "gloss": "The level of a qualification within the Australian Qualifications Framework, from Certificate I at level 1 to doctoral degrees at level 10, with bachelor degrees at level 7.", "say": "The learning outcomes as drafted read closer to *AQF level* 6 than 7; the verbs are about knowing, not judging.", "note": "Lands in course accreditation. Know the numbers cold: bachelor 7, honours and graduate diploma 8, masters 9, doctorate 10.", "level": 1}, {"id": "university-governance-34", "field": "University governance and academic life", "term": "volume of learning", "gloss": "The AQF's measure of the duration, in equivalent full-time years, typically required for a learner to achieve a qualification's outcomes.", "say": "A one-year masters for graduates without a cognate degree sits awkwardly with the AQF *volume of learning*.", "note": "A term insiders use in accreditation papers. Do not confuse it with contact hours.", "level": 2}, {"id": "university-governance-35", "field": "University governance and academic life", "term": "articulation", "gloss": "A formal arrangement giving students guaranteed admission and specified credit from one qualification into another.", "say": "An *articulation* agreement with the school program would give our strongest players a known pathway.", "note": "Precise in tertiary admissions. In secondary rooms it may be mistaken for the musical sense, so frame it.", "level": 2}, {"id": "university-governance-36", "field": "University governance and academic life", "term": "course learning outcomes versus unit learning outcomes", "gloss": "Course outcomes describe what a graduate of the whole program can do; unit outcomes are the narrower, assessable outcomes of a single unit, mapped to the course outcomes.", "say": "The *unit learning outcomes* are fine; the problem is that nothing maps to the *course learning outcome* on collaboration.", "note": "Insiders talk in mappings. Lands when you have actually read the matrix.", "level": 1}, {"id": "university-governance-37", "field": "University governance and academic life", "term": "external referencing", "gloss": "Comparing a course's outcomes, standards or student achievement against those of other institutions or external benchmarks, as the Threshold Standards expect.", "say": "Has anyone done *external referencing* of the recital standard against another conservatorium?", "note": "A quality term that regulators recognise. Asking for it is a legitimate and non-hostile challenge.", "level": 2}, {"id": "university-governance-38", "field": "University governance and academic life", "term": "external examiner", "gloss": "An academic from outside the institution who examines a thesis or reviews assessment standards, providing independent judgement.", "say": "For a creative doctorate the choice of *external examiner* matters as much as the work.", "note": "Standard in HDR settings. In Australia doctoral theses are normally examined by external examiners without a viva, though practice is changing.", "level": 1}, {"id": "university-governance-39", "field": "University governance and academic life", "term": "higher degree by research", "gloss": "A research masters or doctorate, abbreviated HDR, examined primarily on a thesis or creative work with exegesis rather than on coursework.", "say": "Our *HDR* candidates in performance need supervisors who are active practitioners, not just published ones.", "note": "Say 'HDR' in university rooms; spell it out once elsewhere.", "level": 1}, {"id": "university-governance-40", "field": "University governance and academic life", "term": "confirmation of candidature", "gloss": "The formal early milestone at which a research candidate's proposal is reviewed and their continued enrolment confirmed.", "say": "The *confirmation of candidature* panel should include someone who understands practice-led methods.", "note": "The key gate in Australian doctoral programs. Terminology varies slightly by university.", "level": 2}, {"id": "university-governance-41", "field": "University governance and academic life", "term": "exegesis", "gloss": "The written component accompanying a creative work in a practice-based research degree, situating and theorising the work as research.", "say": "The recordings are outstanding; the *exegesis* has to show what they contribute to knowledge.", "note": "Pronounced ek-se-JEE-sis. Do not use it for any old program note.", "level": 2}, {"id": "university-governance-42", "field": "University governance and academic life", "term": "practice-led versus practice-based research", "gloss": "Commonly distinguished as research leading to new understanding about practice (practice-led) versus research where the creative artefact is itself the basis of the contribution (practice-based).", "say": "Is this project *practice-led* or *practice-based*? It changes what the examiners should be reading for.", "note": "The distinction is drawn differently by different scholars, so state the definition you are using.", "level": 3}, {"id": "university-governance-43", "field": "University governance and academic life", "term": "non-traditional research outputs", "gloss": "Research outputs other than publications, such as original creative works, live performances, recorded works and curated exhibitions; abbreviated NTROs.", "say": "A festival premiere is a legitimate *non-traditional research output* if it comes with a research statement.", "note": "Essential vocabulary for arts academics. Pronounced as letters or 'en-trows'; follow local habit.", "level": 2}, {"id": "university-governance-44", "field": "University governance and academic life", "term": "research statement", "gloss": "A short statement accompanying an NTRO that sets out its research background, contribution and significance.", "say": "Without a *research statement*, the album is a career achievement, not a research output.", "note": "Lands with research committees. Sounds bureaucratic to artists, so explain why it matters.", "level": 2}, {"id": "university-governance-45", "field": "University governance and academic life", "term": "research impact versus research engagement", "gloss": "Impact is the contribution research makes beyond academia, to the economy, society, culture or policy; engagement is the interaction with end users through which impact may occur.", "say": "Workshops with schools are *engagement*; a changed syllabus would be *impact*.", "note": "A distinction research offices care about. Conflating them weakens an impact case.", "level": 1}, {"id": "university-governance-46", "field": "University governance and academic life", "term": "pathway to impact", "gloss": "The described route by which research outcomes are expected to reach and benefit users beyond academia.", "say": "The application has excellent science and no *pathway to impact*; who is actually going to use this?", "note": "Standard in grant language. Ask it of others' proposals gently; it is a sore point.", "level": 2}, {"id": "university-governance-48", "field": "University governance and academic life", "term": "Category 1 income", "gloss": "Research income from Australian competitive grants, such as ARC and NHMRC schemes, under the national research income categories.", "say": "Arts faculties rarely carry much *Category 1 income*, which is exactly why our NTROs need to be documented.", "note": "Insider shorthand from research finance. Leave it out of rooms without university research staff.", "level": 3}, {"id": "university-governance-50", "field": "University governance and academic life", "term": "audit culture", "gloss": "The spread of auditing logics and performance indicators into professional and academic life, often displacing professional judgement with verifiable compliance.", "say": "I worry we are building an *audit culture* in which documenting the rehearsal matters more than the rehearsal.", "note": "Strathern's edited volume made the term current. Persuasive in faculty critique, but sounds sour if used against every reporting request.", "level": 3}, {"id": "university-governance-51", "field": "University governance and academic life", "term": "managerialism", "gloss": "The application of corporate management techniques and hierarchies to public and academic institutions, often at the expense of collegial authority.", "say": "The concern is not management, which we need, but *managerialism*, which treats academic judgement as a cost centre.", "note": "Distinguishing it from management is what keeps it from sounding like a complaint.", "level": 3}, {"id": "university-governance-52", "field": "University governance and academic life", "term": "workload model", "gloss": "The formal scheme by which an institution allocates and counts academic work across teaching, research and service, usually in hours or points.", "say": "One-to-one studio teaching is badly undercounted in the *workload model*; it is treated as a tutorial.", "note": "The single most consequential document in many academics' lives. Know your institution's units.", "level": 1}, {"id": "university-governance-53", "field": "University governance and academic life", "term": "the 40/40/20 split", "gloss": "The conventional nominal allocation for teaching-and-research academics of 40 per cent teaching, 40 per cent research and 20 per cent service.", "say": "On paper she is *40/40/20*; in practice the studio teaching swallows the research forty.", "note": "Widely understood shorthand. Do not present it as a regulatory requirement; it is a convention.", "level": 2}, {"id": "university-governance-54", "field": "University governance and academic life", "term": "teaching and research versus education-focused roles", "gloss": "Academic roles with research expectations versus roles weighted to teaching and educational scholarship with little or no research allocation.", "say": "Converting performers to *education-focused* roles saves money, but it removes them from the research case.", "note": "Current Australian terminology varies (teaching-focused, teaching-specialist). Use the local term.", "level": 1}, {"id": "university-governance-55", "field": "University governance and academic life", "term": "service", "gloss": "The component of academic work comprising committee work, leadership, professional and community contribution.", "say": "Running the festival is real *service* to the discipline, and the workload model should say so.", "note": "In academic usage it is a category of work, not a favour. Do not let it be treated as invisible.", "level": 1}, {"id": "university-governance-56", "field": "University governance and academic life", "term": "sessional staff", "gloss": "Academic staff employed casually or on short contracts, typically paid by the hour for teaching.", "say": "Most of the instrumental teaching is done by *sessional staff*, and their continuity is the quality issue nobody minutes.", "note": "'Sessional' is the respectful term; 'casuals' is common but blunter.", "level": 1}, {"id": "university-governance-57", "field": "University governance and academic life", "term": "Commonwealth supported place", "gloss": "A university place for which the Australian Government pays part of the cost and the student pays a student contribution, abbreviated CSP.", "say": "If the new major is not funded as a *Commonwealth supported place*, it will be full-fee and the enrolments will tell us so.", "note": "Basic sector literacy. Funding cluster details change, so do not quote rates from memory.", "level": 1}, {"id": "university-governance-58", "field": "University governance and academic life", "term": "EFTSL", "gloss": "Equivalent full-time student load: the standard measure of a student's study load, used for funding and planning.", "say": "The proposal adds headcount but almost no *EFTSL*; the money follows load, not bodies.", "note": "Pronounced EFF-tsl by some and spelt out by others. Lands with planning and finance people.", "level": 2}, {"id": "university-governance-60", "field": "University governance and academic life", "term": "the studio model", "gloss": "Conservatorium teaching built on one-to-one tuition between a principal study teacher and student, in the master and apprentice tradition.", "say": "The *studio model* is expensive, but it is also the pedagogy the profession recognises.", "note": "Defend it with outcomes, not nostalgia. 'Master and apprentice' can sound gendered; 'principal teacher' avoids it.", "level": 2}, {"id": "university-governance-61", "field": "University governance and academic life", "term": "principal study", "gloss": "The student's major instrument, voice or creative discipline in a conservatorium degree, carrying the largest weighting.", "say": "Ensemble credit should not be cannibalised to give more hours to *principal study*.", "note": "Native terminology in Australian conservatoria. 'Major study' is used in some institutions.", "level": 1}, {"id": "university-governance-62", "field": "University governance and academic life", "term": "conservatorium versus conservatoire", "gloss": "Australian institutions generally style themselves conservatorium; conservatoire is the British and French form; both denote a specialist music school.", "say": "The *conservatorium* is embedded in a university, which changes its governance entirely from a stand-alone *conservatoire*.", "note": "Use the institution's own name. Conservatoire is pronounced con-SER-va-twah.", "level": 2}, {"id": "university-governance-63", "field": "University governance and academic life", "term": "embedded institution", "gloss": "A specialist school, such as a conservatorium or academy, that sits within a larger university and is subject to its governance and budget rules.", "say": "Because we are an *embedded institution*, our case has to be made in the university's language, not the profession's.", "note": "Useful framing for strategy discussions; avoid it if it sounds like a complaint about the host.", "level": 3}, {"id": "university-governance-64", "field": "University governance and academic life", "term": "panel examination", "gloss": "Assessment of a performance by a panel of examiners rather than by the student's own teacher, intended to secure independence and comparability.", "say": "A *panel examination* protects the student as much as the standard; nobody marks their own studio.", "note": "In US usage this is a jury. Say 'panel' in Australia.", "level": 2}, {"id": "university-governance-65", "field": "University governance and academic life", "term": "Chancellor versus Vice-Chancellor", "gloss": "The Chancellor chairs the governing body and presides at ceremonies; the Vice-Chancellor is the chief executive and senior academic officer.", "say": "That decision is the *Vice-Chancellor's*; the *Chancellor* chairs Council and should not be lobbied on it.", "note": "Outsiders confuse them constantly. Getting it right is a small, reliable signal.", "level": 1}, {"id": "university-governance-66", "field": "University governance and academic life", "term": "Senate, Council and Academic Board", "gloss": "The governing body of an Australian university is usually its Council (at UWA, its Senate); the Academic Board, or Academic Senate in some universities, is the peak academic body reporting to it.", "say": "At UWA the *Senate* is the governing body, so do not assume it is the academic forum it would be elsewhere.", "note": "Nomenclature varies by statute; check each institution's Act before using it in writing.", "level": 2}, {"id": "university-governance-67", "field": "University governance and academic life", "term": "emeritus", "gloss": "An honorary title retained by a retired professor in recognition of distinguished service.", "say": "Professor *Emeritus*, or *Emeritus* Professor, depending on the university's style.", "note": "Pronounced e-MER-i-tus. It is honorific, not a rank of current employment.", "level": 2}, {"id": "university-governance-68", "field": "University governance and academic life", "term": "honoris causa", "gloss": "For the sake of honour: designating a degree conferred as an honour rather than earned by study.", "say": "She holds a doctorate *honoris causa*, so 'Dr' is a courtesy many recipients choose not to use.", "note": "Ceremonial register. Pronounced on-OR-is COW-za. Overused in conversation it sounds pompous.", "level": 3}, {"id": "university-governance-69", "field": "University governance and academic life", "term": "the academy", "gloss": "The community of scholars and the institutions of higher learning considered collectively.", "say": "The *academy* tends to underrate the knowledge held by practising musicians who never wrote it down.", "note": "Lands in reflective and strategic talk. In a funding meeting it can sound aloof.", "level": 2}, {"id": "university-governance-70", "field": "University governance and academic life", "term": "academic freedom", "gloss": "The freedom of academic staff to teach, research and publish according to scholarly judgement, and to express views about their institution, without institutional penalty.", "say": "*Academic freedom* protects the scholarly judgement; it does not protect a refusal to teach the curriculum.", "note": "Draw its boundaries yourself before someone else does. Distinct from general freedom of speech.", "level": 1}, {"id": "university-governance-71", "field": "University governance and academic life", "term": "the French Model Code", "gloss": "The Model Code for the Protection of Freedom of Speech and Academic Freedom, recommended in Robert French's 2019 review and adopted in some form by Australian universities.", "say": "Our draft policy should be read against the *French Model Code*; it is the benchmark the sector adopted.", "note": "Accurate and current. Lands in policy committees; overkill in a staff meeting.", "level": 3}, {"id": "university-governance-72", "field": "University governance and academic life", "term": "institutional autonomy", "gloss": "The freedom of a university as an institution to govern its own academic affairs, admissions, appointments and curriculum without external direction.", "say": "Accepting the tied funding is legal, but it chips at *institutional autonomy*, and we should say that we know it.", "note": "Distinguish it from academic freedom, which belongs to individuals; confusing the two is a common slip.", "level": 2}, {"id": "university-governance-73", "field": "University governance and academic life", "term": "peer review", "gloss": "Evaluation of scholarly or creative work by experts in the same field before publication, funding or acceptance.", "say": "The festival commission went through *peer review*, which is what lets us count it as research.", "note": "Name the type where it matters: double-blind, single-blind, open.", "level": 1}, {"id": "university-governance-74", "field": "University governance and academic life", "term": "double-blind review", "gloss": "Peer review in which neither the author nor the reviewers know each other's identity; single-blind conceals only the reviewers.", "say": "In a field this small, *double-blind review* is more aspiration than reality; everyone recognises the playing.", "note": "Shows you understand the limits as well as the ideal. 'Anonymised' is the increasingly preferred term.", "level": 2}, {"id": "university-governance-75", "field": "University governance and academic life", "term": "research integrity versus academic integrity", "gloss": "Research integrity concerns the responsible conduct of research by researchers; academic integrity concerns honesty in students' and staff learning and assessment work.", "say": "That is an *academic integrity* matter for the unit coordinator, not a *research integrity* matter for the Research Office.", "note": "Routing the matter correctly is half the competence. Both have separate policies in every university.", "level": 1}, {"id": "university-governance-76", "field": "University governance and academic life", "term": "the Australian Code", "gloss": "The Australian Code for the Responsible Conduct of Research (2018), the national principles-based framework for research integrity.", "say": "Under the *Australian Code*, authorship follows contribution; rank does not earn a byline.", "note": "Insiders call it 'the Code'. Supported by guides on specific topics such as authorship and breaches.", "level": 2}, {"id": "university-governance-77", "field": "University governance and academic life", "term": "low and negligible risk", "gloss": "A category of human research under the National Statement on Ethical Conduct in Human Research where the foreseeable risk is no more than discomfort, allowing lighter ethics review.", "say": "Surveying our own ensemble students is probably *low and negligible risk*, but the power relationship still has to be addressed.", "note": "Abbreviated LNR. The National Statement is the governing document; the HREC decides, not the researcher.", "level": 2}, {"id": "university-governance-78", "field": "University governance and academic life", "term": "epistemic community", "gloss": "A network of professionals with recognised expertise and shared standards of validity in a domain, whose authority rests on that shared judgement.", "say": "Jazz performers are an *epistemic community* with rigorous standards; they simply do not write them in journal articles.", "note": "Precise and useful in arguing for practitioner knowledge. Affected if used outside scholarly settings.", "level": 3}, {"id": "university-governance-79", "field": "University governance and academic life", "term": "the Universities Accord", "gloss": "The Australian Universities Accord, the national review of higher education whose final report was released in February 2024.", "say": "The *Accord* talks about equity targets; a selective music pathway is one place we could actually show what that means.", "note": "Recommendations have been adopted unevenly, so speak of its recommendations rather than its outcomes.", "level": 2}, {"id": "university-governance-80", "field": "University governance and academic life", "term": "constituency", "gloss": "The group a member is understood to represent or draw legitimacy from, whose interests are distinct from the institution's as a whole.", "say": "I hear the music staff as a *constituency*, but Council has to weigh them against every other one.", "note": "Lands when you want to acknowledge a group without being captured by it.", "level": 3}]}, {"field": "Curriculum, assessment and standards", "intro": "This is a field where precise distinctions carry the argument: validity against reliability, standard against criterion, comparable against identical. Sound native by treating every result as an inference, every cut score as a judgement and every design choice as a trade-off that can be named.", "entries": [{"id": "curriculum-assessment-1", "field": "Curriculum, assessment and standards", "term": "standards-referenced assessment", "gloss": "Judging student work against described standards of quality, such as grade descriptions, so that a grade reports what was achieved regardless of how others performed.", "say": "A *standards-referenced* system means a whole cohort can earn an A, and we should not apologise for that.", "note": "The SCSA frame for WACE courses. Distinguish it from criterion-referenced, which it is often loosely called.", "level": 1}, {"id": "curriculum-assessment-2", "field": "Curriculum, assessment and standards", "term": "norm-referenced assessment", "gloss": "Interpreting a result by its position relative to other candidates, so that grades are distributed by rank rather than against a fixed standard.", "say": "Allocating a fixed number of A grades per ensemble is *norm-referencing* by stealth.", "note": "Lands when exposing a quota masquerading as a standard. Do not confuse it with scaling, which is a different operation.", "level": 1}, {"id": "curriculum-assessment-3", "field": "Curriculum, assessment and standards", "term": "criterion-referenced versus standards-referenced", "gloss": "Criterion-referenced assessment checks whether specified criteria are met, often as pass or fail; standards-referenced assessment judges the quality with which they are met against described levels.", "say": "A checklist tells you whether the scale was played; it is *criterion-referenced*. The standard is about how well.", "note": "Sadler's work underpins much of the Australian usage. Getting this distinction right marks you as an assessment person.", "level": 2}, {"id": "curriculum-assessment-4", "field": "Curriculum, assessment and standards", "term": "construct", "gloss": "The underlying attribute an assessment is designed to measure, such as aural discrimination or stylistic understanding, inferred rather than directly observed.", "say": "What is the *construct* here? If it is improvisation, a transcription test is measuring something else.", "note": "The first question in any validity conversation. Asking it calmly usually reorganises the discussion.", "level": 1}, {"id": "curriculum-assessment-5", "field": "Curriculum, assessment and standards", "term": "validity", "gloss": "The degree to which evidence and theory support the interpretations and uses of assessment results; a property of inferences, not of instruments.", "say": "The test is not *valid* or invalid in itself; the question is whether this inference from these scores is justified.", "note": "The modern unitary view, associated with Messick. Saying 'a valid test' marks an outsider in expert rooms.", "level": 1}, {"id": "curriculum-assessment-6", "field": "Curriculum, assessment and standards", "term": "reliability", "gloss": "The consistency of assessment results across occasions, tasks, markers or forms; a necessary but not sufficient condition for valid interpretation.", "say": "The marking is highly *reliable*, which tells us markers agree; it does not tell us they are rewarding the right thing.", "note": "The validity and reliability distinction is the core insider marker. Do not use reliability to mean trustworthiness in general.", "level": 1}, {"id": "curriculum-assessment-7", "field": "Curriculum, assessment and standards", "term": "the trade-off between reliability and validity", "gloss": "The tension whereby tightening task conditions and criteria to increase marking consistency can narrow what is assessed and weaken the construct.", "say": "Every step that made the composition task more marker-proof made it less like composition; that is the *trade-off between reliability and validity*.", "note": "A senior argument for authentic tasks. Concede that some reliability is non-negotiable in high-stakes settings.", "level": 2}, {"id": "curriculum-assessment-8", "field": "Curriculum, assessment and standards", "term": "construct-irrelevant variance", "gloss": "Variation in results caused by factors other than the target construct, such as reading load, test format or performance anxiety.", "say": "The written aural paper carries a lot of *construct-irrelevant variance*; weaker readers score lower on listening.", "note": "Messick's term, and a precise weapon in syllabus review. Heavy for a parent audience.", "level": 3}, {"id": "curriculum-assessment-9", "field": "Curriculum, assessment and standards", "term": "construct under-representation", "gloss": "The failure of an assessment to sample important aspects of the construct, so that results reflect only part of what is claimed.", "say": "A twelve-bar solo on one standard is *construct under-representation* if we are claiming to assess improvisation.", "note": "Pairs naturally with construct-irrelevant variance; use the pair to frame validity threats.", "level": 3}, {"id": "curriculum-assessment-10", "field": "Curriculum, assessment and standards", "term": "consequential validity", "gloss": "The aspect of validity concerned with the intended and unintended consequences of assessment use, including effects on teaching and learning.", "say": "The *consequential validity* of dropping the practical exam is the decline in instrumental enrolments we would see within two years.", "note": "Messick placed consequences within validity; some theorists dispute that. Acknowledge the debate if challenged.", "level": 3}, {"id": "curriculum-assessment-11", "field": "Curriculum, assessment and standards", "term": "face validity", "gloss": "Whether an assessment appears, to non-specialists, to measure what it claims; not a technical form of validity.", "say": "The task has strong *face validity* with parents, which is useful, but it is not evidence that it measures the construct.", "note": "Use it to separate optics from evidence. Claiming face validity as a strength in an expert room backfires.", "level": 2}, {"id": "curriculum-assessment-12", "field": "Curriculum, assessment and standards", "term": "content validity", "gloss": "The extent to which an assessment samples the content and skills of the domain in appropriate proportion; now usually treated as content-related evidence for validity.", "say": "Two set works out of eight is thin *content-related evidence*; students can bet on which ones appear.", "note": "The phrasing 'content-related evidence' signals current thinking.", "level": 2}, {"id": "curriculum-assessment-13", "field": "Curriculum, assessment and standards", "term": "argument-based validation", "gloss": "The approach, associated with Michael Kane, of stating the chain of inferences from performance to score to interpretation to decision, and testing each link.", "say": "Using *argument-based validation*, the weak link is from the performance to the score, not from the score to the grade.", "note": "Lands with measurement specialists at SCSA level. Too technical for most school rooms.", "level": 3}, {"id": "curriculum-assessment-14", "field": "Curriculum, assessment and standards", "term": "evidence-centred design", "gloss": "A framework, associated with Robert Mislevy and colleagues, that builds assessments from the claims to be made, through the evidence needed, to the tasks that elicit it.", "say": "If we used *evidence-centred design*, we would start with the claim about the student and only then write the task.", "note": "Accurate and current. Name it once; then talk in its logic of claim, evidence and task.", "level": 3}, {"id": "curriculum-assessment-16", "field": "Curriculum, assessment and standards", "term": "standard error of measurement", "gloss": "The estimated spread of a candidate's observed scores around their true score, quantifying the imprecision of an individual result.", "say": "A two-mark difference is inside the *standard error of measurement*; we cannot rank those students meaningfully.", "note": "Decisive in arguments about fine-grained ranking. Use only if you can explain it in one sentence.", "level": 2}, {"id": "curriculum-assessment-17", "field": "Curriculum, assessment and standards", "term": "generalisability", "gloss": "The extent to which a result on a particular set of tasks, markers and occasions would hold across the wider universe of possible ones; formalised in generalisability theory.", "say": "One recital, one panel, one day: the *generalisability* of that mark is lower than its precision suggests.", "note": "Technical register. Spelling: Australian English uses 's'.", "level": 3}, {"id": "curriculum-assessment-18", "field": "Curriculum, assessment and standards", "term": "inter-rater reliability", "gloss": "The consistency of judgements between different markers assessing the same work.", "say": "Before we argue about the rubric, let us check *inter-rater reliability* on five recordings.", "note": "Proposing an empirical check usually ends unproductive debate.", "level": 1}, {"id": "curriculum-assessment-19", "field": "Curriculum, assessment and standards", "term": "intra-rater reliability", "gloss": "The consistency of a single marker's judgements across time, order and fatigue.", "say": "Marks drop after the fortieth script; that is an *intra-rater reliability* problem, not a cohort problem.", "note": "Less commonly named than inter-rater, so it signals depth.", "level": 2}, {"id": "curriculum-assessment-20", "field": "Curriculum, assessment and standards", "term": "rater severity and leniency", "gloss": "Systematic tendencies of individual markers to award lower or higher marks than others for equivalent work.", "say": "We know which examiner is *severe*; the question is whether the process corrects for it.", "note": "Frame as systematic, not personal, or it reads as an attack on a colleague.", "level": 2}, {"id": "curriculum-assessment-21", "field": "Curriculum, assessment and standards", "term": "halo effect", "gloss": "The tendency for a positive impression on one feature, or prior knowledge of the student, to raise judgements on unrelated criteria.", "say": "Blind marking of the written component would reduce the *halo effect* from their reputation as players.", "note": "Well understood; name the specific mechanism to keep it from sounding like a generic bias claim.", "level": 2}, {"id": "curriculum-assessment-23", "field": "Curriculum, assessment and standards", "term": "consensus moderation", "gloss": "Moderation in which teachers compare and discuss samples of work against the standard to reach agreed judgements.", "say": "*Consensus moderation* across schools would do more for our practical marks than another rubric revision.", "note": "Builds assessment literacy as a by-product, which is its strongest argument.", "level": 2}, {"id": "curriculum-assessment-24", "field": "Curriculum, assessment and standards", "term": "statistical moderation", "gloss": "Adjusting school-based marks using an external reference such as examination results, preserving the school's rank order while aligning its scale.", "say": "*Statistical moderation* keeps our rank order but moves our marks; so the rank order is what we have to get right.", "note": "This is how school marks in WACE ATAR courses are adjusted against the examination. The insight about rank order is what insiders understand.", "level": 2}, {"id": "curriculum-assessment-25", "field": "Curriculum, assessment and standards", "term": "comparability", "gloss": "The degree to which results from different schools, markers, tasks or years can be treated as reporting the same standard.", "say": "Without some cross-school sample, we have no evidence of *comparability*, only confidence.", "note": "Comparability is a claim that needs evidence. Saying so is fair and usually unanswerable.", "level": 1}, {"id": "curriculum-assessment-26", "field": "Curriculum, assessment and standards", "term": "comparable, not identical", "gloss": "The principle that comparability requires equivalence of demand and standard, not identical tasks or conditions.", "say": "The recital programs need to be *comparable, not identical*; a bassist and a flautist should not play the same piece.", "note": "Defuses the naive demand for uniformity. Be ready to explain how equivalence of demand will be judged.", "level": 3}, {"id": "curriculum-assessment-28", "field": "Curriculum, assessment and standards", "term": "exemplars", "gloss": "Selected pieces of student work that illustrate particular standards, used to communicate and stabilise judgement.", "say": "Our *exemplars* are all from strong students; we have nothing that shows what a C actually sounds like.", "note": "Exemplars should span the range, especially the borderlines. Say it.", "level": 1}, {"id": "curriculum-assessment-29", "field": "Curriculum, assessment and standards", "term": "grade descriptions", "gloss": "Statements describing the typical characteristics of achievement at each grade in a course, used to make and report on-balance judgements.", "say": "The *grade descriptions* talk about stylistic awareness; our tasks barely ask for it.", "note": "The SCSA instrument for standards-referenced grading. Treat them as descriptive of typical performance, not as checklists.", "level": 1}, {"id": "curriculum-assessment-30", "field": "Curriculum, assessment and standards", "term": "achievement standards", "gloss": "Descriptions in the Australian Curriculum of the expected quality of learning at each year level or band.", "say": "The *achievement standard* for Years 9 and 10 asks for evaluation; most of our assessment asks for description.", "note": "Use the exact phrase; 'outcomes' belongs to older frameworks.", "level": 1}, {"id": "curriculum-assessment-31", "field": "Curriculum, assessment and standards", "term": "marking key versus rubric", "gloss": "A marking key specifies how marks are awarded for a particular task or question; a rubric sets out criteria against levels of quality, often applicable across tasks.", "say": "The examination has a *marking key*; the practical needs a *rubric*, because the evidence is not item by item.", "note": "SCSA uses 'marking key' for examinations and tasks. Using the right term in the right setting is noticed.", "level": 1}, {"id": "curriculum-assessment-32", "field": "Curriculum, assessment and standards", "term": "analytic versus holistic marking", "gloss": "Analytic marking scores separate criteria and sums them; holistic marking makes a single overall judgement of the work.", "say": "*Analytic marking* is more reliable at the criterion level, but for performance a *holistic* judgement may be more valid.", "note": "Both have defenders. A senior position acknowledges what each trades away.", "level": 2}, {"id": "curriculum-assessment-33", "field": "Curriculum, assessment and standards", "term": "on-balance judgement", "gloss": "An overall judgement of which standard best describes the work as a whole, weighing strengths and weaknesses rather than adding parts.", "say": "Taken *on balance*, this is A-grade work; the weak sight-reading does not outweigh the rest.", "note": "The proper mode for using grade descriptions. Also called 'best fit'.", "level": 2}, {"id": "curriculum-assessment-34", "field": "Curriculum, assessment and standards", "term": "atomistic criteria", "gloss": "Criteria broken into many small, separately scored components, which can lose the qualities that only appear in the work as a whole.", "say": "The rubric has become *atomistic*; it can award high marks to a performance no musician would call good.", "note": "Sadler's critique of analytic decomposition gives this weight. It reads as critique, so offer an alternative.", "level": 3}, {"id": "curriculum-assessment-35", "field": "Curriculum, assessment and standards", "term": "criteria compliance", "gloss": "Harry Torrance's term for the tendency of explicit criteria and feedback to shift learning toward meeting the criteria rather than learning the domain.", "say": "We have taught them *criteria compliance*: they now write program notes that tick every descriptor and say nothing.", "note": "Accurate and pointed. Use it to argue against over-specification, not against criteria themselves.", "level": 3}, {"id": "curriculum-assessment-36", "field": "Curriculum, assessment and standards", "term": "connoisseurship", "gloss": "Elliot Eisner's notion of the expert's refined capacity to perceive and appreciate qualities in a domain, as the basis of educational judgement and criticism.", "say": "Judgement-based assessment rests on *connoisseurship*, which is why we cannot replace the panel with a checklist.", "note": "Lands with arts educators and assessment scholars. Elsewhere it can sound like special pleading for experts.", "level": 3}, {"id": "curriculum-assessment-37", "field": "Curriculum, assessment and standards", "term": "judgement-based assessment", "gloss": "Assessment in which qualified assessors make holistic or qualitative judgements of complex performances against standards, rather than scoring discrete items.", "say": "Music is a *judgement-based assessment* discipline; the job is to make the judgement defensible, not to remove it.", "note": "Positions expert judgement as rigorous rather than subjective. Pair it with moderation to be credible.", "level": 2}, {"id": "curriculum-assessment-38", "field": "Curriculum, assessment and standards", "term": "tacit knowledge of standards", "gloss": "Knowledge of quality that experienced assessors hold and use but cannot fully articulate in written criteria.", "say": "Much of the standard is *tacit*; exemplars and moderation are how we make it shareable.", "note": "Draws on Polanyi and on Sadler's work on standards. Do not use it to exempt judgements from scrutiny.", "level": 3}, {"id": "curriculum-assessment-39", "field": "Curriculum, assessment and standards", "term": "authentic assessment", "gloss": "Assessment tasks that replicate the demands, conditions and standards of real practice in the discipline.", "say": "The most *authentic assessment* we run is the jazz festival set, and it counts for nothing in the school marks.", "note": "Authenticity is a matter of degree. Saying a task is 'more authentic' is more defensible than calling it authentic.", "level": 1}, {"id": "curriculum-assessment-40", "field": "Curriculum, assessment and standards", "term": "performance assessment", "gloss": "Assessment requiring students to demonstrate a skill directly, through doing, rather than by selecting or describing.", "say": "The *performance assessment* carries half the weighting, so its reliability matters as much as the exam's.", "note": "Familiar to everyone; precision lies in what you claim about it.", "level": 1}, {"id": "curriculum-assessment-41", "field": "Curriculum, assessment and standards", "term": "washback", "gloss": "The effect of an assessment, especially a high-stakes one, on teaching and learning before it; also called backwash.", "say": "The *washback* from a listening exam built on set works is that schools stop teaching unfamiliar listening.", "note": "Neutral in itself: washback can be positive. Saying so shows command of the concept.", "level": 2}, {"id": "curriculum-assessment-44", "field": "Curriculum, assessment and standards", "term": "high-stakes assessment", "gloss": "Assessment whose results carry significant consequences for students, teachers or schools, such as tertiary entrance or accountability.", "say": "Because the exam is *high-stakes*, small reliability problems become large fairness problems.", "note": "Frames why rigour matters. Overused, it becomes a way of shutting down innovation.", "level": 2}, {"id": "curriculum-assessment-45", "field": "Curriculum, assessment and standards", "term": "cut score", "gloss": "The point on a score scale that separates one performance category from the next, set through a standard-setting process.", "say": "Where we put the *cut score* between B and A is a policy decision dressed as a measurement.", "note": "Insiders stress that cut scores are judgements. Saying so earns respect in standards committees.", "level": 2}, {"id": "curriculum-assessment-46", "field": "Curriculum, assessment and standards", "term": "standard setting", "gloss": "Structured methods by which panels of experts determine cut scores, typically by judging what borderline candidates can do.", "say": "We need a proper *standard setting* exercise for the new syllabus, not last year's boundaries carried over.", "note": "A distinct technical field. Asking for it shows you know cut scores do not set themselves.", "level": 2}, {"id": "curriculum-assessment-47", "field": "Curriculum, assessment and standards", "term": "the Angoff method", "gloss": "A standard-setting method in which judges estimate, item by item, the probability that a minimally competent candidate would answer correctly.", "say": "An *Angoff* exercise works for the theory paper; it does not translate to a recital.", "note": "Pronounced ANG-off. Name it only in a room that knows standard setting.", "level": 3}, {"id": "curriculum-assessment-48", "field": "Curriculum, assessment and standards", "term": "the bookmark method", "gloss": "A standard-setting method in which judges place a bookmark in a booklet of items ordered by difficulty at the point where a borderline candidate would stop succeeding.", "say": "With item response data, a *bookmark* procedure would make the boundary less arbitrary.", "note": "Technical, used by testing agencies. Overreach in most school rooms.", "level": 3}, {"id": "curriculum-assessment-49", "field": "Curriculum, assessment and standards", "term": "the borderline candidate", "gloss": "The hypothetical candidate just at the boundary of a performance category, whose likely performance anchors standard-setting judgements.", "say": "Before we argue about the A band, can we agree what the *borderline* A candidate sounds like?", "note": "Moves a panel from abstraction to a concrete reference point. Often a turning point in discussion.", "level": 3}, {"id": "curriculum-assessment-51", "field": "Curriculum, assessment and standards", "term": "the TEA and the ATAR", "gloss": "In WA, the Tertiary Entrance Aggregate is computed from a student's best scaled scores; the ATAR is a percentile rank derived from the TEA.", "say": "The *ATAR* is a rank, not a mark; a student cannot 'get 95 per cent' in it.", "note": "Lands with parents and principals alike. Avoid quoting bonus rules or exact formulas from memory.", "level": 2}, {"id": "curriculum-assessment-52", "field": "Curriculum, assessment and standards", "term": "ATAR course versus General course", "gloss": "In the WACE, ATAR courses are examinable and contribute to tertiary entrance; General courses are school-assessed and do not.", "say": "Moving a strong student from the *ATAR course* to the *General course* changes her entrance pathway, not her musicianship.", "note": "Basic WA sector literacy. Capitalise as SCSA does.", "level": 1}, {"id": "curriculum-assessment-53", "field": "Curriculum, assessment and standards", "term": "externally set task", "gloss": "In WACE General courses, a task set by SCSA and completed in Year 12 under school supervision, used to support comparability; abbreviated EST.", "say": "The *externally set task* gives us the one comparison point we have across General course schools.", "note": "WA-specific. Check current weighting and timing before citing them.", "level": 2}, {"id": "curriculum-assessment-54", "field": "Curriculum, assessment and standards", "term": "assessment outline", "gloss": "The school's document setting out, for a course, the assessment tasks, types, weightings and timing, as SCSA requires.", "say": "If the *assessment outline* says 30 per cent, the task cannot quietly become 20 per cent in Term 3.", "note": "A compliance document with real consequences in appeals. Treat amendments formally.", "level": 1}, {"id": "curriculum-assessment-55", "field": "Curriculum, assessment and standards", "term": "syllabus", "gloss": "The authority-issued document prescribing the content and, often, the assessment requirements of a course.", "say": "The *syllabus* prescribes the content; it is the school's program that decides sequence and emphasis.", "note": "In SCSA usage the syllabus is mandated. Distinguish it from the school's program and teaching outline.", "level": 1}, {"id": "curriculum-assessment-56", "field": "Curriculum, assessment and standards", "term": "scope and sequence", "gloss": "A document setting out what is taught (scope) and in what order and progression (sequence) across a year or stage.", "say": "Our *scope and sequence* introduces modes in Year 7 and never returns to them; that is a sequence problem.", "note": "Talk in both halves: scope and sequence fail in different ways.", "level": 1}, {"id": "curriculum-assessment-57", "field": "Curriculum, assessment and standards", "term": "the intended, enacted and attained curriculum", "gloss": "The distinction between what the syllabus specifies, what is actually taught, and what students actually learn.", "say": "The *intended curriculum* includes improvisation; the *enacted curriculum* in most schools does not.", "note": "From comparative studies such as TIMSS. A precise way to locate a problem without blaming the syllabus writers.", "level": 2}, {"id": "curriculum-assessment-59", "field": "Curriculum, assessment and standards", "term": "content descriptions versus elaborations", "gloss": "In the Australian Curriculum, content descriptions specify what is to be taught and are mandatory; elaborations are illustrative examples and are not.", "say": "That example is an *elaboration*, not a *content description*; we are not obliged to teach it.", "note": "A distinction that frequently settles curriculum arguments. Check whether a state has adapted the structure.", "level": 2}, {"id": "curriculum-assessment-60", "field": "Curriculum, assessment and standards", "term": "degree of prescription", "gloss": "How tightly a syllabus specifies content, works, methods or assessment, as against leaving them to schools.", "say": "A higher *degree of prescription* in set works would help comparability and hurt programs like ours.", "note": "Frames the policy trade-off neutrally. Good opening for a syllabus committee position.", "level": 1}, {"id": "curriculum-assessment-61", "field": "Curriculum, assessment and standards", "term": "powerful knowledge", "gloss": "Michael Young's term for specialised, disciplinary knowledge that allows students to think beyond their own experience, as an entitlement of schooling.", "say": "Harmony and voice-leading are *powerful knowledge*; leaving them out is not student-centred, it is a loss.", "note": "Persuasive in curriculum debates; contested by some as elitist. Be ready to defend breadth of repertoire too.", "level": 3}, {"id": "curriculum-assessment-62", "field": "Curriculum, assessment and standards", "term": "substantive versus disciplinary knowledge", "gloss": "Substantive knowledge is the content established in a discipline; disciplinary knowledge is how the discipline makes, tests and revises its claims.", "say": "Knowing the chord symbols is *substantive*; knowing how a musician decides a reharmonisation works is *disciplinary*.", "note": "Current in curriculum design discourse. Precise and non-partisan.", "level": 2}, {"id": "curriculum-assessment-63", "field": "Curriculum, assessment and standards", "term": "threshold concepts", "gloss": "Meyer and Land's idea of transformative, often troublesome concepts that, once grasped, reorganise a learner's understanding of a discipline.", "say": "Hearing function rather than chord quality is a *threshold concept*; students are different musicians on the other side.", "note": "Lands with university colleagues. Overclaiming every concept as a threshold one empties the term.", "level": 3}, {"id": "curriculum-assessment-64", "field": "Curriculum, assessment and standards", "term": "the SOLO taxonomy", "gloss": "Biggs and Collis's hierarchy of increasing structural complexity in responses, from prestructural through unistructural, multistructural and relational to extended abstract.", "say": "Most of the analysis answers are *multistructural*: lots of correct observations with nothing connecting them.", "note": "More useful than Bloom for marking extended responses. Know the five levels if you name it.", "level": 2}, {"id": "curriculum-assessment-65", "field": "Curriculum, assessment and standards", "term": "command terms", "gloss": "The verbs in a task or question, such as describe, analyse, evaluate, that specify the kind of thinking required.", "say": "The *command term* is 'evaluate', but the marking key rewards description; one of them has to change.", "note": "Lands in examination and marking key review. Precise and hard to argue with.", "level": 2}, {"id": "curriculum-assessment-66", "field": "Curriculum, assessment and standards", "term": "learning progression", "gloss": "An evidence-based description of the typical pathway by which learners develop increasing sophistication in a domain.", "say": "Without a *learning progression* for aural skills, our year-level standards are guesses.", "note": "Distinguish from a sequence of content. Good in scope and sequence work.", "level": 2}, {"id": "curriculum-assessment-67", "field": "Curriculum, assessment and standards", "term": "item facility and discrimination", "gloss": "Facility is the proportion of candidates answering an item correctly; discrimination is how well the item separates stronger from weaker candidates.", "say": "That question has high *facility* and almost no *discrimination*; everyone got it, so it told us nothing.", "note": "Item analysis language from examining. Use it in exam review panels, not with parents.", "level": 3}, {"id": "curriculum-assessment-68", "field": "Curriculum, assessment and standards", "term": "ceiling effect", "gloss": "When a task is too easy for a cohort, so that many score at or near the maximum and differences among them cannot be detected; the floor effect is its converse.", "say": "The sight-reading test has a *ceiling effect* for the specialist students; it cannot tell a good reader from an exceptional one.", "note": "Clear and concrete. Ask for the distribution rather than asserting it.", "level": 2}, {"id": "curriculum-assessment-69", "field": "Curriculum, assessment and standards", "term": "differential item functioning", "gloss": "When candidates of equal ability from different groups have different probabilities of success on an item, indicating possible bias.", "say": "Before we keep the jazz listening item, we should check it for *differential item functioning* across school types.", "note": "Technical; abbreviated DIF. Use it in examining contexts where data exist.", "level": 3}, {"id": "curriculum-assessment-70", "field": "Curriculum, assessment and standards", "term": "reasonable adjustment", "gloss": "A change to assessment conditions that enables a student with disability to demonstrate learning on the same basis as others, without changing the standard.", "say": "Extra time is a *reasonable adjustment*; lowering the standard of the recital would not be.", "note": "Grounded in the Disability Standards for Education 2005. The distinction between conditions and standard is the essential point.", "level": 1}, {"id": "curriculum-assessment-71", "field": "Curriculum, assessment and standards", "term": "fitness for purpose", "gloss": "The principle that an assessment's design should be judged against the specific decision or use it serves.", "say": "A task can be excellent for feedback and unfit for ranking; *fitness for purpose* is the question.", "note": "Good for resolving arguments where people are defending different purposes without realising it.", "level": 2}, {"id": "curriculum-assessment-72", "field": "Curriculum, assessment and standards", "term": "conjunctive versus compensatory scoring", "gloss": "Compensatory scoring lets strength in one component offset weakness in another; conjunctive scoring requires a minimum on each.", "say": "Under *compensatory scoring* a student can fail the performance and still pass the course; is that the standard we want?", "note": "Exposes a design choice most rooms have never noticed they made. Senior and precise.", "level": 3}, {"id": "curriculum-assessment-73", "field": "Curriculum, assessment and standards", "term": "ipsative assessment", "gloss": "Assessment against the learner's own previous performance rather than against others or a fixed standard.", "say": "*Ipsative* feedback suits the practice journal; it cannot carry a reported grade.", "note": "Pronounced IP-sa-tiv. Rare and useful; explain it briefly.", "level": 3}, {"id": "curriculum-assessment-74", "field": "Curriculum, assessment and standards", "term": "standards drift", "gloss": "The gradual change over time in the level of achievement associated with a grade or mark, without any deliberate decision.", "say": "Comparing this year's A recordings with 2019's would tell us whether we have *standards drift*.", "note": "Asking for evidence is better than asserting drift. Applies upward as well as downward.", "level": 2}, {"id": "curriculum-assessment-75", "field": "Curriculum, assessment and standards", "term": "assessment literacy", "gloss": "The knowledge and skill needed to design, use and interpret assessment soundly, including its limits.", "say": "The fastest way to raise *assessment literacy* in the department is to moderate together, not to run a workshop.", "note": "Can sound patronising if aimed at colleagues; frame it as collective.", "level": 2}, {"id": "curriculum-assessment-76", "field": "Curriculum, assessment and standards", "term": "ecological validity", "gloss": "The extent to which assessment conditions resemble the real settings in which the capability will be used.", "say": "Playing to an empty room for three examiners has limited *ecological validity* for a performer.", "note": "Borrowed from psychology. Distinguish it from authenticity, which concerns the task rather than the setting.", "level": 3}, {"id": "curriculum-assessment-77", "field": "Curriculum, assessment and standards", "term": "sufficiency of evidence", "gloss": "Whether enough evidence has been gathered, across tasks and occasions, to support the judgement being made.", "say": "One composition is not *sufficient evidence* to report a standard in composing for the year.", "note": "A quiet but decisive challenge to thin assessment programs.", "level": 2}, {"id": "curriculum-assessment-78", "field": "Curriculum, assessment and standards", "term": "triangulation", "gloss": "Drawing on several independent sources or types of evidence to support a judgement.", "say": "We have the exam, the recital and the portfolio; *triangulating* those is the real basis for the grade.", "note": "Useful with parents and principals. Keep the methodological sense; it is not just 'checking'.", "level": 1}, {"id": "curriculum-assessment-79", "field": "Curriculum, assessment and standards", "term": "assessment as accountability versus improvement", "gloss": "The tension between assessment used to judge schools and teachers and assessment used to improve learning, which often pull design in different directions.", "say": "This data was collected for *improvement* and is now being used for *accountability*; that changes how people will behave.", "note": "Precise framing for policy discussions. Avoid sounding as if accountability is illegitimate.", "level": 2}, {"id": "curriculum-assessment-80", "field": "Curriculum, assessment and standards", "term": "consultation draft", "gloss": "A version of a syllabus or policy released for formal comment before finalisation.", "say": "Our response to the *consultation draft* should propose wording, not just objections.", "note": "Practical process vocabulary. Proposing text is what moves authorities.", "level": 1}]}], "moves": [{"id": "the-reframe", "name": "The reframe", "family": "Framing", "what": "Changes the question the room believes it is answering, so that the options already on the table are judged against a different and better test.", "when": "When the discussion has settled into a binary that neither side can win, or when the room is about to produce a sound answer to the wrong question.", "teach": "A committee rarely disagrees about answers as much as it assumes it shares a question. Once a question has been posed and two or three people have spoken to it, its terms harden, and every later contribution is heard as for or against the matter as framed. The reframe works upstream of that. It does not argue for an option; it changes the test by which options are judged, so that the arguments already in the room rearrange themselves. Done well, people find they agree with you without having conceded anything, because you have not attacked their position. You have relocated it.\n\nWith highly educated audiences this is among the most efficient moves available. Academics and senior officials are trained to evaluate arguments within a frame and are often less vigilant about the frame itself. They also respect a frame shift as an intellectual contribution in its own right: it signals that you have been thinking about the problem rather than waiting for your turn.\n\nSenior practitioners execute it in three beats. First, state the current question accurately, in a form its proponents would accept: 'We have been treating this as a question of whether to keep the elective.' Second, offer the alternative as an observation, not a correction: 'I wonder whether it is really a question about what the course is for.' Third, show briefly what follows, one consequence rather than five. Then stop. The silence after a good reframe is the room recalculating, and filling it with elaboration lets people retreat to the familiar frame.\n\nTiming matters more than wording. Reframe too early and you seem to be dodging a question nobody yet finds difficult. Reframe too late, once a vote is foreshadowed, and it reads as obstruction. The ideal moment is when the second or third speaker repeats an argument already made, because repetition signals that the existing frame is exhausted.\n\nKeep it under forty-five seconds. Speak slightly slower than usual and a little lower, and address the chair or the whole table rather than the last speaker, so the move does not land as a rebuttal of one person. Stillness helps; a reframe delivered while leaning forward looks like a pitch.\n\nIt goes wrong in three ways. The clever reframe that is merely different: if the new question does not make the decision easier or better, it is a tangent with good posture. The hostile reframe, which implies the room has been foolish ('the real issue, which nobody has mentioned'). And the reframe that multiplies: a new question, then a qualification, then a second new question, until the room has nothing to hold.", "phrasings": ["We have been treating this as a question of cost. I wonder whether it is more usefully a question of purpose.", "Could I suggest a slightly different way of putting the question? If we ask what the course is for, rather than how it is weighted, several of the points already made fall into place.", "What strikes me is that both positions share an assumption: that the format has to stay as it is. If we set that aside, the choice looks rather different.", "Suppose we approached this not as a timetabling matter but as a decision about the status of the program. What would we then need to know?", "The arguments on both sides are good ones. I am not sure they are arguments about the same thing.", "Perhaps the question is less whether we should do this than what we would be committing ourselves to if we did.", "Seen from the point of view of the student who sits this course in three years' time, the question is not how much the section is worth but what it is evidence of.", "I would put the question this way, and I offer it tentatively: not whether this is risky, but whether it is the kind of risk we exist to take."], "avoid": ["With respect, I think everyone has missed the real issue here : it tells the room it has been foolish and turns the frame into a contest of status rather than ideas.", "I just want to throw something out there, and it might be completely off track, but : the hedge invites the room to dismiss the reframe before it has been heard.", "If I could step back and look at the meta-question, the paradigm we are operating within : abstraction and jargon signal performance and give the room no new question to hold.", "So it is not really about the elective at all, it is about who controls the timetable : a reframe that imputes motive converts a policy discussion into an accusation."], "scenarios": [{"id": "the-reframe-s1", "room": "SCSA syllabus committee", "setup": "A SCSA committee for Music is reviewing the written examination for the ATAR course. A university assessment specialist has tabled marker-consistency data showing that the aural section produces the widest spread between markers, and proposes reducing its weighting. Two members have already spoken for and against, largely on whether the data are robust. The decision will shape what teachers across the state prioritise for years.", "said": "I have no quarrel with aural skills as such. My concern is narrower and, I think, hard to answer: if a section of the paper cannot be marked consistently, it should not carry the weight it does. We owe candidates a result they can trust. Either we fix the marking or we reduce the exposure.", "task": "Move the question from marker reliability to what the examination is meant to be evidence of, without dismissing the reliability concern.", "seconds": 60, "models": [{"text": "I agree we owe candidates a result they can trust, and I don't want to wave the data away. But I think we've been treating this as a question about reliability, when underneath it is a question about validity. If aural perception is central to what the course claims to certify, then reducing its weight makes the result more consistent and less true. So perhaps the question for us is not how much weight the section deserves, but whether it is the right instrument for something we agree matters.", "why": "It grants the speaker's premise in full, then names the criterion the current frame has left out. It ends on a question both sides can work on together."}, {"text": "Could I put the question slightly differently? Suppose we asked what a teacher in Kalgoorlie will do in 2028 if we halve this weighting. My guess is that aural work quietly disappears from Year 11. If that's right, we're not really deciding a marking question; we're deciding what the subject is. That doesn't settle it, but it does suggest we should be looking at the design of the items before the weighting.", "why": "It reframes through consequence, using the effect of assessment on teaching, which makes an abstract point concrete and statewide. It proposes a next step rather than a verdict."}], "checks": ["I stated the reliability concern in a form its proponent would accept before shifting.", "I named the new question in a single sentence.", "I offered one consequence, not a list.", "I stopped talking once the new question was on the table."]}, {"id": "the-reframe-s2", "room": "school council", "setup": "At a school council meeting, the principal presents a proposal to increase the charge for the optional interstate tour attached to the specialist music program. A parent representative has argued, with figures, that the increase will price out families on lower incomes; the business manager has replied that the tour cannot be subsidised further without cutting elsewhere. The discussion has become a contest between affordability and solvency. You are a member of the council and the program is your responsibility.", "said": "I understand the budget is tight, and I'm not suggesting anyone is being careless. But every time we raise this charge we're told it's modest, and every time a few more families quietly withdraw. At some point we have to decide whether this tour is for the program or for the families who can afford it.", "task": "Shift the question from the size of the charge to what the tour is for, so the council can decide its design rather than its price.", "seconds": 60, "models": [{"text": "I think both points are right, and that's why we keep circling. We're arguing about price, but the price follows from a prior decision we've never made explicitly: is this tour a core part of the program's education, or an enrichment option for those who choose it? If it's core, we have to find a way for every student to go, and the design changes. If it's enrichment, a higher charge is defensible, but we should say so honestly to families. I'd suggest we settle that first.", "why": "It honours both speakers and exposes the unmade decision that sits beneath their disagreement. The two branches show the council exactly what each answer would commit it to."}, {"text": "Could I suggest we stop asking what the tour should cost and ask what it is for? If the answer is performing interstate, there may be a cheaper way to do that. If the answer is the cohort travelling and performing together, then the withdrawals the parent representative describes are the very thing the tour is meant to prevent. Either way, the charge becomes a consequence of the purpose rather than the subject of the argument.", "why": "It turns the parent's evidence into an argument about purpose, which takes it more seriously than the price frame did. The closing sentence names the shift explicitly so the room can follow it."}], "checks": ["I acknowledged both the affordability and the solvency case.", "I named a question that sits prior to price.", "I showed what each answer to that question would mean in practice.", "I did not propose a figure."]}, {"id": "the-reframe-s3", "room": "festival association board", "setup": "The board of the association that runs the state's schools' jazz festival is discussing a proposal from the festival director to cap entries at two bands per school, because last year's sessions ran up to ninety minutes late. A board member who is a long-serving principal opposes the cap as penalising schools that have built large programs. The director has replied that the venue and the adjudicators cannot stretch further. You are the treasurer.", "said": "I accept the schedule has become unmanageable. But the schools that would be hit by this cap are precisely the ones that have done what we've spent twenty years asking them to do: build depth. We'd be punishing success. If the problem is time, let's find more time, not fewer bands.", "task": "Move the board from a capacity argument to a question about what the festival exists to do.", "seconds": 60, "models": [{"text": "I think we've framed this as a capacity question, and on that framing there's no good answer: either the schedule breaks or the large programs lose out. But the cap is really a proxy for something we haven't discussed. Is the festival primarily a showcase of the state's strongest school bands, or a developmental event where every band receives expert feedback? Those purposes want different formats. If we can agree which comes first, the number of bands per school more or less decides itself.", "why": "It shows why the current frame cannot produce a good answer before offering a new one. The question it poses is one only the board can answer, which is where governance belongs."}, {"text": "Can I come at this from the treasury side, though not about money? Every option on the table assumes the festival has to look the way it does now: every band, one adjudication model, the same session structure. Perhaps the question isn't how many bands fit, but whether one format still serves both the schools starting out and the schools with three senior bands. If it doesn't, the cap is solving the wrong problem.", "why": "It surfaces the shared assumption beneath both positions and questions that instead. Opening from his office as treasurer gives the move authority while its content stays educational."}], "checks": ["I named why the capacity frame leaves no good answer.", "I posed a question about purpose or format, not numbers.", "I did not take sides between the principal and the director.", "I finished in under a minute."]}, {"id": "the-reframe-s4", "room": "university academic board", "setup": "A university academic board is considering a proposal to introduce a commercial music major within the Bachelor of Music. A senior professor of musicology has argued that the major's admission and assessment standards are lower than those of the existing classical and jazz majors. The proposing school has defended it on enrolment demand and industry relevance. You sit on the board as an external member from the schools sector.", "said": "No one doubts there's demand. My objection is that we would be awarding the same degree for demonstrably different levels of achievement. Relevance is not a standard. If we want this major, its entry and exit requirements should be equivalent to those of the majors we already offer, and on the paper in front of us, they are not.", "task": "Shift the argument from 'standards versus relevance' to what any holder of the degree must be able to do.", "seconds": 70, "models": [{"text": "I think the professor is right that relevance is not a standard, and I'd resist any argument for this major that rested on demand alone. But I'm not sure the question is whether the new major resembles the existing ones. The question is what anyone holding a Bachelor of Music from this university can be relied upon to do as a musician. If the commercial major delivers that, its difference is a strength. If it doesn't, no level of demand should carry it, and the paper should be sent back.", "why": "It concedes the professor's sharpest line and then relocates the test from resemblance to capability, which is a standard both sides can accept. It also states plainly what failure would mean."}, {"text": "We seem to be choosing between standards and relevance, which is a choice no academic board should want to make. Could we instead ask what the degree promises? If it promises a common core of musicianship, aural, theoretical, historical, at a stated level, then every major should be judged against that core, and this proposal can be tested on evidence rather than on genre. That's a question the school could answer before our next meeting.", "why": "It refuses the false binary and replaces it with a test that is neutral between genres. Ending with a concrete request gives the board something to resolve rather than a debate to continue."}], "checks": ["I agreed explicitly with the strongest part of the professor's case.", "I replaced the binary with a single test.", "I did not defend commercial music on taste or popularity.", "I gave the board something it could ask for or decide."]}, {"id": "the-reframe-s5", "room": "arts funding panel", "setup": "A state arts funding panel is assessing an application from a small community organisation that runs a free youth jazz workshop series in the outer suburbs. The artistic case is strong, but the organisation has a thin financial history and one previous grant acquitted late. A panel member with a finance background has recommended against on risk grounds, and two others have agreed reluctantly.", "said": "I'm sympathetic to the project; the support letters are excellent. But we're stewards of public money, and the track record here is one late acquittal and no reserves. If this organisation folds halfway through, the young people are let down and we are the panel that funded it against the evidence. I don't see how we recommend it.", "task": "Move the panel from 'is this risky?' to what kind of risk this funding program exists to take.", "seconds": 60, "models": [{"text": "I agree it's a risk, and I don't think we should pretend otherwise. But I wonder whether the question is whether it's risky, or whether it's the kind of risk this program was set up to carry. The guidelines speak of emerging organisations and under-served communities, and almost by definition those applicants won't have reserves. If we fund only applicants with clean histories, we're running a different program from the one we've been asked to run. That suggests managing the risk, through staged payments or a milestone, rather than declining it.", "why": "It grounds the reframe in the panel's own guidelines, so the new question has authority beyond his opinion. It converts the finance member's concern into a design condition rather than a veto."}, {"text": "Can I ask the question from the other side? If we decline, what's the most likely outcome for these young people? Probably not a better-run workshop; probably no workshop. So the comparison isn't this organisation against a safe one. It's this organisation against nothing. That doesn't remove the stewardship concern, but it changes what we're weighing it against.", "why": "It reframes by changing the counterfactual, which is a fair and rigorous move that a finance-minded member will recognise. Its brevity lets the point land without argument."}], "checks": ["I accepted that the risk is real.", "I changed the comparison or the test, not the facts.", "I referred to the program's purpose or guidelines.", "I offered a way to manage the concern rather than dismiss it."]}, {"id": "the-reframe-s6", "room": "principal's office", "setup": "You are meeting the principal and an associate principal about next year's timetable. To reduce supervision costs and early starts, they propose moving all ensemble rehearsals from before school into a single after-school block. The associate principal has the timetable model on the table and has clearly done careful work. They are presenting it as a straightforward operational change.", "said": "We've modelled this carefully. Moving everything to one afternoon block saves us roughly forty hours of supervision a term and means no one is in the building at seven. I appreciate it's a change for the ensembles, but it's a timetabling matter, and frankly it's the kind of efficiency the system expects us to find.", "task": "Reframe the conversation from a timetabling efficiency to a decision about the status of the ensemble program in the school's curriculum.", "seconds": 60, "models": [{"text": "I can see the work in this, and the saving is real. I'd just like us to be clear about what we'd be deciding. If ensembles move into a single afternoon block, they'll compete with sport, part-time jobs and the bus timetable, and in practice they'll become optional. That might be the right call, but it's a decision about whether ensemble playing is part of the specialist program or an extra to it. I'd rather we made that decision deliberately than by timetable.", "why": "It credits the associate principal's work and does not contest the figures. It names the decision hidden inside the operational change, which a principal will recognise as a matter for leadership rather than scheduling."}, {"text": "Could I ask what the before-school rehearsals are, in the school's view? If they're co-curricular, this is a sensible efficiency and I'd want to talk about detail. If they're the practical core of a selective program we audition children into, then supervision isn't an overhead; it's the cost of delivering what we promised families at enrolment. I suspect the answer is the second, and if so, we're looking at a different question.", "why": "It reframes through a genuine question, which lets the principal arrive at the new frame rather than be pushed into it. The reference to what families were promised raises the stakes without raising the voice."}], "checks": ["I acknowledged the modelling before questioning its premise.", "I did not dispute the supervision figure.", "I named the decision about the program's status explicitly.", "My tone stayed collegial rather than defensive."]}]}, {"id": "naming-the-real-question", "name": "Naming the real question", "family": "Framing", "what": "Fixes the boundaries of the discussion by stating plainly what decision is actually before the room, what it commits the body to and what it leaves open.", "when": "When a capable room is holding a good discussion about a question that is not on the agenda, or when adjacent decisions have begun to run together and the one with a deadline is being lost.", "teach": "Committees of clever people are prone to a particular failure: they hold an excellent discussion about a question that is not before them. The paper asks for approval to consult; the room debates whether the policy is wise. The resolution is to receive a report; members argue its ninth recommendation. Nothing is decided, or something is decided by drift that nobody would have approved if asked.\n\nNaming the real question is a scoping move. It states what the body is actually being asked to do, what that commits it to and what it does not. It differs from the reframe: the reframe changes the test, this move fixes the boundaries. It draws on distinctions every experienced member knows but which slip under argument: to note, receive, endorse, approve or adopt; in principle or final; this cycle or the next review; the body's own decision or advice to another body.\n\nHighly educated people tend to welcome it, because it offers relief without condescension. Most sense the drift; few want to say so, because it can sound like impatience. Done carefully, the move does not shut down the wider conversation. It places it: 'That is the question for the review; today's question is narrower.' The aim is not to win but to make the decision legible, so that people can agree or disagree with something definite.\n\nSenior practitioners usually put it as a question to the chair or to the paper's author rather than as an assertion: 'Could I check what we are being asked to decide?' This has two advantages. It invites the person with authority to confirm the scope, which gives the move legitimacy, and if you have misread the paper you learn so cheaply. Where the scope is genuinely unclear, offer a formulation the room can accept or amend: 'I take it we are deciding X, and that Y and Z remain open.' Then propose where the displaced concern should go, so that the person who raised it is not simply silenced.\n\nTiming: after the drift has become visible but before anyone has invested in a position on the wider question. Length: two or three sentences. Speak to the chair, evenly, with your papers in front of you; a glance at the recommendation as you speak quietly anchors the room to the document.\n\nIt goes wrong in three ways. It can be used to suppress a legitimate concern ('that is out of scope') that does bear on today's decision. It can be delivered as a procedural rebuke. Or you can name the question wrongly, in which case you look as though you had not read the paper. Read the recommendation literally before you speak.", "phrasings": ["Could I check what we are being asked to decide today?", "As I read the recommendation, we are approving this for consultation, not adopting it. If that is right, several of the concerns raised belong to the next stage.", "I think there are three decisions in play, and only one of them is ours today.", "Before we go further, it might help to separate what this meeting has to settle from what it would be good to discuss.", "The question in front of us is narrower than our discussion: it is simply whether the outlines go forward for 2028.", "Is the board being asked for a decision or for advice? The answer changes how I would speak to it.", "If we resolve only one thing before we leave, I think it has to be this.", "Whatever view we take of the larger question, the decision needed by the end of the month is whether to hold the booking."], "avoid": ["This is all very interesting, but it is not actually on the agenda : it dismisses colleagues' contributions and sounds like impatience rather than clarity.", "Point of order, Chair: we are out of scope : reaching for procedure to solve a scoping problem escalates it into a dispute about the rules.", "I think what we are really deciding, or maybe what we should be deciding, or at least one way of looking at it, is : hedging defeats a move whose entire value is definiteness.", "Can we just make a decision? We have been going round in circles : it voices frustration but names no decision, so the room has nothing to agree to."], "scenarios": [{"id": "naming-the-real-question-s1", "room": "faculty curriculum committee", "setup": "A university education faculty's curriculum committee is considering a proposal to restructure the secondary music method units in the Master of Teaching. The paper asks the committee to approve the revised unit outlines for submission to the academic board for 2028 commencement. For twenty minutes, discussion has ranged over the philosophy of teacher education, popular music pedagogy and the accreditation environment. You are on the committee as a practitioner member.", "said": "I think this goes to the heart of what kind of teachers we're producing. If we're serious about culturally responsive pedagogy, we can't keep treating the Western art canon as the default and everything else as an elective. I'd want to see that principle stated explicitly in the unit outcomes, and frankly I'd want to see it across the whole program, not just in music.", "task": "Bring the committee back to the decision it must make today, while giving the wider concern a proper place.", "seconds": 60, "models": [{"text": "That's a principle I'd support, and I'd like to see it taken further. Can I check the scope, though? As I read the recommendation, we're deciding whether these outlines go to academic board for 2028. The program-wide question is larger and probably belongs with the course review. So perhaps the useful question today is narrower: do these outlines, as drafted, make that principle possible, or do they block it? If they make it possible, we can approve and refer the wider point.", "why": "It supports the speaker before scoping, and it converts her principle into a test the outlines can be measured against today. The wider point is referred, not discarded."}, {"text": "Chair, I think we have two decisions running together. One is whether these four outlines are fit to go to academic board. The other is what the whole program should say about repertoire and pedagogy. Only the first is before us, and it has a deadline. Could we resolve that one, with a note to the board that this committee wants the second taken up in the review?", "why": "It is brief and addressed to the chair, which gives the scoping procedural legitimacy. The proposed note to the board gives the concern a destination and a record."}], "checks": ["I said what the recommendation actually asks for.", "I named where the wider concern should go.", "I did not use the words 'out of scope'.", "I spoke to the chair rather than to the last speaker."]}, {"id": "naming-the-real-question-s2", "room": "SCSA reference group", "setup": "A SCSA reference group is discussing whether to permit recorded performance submissions as an alternative to live practical examinations for candidates in regional and remote schools. The paper proposes a trial for 2027 under defined conditions. Members have begun debating the entire practical examination model: panel composition, moderation and whether live examination is still defensible at all.", "said": "If we accept recordings for regional candidates, we've conceded the principle. Within three years every school will want the option, and we'll be assessing production quality as much as playing. I'd rather we had the honest conversation now about whether the live practical has a future than slide into a two-tier system by stealth.", "task": "Establish that the decision before the group is a bounded trial, and locate the larger question without dismissing it.", "seconds": 60, "models": [{"text": "I think that's the right conversation to have, and I'd want to be part of it. But it isn't the decision on this paper. What we're asked is whether to trial recorded submissions in 2027, for a defined group, under stated conditions. A trial is how we'd get evidence for exactly the debate you're describing. So could we ask a narrower question: are the conditions tight enough that the trial doesn't concede the principle? If they're not, let's tighten them.", "why": "It treats the trial as the means of answering the larger question, which turns a scoping move into an ally of the objector. It ends with a task the group can complete today."}, {"text": "Can I ask the Authority's officer to confirm what our recommendation would commit us to? My reading is a one-year trial, with an evaluation, and no change to the examination model. If that's right, the risk of drift is real but manageable, and it might best be addressed by writing the evaluation criteria into today's recommendation. The future of the live practical deserves a paper of its own.", "why": "Asking the officer to confirm scope draws on the person with authority and protects him if his reading is wrong. It offers a concrete safeguard against the drift the objector fears."}], "checks": ["I stated the decision as a one-year trial with conditions.", "I gave the larger question a proper future home.", "I offered a safeguard that addresses the objector's fear.", "I did not argue the merits of recordings themselves."]}, {"id": "naming-the-real-question-s3", "room": "music department meeting", "setup": "You are chairing a music department meeting of nine staff, including visiting instrumental teachers. The item is next year's concert calendar. Within minutes it has become a discussion about workload, the failure to replace a retired colleague, the auditorium's lighting rig and last year's late reports. Each grievance is legitimate; together they are swamping the item. The calendar must go to the principal on Friday.", "said": "With respect, I don't think we can talk about the calendar without talking about the fact that we're one person down and nobody has told us whether the position will be filled. We ran four concerts last year on goodwill. Before we commit to anything, I'd want some assurance we're not doing that again.", "task": "As chair, name the narrow decision the meeting must reach on the calendar while dealing honestly with the staffing concern.", "seconds": 50, "models": [{"text": "That's fair, and it needs an answer I don't have yet. So let me separate them. The decision we need today is only this: how many full-department concerts go in the calendar, and in which terms. The staffing question I'll take to the principal this week, in writing, with your concern attached. And if the answer is that we stay one person down, the calendar we agree today should be one we could still deliver. Can we work on that basis?", "why": "It gives the staffing concern a specific action and owner before narrowing the item. The closing condition ties the two together, so the colleague's point shapes the decision rather than being parked."}, {"text": "I think the staffing point actually tells us what today's question is. We're not deciding the shape of the program for next year; we're deciding what goes to the principal on Friday. So I'd suggest we agree a calendar that's deliverable at current staffing, and note that anything more depends on the position being filled. That way the calendar makes the argument for us.", "why": "It absorbs the grievance into the scoping, so the colleague hears his point adopted rather than deferred. The strategic note at the end gives staff a reason to accept a narrow decision."}], "checks": ["I acknowledged the staffing concern as legitimate.", "I stated the decision in one sentence with the Friday deadline.", "I committed to a specific action on the wider grievance.", "I asked the room to agree to the narrowed question."]}, {"id": "naming-the-real-question-s4", "room": "festival association board", "setup": "The festival association's board has received an offer from a regional bank to sponsor next year's festival in exchange for naming rights and branding on adjudication sheets. Discussion has moved into a broad debate about the commercialisation of school arts events and whether the association should have a sponsorship policy. The bank wants an answer within a fortnight. As treasurer, you know the festival needs the money.", "said": "My worry isn't this bank; it's the precedent. Once our name has a sponsor in front of it, every future negotiation starts from there, and I've seen festivals lose their identity that way. I think we need a sponsorship policy, discussed properly, before we sign anything with anyone.", "task": "Clarify what the board must decide within the fortnight, separating it from the policy question.", "seconds": 60, "models": [{"text": "I agree we need a policy, and I'd be glad to help draft one. But the bank isn't asking us to sign; it's asking whether we'll negotiate. So perhaps the decision tonight is simply whether to authorise the President and me to open discussions, with two conditions we set now: no naming rights over the festival title, and nothing on the adjudication sheets. That protects the precedent you're worried about and keeps the offer alive while the policy is written.", "why": "It identifies the true decision, authority to negotiate, which is smaller than the one the room was fearing. The two conditions convert the objector's concern into binding terms."}, {"text": "Could I try to pin down what we actually have to decide? There are three questions on the table: whether we want sponsorship at all, on what terms we'd accept this offer, and what our general policy should be. Only the second has a deadline, and answering it doesn't commit us on the other two. If we set the terms tonight, the policy can come to the next meeting, informed by what we learn from the negotiation.", "why": "It lays out the three questions in sequence and shows that only one is pressing. Stating that the narrow decision does not prejudge the others is what makes the objector able to agree."}], "checks": ["I named the decision with the fortnight deadline precisely.", "I gave the policy concern a timeline.", "I did not argue that the festival needs the money.", "I proposed terms or conditions the board could minute."]}, {"id": "naming-the-real-question-s5", "room": "music parents' association", "setup": "At a meeting of the music parents' association, the committee is discussing a proposed interstate tour for the senior ensembles. Parents have raised cost, dates that may clash with examinations, supervision ratios and whether younger students should be included. The tour company needs a commitment by the end of the month to hold flights and accommodation. You attend as Teacher in Charge.", "said": "I don't think we can commit to anything until we know exactly what each family will pay, what the itinerary looks like and whether the exam clash is real. Some of us have two children in the program. We've been burned before by tours where the final cost came in a third higher than the estimate.", "task": "Separate the decision due this month from the questions that can properly be settled later.", "seconds": 60, "models": [{"text": "Those are exactly the questions families should ask, and you'll have answers to all of them before anyone commits money of their own. What the committee needs to decide tonight is narrower: whether to hold a provisional booking by the end of the month, so the tour stays possible. The final cost, the itinerary and the exam dates come back to this committee in March, and that's the point at which families decide. If we don't hold the booking, those questions become academic.", "why": "It validates the parent's caution and promises a specific forum and date for every concern. The final sentence shows why the narrow decision is needed now without pressuring anyone."}, {"text": "Can I suggest we name tonight's decision precisely? It isn't whether the tour goes ahead; it's whether we keep the option open. If the answer is yes, I'll bring a costed itinerary and a checked exam calendar to the March meeting, and the decision to proceed is made then, against a cost ceiling we agree tonight. If the final price comes in above the ceiling, the tour doesn't go.", "why": "It distinguishes keeping an option open from committing to the tour. The agreed ceiling answers the history of cost blowouts directly, which a parent who has been burned will value."}], "checks": ["I distinguished holding an option from committing to the tour.", "I gave a date when the parents' questions will be answered.", "I addressed the history of cost increases directly.", "I avoided sounding as though I was selling the tour."]}, {"id": "naming-the-real-question-s6", "room": "university academic board", "setup": "A university academic board is considering the report of a working party on assessment in the creative and performing arts. The report contains fourteen recommendations. The resolution before the board is to receive the report and refer the recommendations to faculties for response. Two members have spent fifteen minutes debating recommendation nine, on panel grading in studio assessment, which one of them strongly opposes.", "said": "I'm sorry to labour this, but recommendation nine would be a serious mistake. Panel grading in the studio dilutes the judgement of the principal teacher, who knows the student's development better than anyone. If the board endorses this, we'll be imposing a model the discipline has rejected for good reasons.", "task": "Clarify that the board is receiving and referring, not endorsing, and direct the objection to where it will be effective.", "seconds": 50, "models": [{"text": "I think that objection deserves to be heard properly, and it will be. Could I just check the resolution with the Chair? As I read it, we're receiving the report and referring all fourteen recommendations to faculties for response. Receiving isn't endorsing. So the place for this argument is the faculty response on recommendation nine, and if it's as strong as I suspect, it should shape what comes back to this board.", "why": "It checks the scope through the chair and states the distinction in three words. It tells the objector where his argument will actually have force, which is better than telling him to stop."}, {"text": "Chair, it might help to be clear about the verb. We're asked to receive, not to adopt. If the board receives the report, nothing in recommendation nine binds anyone; it goes to faculties, who can reject it. If members are uneasy even about receiving, the remedy might be a sentence in the resolution noting that the board has formed no view on any recommendation. That would protect the concern without holding up the other thirteen.", "why": "It locates the issue precisely in the verb of the resolution. The proposed amendment offers the objector a formal protection, which is more persuasive than reassurance."}], "checks": ["I named the exact verb in the resolution.", "I said where the objection would be effective.", "I did not express a view on panel grading.", "I offered a remedy if the objector remained uneasy."]}]}, {"id": "drawing-a-distinction", "name": "Drawing a distinction", "family": "Framing", "what": "Separates two things the room has been treating as one, so that people discover where they actually agree and where the real disagreement lies.", "when": "When two capable speakers are using the same word in different senses and each hears the other as wrong, or when an argument is escalating while remaining unresolvable.", "teach": "A great deal of unproductive disagreement in committees comes from a single word doing two jobs. 'Standards' means both entry requirements and exit outcomes; 'access' means both who may enrol and who can afford to; 'fairness' means both equal treatment and equal effect. When two speakers use the word in different senses, each hears the other as wrong, and the argument escalates while remaining unresolvable. Drawing a distinction separates the two things, names them, and usually shows that the room agrees about one and disagrees about the other.\n\nThis is the native move of the academy, which is why it works so well there. Philosophers, lawyers and historians are trained to respect a well-drawn distinction and to recognise one quickly; a crisp distinction is among the few things that will make a room of experts stop and write something down. It also does valuable social work. It allows both earlier speakers to be right, each about a different thing, which lowers the temperature without anyone having to retreat.\n\nThe craft is economy. Name the two things in parallel form, give each a short label, and attach one consequence: 'There are two questions here. One is who we admit; the other is what we require of them by the end. On the first, I think we are close. The second is where the real difference lies.' Parallel syntax does much of the work, because the ear registers the structure before the mind weighs the content. Labels matter too: a good label becomes the room's vocabulary for the rest of the meeting, and whoever supplied it has quietly shaped the discussion.\n\nDeliver it with slight deliberateness: a small pause between the two halves, perhaps a hand marking each, but no more. It should sound like clarification, not cross-examination. Address the room rather than the two speakers, and credit both: 'I think Helen and Mark are each right about a different thing.'\n\nThere are three common failures. The first is the distinction without a difference: separating two things that do not need separating, which reads as pedantry and slows the room for nothing. The second is the proliferating taxonomy: three, four, five distinctions, at which point you are lecturing. The third is using the distinction to smuggle in a conclusion, defining the terms so that your preferred option wins by definition. A sophisticated room notices this quickly, and the credibility you lose is hard to recover. The test of a good distinction is that both sides would accept it as fair, even if it then happens to work in your favour.", "phrasings": ["I think there are two things here that we have been treating as one.", "It might help to distinguish coverage from depth. On the first, I suspect we largely agree; on the second, we genuinely differ.", "When we say 'standards', we may mean the bar for entry or the bar for graduation. Those pull in different directions.", "There is a difference between whether the policy is right and whether it was applied well in this case.", "I wonder whether we are running together the question of who decides with the question of what they should decide.", "Both of those points seem to me right, because they are about different things.", "Can I separate the principle from its timing? I support the first; I am less sure about the second.", "Let me call one of these 'access' and the other 'affordability', because the remedies are quite different."], "avoid": ["Well, technically, if we are being precise about our terms : it signals point-scoring rather than clarification and invites the room to resent the precision.", "There are actually five different things being confused here. First : a taxonomy turns a clarification into a lecture and loses the room by the third item.", "You are conflating two completely different things : addressed to a person, a distinction becomes an accusation of muddled thinking.", "If we define quality properly, it is obvious the proposal fails : a distinction drawn to deliver a conclusion by definition will be detected and discounted."], "scenarios": [{"id": "drawing-a-distinction-s1", "room": "SCSA syllabus committee", "setup": "A SCSA syllabus committee is reviewing a draft revision of the Year 11 and 12 Music syllabus. The draft reduces the number of prescribed works in the context component. A university member has argued that this lowers rigour; a teacher member has replied that the current load forces superficial coverage. The discussion is becoming a proxy war over whether the course is getting easier.", "said": "I'm not against the teaching being more manageable. But we have to be honest that fewer works means less. When I look at what first-year students arrive knowing, the breadth has already thinned. Reducing the list further sends a signal to schools that depth of knowledge matters less than it did.", "task": "Separate rigour from volume so the committee can argue about the one that matters.", "seconds": 60, "models": [{"text": "I think we're using 'rigour' to mean two different things. One is coverage: how many works a student encounters. The other is depth: how demanding the analysis of any one work is. The draft reduces coverage; whether it reduces depth depends entirely on what the assessment then asks for. If the examination requires sustained analysis of fewer works, I'd call that more rigorous, not less. So perhaps the test of this draft lies in the assessment design rather than the list.", "why": "It splits the contested word cleanly and shows that the draft touches only one half. The proposed test moves the committee to the place where its decision actually has effect."}, {"text": "It might help to separate what students know from what they can do with it. Breadth of repertoire is the first; the capacity to analyse an unfamiliar score is the second, and it's the one universities most often say they want. Both matter, but they're different levers. A shorter list could weaken the first and strengthen the second. I'd like us to decide which we're prioritising, and then judge the list against that.", "why": "It uses a knowledge and capability distinction that the university member will recognise from his own discipline. It treats both speakers as right and asks the committee for a priority rather than a verdict."}], "checks": ["I named both halves of the distinction in parallel form.", "I credited the university member's concern as real.", "I stated what follows from the distinction for this draft.", "I drew one distinction, not several."]}, {"id": "drawing-a-distinction-s2", "room": "school council", "setup": "At a school council meeting, a parent member raises a complaint that her child was penalised for a late practical assessment despite a medical certificate. She asks the council to review the decision and to consider whether the assessment policy is too rigid. Other members are unsure whether this is their business. The principal is present.", "said": "I'm not asking for special treatment. But if the policy allows a teacher to disregard a medical certificate, then the policy is wrong, and this council has a say in the school's policies. I think we have a responsibility to look at what happened here and make sure it doesn't happen to another family.", "task": "Distinguish the individual case, which is the principal's to resolve, from the policy question, which the council may properly consider.", "seconds": 60, "models": [{"text": "I think there are two matters here, and the council has a proper role in only one of them. Your child's case is a question of how the policy was applied, and that sits with the principal, who I know will look at it. Whether the policy handles medical evidence well is a fair question for this council, as part of its interest in the school's general directions. Could we ask the principal to resolve the individual case through the usual process, and bring the assessment policy to a future meeting for review?", "why": "It tells the parent that her concern is legitimate and gives each half of it the right forum. It keeps the council within its role without lecturing anyone on governance."}, {"text": "There's a difference between whether the policy is right and whether it was applied correctly. If it was misapplied, the remedy lies with the principal, and quickly. If it was applied as written and still produced this result, the policy needs looking at, and that's a conversation the council can have without discussing any individual student. I'd suggest we keep the two separate, partly to protect your child's privacy.", "why": "The two conditional branches show the parent that both possible outcomes have a remedy. Grounding the separation in her child's privacy makes it a protection rather than a deflection."}], "checks": ["I did not discuss the details of the individual case.", "I named the principal as responsible for the case itself.", "I affirmed the council's legitimate interest in the policy.", "I proposed a concrete next step for each half."]}, {"id": "drawing-a-distinction-s3", "room": "arts funding panel", "setup": "An arts funding panel is assessing a commission application from an established jazz composer for a large-scale work with a youth orchestra. Two assessors rate it highly on artistic merit. A third rates it low, citing an unconfirmed venue, an unsigned partnership with the orchestra and a timeline that depends on another grant. Scores are averaging to a borderline result.", "said": "I'm not questioning the composer. Her work is outstanding and the concept is exciting. But the application as submitted isn't ready. There's no confirmed partner, no venue, and the budget assumes money from a source that hasn't decided yet. Merit can't compensate for that.", "task": "Separate artistic merit from project readiness so that the panel can reach a better recommendation than a borderline average.", "seconds": 55, "models": [{"text": "I think we agree on more than the scores suggest. On artistic merit, all three of us rate it highly. On readiness, I think the concerns are well founded. Those are separate criteria, and averaging them hides the fact that this is an excellent project that isn't yet ready. That points to a different outcome from a borderline score: perhaps funding conditional on the partnership being confirmed, or a clear invitation to reapply next round.", "why": "It shows the panel that its apparent disagreement is really agreement on two separate axes. Naming what the average conceals leads directly to a better form of recommendation."}, {"text": "Could we score merit and readiness separately for a moment? I suspect we'd find the panel unanimous on the first and broadly agreed on the second. That's a much clearer message to the applicant than a mark in the middle, and it's fairer to the composer, whose work isn't borderline at all. The question for us then becomes whether our guidelines allow conditional funding.", "why": "It turns the distinction into a procedure the panel can perform at once. The point about fairness to the composer gives the separation an ethical as well as a technical justification."}], "checks": ["I credited the third assessor's concerns as valid.", "I named merit and readiness as distinct criteria.", "I showed what the averaged score conceals.", "I pointed towards a concrete form of recommendation."]}, {"id": "drawing-a-distinction-s4", "room": "university school of music admissions committee", "setup": "A university school of music's admissions committee is reviewing a proposal for an alternative entry pathway for applicants from schools without specialist music programs. These applicants would be admitted on portfolio and interview with a lower audition threshold, supported by a bridging year. A senior performance lecturer opposes it; the school's equity lead supports it. You are an external member from the schools sector.", "said": "I'm all for widening participation in principle. But an audition threshold exists for a reason. If we admit students below it, we either fail them in second year or we pass them and the degree means less. I don't see a third option, and I don't think it's kind to pretend there is one.", "task": "Separate the standard for entry from the standard for graduation, and show that the proposal touches only the first.", "seconds": 65, "models": [{"text": "I think there is a third option, and it turns on a distinction. The audition measures where a student is at entry. The degree standard is where they must be at the end. The proposal lowers the first and leaves the second untouched; the bridging year is the mechanism meant to close the gap. So the question isn't whether we're lowering standards. It's whether the bridging year is good enough to do that work, and that's something we can test.", "why": "It answers the lecturer's 'no third option' directly and precisely. Relocating the question to the bridging year gives the sceptic something legitimate to scrutinise."}, {"text": "Can I separate two things? One is the bar students must clear to graduate, and I'd defend that as strongly as anyone here. The other is the evidence we use to predict who will clear it. An audition is one predictor; for a student who has never had a teacher, it may be a poor one. The proposal changes the predictor, not the bar. If we hold the exit standard fixed and track progression, we'll know within two intakes whether it works.", "why": "Framing the audition as a predictor rather than a standard is the sharper version of the distinction, and the lecturer will recognise it as a fair point about evidence. The monitoring proposal answers his concern about kindness."}], "checks": ["I stated my own commitment to the graduating standard.", "I named entry and exit as separate things.", "I identified what the proposal changes and what it leaves fixed.", "I gave the sceptic a legitimate thing to test."]}, {"id": "drawing-a-distinction-s5", "room": "festival association board", "setup": "The festival association's board is considering whether to stop announcing first, second and third places in each division, after several schools complained that placings demoralise developing bands. Every band also receives a gold, silver or bronze rating against published criteria. A long-serving adjudicator on the board argues that without placings the festival loses its rigour and its draw. A newer board member supports the change.", "said": "Schools come to this festival because the result means something. Take away the placings and you've got a series of concerts with comment sheets. Directors use those results to argue for their programs with principals. If we remove them, we'll be making the festival nicer and less useful.", "task": "Distinguish assessment against a standard from ranking against other bands, so that the board can keep what serves schools.", "seconds": 55, "models": [{"text": "I think the ratings and the placings do different jobs. A rating tells a school how its band measured against a published standard; that's what a director can take to a principal, and it's where the festival's rigour lives. A placing tells a school how it measured against the bands that happened to be in the same session. The complaints, as I read them, are almost all about the second. We could keep the standard and drop the league table.", "why": "It shows that the adjudicator's strongest argument actually belongs to the ratings, which nobody proposes to remove. The closing phrase gives the board a memorable label for the decision."}, {"text": "There's a difference between being assessed and being ranked. Assessment against criteria is the educational heart of what we do, and I'd resist anything that softened it. Ranking is a choice about presentation, and it has costs for exactly the developing bands we most want to keep coming. If we separate the two, I suspect the board can agree to keep one and review the other.", "why": "It states his own commitment to rigour first, which disarms the charge that the change is soft. Calling ranking 'a choice about presentation' makes it easier for the adjudicator to let go of."}], "checks": ["I distinguished rating from placing in one or two sentences.", "I linked the adjudicator's concern to the part being kept.", "I did not dismiss the value of results to schools.", "I gave the board a decision it could take."]}, {"id": "drawing-a-distinction-s6", "room": "principal's office", "setup": "In a budget meeting, the principal explains that every learning area will take a uniform eight per cent reduction in its operating budget next year. Most of the music program's operating budget is spent maintaining a large fleet of school-owned instruments. The principal is keen to be seen as even-handed across departments.", "said": "I know no one likes this, and I'm genuinely sorry. But the only way I can do it fairly is to do it evenly. If I start making exceptions, every head of learning area will have a case, and most of them will be good cases. Eight per cent across the board is the least bad option.", "task": "Distinguish equal treatment from equal effect without asking for special treatment.", "seconds": 60, "models": [{"text": "I understand why an even cut is the fairest way to present it, and I'm not asking for an exception. I'd only point out that an even cut can have very uneven effects. For most areas, eight per cent means fewer consumables. For us, most of the operating budget maintains instruments the school owns, so eight per cent means instruments out of service and students without one. Could we look at the effect rather than the rate, perhaps with the business manager, so the cut lands as evenly as the percentage suggests?", "why": "It renounces an exception at the outset, which removes the principal's main fear. The concrete contrast between consumables and assets makes the distinction undeniable."}, {"text": "I think there are two kinds of fairness here. One is treating every area the same; the other is asking every area to bear the same burden. Usually they coincide. Where a budget is mostly the upkeep of fixed assets, they don't, because the cut falls on the assets rather than the discretionary spending. I'm happy to carry the same burden as everyone else. I'd just like us to measure it the same way.", "why": "It names the distinction in its general form, which lets the principal apply it consistently to other areas. The final line turns his request into a demand for greater fairness, not less."}], "checks": ["I said explicitly that I was not asking for an exception.", "I named equal treatment and equal effect as distinct.", "I gave one concrete example of the uneven effect.", "I proposed a way of measuring the burden rather than a different number."]}]}, {"id": "raising-the-altitude", "name": "Raising the altitude", "family": "Framing", "what": "Moves the discussion from the particular to the principle that governs it, or from management to governance, so that the body decides what only it can decide.", "when": "When a board or committee has descended into operational detail that belongs to staff or delegates, or when an individual case is being argued at length while the policy question it exposes goes unaddressed.", "teach": "Boards and councils drift downward. Detail is concrete, easy to discuss and satisfying to resolve, so members with expertise in an operational area gravitate to it, and an hour can pass on the wording of a letter or the choice of a caterer while the body's actual responsibilities go unattended. Raising the altitude moves the discussion from the particular to the principle that governs it, or from management to governance: from what should be done in this instance to what the body should decide, set or delegate so that such instances are handled well without it.\n\nThe move rests on a distinction every competent board understands in theory. The body sets direction, policy, risk appetite and accountability; staff or delegates execute. The committee of an incorporated association is responsible for managing its affairs, but a well-run committee does that by setting frameworks and holding people to them, not by doing their jobs. A school council's functions in Western Australia concern the school's objectives, priorities and general policy directions, not the day-to-day running of the school, which remains the principal's. You rarely need to say any of this. The room knows; it has simply slipped.\n\nThe difficulty is tone. Raising the altitude implies that the room has been at the wrong level, and highly educated people dislike being told so. Senior practitioners manage this in three ways. They honour the detail first, briefly and genuinely, often by saying that it has revealed something: 'This case is useful, because it shows we have no policy for it.' They frame the move as a service to those who will execute, not a criticism of those who have been discussing: 'Could we give Sarah a principle to work from, so this does not need to come back to us?' And they offer the principle concretely, in a sentence that could be minuted, rather than gesturing at 'the bigger picture'.\n\nTiming: once the detail has been explored enough to expose the principle at stake, but before anyone has committed to a position on the instance. Length: twenty to forty seconds. A slightly slower delivery and an open posture, sitting back rather than forward, signal perspective rather than impatience.\n\nIt fails in three ways. The first is condescension: any phrasing that implies others cannot see what you see. The second is abstraction, rising so high that the principle is a platitude ('we should act in the students' best interests') that decides nothing. The third is abandoning a detail that genuinely matters. Sometimes the instance is itself the governance question, a material risk, a legal exposure or a conflict of interest, and the body must deal with it directly. Altitude is a judgement, not a reflex.", "phrasings": ["This case is useful, because it shows us something we do not yet have a policy for.", "Could we give the director a principle to work from, so that decisions like this need not come back to the board?", "I wonder whether our role here is to settle the detail or to settle the criteria by which it will be settled.", "Taking this one step up: what would we want to be true in every case like this one?", "I am conscious that we may be doing the executive's job rather than our own. What is it this board needs to decide?", "If we agree the principle, I would be content to leave the particulars to those closest to them.", "There is a governance question underneath this, and I think it is the one we are best placed to answer.", "Let me try to put the principle in a sentence that could be minuted."], "avoid": ["Can we lift our heads out of the weeds for a moment? : the metaphor tells colleagues they have been in the weeds, which is condescension however lightly meant.", "I think we need to think about the bigger picture here : it gestures at altitude without offering a principle anyone could decide.", "This is really an operational matter and should not be at board level : true or not, it rebukes whoever raised it and gives the room nothing to do instead.", "Ultimately, what matters is the students : a principle so high it decides nothing, and it implies others had forgotten them."], "scenarios": [{"id": "raising-the-altitude-s1", "room": "school council", "setup": "You chair a school council. Under general business, a member has raised the arrangements for the school's community fair: stall allocations, food handling, whether a particular vendor should be invited. Members have spent twenty minutes on catering, and the business manager, who is running the event, is trying to take notes on preferences that contradict one another. Two strategic items remain on the agenda.", "said": "I just think if we're going to have food trucks, we need at least one that does proper vegetarian food, and frankly the coffee last year was a disgrace. These things matter to families. It's the one day a year the community really sees the school, and the details are what people remember.", "task": "As chair, lift the discussion from the event's details to what the council should properly decide, without dismissing the member.", "seconds": 60, "models": [{"text": "I think that's right: the details are what people remember. And that's exactly why I'd like to leave them with Anna, who's running the day and is far better placed than we are to get them right. What council can usefully do is say what the fair is for and what we'd want to be true of it: that it's inclusive, that it's safe and that it's affordable for families. If we're agreed on that, Anna can work within it and report back. Can I take that as the council's view?", "why": "It agrees with the member's final point and uses it as the reason to delegate. The three principles are concrete enough to minute and to guide the business manager."}, {"text": "Can I suggest we give Anna a brief rather than a list? From what's been said, I'm hearing three principles: food that caters for the whole community, a standard of quality that reflects well on the school, and costs that keep it accessible. If council endorses those, the specific choices are hers. I'd then like to move to the two strategic items, which only this council can decide.", "why": "It distils the room's own preferences into principles, so members hear their contributions honoured. The final sentence names the governance work waiting without criticising the time spent."}], "checks": ["I agreed with something the member said before moving up.", "I named the person who will execute and expressed confidence in them.", "I stated principles concrete enough to minute.", "I moved the meeting on to the strategic items."]}, {"id": "raising-the-altitude-s2", "room": "festival association board", "setup": "The festival board is discussing the adjudication panel for next year. One board member has proposed a specific interstate adjudicator; another has concerns about his manner with young bands; a third suggests a local alternative. The festival director, who normally appoints adjudicators, is at the table and has said little. There is no written policy on adjudicator appointment.", "said": "I've heard him at three festivals and he's brilliant with senior bands, but I watched him reduce a Year 8 trumpet section to tears in Melbourne. I'm not comfortable with him in the junior divisions. If we're going to bring him over, it should be for the top division only.", "task": "Raise the board from choosing a particular adjudicator to setting the criteria and the delegation for appointing adjudicators.", "seconds": 60, "models": [{"text": "That's useful, and I suspect it's exactly the kind of judgement our director makes every year without the board's help. What strikes me is that we have nothing in writing about what we expect of adjudicators: how they work with developing bands, the balance of local and interstate, and who appoints. Could we ask the director to draft a short set of criteria for the next meeting, and agree that within those the appointments are hers? Then this conversation needn't happen at board level again.", "why": "It credits the speaker's observation and returns authority to the director who has been sidelined. Naming the missing policy turns an awkward personnel discussion into a governance task."}, {"text": "I wonder whether the board's job is to choose the adjudicator or to say what a good one looks like. The concern about the junior divisions is a principle in disguise: adjudicators must be able to give developmental feedback to beginning bands. If we put that, and two or three similar standards, in writing, the director can make the appointment, and we've protected every junior band, not just next year's.", "why": "It extracts the general principle from the anecdote, so the speaker's concern becomes policy rather than a comment on one person. It also avoids discussing a named individual's conduct at length."}], "checks": ["I did not express a view on the individual adjudicator.", "I turned the specific concern into a general standard.", "I returned the appointment to the director explicitly.", "I proposed a written outcome the board could ask for."]}, {"id": "raising-the-altitude-s3", "room": "SCSA assessment committee", "setup": "A SCSA committee is reviewing sample examination questions to be published as exemplars for the revised ATAR Music course. Members have spent some time debating whether one aural question should specify 'jazz' or 'contemporary' as its stylistic context. Both positions have merit. The broader purpose of the exemplar set has not been discussed.", "said": "I'd strongly prefer 'jazz'. 'Contemporary' is so broad it's almost meaningless, and if we're vague in an exemplar, teachers will be vague in their programs. A specific label tells schools exactly what to prepare.", "task": "Lift the committee from one question's wording to the principle governing what the exemplars should signal.", "seconds": 55, "models": [{"text": "That last point seems to me the important one: teachers read exemplars as instructions. So before we settle this question, perhaps we should agree what the set as a whole is meant to tell schools. If the exemplars are meant to indicate the range of contexts the examination might draw on, a specific label here needs balancing elsewhere. If they're meant to model the type of question rather than its content, the label matters less. Once we've decided that, I suspect this question will be easy.", "why": "It builds on the speaker's own reasoning, which makes the move feel like agreement taken further. The two options for the set's purpose give the committee a clear decision at the right level."}, {"text": "I'm persuaded that exemplars shape programs, and I think that applies well beyond this question. Could we agree a principle for the whole set: that the stylistic contexts across the exemplars should reflect the breadth of the syllabus, and that no single exemplar should be read as a prediction? Then 'jazz' here might be entirely right, provided the others range more widely.", "why": "It proposes the principle in a minutable sentence and then shows that it can accommodate the speaker's preference. The committee gets both a policy and a resolution of the detail."}], "checks": ["I took up the speaker's reasoning rather than opposing it.", "I stated a principle for the exemplar set as a whole.", "I showed how the detail would be resolved once the principle was agreed.", "I did not use the phrase 'bigger picture'."]}, {"id": "raising-the-altitude-s4", "room": "university academic board assessment subcommittee", "setup": "A university academic board's assessment subcommittee is considering a report showing a sharp rise in special consideration applications in performance units, concentrated around end-of-semester recitals. Members have been discussing particular cases from the de-identified appendix and debating whether specific applications should have been granted. The subcommittee's terms of reference are to advise the board on assessment policy.", "said": "Look at case fourteen. Performance anxiety, a GP's letter, granted a deferral. And case twenty-two, almost identical, refused. Either the criteria are being applied inconsistently or we have no criteria. I'd want to know who made these decisions.", "task": "Raise the discussion from individual case outcomes to what the pattern says about policy and assessment design, consistent with the subcommittee's advisory role.", "seconds": 65, "models": [{"text": "The inconsistency is real and should be looked at, through the faculty, as a matter of process. But I'd suggest our role is a level above the cases. What the pattern tells me is that a single high-stakes recital at the end of semester generates a predictable demand for special consideration. That's an assessment design question. Perhaps our advice to the board should be whether performance units need more than one assessment point, which would lower the stakes for everyone, including the students in cases fourteen and twenty-two.", "why": "It directs the inconsistency to the proper process and then reads the data as evidence about design. The advice it proposes is squarely within the subcommittee's terms of reference."}, {"text": "I think these two cases are telling us something general, which is what we're here to advise on. Either the policy doesn't say how performance anxiety is to be treated, or it does and isn't being followed. Both are policy questions, and neither requires us to revisit individual decisions already made by those responsible for them. Could we ask for the relevant policy clause and frame our recommendation around that?", "why": "It turns the speaker's either-or into two policy questions and keeps the subcommittee out of individual adjudication. The request for the clause gives the discussion a precise object."}], "checks": ["I accepted that the inconsistency matters.", "I referred to the subcommittee's advisory role.", "I named a policy or design question the pattern raises.", "I did not discuss the merits of either individual case."]}, {"id": "raising-the-altitude-s5", "room": "arts funding panel", "setup": "An arts funding panel is assessing a mid-sized application from a regional music festival. One panel member, an accountant, has been going through the budget line by line, querying catering, artist accommodation rates and a marketing figure. The panel has thirty applications to assess today and has spent fifteen minutes on this budget. The guidelines ask panels to assess whether a budget is realistic and represents value for money.", "said": "And I'd query the accommodation line too. Four hundred a night is a lot for a regional town. If they used the motel instead of the hotel, they'd save nearly two thousand. I don't think we should be funding a budget that hasn't been looked at this carefully.", "task": "Raise the panel from re-budgeting the application to the assessment the guidelines actually ask for.", "seconds": 50, "models": [{"text": "Those are fair queries, and it's the pattern I'd take from them. Our guidelines ask us to judge whether the budget is realistic and good value overall, not to rebuild it. Taken together, these lines suggest a budget that's somewhat generous but not unrealistic. If that's our view, we can say so in the feedback, with the accommodation line as the example, and score it accordingly. Otherwise I worry we'll give this applicant far more scrutiny than the other twenty-nine.", "why": "It converts the accountant's line items into a single overall judgement, which is what the guidelines require. The point about consistency across applicants is a fairness argument he will respect."}, {"text": "Could I ask the panel a slightly different question? Leaving the individual lines aside, do we think this organisation can deliver what it proposes for the money it's asking? If yes, a few generous items are a matter for the feedback. If no, it isn't the catering that's the problem. I suspect we're broadly at yes.", "why": "It poses the governing question in a single sentence and shows how both answers dispose of the detail. Its brevity models the pace the panel needs."}], "checks": ["I acknowledged the queries as legitimate.", "I named the test the guidelines actually set.", "I proposed where the detailed points should go.", "I kept my contribution under fifty seconds."]}, {"id": "raising-the-altitude-s6", "room": "principal's office", "setup": "You are meeting the principal and the business manager about an invoice for an expensive overhaul of a school-owned baritone saxophone. The business manager has questioned whether an instrument of its age is worth repairing. The conversation has narrowed to whether this one repair should be approved. The school has a large instrument fleet and no replacement schedule.", "said": "I'm not saying no. But this is the third major repair on that instrument in four years, and the quote is getting close to what a replacement would cost. At some point we're throwing good money after bad. I need a reason to approve it beyond 'we always have'.", "task": "Raise the conversation from a single invoice to an asset management principle the school can apply to the whole fleet.", "seconds": 55, "models": [{"text": "That's a fair test, and honestly I'd apply it more widely. This instrument is a symptom: we have a fleet worth a great deal of money and no schedule for replacing any of it, so every repair becomes a one-off decision like this. Could I bring you a simple fleet register next term, with each instrument's age, condition and repair history, and a replacement threshold we agree? Then decisions like this make themselves, and you're not dependent on my say-so.", "why": "It accepts the business manager's test and generalises it, which makes him an ally. Offering to remove the dependence on the TIC's judgement is a mark of good governance that a business manager values."}, {"text": "I'd suggest we approve this one, because the student who plays it has no alternative this year, and use it as the occasion to set a rule. Something like: once cumulative repairs reach half the replacement cost, the instrument goes on the replacement list. That gives you a defensible principle, it gives me a planning horizon, and it means the next one of these doesn't need a meeting.", "why": "It resolves the instance and the principle in one move, which is often what a busy executive wants. The proposed threshold is specific enough to adopt on the spot."}], "checks": ["I accepted the business manager's concern as a fair test.", "I proposed a principle that applies to the whole fleet.", "I made the principle specific enough to adopt.", "I dealt with or deliberately parked the immediate invoice."]}]}, {"id": "opening-a-discussion-well", "name": "Opening a discussion well", "family": "Framing", "what": "Uses the first contribution on an item to set the vocabulary, the scale and the question to which everyone else will respond, without closing the discussion down.", "when": "When the chair invites the first comment on an item, when you are lead assessor or designated opener, or when you chair and must introduce the item yourself.", "teach": "The first contribution on an agenda item has disproportionate influence. Whoever speaks first sets the vocabulary, the scale and the implied question, and later speakers tend to respond to that opening rather than to the paper itself. Any experienced chair knows this: the first speaker hands the room its terms. Opening well means using that influence responsibly, so that the discussion begins at the right level and on the right question, without your foreclosing it.\n\nA strong opening has three parts and rarely runs past sixty seconds. It orients: a sentence on what the item is and what kind of decision it asks for. It evaluates: a short, honest view of the paper's strength and its central difficulty, named precisely. And it invites: it hands the room a question that is genuinely open and that others are better placed than you to answer. The invitation is the part most people omit, and it is what distinguishes an opening from a speech. It is also a courtesy to the expertise around the table.\n\nWith a highly educated room the risk is not that you will say too little but that you will say everything. The opener who covers every concern has in effect closed the item, and the room will either defer, which wastes its intelligence, or react against the completeness, which makes you the topic. Choose the one or two points that matter most and leave the rest for others; if nobody raises them, you can do so later, now as a response rather than an opening.\n\nSenior practitioners often begin by acknowledging the author's work in one specific clause, which is far more credible than general praise: 'The modelling in section three is what I found most useful.' They then state a position provisionally, as a view open to revision: 'My instinct is to support it, though I can see the case against.' When opening as chair, the balance shifts further. The chair's opening should frame the decision and the criteria and withhold the chair's own view, or give it last.\n\nVoice: settled and unhurried, as though you had expected to be asked, which you should have. Prepare the opening of every item where you might plausibly be called first. Do not begin with apology or throat-clearing ('I suppose I can start'). Make eye contact around the table as you reach the invitation, not only with the chair, because the invitation is to everyone.\n\nIt goes wrong in three ways: the comprehensive opening that exhausts the item; the opening that announces a conclusion so firmly that disagreement becomes personal; and the merely procedural opening ('I'm happy to go first') that wastes the anchor altogether.", "phrasings": ["Thank you for the paper. The section I found most useful was the modelling of enrolment trends, and it is also where my main question lies.", "This seems to me to ask us for a decision in principle, with the detail to follow. On that basis my instinct is to support it, with one reservation.", "Let me offer a starting view, which I hold provisionally.", "I think the paper is strongest on governance and least developed on resourcing, and I would be interested to hear whether others read it the same way.", "There are, as I see it, two questions here, and it may help to take them in turn.", "Before I give a view, could I ask the author one question of fact? The answer will shape what I think.", "As chair, I will hold my own view for now. What I would like us to settle is whether the proposal meets the criteria in section two, and I suggest we take them in order.", "I will be brief, because others will know more than I do about the accreditation implications, but from the schools' side the central issue is this."], "avoid": ["I suppose I can kick things off, though I have not had a chance to look at it properly : it apologises for the anchor and discounts your view before you have given it.", "I have about eight points, so bear with me : the comprehensive opening exhausts the item and makes others respond to you rather than to the paper.", "This is clearly unworkable and I cannot see how anyone could support it : an opening this categorical makes disagreement personal and closes the discussion before it starts.", "Great paper, really great work, well done to everyone involved : generic praise is noise to an expert room and delays the substance."], "scenarios": [{"id": "opening-a-discussion-well-s1", "room": "SCSA syllabus committee", "setup": "A SCSA syllabus committee is considering the summary of statewide consultation on a draft revised Music syllabus. The summary is long and reports mixed responses: support for a new contemporary context, concern about workload in the composition component and polarised views on aural assessment. The chair has introduced the item and invited comment. No one has yet spoken.", "said": "Thank you, everyone. You'll have seen the consultation report; there's a great deal in it, and some of the responses pull in opposite directions. I don't want us to try to resolve everything today. Who would like to start us off?", "task": "Open the item by setting the terms of the discussion: what the committee should focus on and in what order.", "seconds": 60, "models": [{"text": "I'm happy to start. My reading of the report is that most of it confirms the direction we've already agreed, and that the real work lies in two places: workload in composition, and aural assessment. On composition, the concern looks practical and fixable. On aural, the responses suggest a genuine disagreement about the subject, and I don't think we'll settle it by counting submissions. I'd suggest we spend most of our time there, and I'd be interested in how colleagues from the universities read those responses.", "why": "It orients, evaluates and invites in under a minute, and it ranks the issues so the committee knows where its time should go. The closing invitation is directed at members with relevant expertise."}, {"text": "Thank you, Chair. Could I suggest a way in? The responses seem to sort into three kinds: those that support the draft, those that want it adjusted, and those that question its premise. The third group is small, but it includes some careful submissions on aural assessment. If we deal quickly with the first two, we'll have time to take the third seriously, which I think it deserves.", "why": "It offers a structure for the whole item rather than a view on any part of it, which is a generous use of the anchor. Singling out the minority submissions protects them from being outvoted by volume."}], "checks": ["I did not apologise or clear my throat before starting.", "I identified no more than two priority issues.", "I suggested an order or allocation of time.", "I ended with an invitation to others."]}, {"id": "opening-a-discussion-well-s2", "room": "school council", "setup": "You chair a school council. The next item is the school's draft business plan priorities for the next three years, presented by the principal. The council has a formal role in endorsing the plan. Several members are new and unsure how much they are expected to contribute; one experienced member has strong views about the absence of a priority on student wellbeing. You must open the discussion as chair.", "said": "That's the draft, and I'd welcome the council's views. I've tried to keep it to five priorities so that it's achievable, but I'm very open to hearing where you think we have the emphasis wrong.", "task": "As chair, open the discussion by framing the decision and the criteria, without giving your own view first.", "seconds": 50, "models": [{"text": "Thank you, Principal. Just to be clear about our role: the council is asked to endorse the priorities, not to write the operational plan, so the useful questions for us are whether these five are the right ones and whether anything important is missing. I'd suggest we take those in order. I'll hold my own view until others have spoken, and I'd particularly like to hear from our newer members, who may see the draft with fresher eyes than the rest of us.", "why": "It states the council's role without lecturing, sets a sequence and explicitly makes room for new members. Withholding the chair's view protects the quality of the discussion."}, {"text": "Thank you. I'd suggest three questions to guide us. Do these priorities reflect what this community most needs from the school over the next three years? Is anything significant missing? And are they framed so that the council can tell, in three years, whether they've been achieved? Perhaps we could start with the second, since I suspect it's where members have most to say.", "why": "The three questions give the council criteria rather than opinions, including an accountability test that only a governing body would ask. Starting with omissions creates a natural place for the wellbeing concern."}], "checks": ["I did not state my own view on the priorities.", "I named what the council is being asked to do.", "I gave the room criteria or questions to work from.", "I made explicit space for members who might otherwise stay silent."]}, {"id": "opening-a-discussion-well-s3", "room": "festival association board", "setup": "The festival board is considering a proposal to move the festival from its long-standing university venue to a new performing arts centre in the city. The new venue offers better acoustics and more stages, but at a significantly higher hire cost. As treasurer you have prepared the financial comparison, and the President has asked you to open the item.", "said": "We've all had a chance to read the proposal, and I know there are strong feelings about leaving the university after so many years. Before we get into that, I've asked our treasurer to open, since the numbers will frame a lot of what follows.", "task": "Open with the financial picture in a way that informs the decision without letting finance become the only frame.", "seconds": 60, "models": [{"text": "Thank you. The short version is that the move is affordable, but only just, and only if entries hold at this year's level. The new venue costs substantially more, and we'd need either a modest rise in entry fees or a sponsor to cover the gap. So money doesn't rule the move out, but it does make it a decision with a real cost to schools. The question I'd put to the board is whether what the new venue offers educationally is worth that cost, and on that I'm one voice among many.", "why": "It gives the financial verdict in one sentence and then hands the decision back to the board on educational grounds. The final clause deliberately reduces the weight of the treasurer's voice on the question that follows."}, {"text": "I'll keep the numbers brief, because I don't think they should decide this on their own. Both options are viable. The new venue is more expensive and carries more risk if entries fall; the current one is cheaper, but we're already turning bands away. So the financial question is really a question about growth: if we believe the festival will keep growing, the move pays for itself within a few years. I'd be interested in whether the board thinks that's realistic.", "why": "It translates the numbers into the strategic assumption on which they depend, which is the question the board should debate. It neither advocates nor obstructs the move."}], "checks": ["I gave the financial position in one or two sentences.", "I named the non-financial question the board must answer.", "I did not use the numbers to decide the matter.", "I ended by inviting the board's view."]}, {"id": "opening-a-discussion-well-s4", "room": "university course advisory committee", "setup": "A university school of music's course advisory committee, which includes external members from the profession and the schools sector, is considering a proposal for a new jazz performance pathway within the Bachelor of Music. The proposal is detailed and well argued and has internal support, but its assumptions about the preparedness of incoming students are optimistic. You are the first external member invited to speak.", "said": "The proposal has been through the school's internal processes, so I'm particularly keen to hear from our external members today. Dijon, you see these students before we do. Would you like to begin?", "task": "Open the external members' contribution by affirming what is strong and naming the one assumption that most needs testing.", "seconds": 60, "models": [{"text": "Thank you. I think this is a strong proposal, and the sequencing of the improvisation units in particular is better than anything I've seen in a comparable course. My one substantial question is about who arrives. The proposal assumes entrants with secure jazz language, and from the schools' side, outside a handful of programs, that isn't who's coming through. That doesn't undermine the pathway, but it may mean the first year needs a different shape. I'd be interested in whether colleagues in the profession see the same gap.", "why": "The specific praise is credible and earns the right to the critical point that follows. It names one assumption from evidence only he holds and hands the next question to the other external members."}, {"text": "I'd like to start with a question rather than a view, if I may. What does the school expect a typical entrant to be able to do on the first day? I ask because the answer shapes almost everything else. If the expectation is fluent jazz language, the proposal is excellent but will serve a small cohort. If it's strong general musicianship, the first-year units may need rethinking. Either is defensible; I think the committee needs to know which it's endorsing.", "why": "Opening with a question of fact is a disciplined way to surface the weak assumption without asserting it. The two branches make clear that he is testing the proposal, not opposing it."}], "checks": ["I praised one specific feature, not the proposal in general.", "I named a single central assumption.", "I drew on what I see from the schools' side.", "I left room for the other external members."]}, {"id": "opening-a-discussion-well-s5", "room": "arts funding panel", "setup": "An arts funding panel follows the convention that a designated lead assessor opens discussion of each application. You are lead assessor for an application from a community choir seeking funds to commission and premiere a new work by a Noongar composer, in partnership with an Aboriginal community organisation. The application is artistically strong and its budget is sound, but the partnership documentation is thin.", "said": "Next is the choral commission. Dijon, you're lead on this one. We're running about ten minutes behind, so if you can give us your recommendation and the key issues, that would help.", "task": "Open with a clear provisional recommendation, the application's strength and its key issue, in a way that structures the panel's discussion.", "seconds": 50, "models": [{"text": "Thank you. My provisional recommendation is to fund, with one condition. Artistically this is among the strongest applications in the round, and the budget is realistic. The issue is the partnership. For a work of this kind, the relationship with the community organisation is not an administrative detail; it's central to whether the project is done properly. At present the evidence is one brief letter. I'd suggest funding conditional on a fuller partnership agreement, including cultural protocols and the composer's rights. I'd welcome views on whether that condition is enough.", "why": "It leads with the recommendation, as the chair asked, and explains why the gap matters substantively rather than procedurally. The proposed condition is specific and the invitation is precise."}, {"text": "Briefly: strong, sound, with one gap that matters. The artistic case and the budget are both clear. The partnership is described rather than evidenced, and for a commission involving a Noongar composer and community, that's the part I'd most want to be confident about. So my question for the panel is whether we treat it as a condition to attach or a reason to defer. I lean towards a condition, but I'd value others' views.", "why": "Its compressed opening respects the time pressure and gives the panel the shape of the issue at once. Posing condition versus deferral structures the discussion around the one real choice."}], "checks": ["I gave a recommendation in my first or second sentence.", "I named the partnership issue as substantive, not administrative.", "I offered a specific condition or choice for the panel.", "I kept within the time the chair asked for."]}, {"id": "opening-a-discussion-well-s6", "room": "conference panel", "setup": "You are on a panel at a national music education conference titled 'The future of specialist music programs in public schools'. The other panellists are a professor of music education, a senior education department official and a well-known performer. The moderator, an academic, has introduced the panel and turns to you first. The audience is several hundred teachers and administrators.", "said": "We've framed this session around the future, but I suspect many people here are anxious about the present: staffing, funding, the pressure on the timetable. So let me start with someone who runs one of these programs day to day. What do you think the real question is for specialist programs over the next decade?", "task": "Open the panel with a clear, arguable starting position that sets useful terms for the other panellists.", "seconds": 75, "models": [{"text": "Thank you. I'd put the question this way: not whether specialist programs survive, but whether they can justify themselves as public goods rather than private advantages. A selective program in a public school has to answer to every family whose child didn't get in. Programs that can show what they give back, to the whole school, to feeder primaries, to the wider community, will thrive. Those that can't will be the first to go when budgets tighten, and some of that scrutiny will be deserved. I'll be interested to hear whether the Department sees it the same way.", "why": "It takes a position sharp enough to argue with, which is what a panel opening needs. Ending on the official gives the moderator an obvious next move and puts policy in the conversation early."}, {"text": "I think the real question is about the pipeline. Specialist programs at secondary level depend on children arriving who have already started an instrument, and in much of the public system the primary years are where that is eroding. If that's right, the future of these programs is being decided in schools that don't have them. I offer that as a hypothesis, and I suspect the professor has better evidence than I do on whether it holds.", "why": "It relocates the question from the anxious present to its structural cause, which reorients the whole session. Offering it as a hypothesis and naming the professor turns the opening into a genuine invitation."}], "checks": ["I stated one clear position in my first two sentences.", "My position was arguable rather than a platitude.", "I handed the next question to a named fellow panellist.", "I spoke to the audience as well as to the moderator."]}]}, {"id": "testing-an-assumption", "name": "Testing an assumption", "family": "Testing", "what": "It moves the discussion from a proposal's conclusion to the unstated premise it depends on, and puts that premise to the room as an open question.", "when": "When a proposal is gathering momentum, its reasoning is sound, and everything turns on a premise nobody has stated, or one that was true once and may no longer be.", "teach": "Arguments in committee are rarely invalid. Where they fail is in their premises, and clever people defend conclusions far better than they inspect premises, because the premises arrive already agreed in the framing of the paper. Testing an assumption relocates the debate to the ground where the decision is actually being made. It works with highly educated rooms because it is the form of their own disciplines: the referee's report that says the analysis is careful but rests on a condition that has not been shown. It is also generous. You are not saying the proposer is wrong; you are asking whether a condition holds, and you are giving them the first chance to check it.\n\nThe craft is in choosing the premise. Not the weakest premise, the load-bearing one: the one which, if it fell, would take the recommendation with it. A weak but decorative premise wastes the move and spends your credit. Before you speak, run the test silently: if this were false, would we still be doing this? If the answer is yes, find another premise or say nothing.\n\nState the premise neutrally and in the proposer's own terms, ideally more clearly than they put it, so they recognise it as theirs. Acknowledge the reasoning in a clause, not a paragraph. Then ask a genuine question, one whose answer you do not presume. The whole contribution should take under thirty seconds.\n\nTiming matters more than wording. The move belongs after the proposal has been fully put and before the chair turns to implementation. Once the room is discussing dates and budgets, the premise has been silently adopted, and raising it then sounds like relitigating.\n\nThe voice is level and slightly slower on the premise itself, which is the sentence you want remembered. Look at the proposer as you state it, then open your gaze to the room as you ask. After the question, stop. Let the silence do the work; filling it with your own answer converts an inquiry into an argument.\n\nIt goes wrong in three familiar ways. Listing several assumptions reads as general scepticism and lets the room answer the easiest one. Asking a question to which you plainly know the answer is a trap, and a room of lawyers and academics will see it as one. And misreading the premise: if you are corrected, accept the correction at once and with grace. The room credits a member who clarifies more than one who wins.", "phrasings": ["The reasoning follows if one thing holds, and I would like us to check it: are we confident that the drop in numbers is about demand?", "Can I surface something the paper takes as given?", "Before we turn to the detail, it might help to name what this depends on. As I read it, everything turns on the venue being available on weekdays.", "I think the proposal is sound on its own premises. My question is about one of them.", "If it turned out that the cohort had not changed, would we still recommend this?", "What would have to be true for this to work? I count one thing, and I am not sure we have tested it.", "That was certainly the case when the policy was written. Is it still the case?", "I may be reading too much into the paper, but it seems to assume that schools will release students in term time. Do we know that?"], "avoid": ["I just think there are a lot of assumptions in here. : vague; it invites reassurance rather than inspection, and nobody can answer it.", "With respect, the whole thing rests on a false premise. : it delivers the verdict before the question, so the proposer has to defend rather than examine.", "Surely nobody seriously believes students will pay that. : sarcasm; it insults whoever does believe it, possibly the chair.", "Have we stopped to ask whether any of our assumptions are right? : a seminar gesture with no purchase; it names no premise and tests nothing."], "scenarios": [{"id": "testing-an-assumption-s1", "room": "SCSA syllabus committee", "setup": "The Music ATAR syllabus committee is reviewing the weighting of the aural and analysis examination. An Authority officer has tabled four years of cohort data and a recommendation to reduce the aural component's weighting. Several members teach in non-specialist schools and are sympathetic. The recommendation will go to the Board of the Authority if the committee endorses it today.", "said": "The data are fairly unambiguous. Aural scores have drifted down for four successive cohorts, and the drift is steepest in schools without a specialist program. I think we have to accept that the student coming through now has a different musical formation, and the weighting ought to reflect what we can reasonably expect of them.", "task": "Surface the premise that the decline reflects students rather than the paper or the provision, and ask whether it has been tested.", "seconds": 60, "models": [{"text": "The trend is clear, and I do not doubt it. What I would like to check is the causal step. The recommendation assumes the drift is in the students, rather than in the paper or in the provision. Over those four years, did the item types or the marking guide change? And did specialist-program students drift as well? If they held steady, we may be looking at a provision problem, and reweighting would treat the symptom.", "why": "It concedes the data in one sentence, isolates the causal premise precisely, and proposes two checks that could actually separate the explanations."}, {"text": "Can I ask about what the recommendation takes as given? It reads the decline as a change in students' musical formation. That may well be right. But if the cause were reduced timetabled aural teaching in non-specialist schools, the remedy would be support for teachers, not a change to the weighting. Do we have anything that distinguishes those two?", "why": "It frames the premise as a reading of the paper, grants that it may be right, and shows the room why the answer changes the remedy."}], "checks": ["I named one premise, not several.", "I acknowledged the data before questioning the inference.", "I asked a question whose answer I did not already claim to know.", "I stopped speaking after the question."]}, {"id": "testing-an-assumption-s2", "room": "university academic board", "setup": "The academic board of a Perth university is considering a proposal from the Deputy Vice-Chancellor (Education) to move first-year lectures across several schools, including the conservatorium's history and analysis units, to recorded-only delivery. Attendance has fallen sharply since 2020. The proposal frees lecture theatres and staff time for small-group teaching. It has the support of most deans.", "said": "Attendance at first-year lectures is now below a third in most units. Students are voting with their feet; they are telling us, quite clearly, that the live lecture is not where they learn. I would rather redeploy that effort into tutorials, where the evidence for active learning is strong, than keep performing to empty rooms.", "task": "Name the premise that low attendance means students do not value live teaching, and ask whether another explanation has been ruled out.", "seconds": 55, "models": [{"text": "I am sympathetic to the redeployment. The step I would want to test is the reading of the attendance figure. The proposal treats it as a judgement on the lecture. It could equally be a judgement on the timetable: students working twenty hours a week will choose the recording whatever its merits. Do we know which it is? Because if it is the timetable, we may be removing the thing they would attend if they could.", "why": "It accepts the destination, isolates the inference, and offers a concrete rival explanation without claiming it is the right one."}, {"text": "One question before we vote. The case rests on attendance being a signal of value. Has anyone asked the students who stopped coming why they stopped? If the answer is paid work, we have a scheduling problem; if the answer is that the lectures add nothing, the proposal is right. The survey would take a fortnight.", "why": "It reduces the premise to a single testable question and attaches a cheap, quick way to answer it."}], "checks": ["I stated the premise in the proposer's terms.", "I offered one rival explanation, not a list.", "I did not argue against the redeployment itself.", "I kept to under a minute."]}, {"id": "testing-an-assumption-s3", "room": "school council", "setup": "The school council is endorsing the next year's contributions and charges schedule. The business manager proposes raising the specialist music program's instrumental levy by a substantial amount to cover rising repair and hire costs. The council includes parent representatives, two staff members and a community member. Approval is expected to be a formality.", "said": "We benchmarked against comparable programs and we are still below most of them. More to the point, we raised it modestly last year and received no complaints at all, not one. I think that tells us the families in this catchment can absorb it.", "task": "Test the premise that the absence of complaints indicates capacity to pay.", "seconds": 45, "models": [{"text": "The benchmarking is useful. I would be cautious about the second point, though. The proposal reads silence as capacity. In my experience, the families who struggle with a charge rarely complain; they quietly do not enrol, or they withdraw at the end of Year 9. Do we know whether last year's rise changed our uptake or our withdrawals? If it did not, I am comfortable. If it did, the silence was telling us something else.", "why": "It brings practitioner knowledge to bear on the premise without accusation, and makes approval conditional on a fact the business manager can check."}, {"text": "Can I ask about what we are inferring from no complaints? Families do not usually complain to a school council about a voluntary charge; they make a private decision. Before we read silence as consent, could we look at how many families applied for hardship assistance after last year's increase?", "why": "It explains briefly why the inference is weak and redirects to an existing, more reliable measure."}], "checks": ["I credited the benchmarking before testing the inference.", "I identified the specific inference: silence as capacity.", "I proposed a measure that already exists.", "I did not imply the business manager was indifferent to families."]}, {"id": "testing-an-assumption-s4", "room": "festival association board", "setup": "The board of the association that runs the state schools' jazz festival is reviewing the next festival's dates. Venue hire on weekends has risen sharply, and the president proposes moving the festival to a Thursday and Friday during term. The treasurer has confirmed the saving would be real. Registrations open in six weeks.", "said": "The saving is significant, roughly a fifth of our venue budget. And frankly, a weekday festival is more educational: it treats jazz as part of the school week, not an extracurricular hobby. Schools release students for sport carnivals all the time; I cannot see why music would be different.", "task": "Surface the assumption that principals will release ensembles in term time on the same basis as sport, and ask how it has been tested.", "seconds": 50, "models": [{"text": "I like the argument about jazz belonging in the school week. The whole saving depends on one thing, though: that principals release twenty-piece ensembles for two days in Term 3 the way they release teams for a carnival. Sport carnivals are usually whole-school events with relief already budgeted. Have we asked any principals? If three or four of our large schools say no, the saving disappears with the registrations.", "why": "It honours the educational argument, names the premise exactly, and shows its connection to the financial case the board cares about."}, {"text": "Could we test the comparison with sport before we commit? It assumes the release decision is the same. For a festival it often falls to the music teacher to argue for relief cover across several year groups. I would be happy to ring five principals this week and report back before registrations open.", "why": "It questions the analogy precisely and volunteers the test, which turns a doubt into a contribution."}], "checks": ["I named the premise in one sentence.", "I connected the premise to the money, not just to principle.", "I suggested a way to test it within the timeline.", "I avoided predicting that schools would refuse."]}, {"id": "testing-an-assumption-s5", "room": "principal's office", "setup": "The principal and an associate principal have asked to meet with you about restructuring performing arts leadership. Their proposal is to create a single Head of Performing Arts across music, drama and dance, with the current Teacher in Charge roles reduced. You have a stake in the outcome, and they know it. The discussion is informal but the direction is largely set.", "said": "We think a single line of leadership is cleaner. At the moment, when something goes wrong between music and drama, there's no one person who owns it, and it ends up on my desk. One head, one budget, one voice at executive. It's the model most large schools are moving to.", "task": "Test the premise that the problem is the number of leaders rather than the clarity of their authority, without sounding self-interested.", "seconds": 60, "models": [{"text": "I can see why one line of accountability is attractive, and I would rather say this now than after the structure is set. The proposal assumes the difficulty is that there are two leaders. My reading is that the difficulty is that neither has defined authority over shared resources: the theatre, the calendar, the budget lines. If that is right, one head would solve it, but so would a clear protocol, at lower cost. Which problem are we solving?", "why": "It names the premise plainly, offers a rival diagnosis with concrete examples, and ends on a question that keeps the principal's options open."}, {"text": "Can I test one thing, knowing I am not a neutral party? The case assumes the conflicts come from structure. When you think of the last three that reached your desk, were they about who was in charge, or about the calendar and the theatre? If they were about who was in charge, I take the point. If they were about shared resources, I wonder whether we are fixing the right thing.", "why": "Declaring the interest disarms the obvious objection, and asking the principal to test the premise against her own cases lets her reach the conclusion herself."}], "checks": ["I acknowledged the appeal of the proposal first.", "I distinguished the number of leaders from the clarity of authority.", "I did not argue for my own role.", "I ended with a question the principal could answer from her own experience."]}, {"id": "testing-an-assumption-s6", "room": "arts funding panel", "setup": "You are on a state arts funding panel assessing youth music applications. A regional youth orchestra has applied for a two-year program. Another panel member, an experienced program manager, recommends against, citing previous acquittals. The panel is ranking applications against a limited pool and has three more to discuss before lunch.", "said": "I've seen a number of these regional applications over the years and the pattern is consistent: they project ambitious participant numbers and they underdeliver at acquittal, sometimes by forty per cent. I don't doubt the intent, but past performance is the best predictor we have, and the guidelines ask us to weigh deliverability.", "task": "Test whether past underdelivery reflects the applicant's capacity or a condition of the funding itself.", "seconds": 45, "models": [{"text": "Deliverability is the right criterion. I would want to know what the pattern is a pattern of. If regional underdelivery tracks the timing of our own grant releases, so that funds arrive after the school term has planned around them, then the acquittals measure our process as much as their capacity. Do we know when those previous grants were paid relative to the project start?", "why": "It accepts the criterion, questions what the evidence measures, and points to a fact the panel's own records can supply."}, {"text": "Can I ask whether those were acquittals from this organisation or from regional applicants generally? The recommendation seems to assume that the general pattern predicts this applicant. Their own last acquittal is in the attachments, and as I read it they delivered close to target.", "why": "It distinguishes a base rate from the specific case, and brings the room back to the applicant's own record without making it a contest."}], "checks": ["I accepted the guideline the colleague cited.", "I identified what the evidence was actually measuring.", "I pointed to information available in the room.", "I did not accuse the colleague of bias against regional applicants."]}]}, {"id": "the-evidence-question", "name": "The evidence question", "family": "Testing", "what": "It asks what a claim rests on in a way that invites the speaker to show their working, and lets the room set the weight of the claim to match its support.", "when": "When an assertion is carrying more of the decision than its stated basis can bear, or when 'the research shows' is being used to close a discussion rather than inform it.", "teach": "In committee, claims gather authority through confidence, seniority and repetition. By the third time a claim has been cited, it has become the room's premise, whatever its origin. The evidence question restores proportion. It works with highly educated audiences because asking for the basis of a claim is a norm of their own professions; nobody in an academic board or an Authority committee can object to the question in principle. The whole risk is in tone. Asked as curiosity, it is collegial. Asked as challenge, it is cross-examination, and the speaker's first task becomes defending face rather than producing evidence.\n\nAsk for the kind of evidence, not for proof. Is this from our own data or the wider literature? How many schools does it reflect? Was that a review or a single study? These questions are specific, answerable and neutral. Where it is plausible, give the speaker a graceful route: it may be in an appendix I have not absorbed. That small courtesy changes the temperature of the exchange.\n\nThe senior part of the move is what you do when the evidence turns out to be weaker than presented: anecdotal, a single site, a different jurisdiction, a different population. Do not pounce. Resize the claim instead of refuting it. Restate it at the strength the evidence supports, and propose that the decision be scaled to match: a strong signal from one school is enough to pilot on and not yet enough to mandate. This leaves the speaker's judgement intact, gives the room a better decision, and makes you the member who improved the proposal rather than the one who embarrassed its author.\n\nTiming is early. The question belongs in the first few minutes after the claim is made, before others build on it. The voice is interested and a little lighter than your ordinary register; the body is open, hands visible, attention on the speaker as someone whose answer you want.\n\nIt goes wrong in four ways. Asking for evidence only of claims you dislike: the room notices the asymmetry quickly, and your standing as an honest broker goes with it. Demanding a standard the domain cannot supply, such as randomised trials in arts education, which looks like obstruction. Following an honest 'I do not have that to hand' with a second, harder question, which turns inquiry into humiliation. And failing to apply the same question to your own claims, which is the surest way to lose the right to ask it.", "phrasings": ["That is an important claim for this decision. What is it based on?", "Is that from our own data or from the wider literature? Both matter, but in different ways.", "I would find it helpful to know how strong that finding is before we lean on it.", "When we say the research is clear, which research are we thinking of?", "How many schools does that reflect?", "So, if I have understood, it is a strong signal from one site. That seems enough to pilot on, and not yet enough to mandate.", "Would it be possible to have that tabled before we finalise? I suspect it supports the recommendation, and I would like the minutes to show it.", "Do we know that, or is it what we would expect to find?"], "avoid": ["Where is your evidence for that? : bare and prosecutorial; the speaker must defend their standing before they can address the substance.", "I have read the literature and it says the opposite. : trumps rather than inquires, and turns the meeting into a citation contest.", "That is just anecdotal. : dismissive, and many committee decisions properly rest on practitioner judgement.", "I am not sure that would survive peer review. : condescending in a room that is not a journal, and it implies the speaker is not a scholar."], "scenarios": [{"id": "the-evidence-question-s1", "room": "SCSA assessment committee", "setup": "A Curriculum and Assessment committee is considering whether to move the Music ATAR practical examination from school-based recording to externally set, live examination at central venues. The change would be expensive and logistically demanding for regional schools. An Authority officer is presenting the case. Members are inclined to agree because the language of equity is persuasive.", "said": "There is good research showing that externally conducted practical examinations reduce inequity between schools. When the conditions are standardised, the advantage of well-resourced schools with recording studios largely disappears. That is the core of the case.", "task": "Ask what the research is, and if it proves narrower than described, scale the claim to what it supports.", "seconds": 60, "models": [{"text": "That would be a strong argument, and I would like us to have it in front of us. Which studies are we drawing on? I ask because the equity effects can run both ways: standardised venues remove the studio advantage, but they add travel and accommodation costs for regional students. If the research is from a jurisdiction with comparable distances, it carries real weight here. If not, we might want to model the regional cost before deciding.", "why": "It asks for the source warmly, explains exactly why the context of the research matters, and proposes a proportionate next step."}, {"text": "Thank you. Can I ask a follow-up, since I take the point seriously? Is that research a review of several systems, or one interstate evaluation? If it is one evaluation, I would still find it persuasive as a direction. I would want to pilot central examination in one region and compare before committing every school to it.", "why": "It anticipates the likely answer and resizes the claim in advance, offering a pilot rather than a rejection."}], "checks": ["I asked for the kind of evidence, not for proof.", "I explained why the source mattered to this decision.", "I proposed a decision scaled to the evidence.", "My tone was curious rather than adversarial."]}, {"id": "the-evidence-question-s2", "room": "faculty curriculum committee", "setup": "A university faculty curriculum committee is reviewing the Bachelor of Music structure. The Head of School proposes cutting a core theory unit to make room for a compulsory ensemble-practice unit across all majors. The proposal has been framed around graduate employability. You are a practitioner member of the committee.", "said": "Employers are telling us, very consistently, that our graduates lack ensemble skills. They can play, but they cannot rehearse, lead a section or adapt in real time. That is the feedback we keep hearing, and I think we have an obligation to respond to it.", "task": "Find out what the employer feedback consists of, without diminishing it, and calibrate the decision accordingly.", "seconds": 50, "models": [{"text": "That rings true to me, and I want to make sure we can defend it. When we say employers, who are we hearing from, and how? If it is a structured survey of the people who hire graduates, it is a strong basis for restructuring the core. If it is conversations at industry evenings, it is still valuable, but I would want to test it before we remove a unit everyone else relies on.", "why": "It affirms the claim's plausibility, distinguishes types of evidence clearly, and connects the strength of evidence to the size of the change."}, {"text": "Could we say a bit more about the feedback? I ask partly because I would expect bandleaders and orchestral managers to describe the problem differently, and the unit we design would differ depending on which we are hearing. Is there anything written down we could circulate?", "why": "It turns the evidence question into a design question, which makes it clearly constructive."}], "checks": ["I said the claim sounded plausible.", "I asked who the employers were and how they were consulted.", "I linked the evidence to the scale of the change.", "I did not dismiss informal feedback as worthless."]}, {"id": "the-evidence-question-s3", "room": "school council", "setup": "The school council is discussing a parent submission proposing that the school day start forty minutes later for wellbeing reasons. The proposal would affect bus contracts, instrumental tuition schedules and before-school ensemble rehearsals. A parent representative is presenting it with conviction. The council can recommend the proposal for consultation or decline it.", "said": "Most families want this, and the adolescent sleep research is overwhelming. We ran a poll and the response was clear. I really don't think this is controversial among parents.", "task": "Separate the sleep research from the claim about parent opinion, and ask what the poll can tell the council.", "seconds": 55, "models": [{"text": "The sleep research is well established, and I think it deserves a proper look. The claim about families is a separate question. How was the poll run, and how many responded? If it was a social media poll, it tells us there is real interest, which is enough to justify a formal survey. I would be glad to support consultation on that basis. I would be cautious about telling the community that most families already want it.", "why": "It grants the strong part of the claim, inquires precisely about the weaker part, and supports the next step the evidence warrants."}, {"text": "Can I check the poll? I am not doubting the interest; I would like to know whose views it captures. Families with students in before-school ensembles would be affected most, and they may not have seen it. If we take this to consultation, could we make sure the survey reaches them directly?", "why": "It identifies a likely gap in the sample and converts it into a practical improvement to the process."}], "checks": ["I distinguished the research from the poll.", "I asked how the poll was conducted.", "I supported the step the evidence justified.", "I did not suggest the parent representative had misled the council."]}, {"id": "the-evidence-question-s4", "room": "principal's office", "setup": "You are in a planning meeting with the principal, a deputy and the head of learning data. The deputy is reviewing the school's ATAR results and has suggested that the music courses are lowering the school's median. You have an obvious interest in the answer, and everyone in the room knows it. How you ask matters as much as what you ask.", "said": "I've looked at the subject-level results and music is sitting below the school median. When you consider how many of our strongest students are in the specialist program, I would have expected the reverse. I think we need to ask whether the course is serving them.", "task": "Ask what the analysis is based on, in a way that is clearly not defensive, and propose how to read it properly.", "seconds": 65, "models": [{"text": "That is a fair question to ask of any course, and I would rather we look at it together than have it sit unresolved. Can I ask which figure we are using? Raw course marks, scaled scores and ATAR contributions tell different stories in music, because scaling depends on the whole state cohort. If we can see our students' scaled results against their results in their other subjects, we will know whether the course is serving them. I am happy to be held to that.", "why": "It welcomes scrutiny, asks precisely which measure is in use, names the technical distinction that matters, and proposes the right comparison."}, {"text": "I want to take this seriously, so may I ask how the comparison was made? With a cohort of our size, two or three results can move the median noticeably. If the head of data could show us the last three years rather than this one, and compare each student's music result with their other subjects, I think we would have a fair test.", "why": "It identifies the small-number problem without sounding defensive, and offers a test the data team can run, with the speaker accepting its outcome in advance."}], "checks": ["I made clear I welcomed the scrutiny.", "I asked which measure was being used.", "I proposed a fairer comparison rather than rejecting the finding.", "I avoided sounding defensive about my own program."]}, {"id": "the-evidence-question-s5", "room": "festival association board", "setup": "The festival association board is discussing a drop in school registrations for the competitive sections of the jazz festival. A board member proposes abolishing adjudicated placings and moving to a non-competitive, feedback-only format. The board has a long tradition of awards and sponsors attached to them. A decision affects next year's sponsorship agreements.", "said": "It's clear that the competitive format is putting schools off. That's why registrations have dropped two years running. I've heard it directly from teachers; one told me in writing that her principal won't let the band enter if they might come last.", "task": "Treat the teacher's account seriously while establishing how widely it applies before restructuring the festival.", "seconds": 50, "models": [{"text": "That is important, and I am glad we have it in writing. Before we change the format, could we find out how representative it is? Registrations can fall for several reasons: term dates, staffing, the cost of buses. If we sent a short survey to the schools that did not return, we would know within a month whether placings are the cause. If they are, I would support the change.", "why": "It honours the evidence offered, lists alternative causes briefly, proposes a quick test, and commits in advance to the outcome."}, {"text": "So we have one clear account and a sense from conversations. That is enough, I think, to try a non-competitive stream alongside the existing sections next year and see who registers for it. It is not yet enough, for me, to remove placings our sponsors have funded for years.", "why": "It resizes the claim exactly to its support and translates that into a proportionate decision."}], "checks": ["I treated the teacher's account as real evidence.", "I named other plausible causes.", "I proposed a decision scaled to the evidence.", "I committed to acting if the evidence confirmed the claim."]}, {"id": "the-evidence-question-s6", "room": "conference panel", "setup": "You are on a panel at a national music education conference on senior secondary assessment. A fellow panellist, a respected assessment researcher, has argued that improvisation should not be assessed for university entrance purposes. The audience includes teachers and Authority staff from several states. The exchange is public and being recorded.", "said": "I'd love it to be otherwise, but the reliability evidence simply isn't there. Inter-rater agreement on improvisation is consistently weaker than on prepared repertoire, and for a high-stakes measure that's disqualifying. We can value improvisation enormously and still decline to put an ATAR on it.", "task": "Ask what the reliability claim rests on, in public and with collegial grace, and keep the question open rather than scoring a point.", "seconds": 55, "models": [{"text": "That is an argument I take seriously, so may I ask about the studies? My understanding is that agreement rises considerably when markers are trained on exemplars and the criteria are specific to the style. Were the studies you have in mind using general performance criteria, or style-specific ones? If it is the former, I wonder whether the problem lies in the rubric rather than the activity.", "why": "It acknowledges the colleague's expertise, asks a precise methodological question, and raises an alternative explanation as a question, not a verdict."}, {"text": "I would be interested to know the comparison point. Is improvisation less reliable than prepared performance, or less reliable than a threshold we have set for high-stakes use? Prepared repertoire is not perfectly reliable either. If we could see where both sit against that threshold, the audience could judge the gap for themselves.", "why": "It reframes the claim as a comparison and invites the evidence into the open, without contesting the researcher's data."}], "checks": ["I acknowledged the researcher's expertise.", "I asked about method, not about competence.", "I offered an alternative explanation as a question.", "I left the panellist room to answer well in public."]}]}, {"id": "the-precise-question", "name": "The precise question", "family": "Testing", "what": "It replaces a general debate with one exact question whose answer decides the matter, so the room stops trading positions and starts answering something.", "when": "When discussion has gone round twice, when speakers are agreeing or disagreeing past each other, or when a single unknown fact or commitment is the real hinge of the decision.", "teach": "Committee discussion tends towards breadth. Each speaker adds a consideration, and within twenty minutes the room holds a dozen of them and no way of choosing between them. The precise question collapses that space. A question that can be answered with a number, a yes or no, a name or a date moves the conversation onto ground where it can resolve. Clever rooms respect it because it does their work for them: it shows you have listened to everything and found the hinge.\n\nThe question must pass two tests. It must be answerable, by someone present or by someone who can be asked before the next meeting. And it must be decisive: whatever the answer, the room knows what to do next. Run the test silently before you speak. If the answer were yes, what follows? If no? If both answers lead to the same place, it is not the question, however sharp it sounds.\n\nAddress it to the person who can answer it, by name or role. Ask it once. Do not preface it with three sentences explaining why you are asking. Ask first; then, if the room needs it, add one sentence after the question on why its answer decides the matter. Do not attach a second question, because the double question lets the respondent choose the easier half. The question itself should ideally be under fifteen words. Its brevity is its authority.\n\nTiming is after the second circuit of discussion, when the room has felt that it is not converging, or just before the chair begins a summary that would paper over the hinge. Asked too early, the precise question can seem to cut short people who have not yet been heard.\n\nThe voice should fall at the end of the question rather than rise. A falling contour marks a question that expects an answer; a rising one sounds like wondering aloud, and invites more wondering. Then stop and hold the gaze of the person you asked. The silence that follows is part of the move, and it is uncomfortable only for the person who must answer.\n\nIt goes wrong in four ways. The question is precise but not decisive: trivia, which costs credibility. The question exposes someone publicly when it could have been asked in the corridor; a precise question about a colleague's missed deadline is a reprimand. The overloaded question, with nested clauses, which nobody can answer cleanly. And the leading question, which clever rooms detect at once and resist on principle.", "phrasings": ["Can I ask one question? Who owns this after July?", "What would it cost, in dollars, to run it for one more year?", "Is this a decision for us, or a recommendation to the principal?", "If we do nothing, what happens on the first of February?", "Does the Authority require this, or permit it?", "Has anyone asked the students?", "If we could fund only one of these, which would it be?", "Do we need to decide this today?"], "avoid": ["I suppose my question, and it is more of a comment really, is... : the preface dissolves the question before it arrives.", "Can I ask what we think about the broader implications for the program and, relatedly, whether the timeline works? : two questions, neither exact; the room will answer the vaguer one.", "Is it not the case that we would obviously lose money? : leading; it invites resistance rather than an answer.", "Just so I am really clear, and correct me if I am wrong, because I may have missed something... : the hedging buries the question and signals that it need not be answered."], "scenarios": [{"id": "the-precise-question-s1", "room": "school council", "setup": "The school council has spent forty minutes on the uniform supply arrangement. Parents have raised quality and price; staff have raised the administrative burden of changing supplier; the chair is trying to find a way to close the item. Everyone has spoken, and the positions have not moved. The business manager is present.", "said": "I think we're all agreed the current arrangement isn't ideal. The question is whether the disruption of changing is worth it. My instinct is that we go to market but take our time over it, perhaps eighteen months, so families aren't caught mid-cycle.", "task": "Ask the one factual question that determines whether the council can act at all.", "seconds": 30, "models": [{"text": "We have heard every side of this, and I think one fact governs it. Can I ask the business manager: does the current contract allow us to exit before 2028 without penalty? If it does, we can go to market on the chair's timeline. If it does not, the question answers itself for now.", "why": "It is a single, answerable question directed at the right person, and the answer determines whether the rest of the discussion is relevant."}, {"text": "One question that might settle the timing. What is the earliest date we can give notice under the existing contract? If it is next year, the eighteen-month plan works neatly. If it is later, we should be planning around that date rather than debating the principle.", "why": "It narrows the chair's own proposal to the fact that governs it, and gives the room a date to plan around."}], "checks": ["I asked one question.", "I addressed it to the person who could answer it.", "Both possible answers lead to a clear next step.", "I did not preface it with an explanation."]}, {"id": "the-precise-question-s2", "room": "SCSA syllabus committee", "setup": "The Music syllabus committee is debating whether the revised ATAR course should include a prescribed list of set works for each context, or only list the musical elements and concepts to be studied. Teachers on the committee are split: some want certainty, some want freedom to choose repertoire. The debate has gone round twice. An Authority officer with responsibility for examination design is present.", "said": "I understand the appeal of a list, but every list dates, and it narrows what teachers can do with their particular students. If the syllabus specifies concepts clearly enough, any well-chosen work will do. I'd rather trust teachers than prescribe.", "task": "Ask the question about the examination that will determine whether teachers actually have the freedom being described.", "seconds": 35, "models": [{"text": "Could I ask the examination officer one thing? Would a question in the written paper ever require knowledge of a specific work that is not named in the syllabus? If the answer is no, teachers genuinely have the freedom we are describing. If it is sometimes, they will build their own list anyway, and we would do better to write it.", "why": "It moves the debate from philosophy to the examination, which is what teachers actually respond to, and the answer resolves whether freedom of choice is real."}, {"text": "Can I put one question to the officer? Under the concepts-only model, how would the examiners write the unseen analysis questions so that no school's choice of repertoire becomes an advantage? I ask because that is where teachers will feel the difference between the two models, far more than in the syllabus document.", "why": "It asks how the preferred model would actually work in the paper, which tests it constructively rather than opposing it."}], "checks": ["I asked about the examination, not about principle.", "I directed the question to the officer responsible.", "The answer would change what the committee decides.", "I asked it in one sentence."]}, {"id": "the-precise-question-s3", "room": "festival association board", "setup": "The festival association board is choosing between two venues for next year's festival. One is cheaper but further from public transport; the other is central but more expensive. Board members have spent half an hour on acoustics, parking, staging and atmosphere. The president is ready to call for a show of hands. The events coordinator has been negotiating with both venues.", "said": "On balance I lean towards the central venue. It's more expensive, but it's a better experience for the students and it lifts the profile of the festival. I think sponsors will notice the difference.", "task": "Ask the question about the central venue that determines whether choosing it is safe.", "seconds": 30, "models": [{"text": "Before we vote, one question for the coordinator. Has the central venue confirmed our dates in writing, with a refundable deposit? If it has, I am happy to support it. If it has not, we would be choosing a venue we may not actually have, and the cheaper option becomes the safer one.", "why": "It identifies the practical condition that would make the board's preference meaningless if unmet, and says in one sentence what each answer means."}, {"text": "One question that might decide it. If we choose the central venue, what is the total cost difference once the sponsor contribution we have already been offered is taken into account? If the gap is small, the case for the central venue is strong. If it is large, the board should see the number before it votes.", "why": "It reduces the debate about atmosphere and profile to a single figure that the board can weigh honestly."}], "checks": ["I asked before the vote, not after.", "The question concerned a fact, not an opinion.", "I directed it to the coordinator.", "I did not restate the arguments already made."]}, {"id": "the-precise-question-s4", "room": "university academic committee", "setup": "A university academic committee is considering a new double degree combining music and education. The proposal has strong support from both deans. Discussion has focused on curriculum design, accreditation and student demand. Nobody has addressed what happens if enrolments are modest. A finance representative is present.", "said": "The market research is encouraging and the accreditation pathway is clear. I think the design is elegant, and it answers a real need in the state for specialist music teachers. My only concern is that we launch it well.", "task": "Ask the question about risk that the enthusiasm in the room has bypassed.", "seconds": 35, "models": [{"text": "I share the enthusiasm, and I would like the degree to survive its first few years. So, one question: which faculty carries the cost if first-year enrolments fall below the threshold for viability? If that is agreed now, a slow start is manageable. If it is not, it becomes a dispute between two deans in the second year.", "why": "It names the risk that both deans have an interest in not raising, in a single answerable question."}, {"text": "One question for finance. What is the minimum intake at which the degree covers its own teaching costs, and how does that compare with the market research? If the research comfortably clears it, we can approve with confidence. If it barely clears it, we should build in a review point.", "why": "It turns optimism into a number, which allows the committee to support the degree with its eyes open."}], "checks": ["I asked about the risk the room had avoided.", "The question had a factual answer.", "I did not oppose the degree.", "I asked it of the person who could answer it."]}, {"id": "the-precise-question-s5", "room": "principal's office", "setup": "You are meeting with the principal and the business manager about next year's staffing. The business manager has proposed reducing contemporary ensemble provision by one group. The discussion has moved between budget, timetabling and student numbers without settling. You suspect the real constraint has not been named.", "said": "I appreciate the ensembles matter to students, but we have to find savings somewhere, and the contemporary groups are the most expensive per student. I'm not trying to target music; I'm trying to balance a budget.", "task": "Ask the question that identifies whether the constraint is money or staffing allocation, because the remedies differ.", "seconds": 40, "models": [{"text": "Can I ask one thing? Is the constraint the dollar budget, or the staffing allocation? If it is the dollars, there may be other places to find them, and I would be glad to look at the music budget with you. If it is the teacher hours, that is a different conversation, and I would rather we had that one directly.", "why": "It asks a single decisive question and explains in two short sentences why the answer matters, which keeps it collaborative."}, {"text": "Is the saving you need in dollars, or in teacher hours? I ask because the two lead to quite different answers. One might be solved through charges or sponsorship; the other only through the timetable. Once I know which, I can come back to you with options rather than objections.", "why": "In the starkest form, it gives the business manager a question that can be answered directly and that frames everything after."}], "checks": ["I asked one question.", "The answer would change the solution.", "I did not argue for the ensemble before the question was answered.", "My tone was collaborative."]}, {"id": "the-precise-question-s6", "room": "arts funding panel", "setup": "The arts funding panel is discussing a new chamber music project from a small organisation. Members have debated whether the organisation is 'ready' for the grant size it has requested. The conversation has been about reputation, past work and the artistic director's experience. There is not enough in the pool to fund everything at the requested level.", "said": "I like the artistic vision very much, but I'm not sure they're ready for a grant of this size. It's a big step up for a small organisation, and I'd hate to see them overextend themselves.", "task": "Ask the question that turns 'readiness' into something the panel can decide.", "seconds": 35, "models": [{"text": "Could we ask a more specific question than readiness? If we funded at sixty per cent of the request, would the project still happen? If it would, we can support a strong vision at a scale that limits the risk. If it would not, we are deciding whether to back them in full, and we should say so.", "why": "It makes 'readiness' concrete and gives the panel a decision it can make within its limited pool."}, {"text": "May I ask the program officer one thing? Does the budget show a version of the project at a lower scale, or is it all or nothing? The application should tell us, and the answer would let us move from whether they are ready to what we can actually fund from this pool.", "why": "It asks a factual question about the application itself, which the panel can answer from the papers in front of them."}], "checks": ["I turned an abstract concern into a concrete question.", "The question could be answered from the application or by the officer.", "I did not dismiss the colleague's concern.", "The answer leads directly to a funding decision."]}]}, {"id": "stress-testing-with-a-scenario", "name": "Stress-testing with a scenario", "family": "Testing", "what": "It moves a proposal from the abstract into a concrete case, a counterfactual or an edge case, so the room can see how it behaves under pressure before it is adopted.", "when": "When a policy is being agreed in principle and nobody has walked it through a real instance, especially a rule about eligibility, exceptions, appeals, money or safety.", "teach": "Abstract policies are agreed easily because each member imagines the typical case, and each imagines a slightly different one. A scenario makes everyone imagine the same case, and usually an untypical one. It is how law, medicine and engineering test their rules: the hard case, the hypothetical, the load at the limit. Highly educated audiences recognise the method and respect it. It is also face-saving. You are not attacking the proposal; you are offering it a trial run, and if it passes, you have strengthened it.\n\nChoose a case that is plausible, specific and brief. Use roles, not people; a date; a number. 'Say a Year 11 student transfers in Term 2 with Grade 7 performance and no portfolio.' Then ask the room, not yourself, what the policy does with that student, and let them run it. If the proposal handles the case, say so plainly. If it does not, offer a drafting fix or ask the proposer for one.\n\nThe best scenario is the most likely difficult case, not the most extreme. The extreme case lets everyone say that will never happen, and the move is lost. Two kinds of case are especially productive. The boundary case: the student who scores exactly the threshold, or one mark either side. And the conflict case: two rules that point in different directions, so the room must decide which wins. A third is the backward test: if this rule had been in place last year, what would we have done in the situation we all remember?\n\nTiming is once in-principle support is forming and before the wording is finalised. Earlier, the scenario seems premature; later, it seems obstructive. Put the scenario in two sentences and the question in one.\n\nThis is the one Testing move that permits narrative pace. Slow slightly, as you would when telling a story, so the room can picture it. Lean in a little; the hands can sketch the case. Then hand it over with the question and wait.\n\nIt goes wrong in four ways. The parade of hypotheticals: one, perhaps two, and more makes you look as though you are obstructing. The lurid edge case, the litigation or the injury, which raises anxiety without informing the drafting. The scenario that is really a disguised anecdote about an identifiable student or colleague, which breaches confidentiality and makes the debate personal. And answering the scenario yourself before the room has tried, which turns a test into a speech.", "phrasings": ["Could we walk it through one case?", "Say it is the night before the festival and the adjudicator withdraws. Under this policy, who decides?", "What happens to the student who scores exactly sixty-five?", "Imagine it is 2029 and enrolments have halved. Does this still work?", "Let me give it the hardest likely case rather than the hardest possible one.", "Suppose both conditions apply at once. Which one prevails?", "If this had been in place last year, what would we have done differently in Term 3?", "I think it passes that test, which is reassuring. May I try one more?"], "avoid": ["What if a meteor hits the concert hall? : an absurd case trivialises the method and the proposer will rightly ignore it.", "Well, what if this, and what if that, and also what if... : a parade of cases reads as obstruction, and none gets examined properly.", "What about the girl in 9B whose father wrote to us last week? : identifies an individual, breaches confidentiality, and makes the policy debate personal.", "I can tell you exactly what will happen: chaos. : predicts instead of testing, and asks the room to trust your pessimism over their own reasoning."], "scenarios": [{"id": "stress-testing-with-a-scenario-s1", "room": "school council", "setup": "The school council is reviewing a draft policy on academic standing for students in the selective specialist music program. The draft says that any program student who receives a grade below C in two or more subjects in a semester will be placed on review, with possible exit from the program. The intention is to protect students' broader achievement. In-principle support is forming.", "said": "I think this strikes the right balance. It's clear, it's fair, it applies to everyone equally, and it sends the message that the specialist program isn't an excuse to neglect other subjects. I'd be happy to endorse it.", "task": "Test the policy against a plausible difficult case, such as a student with a documented illness, before it is endorsed.", "seconds": 55, "models": [{"text": "I support the intent. Can I walk it through one case? Say a Year 8 student has six weeks off in Term 2 with a documented illness, and comes back with two D grades because of missed assessments, while still achieving an A in Music. Under the draft, she goes on review. Is that what we want? If not, we may need a clause about documented absence.", "why": "It offers a specific, plausible case, lets the council see the consequence, and suggests the drafting fix."}, {"text": "One test, if I may. If this had been in place last year, how many students would have gone on review, and would we have wanted to review all of them? If the business manager or the deputy could check the numbers, we would know whether the threshold is in the right place.", "why": "It uses the backward test with real data, which grounds the policy without any hypothetical at all."}], "checks": ["I gave one scenario, not several.", "The case was plausible, not extreme.", "I asked the council what the policy would do.", "I suggested a fix rather than opposing the policy."]}, {"id": "stress-testing-with-a-scenario-s2", "room": "festival association board", "setup": "The festival association board is considering a new eligibility rule: every ensemble must consist of at least eighty per cent students enrolled at the entering school. The aim is to stop schools bringing in outside players to strengthen their bands. Several board members have experienced the problem and the rule is popular. It would take effect for next year's festival.", "said": "This is overdue. We've all seen bands turn up with three ring-ins from the local university, and it's unfair on schools that play by the rules. Eighty per cent is generous. I'd like to move that we adopt it.", "task": "Test the rule against a legitimate arrangement it might accidentally exclude, such as a combined small-school ensemble.", "seconds": 45, "models": [{"text": "I support the principle. Let me give it the hardest likely case. Two small regional schools combine to make one viable big band, because neither has enough players alone. Each contributes about half the band. Under this rule, neither school can enter. Is that the outcome we intend? If not, a clause recognising registered combined ensembles would solve it.", "why": "It identifies a real, sympathetic case the rule would catch, and offers a precise drafting solution."}, {"text": "One case before we vote. A student leaves the school in Term 2 but has rehearsed with the band all year, and the festival is in August. Does she count towards the eighty per cent? I suspect the answer should be yes, but the draft does not say.", "why": "It tests a boundary of the rule's definition and shows that the wording needs one more line."}], "checks": ["I supported the principle first.", "The scenario was a legitimate arrangement, not an abuse.", "I proposed a fix to the wording.", "I kept to one case."]}, {"id": "stress-testing-with-a-scenario-s3", "room": "SCSA assessment committee", "setup": "An assessment committee is considering allowing ATAR practical examination candidates to submit a video recording in place of a live examination, to reduce travel and improve access. The proposal includes broad guidelines on recording conditions. Members are broadly in favour, especially those representing regional schools. The recommendation will go forward if the committee endorses it today.", "said": "This is a clear gain for access and I think the guidelines are adequate. Candidates record in a supervised session, the school verifies it, and the examiners mark the recording. It is simpler for everyone.", "task": "Test the proposal against a case that exposes an equity or integrity problem the guidelines have not addressed.", "seconds": 60, "models": [{"text": "I can see the access benefit. Can we test it against one case? One candidate records in a single supervised take in a school hall. Another records in a studio, with a teacher present, and selects the best of fifteen takes. Both are verified by their schools. Would the guidelines treat those as equivalent? If not, we may need to specify the number of takes and the conditions.", "why": "It pairs two plausible cases to show the gap in the guidelines, and the solution follows naturally."}, {"text": "Suppose the recording fails, or the file is corrupted, after the supervised session. Under the guidelines, does the candidate re-record, sit a live examination, or receive a mark based on the school assessment? We should decide that now rather than in November, when a candidate and their family are waiting for an answer.", "why": "It tests the failure case, which is likely and which nobody had considered, and gives the reason for deciding now."}], "checks": ["I acknowledged the access benefit.", "The scenario was plausible.", "I asked what the guidelines would do.", "I indicated where the guidelines needed strengthening."]}, {"id": "stress-testing-with-a-scenario-s4", "room": "university academic board", "setup": "The academic board is considering a new quality rule: any unit with a failure rate above twenty-five per cent will automatically trigger a formal review. The rule is designed to identify units where teaching or assessment design is failing students. It applies to all faculties, including the conservatorium. The board is close to endorsing it.", "said": "It's a simple, transparent trigger. It doesn't presume fault; it just ensures that we look. Twenty-five per cent is a reasonable line, and it gives us a consistent approach across the institution.", "task": "Test the rule against a small-enrolment unit to show how the threshold behaves.", "seconds": 40, "models": [{"text": "Can I try it on a small unit? Take an advanced composition seminar with eight students. Two fail, perhaps for reasons unrelated to the teaching. That is exactly twenty-five per cent, and a third failure would trigger a formal review. Does the rule need a minimum enrolment, or a requirement that the rate be sustained over two offerings?", "why": "It shows how a sensible threshold misbehaves at small numbers, and suggests two possible fixes."}, {"text": "Imagine the rule applied to last year's results. Do we know how many units would have triggered review, and how many of those were under fifteen students? If the small units dominate, the rule will spend its effort in the wrong place.", "why": "It applies the backward test, asking for data that would tell the board whether the threshold targets what it intends."}], "checks": ["I tested the rule rather than opposing it.", "The case was specific and realistic.", "I proposed a fix.", "I spoke for under a minute."]}, {"id": "stress-testing-with-a-scenario-s5", "room": "arts funding panel", "setup": "The panel is reviewing new guidelines for a multi-year organisational funding round. One proposed condition is that at least half of each funded organisation's activity must take place in regional Western Australia. The policy intent is to address the concentration of funded activity in Perth. Panel members broadly support it.", "said": "Regional access has been a priority for years and nothing has changed. A clear percentage is the only way to make organisations take it seriously. I'd keep it simple: fifty per cent, measured by activity.", "task": "Test what 'activity' means against a case where an organisation reaches regional audiences differently.", "seconds": 50, "models": [{"text": "I support the intent. Can we test it on one case? A chamber ensemble streams its full season live to regional schools and runs workshops online, reaching more regional students than it could by touring. Is that regional activity under the guideline? If yes, we should say so. If no, we may be rewarding travel rather than reach.", "why": "It forces the panel to define the key term by putting a plausible case to it, and states both consequences."}, {"text": "Suppose an organisation tours regionally twice a year but every concert is in a regional city of more than thirty thousand people. Is that the regional access we are trying to fund? If not, we may need to distinguish regional centres from remote communities.", "why": "It tests a boundary within the definition of 'regional' and shows the guideline needs one more distinction."}], "checks": ["I supported the policy intent.", "My scenario tested a term in the guideline.", "I stated what follows from each answer.", "I chose a likely case, not an extreme one."]}, {"id": "stress-testing-with-a-scenario-s6", "room": "department meeting", "setup": "You are chairing a music department meeting about the instrumental tuition timetable. A rotating schedule is proposed so that no student misses the same academic class more than twice a term for lessons. Instrumental teachers favour it; classroom teachers have long asked for it. The deputy principal is attending.", "said": "This is the fairest system we've looked at. It spreads the disruption evenly, classroom teachers can plan around it, and parents will understand it. I think we can adopt it from Term 1.", "task": "Test the schedule against a case where two needs collide, such as a Year 12 student with an assessment in a lesson slot.", "seconds": 50, "models": [{"text": "I think it is the right model. Let me test it against one collision. A Year 12 student's tuition rotates into a period where she has an ATAR chemistry test. Does the lesson move, or does she miss it? If the lesson moves, who arranges it, and does that count as one of her two? Settling that now will save the instrumental staff a great deal of negotiation in Term 3.", "why": "It finds the conflict case the system has not addressed and frames the solution as a benefit to the staff who favour it."}, {"text": "Can we run it backwards against last year? If the instrumental staff take one week of last year's timetable and apply the rotation, we would see quickly where it breaks, particularly for Year 12. I would rather discover that in a spreadsheet than in March.", "why": "It proposes a practical test using real data, which tests the scheme without arguing about it."}], "checks": ["I supported the model before testing it.", "My scenario involved a genuine collision of rules.", "I asked who would decide.", "I proposed a practical way to test it."]}]}, {"id": "exposing-a-conflation-or-false-choice", "name": "Conflations and false choices", "family": "Testing", "what": "It separates two ideas the room is treating as one, or opens a further option in a choice presented as binary, so the decision can be made on its actual terms.", "when": "When a debate has hardened into two camps and each seems right about something different, or when one word is carrying two meanings and the disagreement depends on it.", "teach": "Much committee deadlock is not disagreement but equivocation. 'Standards' means rigour to one speaker and standardisation to another. 'Access' means entry to one and affordability to another. 'Quality' means outcome to one and reputation to another. Or the deadlock comes from an artificial binary: keep it or cut it, when the real options include keeping it in a different form, deferring, piloting or sharing it. Separating the terms often dissolves half the disagreement and reveals the real one, which is usually smaller and easier to decide.\n\nThe move lands strongly with highly educated audiences because conceptual clarity is their trade. Done well, it reads as intelligence rather than opposition, and it gives both camps something: each turns out to be right about one of the separated things. That is why it is one of the most effective ways for a member to change a room's direction without appearing to take a side.\n\nExecution has three parts. Name the two meanings or the two questions, anchoring each in a concrete example rather than a label. Give each camp its term fairly, so neither feels caricatured. Then ask which one the decision actually turns on, or propose the specific further option. With a false choice, do not merely announce that there is a middle way; specify it, or ask what prevents it. 'What would stop us doing both, in sequence?' is a better contribution than 'surely there is a compromise'.\n\nTiming is after both positions have been clearly stated and the room has felt the deadlock. Too early, and the separation seems pedantic, because nobody yet feels the confusion it resolves. The contribution should take under a minute.\n\nThe voice is clarifying and even-handed. Look at each camp in turn as you name its term. A slight warmth helps; this is the move that lets people climb down without losing face, and the tone should make that easy. Use 'we': we seem to be using this word in two ways. Never 'you'.\n\nIt goes wrong in four ways. Pedantry: separating terms the decision does not depend on, which wastes the room's patience. The further option that is merely compromise without principle, splitting the difference so that nobody gets what they need. Implying that people have been confused, which insults them; the conflation should be presented as natural and shared. And the false false choice: sometimes the binary is real, a budget either is or is not approved, and insisting otherwise looks evasive.", "phrasings": ["I wonder whether we are using 'standards' in two senses.", "I think both sides are right, about different things.", "Is the choice really between cutting it and keeping it as it is? There seems to be a third shape it could take.", "We seem to be treating access and affordability as the same question. They have different remedies.", "Could we separate whether we should do this from whether we can afford it this year?", "Two questions are travelling together here. Could we take them one at a time?", "What would stop us doing both, in sequence?", "If we split the motion, I suspect we could agree on the first half today."], "avoid": ["You are conflating two completely different things. : it accuses, and 'you' places a person in the dock instead of the idea.", "There is always a middle ground. : a platitude that offers no specific option and suggests the speaker has not thought one through.", "It depends what you mean by 'quality', does it not? : a seminar-room reflex that gestures at the distinction without stating it.", "Well, technically, semantically speaking... : a pedantic register that signals point-scoring rather than clarification."], "scenarios": [{"id": "exposing-a-conflation-or-false-choice-s1", "room": "SCSA syllabus committee", "setup": "The committee is reviewing the General Music course. One group argues the course has become too demanding for the students who take it; another argues that lowering demands would erode its standing. The debate has been framed as rigour against accessibility and has gone round several times. The Authority officer wants a recommendation today.", "said": "I'm not prepared to dilute the course. If we make it accessible by making it easier, we confirm every prejudice about General courses being second-class. Rigour is the whole point. Students rise to what we expect of them.", "task": "Separate the rigour of the content from the difficulty of the assessment format, so the committee can agree on both.", "seconds": 60, "models": [{"text": "I wonder whether we are using 'rigour' in two senses. One is the depth of the musical thinking we expect, which I think everyone here wants to keep. The other is the format of assessment: extended written analysis under timed conditions, which is where many General students actually struggle. We could keep the musical demand and change how students show it, through annotated recordings or practical demonstrations. Would that satisfy both concerns?", "why": "It separates the two meanings concretely, gives each side what it needs, and proposes a specific option rather than a compromise."}, {"text": "I think both sides are right, about different things. Those defending rigour are talking about what students understand. Those raising accessibility are talking about the barrier of written expression. Could we take those as separate questions: first, what musical understanding the course requires; then, which assessment forms best reveal it?", "why": "It reframes the deadlock as two sequential questions, giving the committee a process it can follow today."}], "checks": ["I named both meanings concretely.", "I used 'we', not 'you'.", "Each side could recognise its own concern.", "I proposed a specific option or a sequence."]}, {"id": "exposing-a-conflation-or-false-choice-s2", "room": "school council", "setup": "The school council is discussing priorities for the school's business plan. A parent member argues that the proposed refurbishment of the music performance space should be deferred in favour of additional wellbeing staffing. Staff members disagree. The discussion has become a contest between the arts and student wellbeing.", "said": "I'm sorry, but I can't support a performance space when we have students waiting weeks to see a psychologist. It's a question of priorities. Facilities can wait; children's mental health can't.", "task": "Show that the choice depends on two different funding sources, without diminishing the wellbeing concern.", "seconds": 55, "models": [{"text": "The wellbeing concern is serious, and I would support pressing for more staffing. Can I check whether these are actually competing? The refurbishment is drawing on capital funding, which as I understand it cannot be spent on salaries. If that is right, deferring the refurbishment would not fund a single extra hour of psychology. Could the business manager confirm? If so, we can pursue both.", "why": "It affirms the concern, identifies the factual basis of the false choice, and asks for confirmation rather than asserting it."}, {"text": "I think two questions are travelling together. One is whether the school should put more into wellbeing, and I suspect we all agree it should. The other is whether this particular refurbishment should go ahead. If the funding sources are separate, could we take those as two items, and make a clear recommendation on wellbeing staffing today?", "why": "It separates the issues and gives the parent member an immediate outcome on the matter they care about most."}], "checks": ["I took the wellbeing concern seriously.", "I identified the factual basis for separating the issues.", "I asked for the fact to be confirmed.", "I avoided setting music against wellbeing."]}, {"id": "exposing-a-conflation-or-false-choice-s3", "room": "festival association board", "setup": "The festival association board is debating the future of its competitive format. One faction wants to keep placings and awards; another wants a purely educational, non-competitive festival. Sponsors, tradition and educational philosophy are all in play. The board needs a direction before planning begins.", "said": "We have to decide what this festival is for. Either it's a competition, with all the drive and excellence that brings, or it's an educational event, where every band gets support. I don't think we can be both, and I think the fudge is what's been hurting us.", "task": "Separate adjudication from ranking, so the board can see that feedback and placings are distinct choices.", "seconds": 55, "models": [{"text": "I agree we should be clear about what the festival is for. I wonder whether we are bundling two things: adjudication, meaning expert feedback on every band, and ranking, meaning placings. Most teachers value the first enormously. The disagreement is really about the second. We could keep rigorous adjudication for everyone and offer placings only in sections where schools opt in. That is not a fudge; it is two distinct choices.", "why": "It accepts the speaker's call for clarity, separates the two elements precisely, and proposes a principled structure."}, {"text": "Is the choice really between competition and education? Many of the most educational festivals I know use adjudication against a published standard, gold, silver, bronze, rather than ranking bands against each other. That keeps the drive for excellence without anyone coming last. Could we look at that model?", "why": "It names a specific alternative that preserves what each faction values, and asks the board to consider it."}], "checks": ["I agreed with the call for clarity.", "I separated adjudication from ranking.", "I offered a principled alternative, not a compromise.", "I made it easy for both factions to agree."]}, {"id": "exposing-a-conflation-or-false-choice-s4", "room": "faculty curriculum committee", "setup": "A university faculty curriculum committee is revising its jazz studies major. A senior academic argues the program should be anchored in the historical canon; a younger lecturer argues it should focus on contemporary practice. The debate has become generational and slightly heated. You are a practitioner member.", "said": "If we abandon the canon, we produce graduates who can't swing, can't play a standard and don't know where the music came from. Contemporary practice is built on that foundation. You can't skip to the end.", "task": "Separate the canon as repertoire from the canon as a method of transmission, opening an option both sides could accept.", "seconds": 65, "models": [{"text": "I think 'the canon' is doing two jobs in this conversation. One is a body of repertoire: the standards, the recordings. The other is a way of learning: transcription, imitation, playing in the tradition until you have a voice. Contemporary players learn that way too. We could keep the method as non-negotiable and broaden the repertoire it is applied to. Would that address the concern about foundations?", "why": "It draws a distinction a practitioner would recognise, gives the senior academic his core concern, and lets the younger lecturer broaden the material."}, {"text": "Is it really a choice between the canon and contemporary practice? Every contemporary player I respect learned the language first and then left it deliberately. Could we sequence it: the tradition as the foundation in first and second year, contemporary practice as the focus of third year? That seems to me what the music itself did.", "why": "It turns the binary into a sequence, supported by a practitioner's observation, and offers a concrete structure."}], "checks": ["I identified two senses of 'canon'.", "Both colleagues could see their concern addressed.", "I offered a specific structure.", "I lowered the temperature of the exchange."]}, {"id": "exposing-a-conflation-or-false-choice-s5", "room": "principal's office", "setup": "The principal has asked for a conversation about the selective audition for the specialist music program. A board member has raised concerns that selective entry is elitist. The principal is weighing whether to recommend removing the audition. You run the audition process and believe it can be defended, but you also take the equity concern seriously.", "said": "I'm caught between two things. If we keep the audition, we're open to the charge that we're selecting children whose parents can afford private lessons. If we drop it, we probably lose what makes the program distinctive. I don't see a way through.", "task": "Separate selection for aptitude from selection for prior tuition, and show what that makes possible.", "seconds": 60, "models": [{"text": "I think there is a way through, because those are two different things. Selecting for prior tuition does favour families who can pay. Selecting for aptitude, such as pitch, rhythm and musical memory, does not, because those can be tested without an instrument. We could weight the audition more heavily towards aptitude, so that a child who has never had a lesson can still gain a place. That answers the equity concern and keeps the program selective.", "why": "It separates the two meanings of 'selection' and turns the principal's dilemma into a specific reform she could take to the board."}, {"text": "Can I suggest that the question is not whether we select, but what we select for? If the concern is that we reward prior tuition, we could check that directly: how many of our students last year had no private lessons before entry? If the number is reasonable, we have an answer for the board. If it is low, we know what to change.", "why": "It reframes the binary as a question about criteria and proposes a factual test the principal can use."}], "checks": ["I distinguished aptitude from prior tuition.", "I took the equity concern seriously.", "I offered a specific option or test.", "I did not become defensive about the program."]}, {"id": "exposing-a-conflation-or-false-choice-s6", "room": "arts funding panel", "setup": "The arts funding panel is assessing an application from a community choir that wants to commission and perform new Western Australian works. One panel member argues it belongs in the community category; another argues it meets the excellence criterion. The category determines which pool the application draws on. The panel is unable to agree.", "said": "This is lovely work, but it's community work. These are amateur singers. The excellence stream is for professional artists working at a professional standard. If we blur that line, every community group will apply for it.", "task": "Separate a professional standard of work from the professional status of the participants, and ask which the guideline requires.", "seconds": 50, "models": [{"text": "I think the guideline may be ambiguous here. It refers to a professional standard of work, which is about the outcome. The concern raised is about professional status, which is about who is performing. Those are not the same. The composers, the conductor and the commissioning are all professional. Could we ask the program officer which reading the guideline intends? That would settle it for this and future applications.", "why": "It separates two meanings in the guideline itself and asks for an authoritative reading, which resolves the deadlock beyond this case."}, {"text": "I wonder whether we are asking one question when there are two. Is the work excellent? And are the performers professional? If the guideline only requires the first, the application belongs in excellence. If it requires both, we should say so explicitly in the next round, because I suspect other panels have read it differently.", "why": "It names the two questions plainly and makes a constructive recommendation about the guideline for future rounds."}], "checks": ["I located the conflation in the guideline itself.", "I separated outcome from status.", "I asked for an authoritative reading.", "I did not imply the colleague had misread the guideline."]}]}, {"id": "building-on-a-colleague", "name": "Building on a colleague", "family": "Building", "what": "It credits a colleague's point exactly enough that they recognise it as theirs, then carries it one step further than they took it, so the idea gains weight and you gain an ally.", "when": "When someone has said something right but unfinished, and the room is about to move past it; or when your own point will travel further attached to a respected colleague's than standing alone.", "teach": "The move has two halves, and most people only perform the first. The credit must be precise: not 'as Margaret said', but the specific claim, in a phrase close to her own words, so that she nods and the room hears the idea a second time. Precision is what separates a genuine build from a social courtesy. Clever people notice when they have been paraphrased into something softer, and they notice equally when someone has actually listened. The extension is the second half, and it must be a real step: a consequence she did not draw, a case it explains, a mechanism it implies, or a decision it makes possible. If you only restate, you have spent the room's time agreeing.\n\nWith highly educated audiences this works because it honours the norms of scholarship. Attribution is the currency of the academy; a contribution that cites before it extends reads as rigorous rather than competitive. It also changes the arithmetic of the meeting. A point made by one person is an opinion; a point made by one and developed by another is a position, and the chair starts to treat it as such.\n\nSenior practitioners keep the credit to one sentence and spend the rest on the extension. They name the colleague once, look at her briefly as they do it, then turn to the chair or the room for the new step, which signals that the idea now belongs to the meeting. The verb matters: 'Margaret's point about moderation load implies...' is stronger than 'I agree with Margaret, and...'. Aim for thirty to sixty seconds. The voice should drop slightly and slow on the extension, because that is the part you want minuted.\n\nIt goes wrong in three ways. First, the credit becomes a hijack: you name her and then make an unrelated point of your own, and she feels used. Second, the extension overreaches, taking her somewhere she would not go, so she has to correct you publicly; if in doubt, offer the step as a question to her ('Would it follow that...?'). Third, inflation: 'Margaret's brilliant point' sounds like flattery and makes the room discount both of you. Plain, exact attribution is the compliment.", "phrasings": ["Professor Hale put her finger on something a moment ago: that the problem is not the rubric but who moderates it. If that's right, it changes where the money should go.", "I want to stay with Ian's point about the timing of the second sitting, because I think it has a consequence we haven't drawn yet.", "If I've understood Sarah correctly, the risk is less the cost than the precedent. Taking that one step further:", "Building on what Dr Ng said about load, there's a second implication for the smaller schools.", "David's distinction between access and attainment seems to me the most useful thing said this morning. What it lets us do is separate the two recommendations.", "Can I test whether Anna's argument extends to the regional cohort? Because if it does, it answers the question we parked earlier.", "That's exactly the right test, and I think it cuts further than Peter suggested.", "I'd like to put Helen's observation to work rather than just agree with it."], "avoid": ["As everyone has said, I totally agree with that : it credits no one in particular and adds nothing, so it reads as filler.", "To echo Margaret's absolutely brilliant point : the inflation sounds like flattery and makes the room discount the point and you.", "Margaret mentioned assessment, which reminds me of something quite different : it uses her as a doorway to a tangent, and she will feel hijacked.", "What Margaret is really trying to say is : it implies she said it badly and invites her to correct you in public."], "scenarios": [{"id": "building-on-a-colleague-s1", "room": "SCSA syllabus committee", "setup": "The committee is reviewing the draft Music ATAR course, specifically the weighting of the aural and analysis examination against the practical. A university musicologist has raised a point about construct validity that the chair acknowledged and then moved past. You think her point is the key to the weighting question and do not want it lost.", "said": "\"I'd only observe that the aural paper as currently designed tests recognition of conventions far more than it tests listening. Those are related, but they aren't the same construct, and the weighting assumes they are. Anyway, I'll leave that with the committee.\"", "task": "Pick up her construct point precisely and show what it implies for the weighting decision now on the table.", "seconds": 50, "models": [{"text": "Before we move on, I'd like to stay with Dr Albrecht's distinction between recognising conventions and actually listening, because I think it settles the weighting question. If the paper mostly tests recognition, then raising its weight rewards students who have memorised style markers, which is not what the rationale says the course values. The consequence would be to fix the paper's design first and set the weighting after, rather than the other way round.", "why": "It restates her distinction in her terms, then draws a procedural consequence she did not draw, which converts a passing remark into a sequencing decision."}, {"text": "Chair, may I take Dr Albrecht's point one step further? She's said the paper conflates two constructs. In my experience marking it, the practical has the mirror problem: it claims to assess interpretation, but students lose most marks on technical accuracy. So both components are drifting from what they claim to measure. I'd suggest we ask the writers to state the construct for each component before we argue about the split between them.", "why": "It extends her point by symmetry into the component she did not address, bringing his practitioner knowledge in under her academic authority."}], "checks": ["Did I restate her claim in words close enough to her own that she would recognise it?", "Is my extension a genuine consequence, not a restatement?", "Did I keep the credit to one sentence?", "Did I turn to the chair, not to her, for the new step?"]}, {"id": "building-on-a-colleague-s2", "room": "festival association board", "setup": "The association board is discussing whether to move the schools' jazz festival from its traditional venue to a larger one. The secretary, a retired accountant who rarely speaks on artistic matters, has made a remark about volunteer numbers. The president is keen on the bigger venue. You think the secretary's point is the real constraint.", "said": "\"I'm not across the artistic side, so take this for what it's worth. But on the volunteer roster, we have barely managed the current venue the last two years. A second stage would need perhaps another fifteen people we don't have.\"", "task": "Give the secretary's point the weight it deserves and extend it into a condition the board can actually use.", "seconds": 45, "models": [{"text": "I don't think Robyn should discount that at all; I think it's the decisive point. If we need fifteen more volunteers to run a second stage, the bigger venue isn't an artistic decision, it's a workforce one. So perhaps the motion should be conditional: we move only if we can name those fifteen people by the end of Term 1, and we ask each member school to commit two.", "why": "It refuses her self-deprecation on her behalf, reframes the decision through her point, and turns it into a conditional motion with a deadline."}, {"text": "Robyn's figure deserves a second look, because it cuts deeper than the roster. Fifteen more volunteers is also fifteen more Working with Children checks, more inductions, more people to supervise on the day. That's a governance load on this board, not just a staffing gap. I'd like us to cost that properly before we vote on the venue.", "why": "It extends the point into a consequence (compliance and supervision) she did not raise but that follows directly from it."}], "checks": ["Did I treat her contribution as serious rather than kind?", "Did I use her figure, not a vaguer version of it?", "Did I offer the board something it can decide?", "Did I avoid making it a contest with the president?"]}, {"id": "building-on-a-colleague-s3", "room": "university academic board", "setup": "You sit on a university academic committee as an external member from the schools sector. The committee is debating whether to keep an audition requirement for entry to the Bachelor of Music. A deputy dean has argued that the audition is a barrier for students from schools without specialist programs. A colleague from education has made a brief point that the deputy dean has not picked up.", "said": "\"The audition doesn't so much measure talent as measure access to tuition. Students who've had eight years of private lessons look talented at eighteen. That's not a moral point, it's a measurement point.\"", "task": "Credit the measurement framing precisely and extend it into a design option rather than a yes or no.", "seconds": 60, "models": [{"text": "Professor Okafor's phrase, that it's a measurement point rather than a moral one, is what moves this forward. If the audition measures accumulated tuition, then the question isn't whether to keep it but what else it should measure. From the schools side, rate of learning is visible in a workshop format: give an applicant a short unfamiliar passage, teach it for ten minutes, see what they do with it. That measures potential rather than history.", "why": "It quotes her framing and converts it into a concrete alternative design that only a practitioner would know is workable."}, {"text": "I'd like to follow Professor Okafor's logic through, because it has an awkward implication for us. If the audition rewards access to tuition, then removing it doesn't remove the advantage, it just moves it to first-year attrition, where students without that history fall behind. So the honest choice is between selecting on history at entry or supporting the gap after it. Either is defensible, but we should choose knowingly.", "why": "It extends her argument to a consequence that complicates the deputy dean's position, keeping the credit with her while sharpening the real decision."}], "checks": ["Did I quote or closely echo her phrasing?", "Did my extension go somewhere she would accept?", "Did I offer an option or a sharper choice, not just agreement?", "Was it under a minute?"]}, {"id": "building-on-a-colleague-s4", "room": "school council", "setup": "You chair the school council. A parent representative with a background in public health has made a point about student wellbeing during the camp program. A staff member has responded defensively. As chair you want to give the parent's point its due without taking sides against the staff member.", "said": "\"I'm not criticising the camp. My concern is narrower: we evaluate it on whether the activities ran, not on whether the students who were most anxious beforehand came back better or worse. We don't know, because we don't ask.\"", "task": "As chair, credit the precise concern and extend it into something the council can request without implying blame.", "seconds": 45, "models": [{"text": "Thank you, Fiona. I think the distinction you're drawing is between evaluating delivery and evaluating effect, and that's a fair one for a council to raise. It also gives us a way forward that doesn't question anyone's work: we could ask the school to add a short pre and post check for the next camp and report back. Mr Davies, would that be feasible?", "why": "It names her distinction cleanly, separates it from criticism, and hands the staff member a role in the solution."}, {"text": "Fiona's point is about what we measure, not how well the camp was run, and I think Mr Davies and Fiona actually agree on the second. If I take Fiona's point one step further, it applies to most of our programs: we report activity, not effect. Perhaps the council could ask for that across the board next year, so camp isn't singled out.", "why": "It generalises her point to the whole program, which protects the staff member and raises the council's oversight to a policy level."}], "checks": ["Did I state her concern without amplifying it into criticism?", "Did I keep the chair's neutrality?", "Is the extension something the council has authority to ask for?", "Did I bring the staff member back in?"]}, {"id": "building-on-a-colleague-s5", "room": "arts funding panel", "setup": "You are on a state arts funding panel assessing applications for youth music programs. Another assessor, a composer, has made a sharp observation about one application's budget. The panel convenor is inclined to rank it highly on artistic merit. You think the composer has spotted something important but stated it too briefly to register.", "said": "\"The artistic case is strong, I don't dispute that. But look at the budget: almost nothing goes to the participating musicians. The artists are paid, the venue is paid, the young people are the product.\"", "task": "Make the composer's point land with the convenor by giving it a precise extension tied to the assessment criteria.", "seconds": 50, "models": [{"text": "I think Tom's last line, that the young people are the product, is worth the panel pausing on. Our third criterion is benefit to participants, and on that criterion the budget is the evidence. It shows no tuition, no travel support, no recording for the students to keep. So I'd separate the scores: high on artistic merit, as Tom says, but materially lower on participant benefit. That's where the ranking should move.", "why": "It anchors his remark to the panel's own criteria and translates it into a specific scoring adjustment."}, {"text": "Can I extend Tom's point with a question to the applicant rather than a lower score? If the budget really sends almost nothing to the participants, the program could be strong and still exploit them. We could fund conditionally, asking them to show how ten per cent of the grant reaches the young musicians directly. That keeps the artistic strength Tom acknowledged and fixes what he identified.", "why": "It credits both halves of his comment and extends it into a funding condition, which gives the convenor a way to agree without reversing."}], "checks": ["Did I keep his sharpest phrase?", "Did I tie the extension to the panel's criteria or powers?", "Did I propose a specific consequence for the ranking or the grant?", "Did I avoid turning it into a disagreement with the convenor?"]}, {"id": "building-on-a-colleague-s6", "room": "department meeting", "setup": "You run the music department meeting. A younger instrumental teacher has, somewhat tentatively, suggested that sectional rehearsals be scheduled by repertoire need rather than by fixed weekly slot. Two senior staff have reacted coolly. You think the idea is right and want it to survive.", "said": "\"It's probably not practical, but I just wonder if we're holding sectionals because it's Thursday, not because the brass actually need them that week. Some weeks they're fine and the woodwinds are drowning.\"", "task": "Give her idea authorship and standing, and extend it into something trialable that addresses the senior staff's likely objection.", "seconds": 40, "models": [{"text": "I don't think it's impractical, Emma, I think it's the right question: scheduling by need rather than by habit. What would make it workable is a simple rule. Each conductor flags the sections they need by Monday, and Thursday's rooms are allocated from that. Let's trial it for the five weeks before the concert and see whether the rehearsals are better.", "why": "It restates her principle in a crisp phrase, removes her hedge, and adds the mechanism that answers the practicality concern."}, {"text": "Emma's put her finger on something: the slot is fixed but the need moves. I'd add that it moves predictably, because we know the concert program weeks in advance. So we could plan sectionals from the repertoire backwards at the start of term, rather than week by week. Would that address your concern, Greg, about tutors needing notice?", "why": "It extends her insight with a planning mechanism that also meets the senior teacher's objection directly, inviting him into it."}], "checks": ["Did I remove her hedge without patronising her?", "Did I keep her as the author of the idea?", "Does the extension answer the objection likely in the room?", "Is there a concrete next step?"]}]}, {"id": "the-synthesis", "name": "The synthesis", "family": "Building", "what": "It gathers the threads of a sprawling discussion into a single position that most people can recognise their own contribution in, and offers it to the room as something to adopt.", "when": "When the discussion has produced several good points that are being treated as competing, energy is dropping, and nobody has yet said what the room actually thinks.", "teach": "A synthesis is not a summary. A summary lists what was said; a synthesis shows how the points fit together and what follows from them. The test is whether it contains a claim that no single speaker made, but that every speaker can accept. That is why it carries authority: the person who synthesises is seen to have understood the whole discussion, and in committees that is very close to being seen as its leader, whatever the formal chair.\n\nThe mechanism works with clever audiences because they rarely disagree as much as their language suggests. Academics and senior officials argue in distinctions, and two people defending different distinctions are often defending compatible halves of one position. The synthesis names the underlying agreement and puts the real disagreement, if any remains, into a much smaller box. It also relieves the room, because intelligent people tire of circling and are grateful to whoever lets them land.\n\nSenior practitioners build it in three beats. First, a signpost that claims the floor for a different kind of contribution: 'May I try to pull this together?'. Second, the threads, named briefly with their owners, usually three, never more than four. Third, the position, stated as a single sentence that could be minuted, followed by an explicit offer: 'Is that where we are?'. The offer is essential, because it converts your synthesis into the room's. Keep it under seventy-five seconds; a long synthesis becomes another speech. Speak a little more slowly than in debate, sit back slightly rather than forward, and look around the table as you name each thread so that each owner sees themselves included.\n\nTiming is the craft. Too early and you flatten a discussion that still needs to happen; people will resent being summarised before they have spoken. Too late and the chair has already done it, or the room has fractured. The right moment is usually the second time a point is repeated: repetition means the room has run out of new material.\n\nIt fails in three ways. The false synthesis smuggles your own view in as the consensus, and sharp people notice at once. The lowest common denominator produces something everyone can accept because it commits nobody to anything. And the synthesis that leaves someone out, particularly a dissenter, which reads as an attempt to bury them. Name the remaining disagreement honestly; it makes the rest more credible.", "phrasings": ["May I try to pull this together, because I think we're closer than it sounds.", "I've heard three things this morning, and I don't think they're in tension.", "Let me see whether this captures where the room is, and people can tell me where I've got it wrong.", "It seems to me the disagreement has narrowed to one question, and everything else we broadly share.", "If I put Professor Hale's concern and Ian's together, what we have is not two positions but one condition.", "Could I offer a form of words that I think most of us could live with?", "Underneath the different vocabularies, I think we agree on the principle and differ only on the timing.", "So the position might be this: we support the change, on two conditions, and we review it in a year. Is that where we are?"], "avoid": ["So basically everyone agrees with me, then : it disguises your own view as the consensus and will be resisted.", "To summarise what everyone has said in order : that is minutes, not synthesis, and it adds no claim.", "I think we all just want what's best for the students : the lowest common denominator commits nobody and ends nothing.", "Leaving aside David's objection for a moment : burying the dissenter makes the whole synthesis suspect."], "scenarios": [{"id": "the-synthesis-s1", "room": "SCSA syllabus committee", "setup": "The committee has spent forty minutes on whether the revised Music General course should include a compulsory composition component. A university lecturer wants it compulsory; a regional principal worries about teacher capacity; an assessment officer is concerned about moderation; a teacher member wants flexibility. Positions are being restated. The chair looks at the clock.", "said": "\"I keep coming back to the same place. If composition isn't compulsory, schools without specialist staff will simply never teach it, and those students will be the ones who most need it. I don't see how optional serves equity.\"", "task": "Draw the four threads into a single position the committee could minute.", "seconds": 70, "models": [{"text": "May I try to pull this together? I think we agree on more than it sounds. Dr Reid says composition matters for equity; Mr Hart says teachers outside the metro area can't yet deliver it; Ms Lim says it must be moderated reliably; Kate wants room for local context. Those fit together as a sequence: make it compulsory, but from a later start date, with a supported task bank that solves both capacity and moderation. Is that where we are?", "why": "It names each owner, shows the points are compatible rather than competing, and offers a single minutable position with an explicit check."}, {"text": "I think the disagreement has narrowed to one question: whether compulsion comes before support or after it. Nobody has argued composition is unimportant, and nobody has argued teachers are ready now. So perhaps the recommendation is that composition be compulsory from 2029, conditional on professional learning and an exemplar bank in place by 2028. If the conditions aren't met, it comes back to us.", "why": "It reduces a four-way debate to a single question of sequence and builds the safeguard in, which addresses the sceptics without diluting the principle."}], "checks": ["Did my synthesis contain a claim no single speaker made?", "Did I name no more than four threads?", "Did I end with an explicit offer to the room?", "Did I avoid smuggling my own preference in as the consensus?"]}, {"id": "the-synthesis-s2", "room": "festival association board", "setup": "The board has been discussing complaints after this year's festival: adjudication felt inconsistent, the schedule overran, and a smaller school felt sidelined in the program. Different members have taken each complaint in turn and the discussion has become a list of grievances. As treasurer you can see a pattern.", "said": "\"And the overrun isn't only a scheduling problem. When we ran forty minutes late, the adjudicators had less time per band, and the last few schools got rushed comments. I'd be upset too.\"", "task": "Show the board that the three complaints share one cause and propose a position on it.", "seconds": 55, "models": [{"text": "I think Jo has just connected the three complaints. The overrun squeezed the adjudicators, which made their comments uneven, and the schools that suffered were the ones scheduled last, which this year happened to be the smaller ones. So we don't have three problems, we have one: a schedule with no buffer. If we build in fifteen minutes every two hours, I think most of the rest takes care of itself. Can we agree that as the first fix?", "why": "It credits the member who saw the link, turns a list into a causal chain, and offers one action the board can agree before anything else."}, {"text": "Can I suggest a way of seeing all of this? Every complaint we've heard is about fairness of experience between schools. Inconsistent comments, uneven time, a small school feeling marginal. So perhaps the principle for next year is that every band gets the same conditions, and we test the schedule, the adjudication brief and the program order against that one principle before we publish.", "why": "It synthesises at the level of principle rather than cause, which gives the board a test it can apply to future decisions too."}], "checks": ["Did I turn a list of complaints into one claim?", "Did I credit whoever started the connection?", "Is the proposed position specific enough to act on?", "Did I avoid defending last year's decisions?"]}, {"id": "the-synthesis-s3", "room": "principal's office", "setup": "You are in a meeting with the principal, the associate principal and the business manager about the music program budget for next year. The business manager has argued for cutting instrumental tuition hours; the associate principal has talked about timetabling pressure; the principal has spoken about the program's reputation. Each has a point and the meeting is drifting.", "said": "\"I'm not against music, Dijon, I want to be clear about that. But I've got a deficit to close, and tuition is the biggest variable line in the faculty. If not there, where?\"", "task": "Bring the three perspectives together into a proposition the principal can decide on, without merely defending your budget.", "seconds": 65, "models": [{"text": "Can I try to put the three concerns together? Mark needs a saving that's real; Steve needs fewer timetable clashes; Lesley needs the program's reputation intact. I think one change serves all three: consolidate tuition into larger group lessons in Years 7 and 8, where the pedagogy actually supports it, and protect individual lessons from Year 9 up. That saves hours, reduces pull-outs, and protects the senior results that carry the reputation.", "why": "It names each person's real interest and shows a single proposal satisfies all three, which moves him from defender of a budget to author of the solution."}, {"text": "I hear three questions, and I think they have the same answer. Where can we save, what can the timetable bear, and what can't we lose? The answer to all three is the junior years. That's where tuition is least efficient, where pull-outs hurt most, and where reputation is least at stake. So I'd propose we find Mark's figure there, and hold the senior program steady. Is that a basis to work from?", "why": "It reframes the drifting discussion as three questions with one answer, then hands the decision back with an explicit check."}], "checks": ["Did I state each person's interest in terms they would accept?", "Did I offer a proposition, not a defence?", "Did I concede the need for a saving?", "Did I end by handing the decision back?"]}, {"id": "the-synthesis-s4", "room": "university academic board", "setup": "A university curriculum committee is debating whether a new interdisciplinary unit on music and technology should sit in the music school or the engineering faculty. Academics from both sides have made territorial but intelligent arguments. A student representative has asked who students will actually go to for help.", "said": "\"With respect, it's not a question of ownership. The pedagogy of the unit is critical listening; that's our discipline. The tools may be engineering tools, but a microscope doesn't make biology a branch of optics.\"", "task": "Synthesise the two disciplinary claims and the student's question into a governance position.", "seconds": 70, "models": [{"text": "If I may try to draw this together: Professor Marsh is right that the pedagogy is critical listening, and Dr Patel is right that students will need the engineering infrastructure. The student representative's question tells us what matters to the people taking it: one place to go. So perhaps the unit is owned by music for curriculum and assessment, co-taught with engineering, with a single named coordinator. Ownership and resourcing don't have to sit in the same place.", "why": "It grants each side its strongest claim, uses the student's question as the tiebreaker, and separates ownership from resourcing."}, {"text": "I think we've been arguing about the wrong noun. Both faculties have made a case about who owns the unit; the student representative asked about who supports the student. If we organise around the second question, the answer is joint: academic ownership with music, as Professor Marsh says, technical support from engineering, and one coordinator for students. Could we minute that as the principle and leave the workload split to the deans?", "why": "It shifts the room's question rather than splitting the difference, and delegates the contested detail to the right level."}], "checks": ["Did I credit both disciplinary claims fairly?", "Did I use the student's question rather than ignore it?", "Is there a minutable principle?", "Did I keep the detail out of the room?"]}, {"id": "the-synthesis-s5", "room": "school council", "setup": "You chair the school council. The agenda item is the school's draft mobile phone policy. A parent member wants a total ban; a student representative argues phones are used for learning; a community member raises safety on the way home; a staff member worries about enforcement. Twenty minutes have passed and positions have hardened.", "said": "\"I just don't think a policy we can't enforce is worth having. If staff are spending half of every period confiscating phones, we've made things worse, not better.\"", "task": "As chair, synthesise the four positions into a draft position the council can endorse or amend.", "seconds": 60, "models": [{"text": "Let me see if I can capture where we are, and people can correct me. Everyone here wants phones out of lessons. Jess has a point that some learning uses them; Paul wants them available for the trip home; Mr Kaur needs something staff can enforce. So the shape might be: phones off and away during the school day, teacher-directed use as the only exception, and phones available at the gate. Is that something the council could endorse?", "why": "It opens with the shared ground, then fits each concern in as a feature of the policy, and ends with a clear question to the council."}, {"text": "I think the disagreement is smaller than it sounds. Nobody wants phones out in lessons and nobody wants students without them on the way home. The real question is enforcement, as Mr Kaur says. So perhaps the council's position is the principle, phones away during the school day, and we ask the school to bring back an enforcement model it is confident in. That respects the council's role and the school's.", "why": "It narrows the dispute to the one live issue and allocates it correctly between council and school, which is good governance as well as good synthesis."}], "checks": ["Did I start from what everyone shares?", "Did each member hear their concern reflected?", "Did I respect the line between council and school operations?", "Did I end with a decision the council can make?"]}, {"id": "the-synthesis-s6", "room": "conference panel", "setup": "You are on a panel at a state music education conference on the future of specialist programs. Two co-panellists, a university researcher and a department official, have disagreed about whether selective programs entrench advantage. The moderator turns to you with five minutes left. The audience is largely teachers.", "said": "\"I accept the research on selection effects. My point is that the alternative isn't a level playing field, it's private tuition for those who can afford it. Public specialist programs are the only route some of these students have.\"", "task": "Draw the two panellists' positions into a synthesis that gives the audience something to take away.", "seconds": 75, "models": [{"text": "I'd like to try to put Dr Moore and Ms Fischer together, because I don't think they're as far apart as the last ten minutes suggest. Dr Moore shows that selection by audition favours students with prior tuition. Ms Fischer argues that public programs are the counterweight to private tuition. Both are true, and together they tell us where the work is: not abolishing selection, but making the path to audition public. Primary school programs, feeder bands, free tuition before Year 7. That's the agenda I'd leave this room with.", "why": "It holds both claims as true, derives a third claim from them, and gives a teacher audience something actionable."}, {"text": "What I hear is agreement on the problem and disagreement only on the remedy. Both panellists accept that talent at twelve largely reflects opportunity before twelve. Dr Moore would change the selection; Ms Fischer would protect the programs. The synthesis, I'd suggest, is to select on potential rather than attainment, and to widen the pool well before anyone is selected. Both are things the people in this room can actually do on Monday.", "why": "It locates the precise point of difference, resolves it with a design principle, and finishes on the audience's agency."}], "checks": ["Did I treat both panellists' claims as true rather than splitting the difference?", "Did the synthesis produce a new claim?", "Did I land it for this audience?", "Did I stay inside the time?"]}]}, {"id": "the-amendment", "name": "The amendment", "family": "Building", "what": "It accepts the purpose of a proposal and changes one feature of it, so that the room keeps its momentum and the proposal is improved rather than defeated.", "when": "When a proposal is broadly right but has a flaw that will cause harm if adopted as it stands, and opposing it outright would either lose or cost more goodwill than the flaw is worth.", "teach": "The amendment is the most efficient form of disagreement available in a committee. Opposition asks the proposer to lose; amendment asks them to win slightly differently. People who argue for a living understand this immediately, and most will accept a well-made amendment because it lets them keep authorship of the idea. The result is that you often achieve more of what you want by amending than you would have by defeating the motion and starting again.\n\nThe mechanism has three parts. First, an explicit acceptance of purpose, stated in the proposer's terms, so that the room knows you are not an opponent in disguise: 'I support what this is trying to do.' Second, the specific change, as narrow as possible: one word, one condition, one date, one exception. The narrower the amendment, the harder it is to resist. Third, the reason, given as a benefit to the proposal itself rather than as a criticism of it: 'This makes it more likely to survive implementation' rather than 'This fixes the problem with it.' In formal meetings, Australian practice treats an amendment that simply negates the motion as out of order; the discipline is worth keeping even informally, because an amendment that guts the proposal will be recognised as opposition and resisted as such.\n\nSenior practitioners usually speak to the proposer before the meeting, if they can, and offer the change privately. A proposer who accepts it as a friendly amendment will often move it themselves, which is the best outcome. In the room, aim for forty to sixty seconds, and have the exact wording ready, ideally written, because chairs and secretaries will ask for it. Deliver the acceptance warmly and the change plainly; do not apologise for the change, and do not pause between purpose and change long enough for anyone to hear a 'but'. Use 'and' or 'so that' rather than 'but'.\n\nIt goes wrong in three ways. The wrecking amendment changes so much that the proposal no longer does what it was for; sharp people will name it, and you will lose credibility. The cascade happens when one amendment invites several others and the proposal is redesigned on the floor; if that begins, suggest it go back to its author with the points noted. And the vague amendment ('perhaps we could soften it a little') hands the chair nothing to put and is simply discarded. An amendment is only as strong as its wording.", "phrasings": ["I support the proposal, and I'd like to suggest one change that I think makes it more durable.", "Could I offer a friendly amendment? Replace 'all students' with 'all students in Years 7 to 10'.", "I agree with the direction entirely. My amendment is about the date, not the substance.", "Would the mover accept adding a review clause, so that the board revisits this after the first festival?", "I think this is right, and I'd like it to survive contact with the timetable. One condition would do that.", "Rather than oppose the motion, may I propose that it read 'up to' rather than 'not less than'?", "I'd move that we adopt the recommendation with one exception, which I'll put in writing for the secretary.", "If Professor Hale is willing, I'd add a single clause so that the smaller schools aren't caught by this unintentionally."], "avoid": ["I support this in principle, but : 'in principle, but' signals opposition and undoes the acceptance before the change is heard.", "Maybe we could just water it down a bit : vague, gives the chair nothing to put, and insults the proposal.", "I'd like to amend it so that it doesn't actually apply this year : a wrecking amendment, which will be recognised as opposition.", "With all due respect, the proposal is flawed, so I'd suggest the following changes : leads with criticism and invites the proposer to defend instead of accept."], "scenarios": [{"id": "the-amendment-s1", "room": "school council", "setup": "A parent member has moved that the council endorse a proposal that every Year 7 student take part in a compulsory term of instrumental music. You admire the intent, but the school has nowhere near the instruments or tutors to deliver it next year, and the specialist program would be stretched. The motion has general warmth around the table.", "said": "\"I'll keep this brief. Every child deserves the chance to learn an instrument, not just the ones who auditioned. I'm moving that council endorse a compulsory term of instrumental music for all Year 7s from next year.\"", "task": "Support the purpose and amend the motion so it can actually be delivered.", "seconds": 50, "models": [{"text": "I'd like to support this, and to help it happen. I'd ask whether the mover would accept one amendment: replacing 'from next year' with 'from 2028, subject to the school reporting to council on instrument and staffing requirements by Term 2'. That keeps the principle exactly as moved, and gives us a date we can honestly meet.", "why": "It accepts the principle in the mover's words and narrows the change to a date and a reporting condition, worded ready for the minutes."}, {"text": "I support the principle without reservation, and I'd like to propose a small amendment so that it lands well. Council endorses the aim of a term of instrumental music for every Year 7 student, and asks the school to bring back a staged plan, beginning with a pilot class next year. Would the mover accept that as friendly?", "why": "It reframes endorsement of an action as endorsement of an aim plus a staged plan, which respects the line between council and school operations."}], "checks": ["Did I accept the purpose in the mover's terms?", "Is my change narrow and worded?", "Did I avoid the word 'but'?", "Did I ask whether the mover would accept it?"]}, {"id": "the-amendment-s2", "room": "SCSA syllabus committee", "setup": "The committee is considering a recommendation from the writing group that all Music ATAR candidates submit a recorded performance rather than performing live for examiners. The rationale is equity for regional students and reduced logistics. You support the direction but believe recordings without controls will advantage students with access to studios and editing.", "said": "\"The writing group's view is that a recorded submission is fairer to regional candidates, reduces examiner travel, and reflects how musicians actually work now. We'd ask the committee to endorse the recommendation as drafted.\"", "task": "Accept the move to recorded submissions and amend it to close the production-advantage problem.", "seconds": 60, "models": [{"text": "I think the writing group is right, and the equity case for regional students is strong. I'd like to propose one condition so the recommendation achieves what it intends: that the recording be a single unedited take, made on school premises, with a teacher declaration. Otherwise we replace one advantage, proximity to Perth, with another, access to a studio. With that clause, I'd support it fully.", "why": "It endorses the rationale, then frames the amendment as protecting that rationale, and names the specific condition."}, {"text": "I'd support the recommendation with a single amendment: 'recorded submissions must be continuous and unedited, with audio and video from a fixed position'. It costs regional schools nothing extra, and it means the recording measures the performance rather than the production. The writing group's aim stays intact, and arguably becomes easier to defend.", "why": "It offers exact wording the secretary can minute, shows the amendment does not erode the equity rationale, and keeps authorship with the writing group."}], "checks": ["Did I endorse the equity rationale before changing anything?", "Is my amendment specific enough to draft?", "Did I frame it as serving the proposal's purpose?", "Did I keep the writing group as author?"]}, {"id": "the-amendment-s3", "room": "festival association board", "setup": "The president has proposed that the festival introduce a ranked awards ceremony with first, second and third places in each division, arguing it will raise standards and attract sponsors. Several directors like it. You believe outright ranking will discourage smaller and newer school bands from entering, but the sponsor argument is real.", "said": "\"I'll be honest, the sponsors have asked for it. They want winners they can put on a banner. And I think a bit of competition would lift the standard across the board. I'd like us to approve ranked placings from next year.\"", "task": "Keep the sponsor-friendly recognition and amend the format so it does not drive small schools away.", "seconds": 55, "models": [{"text": "I'm with you on recognition, and the sponsor point is one we have to take seriously. Could I suggest an amendment that gives sponsors their winners without losing the new bands? Rather than first, second, third, we award gold, silver and bronze standards against criteria, with one sponsor's award for the outstanding band in each division. Sponsors get a banner, and every band has something to aim for.", "why": "It accepts both of the president's aims, recognition and sponsorship, and changes only the mechanism to a criterion-referenced model."}, {"text": "I'd support ranked placings with one amendment: that they apply only to the open and advanced divisions, and the developing divisions stay non-competitive with adjudicator commendations. That gives the sponsors their winners where the standard is highest, and protects the bands we most need to keep entering. Could that be put as a friendly amendment?", "why": "It narrows the scope of the proposal rather than changing its nature, which is easier for the president to accept."}], "checks": ["Did I take the sponsor point seriously?", "Did I change the mechanism rather than reject the idea?", "Is the amendment clear enough to minute?", "Did I avoid lecturing about competition?"]}, {"id": "the-amendment-s4", "room": "faculty curriculum committee", "setup": "A university faculty committee is considering a proposal from the dean's office to reduce first-year contact hours in the Bachelor of Music by replacing some aural tutorials with online modules. You are an external member. You think online modules suit theory content but not the sung and dictated parts of aural training, which depend on live correction.", "said": "\"The proposal is cost neutral in year one and saves about fifteen per cent of tutorial load by year two. The modules are well designed and have strong completion data from other institutions. I'd suggest we can adopt it without further delay.\"", "task": "Accept the efficiency case and amend the proposal to protect live aural work.", "seconds": 60, "models": [{"text": "I'm persuaded on the theory content, and I don't doubt the modules. Could I propose that the change apply to the written and analytical components only, and that sight-singing and dictation remain in live tutorials? Those depend on someone hearing the student and correcting in the moment. That would still give the faculty most of the saving, perhaps ten of the fifteen per cent, while keeping the part that doesn't translate online.", "why": "It concedes the efficiency case, draws a principled line, and quantifies the trade so the dean's office can see the cost of accepting."}, {"text": "I'd support this with a review amendment: adopt the modules for one year, with a comparison of first-year aural results against the previous cohort, reported to this committee before the change is made permanent. If the results hold, we've confirmed the saving; if they don't, we've caught it in one cohort rather than four.", "why": "It does not contest the proposal's design at all, it adds an evidence condition, which is very hard for a data-led proposer to refuse."}], "checks": ["Did I accept the efficiency argument openly?", "Is the line I've drawn principled, not just preferential?", "Did I give the proposer something they can say yes to?", "Was the wording exact?"]}, {"id": "the-amendment-s5", "room": "department meeting", "setup": "In the Performing Arts learning area meeting, the head of learning area has proposed a single common assessment calendar across drama, dance and music, with all major assessments in the same two weeks of each term. You see the coordination benefit, but music's ensemble performances depend on concert dates that are set by external venues. The proposal has broad support, and you do not want music to look like the exception everyone has to work around.", "said": "\"I want us to stop treading on each other's toes. Students tell me they have three practical assessments in one week and a production on top. One calendar, two assessment windows a term, and everyone works to it.\"", "task": "Back the shared calendar and amend it to accommodate fixed external performance dates.", "seconds": 45, "models": [{"text": "I think one calendar is the right fix, and music will work to it. I'd propose one exception: assessments tied to an external performance date, a concert at a booked venue for instance, can sit outside the window, provided they're entered on the shared calendar at the start of the term. That keeps the coordination you want and means we're not assessing ensembles at an artificial date.", "why": "It commits music to the plan first, then asks for a defined, bounded exception with a transparency condition."}, {"text": "Can I support this and add a clause? Each learning area nominates its fixed external dates in Week 1, and the windows are set around them. That way the calendar is built once, with everything visible, and no one has to negotiate exceptions halfway through the term.", "why": "It amends the process rather than asking for an exception, which turns music's constraint into a design feature for everyone."}], "checks": ["Did I commit to the plan before amending it?", "Is my exception bounded and transparent?", "Did I avoid making music sound special?", "Is it short?"]}, {"id": "the-amendment-s6", "room": "arts funding panel", "setup": "On a state arts funding panel, the convenor proposes a new rule for the youth music round: no organisation may receive funding in two consecutive years. The aim is to spread funding to new applicants. You agree with the aim, but several of the strongest ongoing programs for disadvantaged young people would lose continuity.", "said": "\"We keep funding the same dozen organisations. They're good, but the round is meant to grow the sector, not sustain incumbents. A one-year stand-down gives new applicants a real chance.\"", "task": "Support the aim of spreading funding and amend the rule so it does not break ongoing programs that depend on continuity.", "seconds": 55, "models": [{"text": "I share the aim, and the pattern you describe is real. I'd propose an amendment: instead of a stand-down, reserve a fixed share of the round, say forty per cent, for first-time applicants. That guarantees new entrants their chance without forcing an established program for at-risk young people to stop for a year and lose its students.", "why": "It achieves the convenor's goal by a different mechanism and makes the cost of the original rule concrete."}, {"text": "I'd support the stand-down with one exception: programs that have been funded for three or more consecutive years may apply for multi-year funding through the separate stream, and are exempt from the stand-down if successful. That moves incumbents out of this round, which is what you want, without cutting them off.", "why": "It keeps the rule intact and redirects the strongest incumbents elsewhere, which serves the convenor's purpose more fully than opposing it would."}], "checks": ["Did I state agreement with the aim before the change?", "Does my amendment achieve the convenor's goal, not dodge it?", "Did I name the cost of the unamended rule specifically?", "Is the amendment precise enough to adopt?"]}]}, {"id": "principled-dissent", "name": "Principled dissent", "family": "Dissenting", "what": "It puts a clear disagreement on the record, on the substance and with evident respect, so that the room has to weigh it even if it decides the other way.", "when": "When a senior person or a majority is moving towards a decision you believe is wrong on the merits, and silence would be read as assent.", "teach": "Principled dissent is not the same as opposition. Its first purpose is not to win the vote but to make sure the decision is taken with the objection in view. That changes how it is delivered. You are not trying to defeat the senior person; you are giving the room the information it needs to decide well, and putting your own judgement on the record. Clever rooms respect this, often more than they respect agreement, because most of them have watched institutions make avoidable mistakes in silence.\n\nThe structure is simple and must be audible. State your position in the first sentence, without preamble: 'I disagree with the recommendation.' Then give the single strongest reason, not three. Then state what would change your mind, or what you would support instead. Finish by acknowledging the room's right to decide: 'If the committee goes the other way, I'd ask that my view be noted.' That last line matters. It shows you understand the difference between dissent and obstruction, and in formal settings it is the conventional way of recording a minority view.\n\nWith a senior person, the respect must be in the form, not in qualifiers. Use their title or name once, address the argument rather than the person, and avoid every softener that sounds like apology: 'I may be wrong', 'just', 'I wonder if'. Those make the dissent easy to dismiss and, paradoxically, sound less respectful, because they imply the senior person cannot take a direct disagreement. Senior practitioners speak more slowly and slightly more quietly when dissenting, keep their hands still, and hold eye contact with the person whose view they are opposing for the first sentence before turning to the chair. Forty to seventy seconds is ample. A long dissent reads as grievance.\n\nIt goes wrong in three ways. The first is the hedge: the disagreement is wrapped in so many concessions that nobody is sure it happened, and the minutes record consent. The second is the personal turn, where the dissent drifts from the proposal to the proposer's motives or competence; that converts a disagreement on substance into a conflict, and the room will side with the senior person. The third is the repeat: having been heard and outvoted, the dissenter raises it again under the next item. Dissent once, clearly, and then let the decision stand unless new facts emerge. That restraint is what earns the right to be listened to next time.", "phrasings": ["I disagree with the recommendation, and I'd like to say why briefly.", "Principal, I have a different view on this, and I think it should be in front of the meeting before we decide.", "I can't support this as it stands. My reason is a single one, and it's about the students in the smaller schools.", "I'm conscious I'm in the minority here, so I'll be brief, but I think we're about to make a mistake.", "My objection isn't to the aim, which I share. It's to the evidence we're relying on.", "I'd change my view if we had data on attrition. Without it, I think the risk is ours, not the applicants'.", "If the board decides otherwise, I'll accept that, and I'd ask that my dissent be minuted.", "With respect, Professor, I read the same report and came to the opposite conclusion."], "avoid": ["I might be totally wrong here, and I don't want to rock the boat, but : the hedge makes the dissent disappear and the minutes record consent.", "I think we all know why this is really being pushed : imputes motive and turns a disagreement on substance into a personal conflict.", "I just want to say again, as I said last time : repetition after being outvoted reads as obstruction and costs credibility.", "Frankly this is a terrible idea : contempt for the proposal makes the room defend it, and you lose the substance."], "scenarios": [{"id": "principled-dissent-s1", "room": "principal's office", "setup": "You are meeting the principal and associate principal about next year's timetable. They have decided, subject to your view, to move senior ensemble rehearsals from timetabled lessons to before-school sessions to free up teaching periods. The principal is a personal friend and a capable leader. You believe the change will reduce participation among students who rely on public transport.", "said": "\"I know it isn't ideal, Dijon, but it's the cleanest way to get the periods back. Plenty of schools run ensembles before school. I'd rather we just make the call and move on. Can you live with it?\"", "task": "Disagree clearly with the decision on its merits, without straining the relationship or making it about her judgement.", "seconds": 50, "models": [{"text": "Honestly, Lesley, I can't support it as it stands, and I'd rather say so now than later. My reason is one: a large share of our senior players come in by train and bus from outside the area, and a 7.15 start isn't reachable for many of them. We'd lose those students first, and they're the ones the program exists for. If we need the periods, I'd rather find them elsewhere in the music allocation, and I'll bring you an option by Friday.", "why": "It answers the direct question directly, gives one concrete reason, and offers an alternative with a date, which keeps the dissent constructive."}, {"text": "I'll give you a straight answer: no, I don't think I can. I accept that other schools run ensembles before school. Ours is different because it's a selective program drawing from a wide catchment, and the students who'd drop out are the ones furthest away. I may be outvoted on this, and that's your call. I'd just ask that we look at the travel data first.", "why": "It concedes the factual point, identifies why it doesn't transfer, acknowledges her authority, and asks for one specific thing."}], "checks": ["Did I say 'no' or 'I disagree' in the first sentence?", "Did I give one reason rather than several?", "Did I acknowledge her right to decide?", "Did I avoid apologising for disagreeing?"]}, {"id": "principled-dissent-s2", "room": "SCSA syllabus committee", "setup": "A senior academic who chairs a subcommittee has recommended removing the jazz and contemporary context from the Music ATAR course to make room for an expanded Western art music analysis component. Several members are nodding. You believe the change narrows the course's cultural range and will disadvantage students in contemporary-focused programs.", "said": "\"The analytical demands of the Western art tradition give students a rigour that the contemporary contexts, frankly, have struggled to provide. I think we've been spreading the course too thinly, and this is the moment to consolidate around depth.\"", "task": "Dissent from a senior academic's recommendation on substance, with respect, and make the objection impossible to overlook.", "seconds": 65, "models": [{"text": "Professor Lang, I disagree with the recommendation, and I'd like to say why. The rigour of a context depends on how it is examined, not on the repertoire itself. Jazz has an analytical tradition as demanding as any, harmonic, formal and historical. If the contemporary papers have lacked rigour, that is a design problem we can fix, not a reason to remove the context. I'd support strengthening the analytical demand across all contexts. If the committee proceeds, I'd ask that my view be minuted.", "why": "It addresses the stated premise directly, offers an alternative that shares his goal, and formally records the dissent."}, {"text": "I'd like to put a contrary view on the record. I agree with the aim of depth, but removing the jazz and contemporary context would make this a narrower course than the one students and schools actually take. Many of the schools that offer this course are built around contemporary programs. We'd be asking their candidates to study a tradition their schools barely teach, which isn't depth, it's attrition. I'd rather we deepen within each context.", "why": "It grants the aim, then argues from the consequence for candidates, which shifts the ground from taste to impact."}], "checks": ["Did I name the disagreement before the reasons?", "Did I contest the premise, not the person?", "Did I offer something I would support?", "Did I avoid a claim or figure I can't back up?"]}, {"id": "principled-dissent-s3", "room": "festival association board", "setup": "The board is voting on a proposal to accept naming sponsorship from a gambling-adjacent company for the schools' jazz festival. The offer would cover a major shortfall. The majority favour accepting. As treasurer you know the money matters, but you believe the association's reputation with schools and parents is at risk.", "said": "\"Look, I share people's discomfort, but this is a lot of money and we're not in a position to be precious. The sponsor isn't asking for anything other than signage. I think we accept, with gratitude, and move on.\"", "task": "Dissent from the majority on the merits, as treasurer, without suggesting your colleagues lack principles.", "seconds": 60, "models": [{"text": "As treasurer, I'd like to vote against this, and I'll explain from the treasurer's side. Our income is mostly school entry fees, and principals sign those off. If even a handful of schools withdraw because of the sponsor, we lose more than the sponsorship brings in, and we lose it every year after. I'd support a motion to find the shortfall through a revised fee structure instead, and I'll prepare one. If the board goes the other way, I'll accept that, and I'd ask that my dissent be noted.", "why": "It frames the dissent in financial terms that respect the majority's reasoning, offers an alternative, and records the view formally."}, {"text": "I disagree, and not because I think anyone here is being careless. We exist to serve schools, and schools have obligations to parents about what they promote to children. A sponsor like this puts every member school in an awkward position. I'd rather run a leaner festival this year than ask them to defend our signage. I'll accept the board's decision, but I want to be clear I'm voting no.", "why": "It disowns any accusation of carelessness at the start, grounds the objection in the association's purpose, and states the vote unambiguously."}], "checks": ["Was my vote unambiguous?", "Did I frame the objection in terms the majority accepts?", "Did I avoid implying anyone lacks integrity?", "Did I ask for my dissent to be recorded?"]}, {"id": "principled-dissent-s4", "room": "university academic board", "setup": "A university academic board is about to approve a proposal to replace the final-year recital in the performance degree with a portfolio of recorded work. The proposal comes from the head of school, and there is a strong consensus. You, as an external member, believe the live recital is the professional threshold students most need to cross.", "said": "\"I think we can take this as broadly agreed. The portfolio better reflects the profession as it is now, students produce more and better work, and the anxiety associated with a single recital has been a recurring concern. Unless there's anything further, I'll put it.\"", "task": "Stop the item being put without objection, and dissent clearly and briefly on substance.", "seconds": 55, "models": [{"text": "Before it's put, Chair, I'd like to record a different view. I agree the portfolio reflects much of the profession. But performers are still hired on what they do live, under pressure, with no second take, and the recital is the only point in the degree where that's assessed. Removing it trades a real professional threshold for a more comfortable one. I'd support keeping a shorter recital alongside the portfolio. If the board disagrees, I'd ask that my view be minuted.", "why": "It intervenes before the item is closed, concedes the true part of the case, and identifies the specific thing that would be lost."}, {"text": "Chair, I'm not able to take it as agreed. I've performed professionally for twenty-six years, and the skill that most separates working musicians from others is recovery under live conditions. A portfolio can't assess that. I take the point about anxiety seriously, which is why I'd rather we teach to it than remove it. I'll accept the board's decision, but I don't want silence to be read as consent.", "why": "It uses his professional standing economically, addresses the strongest argument for the proposal, and makes clear why he is speaking."}], "checks": ["Did I speak before the item was closed?", "Did I concede what is true in the proposal?", "Did I name the specific loss?", "Did I make clear silence would have been read as consent?"]}, {"id": "principled-dissent-s5", "room": "school council", "setup": "You chair the school council. The council is about to endorse the school's annual report. The principal's draft describes the music program's results in terms you know overstate the outcomes: participation figures include students who withdrew mid-year. Every other member is ready to endorse. As chair you must be careful, but you cannot let the inaccuracy pass.", "said": "\"It's a strong report, and I think it tells our story well. I'd move that council endorse it as presented so it can go to the community next week.\"", "task": "As chair, dissent from endorsing the report as presented, on accuracy, without discrediting the principal or the report.", "seconds": 50, "models": [{"text": "Before I put that, I need to raise one point, and I raise it because the report is strong. The music participation figure includes students who withdrew during the year. I'd be uncomfortable with council endorsing a figure we know to be overstated, because it's our endorsement on the document. I'd suggest we endorse the report subject to that figure being corrected, which I'm sure the principal would prefer too.", "why": "It frames the dissent as protecting the report and the council, offers a conditional endorsement, and gives the principal a dignified way to agree."}, {"text": "I'm not able to put the motion as moved, and I want to be clear the issue is narrow. The participation figure on page six counts enrolments at the start of the year, not completions. When council endorses the report, we're vouching for it. A small correction resolves it. Could we endorse it with that figure revised?", "why": "It names the specific figure and why it matters, states plainly what the council's endorsement means, and keeps the remedy small."}], "checks": ["Did I make the dissent narrow and specific?", "Did I protect the principal's standing?", "Did I explain why the council's endorsement matters?", "Did I offer a way to endorse?"]}, {"id": "principled-dissent-s6", "room": "conference panel", "setup": "At a national music education conference, you are on a panel with a high-profile academic who argues that ensemble programs in schools are a nineteenth-century model and should give way to individual, technology-based music making. The audience is receptive. The moderator invites you to respond.", "said": "\"The large ensemble is a relic of military bands and the factory model of schooling. Young people today make music alone, on laptops, and share it globally. We should meet them there rather than drill them in rows.\"", "task": "Disagree publicly, on substance, with a senior and popular speaker, in a way the audience will respect.", "seconds": 70, "models": [{"text": "I disagree, and I'll say why with real respect for the argument. The history is right: school bands came partly from military models. But the case for ensembles isn't historical, it's musical. Playing in a group trains things a laptop can't: listening while playing, adjusting time and intonation to other people in real time, subordinating yourself to a shared result. Those are the skills professional musicians actually trade on. I'd keep both, and I'd resist the idea that one has to replace the other.", "why": "It grants the historical claim, contests the inference, and grounds the dissent in observable musical skills rather than nostalgia."}, {"text": "I'm going to take a different view, Professor Ellis. Making music alone on a laptop is real and valuable, and I teach it. But what I see in students is that it rarely teaches them to listen to anyone else. The ensemble is one of the few places a young person learns that their part only makes sense in relation to others. That's not drill, it's an education in attention. I'd argue we need it more now, not less.", "why": "It names the speaker respectfully, reframes 'drill' as attention, and argues that the speaker's own premise strengthens the case for ensembles."}], "checks": ["Did I state the disagreement clearly at the start?", "Did I concede what was accurate?", "Did I contest the inference rather than mock the view?", "Did I avoid sentimentality?"]}]}, {"id": "concede-to-gain", "name": "Concede to gain", "family": "Dissenting", "what": "It states the opposing case at its strongest, concedes the part of it that is true, and then places the real objection exactly where it cannot be dismissed as a misunderstanding.", "when": "When the other side has a genuinely strong point that you cannot credibly deny, and your objection will only be heard once the room sees you have understood and accepted it.", "teach": "Clever people discount any objection that seems not to have grasped their argument. The concession removes that defence. By putting their case better than they did, and granting what is true in it, you show that your disagreement survives full understanding, which is the only kind of disagreement a serious room takes seriously. The technique is old: it is the discipline of the steel-man, and in advocacy the practice of conceding the bad facts before your opponent can use them. It works because it changes your position in the room from adversary to judge. Having conceded, you are visibly fair, and the objection that follows inherits that fairness.\n\nThe structure has three beats and the proportions matter. First, the steel-man: their argument in its strongest form, in one or two sentences, ideally stronger than they put it. Second, the concession, explicit and unhedged: 'That's right, and I accept it.' Third, the pivot to the remaining objection, which must be narrow, precise and genuinely untouched by the concession. The pivot word should be quiet; 'and yet', 'what that doesn't settle is', or simply a new sentence. 'However' is acceptable; 'but' after a concession often sounds as if the concession was insincere.\n\nSenior practitioners make the concession real. They concede something that costs them, not a trivial point; a concession of a minor matter while the major one goes uncontested reads as a debating trick, and a room of academics will name it. They also pause after the concession, a full beat, so that the room registers it before the objection arrives. The voice on the steel-man should be warm and unhurried, as though the argument were their own; on the objection, it should become a little lower and more exact. Keep the whole thing to under a minute; the concession earns attention, and the objection must use it before it expires.\n\nIt goes wrong in three ways. The token concession concedes nothing of weight, which signals manipulation. The over-concession grants so much that nothing remains to object to, and the room rightly asks why you are still speaking. And the straw steel-man restates the opposing case in a slightly weakened form; the person whose argument it is will correct you, and you will have handed them the floor. If you are not sure you can state their case well, ask them to confirm your version before you object to it.", "phrasings": ["Let me put the case for this as strongly as I can, because I think it is strong.", "I accept the point about cost entirely; I don't think it's contestable. What it doesn't settle is the question of who bears it.", "You're right that the current model favours students with prior tuition. I'd go further: it was designed by people who'd had it. And yet abolishing the audition doesn't fix that.", "If I've understood the argument at its best, it's this. I agree with all of it except the final step.", "That's a fair hit, and I'd rather concede it than argue around it. The objection I still have is narrower.", "Professor Hale's evidence on attrition is better than anything I can offer, and I accept it. My concern is about what it measures.", "Grant everything you've said about efficiency. The question remaining is whether we can reverse it.", "I'll concede the principle. Where I part company is the timing."], "avoid": ["I see where you're coming from, but : a vague, non-committal acknowledgement that concedes nothing and signals the 'but' is the real point.", "Obviously you have a point, though it's not really relevant : the concession is withdrawn as it is offered, which reads as contempt.", "So what you're saying is that you don't care about the regional students : a straw steel-man that misstates their view and invites correction.", "I completely agree with everything you've said, and I just have a tiny concern : over-concession that leaves nothing standing for the objection."], "scenarios": [{"id": "concede-to-gain-s1", "room": "principal's office", "setup": "The principal and business manager are proposing to reduce the music program's instrumental tutor budget by moving to group lessons in all year levels. The business manager has strong data showing group tuition is more cost effective and that other schools have done it without obvious harm. You think group lessons are fine in junior years but will damage senior performance standards.", "said": "\"I've looked at four other specialist programs. Three have moved to group tuition in every year, and their ATAR music results haven't dropped. It's simply more efficient. I'd want a compelling reason not to follow them.\"", "task": "Concede the strength of the efficiency evidence, then land the specific objection about senior students.", "seconds": 60, "models": [{"text": "That's good evidence, Mark, and I won't argue with it. Group tuition is more efficient, and for most students in Years 7 to 10 I think it's as good or better. What the ATAR results don't show is the top end: the students who go on to audition for WAAPA or interstate conservatoriums. They need individual work on repertoire that a group can't share. So I'd accept group lessons through Year 10, and ask that we protect individual lessons for the Year 11 and 12 performance students.", "why": "It concedes the evidence fully and precisely, then identifies the specific population the evidence does not capture."}, {"text": "I'll put your case at its strongest: group tuition costs less, the results elsewhere haven't fallen, and nobody can show harm. I accept all three. My objection is that ATAR averages wouldn't show harm to the five or six students a year whose next step is a tertiary audition. That's where individual lessons earn their cost. Could we keep them for that group alone?", "why": "It restates the opposing argument better than it was put, concedes each limb, and confines the objection to a small, costable group."}], "checks": ["Did I concede something that costs me?", "Was my steel-man at least as strong as theirs?", "Is my remaining objection untouched by what I conceded?", "Did I pause after the concession?"]}, {"id": "concede-to-gain-s2", "room": "SCSA syllabus committee", "setup": "The committee is debating whether to retain a compulsory set work in the Music ATAR course. A respected assessment expert argues that set works narrow teaching, encourage rote preparation, and disadvantage schools with fewer resources. Her argument is strong and the room is persuaded. You believe a set work gives every student a shared analytical anchor that open choice will not.", "said": "\"Set works produce exactly what you'd predict: teaching to the work, memorised analyses, and a generation of students who can talk about one symphony and nothing else. Open choice of repertoire would let teachers build genuine analytical skill.\"", "task": "Concede the strongest parts of her case and then show what open choice would lose.", "seconds": 65, "models": [{"text": "Dr Chen is right about the rote preparation, and I'd add that the published study guides make it worse: some students learn an analysis rather than learn to analyse. I accept that. What open choice would lose is comparability. If every candidate brings different repertoire, the examiner can't set a question that tests the same thing for everyone, and the paper drifts toward generic questions that reward fluent writing. I'd keep a set work, and change how it's examined.", "why": "It concedes more than she claimed, then names a precise assessment consequence of her alternative."}, {"text": "I'd like to put Dr Chen's case in its strongest form. Set works reward preparation over thinking, they favour well-resourced schools, and they bore students. All of that is true. And yet the schools least able to build their own repertoire program are the ones who benefit most from a shared work with shared resources. Open choice frees the strong schools and leaves the others on their own. That's the part I can't accept.", "why": "It steel-mans her case including the equity argument, then turns equity against the remedy, which is the strongest available objection."}], "checks": ["Did I concede her points in words she'd accept?", "Did my objection follow from a different consideration, not a denial?", "Did I avoid conceding only the trivial point?", "Did I keep the objection to one idea?"]}, {"id": "concede-to-gain-s3", "room": "festival association board", "setup": "A new board member, a marketing professional, argues that the festival should move from a weekday format to a weekend to attract larger audiences and better media coverage. The case is well prepared and several directors are impressed. You know weekend scheduling will lose many teachers, who volunteer on school time.", "said": "\"Weekday events get no audience apart from the bands themselves. Parents can't come, the media won't cover it, and sponsors notice. A Saturday festival would double the audience, and that's what keeps us viable.\"", "task": "Concede the audience and sponsorship argument, then land the workforce objection.", "seconds": 50, "models": [{"text": "You're right, and I'd go further: a weekday festival is invisible to most families, and that's been a problem for years. I don't dispute any of it. The difficulty is who runs it. Almost every band director and volunteer adjudicator gives us their time on school days because their employers release them. On a Saturday they're unpaid and have families. We'd gain an audience and lose the people who make the festival.", "why": "It concedes beyond what was argued, then introduces a constraint the proposal had not considered."}, {"text": "I think the audience case is unanswerable, and I accept it. What I'd want to test is whether a Saturday is the only way to get it. A weekday festival with a Saturday showcase for the award-winning bands might give us the audience and the media you want, without asking forty teachers for an unpaid weekend. Would you be willing to look at that?", "why": "It concedes the goal entirely and redirects the objection into a hybrid that preserves the proposer's aims."}], "checks": ["Did I concede the core of his argument?", "Did I introduce a consideration he hadn't addressed?", "Did I keep the tone collegial towards a new member?", "Did I avoid a long list of practical problems?"]}, {"id": "concede-to-gain-s4", "room": "university academic board", "setup": "On a university academic committee, a dean argues for consolidating three small specialist music electives, including jazz improvisation, into one general musicianship unit. The financial and pedagogical case is well made. You believe jazz improvisation is a distinct discipline that will disappear inside a general unit.", "said": "\"Each of these electives runs with eight or nine students. A combined unit would run at thirty, with a stronger peer cohort, and students would get exposure to three traditions instead of one. I don't think that's a loss; I think it's a gain.\"", "task": "Concede the strength of the dean's case, then make the objection about improvisation land.", "seconds": 60, "models": [{"text": "I think that's largely right, Dean. Classes of eight are hard to justify, and exposure to three traditions is a real gain for most students. I accept both. What a combined unit can't do is teach improvisation, because improvisation isn't content you can cover in four weeks; it's a practice built over hundreds of hours of playing. A general unit would introduce it and stop. I'd support consolidating the other two, and keep improvisation as a stand-alone elective.", "why": "It concedes the class-size and breadth arguments fully, then distinguishes improvisation on the nature of the learning, not its prestige."}, {"text": "Let me grant the case completely: the numbers are unsustainable, and the combined unit would be a good unit. My concern is narrower. The students who take jazz improvisation do so because they intend to work in it, and the skill takes sustained practice that a survey unit can't give. If it's absorbed, those students will simply go elsewhere. Could we consolidate, but run improvisation as a strand with its own assessed component?", "why": "It concedes everything, then makes the objection about who is lost and offers a design fix the dean can accept."}], "checks": ["Did I accept the financial case without qualification?", "Did my objection rest on the nature of the discipline, not its status?", "Did I offer something the dean can say yes to?", "Was it under a minute?"]}, {"id": "concede-to-gain-s5", "room": "parent body", "setup": "At a meeting of the music program's parent support group, a parent who is a senior lawyer argues that the program's commitment rule, requiring students to stay in the program through Year 9, is unfair and should be dropped. She is articulate and has the sympathy of the room. You enforce the rule and believe it protects ensembles and the students themselves.", "said": "\"A twelve-year-old can't meaningfully commit to three years of anything. Holding a child to an enrolment decision made in Year 6 is, frankly, not something we'd accept in any other area of school. If they've lost interest, why keep them?\"", "task": "Concede the strength of her argument, then land the reason for the rule.", "seconds": 60, "models": [{"text": "I think that's a fair argument, and I'll accept the premise: a twelve-year-old can't fully understand a three-year commitment. That's precisely why the rule exists. In my experience, the point at which students most want to stop is the middle of Year 8, when the instrument gets harder before it gets easier. If they stop there, many of them later regret it. The rule isn't there to hold children to a decision; it's to carry them through the plateau. And we do release students where there's genuine reason.", "why": "It accepts her premise outright and turns it into the rationale for the rule, which is the strongest form of concede-to-gain."}, {"text": "Let me put your case fairly: children change, interests change, and an enrolment decision made at eleven shouldn't bind a child at fourteen. I agree with that as a general principle. What's different here is that each student holds a place in an ensemble someone else auditioned for and missed. When one leaves, the ensemble loses a part and another child lost the chance. That's the obligation the rule protects.", "why": "It steel-mans the principle and then introduces the fact that changes its application: places are scarce and shared."}], "checks": ["Did I accept her premise rather than argue around it?", "Did my objection rest on a fact she hadn't addressed?", "Did I avoid sounding defensive about the rule?", "Did I mention that exceptions exist?"]}, {"id": "concede-to-gain-s6", "room": "arts funding panel", "setup": "On an arts funding panel, a fellow assessor argues that an application from a long-standing youth jazz orchestra should be ranked below a newer, community-based program because the orchestra serves students who are already advantaged. The assessor is persuasive and the equity criterion is real. You think the orchestra's application is strong on its own merits: its outreach section, on page eight, proposes free workshops in eleven public schools, three of them schools the community program also serves.", "said": "\"The orchestra is excellent, nobody disputes that. But its players come overwhelmingly from selective programs and private tuition. The community program reaches kids who've never touched an instrument. On our equity criterion, it isn't close.\"", "task": "Concede the equity argument as stated, then make the case for the orchestra's application on the evidence in front of the panel.", "seconds": 55, "models": [{"text": "On the criterion as you've framed it, I agree, it isn't close: the community program reaches students the orchestra doesn't. I accept that. What I'd ask the panel to look at is the orchestra's outreach section on page eight: they're proposing free workshops for eleven public schools, and three of those schools are on the community program's list. If we fund both, the community program's students have somewhere to go next. That's a pathway, not a competition.", "why": "It concedes the equity judgement fully, then uses the application's own evidence to reframe the two bids as complementary."}, {"text": "I'd concede the point entirely on current membership. The orchestra's players are mostly advantaged, and that should count. Where I'd differ is that our criterion asks about the program's reach, not just its membership, and this application is largely about extending that reach. I'd score it lower on current equity, as you would, and higher on the outreach, and I think that puts it in the fundable band.", "why": "It concedes the factual point and the scoring consequence, then separates two elements of the criterion so the objection lands on the scoring itself."}], "checks": ["Did I concede the equity point without qualification?", "Did I point to specific evidence in the application?", "Is my objection about the criterion, not about the other assessor?", "Did I avoid arguing that excellence should outweigh equity?"]}]}, {"id": "naming-what-the-room-is-avoiding", "name": "Naming what the room is avoiding", "family": "Dissenting", "what": "It says aloud the issue that everyone in the room is aware of and nobody has raised, so that the discussion can deal with the real question instead of circling a proxy for it.", "when": "When the discussion keeps returning to safe surface issues, people are visibly talking around something, and the decision will be poor or unworkable unless the unspoken matter is put on the table.", "teach": "Every committee has topics it prefers not to touch: a colleague's performance, a funding source, a decision already made elsewhere, a conflict of interest, the fact that the proposal cannot actually be delivered. Highly educated people are very good at avoiding these elegantly. They discuss the proxy instead, at length and with real intelligence, and the meeting ends with a decision that does not address what everybody knew. Naming the avoided issue is one of the most valuable contributions a member can make, and one of the rarest, because it costs the speaker something: they become the person who said it.\n\nThe mechanism is relief. Most rooms are not avoiding the issue because they disagree about it, but because nobody wants to be first. Once it is named in a measured way, the room usually follows quickly, and the speaker is often privately thanked. What makes the difference between relief and embarrassment is the manner of the naming. It must be impersonal, descriptive and brief, and it must be framed as being in the room's interest: 'I think we may be circling something, and it would help to say it.' The issue is then stated factually, without accusation, and followed at once by a question that gives the room a next step.\n\nSenior practitioners name the avoided issue as an observation about the discussion, not about any person. They describe what is happening ('We've spent twenty minutes on the timetable, and I suspect the real question is staffing') rather than what anyone is doing. They speak calmly and a little more slowly than usual, and they sit still; any visible tension in the body reads as confrontation. They name it once, then stop and let the silence do its work. A gap of several seconds after the naming is normal and should not be filled. Thirty to fifty seconds is right; the shorter the better.\n\nIt goes wrong in three ways. The first is naming a person rather than an issue, which turns the observation into an attack and makes the room close ranks. The second is mistaking your own private grievance for the room's avoidance; if only you are thinking it, you have not named the room's issue, you have raised your own, and you should do so in the ordinary way. The third is the wrong forum: some issues, such as an individual's conduct or health, should be named to the chair privately, not to the meeting. Part of the judgement is knowing which room the avoided issue belongs in.", "phrasings": ["I wonder if we're circling something, and it might help to name it.", "Can I say what I think is underneath this, so we can deal with it directly?", "We've spent half an hour on the timetable, and I suspect the real question is whether we have the staff.", "I may be the only one thinking this, but I don't think I am: is this decision already made elsewhere?", "It seems to me the difficulty isn't the policy, it's that we don't have the money to implement it. Should we say so in the recommendation?", "I'd rather we discussed the thing we're all aware of than vote on something that doesn't address it.", "If it would help, I'll put it plainly: the question is whether the current arrangement is working.", "I think we're being very careful around one issue, and I'd suggest we'll make a better decision if we aren't."], "avoid": ["Let's be honest, the real problem here is Greg : names a person rather than the issue, turning observation into accusation.", "Am I the only one who sees the elephant in the room? : cliché and slightly theatrical, it performs courage rather than doing the work.", "I don't want to say anything controversial, but there might possibly be an issue nobody's mentioned : so hedged the room cannot tell what the issue is.", "Nobody here has the courage to say it, so I will : implies the room is cowardly and makes everyone defensive."], "scenarios": [{"id": "naming-what-the-room-is-avoiding-s1", "room": "festival association board", "setup": "The board is discussing the decline in entries for the open division of the festival over three years. Members have proposed changes to the entry fee, the date and the adjudication format. Everyone knows, but no one has said, that the decline began when a long-serving adjudicator with a reputation for harsh public comments was appointed head adjudicator. He is a respected figure and a friend of several board members.", "said": "\"Perhaps it's the timing in the school year. Term 3 is very full for schools. If we moved to Term 2, we might pick up some of the bands that have dropped away.\"", "task": "Name the issue the board is avoiding, impersonally and in the board's interest, without attacking the adjudicator.", "seconds": 45, "models": [{"text": "Can I raise something that I think may be underneath this? We've looked at fees, date and format, and the decline began the year we changed the adjudication panel. I'm not suggesting anyone is at fault, but I've heard from several directors that the public comments have discouraged students. Should we ask schools directly before we change anything else?", "why": "It ties the issue to observable timing, attributes the concern to others' reports rather than to his view, and ends with an evidence-gathering step."}, {"text": "I think we may be circling one thing. Every director I've spoken to about not entering has mentioned the tone of adjudication, not the date or the fee. I'd suggest we survey last year's non-entering schools before we vote on anything, and include a question about adjudication. If I'm wrong, the survey will show it.", "why": "It names the issue as data rather than opinion and proposes a method that lets the board confirm or dismiss it without anyone losing face."}], "checks": ["Did I describe a pattern rather than name a person?", "Did I frame it as in the board's interest?", "Did I give the board a next step?", "Did I stop and let the silence sit?"]}, {"id": "naming-what-the-room-is-avoiding-s2", "room": "SCSA syllabus committee", "setup": "The committee has spent an hour refining the wording of a new practical assessment for the Music ATAR course. Everyone knows that the Authority has already indicated it will not fund the additional examiners the new assessment would require, but the committee is carrying on as though it might. The chair has not raised it.", "said": "\"I think the descriptors for the improvisation criterion are much stronger now. If we tighten the language around stylistic awareness, I think this is ready to go forward.\"", "task": "Name the resourcing issue the committee is avoiding, so the recommendation is realistic.", "seconds": 55, "models": [{"text": "Before we finalise, could I name something I think we're all aware of? The Authority indicated last term that additional examiners aren't funded. If that's still the position, this assessment can't be delivered as written, however good the descriptors are. I'd suggest we either recommend it with an explicit resourcing condition, or design a version that works within existing examiner numbers. I'd rather we chose that knowingly.", "why": "It names the fact plainly, separates it from the quality of the work, and offers two realistic paths."}, {"text": "I think the descriptors are excellent, and I'm reluctant to say this. But we're refining an assessment that, on what we've been told, won't be resourced. It would help me to know from the chair whether that's changed. If not, I'd want our recommendation to say so explicitly, so the decision about resourcing is made by the people who hold it.", "why": "It credits the work, names the issue as a question to the chair, and puts responsibility where it belongs."}], "checks": ["Did I separate the issue from the quality of the work?", "Did I state the fact, not speculate?", "Did I offer a way to proceed?", "Did I avoid implying the chair was concealing it?"]}, {"id": "naming-what-the-room-is-avoiding-s3", "room": "school council", "setup": "You chair the school council. A proposal to introduce a new enrichment program is on the table. The school's facilities budget is overcommitted, and the council has been told at a previous meeting that the music building's refurbishment is deferred. Members keep discussing the enrichment program's merits. Nobody has connected it to the deferred refurbishment, which they all know about.", "said": "\"I think it's a wonderful idea. Our families have been asking for exactly this kind of program, and it would put us on a level with the private schools in the area.\"", "task": "As chair, name the budget trade-off the council is avoiding, neutrally.", "seconds": 45, "models": [{"text": "I'd like to name something that I think belongs in this discussion. We were told in Term 2 that the music building refurbishment is deferred for budget reasons. This program would draw on the same facilities budget. So the real question for council may be whether this is a higher priority than the refurbishment. I'd rather we answered that directly than endorse both.", "why": "It cites what the council was formally told, names the trade-off as the real question, and declines a false consensus."}, {"text": "Before we go further, I think it would help to say aloud what's implied. Supporting this program means choosing it over something else in a constrained budget, and the obvious candidate is the deferred refurbishment. I've an interest in that, so I'll declare it. Could the principal tell us what the program would cost, so council can weigh it fairly?", "why": "It names the trade-off and, crucially, declares his own interest as head of music, which keeps the naming clean."}], "checks": ["Did I declare my interest if I have one?", "Did I name a trade-off, not a preference?", "Did I cite something the council already knows?", "Did I stay neutral as chair?"]}, {"id": "naming-what-the-room-is-avoiding-s4", "room": "university academic board", "setup": "A university committee is reviewing a performance program whose enrolments have halved. The discussion has focused on marketing, scholarships and competitor programs. Everyone in the room knows that two senior staff in the program have been in open conflict for some time and that students have spoken about it, but no one has mentioned it.", "said": "\"I suspect the marketing has simply not kept pace. Our competitors are on every platform students use. We need a proper digital strategy and probably some targeted scholarships.\"", "task": "Name the issue being avoided in a way that is appropriate to the forum and does not single out individuals in the meeting.", "seconds": 50, "models": [{"text": "Those are reasonable measures. Could I add one thing that I think we may be leaving aside? The exit surveys mention the culture of the program more often than marketing or cost. I'm not in a position to judge what's behind that, and this may not be the forum to explore it. But I'd suggest the review's terms include the student experience, so that it can be looked at properly.", "why": "It names the issue at the level of evidence and culture, not individuals, and recognises this is not the forum for the detail."}, {"text": "I'd like to suggest the review look beyond recruitment. Enrolment is one measure; whether students recommend the program to others is another, and that's partly about how the program feels from the inside. I'll leave the detail to the dean, who is better placed than this committee. But I don't think we'll fix the numbers by marketing alone.", "why": "It names the area without naming the conflict, hands it to the right person, and stops the room from settling on an incomplete answer."}], "checks": ["Did I avoid naming individuals?", "Did I use evidence rather than rumour?", "Did I respect the limits of this forum?", "Did I prevent the room settling on a proxy?"]}, {"id": "naming-what-the-room-is-avoiding-s5", "room": "department meeting", "setup": "In a music department meeting, the team is discussing why the junior concert band's results have slipped. Suggestions include rehearsal times and repertoire choice. Everyone knows the junior band has had four different conductors in eighteen months because of staffing changes, and that this is the most likely cause. Nobody wants to say it, partly because one of those conductors is in the room.", "said": "\"I wonder if the repertoire's been pitched too high. Some of those charts were ambitious for Year 7 and 8 players.\"", "task": "Name the continuity issue without implying any conductor is at fault.", "seconds": 40, "models": [{"text": "Repertoire may be part of it. I think the larger factor is one we all know: that band has had four conductors in eighteen months. None of that is anyone's fault; it's staffing. But young players need one consistent voice, and they haven't had it. I'd suggest we commit one conductor to them for the next two years, and judge the results after that.", "why": "It names the cause plainly, explicitly removes individual fault, and turns it into a structural commitment."}, {"text": "Can I put something on the table that I think is under this? Every conductor that band has had has been good. The problem is that there have been four of them. I'd rather we fixed continuity first, and then see whether the repertoire is still an issue.", "why": "It credits every conductor, including the one in the room, before naming the issue, and offers a sequence for testing it."}], "checks": ["Did I remove fault from any individual?", "Did I name the structural cause?", "Did I propose a concrete fix?", "Was it brief?"]}, {"id": "naming-what-the-room-is-avoiding-s6", "room": "principal's office", "setup": "You are meeting the principal and associate principal about a proposed restructure of performing arts leadership. They are discussing reporting lines and meeting schedules. You know, and they know you know, that the restructure has been shaped to resolve a long-running tension between two senior colleagues, and that the reporting lines being discussed reflect that rather than the needs of the program.", "said": "\"I think if we have music and drama both reporting through the performing arts head, and a monthly coordination meeting, that should make communication much clearer for everyone.\"", "task": "Name the purpose the structure is really serving, respectfully, so the decision can be made on its actual merits.", "seconds": 55, "models": [{"text": "Can I be candid, because I think it will help us get this right? I understand the structure is partly meant to settle some of the tension we've had in the area. That's a legitimate aim. But if that's the real purpose, I'd rather we designed for it directly, because reporting lines won't fix a working relationship. They'll just formalise it. Could we separate the two questions?", "why": "It names the purpose respectfully, grants that it is legitimate, and shows why conflating it with structure will fail."}, {"text": "I'd like to ask a direct question, because I think it's underneath this. Is the structure being designed around the program's needs, or around a difficulty between people? I can work with either, but they lead to different structures, and if it's the second, I'd want to be part of solving that properly, rather than having the reporting lines carry it.", "why": "It frames the naming as a question, keeps it to the issue rather than the individuals, and offers himself as part of the remedy."}], "checks": ["Did I name the purpose rather than the people?", "Did I grant the legitimacy of the aim?", "Did I explain why the avoided issue matters to the decision?", "Did I stay calm and brief?"]}]}, {"id": "procedural-intervention", "name": "The procedural intervention", "family": "Procedure", "what": "It steps outside the substance to correct the frame the substance is travelling in: what the item is, what the body may decide, what process was agreed, or whether the rules are being kept.", "when": "When the room is about to decide something it has no remit to decide, by a path it has not agreed, or on a proposal nobody has actually stated. It is strongest early, before positions harden, or at a seam where a motion is being framed.", "teach": "A procedural intervention addresses the frame rather than the picture. Its force lies in the fact that it is not an argument. In a room of people who argue for a living, a substantive objection invites a substantive rebuttal; a procedural one changes the terms of the exchange. Clever people concede a question of jurisdiction or sequence far more readily than a counter-argument, because it appeals to a standard everyone has already accepted and asks no one to admit they were wrong.\n\nSenior practitioners keep four species distinct. Scope asks whether the matter falls within the item, or within the body's remit at all. Process asks whether the agreed path (a paper, a consultation, a referral, a first reading) has been followed. Clarification asks what is actually being proposed before anyone debates it. A point of order proper is narrower: it alleges a breach of the rules or standing orders, it is addressed to the chair, and the chair rules on it before debate resumes, which is how Joske and Renton both treat it. Calling something a point of order when it is really a disagreement is the quickest way to spend credibility you will need later.\n\nExecution is short and addressed to the chair, not to the speaker. Name the category in the first clause ('A question of scope, Chair'), state the problem in one sentence, and offer a remedy: defer, refer, separate the motion, or note the concern and proceed. Then stop. The chair now owns the problem, and a good chair will be grateful to have been handed a solution with it. Twenty seconds is usually enough; forty is the outer edge.\n\nThe register is administrative, almost unexcited, which signals housekeeping rather than combat. Drop the voice a little and slow it. A light raised hand or a hand flat on the table catches the chair's eye; look at the chair while you speak and at nobody else, so that no colleague feels personally corrected.\n\nTiming carries much of the meaning. At a seam, the intervention reads as stewardship. In the middle of a colleague's best paragraph it reads as a tactic, however correct it is.\n\nIt goes wrong in three ways. Used often, it marks you as someone who hides behind process, and the room starts discounting you. Used as disguised substance, a 'point of order' that is really 'I disagree', it invites a ruling against you in front of everyone. And used without a remedy, it merely stalls the room and leaves the chair irritated rather than helped.", "phrasings": ["A question of scope, Chair, before we go further: is this item about the assessment weighting or the content itself? The paper only authorises us to consider the first.", "Chair, may I raise a point of order? The motion now before us differs materially from the one circulated, and our rules require notice of that change.", "Purely on process: has the reference group seen this draft? If not, I wonder whether we are in a position to endorse it today rather than note it.", "Before we debate it, could we be clear about what is being proposed? I think I have heard two versions in the last ten minutes, and they are not the same.", "I raise this only as a matter of sequence. We are settling the budget line before we have agreed the program it is meant to fund.", "Chair, under our terms of reference I think this sits with the finance subcommittee. Might we refer it there and take a recommendation at the next meeting?", "Might we take the two parts of the motion separately? I suspect we agree on the first and not on the second, and I would rather not lose the first.", "A procedural observation, nothing more: we are past time on this item, and the next one carries a deadline. Do you want to extend, or carry it over?"], "avoid": ["Point of order! That's not what we're here to discuss. : Exclamatory and accusatory, and almost certainly not a point of order; the chair will rule against you and the room will remember the tone.", "With the greatest respect, I really must insist that we follow proper procedure here. : Pompous and vague; it names no rule, offers no remedy, and implies colleagues are behaving improperly.", "I'm not sure if this is a procedural thing, but I just wonder whether maybe we should possibly be talking about this at all? : Hedged into nothing; the move lives on its precision, and this has none.", "Just procedurally, I think this whole proposal is misconceived. : Substance in a procedural costume; it forfeits the neutrality that gives the intervention its force."], "scenarios": [{"id": "procedural-intervention-s1", "room": "SCSA syllabus committee", "setup": "The committee is reviewing the content section of the revised Year 11 Music syllabus. The agenda item is content only; assessment is scheduled for a separate meeting with the assessment officers present. A senior moderator has begun arguing that the practical component's weighting should move, and two members are warming to the idea. If the committee drifts into an informal view on weighting, it will be cited later as the committee's position.", "said": "I take the point that this is a content item, but honestly the content and the weighting are inseparable. If we are expanding the composition strand, then forty per cent on performance is simply indefensible. I would like the committee to signal, today, that we expect that figure to come down.", "task": "Hold the item to its scope without dismissing the substance of her point.", "seconds": 40, "models": [{"text": "A question of scope, Chair. I suspect Margaret is right that the two are linked, and that is exactly why I would be cautious about signalling anything today. Weighting is listed for the assessment meeting, when the officers who model the consequences will be at the table. Could we minute that the committee considers the composition change has implications for weighting, and refer that explicitly to the assessment meeting? That preserves the point without pre-empting it.", "why": "It concedes the link, names the scope problem, and turns her concern into a minuted referral, so she loses nothing and the committee commits to nothing."}, {"text": "Chair, may I ask what we are being invited to do? If it is to note that composition and weighting interact, I think we all agree. If it is to form a view on the forty per cent, I do not think this item, or this group without the assessment officers, is the place for it. I would be grateful for a ruling before we go further.", "why": "It separates the harmless version of the proposal from the improper one and puts the decision squarely with the chair."}], "checks": ["Did I name the category (scope) in the first sentence?", "Did I address the chair rather than the moderator?", "Did I offer a remedy, such as a minuted referral, rather than just a refusal?", "Did I stay under forty seconds and stop?"]}, {"id": "procedural-intervention-s2", "room": "festival association board", "setup": "He is treasurer of the association that runs the state schools' jazz festival. Late in general business, a board member moves that school entry fees rise by fifteen per cent for next year. The association's rules require fee changes to be on notice in the agenda. The proposal may well be sound, but a fee rise passed without notice would be open to challenge by member schools.", "said": "I know this is general business, but we are all here and the numbers are obvious. Costs are up, the venue has gone up, and we keep subsidising entries. I move that entry fees rise by fifteen per cent from next year. I would rather we just decide it than lose another two months.", "task": "Raise a genuine point of order without opposing the fee rise itself.", "seconds": 35, "models": [{"text": "Point of order, Chair. Under our rules a change to fees has to be on notice, and this was not on the agenda. As treasurer I am sympathetic to the substance, and I would be happy to bring a costed paper to the next meeting so the motion can be properly moved. If we pass it tonight, any member school could fairly challenge it.", "why": "It is a true point of order, cites the rule, separates procedure from substance, and offers the treasurer's own work as the remedy."}, {"text": "Chair, before this is seconded: I think fee changes require notice under the rules, and I would ask you to rule on that. If that is right, might the board instead resolve tonight that the treasurer bring a fee proposal on notice next meeting? That gets us most of the way without leaving the decision exposed.", "why": "It asks for a ruling rather than asserting one and offers a resolution the board can pass tonight, so the mover's urgency is honoured."}], "checks": ["Did I cite a rule rather than a preference?", "Did I make clear I was not opposing the fee rise?", "Did I propose a way to proceed tonight?", "Did I leave the ruling to the chair?"]}, {"id": "procedural-intervention-s3", "room": "university academic board", "setup": "A new Bachelor of Music major is listed for final approval. He sits on the board as an external member from the school sector. The version before the board is the third draft; the faculty curriculum committee approved the second, and the third changes the prerequisite structure substantially. The Deputy Vice-Chancellor is keen to approve it so that it can be marketed for next year's intake.", "said": "I appreciate there have been late refinements, but these are improvements and the faculty has been through this in great detail. We are under real time pressure with the prospectus. Unless anyone has a substantive objection, I would propose we approve the major as presented.", "task": "Raise the process gap without appearing to obstruct a proposal the DVC wants.", "seconds": 45, "models": [{"text": "I have no substantive objection, Deputy Vice-Chancellor, and the changes look sensible. My concern is narrower. The faculty committee approved the second draft, and the prerequisite changes in the third are the kind it would normally see. Is there a way to approve today subject to the faculty chair confirming the revised prerequisites out of session? That protects the timetable and the process at once.", "why": "It removes any suggestion of obstruction, names the specific gap, and proposes a conditional approval that keeps the DVC's deadline intact."}, {"text": "Purely on process: could the secretary confirm whether the faculty committee has seen the current prerequisites? If it has, I am content. If it has not, I would prefer we approve in principle and delegate final sign-off to the chair once the faculty has confirmed, rather than have the board approve something its own committee has not.", "why": "It frames the point as a question of fact first, so it can dissolve cleanly, and offers a delegated remedy if it does not."}], "checks": ["Did I say early that I had no objection to the substance?", "Did I identify the exact step that was skipped?", "Did my remedy keep the deadline intact?"]}, {"id": "procedural-intervention-s4", "room": "arts funding panel", "setup": "He sits on a state arts funding panel assessing project grants against four published criteria. Discussion of a contemporary jazz collective's application has turned to the leader's public comments about the funding body itself. Two panellists are using those comments to mark the application down. Assessment outside the published criteria would expose the decision to complaint and is unfair to the applicant.", "said": "I just find it hard to separate the application from the person. He has been openly contemptuous of this agency in the press. Surely capacity to deliver includes some judgement about whether this is someone we can work with. I would mark it down on the third criterion.", "task": "Return the panel to the published criteria without defending the applicant's comments.", "seconds": 45, "models": [{"text": "Could I bring us back to the criteria for a moment? Capacity to deliver, as published, is about track record, resources and the project plan. His public comments may well be unwise, but they are not evidence on any of those. If we let them shape the score, we are assessing something applicants were not told we would assess, and the decision becomes hard to defend.", "why": "It concedes nothing about the comments themselves, reads the criterion back to the room, and names the institutional risk calmly."}, {"text": "A question of scope, Chair. I think the panel's remit is the four published criteria and nothing else. If there is a genuine concern about the working relationship, that seems a matter for the agency's officers, not for scoring. Might we score on the published criteria and note the concern separately for staff?", "why": "It gives the concern a legitimate destination so the panellists are not simply told to drop it."}], "checks": ["Did I avoid defending or criticising the applicant personally?", "Did I state the criterion's actual content?", "Did I offer a proper place for the concern?"]}, {"id": "procedural-intervention-s5", "room": "faculty curriculum committee", "setup": "A university faculty curriculum committee is considering a motion from the head of performance. The motion bundles two things: replacing the first-year aural unit with a broader musicianship unit, and cutting contact hours for that unit from three to two. He supports the first and has serious doubts about the second. If the motion is voted as one, he will have to oppose a change he wants.", "said": "These two things really go together. The new unit is designed to be leaner, and the hours are part of the design. I would rather we take the motion as it stands than unpick it. We have discussed this at length and I think the committee is ready.", "task": "Ask for the motion to be divided, with a reason that does not reopen the whole debate.", "seconds": 35, "models": [{"text": "Chair, might we take the motion in two parts? I strongly support the new musicianship unit and would like to vote for it. I am less persuaded about the reduction in hours, and I suspect I am not alone. Dividing it lets the committee show its real support for the first part without forcing anyone into a single vote on both.", "why": "It leads with support, gives a reason that serves the committee rather than himself, and asks the chair for a standard procedural remedy."}, {"text": "Could I ask that the question be divided? The unit and the contact hours are separable on the face of the motion, and I think the minutes would more accurately reflect the committee's view if each were put separately. If the hours are as integral as Paul says, the committee will carry both.", "why": "It justifies the division on accuracy and quietly invites the mover to trust that his argument will survive a separate vote."}], "checks": ["Did I state which part I support?", "Did I avoid rearguing the hours?", "Did I ask the chair rather than the mover?"]}, {"id": "procedural-intervention-s6", "room": "school council", "setup": "He chairs the school council. During general business, a parent member, an experienced barrister, raises complaints about a particular teacher's marking and asks the council to request a report on that teacher. Staff management is outside the council's functions and belongs to the principal. The principal is present. The parent's underlying concern about consistency of marking is legitimate.", "said": "I think the council has a responsibility here. Several families have raised this with me, and the pattern is consistent. I am not asking for anything disciplinary, simply for the principal to report back to council on this teacher's marking practices so we can be satisfied.", "task": "As chair, rule on scope firmly but courteously, and redirect the legitimate concern.", "seconds": 50, "models": [{"text": "Thank you, Catherine. I am going to rule that a report on an individual teacher is outside the council's functions; staff matters sit with the principal, and I think council needs to be scrupulous about that line. What is within our remit is the school's approach to marking consistency in general. If you would like, we could ask the principal for a short paper on moderation practice across the school for the next meeting.", "why": "It rules clearly as chair, explains the boundary in one sentence, and converts the complaint into an agenda item the council can properly hold."}, {"text": "Catherine, I think we need to separate two things. Concerns about an individual teacher are for the principal, and I would encourage the families to raise them directly, perhaps with your help. The broader question of how marking is moderated is properly ours. I will put that on the next agenda. Lesley, would that be workable from your side?", "why": "It draws the line without the word 'ruling', gives the families a route, and brings the principal in as a partner rather than a defendant."}], "checks": ["Did I decide clearly as chair rather than inviting debate on the boundary?", "Did I avoid any comment on the individual teacher?", "Did I give the legitimate concern a proper destination?", "Did I bring the principal in?"]}]}, {"id": "taking-it-on-notice", "name": "Taking it on notice", "family": "Procedure", "what": "It converts a question you cannot answer well in the moment into a precise commitment, so that the gap in your knowledge becomes evidence of rigour rather than of weakness.", "when": "When a question turns on a fact, figure or source you do not have to hand, or on advice you have no standing to give, and an improvised answer would be wrong, partial or later quoted against you.", "teach": "Learned rooms punish bluffing and reward exactness about the limits of what one knows. Taking a question on notice uses that norm. Australians in public life will recognise the convention from Senate estimates, where officials routinely take questions on notice and the committee expects written answers. The practice works because it has a shape: a clear statement of what will be provided, by when, and through which channel. Without that shape, it is simply evasion with better manners.\n\nThe move has four parts. First, show you have understood the question, ideally by restating its core in a clause: 'the number of schools whose practical marks would move'. This proves the deferral is not a failure of comprehension. Second, give whatever you can responsibly say now, briefly: the principle, the direction, the order of magnitude, or what the answer depends on. Third, make the commitment specific: the deliverable, the date, and the route (to the secretary for circulation, in the next treasurer's report, by email to the questioner). Fourth, stop, and return the floor to the chair.\n\nThe partial answer is where most people undo themselves. Anxious speakers, and fast associative thinkers in particular, begin with a sentence of context and find themselves improvising the full answer they have just said they cannot give. Two sentences at most. If you notice yourself saying 'and also', you have left the move.\n\nThe voice should be level and unhurried, with no apologetic laugh. Look at the questioner while restating their question, then at the chair when committing, so the commitment is made to the meeting and will be minuted. If the chair does not minute it, ask: 'Could the minutes record that?' Then deliver, on time, and refer to it at the next meeting. That last step is what makes the move credible the next time you use it.\n\nDistinguish fact questions from judgement questions. A figure can go on notice; your view usually cannot. If someone asks whether you support a change, taking it on notice looks like ducking. Answer the principle now and take the supporting data on notice.\n\nIt goes wrong in three ways. As a reflex for every hard question, it becomes a reputation for never knowing. Vaguely ('I'll get back to you on that'), it commits to nothing and everyone hears it. And undelivered, it converts one small gap into a lasting doubt about your reliability.", "phrasings": ["That is a fair question, and I would rather give you the right number than a near one. I will take it on notice and circulate it through the secretary by Friday.", "I can tell you the direction now: the figure has risen. The exact variance I would like to confirm against the ledger, and I will include it in the next treasurer's report.", "Let me take the detail on notice. The principle I can answer now: I would not support any change that disadvantages regional candidates.", "I do not want to paraphrase the study loosely in this company. May I send you the reference and the relevant page this afternoon?", "That turns on legal advice we have not sought. I would propose the council take it on notice and that I write to the department this week.", "If I understand you, the question is how many schools would see their practical marks move. I do not have that modelling, but I can ask the assessment officers for it before the next meeting.", "I will take that on notice. Could the minutes record it against my name, with a date of the twelfth?"], "avoid": ["Um, I'm not sure off the top of my head, I'd have to check, I think it might be around... : Thinking aloud in public; it signals unpreparedness and produces a half-number that will be quoted back to you.", "I'll get back to you on that. : No deliverable, no date, no channel; educated listeners hear it as polite evasion.", "I'm terribly sorry, I really should know this, I apologise, I'll find out. : Over-apology turns a routine deferral into a confession and lowers your standing for the rest of the meeting.", "I'd rather not answer that now. : Sounds like concealment; a deferral needs a reason and a commitment, not a refusal."], "scenarios": [{"id": "taking-it-on-notice-s1", "room": "SCSA assessment committee", "setup": "The committee is weighing a proposal to change how the ATAR Music practical examination is scaled against the written component. An assessment officer from a university asks him a precise question about how many schools would be materially affected. He knows the general pattern from his own and neighbouring schools but has no system data. The proposal will go to consultation in a month.", "said": "Dijon, you have been the most supportive of this change, so let me ask you directly. How many schools would actually see a material shift in their candidates' combined marks? If it is a handful, this is a technical adjustment. If it is half the cohort, we are talking about something else entirely.", "task": "Take the figure on notice while keeping his position credible.", "seconds": 45, "models": [{"text": "That is exactly the right question, and I do not have the system figure. From my own school and the three I moderate with, the shift would be small and in both directions, but four schools are not a cohort. I would suggest we ask the officers to model it on last year's data before the consultation draft goes out, and I am happy to frame the request with them this week.", "why": "It admits the gap plainly, offers a properly qualified partial answer, and turns the deferral into a concrete request with an owner."}, {"text": "Let me take that on notice rather than guess. What I can say is that my support rests on the principle, that the practical should not be scaled down for being practical, and I would want to see the numbers before I claimed it was merely technical. Could we minute a request to the officers for that modelling by the next meeting?", "why": "It separates his judgement, which he answers now, from the fact, which he defers, and asks for the commitment to be minuted."}], "checks": ["Did I restate or acknowledge the exact question?", "Did I limit the partial answer to two sentences?", "Did I name who will provide what, by when?", "Did I avoid apologising?"]}, {"id": "taking-it-on-notice-s2", "room": "festival association annual general meeting", "setup": "He is presenting the treasurer's report to member schools at the festival association's AGM. A deputy principal from a member school, a former accountant, notices that sponsorship income is down by a significant amount on last year and asks why. He knows one sponsor did not renew but has not reconciled whether the rest is timing or a real loss. Members are paying close attention to fees.", "said": "Thank you for the report. Can I ask about sponsorship? It is down quite markedly on last year. Is that a lost sponsor, a timing issue, or something more structural? Because if it is structural, it rather changes how we should think about the fee discussion later.", "task": "Answer what he can, take the rest on notice, and protect confidence in the accounts.", "seconds": 50, "models": [{"text": "Part of it I can explain now: one major sponsor did not renew this year, and that accounts for a good share of the gap. Whether the remainder is timing or a genuine fall I want to confirm against the agreements rather than guess. I will circulate a short note to members within a fortnight, and I would suggest the fee discussion proceeds on the assumption that it is structural until I show otherwise.", "why": "It gives the known fact, defers the unknown, sets a date, and handles the link to fees conservatively, which a former accountant will respect."}, {"text": "That is the right distinction to draw. I can confirm one non-renewal; the rest I have not yet reconciled, and I would rather not characterise it until I have. I will take it on notice and report to members by the end of the month. If that leaves the fee item uncertain, the board could reasonably defer the fee decision to its next meeting.", "why": "It credits the questioner's framing, commits to a date, and offers the room a procedural consequence instead of leaving the uncertainty hanging."}], "checks": ["Did I give the known fact before the deferral?", "Did I commit to a date and channel for members?", "Did I address the implication for the fee discussion?"]}, {"id": "taking-it-on-notice-s3", "room": "principal's office", "setup": "He is meeting the principal and the associate principal about next year's staffing and resourcing for the specialist music program. The associate principal, who is rigorous with numbers, asks for the per-student cost of the program compared with the school's other specialist programs. He knows his own program's costs closely but not how the other programs' figures are calculated. A loose comparison could damage his program.", "said": "Before we go further, it would help me to know what the program costs per student, set against the other specialist programs. I am not suggesting it is out of line. But if we are going to argue for more instrumental time, I need to be able to defend the number to the business manager.", "task": "Defer the comparison without seeming to avoid scrutiny.", "seconds": 40, "models": [{"text": "I can give you our own figure with confidence. What I would be wary of is putting it beside the other programs before we know they are counted the same way, because instrumental staffing is often booked differently. Could I work with the business manager on a like-for-like comparison and bring it to you by the end of next week?", "why": "It volunteers his own number, names the methodological risk without sounding defensive, and offers a joint process with a date."}, {"text": "Let me take the comparison on notice. I would rather it be right than quick, and I would rather the business manager agree the method before either of us quotes it. I will have something to you by Friday week, and in the meantime I can send our own costing today.", "why": "It frames the deferral as protecting the associate principal's credibility as much as his own, and gives him something immediately."}], "checks": ["Did I offer what I did know straight away?", "Did I explain the reason for deferring in one sentence?", "Did I name a date?"]}, {"id": "taking-it-on-notice-s4", "room": "university music school advisory committee", "setup": "He sits on a university music school's advisory committee as a school-sector representative. The committee is discussing a proposed first-year theory unit. A professor asks whether the unit's assumed entry knowledge matches what students actually bring from the ATAR Music course. He knows the syllabus intimately but not the latest examiners' report on theory performance.", "said": "You would know better than anyone here. Are we pitching this at what students genuinely arrive with? My impression is that harmonic analysis has been weakening for some years, but impressions are cheap. I would rather design the unit from evidence than from anecdote.", "task": "Give what the syllabus establishes and take the performance evidence on notice.", "seconds": 45, "models": [{"text": "On what the syllabus requires, I can answer now: the harmonic content examinable in each unit is set out explicitly, and it is more demanding than people often assume. On how well students actually perform on it, I would rather not trade impressions either. The examiners' reports comment on that each year. I will pull the relevant sections from the last three and send them to you before the next meeting.", "why": "It separates the documentary fact from the performance evidence and commits to exactly the evidence the professor asked for."}, {"text": "Let me take the performance question on notice. I can say what is assessed; whether students are meeting it is a separate matter, and the examiners' reports are the proper source. I will circulate the relevant extracts through the secretary within a fortnight, and if useful I can ask two colleagues who mark the theory paper for their reading.", "why": "It honours his empirical stance and adds a second, credible source without overpromising."}], "checks": ["Did I separate what I know from what I do not?", "Did I name the source I will use?", "Did I avoid offering my own impression as evidence?"]}, {"id": "taking-it-on-notice-s5", "room": "school council", "setup": "He chairs the school council. A parent member asks whether the school can lawfully require families to pay instrumental hire charges for students in the specialist program, or whether these must be voluntary. He has views but no legal advice, and the question has implications beyond music. The principal is present but has not been asked.", "said": "I am asking this as a genuine question, not a complaint. Several families have asked me whether the instrument hire charge is compulsory or voluntary. I am not sure the school's documents are clear, and I think council should know where we stand before families start refusing to pay.", "task": "As chair, take the legal question on notice properly and avoid giving an informal answer.", "seconds": 45, "models": [{"text": "Thank you, that is a proper question for council, and I do not think any of us should answer it informally. I would propose that we take it on notice, that the principal seek the department's position on the charge as it is currently framed, and that we receive a short written answer at the next meeting. Lesley, is that workable? I will ask the minutes to record it.", "why": "It refuses to improvise a legal view, routes the question to the proper authority, and secures the principal's agreement and a minute."}, {"text": "I am going to take that on notice for council. It turns on the department's rules about charges, and I would rather we have the actual position in writing than our impressions of it. Could I ask the principal to obtain it, and in the meantime could the families be told the question is being considered? We will return to it next meeting.", "why": "It defers the substance but responds to the immediate concern about families, so the deferral does not leave the parent empty-handed."}], "checks": ["Did I avoid stating a legal position myself?", "Did I name who will obtain the answer?", "Did I ensure it was minuted with a timeframe?"]}, {"id": "taking-it-on-notice-s6", "room": "conference panel", "setup": "He is speaking on a panel at a national music education conference about sustaining specialist programs in public schools. In passing he referred to research on the link between ensemble participation and retention. A university researcher in the audience asks for the specific study. He remembers the finding but not the authors or year with certainty.", "said": "Thank you, that was really interesting. You mentioned research linking ensemble participation to retention in the later years. Could you tell us which study that is? I ask because the literature I know is rather more mixed than that, and I would like to look at the design.", "task": "Decline to cite from uncertain memory and commit to providing the source.", "seconds": 35, "models": [{"text": "A fair challenge, and I would rather not cite it from memory in a room that will check. Let me send you the reference this afternoon. You may well be right that the wider literature is mixed; my point was narrower, about our own program, and I should have framed it that way.", "why": "It refuses to bluff, makes a quick commitment, and gracefully narrows the claim without collapsing it."}, {"text": "I will take that on notice: I want to give you the exact paper, not my recollection of it. If you find me afterwards I will take your email. And I take the point about the mixed evidence. I would be interested in what you are reading, because the design question matters.", "why": "It converts a public challenge into a professional exchange and signals respect for the questioner's expertise."}], "checks": ["Did I avoid naming an author or year I was not sure of?", "Did I make a specific commitment?", "Did I adjust the claim honestly rather than defending it?"]}]}, {"id": "declaring-an-interest", "name": "Declaring an interest", "family": "Procedure", "what": "It puts a personal, financial or role-based interest on the record before the item is discussed and proposes how you will participate, so the decision stays clean and your standing on everything else rises.", "when": "At the adoption of the agenda or the opening of the item, whenever a reasonable observer might think your judgement could be affected: money, a close relationship, a second role, or an outcome that benefits you or an organisation you represent.", "teach": "In rooms of senior people, the legitimacy of a decision usually matters more than any single member's view on it. A clean declaration protects the decision, and it also does something for the declarer: it signals that you understand the institution better than your own interest. A declaration made early and economically raises your credibility on every other item. One that is extracted by someone else, or made after you have already spoken, does lasting damage even where the interest was trivial.\n\nSenior practitioners distinguish three kinds. A material personal interest is the familiar one: money, property, employment, a family member's benefit. For incorporated associations in Western Australia, the Associations Incorporation Act 2015 (WA) requires a committee member with a material personal interest in a matter being considered to disclose it, and the association's rules will usually say what follows. A conflict of duty arises when you wear two hats, for example as an association officer and as the head of a department whose students compete under that association. A perceived interest is one a fair-minded observer might suspect even where none exists in fact. All three are declared; the remedy differs.\n\nThe declaration itself has three parts: the interest, stated precisely and without decoration ('I have performed regularly with one of the nominated adjudicators'); a proposal for how you will participate (remain and not vote, leave for deliberation, or remain at the chair's discretion); and a handing back to the chair, who decides. Ask for it to be minuted. Do not add an assurance that it will not affect you. The disclosure exists precisely because no one, including you, can be the judge of that.\n\nStepping back gracefully is physical. If you are leaving, gather your papers and go without commentary. If you are staying but not voting, sit back, put the pen down, and keep silent unless the chair asks you a question of fact. If you are asked for information, give it in the form of facts, then say, 'I will leave the deliberation to colleagues.' If you chair, hand the chair to the deputy for the item, and move seats if the room allows it.\n\nIt goes wrong in three ways. Over-declaring every acquaintance clutters the minutes, reads as anxiety, and can be used to exclude you from items where your expertise is needed. Declaring and then advocating, from the floor or by facial expression, makes the declaration worse than useless. And a speech about your integrity invites exactly the scepticism it is meant to forestall.", "phrasings": ["Before we take this item, Chair, I should declare an interest: one of the nominated adjudicators is a regular colleague of mine in performance. I would propose to leave the room for the discussion.", "I need to declare a conflict of duty on this item. My own school is a likely applicant, so I will not take part in the deliberation or the vote. Could that be minuted?", "For the record, I co-wrote one of the resources on the shortlist. I am happy to answer factual questions about it if that helps, and then to step out.", "I should note that I know this applicant well; she was a student of mine some years ago. I leave it to you, Chair, whether I should remain for this one.", "I will hand the chair to Rachel for this item, since it concerns my own department's program, and I will speak, if at all, only to matters of fact.", "I have a perceived interest rather than an actual one, but I would rather it be on the record. I propose to remain, take no part, and abstain.", "Thank you. I will leave the deliberation to colleagues."], "avoid": ["I suppose I should probably mention that I sort of know one of these people, but it honestly won't affect me at all. : Hedged and self-exonerating; the assurance is exactly what a declaration cannot give.", "In the interests of complete transparency, and because integrity matters a great deal to me, I want to make a full and frank disclosure... : A speech about one's own virtue invites suspicion and wastes the room's time.", "I'll declare an interest, but I do want to say a few words in support before I step out. : Declaring and then advocating is worse than not declaring; it shows you knew and spoke anyway.", "I should declare that I have met everyone on this list at some conference or other. : Over-declaring trivialises the mechanism and can be used to sideline you from items where your expertise is needed."], "scenarios": [{"id": "declaring-an-interest-s1", "room": "festival association board", "setup": "The festival association board is appointing adjudicators for next year's festival. He is treasurer. One of the three recommended jazz adjudicators is a pianist he performs with regularly and has done for years. He believes she is the best candidate. His own school's ensembles will be adjudicated.", "said": "The subcommittee has recommended three names for the jazz panel, and I think they are strong. Unless there is discussion, I would like to move that we appoint all three. Dijon, you know the scene better than any of us. Do you have a view on the pianist?", "task": "Declare the interest before giving any view, and step back from the decision.", "seconds": 40, "models": [{"text": "Before I say anything, I should declare two interests. I perform regularly with her, and my own school's bands will be adjudicated at the festival. I do not think I should offer a view on her appointment, so I propose to leave the room for this item and take no part in the vote. Could that be minuted, please?", "why": "It declares both the relationship and the conflict of duty, refuses the invitation to comment, and secures the minute."}, {"text": "Thank you, but I need to declare an interest first. She and I play together regularly, and my school competes. I can confirm factual matters, such as her adjudicating experience elsewhere, if the board wants them, and then I will step out. I would rather the board's view not be coloured by mine.", "why": "It offers usefulness strictly confined to facts, makes the exit clear, and frames it as protecting the board."}], "checks": ["Did I declare before saying anything about her merits?", "Did I name both the relationship and my school's involvement?", "Did I propose a specific form of withdrawal?", "Did I ask for the minute?"]}, {"id": "declaring-an-interest-s2", "room": "SCSA syllabus committee", "setup": "The committee is selecting recommended listening and analysis resources to accompany the revised syllabus. One of the five shortlisted packages is a resource set he co-authored with colleagues, which has been used widely across the state. The other committee members know he is a co-author but nobody has raised it. The selection will be published under SCSA's name.", "said": "We have five packages. Frankly I think two of them are clearly stronger than the others, and one of those is the set many of us already use in schools. I would welcome views. It would be good to get this to two names before the break.", "task": "Declare his authorship and decide how far to participate.", "seconds": 40, "models": [{"text": "Chair, I should declare a direct interest: I am a co-author of one of the shortlisted packages. I think that means I should take no part in comparing them. I am happy to answer factual questions about how the set was built, if the committee wants that, but I propose to step out for the deliberation itself.", "why": "It names the interest plainly, accepts the consequence, and offers factual help in a bounded way."}, {"text": "Before views are taken, I need to put on the record that I co-wrote one of these. Given it will be published under the Authority's name, I think the cleanest course is for me to leave the room for this item. I will not comment on the others either, because any comparison from me would be compromised.", "why": "It reasons from the publication stakes and recognises that commenting on rival packages is just as compromised as praising his own."}], "checks": ["Did I declare even though everyone already knew?", "Did I refrain from commenting on any of the five?", "Did I state the remedy rather than leave it hanging?"]}, {"id": "declaring-an-interest-s3", "room": "arts funding panel", "setup": "He sits on a state arts funding panel for music projects. The next application is from a young bandleader he taught at school eight years ago and has since hired twice for gigs. He has no financial stake. The panel is small and his jazz expertise is the main reason he was appointed.", "said": "Next is the quintet recording project. I will be honest, I am not sure how to assess the jazz side of this, so I was hoping Dijon would lead us through it. It looks ambitious, and the budget is tight.", "task": "Declare a moderate interest proportionately, and let the chair decide on participation.", "seconds": 45, "models": [{"text": "I should declare an interest before I lead anything. I taught the bandleader at school and have hired him twice since. There is no financial connection, but a reasonable observer might see a relationship. I am in your hands, Chair. I can offer general comments on what a project like this usually costs and requires, without scoring it, or I can step out entirely.", "why": "It discloses proportionately, gives the chair two real options, and preserves the panel's access to his general expertise."}, {"text": "Before I do, I have a relationship to declare: he was my student and I have since employed him on gigs. I think I should not score this one. If it would help, I can describe the general standards the panel might apply to a jazz recording project, and leave the assessment of this application to colleagues.", "why": "It proposes its own remedy and offers help at the level of criteria rather than the applicant."}], "checks": ["Did I state the relationship precisely without minimising it?", "Did I separate general expertise from this application?", "Did I leave the final call to the chair?"]}, {"id": "declaring-an-interest-s4", "room": "school council", "setup": "He chairs the school council. The next item is the principal's proposal to reallocate a portion of the specialist program budget across the arts programs, including the music program he leads. Council's role is to endorse the budget framework. Several members will look to him for a view.", "said": "That brings us to the arts program allocation. Lesley's paper is in the pack. I imagine you will want to lead this one, Dijon, given it is your area. Should we just go straight to questions?", "task": "Hand the chair to the deputy and limit his own role to facts.", "seconds": 40, "models": [{"text": "Thank you, but I think I should step out of the chair for this item. It directly concerns the program I lead, and I have an obvious interest in its outcome. Rachel, would you take the chair? I will remain only to answer questions of fact if members have them, and I will abstain on any endorsement. Could the minutes record that?", "why": "It hands the chair over cleanly, defines exactly how far he will participate, and asks for the minute."}, {"text": "I need to declare an interest here, as head of one of the affected programs, so I will hand over to the deputy chair for this item. If council wants to know how the music figures were built, I can explain the arithmetic. I will not offer a view on the allocation itself.", "why": "It draws a clear line between arithmetic and advocacy, which is the line that matters."}], "checks": ["Did I hand over the chair rather than chairing my own item?", "Did I define the limit of my participation?", "Did I abstain from any endorsement?"]}, {"id": "declaring-an-interest-s5", "room": "university academic board", "setup": "He sits on a university academic board as an external member. A proposal is tabled for an early-entry pathway that would give guaranteed interviews to students from a small number of schools with specialist music programs, his own school among them. The pathway would help his students. Other schools in the state are not included.", "said": "This pathway is modest in scale and the faculty has worked hard on it. It recognises the quality of the specialist programs. Dijon, you are closer to those programs than anyone here. I would be glad to hear whether it would work on the ground.", "task": "Declare his school's benefit and contribute only in a way that does not compromise the board's decision.", "seconds": 50, "models": [{"text": "I should declare an interest first: my school is one of the named schools, and my students would benefit directly. So I do not think I should comment on whether it is a good idea. I can say, as a matter of fact, how specialist programs in the state are structured, if that helps the board judge the selection of schools. Beyond that I will abstain.", "why": "It declares the benefit, refuses the evaluative question, and offers only the factual context that bears on fairness."}, {"text": "Thank you, but I need to declare that my own school would benefit, so my view on whether it works would not be disinterested. If the board wants information, I would suggest the faculty consult principals from schools both inside and outside the proposed list. I will leave the decision to colleagues and abstain.", "why": "It goes further than self-exclusion and suggests a fairer evidential process, which shows institutional judgement."}], "checks": ["Did I declare before answering the question put to me?", "Did I avoid evaluating the proposal?", "Did I abstain explicitly?"]}, {"id": "declaring-an-interest-s6", "room": "school selection panel", "setup": "He is on a three-person panel to appoint a new instrumental teacher, with a deputy principal and a human resources officer. Reading the shortlist, he sees that one candidate is a bassist he has performed with for years and considers a friend. Interviews start tomorrow. Panel procedures require disclosure of any close relationship with a candidate.", "said": "So we have five on the shortlist. I suggest we interview all five and score independently before we compare. Before we lock the schedule, is there anything anyone needs to raise about any of the candidates?", "task": "Disclose the friendship and accept the procedural consequence without fuss.", "seconds": 35, "models": [{"text": "Yes, I need to declare a close relationship. One of the candidates is a long-standing performing colleague and a friend. I think the procedure requires that to be noted, and I suspect it means I should not sit on that interview, or perhaps on the panel at all. I would rather you and HR decide which, and I will accept either.", "why": "It discloses fully, names the consequence honestly, and leaves the decision with the people properly placed to make it."}, {"text": "I do. One of the five is a close friend I have performed with for many years. I would ask that it be recorded, and I would suggest a replacement panellist for the whole process, so that no candidate can argue the scoring was uneven. I can recommend someone with the right instrumental background if that helps.", "why": "It takes the more protective option and offers to solve the problem it has created."}], "checks": ["Did I describe the relationship as 'close' rather than minimise it?", "Did I propose a remedy?", "Did I avoid any comment on the candidate's suitability?"]}]}, {"id": "handling-the-dominant-member", "name": "Handling the dominant member", "family": "Procedure", "what": "It redistributes airtime away from a member who is monopolising the discussion, without contesting their status or turning the room's attention into a contest.", "when": "When one person has spoken repeatedly, others have visibly withdrawn, and the discussion has narrowed to that person's frame; or when quieter members with relevant expertise have not yet been heard.", "teach": "Most dominant members are not trying to silence anyone. They repeat because they do not feel registered, or because nobody has given the room a structure that makes them stop. The move therefore has two components: an accurate acknowledgement that removes the motive for repetition, and a structural change that redistributes the floor. Educated rooms close ranks quickly against public correction of a colleague, so status must be preserved; the aim is to make the dominant member's point part of the record and to make it natural for others to speak.\n\nAs chair, you have structural tools. Summarise the member's point, accurately and generously, in one sentence, then say that you will now hear from others. Go round the table. Name a specific person whose expertise is relevant: 'Anna, you have run this assessment in a regional school; how does it look from there?' Set a rule that applies to everyone ('Let us hear from those who have not yet spoken'). If the member interjects, a flat palm and 'I will come back to you, Robert' is usually enough. The first intervention should be light; the second, if needed, is firmer and shorter.\n\nAs a member, you have fewer tools and must not usurp the chair. The usual approach is a bridge: take the dominant point in a clause, then turn it into a question addressed to someone else. 'Robert's point about workload seems central. I would be interested to know whether the regional members see it the same way.' Alternatively, address the chair: 'Chair, I would value hearing from colleagues who have not spoken yet.' If the dominant member is the chair, the bridge is the only safe tool, and it should be framed as a request for information, never as a complaint about process.\n\nWait for a breath or the end of a sentence before you come in. Interrupting mid-clause turns the move into a contest. The voice is warm and decisive, slightly quicker than usual, and uses the member's name. An open hand turned towards the next speaker does a great deal of the work.\n\nIt goes wrong in four ways. Visible irritation or a sardonic summary shames the member and the room sympathises with them. Summaries that are too long become condescending. Fighting fire with fire, talking over the member, makes you the second dominant voice. And waiting too long means frustration leaks into your tone, which is far harder to recover from than an early, light redirection.", "phrasings": ["Thank you, Robert; I think the point is clear, and it is on the record. I would like to hear now from those who have not yet spoken.", "Let me go round the table on this, starting with Anna, and come back to you at the end, Robert.", "Professor Hale's concern is that the change compromises rigour. I would be interested to know whether the teachers in the room see the same risk in their classrooms.", "Chair, I would value hearing from the regional members before we go further; they are the ones who will carry this.", "I want to make sure I have your point exactly, so that we do not lose it: you think the timeline is unrealistic. Now, Sam, you have done this before. Is it?", "I am going to hold you there, if I may. I will come back to you.", "Let us hear three views we have not had yet, briefly, and then I will bring us back to the motion."], "avoid": ["Can someone else please get a word in? : Shames the member publicly and expresses frustration rather than offering a structure; the room will side with the person rebuked.", "With respect, Robert, you've made that point several times now. : Accurate but hostile; it forces a status contest and will often prompt a defensive repetition.", "Sorry, sorry, I just, can I just jump in here quickly? : Apologetic and scrambling; it sounds like you are competing for airtime rather than restructuring the conversation.", "I'm sure we'd all agree that some voices have been rather dominant this morning. : Passive aggressive and vague; everyone knows who you mean and nobody respects the indirection."], "scenarios": [{"id": "handling-the-dominant-member-s1", "room": "school council", "setup": "He chairs the school council. The item is a proposed revision to the uniform policy. A parent member, a senior lawyer, has spoken five times, each time returning to her concern about enforcement and student rights. The two student representatives and a staff member have not spoken. The item has twenty minutes left.", "said": "I am sorry to keep returning to this, but I really do think we are underestimating the enforcement problem. If the policy says students must wear the blazer in Terms 2 and 3, then what happens when a student refuses? Detention? Exclusion from class? We need to know, because otherwise the policy is unenforceable.", "task": "As chair, register her point accurately and open the floor to the student representatives and staff.", "seconds": 40, "models": [{"text": "Thank you, Helen. I think the point is now well established: the policy needs a clear and proportionate consequence, or it will not hold. I will make sure it is minuted as a matter for the drafting. I would like to hear now from the student representatives, because they are the ones who will live with it. Jess, Sam, how does it look from where you sit?", "why": "It summarises generously, assures her the point will survive in the record, and opens the floor to specific named members."}, {"text": "Helen, I want to capture that properly, so let me put it as a question for the drafters: what is the consequence of non-compliance, and is it proportionate? I will hold you there for now and go to people who have not spoken. Mark, from a staff point of view, what is enforcement like at the moment?", "why": "It turns her repetition into a concrete drafting question and uses a light, firm hold before moving to a staff voice."}], "checks": ["Did my summary of her point satisfy her accuracy test?", "Did I name specific people to speak next?", "Did I avoid any reference to how often she had spoken?"]}, {"id": "handling-the-dominant-member-s2", "room": "SCSA syllabus committee", "setup": "He is a member, not the chair, of an SCSA syllabus committee reviewing the composition component. A professor of composition has dominated the meeting with a strong argument for raising technical requirements. Two teachers from regional schools have said almost nothing, though their schools would be most affected. The chair is reluctant to intervene with a senior academic.", "said": "I have heard the concern about accessibility, but frankly we cannot design a syllabus around the weakest provision. If the composition component does not demand serious technical command of harmony and orchestration, we will produce students who cannot do the first year of a degree. I would want any revision to raise the bar, not lower it.", "task": "As a member, bridge from the professor's point to the regional teachers without challenging the chair.", "seconds": 45, "models": [{"text": "Professor Hale's point about first-year readiness seems central to me, and I would not want to lose it. But I am conscious that the regional schools will feel any rise in the bar most sharply. Chair, would it help to hear from Anna and Tom on what their students would need in order to meet it?", "why": "It honours the professor's argument, then addresses the chair rather than taking over the floor, and names specific colleagues."}, {"text": "Could I put a question to the regional members, building on that? If the requirement rose as the professor suggests, what support would your students need to meet it, and is that support realistic in a school without a specialist teacher? I think the answer may tell us how to raise the bar rather than whether to.", "why": "It converts the professor's position into a design question that only the quieter members can answer."}], "checks": ["Did I affirm the professor's point before turning away from it?", "Did I avoid presuming the chair's role?", "Did I name the people I wanted to hear from?"]}, {"id": "handling-the-dominant-member-s3", "room": "festival association board", "setup": "He is treasurer on the festival association board. The president is a charismatic, long-standing figure who has spoken over each attempt to discuss the budget for next year's festival, steering repeatedly back to his preferred plan for an expanded gala concert. Two newer board members have tried to raise venue costs and been talked over. He needs the board to look at the numbers.", "said": "Look, the gala is what people remember. Every year we argue about the money, and every year it works out. If we cut the gala we lose the profile, and if we lose the profile we lose the sponsors. I really think we should lock in the expanded format tonight and sort out the detail later.", "task": "Redirect the president to the numbers and bring the newer members back in.", "seconds": 45, "models": [{"text": "I agree the gala carries the profile, Tony, and I do not want to lose it either. Before we lock anything in, though, I think the board should see what the expanded format does to the bottom line. Priya and James raised venue costs earlier, and I do not think we heard them out. Could we hear that now, and then I can put the gala figures alongside it?", "why": "It concedes the president's value point, uses the treasurer's natural authority over numbers, and returns the floor to the members who were talked over."}, {"text": "Tony, could I suggest an order? Let us take ten minutes on the costs, starting with Priya's question about the venue, and then come back to the gala with the figures in front of us. I suspect the expanded format may well survive the scrutiny, and if so we will lock it in with confidence.", "why": "It proposes a sequence rather than opposing the plan, and gives the president a reason to agree."}], "checks": ["Did I affirm what the president values?", "Did I bring the newer members back by name?", "Did I propose a sequence rather than a confrontation?"]}, {"id": "handling-the-dominant-member-s4", "room": "department meeting", "setup": "He leads the music department meeting as Teacher in Charge. A senior colleague with many years in the school has spoken repeatedly about the proposed change to the concert calendar, each time with growing frustration. Three early-career teachers have said nothing. The change affects all of them.", "said": "I just think we are making a mistake moving the winter concert. We did this in 2019 and it was a disaster. Nobody listened then either. I have said it, I will keep saying it, and I would like it noted that I think this is the wrong decision.", "task": "Note the colleague's objection fully and draw out the early-career teachers.", "seconds": 40, "models": [{"text": "It is noted, Graham, and I mean that: I will put it in the minutes as your view that the move repeats the 2019 problem. I would like to hear from everyone before we settle anything. Hannah, Leo, Priya, you will each be running ensembles on that night. What does the change mean for you?", "why": "It gives him the formal registration he is asking for, which removes the motive to keep repeating, and then names each quiet colleague."}, {"text": "Thank you, Graham. Could you tell us in a sentence what went wrong in 2019, so the others have it? Then I would like to go round the table. I want to make sure the people who were not here in 2019 have a chance to weigh in.", "why": "It converts the colleague's grievance into useful institutional memory and then makes the round-table explicit."}], "checks": ["Did I give the colleague a formal record of his objection?", "Did I name each quiet colleague?", "Did I keep my tone warm despite his frustration?"]}, {"id": "handling-the-dominant-member-s5", "room": "faculty curriculum committee", "setup": "He sits as an external member on a university faculty curriculum committee. The chair is the dominant member: she has spoken at length on each item, framed every question, and moved through items with little discussion. A lecturer who designed the unit under discussion has not been asked to speak. He cannot use chairing tools.", "said": "So I think we are broadly agreed. The unit is sound, though I would want the assessment pattern tightened, and I think the reading list is too long. I will ask the school to revise along those lines. Unless anyone objects strongly, we can move to item six.", "task": "As a member, open the floor to the unit's designer without challenging the chair's authority.", "seconds": 35, "models": [{"text": "Before we move on, Chair, could we hear briefly from Dr Okafor? It would help me to know how she sees the assessment pattern and the reading list before the revisions are framed, since she will be making them, and I suspect the committee would value her rationale on the record.", "why": "It is framed as a request for information, not a complaint about process, and gives the chair a reason to agree."}, {"text": "I agree with tightening the assessment, Chair. I would be grateful to hear what Dr Okafor intended with the current pattern, though; there may be a reason it looks the way it does, and it would help the revision to have that on record.", "why": "It agrees with the chair first, then makes the designer's contribution useful to the chair's own outcome."}], "checks": ["Did I frame it as a request, not a criticism?", "Did I agree with something the chair said?", "Did I name the person I wanted to hear?"]}, {"id": "handling-the-dominant-member-s6", "room": "conference panel", "setup": "He is one of four panellists at a national music education conference on equity in instrumental programs. One panellist, a well-known commentator, has taken most of the time, answering questions directed to others. The moderator has not intervened. Twelve minutes remain, and a question from the floor has just been addressed to the youngest panellist.", "said": "If I could just come in on that, because I think it goes to the heart of what I was saying earlier. The real issue is not access, it is aspiration. Until we change the culture around what music education is for, we are just tinkering with logistics. And I would say that to any minister.", "task": "As a fellow panellist, return the question to the panellist it was addressed to without rebuking the commentator.", "seconds": 30, "models": [{"text": "That is a provocative distinction, and I suspect it deserves its own session. But the question was for Mei, and I would love to hear her answer, because she is the one running a program in a school where access is the daily problem.", "why": "It compliments the idea, returns the question to its addressee, and gives the room a reason to want to hear her."}, {"text": "I would like to come back to the aspiration point, because I am not sure I agree with it. But first, Mei, the question was yours, and you are the one running a program where access is the daily reality. I suspect your answer will test that distinction rather well.", "why": "It is short, honours the commentator's frame, and turns Mei's answer into the test of it, which raises her status."}], "checks": ["Did I return the question to its addressee by name?", "Did I avoid any criticism of the commentator?", "Did I keep it under thirty seconds?"]}]}, {"id": "the-chairs-summation", "name": "The chair's summation", "family": "Closing", "what": "It states the sense of the meeting, the residual disagreement and the decision in a form the room can correct, accept and minute.", "when": "When the arguments have been made and are beginning to repeat, when agreement exists but nobody has said so, or when the chair is about to put a question and the room needs to know exactly what it is agreeing to.", "teach": "Articulate rooms produce a great deal of qualified agreement. Each speaker adds a nuance, a reservation, a condition, and nobody wants to be the one who closes, because closing feels like claiming authority over clever colleagues. The summation does that work on the room's behalf. It turns a conversation into a position, and by offering the room a chance to correct it, it secures consent without a vote. Taking the sense of the meeting in this way is ordinary Australian practice for matters that do not require a formal resolution; where a resolution is required, the summation tells the room exactly what the motion should say.\n\nThe structure has three parts. First, the agreement: what the room has converged on, stated more cleanly than anyone stated it. Second, the live disagreement, stated fairly and in its strongest form, attributed to positions rather than people, or to people generously. Third, the decision or next step, with whatever conditions the room attached. Signpost it ('Let me try to state where we are'), deliver it in under a minute, and then ask a tight checking question: 'Is that a fair account?' or 'Is there anything I have misstated?' Pause for a full three seconds. Then direct the minute-taker.\n\nThe minority position is the key to a durable summation. If you leave it out, those who held it will reopen the matter, often under general business or at the next meeting. If you state it well, they can accept the decision because their view is on the record. The experienced chair states the minority view so well that its holders nod.\n\nSlow down and lower the voice. Let the intonation fall at the end of each sentence; rising intonation turns the summation into a series of questions. Sit back slightly and look around the table, not at the paper. A short pause between the three parts helps the room hear the structure.\n\nA member can offer a summation when the chair is struggling: 'Chair, may I try a summary? Correct me if I have it wrong.' This is only safe when you are seen as fair and when you hand the result straight back to the chair.\n\nIt goes wrong in four ways. Summarising your own view as the sense of the meeting is capture, and clever people spot it at once. A long summation re-argues the case. Omitting the minority invites reopening. And closing with 'Is everyone happy?' invites every residual reservation to resurface; ask whether the account is accurate, not whether people are happy.", "phrasings": ["Let me try to state where we are. There is agreement that the aural component should stay. There is a real difference about its format. And I think the decision is to ask the working group for two options by the next meeting. Is that a fair account?", "I think the sense of the meeting is clear, and I will put it in a sentence for the minutes. Correct me if I misstate it.", "We are agreed on the principle and not yet on the timing. I would put the timing view of the minority this way: that a year's delay costs less than an unprepared start.", "Three things, I think. We support the proposal. We want the costings tested. And we want it back in March. Have I missed anything?", "I do not think we need a vote; the room is clearly with the recommendation. Rachel's reservation about workload should be minuted, and the officers asked to address it.", "Chair, may I try a summary? Correct me if I have it wrong, and then I will hand it back to you.", "If that is a fair account, I will ask the minutes to record the decision in those terms and we will move on."], "avoid": ["So basically I think we're all agreeing with what I said at the start. : Capture disguised as summary; it claims the room's view as your own and invites immediate correction.", "Is everyone happy with that? : Invites every residual reservation to resurface; ask whether the account is accurate, not whether people are happy.", "Right, well, I think there's sort of a range of views, and some people think one thing and some think another, so maybe we'll come back to it. : A summation without a decision; it records nothing and guarantees the debate will return.", "In summary, and I'll be brief, the first point was, and this is important, that... : Signposting brevity and then not delivering it; the room hears the gap and loses patience."], "scenarios": [{"id": "the-chairs-summation-s1", "room": "school council", "setup": "He chairs the school council. For forty minutes the council has discussed the principal's proposal to bring forward the specialist program's audition timeline by a term. Members agree the earlier timeline would help families, but two members worry it disadvantages students from primary schools with less music provision. The principal needs a council view to proceed.", "said": "I think I have said what I wanted to. I do support the earlier date. I simply want us to be sure that the primary schools with no music specialist are not left behind. If that can be addressed, I have no objection.", "task": "As chair, state the sense of the meeting including the condition, and land a minutable decision.", "seconds": 60, "models": [{"text": "Let me try to state where we are. Council supports bringing the audition timeline forward a term. There is a clear and shared concern, which Kate has just put well, that students from primary schools without a music specialist must not be disadvantaged. So I would propose council endorse the change, on the condition that the principal reports next meeting on how those students will be reached. Is that a fair account?", "why": "It states the agreement, credits the reservation, converts it into a condition, and closes on a checking question about accuracy."}, {"text": "I think the sense of the meeting is this. We support the earlier date in principle. We want it to come with a deliberate outreach to schools with limited music provision. And we want to see that outreach before the date is finalised. If no one thinks I have misstated it, I will ask the minutes to record council's endorsement on that condition.", "why": "It uses a three-part structure and makes the condition a precondition, which may better reassure the members who raised it."}], "checks": ["Did I name the minority concern fairly?", "Did I convert it into a specific condition?", "Did I ask whether the account was accurate rather than whether people were happy?", "Did I direct the minute-taker?"]}, {"id": "the-chairs-summation-s2", "room": "festival association board", "setup": "He is treasurer, not chair, of the festival association board. The board has discussed next year's budget for an hour. Most agree to hold fees and draw on reserves for one year; two members want a modest fee rise instead. The president, who chairs, turns to him to sum up as treasurer.", "said": "Dijon, you have been following the numbers more closely than any of us. Can you give us a summary of where we have got to, and what you think the board should resolve? I would like to get this done before nine.", "task": "Summarise as a member at the chair's invitation and hand the decision back to the chair.", "seconds": 60, "models": [{"text": "Happy to. The board's clear majority is to hold fees for next year and meet the shortfall from reserves, on the understanding that this is a one-year measure. The alternative view, that a modest rise now is kinder than a larger one later, has real force and should be minuted. On the numbers, reserves can absorb one year comfortably, not two. So I think the resolution is to hold fees, draw on reserves, and review fees by June. Over to you, Tony.", "why": "It states both views fairly, adds the treasurer's one essential fact, frames the resolution, and returns the decision to the chair."}, {"text": "I think we are agreed on the objective, which is affordability for member schools, and divided on the method. Most of us favour holding fees this year and using reserves; Priya and James favour a small rise now. Either is defensible. What reserves will not bear is holding fees two years running. If the chair agrees, I would suggest a resolution to hold fees, with a fee review on the June agenda.", "why": "It frames the disagreement as one of method rather than values, which lowers the temperature, and builds the minority's concern into the resolution."}], "checks": ["Did I represent the minority without diminishing it?", "Did I add only the fact the board needed from me?", "Did I hand the decision back to the chair?"]}, {"id": "the-chairs-summation-s3", "room": "SCSA working group", "setup": "He chairs an SCSA working group reviewing aural assessment in senior music. After two hours, the group agrees aural skills must remain examined. It is divided between those who want an extended, analysis-based aural paper and those who want shorter, skills-based items. The group must report to the full committee in a month.", "said": "I think we have probably exhausted this. I remain of the view that the shorter items are fairer to students from schools with less aural teaching. But I can see the force of the analytical approach. I just do not want our report to pretend we agreed when we did not.", "task": "State the agreement, represent the division honestly, and decide how it goes to the committee.", "seconds": 70, "models": [{"text": "Let me try to state where we are, and Ben, I will take your point about honesty seriously. We agree that aural skills must remain examined. We are divided on format: one view favours extended analytical questions as a better test of musicianship; the other favours shorter skills items as fairer to students with less aural teaching. Rather than force a choice, I propose we report both options to the committee with their trade-offs, and say plainly that the group was divided. Is that a fair account?", "why": "It honours the member's integrity point by building it into the decision, and turns an unresolved split into a usable report."}, {"text": "I think the sense of the group is this. Agreement on retention, real division on format, and good reasons on both sides. I would rather report that division accurately than paper over it. So the decision is to put two options to the committee, each with its rationale and its risks, and to record that we did not reach a single view. I will draft it and circulate it to you all before it goes.", "why": "It is shorter, states the division as a strength of the report, and assigns the drafting to himself with a circulation step."}], "checks": ["Did I state the division without favouring my own view?", "Did I make the honest reporting part of the decision?", "Did I state the next step and who owns it?"]}, {"id": "the-chairs-summation-s4", "room": "department meeting", "setup": "He leads a music department meeting about a clash between the Term 3 jazz night and a year-group camp that removes thirty of his students. Staff have proposed three solutions: moving the jazz night, running it with reduced ensembles, or asking the year coordinator to move the camp. Two staff strongly prefer moving the night. Time is short.", "said": "I just want to say I really do not think we can ask the year coordinator to move the camp. It has been booked for months and they will say no. I would rather we take control of our own date. But I will go with whatever we decide.", "task": "Summarise the three options, register the strong preference, and state the decision.", "seconds": 50, "models": [{"text": "Let me pull this together. We have three options and a clear preference in the room for moving our own date rather than relying on the camp moving. I agree that is the safer course. So the decision is that we move the jazz night to the first Thursday of Term 4, subject to the hall being free. I will check the hall tomorrow and confirm by email. Does that capture it?", "why": "It is brisk, names the preference and his own agreement openly, and closes with a specific action and owner."}, {"text": "Here is where I think we are. Nobody wants to lose thirty players. Asking the camp to move is unlikely to work, as Ellie says, and reduced ensembles would short-change the students. That leaves moving the night, which most of us favour. Unless anyone thinks I have that wrong, that is the decision, and I will confirm a new date by Friday.", "why": "It eliminates options in sequence so the decision feels reasoned rather than imposed, then closes with a date."}], "checks": ["Did I deal with all three options?", "Did I state a single decision?", "Did I attach an owner and a date?"]}, {"id": "the-chairs-summation-s5", "room": "arts funding panel", "setup": "He convenes a state arts funding panel ranking twelve music applications. After the morning, the panel has converged on rankings for eleven. One application, an ambitious multi-venue tour, has split the panel: two think it outstanding, two think it overreached on budget. The panel must submit a ranked list by the end of the day.", "said": "I do not think we are going to persuade each other on the tour. I still think it is the most exciting thing we have seen. But I can see that if the budget is wrong, it fails badly. I would rather we put something forward than argue for another hour.", "task": "As convenor, state the consensus, frame the split fairly, and propose a way to finalise the ranking.", "seconds": 60, "models": [{"text": "Let me state where we are. We have agreed rankings for eleven applications, and I will read them back in a moment. On the tour, the panel is evenly divided: two of us see an outstanding project; two of us see a budget that may not hold. Both views are well grounded. I propose we rank it seventh, within the fundable range, and recommend funding conditional on a revised budget. Is that a fair account?", "why": "It records the consensus, states the split evenly, and offers a ranking that reflects the division and protects the agency."}, {"text": "I think we agree on eleven, and on the tour we agree on the ambition and disagree on the risk. Rather than argue further, I suggest our report says exactly that: a project of high artistic merit with a budget the panel was not confident in. We can rank it at the margin and leave the conditional decision to the agency. Have I put both views fairly?", "why": "It uses the report itself to carry the disagreement, which lets both camps sign off honestly."}], "checks": ["Did I record the eleven agreed rankings before the split?", "Did I state both sides of the split evenly?", "Did I propose a concrete way to finalise?"]}, {"id": "the-chairs-summation-s6", "room": "university academic board subcommittee", "setup": "He is an external member of a university academic board subcommittee on admissions. The chair has lost the thread after a long debate about portfolio-based entry for music. Several members agreed on a pilot but differed on the number of places and whether ATAR should still be required. The chair looks at the clock and hesitates.", "said": "Well, I think we have had a very rich discussion. I am not entirely sure where that leaves us. Perhaps we should take it offline and come back to it next time?", "task": "As a member, offer a summation that rescues the decision, then hand it straight back to the chair.", "seconds": 50, "models": [{"text": "Chair, may I try a summary before we defer? Correct me if I have it wrong. I think we agree on a pilot of portfolio entry. We differ on two details: the number of places and whether an ATAR threshold remains. Those could be settled by the faculty proposing options for next meeting. So the decision today might be to approve the pilot in principle and refer the two details. I will leave it with you.", "why": "It asks permission, compresses the debate into one agreement and two details, proposes a decision, and returns authority to the chair."}, {"text": "Before we take it offline, Chair, I wonder whether we have more agreement than it feels. I heard support for a pilot from almost everyone. The open questions are the size and the ATAR requirement. If the subcommittee approved the pilot in principle today and asked the faculty to bring options on those two, we would not lose the ground we have made.", "why": "It reframes the chair's sense of confusion as hidden progress and offers a way to bank it."}], "checks": ["Did I ask the chair's permission before summarising?", "Did I separate the agreement from the details?", "Did I hand the decision back explicitly?"]}]}, {"id": "landing-the-decision", "name": "Landing the decision", "family": "Closing", "what": "It moves a drifting room from exploration to a resolution with a specific action, a named owner and a date.", "when": "When the arguments have begun to repeat, when the item has used most of its time, or when the room is circling a decision it is capable of making but nobody is making it.", "teach": "Educated rooms drift for good reasons. Exploration is pleasurable, every speaker can see one more consideration, and deciding creates exposure: a decision can be wrong and attributed. Deferral can pass for rigour. The move names the drift without blame and offers the room the smallest decision that will let it move. Its force comes from concreteness. In a room of abstractions, a draft resolution with a verb, a person and a date has a gravitational pull, because it gives everyone something to amend rather than something more to say.\n\nThe practitioner's sequence runs as follows. First, mark the moment: 'We have been round this twice; can I suggest a way to land it?' Second, propose a specific form of words, ideally one that can be minuted unchanged: 'That the board approve venue A for 2027, subject to the treasurer confirming the hire cost by 15 March.' Third, attach an owner, asked rather than assumed, and a date anchored to a calendar fact such as the next meeting or a consultation deadline. Fourth, test it: put the question, or ask the chair whether there is support.\n\nWhere the room is not ready to decide the substance, land a smaller decision. A pilot, an interim decision with a review date, a decision about who will bring options, or a decision about how the decision will be made all count. Distinguish clearly between a decision and a decision to decide, and say which one you are proposing; both are legitimate, but blurring them lets the room think it has done more than it has.\n\nThe voice is brisk and practical; use concrete nouns, numbers and dates. It helps to have a pen in hand, as though you are already writing the minute. Lean slightly forward. If you are not the chair, address the proposal to the chair so that you are offering a resolution rather than seizing the meeting.\n\nVolunteering yourself as owner is often the quickest way to land a decision, but use it with care. Each volunteered action is a commitment you must deliver, and a pattern of volunteering can leave you with work that belongs to others.\n\nIt goes wrong in four ways. Forcing closure on a question that is genuinely not ready creates resentment and guarantees relitigation. A vague action ('someone will look into it') lands nothing. A decision that happens to be your preferred outcome, framed as procedural tidying, will be recognised and resisted. And naming an owner who was not asked produces an action that will quietly fail.", "phrasings": ["We have been round this twice. Can I suggest a way to land it?", "Could I propose a form of words? That the board approve the Perth venue for next year, subject to the treasurer confirming the hire cost by the March meeting.", "I do not think we are ready to decide the substance today, but we could decide the process: that the officers bring two costed options to the next meeting.", "Would a pilot answer most of the concerns? One cohort, one year, reviewed in November.", "Who would be best placed to own that? I am happy to, if that helps, but I do not want to take it from someone better placed.", "So, for the minutes: action, a revised survey draft; owner, Kate; date, circulated by the twentieth. Is that right?", "Chair, is there support for that, or would you like to put it formally?", "Can we be clear whether this is a decision or a decision to decide? I think it is the second, and that is fine, but the minutes should say so."], "avoid": ["OK, we really need to make a decision now, guys. : Impatient and informal; it expresses frustration without offering anything to decide.", "I think someone should probably look into this and report back at some stage. : No owner, no date, no deliverable; it lands nothing and everyone knows it.", "Well, the obvious decision, surely, is to go with option A, so can we just agree that and move on? : Smuggles a preference in as procedural tidying; those who favour option B will rightly resist.", "Let's say Mark will draft that by Friday. : Assigns an owner without asking; the action is likely to fail, and Mark may resent it."], "scenarios": [{"id": "landing-the-decision-s1", "room": "festival association board", "setup": "The festival association board has spent forty minutes on next year's venue. Two venues are under consideration: the current concert hall, which is expensive but loved, and a university venue, which is cheaper but less central. Every argument has been made twice. The concert hall needs confirmation within three weeks or the dates will be released.", "said": "I keep coming back to the atmosphere. The concert hall is the festival for a lot of families. But I take the point about cost, and I take the point about access for the northern schools. I honestly do not know. Perhaps we need more information before we commit either way.", "task": "Land a decision with an owner and a date despite the remaining uncertainty.", "seconds": 60, "models": [{"text": "Could I suggest a way to land this? I think we agree the concert hall is preferred if we can afford it. So I would propose that the board resolve to book the concert hall for next year, subject to my confirming as treasurer, by the fifteenth, that it can be funded without a fee rise above five per cent. If I cannot, the university venue is booked instead. That way we decide tonight and the hall's deadline is met.", "why": "It converts the uncertainty into a single condition, takes ownership as treasurer, and gives the board a decision that holds either way."}, {"text": "I wonder whether the information we need is actually one number: what the hall costs per entry against the university. I can have that to everyone by Friday. If the board agreed tonight that the president and I may confirm the hall should the difference be under a set figure, we would not need another meeting before the deadline.", "why": "It narrows the missing information to one figure and proposes a delegated decision that avoids another meeting."}], "checks": ["Did I name a specific form of decision?", "Did I attach an owner and a date tied to the deadline?", "Did I make the decision hold whichever way the uncertainty resolved?"]}, {"id": "landing-the-decision-s2", "room": "SCSA syllabus committee", "setup": "The SCSA committee has spent an hour on the wording of a single content descriptor about improvisation in the revised syllabus. Three drafts are in circulation. The consultation draft must go to the Authority within a fortnight, and two other sections are still unreviewed.", "said": "I like elements of all three drafts. The second is the most precise, but I think the first captures the creative dimension better. I wonder whether we might ask for a fourth that brings the strengths together. I would be reluctant to settle on wording we are only half happy with.", "task": "Move the committee from crafting wording to a decision that protects the deadline.", "seconds": 50, "models": [{"text": "I think we are close, and we have also run out of time for drafting as a committee. Could I suggest we adopt the second draft as the base, add the clause from the first about creative intent, and authorise the chair and the officer to finalise wording by Friday? The descriptor goes to consultation, where it will be tested anyway. Then we can turn to the two sections still outstanding.", "why": "It proposes a concrete composite, delegates final wording, reminds the room that consultation follows, and names the opportunity cost."}, {"text": "Chair, may I propose a resolution? That the committee approve draft two for consultation, with an amendment to include 'with creative intent'. I suspect a fourth draft will raise as many questions as it solves, and the consultation is precisely where we learn whether teachers read it as we intend.", "why": "It frames a minutable resolution with a one-line amendment and uses the consultation process to lower the stakes of imperfection."}], "checks": ["Did I propose a specific draft as the base?", "Did I delegate the final wording to named people?", "Did I connect the decision to the deadline?"]}, {"id": "landing-the-decision-s3", "room": "school council", "setup": "He chairs the school council. A proposed parent survey on the specialist programs has been discussed across two meetings. Each discussion adds new questions; the draft now has forty items. The principal needs the survey in the field before the end of term to inform next year's planning.", "said": "I just think, if we are going to the trouble of surveying families, we should ask about homework and wellbeing as well. We will not get another chance this year. And I am still not sure about question twelve, which reads a little leading to me.", "task": "As chair, stop the survey expanding and land an action, owner and date.", "seconds": 60, "models": [{"text": "I think we have a strong survey and a risk of a long one. Let me propose a way to land it. The survey stays focused on the specialist programs; homework and wellbeing go on a list for a separate survey next year. Kate and the principal will cut it to no more than twenty items, fixing question twelve, and circulate the final draft by the twentieth. If there are no objections by the twenty-fifth, it goes out. Is there support for that?", "why": "It contains the expansion without dismissing it, names owners and dates, and builds in a silent-approval mechanism that prevents a third discussion."}, {"text": "Can we be clear what we are deciding today? I would like council to decide the survey's scope, which I suggest is the specialist programs only, and to delegate the wording to the principal and Kate, to be circulated by the twentieth. Homework and wellbeing are good questions; they deserve their own survey rather than being squeezed into this one. Can I take that as agreed?", "why": "It separates scope, which council decides, from wording, which it delegates, and gives the new topics a dignified future."}], "checks": ["Did I stop the expansion without rejecting the new topics?", "Did I name owners and a date?", "Did I build in a mechanism that prevents further rounds?", "Did I test for support?"]}, {"id": "landing-the-decision-s4", "room": "principal's office", "setup": "He meets the principal and the business manager about replacing ageing instruments. The conversation has wandered through leasing, grants, a staged replacement plan and the possibility of a parent appeal. The business manager has the budget lines open. The principal has fifteen minutes before another meeting.", "said": "These are all reasonable ideas. I would be happy to look at the leasing model, and a grant could be worth pursuing. I am conscious we have not got a lot of time today. Perhaps you could put something in writing and we can come back to it in a few weeks?", "task": "Secure a decision now rather than a vague deferral.", "seconds": 50, "models": [{"text": "I will certainly put it in writing. Could we decide one thing today, though, so the paper has a frame? If you were comfortable approving the first stage, the two tubas and the double basses, from this year's capital line, I would bring a costed plan for stages two and three, including the leasing comparison, to you by the end of the month.", "why": "It accepts the deferral for the larger plan but lands the urgent part now, with a specific scope and date for the rest."}, {"text": "Can I suggest a decision about the process rather than the whole plan? That the business manager and I meet next week to compare leasing against purchase, and that I bring you a single recommendation by the twenty-eighth. It would help if we agreed today that urgent replacements can proceed under a set figure in the meantime.", "why": "It lands a process decision with owners and a date, and secures a bounded interim authority for urgent needs."}], "checks": ["Did I land at least one concrete decision today?", "Did I propose owners and a date for the rest?", "Did I respect the principal's time?"]}, {"id": "landing-the-decision-s5", "room": "faculty curriculum committee", "setup": "He is an external member of a university faculty curriculum committee. A proposed jazz improvisation elective has been discussed for half an hour. Members support it but keep raising staffing and enrolment risks. Nobody has proposed a resolution, and the chair seems content to let it run.", "said": "I am very sympathetic to the elective. My worry is that we do not know whether enough students will take it, and we may be committing staff we cannot spare. It might be wiser to wait a year and see how the existing electives settle.", "task": "Propose a smaller, landable decision that addresses the risk.", "seconds": 45, "models": [{"text": "Would a pilot answer the worry? The committee could approve the elective for one year, run if it reaches a minimum enrolment of twelve, staffed by sessional appointment rather than a continuing load, and reviewed after the first semester. That tests demand without committing staff. Chair, could that be put as a resolution?", "why": "It answers each stated risk with a specific mechanism and asks the chair to formalise the proposal."}, {"text": "I think we have agreed the elective is worth having and are unsure whether it will run. Those are different decisions. Could the committee approve it in principle today, and ask the school to report by the end of next term on expected enrolment and staffing? Then the decision to offer it can be made on evidence rather than deferred indefinitely.", "why": "It separates the merit decision from the operational one and lands the first while setting a date for the second."}], "checks": ["Did I address each risk raised?", "Did I propose something smaller than full approval?", "Did I put a review date in it?"]}, {"id": "landing-the-decision-s6", "room": "feeder primary principals' meeting", "setup": "He is meeting the principals of five local primary schools that feed the combined primary concert band he runs. The group has discussed rehearsal times for next year for half an hour: some prefer before school, some after school, and transport complicates both. Letters to families need to go out in two weeks.", "said": "I think every option has a cost for one of us. Before school suits most of our parents, but our bus does not arrive until eight-fifteen. After school is easier for us but harder for two of the others. I am not sure there is a perfect answer.", "task": "Land a rehearsal decision acceptable to all five schools with owners and a date.", "seconds": 60, "models": [{"text": "I do not think there is a perfect answer either, so may I propose a workable one? Rehearsals stay before school, starting at eight-twenty rather than seven-forty-five, so Alison's bus arrives in time. I will check that with the hall and confirm by Wednesday. If any school cannot make that work, tell me by Friday; otherwise I will draft the family letter for you all by the following Monday.", "why": "It adjusts the majority option to solve the specific objection, assigns himself the actions, and sets dates working back from the letter deadline."}, {"text": "Could we decide by elimination? After school is harder for two schools; before school is harder for one, and that can be fixed by starting twenty minutes later. So I would suggest before school at eight-twenty. If everyone is content, I will confirm the hall today and send a draft letter to all five of you by the end of next week.", "why": "It makes the reasoning transparent, which helps a group of principals accept the outcome, and closes with clear actions and dates."}], "checks": ["Did I name the specific time?", "Did I solve the stated objection rather than override it?", "Did I set dates that work back from the letter deadline?"]}]}], "craft": [{"id": "craft-1", "title": "The controlling idea", "teach": "Every speech that matters exists to deliver one sentence. Aristotle put it bluntly in the third book of the Rhetoric: a speech has only two necessary parts, the statement of the case and its proof. Everything else is arrangement in service of those two. The Roman rhetoricians called the statement the propositio, and the discipline of finding it belongs to invention, not to style. If you cannot write the proposition before you write the speech, you do not yet have a speech; you have a subject.\n\nThe difference between a subject and a proposition is the difference between \"performance assessment in the Music ATAR course\" and \"the performance examination should be marked against a published repertoire standard, not against the examiner's sense of what a Year 12 should sound like.\" The first is a topic for a committee agenda. The second is a claim that someone in the room could disagree with, which is precisely what makes it worth saying. A useful test: if no intelligent person present could object to your sentence, it is not yet a controlling idea. It is a heading.\n\nThe classical tool for sharpening it is stasis theory, associated with Hermagoras of Temnos. Before speaking, ask where the real disagreement sits. Is it conjectural (did something happen, is something true), definitional (what kind of thing is it), qualitative (is it good, just, expedient), or translative (is this the right body, the right time, the right procedure)? A great many senior meetings go wrong because two people argue at different stases: one says the ensemble program is underfunded, the other says the council is not the body that sets the budget. Locate the stasis and your sentence will locate itself.\n\nEpideictic occasions need a controlling idea as much as deliberative ones. A valedictory for a retiring colleague is not a chronology of her career; it is a claim about her, such as \"she made rigour feel like generosity.\" An award acceptance is not a list of thanks; it is a statement of what the award honours beyond you. A eulogy without a controlling idea becomes an obituary read aloud. With one, every anecdote becomes evidence.\n\nWrite the sentence in no more than about twenty-five words, with a finite verb that commits. Then test it three ways. Can the room repeat it afterwards to someone who was absent? Is it said in your own register rather than in the language of the policy document? Does it tell the room what you want them to do, believe or feel? Qualifications belong in the proof, where they can be handled precisely; they do not belong in the proposition, where they read as hedging.\n\nFor a fast, associative mind the controlling idea is also a governor. Write it at the top of every page of your notes. Every example, aside and digression must answer to it: if a passage does not advance or prove that sentence, it is cut, however good it is. The best material you remove is often the best material you have, and that is the price of a speech that lands.\n\nFinally, decide where the sentence will be heard. In a sympathetic room, state it early and plainly, then prove it. In a sceptical one, you may hold it back and let the narration and proof arrive at it, so that the room reaches your conclusion a moment before you say it. Either way, it should be said aloud, in nearly the same words, at least twice.", "model": "Chair, I want to put one proposition to the committee, | and I will spend my five minutes defending it. || The performance examination should be marked against a *published* repertoire standard, | not against the examiner's private sense of what Year 12 sounds like. || That is the whole of my case. | Everything I say next is evidence for it, | and if the evidence does not persuade you, | the proposition should fall with it. || But I would ask the committee to hold that sentence in mind, | because I will come back to it, | in *exactly* those words, | before I sit down.", "exercise": {"kind": "write", "task": "Take an occasion in your calendar for the next month. Write its controlling idea in no more than twenty-five words, name the stasis at which it sits, and write beneath it the sentence the room would most like you to say instead."}, "checks": ["The sentence has a finite verb and makes a claim an intelligent person present could contest", "It contains no qualifiers such as perhaps, arguably or to some extent", "You can name the stasis (conjecture, definition, quality or procedure) at which it sits", "A colleague who was absent could repeat it after hearing it once"]}, {"id": "craft-2", "title": "Audience and occasion", "teach": "Cicero says in the Orator that nothing is harder in speaking, as in life, than to see what is fitting. The Greeks called it to prepon, the Romans decorum, and it is less a matter of manners than of judgement: the fit between what you say, who says it, to whom, on what occasion, and for what end. Most failures among clever speakers are failures of decorum rather than of argument. The case is sound; it is simply pitched at the wrong room.\n\nBegin with Aristotle's three kinds of oratory, because they tell you what the room is for. Deliberative speech concerns the future and asks what is expedient: a submission to a board, a proposal to the principal, an argument to a minister's adviser. Forensic speech concerns the past and asks what is just: a review of an incident, a response to a complaint, a moderation dispute. Epideictic speech concerns the present and assigns praise or blame: the valedictory, the festival opening, the eulogy, the award. Mixing the kinds without meaning to is a common error. A festival opening that turns into a funding plea has misjudged its occasion; a council report that turns into a celebration has avoided its accountability.\n\nNext, ask what the room must do when you stop. Decide, approve, note, fund, feel, remember. Cicero's three duties of the orator, to teach, to delight and to move (docere, delectare, movere), are weighted differently by that answer. A curriculum committee needs mostly docere, with enough delectare to keep attention in the fortieth minute. A eulogy needs movere, held in check by docere so that grief has something true to rest on. A parent body needs all three, in that order.\n\nAt senior level, audience analysis is political rather than demographic. Who holds the decision? Who holds the veto? Who will be asked about this afterwards, and by whom? There is nearly always a second audience: the minutes, the adviser who briefs the minister, the deputy who reports to the principal, the academic who repeats your sentence in another faculty meeting. Write at least one sentence that is designed to be quoted accurately by someone who was not convinced.\n\nDecorum also governs you. The same proposal sounds different from the Teacher in Charge addressing his principal, the chair addressing his own council, and the treasurer addressing a board on which he is a peer. Your ethos is partly brought into the room (role, reputation, record) and partly made in it. Aristotle is clear that the persuasive character must be produced by the speech itself, not merely assumed. In a room of academics, the speech produces ethos by precision, fair statement of opposing views and economy. In a room of parents, it produces ethos by specific knowledge of their children and plain speaking.\n\nRegister follows. Academic committees reward the exact term and the explicit concession. Funding panels reward their own criteria, named and addressed in order. Australian rooms of almost every kind punish pomposity faster than error, and reward understatement. A senior speaker sounds as if he has said less than he could.\n\nFinally, respect the clock as an element of decorum. Kairos, the right moment, includes the right length. To finish a minute early in a crowded agenda is itself an argument about your judgement.", "model": "Good evening, | and welcome to the opening night of the festival. || Some of you are here because your child plays second trumpet in a band you had not heard of until March. | Some of you are here because you played in this festival yourselves, | in a hall with worse acoustics and better carpet. || And some of you are here because you fund it, | for which, | *thank* you. || Tonight is not a night for argument. | It is a night to listen to young musicians do something difficult, | in public, | *well*. || So I will be brief, | and they will not.", "exercise": {"kind": "write", "task": "For a single upcoming occasion, write four lines: the kind of oratory (deliberative, forensic or epideictic), what the room must do when you stop, who holds the veto, and the one sentence you want quoted accurately in the minutes or afterwards."}, "checks": ["You named the genre and the speech does not drift into another without intending to", "You can state in five words what the room must do when you finish", "At least one sentence is designed to be repeated accurately by someone who disagrees", "The planned length is shorter than the time you have been given"]}, {"id": "craft-3", "title": "Arrangement", "teach": "The canon of arrangement (dispositio) gives you a sequence of parts that has survived two thousand years because it follows the order in which a listener needs things. Cicero's De Inventione names six: exordium, narratio, partitio, confirmatio, reprehensio and conclusio. The Rhetorica ad Herennium gives the same six under slightly different names, with divisio for partition, confutatio for refutation. Modern speechwriters rarely use the Latin, but they nearly all use the sequence.\n\nIn outline: the exordium makes the audience well disposed, attentive and ready to learn. The narratio sets out the situation. The partitio states what is agreed, what is at issue, and the heads you will cover. The confirmatio proves your case. The refutatio deals with the case against you. The peroratio sums up, amplifies, and asks. Aristotle, characteristically, thought most of this was optional and only statement and proof were necessary. Both views are right: the six parts are a checklist of jobs, not a set of compartments, and the jobs can be compressed, merged or reordered.\n\nCompression is the usual modern case. A three-minute report to a school council can perform all six jobs in six sentences: one to frame, one or two of situation, one that names the two matters requiring a decision, two of reasons, one meeting the obvious objection, one stating the motion. Expansion is the other case. A forty-minute keynote can give each part its own movement, and the narration may itself contain a narrative arc with its own small exordium.\n\nReordering is where judgement shows. Aristotle advises that a speaker who follows an opponent should deal with the opponent's arguments first, because a mind already occupied by an objection will not receive your proof. In practice: if the festival board has arrived convinced the new venue is unaffordable, the refutation of cost comes before the case for the venue, not after. Where the room is neutral, proof first and refutation second is stronger, because you define the ground before the objection appears.\n\nWithin the confirmation, the order of proofs matters. The old advice, which the rhetoricians called Nestorian order after Nestor's placing of his weakest troops in the middle in the Iliad, is to put your strongest argument last, your second strongest first, and the others between. Listeners remember beginnings and endings; the middle is where you may safely put the argument that is necessary but unexciting.\n\nDifferent occasions have their own conventional shapes, and decorum requires you to know them. A eulogy tends to move from loss, to the life, to what continues. A toast moves from the occasion, to the person, to the raised glass. An award acceptance moves from gratitude, to what the work was for, to the people who will continue it. A submission to a funding panel follows the panel's criteria in their own order, because a panel scoring against a rubric will not reconstruct your arrangement for you.\n\nThe practical method: before drafting any sentences, write the six job names down the margin and one line against each. If a line is empty, decide deliberately that the job is unnecessary. If two lines say the same thing, you have found redundancy before writing it.", "model": "Chair, this is the music report, | and it needs one decision tonight. || Since the last meeting, | the department has lost access to the hall on Thursday evenings | for the rest of the year. || That leaves two matters: | where the senior bands rehearse, | and who meets the cost. || The library is free, | close, | and large enough. | It is also, | I concede, | not acoustically ideal. || But the alternative is cancelling rehearsals in the term before the festival. || I therefore move that council approve the library as the interim venue, | at no cost, | until the hall is *returned*.", "exercise": {"kind": "write", "task": "Plan your next substantial speech by writing the six job names down the margin and one line against each. Then decide whether refutation should come before or after proof for this particular room, and state why in one sentence."}, "checks": ["Every one of the six jobs has either a line or a deliberate decision to omit it", "The order of refutation and proof is justified by the room's starting position", "Your strongest argument is placed last in the confirmation", "No two lines in the plan say the same thing"]}, {"id": "craft-4", "title": "The exordium", "teach": "The Roman handbooks give the exordium three tasks: to make the hearer well disposed (benevolum), attentive (attentum) and receptive (docilem). They also distinguish two kinds. The direct opening, principium, suits a case that is honourable and an audience that is willing. The subtle approach, insinuatio, suits a case the audience is already set against, or an audience that is tired, and works by indirection: it begins somewhere the room can agree before arriving at the ground it resists. Knowing which you need is the first decision of any opening.\n\nA sophisticated audience grants you perhaps thirty seconds of provisional attention, and spends them deciding whether you are worth more. They are listening less for content than for signals of control: whether you know what you are going to say, whether you will respect their time, whether you think as well as they do. Everything in the first minute should send those signals.\n\nSome openings reliably fail in senior rooms. The protocol list that runs past four names. The dictionary definition. The rhetorical question with an obvious answer. The joke imported from elsewhere. Above all, the apology: \"I'm not much of a speaker\", \"I'll try to be quick\", \"I wasn't sure what to say.\" The classical captatio benevolentiae, the seeking of goodwill, was meant to be graceful, not servile. An apology does not win goodwill from people who argue for a living; it tells them where to press.\n\nOpenings that work tend to do one of five things. They begin with a specific instance: a student, a rehearsal, a document, a date. They state the stakes plainly: what the room will lose or gain. They pose a precise question that the speech will answer. They concede something the audience expects you to deny, which buys trust at once. Or they begin in medias res, in the middle of the matter, and supply context afterwards. Each of these makes the listener do a small amount of work, and attention follows work.\n\nFormalities are part of the exordium but not its substance. In Australia an Acknowledgement of Country is customary at most public occasions; give it properly, in your own sentences, without hurry, and then pause before you begin. The same applies to the protocol of naming dignitaries: do it once, accurately, and then mark the shift with a held silence, so the room hears where the speech itself begins. The worst openings let the formalities blur into the first idea.\n\nFor insinuatio, the classic move is to begin with a value the hostile room holds. Addressing an academic committee sceptical of performance assessment, you might begin with their commitment to validity, not your commitment to music. You arrive at your proposal as an application of their principle. This is not manipulation; it is courtesy to a reasonable opponent, and it is how most minds are actually changed.\n\nWrite the first sentence last, after the speech exists, and then memorise it word for word. Nerves are highest in the first ten seconds, and a memorised first sentence lets you look at the room while you say it. It should be short. It should be yours. And it should be something only this speaker could say on this occasion.", "model": "In February, | a Year 7 trombonist asked me why we play scales in a key nobody writes in. || I gave her the answer I was given at her age, | and I watched her decide, | politely, | that I did not know. || She was *right*. || I want to use the next forty minutes to answer her properly, | because her question is the one this profession has been avoiding for thirty years: | what, | exactly, | is technical work *for*? || I will not be offering you a method. | I will be offering you a reason.", "exercise": {"kind": "speak", "task": "Write and then deliver the first forty-five seconds of an upcoming speech, using one of the five openings that work. Deliver the first sentence from memory while looking at an imagined room, and record it.", "seconds": 45}, "checks": ["The opening contains no apology and no reference to your own nerves or preparation", "The first sentence was spoken from memory with eyes up", "There is an audible pause between the formalities and the first real sentence", "The room knows by the end of the opening what question or stakes the speech will address"]}, {"id": "craft-5", "title": "Narration", "teach": "The narratio tells the room what the situation is. Quintilian and the Rhetorica ad Herennium agree on its three virtues: it should be brief, clear and plausible (brevis, aperta, probabilis). Brief does not mean short; it means containing nothing the case does not need. Clear means the listener never has to reconstruct who did what, when. Plausible means that the account fits what the room already knows of the world, so that nothing in it provokes the thought \"that cannot be the whole story.\"\n\nThe art of narration is that it does not argue. It appears to be a neutral account, and the argument is carried entirely by selection and order. If the narration is well made, the conclusion seems inevitable before you state it, and the room experiences your proposition as the obvious next sentence rather than as a demand. This is why skilled advocates spend more time on the facts than on the submissions: a case that is won in the narration does not need to be won again.\n\nSelection is therefore the whole craft, and it carries an ethical and a tactical obligation at once. Leave out the inconvenient fact and a sophisticated audience will find it, usually in questions, and then distrust everything else you said. Put it in, early and in your own words, and you control its framing. \"The ensemble was over budget last year, by a little under a fifth, and I will come to why\" is far stronger than waiting for the business manager to say it for you. The classical name for this is concessio built into the facts: the narration that concedes what is true earns the right to have the rest believed.\n\nOrder the narration for understanding, not necessarily for chronology. A strict timeline suits a forensic matter, where sequence is the issue. For a deliberative matter, it is often better to begin with the present state, then the cause, then the trend: here is where we are, here is how we got here, here is where it is going. Each sentence should leave the listener knowing one more thing than before, and needing the next.\n\nWrite the narration with agents and verbs. \"The Authority changed the weighting in 2024\" is narration. \"There was a change to the weighting\" is evasion, and a careful listener hears it as such. Passive constructions and nominalisations hide who acted, and in a room of policy people, hidden agency invites the question \"by whom?\"\n\nVividness has a place. The rhetoricians called it enargeia, or in Latin evidentia: the quality that sets the matter before the eyes. One concrete image in a narration, such as the twenty-year-old tuba with a soldered bell that a Year 9 student carries home on the bus, does more than a paragraph of description about the condition of the instrument fleet. Use it once. A second image is decoration; a third is a performance.\n\nThe test of a narration is simple and severe. Read it to someone who knows nothing of the matter and ask them what should happen next. If they give your proposition, the narration has done its work. If they give a different one, either your facts do not support your case or you have chosen the wrong facts. Both are better discovered at your desk than in the room.", "model": "Here is where we are. || The department has forty-one instruments on loan to students. | Eleven are more than twenty years old, | and four of those cannot now be repaired. || One of them is a tuba with a soldered bell | that a Year 9 student carries home on the bus | twice a week. || The replacement budget has not changed since 2019. | The cost of a student tuba has. || So each year, | we replace fewer instruments than we retire, | and the gap is now *wider* than the budget can close. || I will come to what I am asking for. | But I suspect the committee can already see it.", "exercise": {"kind": "write", "task": "Write a narration of no more than 150 words for a matter you are bringing to a decision-maker, including the one fact that most weakens your case. Read it to someone unfamiliar with the matter and ask what they think should happen next."}, "checks": ["The narration contains no evaluative words such as unfortunately, clearly or unacceptable", "The most inconvenient fact appears, in your own words, before anyone else can raise it", "Every action has a named agent and an active verb", "An uninformed listener, asked what should happen next, gave your proposition"]}, {"id": "craft-6", "title": "Partition and signposting", "teach": "A reader can turn back a page; a listener cannot. The partitio exists because the ear needs a map. In Cicero's account it does one of two things: it sets out what is agreed and what remains in dispute, or it lists, briefly, the heads under which you will argue. Both are acts of courtesy. The first tells a sophisticated audience that you know where the real disagreement lies; the second tells them how long they will be asked to hold their attention, and on what.\n\nQuintilian warns against partitions that are too long or too fine. Three heads are easily held; four can be; seven cannot. If your matter genuinely has seven parts, group them under three and partition the groups. Name the heads with nouns that carry content: not \"my first point, my second point\" but \"the case rests on validity, workload and equity.\" Each of those words then becomes a hook to hang everything under it.\n\nOnce you have numbered, you are bound by the number. A speaker who promises three reasons and delivers four sounds disorganised; one who promises three and delivers two sounds as if he has lost his place, which he usually has. Keep the order you announced. When you reach each head, name it again in the same word you used in the partition, so that the listener hears the click of the map aligning with the territory.\n\nBetween the heads, use transitions that do real work. The weakest transitions are purely mechanical: \"moving on\", \"next\", \"secondly.\" The strongest are hinges that close one section and open the next with a question the room now wants answered: \"That is the case for validity. Does it cost more teacher time?\" This is hypophora, the figure of raising a question and answering it, and it is the most efficient signpost in spoken English because it creates the need for the next section before supplying it.\n\nInternal summaries matter more in speech than writers expect. At the end of a long section, one sentence that restates the point made lets late arrivals and wandering minds re-enter. In a forty-minute keynote, two or three such summaries are not redundancy; they are load-bearing.\n\nCalibrate the quantity of signposting to the room. Academics tolerate less metadiscourse than school audiences; too much reads as condescension, as if you think they cannot follow. A crisp partition, heads repeated at arrival, and hinge questions between sections is usually enough. In a short committee contribution, a single sentence of partition (\"two points, one on timing and one on cost\") replaces all of it.\n\nFor a speaker who thinks associatively, the partition is also a discipline on yourself. A digression is permissible if it is flagged and returned from: \"A word on the history, because it bears on the third reason,\" then, after it, \"Which brings us back to equity.\" Without the flag, the room cannot tell digression from structure. Without the return, neither can you. Speakers who are known for their tangents are usually forgiven them when the return is audible and exact.", "model": "The proposal before the board rests on three things: | *validity*, | *workload*, | and *equity*. | I will take them in that order, | and I will be brief on the second. || First, | validity. || [section] || That is the case for validity. | The board's next question will be a fair one: | does it cost more teacher time? || The answer is, | modestly, | yes, | and here is how much. || [section] || Which leaves equity, | and this is the reason I would ask the board to weigh most *heavily*.", "exercise": {"kind": "speak", "task": "Deliver a partition of three named heads for an upcoming contribution, then deliver the hinge sentence between the first and second heads, using a question the room would genuinely ask.", "seconds": 40}, "checks": ["The partition names each head with a content noun rather than an ordinal", "Each head is named again, in the same word, when you arrive at it", "The hinge between sections is a question the room would plausibly ask", "The number of heads promised equals the number delivered"]}, {"id": "craft-7", "title": "Proof", "teach": "Aristotle divides proofs into two kinds. Inartificial proofs (atechnoi) are found rather than made: documents, minutes, contracts, data, the syllabus itself. Artificial proofs (entechnoi) are made by the speaker, and come in three modes: the character of the speaker (ethos), the disposition of the audience (pathos), and the argument itself (logos). In a senior room all three operate at once, but they operate differently in speech than on the page.\n\nThe characteristic form of spoken argument is what Aristotle called the enthymeme: a syllogism with a premise suppressed, which the audience supplies. \"The examination rewards students who can afford private tuition, so it is not measuring what the syllabus says it measures\" leaves unstated the premise that the syllabus intends to measure something other than access to tuition. The listener who supplies that premise has participated in the argument, and owns its conclusion a little. The skill lies in suppressing only premises the room already holds. Suppress one they reject, and the argument falls silently, which is worse than falling loudly.\n\nThe second form is example (paradeigma), the rhetorical equivalent of induction. A single, well-chosen example, fully developed, persuades a listening audience more than five examples listed. The page can carry an enumeration; the ear tends to retain the first and last items and lose the rest. Choose the example that the most sceptical person in the room would find hardest to dismiss, and spend your time on it.\n\nFor structure, Stephen Toulmin's model in The Uses of Argument is useful precisely because it names what speakers leave out: the claim, the grounds that support it, the warrant that connects them, the backing for the warrant, the qualifier, and the rebuttal. Most weak spoken arguments state a claim and grounds and assume the warrant. In an academic room, the warrant is often exactly what is contested. Say it.\n\nEvidence in speech needs a different grammar. Name the source before the claim, not after it, because the listener weighs a statement by its source as it arrives: \"The Authority's own moderation report found\" prepares the ear in a way that a footnote cannot. Quote briefly and exactly. If you have a document, hold it, refer to its page, and do not read long passages: the room will start reading ahead of you and stop listening.\n\nQualifiers belong once, precisely, where they are true. \"In the three schools we examined\" is a qualifier; \"it could perhaps be argued that\" is a hedge. A sophisticated audience respects the first and discounts everything said under the second. If you are anxious, the hedges multiply; strike them from the script and keep only the qualifications you could defend under questioning.\n\nEthos and pathos are proofs, not decorations. Ethos is made by the visible fairness of the argument, by concessions offered before they are demanded, and by evident command of detail. Pathos in a committee rarely means emotion; it means making the room feel the weight of a consequence, usually through one concrete case. After each proof, restate the claim it proves. On the page this would be repetition; aloud it is the only way to bind evidence to conclusion.", "model": "The question is whether the current task measures performance, | or measures access to private tuition. || The Authority's own guidelines say the task assesses *interpretation*. | But the repertoire list rewards pieces that, | in practice, | are only taught in private studios. || Take two students of equal ability. | One has had a private teacher since Year 3; | the other learnt in a school band. | The list allows the first to choose a piece the second has never been *shown*. || If the task measures interpretation, | that difference should not matter. | It plainly does. || So the task, | as written, | measures something the syllabus did not *intend*.", "exercise": {"kind": "write", "task": "Take one claim you need to make in an upcoming committee and write it out in Toulmin's terms: claim, grounds, warrant, backing, qualifier, rebuttal. Then rewrite it as a spoken paragraph of under 120 words built on one fully developed example."}, "checks": ["The warrant connecting grounds to claim is stated aloud, not assumed", "The source of each piece of evidence is named before the evidence itself", "There is at most one qualifier, and it describes a real limit of the evidence", "The claim is restated in the final sentence of the paragraph"]}, {"id": "craft-8", "title": "Refutation and prolepsis", "teach": "Refutation (refutatio, or reprehensio in De Inventione) answers the case against you. Its anticipatory form, raising an objection before anyone has voiced it, is prolepsis, also called procatalepsis. In rooms full of people who argue for a living, prolepsis is often more powerful than proof, because it demonstrates the one thing a sceptical expert most wants to see: that you have thought about this as hard as they have.\n\nBegin by stating the objection at its strongest. Philosophers now call this steelmanning; the older courtesy was simply to state your opponent's case so fairly that he would accept your version of it. \"The strongest objection to this proposal is cost, and it is a serious one\" earns more credit than any rebuttal that follows, because it shows the room that you are not afraid of the objection. Weakening the objection before answering it is visible to everyone, and turns a refutation into a confession.\n\nThen decide which part of the objection you are attacking. Most objections can be met at one of four points. You can deny the premise: the cost is not what it is said to be. You can deny the inference: the cost is real, but it does not follow that the proposal is unaffordable. You can deny the relevance: cost is not the criterion this body is meant to apply. Or you can deny the proportion: the cost is real and relevant, but it is outweighed. Speakers who attack all four at once sound defensive. Choose the strongest ground, say which one it is, and hold it.\n\nConcessio is the companion figure: grant part of the objection in order to win the larger point. \"Yes, the new venue costs more; it also seats twice as many and pays for itself by the second year\" concedes a fact and turns it. A further move is to turn the objection into an argument for your side, showing that the very consideration raised against you, properly understood, supports you. This is the most satisfying refutation to deliver and the most dangerous; if it does not hold completely, it looks like a trick.\n\nNot every objection deserves a reply. Answer the objection the most influential sceptic in the room holds, and perhaps the one the minutes will record. Raising objections nobody holds, in order to defeat them, is the straw man, and academics detect it at once. Answering too many objections suggests that the proposal has too many weaknesses. One or two, handled well, are the norm.\n\nPlacement matters, as noted under arrangement. If the room has arrived with the objection already formed, meet it early, before your proof, or your proof will be heard through it. If the objection is latent, let your proof establish the ground first and then raise it yourself near the end, so that you choose the words in which it enters the discussion.\n\nTone is the final element. Refutation should sound cooler than proof. Lower the pitch slightly, slow down, and avoid all intensifiers. Concession is not the same as hedging: a hedge withdraws your claim a little everywhere; a concession surrenders one precise point completely and holds everything else. The room hears the difference instantly.", "model": "The strongest objection to the new venue is cost, | and the board is *right* to raise it. || The hire fee is a little under double what we pay now. | I will not pretend otherwise. || But the objection assumes that the fee is the whole of the cost, | and it is not. || The present hall cannot seat the finals, | so each year we hire a second room, | and pay twice for staffing. || The new venue removes the second room *altogether*. || So the question for the board is not whether it costs more to hire. | It does. || The question is whether it costs more to *run*.", "exercise": {"kind": "speak", "task": "State aloud the strongest objection to a proposal of yours, in words its most capable opponent would accept, then answer it at one of the four points (premise, inference, relevance, proportion), naming which.", "seconds": 75}, "checks": ["The objection was stated without weakening adjectives or a dismissive tone", "You attacked one point of the objection and named it", "At least one fact was conceded completely and plainly", "Your pitch and pace were cooler in the refutation than in the proof"]}, {"id": "craft-9", "title": "The peroration", "teach": "The peroration is the part of a speech most often remembered and most often wasted. De Inventione and the Rhetorica ad Herennium give it three possible components: the summing up (enumeratio), the amplification that heightens feeling against an opponent or for a cause (indignatio or amplificatio), and the appeal for sympathy (conquestio). Translated into modern practice: recapitulate, elevate, ask. Not every ending needs all three, but every ending needs to do at least one of them on purpose.\n\nRecapitulation is not repetition. It returns to the controlling idea in nearly the words you gave it at the start, and binds the proofs to it in a single sentence: \"Validity, workload and equity all point the same way.\" A sophisticated audience will accept one sentence of summary gladly. Three sentences, and you are re-delivering the speech.\n\nElevation moves the matter from the particular to the principle. The council report about rehearsal space ends on what the space is for; the festival budget ends on what the festival has made possible for a generation of players; the eulogy ends on what the person's life asks of those who remain. It is the one place in a deliberative speech where a little grandeur is decorous, because the case has already been made and the room has earned the lift. Elevation without a case beneath it is merely loud.\n\nThe ask is the deliberative ending. Name precisely what you want the room to do: the motion, the vote, the funding, the change of wording in clause four. Many capable speakers build to an excellent close and then fail to say what they want. In a committee, the chair will then summarise your contribution in his own words, and you will have lost control of it at the moment of greatest leverage.\n\nRing composition, returning at the end to an image or phrase from the exordium, is the oldest closing device in literature and still among the most effective aloud. The Year 7 trombonist who asked why she plays scales comes back in the last minute, now answered. The listener feels the speech close like a phrase resolving to the tonic, and you will recognise the effect from music more readily than most speakers.\n\nSeveral things undo endings. New material introduced at the close tells the room you have not finished thinking. False endings, \"finally\" followed by another point, followed by \"in closing\", spend the room's goodwill in seconds. Trailing off, letting the voice fall into \"so, yes, that's really all I wanted to say\", erases the work of the previous sentence. And the reflexive \"thank you\" spoken as the last words, in the same breath as the final sentence, flattens it. If you wish to thank the room, deliver the final sentence, hold the silence, and then thank them as a separate act.\n\nThe final sentence should be written and memorised, and it should end on its most important word. English places stress naturally at the end of the clause, so build the sentence so that the weight falls there. Shorter is usually stronger. Then stop, hold eye contact for a breath, and do not move until the silence has done its work.", "model": "When she retired, | she left us a timetable, | a cupboard of scores annotated in three colours of pencil, | and a department that assumes, | without ever saying so, | that every child can learn to read music. || That assumption is her legacy. | It was not common when she arrived. | It is not common now. || We will not keep it by remembering her. | We will keep it by *teaching* as if she were still down the corridor, | listening. || So I ask you to raise your glasses, | not to her career, | but to the assumption. || To *Margaret*.", "exercise": {"kind": "speak", "task": "Deliver the last sixty seconds of an upcoming speech: one sentence of recapitulation, one of elevation, and an ask or a closing image. End on the stressed word, hold the silence for three seconds, and record it.", "seconds": 60}, "checks": ["The recapitulation is a single sentence that echoes the controlling idea", "No new argument or fact appears in the final minute", "The last sentence ends on its most important word", "You held three seconds of silence after the last word before moving or thanking"]}, {"id": "craft-10", "title": "Writing for the ear", "teach": "Prose written to be read and prose written to be heard obey different laws, and most educated speakers write the first when they need the second. The reader controls pace, can re-read, and sees the shape of a sentence before finishing it. The listener receives one word at a time, at your pace, once. Writing for the ear means writing for working memory.\n\nAristotle distinguished two styles of sentence in the third book of the Rhetoric: the strung-on style (lexis eiromene), in which clauses follow one another with no end in sight until the matter ends, and the periodic style (lexis katestrammene), in which the sentence is turned back on itself and its sense is complete only at its close. English rhetoric inherited the distinction as the loose and the periodic sentence.\n\nThe loose sentence states its main clause first and then adds: \"The festival has grown every year since 2015, in entries, in audiences, and in the standard of the bands.\" It is easy to follow and has a cumulative, conversational feel. Its risk is sprawl. The periodic sentence suspends its main clause until the end: \"In entries, in audiences, and above all in the standard of the bands, the festival has grown every year since 2015.\" It builds tension and gives the close real weight. Its risk is that the listener forgets what the suspended elements were for. Use periodic sentences sparingly and at moments of emphasis, keep the suspended elements short and parallel, and let loose sentences carry the narrative.\n\nEnd focus is the principle, described in the standard grammars of English, that new and important information falls most naturally at the end of the clause, where the intonation nucleus usually lands. Build sentences so that the given comes first and the new comes last. \"The committee asked for three options, and we have one\" puts the weight on the one. \"We have one option, which is fewer than the committee asked for\" buries it. Many weak spoken sentences end on a qualifier, a date or a reference: \"as I mentioned earlier\", \"in that regard\", \"going forward.\" Move them to the front or cut them.\n\nSentence length should vary, but the average must be shorter than in your written prose. A spoken sentence of more than about twenty-five words needs a structure the ear can track, usually a list or a parallel. A sequence of short sentences followed by one long one, or the reverse, creates rhythm. A paragraph of uniform medium-length sentences creates drone.\n\nCadence is the rhythmic close of a phrase. Cicero was famously attached to particular rhythmic endings, and the Latin tradition of clausulae shows how seriously the ancients took the sound of a sentence's last syllables. In English the practical rule is simpler: a stressed syllable at the end of a sentence sounds final; an unstressed one sounds unfinished. \"That is the decision before us\" closes on a stress. \"That is the decision that is before us here\" dribbles away.\n\nKeep subject and verb close. Prefer verbs to nominalisations: \"we decided\" over \"a decision was made\", \"students improved\" over \"there was improvement.\" Prefer concrete nouns to abstractions. And always read the draft aloud, standing, at speaking pace. Every place you stumble is a sentence written for the eye.", "model": "The committee asked us for three options. | We have *one*. || I should explain why. || We looked at moving rehearsals to the weekend, | and lost half the players. | We looked at sharing the hall with drama, | and lost half the hall. || What remains is the library, | which is free, | which is close, | and which is large enough for every ensemble we run. || It is not beautiful. | It does not need to be. || It needs to be *available*, | and it is.", "exercise": {"kind": "write", "task": "Take a paragraph you have recently written for a report or paper and rewrite it for the ear: shorten the average sentence, put new information at the end of each clause, replace nominalisations with verbs, and make every sentence end on a stressed syllable. Read both versions aloud and note where you stumbled in the first."}, "checks": ["No sentence ends on a qualifier, a date or a phrase such as going forward or in that regard", "Sentences vary in length, with at least one under eight words", "Any periodic sentence has short, parallel suspended elements", "The rewritten version can be read aloud at speaking pace without stumbling"]}, {"id": "craft-11", "title": "Figures that work aloud", "teach": "The figures of speech were catalogued by rhetoricians for the voice before they were studied on the page, and some of them only make full sense heard. Others, admired in print, collapse when spoken. A senior speaker chooses figures by what the ear can register at speaking pace, and by what a sophisticated audience will accept without noticing the machinery.\n\nThe figures of repetition work best aloud because the ear learns the pattern and anticipates its return. Anaphora repeats the opening of successive clauses: \"It is a program for the gifted. It is a program for the persistent. It is a program for the child who has never touched an instrument.\" Epistrophe repeats the ending. Anadiplosis takes the end of one clause and begins the next with it: \"We need a standard, and a standard needs a list.\" Each builds structure that a listener can hold without effort.\n\nAntithesis, the balanced contrast, and isocolon, clauses of matching length, are the backbone of memorable spoken lines. Max Atkinson's study of political oratory, Our Masters' Voices, showed how reliably contrasts and lists of three draw applause, because they let the audience predict the moment of completion. The tricolon is useful in the same way, but a sophisticated audience hears the mechanism of an obvious three. The remedy is to make the third member longer or different in kind, which the older rhetoric would call a rising or climactic arrangement, so that the pattern is fulfilled and exceeded at once.\n\nThe figures of reticence suit Australian rooms and academic ones especially. Litotes, affirming by denying the opposite (\"not a small achievement\"), is native understatement and almost impossible to overuse in Perth. Praeteritio, or paralipsis, mentions a thing by saying you will not mention it: \"I will not dwell on the fact that the hall has flooded twice.\" Used once, it is elegant; used twice, it is sarcasm. Hypophora, raising a question and answering it, gives a speech the rhythm of dialogue and serves as the best of signposts.\n\nRhetorical questions with obvious answers fail in senior rooms because they invite the audience to answer differently in their heads. A genuine question, left open for one held breath, works far better.\n\nSome figures do not survive the voice. Chiasmus (\"ask not what your country can do for you\") works only when short, concrete and rehearsed; an abstract chiasmus with long members is lost before it inverts. Parenthesis, the clause inserted mid-sentence, is clear on the page and confusing aloud unless marked by a clear drop in pitch and kept very short. Puns that depend on spelling, allusions the room does not share, zeugma, sustained alliteration and any acronym that must be decoded all cost more attention than they repay. Extended metaphors are dangerous aloud because the ear cannot check whether they remain consistent; a mixed metaphor, heard, sounds like a joke you did not intend.\n\nThe governing rule is the classical one: figures should arise from the matter and from feeling, not be applied to them. Quintilian's view was that ornament which draws attention to itself defeats its purpose. A single anaphora at the emotional peak of a festival speech is powerful. Six figures in a council report are a performance, and a council is not an audience that wants one.", "model": "This festival is not a competition for the gifted. | It is not a showcase for the well-resourced. | It is a weekend in which a band from a school with no music teacher | plays the same stage, | to the same adjudicators, | as a band that rehearses five days a week. || That is *not* a small thing. || I will not dwell on the fact that we have done it, | for twenty years, | on a budget most schools would spend on a piano. || I will only say that it is worth *doing*, | and that it is worth doing *well*.", "exercise": {"kind": "speak", "task": "Write and deliver a 60-second passage for a ceremonial occasion that uses one anaphora with a lengthened third member and one litotes, and no other figure. Record it and listen for whether the pattern is audible without sounding mechanical.", "seconds": 60}, "checks": ["The anaphora is audible on first hearing and its third member is longer or different in kind", "The litotes sounds natural rather than arch", "No chiasmus, parenthesis or acronym required the listener to decode it", "No rhetorical question with an obvious answer was used"]}, {"id": "craft-12", "title": "The anecdote and the specific detail", "teach": "In a room of experts, the detail that proves you were there outweighs any amount of argument that could have been written by anyone. The classical exemplum, the illustrative case, was a staple of proof; the modern anecdote is its descendant, and its power lies in its particularity. A general claim about student engagement is ignored. The student who practised the bass line of a chart for six weeks on an instrument with a cracked rib, because it was the only one left, is remembered for years.\n\nChoose the detail before the story. The best details are concrete, sensory and slightly unexpected: the rosin dust on the music stand, the borrowed tie at the concert, the parent who stood at the back of the hall because she had come straight from a night shift. One such detail authenticates a whole anecdote. Five turn it into description, and description in a speech is the point at which attention leaves.\n\nAn anecdote has a shape, and it is short. A sentence of setting: who, where, when. A complication: what went wrong or what was asked. A turn: what happened that you did not expect. And a link: the single sentence that binds the anecdote to your controlling idea. Without the link the room enjoys the story and forgets why you told it. In a fifteen-minute speech, an anecdote should rarely run past a minute. In a committee contribution, it should be three sentences.\n\nTense and voice matter. Telling an anecdote in the present tense, the historic present, brings it closer: \"It is the night before the festival. The bus has not arrived.\" Use it for the narrative core, then return to the past tense for the reflection. Direct speech, a short line actually said, is more vivid than reported speech, provided it is brief and you are certain of the words.\n\nAnecdotes involving students carry obligations that are not merely ethical but rhetorical. A child used as a prop, named, identifiable, or made to seem pitiable, costs a speaker the room's trust, especially in a parent body. Anonymise, change identifying particulars, seek permission where the story could be recognised, and never tell a story a student would be embarrassed to hear. The audience knows instinctively whether the speaker respects the person in the story.\n\nStories about yourself need care in the other direction. An anecdote in which you are the hero reads as self-promotion; one in which you were wrong, or learnt something, builds ethos. The best award acceptances and keynotes often turn on the moment the speaker misjudged something and a student or colleague corrected him. That is not false modesty; it is evidence that you are still paying attention.\n\nAvoid stock anecdotes. The starfish on the beach, the teacher and the jar of rocks, the misattributed quotation: a sophisticated audience has heard them, recognises them in the first sentence, and discounts both the story and the teller. A real anecdote from your own work, however small, is always stronger than a borrowed parable, however polished.\n\nFinally, test each anecdote against the controlling idea. If it is a good story that proves nothing, keep it for the dinner afterwards.", "model": "It is the night before the State finals. | The double bass we hired has not arrived, | and our bassist, | who is fifteen, | has been practising for six weeks on a school instrument with a cracked rib. || I offer to find another. | She says, | \"No. I know where *this* one is out of tune.\" || She played it the next day, | and played it *well*. || I tell you this because it is the best definition of musicianship I have heard from anyone, | and it came from a student who knew her instrument better than I did.", "exercise": {"kind": "speak", "task": "Tell one anecdote from your own teaching or playing in under sixty seconds, using the historic present, a single concrete detail, and a final sentence that links it explicitly to a claim. Record it and time it.", "seconds": 60}, "checks": ["The anecdote contains exactly one memorable concrete detail", "The final sentence explicitly links the story to a claim", "No student is identifiable and none is made to seem pitiable", "It ran under sixty seconds"]}, {"id": "craft-13", "title": "Numbers and data", "teach": "A table can hold forty figures; a listener can hold about two. Numbers in a speech are therefore not data in the analytical sense but evidence chosen for its effect on judgement, and every number you speak should have earned its place by changing what the room believes. The discipline is to decide, before writing, which one or two numbers carry the case, and to let the rest live in the written paper that accompanies you.\n\nGive one number per sentence. Two numbers in a sentence invite comparison the ear cannot perform at speaking pace; three produce a blur. If a comparison is the point, make it for the listener: not \"enrolments were 212 in 2019 and 287 this year\" but \"enrolments have risen by about a third since 2019.\" The arithmetic is your job, not theirs.\n\nRound, and say that you are rounding. \"About one in three\" is heard and kept; \"32.7 per cent\" is heard and lost, and in an academic room it can sound like false precision. Keep the exact figure for the moment when exactness is the argument, such as a funding shortfall that the panel will check, and then say it slowly, once, and repeat it.\n\nEvery number needs a comparison, because a number without one has no meaning. Forty-one instruments is a fact; forty-one instruments for a hundred and sixty players is an argument. The comparison can be with the past (a trend), with a peer (a benchmark), with a standard (a requirement), or with something human-scaled. Translating a total into a unit the listener can picture is often the decisive move: a budget figure becomes the cost per student per year, a deficit becomes the price of one tuba.\n\nUse end focus. The number usually belongs early, as given information, and the meaning belongs at the end, where the stress falls: \"We replace four instruments a year, and we retire seven.\" Or, if the number itself is the surprise, hold it to the end: \"The replacement budget, adjusted for prices, has fallen by nearly half.\"\n\nName the source in the sentence, before the figure, and choose the source the room trusts most. In a school council, the business manager's report. In a curriculum committee, the Authority's own published data. In a funding panel, the panel's own guidelines. Never cite a figure you could not reproduce on request; in rooms of policy people, someone will ask.\n\nWatch the familiar distortions, because a sophisticated audience certainly will. A percentage change on a small base (\"a hundred per cent increase\" from two students to four) invites ridicule. Mixing counts and rates confuses. Selecting a start year that flatters the trend will be noticed by anyone who knows the history. The honest framing is also the persuasive one, because it survives questions.\n\nIf you use slides, show the one number large and say what it means; do not show a table and read from it. Slides of dense data divide the room into those reading and those listening, and you lose both.\n\nFinally, repeat the decisive number at the close, in exactly the same form. Repetition is what turns a figure into the room's shared fact.", "model": "I want to give the panel one number, | and then explain what it means. || The department replaces four loan instruments a year. | It retires *seven*. || That gap has held, | on the business manager's figures, | for five years. || Put another way: | each year, | three more students begin a specialist program | without an instrument the school can lend them. || The grant we are seeking would close that gap for three years. | It would cost, | per student, | about what a family pays for one term of private lessons. || Four replaced. | Seven retired. || That is the *case*.", "exercise": {"kind": "write", "task": "Take a report or submission containing at least ten figures. Choose the one or two that carry the case, and write a spoken paragraph of under 120 words that gives each a named source, a comparison and a human-scale translation, and repeats the decisive figure at the end."}, "checks": ["No sentence contains more than one number", "Every number is paired with a comparison or translation", "The source of each figure is named before the figure", "The decisive number is repeated in identical form at the end"]}, {"id": "craft-14", "title": "Humour and gravity", "teach": "The serious treatment of laughter in rhetoric is ancient. In the second book of Cicero's De Oratore, Julius Caesar Strabo gives a long account of wit and distinguishes humour arising from the matter (res) from humour arising from the words (dicta). Quintilian devotes a chapter of the Institutio Oratoria to laughter and admits, candidly, that it is difficult to teach. Both treat humour as a tool of persuasion with real risks, not as decoration.\n\nIn senior rooms, humour almost always means wit rather than jokes. A prepared joke, imported from elsewhere, announces itself and puts the room under an obligation to laugh. Wit arises from the situation: the observed absurdity of the agenda, the gap between the policy and the practice, the thing everyone was thinking and no one had said. Humour of the matter is safer than humour of the words, because it shows you see clearly; wordplay shows only that you prepared.\n\nSelf-deprecation is the Australian default, and it works within limits. Deprecate your circumstances, your taste, your past self, your timekeeping; never your competence on the matter at hand. A speaker who jokes that he does not really understand the budget has invited the room to believe him. Self-deprecation by a senior figure also lowers the temperature for others: it signals that the room is safe for honesty.\n\nThe lines that must not be crossed are the lines of power. Humour at the expense of someone in the room with less standing than you, a junior colleague, a student, a parent, is not wit but a demonstration of rank, and the room recognises it. Humour at the expense of a body that is absent can also return to you in the minutes. The safest target is the shared situation, and the next safest is yourself.\n\nTiming is mechanical and can be learnt. The word that carries the joke goes last in its sentence, where end focus places the stress. A small pause before that word sets it up; a pause after it allows the laugh. If the laugh does not come, do not wait for it. Continue as if the line were simply a good sentence, which, if it was well made, it still is. Never explain a line that missed.\n\nGravity is the other skill, and its signals are physical. When the matter turns serious, the voice drops in pitch, slows, and loses ornament. Figures become fewer; sentences become plainer and often shorter. The transition into gravity should be marked by a held silence, so that the room hears the change of key before the first grave sentence arrives. Moving from a laugh straight into grief, without that rest, jars.\n\nThe two can coexist, and the eulogy shows how. Humour that is true to the person, the story everyone recognises, gives the room permission to grieve by first letting it remember the living person. It must come early or in the middle, never at the close, and it must be warm rather than clever. A good eulogy usually earns one real laugh and spends it on making the loss specific.\n\nA last principle: Australian rooms dislike pomposity more than they dislike error. A speaker who can be grave without sounding solemn, and light without sounding flippant, is understood to be in command of both.", "model": "When the committee asked me to accept this on behalf of the department, | I was told I would have *three* minutes. || As a bass player, | I have never been given three minutes for anything, | so I intend to use every one of them. || [pause] || This award is not for me. | It is for eleven years of rehearsals on Tuesday nights, | in winter, | in a hall with no heating, | by teachers who could have gone home. || [held] || Two of them are not here to see it. | I would like this to be *theirs*.", "exercise": {"kind": "speak", "task": "Deliver a 90-second award acceptance or toast that contains one line of situational wit with its key word last, followed by a held silence and a deliberate shift into one grave sentence. Record it and check that the pitch and pace audibly change at the shift.", "seconds": 90}, "checks": ["The humour is drawn from the situation or yourself, not from anyone with less standing", "The word that carries the line comes last in its sentence", "A held silence marks the shift into gravity", "Pitch drops and pace slows audibly in the grave passage"]}, {"id": "craft-15", "title": "Memory: notes, anchors and the full text", "teach": "Memoria was the fourth canon of classical rhetoric, and the ancients took it seriously because they spoke without paper. The Rhetorica ad Herennium sets out the artificial memory: a series of familiar places (loci) in which the speaker stores vivid images (imagines), walking through them in order as he speaks. Cicero, in De Oratore, attributes the method's invention to Simonides of Ceos, who identified the dead at a collapsed banquet hall by remembering where each had sat. The method of loci still works, and it remains the best technique for memorising a structure, as opposed to a text.\n\nThe modern speaker chooses among four modes, and the choice is a judgement of occasion. The fully scripted text, read or near-memorised. The extemporaneous speech, prepared thoroughly and delivered from notes in words found in the moment. The memorised speech, rare now and risky. And the impromptu remark, which in committees is most of what you do and which is improved by having a controlling idea and a partition ready before you raise your hand.\n\nScript the text when the words themselves carry consequence. A eulogy, because emotion will take your memory and the family deserves exact words. A formal statement of policy, a motion, anything that will be quoted or minuted, because a paraphrase can commit you to something you did not intend. Ceremonial addresses with strict time limits. Any occasion broadcast or recorded. In these cases the craft is to write for the ear and then rehearse until the script is a score you interpret, not a page you read.\n\nSpeak from notes when the room needs responsiveness more than exactness: a council report, a committee contribution, a panel, a department meeting, a talk to parents. Notes allow you to look at people, to adjust to a question in the faces before anyone asks it, and to sound like yourself. Good notes are not sentences. They are keywords in large type, one card or one half-page per section, with the controlling idea written at the top of every card.\n\nAnchors are the hybrid that most senior speakers actually use. Memorise, word for word, the first sentence, the partition, the transitions between sections, any precise formulation (a figure, a motion, a definition), and the final sentence. Everything between the anchors is spoken from keywords. The anchors give the speech its exact architecture; the spaces between give it life. If you lose your place, you need only find the next anchor.\n\nFor a mind that moves associatively, anchors are also the safety line. A digression is permissible as long as you can see the next anchor and know how to return to it. When you notice yourself three tangents deep, stop, look at the card, say the anchor, and the room will hear only a speaker moving to his next point. The danger is not the tangent; it is not knowing the way back.\n\nWhatever the mode, memorise the structure first, using a sequence of places if it helps: the door of the hall for the exordium, the stage for the narration, and so on. Then memorise the anchors. Only then, and only if the occasion requires it, memorise the text. A speaker who knows his structure cold can lose any sentence and keep the speech.", "model": "[Anchor, memorised] | The panel asked us three questions, | and I will answer them in order: | who the festival serves, | what it costs, | and what changes if it *stops*. || [Keywords: regional schools, travel, first stage] | Most of the bands come from schools with no specialist teacher. | For many players, | this is the first time they have played on a stage at all. || [Anchor, memorised] | That is who it serves. | What does it cost? || [Keywords: per player, per day] | ... || [Anchor, memorised] | If it stops, | those players do not find another festival. | They stop *playing*.", "exercise": {"kind": "speak", "task": "For an upcoming contribution, write a single note card with the controlling idea at the top, three keywords per section, and the first sentence, transitions and final sentence written out in full. Memorise only the anchors and deliver the whole contribution from the card.", "seconds": 120}, "checks": ["The first sentence, each transition and the final sentence were delivered verbatim from memory", "Everything between the anchors was spoken from keywords, not read", "You looked at the card only at section boundaries", "If you digressed, you returned through the next anchor"]}, {"id": "craft-16", "title": "Rehearsal and delivery", "teach": "Delivery (actio, or pronuntiatio) was the fifth canon, and Cicero reports the story that Demosthenes, asked what mattered most in oratory, answered delivery, and delivery, and delivery. A written text is a score; delivery is its performance. You already know this from the bandstand better than most speakers do: the notes are necessary and nowhere near sufficient, and the difference between a reading and a performance is time, dynamics and the relationship with the room.\n\nPrepare the script physically. Large type, at least sixteen point, with generous line spacing. Never break a sentence across a page. Number the pages, do not staple them, and slide each finished page aside rather than turning it. Leave a wide right margin for marks. Then mark it as a score: a single slash for a breath, a double slash for a held full stop, an underline for the stressed word in each sentence that carries the meaning, and a marginal note where pace or pitch changes. The markup in the models in this module is a version of exactly that.\n\nPace for a written text should be slower than you think. Nervous speakers accelerate, and a scripted text, being denser than conversation, needs more time per word than talk does. As a planning figure, allow around 130 words a minute for a formal scripted address and cut the script to fit. Vary pace deliberately: faster through narrative and context, slower through the controlling idea, the decisive number and the close.\n\nPause is the most powerful instrument a speaker has and the one inexperienced speakers fear most. A pause before a key word makes the room lean towards it; a pause after a key sentence lets it land. A held silence of two or three seconds, which feels interminable to the speaker, reads to the audience as authority. Your sense of time as a bassist is an asset here: the pause is a rest with a value, not a gap to be filled, and the next phrase should arrive in time, not early.\n\nFor eye contact while reading, the technique is to look down, take the phrase in, look up, and deliver it to one person. Then look down for the next phrase. The end of every sentence, and especially its stressed final word, should be delivered to the room, not to the page. Hold each person for a complete thought rather than sweeping. In a committee, direct the key sentence to the person whose view will decide the outcome, and the refutation to the person who holds the objection.\n\nOwning the room begins before you speak. Arrive early and stand where you will speak. Walk to the lectern without hurry, arrange your pages, look up, and wait for silence before the first word, however long that takes. Plant your feet. Hands rest on the lectern or at your sides; gestures, if any, arise from the meaning. Do not apologise for technology, for time or for yourself. Stillness reads as confidence; movement without purpose reads as nerves.\n\nRehearse aloud, standing, at full voice, and at least once in the room or one like it. Time every run. Record one and listen for the places where you speed up, drop the ends of sentences or add fillers. Rehearse the first minute and the last minute most, because they carry the most nerves and the most weight. A speech rehearsed five times aloud sounds spontaneous; one rehearsed only in your head sounds read.", "model": "She joined the department in 1998, | when the music block was two portable classrooms | and a piano no one would claim. || [slow] | She leaves it with five ensembles, | a waiting list, | and a generation of players who think rehearsing on a Tuesday night in July is *normal*. || [look up] || It is not normal. || It is what she made *possible*. || [held, three seconds] || So tonight, | before the speeches and the cake, | I would ask you to stand, | and to thank her, | *properly*.", "exercise": {"kind": "speak", "task": "Print a two-minute passage from a speech you are giving, mark it as a score (breaths, held stops, one stressed word per sentence, pace notes), and deliver it standing at full voice, looking up for the end of every sentence. Record and time it, and repeat once with every pause doubled.", "seconds": 120}, "checks": ["The end of every sentence was delivered looking at the room, not the page", "Each held stop lasted at least two seconds", "The timed run was within ten seconds of the target length", "You waited for silence before the first word and held still after the last"]}], "types": [{"id": "type-keynote", "name": "Conference paper or keynote", "occasion": "An invited address to music educators at a state or national conference, setting the intellectual tone for the day.", "minutes": [15, 45], "purpose": "Leave a room of expert peers holding one idea they did not walk in with, argued well enough that they can repeat it, test it and act on it on Monday.", "parts": [{"part": "Opening image", "what": "Puts a single concrete scene in front of the room before any claim is made.", "prompt": "What one moment from a rehearsal room contains your whole argument in miniature?", "share": 8}, {"part": "The problem stated", "what": "Names the tension the profession lives with, in terms the room recognises as its own.", "prompt": "What do these people already half-know but have not said aloud?", "share": 12}, {"part": "Thesis", "what": "States the claim once, plainly, in a sentence that could be quoted.", "prompt": "If they remember one sentence, which is it?", "share": 8}, {"part": "Development in three movements", "what": "Builds the argument through practice, evidence and structure, each movement doing a different job.", "prompt": "What three steps take a sceptic from the problem to your thesis?", "share": 42}, {"part": "Concession and limit", "what": "Grants the strongest objection at full strength and draws the boundary of the claim.", "prompt": "What would the cleverest person in the room say against you, and what do you concede?", "share": 12}, {"part": "Return and charge", "what": "Returns to the opening image, transformed, and gives the room something specific to do.", "prompt": "What should they do differently on Monday, and how does the opening scene look now?", "share": 18}], "pitfalls": ["Mistaking coverage for argument: a keynote that surveys the field leaves nothing behind, while one claim developed properly does.", "Spending the opening on thanks and credentials after the chair has already introduced you; acknowledge in a sentence, then begin.", "Citing research you could not defend in questions; one source you know thoroughly outweighs five you have skimmed.", "Ending on a summary of the three points rather than on the image and the charge; an expert room supplies its own summary.", "Writing for the page: nested clauses and long lists that read well and cannot be heard."], "model": {"title": "The Bar Before the Entry", "setting": "A fifteen-minute opening keynote at a state conference of school music educators, delivered from the lectern after a formal introduction by the conference chair.", "paragraphs": [{"text": "Thank you. I want to begin in a rehearsal room, at about twenty past three on a Thursday. A Year 9 bass player has forty-three bars rest. The band is loud, the trumpets are enjoying themselves, and she is counting. Not tapping, not mouthing the numbers: *counting*, | with her whole body still. At bar forty-one she lifts the instrument. At bar forty-three she comes in, | exactly, || and nobody in the room notices. || That is my subject this morning: the musicianship nobody notices, and why our assessment cannot see it.", "note": "Begins with one scene rather than a greeting or a thesis, so the room is listening before it is asked to agree. The held stop after 'nobody in the room notices' lets the paradox land before it is named."}, {"text": "Everyone here knows the shape of the problem. We assess what can be marked: the performance on the day, the aural paper, the analysis essay. Those are real things, and I have marked thousands of them. But the competence that made her entry possible, | sustained attention inside a texture, an internal pulse that survives forty bars of other people's noise, a sense of her own place in a whole, | appears nowhere in a criterion. We teach it every afternoon. || We report it never.", "note": "States the problem in the room's own vocabulary and claims experience in passing rather than as a credential. The list is held between two breaths so it is heard as one long object, and the closing antithesis is set apart by a stop."}, {"text": "So here is the claim, as plainly as I can put it. *The ensemble is not an adjunct to the music curriculum. It is the place where the curriculum's deepest outcomes are actually achieved,* | and until our assessment reflects that, we will keep rewarding the visible and starving the essential.", "note": "The thesis arrives once, in a quotable sentence stressed as a whole. The second clause supplies the stakes so the claim does not sit inert."}, {"text": "Let me make the case in three steps. The first is about what musicians actually do. Ask any working professional what separates a player you want to hire from one you do not, and you will not hear about scales. You will hear about listening, about time, and about whether the person makes the band sound better. I have spent twenty-six years on bandstands in this city, and I have never once been booked because of a theory result. | I have been booked, repeatedly, because I count rests.", "note": "The first movement argues from professional practice and uses his own authority once, briefly. The dry final line earns credibility through understatement rather than claiming it."}, {"text": "The second step is about what students remember. When former students come back, and they do, they do not talk about the composition task in Year 11. They talk about the night the band locked in at the festival, or the rehearsal where the conductor stopped, said nothing for ten seconds, and they finally heard the inner parts. That is not nostalgia. | It is a report from the learner on where the learning happened.", "note": "The second movement shifts from the professional to the learner, so the argument widens rather than repeats. The final sentence converts anecdote into evidence by naming what kind of evidence it is."}, {"text": "The third step is the uncomfortable one. Our timetables and our staffing follow our assessment. What is examined gets classroom hours; what is not gets lunchtimes, early mornings and goodwill. Every one of us has watched an ensemble program survive on the private generosity of a few teachers. | That is not a model of a curriculum. || It is a model of a hobby that happens to be essential.", "note": "The third movement moves from pedagogy to structure, which is where the room's frustration actually lives. The held stop sets up the closing antithesis, 'hobby' against 'essential', which should be delivered slower than anything before it."}, {"text": "Now the obvious objection, and it is a good one. Ensemble achievement is collective. How do you assess an individual inside a group without rewarding passengers, or penalising the student who is carried by a weak section? That is not solved by a rubric written over a weekend. But we already accept considered judgement in other places: the moderated portfolio, the observed practical, the teacher's evidence gathered over time. The question is not whether it can be done. | It is whether we think it is worth the trouble.", "note": "Grants the strongest objection at full strength before answering it, which is what a sceptical expert audience needs to see. The answer does not overclaim; it reframes a technical objection as a question of will."}, {"text": "Let me also be clear about what I am not saying. I am not saying the performance examination should go, or that theory and analysis matter less than we think. They matter a great deal; the best ensemble players I know are formidable analysts. Nor am I saying that every school can staff a full ensemble program tomorrow. I am making a narrower claim, | and I think a harder one to dismiss: that the competences we most value in a musician are being developed in a place our documents treat as optional, || and that this is a design fault, not an accident of history.", "note": "Draws the boundary of the thesis explicitly, which protects it from being caricatured in questions. Narrowing a claim in front of experts makes it stronger, and the final clause restates it in sharper terms."}, {"text": "So I will ask three modest things of you. This term, write down what you actually observe in rehearsal that tells you a student has grown, in words precise enough to put in front of a moderator. Share those words with each other, because that is how criteria begin. And when you sit on a committee, as many of you do, ask where the ensemble lives in the document. | If the answer is 'nowhere', || ask why.", "note": "The charge is concrete, small and achievable, which makes it more likely to happen than a grand appeal. The three requests escalate from private practice to public influence, ending on the one that matters most."}, {"text": "Back to Thursday afternoon. Bar forty-three. The entry nobody noticed. | I would like us to build a curriculum that notices. || Thank you.", "note": "Returns to the opening scene in a handful of short sentences, so the image now carries the whole argument. The last line restates the thesis as an aspiration and stops; there is no summary."}]}}, {"id": "type-council-report", "name": "Report to a school council or board", "occasion": "A formal spoken report to a governing body that must understand performance, risk and the decisions in front of it, within a fixed agenda item.", "minutes": [4, 10], "purpose": "Give members an accurate, proportionate account of performance against what they agreed, surface anything that needs their judgement, and leave a clear resolution or noting on the record.", "parts": [{"part": "Headline", "what": "Gives the bottom line in two sentences so members hear everything after it correctly.", "prompt": "If the council heard only your first thirty seconds, what must they know?", "share": 10}, {"part": "Against the plan", "what": "Reports performance against what the body previously agreed, not against what is convenient to report.", "prompt": "What did the council approve last time, and how did each item actually go?", "share": 30}, {"part": "What did not go well", "what": "States shortfalls and risks plainly, with cause and remedy.", "prompt": "What would a member be annoyed to discover later that you knew tonight?", "share": 20}, {"part": "Matters for judgement", "what": "Separates what the council must decide from what it is simply being told, with a recommendation on each.", "prompt": "What exactly do you need them to resolve, note or endorse, and what do you recommend?", "share": 25}, {"part": "Close", "what": "States the resolution sought in its exact words and invites questions on the paper.", "prompt": "What single sentence should the minutes record?", "share": 15}], "pitfalls": ["Narrating activity instead of reporting outcomes; a governing body needs results against agreed aims, not a diary of the term.", "Placing the risk mid-report so it can later be said to have been mentioned; members notice, and trust in every later report erodes.", "Blurring governance and management: drawing the council into operational detail it should not decide, or withholding something it must.", "Reading the circulated paper aloud; the spoken report should assume the paper has been read and add judgement to it.", "Leaving the decision sought implicit, so the minutes record a discussion rather than a resolution."], "model": {"title": "Chair's Report to Council, Term 4", "setting": "Agenda item 4 at the final school council meeting of the year; the written report was circulated a week earlier and members have read it.", "paragraphs": [{"text": "Thank you. Members have the written report, so I will not read it to you. I will give you the headline, two matters that need your judgement, and one resolution I will ask you to pass tonight. || The headline is this: council did three of the four things it set itself in February, | and the fourth, community consultation, fell short, for reasons I will be candid about.", "note": "Announces the structure and the ask in the first breath, which is a courtesy to members and a discipline for the speaker. The shortfall is admitted in the headline itself, so nothing later can read as a surprise."}, {"text": "Against the plan. We undertook to review progress on the school's business plan priorities at every meeting rather than once a year, and we did, | at all four meetings. We undertook to receive a report from the student council each term; we have received three, and the fourth is on tonight's agenda. And we undertook to review our own procedures. The revised standing orders were adopted in August, and I think members have found our meetings shorter, | and better, for it.", "note": "Reports each commitment in the same form, undertaking then result, so members can check them against February's minutes. The one evaluative remark is brief and is one members can verify from their own experience."}, {"text": "The shortfall. We said we would consult the school community on the draft uniform guidelines, and we received forty-one responses from a community of more than two thousand families. That is not consultation. It is a sample of the already engaged. The cause, I think, was mine: we relied on one newsletter item and one email, in the busiest fortnight of Term 2. || I do not propose that we treat those responses as a mandate.", "note": "States the number, judges it honestly and takes ownership without self-reproach. The final sentence draws the governance consequence, which is what members need from the chair."}, {"text": "That brings me to the two matters for your judgement. The first is whether to reopen the uniform consultation in Term 1, using the student council and the parent association as channels as well as the newsletter. I recommend that we do. The second is a question of scope. A member has asked whether council should receive the detailed budget by cost centre. My view is that council's role is to approve the budget and monitor it against the plan, | not to manage it, || and I would suggest we ask the principal for a one-page summary each term instead. I would welcome other views on both.", "note": "Separates the two matters cleanly and offers a recommendation on each, so discussion has something definite to respond to. The second point marks the line between governance and management without lecturing the member who raised it."}, {"text": "Finally, the resolution. I would ask a member to move that council adopt the 2027 work plan as circulated, including a reopened uniform consultation in Term 1. | If that is agreed, the minutes will record it in those words. I am happy to take questions on anything in the written report.", "note": "Ends with the exact wording sought, so the minute-taker and members know precisely what is being decided. Questions are invited on the paper, which anchors discussion rather than reopening the whole year."}]}}, {"id": "type-submission", "name": "Submission to a committee or panel", "occasion": "An oral submission to a funding panel, review committee or decision-making body, advocating a position or a resource within a strict time limit.", "minutes": [5, 12], "purpose": "Make it easy for the panel to say yes: state the ask, prove the need, meet the panel's own criteria, answer the strongest objection before it is raised, and show exactly what the decision will buy.", "parts": [{"part": "The ask", "what": "States precisely what is sought, for how long and to produce what.", "prompt": "What do you want this panel to decide, in one sentence with a number in it?", "share": 10}, {"part": "The need", "what": "Establishes the problem the ask solves, with evidence the panel could verify.", "prompt": "What is the gap, and how do you know it exists?", "share": 20}, {"part": "The proposal", "what": "Shows what will be done and maps it onto the panel's own criteria, in their language.", "prompt": "Which of the panel's stated criteria does this meet, and where exactly?", "share": 25}, {"part": "The objection answered", "what": "Raises the panel's likely reservation in fair terms and resolves it.", "prompt": "What is the reason they would say no, and what is your answer?", "share": 20}, {"part": "Accountability", "what": "Shows how success will be measured, reported and, if necessary, judged a failure.", "prompt": "How will they know in a year that their decision was right?", "share": 15}, {"part": "The ask restated", "what": "Closes on the ask, in identical terms.", "prompt": "What words do you want them to carry into deliberation?", "share": 10}], "pitfalls": ["Advocating the cause rather than the proposal; the panel is already sympathetic to music and needs reasons to fund this, not reasons to care.", "Ignoring the panel's criteria and vocabulary, which forces members to translate your case into their framework themselves, usually unfavourably.", "Inflating the need with figures you cannot source; one challenged number taints every other one.", "Leaving the obvious objection for questions, where you will answer it defensively instead of on your own terms.", "Softening or changing the ask at the close in response to resistance you have imagined."], "model": {"title": "Regional Ensemble Residencies", "setting": "A seven-minute oral submission to a state arts funding panel on behalf of the schools' jazz festival association, followed by questions from the panel.", "paragraphs": [{"text": "Thank you, Chair. The association is asking the panel for forty-eight thousand dollars over two years to fund eight three-day ensemble residencies in regional schools, | four each year, led by professional jazz musicians, and culminating in performance at the state festival. || That is the whole of the request, and I will spend the next six minutes showing why it deserves funding.", "note": "Opens with the complete ask, amount, period and output, so the panel hears everything after it as justification. The held stop separates the ask from the case."}, {"text": "The need. Last year the festival received entries from one hundred and twelve ensembles. Nine came from schools outside the metropolitan area. Those nine schools told us the same thing in different words: they have students who want to play this music, and no one within three hundred kilometres who can teach the style. The regional music teacher is often the only one in the school, and frequently a generalist from a brass or choral background. | The gap is not enthusiasm. || It is access to people who do this for a living.", "note": "Gives two figures the association holds and can verify, and lets the ratio argue without commentary. The closing antithesis names the precise gap, which forestalls the response that sheet music and a video would do."}, {"text": "The proposal. Each residency places two professional musicians in a regional school for three days. They rehearse the ensemble, take sectionals, and spend one session with the music teacher alone, on repertoire, rhythm section set-up and rehearsal technique. That last session is the one I would ask you to notice, because it is what remains when the musicians leave. Your guidelines ask for benefit sustained beyond the funded period. | *This is where it lives.*", "note": "Describes the activity concretely, then maps it directly onto the panel's own criterion in the panel's own words. The short stressed sentence makes the match impossible to miss."}, {"text": "I expect the panel will ask, reasonably, why three days in a school should change anything. It is a fair question to put to any short intervention. Our answer is the structure around the three days. Each school commits in writing to enter the festival that year; the festival waives the entry fee, and the association contributes to travel. So the residency is not an event. || It is the first rehearsal of a season that ends on a stage in Perth, in front of their peers.", "note": "Raises the panel's likeliest reservation in its own terms and calls it reasonable, which disarms it. The answer is structural rather than rhetorical, and the closing image gives the panel something to remember in deliberation."}, {"text": "Accountability. We will report to the panel on four measures: the number of students reached; the number of residency schools that enter the festival; the number that enter again the following year without support; and the teachers' own account of what they now do differently, gathered through a short structured interview. The third measure is the honest one. If schools do not come back without us, | we will tell you, || and we will not ask for a third year.", "note": "Offers measures the panel can hold the association to, and singles out the hardest. Volunteering a failure condition is the strongest signal of seriousness a submitter can give."}, {"text": "The costs are set out on page four of the written application: musicians' fees at award rates, travel and accommodation, and a small amount for parts. There is no administration charge; the association's volunteers carry that. | So the request, again: forty-eight thousand dollars, over two years, for eight residencies and the festival season each one begins. Thank you. I am happy to take the panel's questions.", "note": "Refers to the written detail rather than reciting it, and makes the one cost point panels always look for. The ask is restated in identical terms so the panel deliberates on exactly what was proposed."}]}}, {"id": "type-festival-opening", "name": "Opening a festival", "occasion": "A short formal address that declares a festival open, usually to a large mixed audience of students, families, adjudicators, sponsors and officials.", "minutes": [3, 6], "purpose": "Mark the threshold: give the event its meaning and its scale, honour the people who made it possible, set the spirit in which it should be played, and get out of the way of the music.", "parts": [{"part": "Acknowledgement", "what": "Acknowledges Country and welcomes those who must be welcomed, efficiently and sincerely.", "prompt": "Who must be acknowledged, in what order, and who can be welcomed collectively?", "share": 12}, {"part": "What this is", "what": "Gives the occasion its meaning in one idea rather than a history.", "prompt": "What is this festival for, in a sentence a fourteen-year-old would remember?", "share": 20}, {"part": "The image of scale", "what": "Makes the size and effort of the event vivid through one or two concrete details.", "prompt": "What single fact shows what it took to get everyone here?", "share": 15}, {"part": "Thanks", "what": "Thanks those whose work is invisible, collectively and with one specific detail.", "prompt": "Whose work would otherwise go unseen, and what did they actually do?", "share": 13}, {"part": "To the performers", "what": "Speaks directly to the students, setting the spirit in which they should play and listen.", "prompt": "What do you want the youngest player here to carry onto the stage?", "share": 30}, {"part": "Declaration", "what": "Opens the festival in a clear ceremonial sentence and hands over at once.", "prompt": "What is the exact sentence of declaration, and what immediately follows it?", "share": 10}], "pitfalls": ["Turning the opening into a roll call of thanks the audience endures rather than hears; name few, thank many collectively.", "Recounting the festival's history at length; one sentence of lineage is enough for an audience that came for music.", "Addressing the officials and sponsors in the front row while eight hundred students sit behind them.", "A declaration that trails off or is buried mid-sentence, so the applause does not know where to land.", "Running over time on a day where every minute is taken from a performing ensemble's schedule."], "model": {"title": "Opening the Schools' Jazz Festival", "setting": "The formal opening of a three-day state schools' jazz festival, delivered from the stage of a concert hall to students, families, adjudicators and guests immediately before the first ensemble plays.", "paragraphs": [{"text": "Good morning. I would like to begin by acknowledging the Whadjuk Noongar people, on whose land we gather, and to pay my respects to their elders, past and present. This country carried music long before any of it was written down, and it is right that a music festival begins by saying so. | I also welcome our adjudicators, our guests, and the teachers and families who got everyone here before nine o'clock on a Friday.", "note": "Gives the acknowledgement a sentence of its own meaning so it is not heard as formula, then welcomes groups rather than individuals. The light final clause warms the room without a joke being announced."}, {"text": "This festival exists for one reason. It is the place where young musicians in this state play this music for each other. Not for a mark, though there will be comments. Not for a prize, though there will be awards. | *For each other.* || Over three days you will hear more than a hundred ensembles, and the best thing that can happen to you this weekend is to hear a band from a school you have never heard of play something that sends you home to practise.", "note": "States the purpose in one idea, set up by two concessions and resolved by repetition. The second half turns the purpose into a concrete hope the students will recognise."}, {"text": "To give you a sense of scale: by Sunday evening more than two thousand students will have walked across this stage. Somewhere in this building there is a spreadsheet with every one of those names and every changeover timed to the minute, and a volunteer who has been looking at it since July. | The adjudicators have between them heard more school bands than anyone in the country should, and they will write something useful about every one. The association's committee, our volunteers, the venue crew and the stage managers are the reason this works, || and I would ask you to thank them now.", "note": "Conveys scale with one figure and one human image rather than a catalogue. The thanks are collective and invite applause, which also gives the speaker a natural breath before turning to the students."}, {"text": "And to the players. Some of you are about to perform in public for the first time; some of you have done this every year since Year 7. Here is what I would say to all of you, as someone who has been walking onto stages in this city for twenty-six years. *Listen* more than you play. Count the rests. When your solo comes, play one idea and mean it. If something goes wrong, keep going; nobody in the audience will remember the mistake, but they will remember how you recovered. And when the band before you sounds good, || let them hear you cheer.", "note": "Speaks directly to the students and invokes his own standing once, briefly, to earn the advice. Four short imperatives build to the last, which is set off by a held stop because it is the real point of the festival."}, {"text": "It is my great pleasure to declare this year's festival open. || Please welcome our first ensemble.", "note": "The declaration stands as a sentence of its own, followed by a held stop so the applause has a place to land. The immediate handover keeps the day moving and removes any temptation to keep talking."}]}}, {"id": "type-concert-address", "name": "Concert address (opening and closing)", "occasion": "The two short spoken frames of a school concert: a welcome before the music and a close that thanks, names what happened and sends the audience home.", "minutes": [2, 5], "purpose": "Open: settle the hall, give the evening one frame and start the music cleanly. Close: honour the people the audience cannot see, name what the evening meant, and send everyone home on the students rather than on administration.", "parts": [{"part": "Opening welcome", "what": "Settles the hall, acknowledges Country and welcomes guests.", "prompt": "What do people need to hear in the first twenty seconds to stop talking and settle?", "share": 15}, {"part": "The evening's frame", "what": "Gives the audience one thing to listen for.", "prompt": "What links tonight's program, or what one thing should they listen for?", "share": 20}, {"part": "Practicalities and handover", "what": "Covers phones and exits in plain sentences and hands over to the first ensemble.", "prompt": "What must be said, and what is the shortest way to say it?", "share": 10}, {"part": "Closing thanks", "what": "Thanks specific unseen groups with one concrete detail each.", "prompt": "Whose work would the audience never see unless you named it?", "share": 25}, {"part": "What tonight meant", "what": "Names, briefly, what the audience has just witnessed.", "prompt": "What happened tonight that was not printed in the program?", "share": 20}, {"part": "Send-off", "what": "Ends cleanly and gives the last moment to the musicians.", "prompt": "What is your last sentence, and what happens immediately after it?", "share": 10}], "pitfalls": ["Front-loading the thanks at the opening when the audience wants music, leaving nothing fresh for the close.", "Explaining the music so thoroughly that the audience listens for the commentary instead of the piece.", "Closing with a thank-you list read from a page, so the evening ends on administration.", "Letting the address drift towards the director's own work rather than the students and their families.", "Improvising the closing words; they are what the audience walks out with, and should be chosen in advance."], "model": {"title": "Winter Concert: Welcome and Close", "setting": "The opening and closing words at a school's winter concert of concert bands, string orchestras and jazz ensembles, spoken from the side of the stage; the first three paragraphs open the concert and the last four close it.", "paragraphs": [{"text": "Good evening, and welcome. I acknowledge the Whadjuk Noongar people, the traditional custodians of this land, and pay my respects to their elders. A particular welcome to our principal, to members of the school council, and to the grandparents I can see who have driven a very long way. | Tonight you will hear three hundred and forty students in eleven ensembles, || and they are rather keen to start.", "note": "Settles the hall with the essentials and one warm, specific welcome. The final figure and the dry understatement tell the audience the music is close."}, {"text": "If I could offer one thing to listen for, it is the passing of a melody. In almost every piece tonight a tune begins in one section and is handed to another: from the clarinets to the violas, from the trumpets to the saxophones. Watch for the moment it changes hands, and for the players who have just let it go and are now listening, very carefully, to someone else. || That is what rehearsal is for.", "note": "Gives one listening idea instead of program notes, which deepens attention without lecturing. The final short line, after a held stop, quietly tells families what their children's term has been about."}, {"text": "Phones on silent, please, and photographs without flash. Exits are at the back and on either side. To begin, Concert Band 3, | with their conductor.", "note": "Dispatches the practicalities in plain sentences, without apology. The breath before the conductor gives the applause its cue."}, {"text": "Thank you. Before you go, a few people who will not take a bow. Our instrumental teachers, who have been hearing these pieces one bar at a time since February. The parent volunteers who ran the interval kiosk and will be stacking chairs at ten o'clock. The students on the lighting desk, who learned the cue sheet in an afternoon. And our families, who drive to rehearsals at seven in the morning, || in winter.", "note": "Thanks four groups, each with one concrete detail instead of an adjective. The held stop before 'in winter' lets the audience's recognition do the work."}, {"text": "What you heard tonight was not only a series of performances. It was three hundred and forty young people agreeing, for two hours, to listen to each other with complete attention. That is rarer than it sounds, | at any age, || and they did it beautifully.", "note": "Names what the evening meant in one idea families can carry home. The breath and the stop slow the delivery so the praise is heard as a considered judgement rather than routine."}, {"text": "For our Year 12 musicians, this was their last winter concert. Some of them joined the program as Year 7s who could barely hold their instruments; tonight several of them led their sections, and two of them conducted. We will miss them, | and we will be listening for them, || wherever they play next. Would the Year 12s please stand.", "note": "Marks a rite of passage briefly and concretely, with two facts that show growth rather than an adjective that claims it. Asking them to stand gives families a moment of recognition without a further speech."}, {"text": "Please join me in one last round of applause for every musician on this stage. | Safe home, and goodnight.", "note": "Ends on the students and a short farewell, with nothing administrative after it. The audience leaves on applause rather than on a reminder about next term."}]}}, {"id": "type-award-acceptance", "name": "Award acceptance", "occasion": "Receiving an honour from a professional body, institution or community, usually immediately after a citation has been read.", "minutes": [1, 4], "purpose": "Receive the honour with grace, share credit truthfully without false modesty, say one thing worth hearing, and sit down.", "parts": [{"part": "Thanks to the giver", "what": "Thanks the body making the award, briefly and specifically.", "prompt": "Who gave this, and what about their purpose or process can you honour?", "share": 15}, {"part": "Lineage", "what": "Places the honour among those who received it before, which is humility without self-deprecation.", "prompt": "Who has held this before, and what do you owe them?", "share": 15}, {"part": "The credit shared", "what": "Names the groups and conditions that made the work possible.", "prompt": "Without whom would this genuinely not have happened?", "share": 25}, {"part": "One idea", "what": "Offers a single thought that gives the moment meaning beyond the speaker.", "prompt": "What is the one thing you believe about this work that this room should hear?", "share": 35}, {"part": "Close", "what": "Ends with gratitude and a clean exit line.", "prompt": "What is your last sentence?", "share": 10}], "pitfalls": ["False modesty that protests so much it draws attention back to the speaker.", "An unedited list of names, which honours no one in particular and loses the room by the fifth.", "Using the platform for a grievance or a policy pitch the occasion did not invite.", "Running long because the moment has made time disappear; write it to two minutes and keep it there."], "model": {"title": "On Receiving an Award for Service to Music Education", "setting": "Acceptance of a state professional association's award for service to music education at its annual dinner, immediately after a citation read by the president.", "paragraphs": [{"text": "Thank you, President, and thank you to the association. I know how these awards are decided: by colleagues, after argument, | which is exactly why this one matters to me.", "note": "Thanks the giver specifically and compliments the process rather than the recipient. The dry final clause sets the register for the whole speech."}, {"text": "I also know some of the names on the list of previous recipients. Several of them taught the teachers who taught my students, and one or two of them, in my early years, | gently told me I was wrong. They were right. To be added to that list is an honour I did not expect.", "note": "Places the speaker within a lineage, the most graceful form of humility because it honours others rather than diminishing oneself. The self-deprecation is concrete and brief."}, {"text": "Nothing in that citation was done alone. It was done with instrumental teachers who say yes to an extra rehearsal they are not paid for; with a principal who backed a program when the numbers were against it; and with families who have trusted us with their children's evenings and weekends for years. And it was done, mostly, by students, who did the practice. I accept this on their behalf, || and I will tell them on Monday.", "note": "Shares credit among three groups and then the students, avoiding a list of individuals. The final line adds a specific, human promise that makes the deflection believable."}, {"text": "I have spent most of my working life moving between two rooms: the classroom in the afternoon and the bandstand at night. For a long time I thought of them as two jobs. I no longer do. What the bandstand teaches, that you are responsible for the time and for the people around you, is exactly what I try to teach in the classroom, | and what the classroom teaches, patience and the long view, || has made me a better player.", "note": "Offers a brief personal reflection that earns the idea to come, without autobiography. The parallel structure of the final sentence makes the two rooms mirror each other audibly."}, {"text": "If I may say one thing. Music in schools is still sometimes described as an enrichment, something added to the real work. Everyone in this room knows otherwise. A rehearsal is one of the few places in a school where a young person learns that their attention has consequences for other people, | in real time, with no second take. It is where they learn to listen to someone they did not choose, and to make something with others that none of them could make alone. *That* is not enrichment. || It is an education.", "note": "Offers a single idea framed as something the room already knows, which includes the audience rather than instructing it. The stressed 'That' and the short final sentence carry the point."}, {"text": "Thank you again, to the association and to the colleagues who put my name forward without telling me. I am very grateful, | and I will try to deserve it.", "note": "Closes in two plain sentences with no reprise. Ending on obligation rather than triumph is the classic note for an acceptance."}]}}, {"id": "type-award-citation", "name": "Presenting an award or citation", "occasion": "Conferring an award, life membership or other honour on someone, by reading a citation before the presentation.", "minutes": [2, 5], "purpose": "Make the room understand why this person, specifically, deserves this honour, so that the applause is for the person and not for the ritual.", "parts": [{"part": "The honour", "what": "States what the award is and what it recognises.", "prompt": "What does this honour stand for, and what is it not given for?", "share": 12}, {"part": "The portrait", "what": "Shows the recipient through checkable facts and one characteristic habit.", "prompt": "What did they actually do, and what habit shows who they are?", "share": 30}, {"part": "The scene", "what": "Gives one concrete moment that reveals character better than any adjective.", "prompt": "What is the one moment that, told plainly, shows this person entirely?", "share": 20}, {"part": "The significance", "what": "Names what the work has meant to other people.", "prompt": "Who is different because of this person, and how?", "share": 23}, {"part": "Conferral", "what": "Builds to the name and formally confers the honour, then hands over the stage.", "prompt": "What is the exact sentence of conferral, and where does the name fall in it?", "share": 15}], "pitfalls": ["A curriculum vitae read aloud: dates and positions without a single scene that shows the person.", "Superlatives in place of evidence; 'tireless' and 'passionate' tell the room nothing it believes.", "Making the citation about the presenter's friendship with the recipient rather than the recipient's work.", "Airing private material the recipient would not want made public, however affectionately meant.", "Losing the moment of conferral in shuffled papers or a swallowed name."], "model": {"title": "Life Membership Citation", "setting": "Conferring life membership of the schools' jazz festival association on a long-serving adjudicator and teacher at the association's annual general meeting; her name is withheld until the conferral.", "paragraphs": [{"text": "Life membership is the highest honour this association can give. It is not awarded for long service alone, though there is plenty of that in this room. It is awarded to someone without whom the association would be recognisably a different thing. | This year the committee was unanimous that such a person is here tonight.", "note": "Defines the honour by what it is not, which raises the bar and the room's attention together. The unanimous committee gives the citation institutional weight before anything about the person is said."}, {"text": "She began teaching in a country high school with a band of eleven students and one working saxophone. She came to the city, built two school jazz programs from nothing, and has adjudicated at this festival for twenty-two consecutive years. By our estimate she has written something over four thousand adjudication sheets. I have read a good number of them. | Every one begins with something the band did well, || and every one ends with a single thing to practise.", "note": "Builds the portrait from specific facts in ascending scale, then turns from quantity to quality. The closing sentence describes a habit, which reveals character more convincingly than praise."}, {"text": "One scene, if I may. Some years ago, at the end of a long Saturday, a junior band from a small school played a ballad very badly and very bravely. Their teacher was close to tears. She came down from the adjudicators' table, which we do not normally do, and spent ten minutes with the rhythm section showing them how to play softly. | That school has entered every year since.", "note": "One concrete scene does the work of a paragraph of tributes. The consequence, stated flatly at the end, turns anecdote into significance."}, {"text": "She is not, it should be said, a soft marker. Directors who have received her sheets will know the phrase 'the time was negotiable', and some of us have had it written about our own bands. But her severity has always been in the service of the students. She has never written a comment a fourteen-year-old could not act on the following week, | and she has never written one to be clever. || In twenty-two years, that is a remarkable discipline.", "note": "Adds a true and slightly sharp edge to the portrait, so the warmth is credible. The quoted phrase lets the room recognise her voice, and the final sentences turn severity into a virtue."}, {"text": "There are teachers across this state, some of them in this room, who learned how to run a rhythm section from her written comments, and former students, now professionals, who still quote them. That is what life membership is meant to recognise: | a contribution that has gone into other people, || and stayed there.", "note": "Moves from the person to her effect on others, which is the true ground of the honour. The definition of the award returns, now filled with evidence."}, {"text": "It is my privilege, on behalf of the committee and the members, to confer life membership of the association on || Ann. | Ann, would you please come up?", "note": "The held stop before the name gives the room its moment of recognition and the applause its cue. Inviting her up by name hands over the stage cleanly."}]}}, {"id": "type-valedictory", "name": "Valedictory or farewell to a colleague", "occasion": "A farewell at a staff function or assembly for a colleague who is retiring or leaving after long service.", "minutes": [4, 8], "purpose": "Honour a career truthfully and warmly, let colleagues recognise the person they worked with, and let the person leaving feel seen rather than eulogised.", "parts": [{"part": "Opening", "what": "Sets a warm, light register through an image everyone shares.", "prompt": "What is the first thing everyone in the room associates with this person?", "share": 12}, {"part": "The career in brief", "what": "Gives the shape of the career in a few accurate facts.", "prompt": "What three or four facts show the arc of their work?", "share": 18}, {"part": "The method", "what": "Shows how they worked, through a habit or principle colleagues will recognise.", "prompt": "What did they do, every week, that nobody else did quite the same way?", "share": 25}, {"part": "A moment of friction", "what": "Acknowledges, with good humour, a time they disagreed with you or the institution and were right.", "prompt": "When did they tell you something you did not want to hear?", "share": 15}, {"part": "The legacy", "what": "Names what will remain after they leave, in terms colleagues can verify.", "prompt": "What will still be true in the department in five years because of them?", "share": 15}, {"part": "The farewell", "what": "Speaks to the person directly and leads the room in a shared action.", "prompt": "What do you want to say to them, directly, as the last thing?", "share": 15}], "pitfalls": ["Writing a eulogy for a living person: solemnity and summary where warmth and specificity are wanted.", "In-jokes that delight six people and exclude sixty, including the family.", "Ignoring a difficult truth everyone knows, such as a hard final year or a reluctant departure; acknowledge it with tact or leave it entirely alone, never half-mention it.", "Drifting into the department's future needs or the replacement, which turns a farewell into a staffing announcement.", "Forgetting to speak to the person directly before the end."], "model": {"title": "Farewell to a Woodwind Teacher", "setting": "A farewell at the end-of-year staff function for an instrumental woodwind teacher retiring after thirty-one years, delivered with members of her family present.", "paragraphs": [{"text": "If you have walked past the woodwind room after four o'clock at any point in the last thirty-one years, you will have heard a reed being scraped, a clarinet student being told to breathe lower, and Ruth laughing. Those three sounds have been the background of this department for longer than some of our staff have been alive. || From next year, | we are going to have to get used to the quiet.", "note": "Opens with a sound image every colleague recognises, which makes the speech about her from the first sentence. The stop and the final line move gently from humour to feeling."}, {"text": "The facts first, because she would want them accurate. Ruth joined the instrumental music service in the mid-nineties, after ten years as a freelance clarinettist. She has taught here longer than anywhere else and, by her own count, more than six hundred students. She has prepared the woodwinds for every major concert we have given, played second clarinet in the staff band whenever we were short, and never once, | that I know of, | let a student leave with a split reed.", "note": "Gives the career in a handful of facts, with a nod to her own precision. The last fact becomes a characteristic habit, softened by the qualifying breaths, and draws a smile."}, {"text": "What the facts do not show is how she taught. Ruth believed a student should leave every lesson able to do one thing they could not do when they walked in. Not five things: one. She would find it in the first two minutes, spend the rest of the lesson on it, and the student always knew what it was. I have watched her do that with a nervous Year 7 and with a student preparing for a conservatorium audition, || and it was exactly the same lesson.", "note": "Moves from facts to method, which is where a teacher's character really lives. The observation that both lessons were the same is the speech's central compliment, delivered as observation rather than praise."}, {"text": "She was also, it should be said, entirely unafraid of me. In my first year in charge of the department I proposed a change that would have moved every woodwind sectional to lunchtime. Ruth came to my office, closed the door, and explained, | kindly and in some detail, | why I was wrong. I changed it. She was right, and I have been grateful for that conversation ever since.", "note": "Admits a moment of friction with good humour and generosity, which makes the warmth elsewhere credible. A senior colleague's self-deprecation honours the person leaving."}, {"text": "What remains when she goes is the method. At least four of our current instrumental teachers learned it from her, and you can hear it in their lessons. The woodwind section of our top concert band plays with a blend that adjudicators keep remarking on. That blend is not an accident. | It is thirty-one years of one thing at a time.", "note": "Names the legacy in terms colleagues can hear and verify for themselves. The closing phrase echoes her method and compresses the whole career into a line."}, {"text": "Ruth, on behalf of everyone in this room and the six hundred who are not: thank you. Enjoy the quiet. | We will try to keep the reeds in order. || Would you all please raise a glass to Ruth.", "note": "Speaks to her directly and calls back to the opening image, closing the circle. The toast gives the room a collective action, so the speech ends in shared feeling rather than on the speaker."}]}}, {"id": "type-eulogy", "name": "Eulogy", "occasion": "A tribute at a funeral or memorial service, usually at the family's request and alongside other speakers.", "minutes": [5, 9], "purpose": "Honour a life truthfully, give the mourners a portrait they recognise, speak to grief without managing it, and leave them with something of the person to carry.", "parts": [{"part": "Opening", "what": "Establishes who is speaking and in what relationship, and steadies the room.", "prompt": "In what capacity are you speaking, and what is the first true thing you can say?", "share": 10}, {"part": "The life", "what": "Gives the shape of the life in a few facts, chosen for meaning rather than completeness.", "prompt": "Which facts of the life would they themselves have chosen?", "share": 20}, {"part": "The person", "what": "Shows character through scenes, habits and, where possible, their own words.", "prompt": "What did they do or say that nobody else would have?", "share": 25}, {"part": "The true flaw", "what": "Admits, affectionately, something less than perfect, so the portrait is recognisable.", "prompt": "What would the family laugh at in recognition?", "share": 12}, {"part": "The loss", "what": "Names the grief honestly through one precise image, without hollow consolation.", "prompt": "What exactly is missing now?", "share": 13}, {"part": "What remains", "what": "Locates what continues in the people present.", "prompt": "What of them is still in this room?", "share": 13}, {"part": "Farewell", "what": "Says goodbye directly, in very few words.", "prompt": "What is the last thing you want to say to them?", "share": 7}], "pitfalls": ["Making the eulogy about the speaker's grief or relationship rather than the person who has died.", "Sanitising the person into a saint the family does not recognise; a true flaw lovingly told honours them more.", "Offering consolations of belief the family may not share; speak to what everyone present can hold.", "Losing composure at an unrehearsed passage; rehearse aloud until the hardest sentence can be spoken.", "Running long when other speakers follow; agree the time with the family and keep to it."], "model": {"title": "Eulogy for a Pianist", "setting": "A eulogy at the funeral of a jazz pianist and longtime bandmate, given at the family's request as the second of three speakers.", "paragraphs": [{"text": "My name is Dijon. I played bass with Peter for twenty-two years, in more rooms in this city than either of us could remember, and I am here because his family asked me to speak for the musicians. It is a privilege I would very much rather not have. || So let me try to do it properly.", "note": "Identifies the speaker's role and relationship at once, as mourners need. The plain admission of reluctance is truthful and steadies both speaker and room before the portrait begins."}, {"text": "Peter was born in Kalgoorlie, the youngest of four, and learned the piano in the back room of his grandmother's house, on an instrument that had, according to him, eleven working notes in the bottom octave. He studied in Perth, played on cruise ships for two years, which he called the best and worst education available, and came home to be, for thirty years, one of the pianists everyone in this town wanted on the gig. He taught hundreds of students. He was married to Helen for thirty-four years. | He was very proud of his daughters, || and slightly embarrassed by how proud.", "note": "Selects facts that carry character, in his own phrasing where possible. The last line moves from biography to affection without announcing the shift."}, {"text": "What was he like to play with? He listened more closely than anyone I have known. He would hear a note I was not sure of, | and on the next chorus he would build a chord underneath it so that it sounded as if I had meant it. He did that for everyone. Nervous singers, young players sitting in for the first time: Peter made them sound like the musicians they were about to become. || I don't think most of them ever knew.", "note": "Shows his character through a musical habit every musician present will recognise and every non-musician can follow. The short final line carries the quiet generosity of it."}, {"text": "He was not a saint. He was late, reliably, for everything except the downbeat. He held opinions about drummers that I will not repeat in a chapel. And he once stopped a tune in the middle of a wedding reception to tell the room that the piano was a crime, | which it was. Helen tells me he was exactly the same at home, and that she would not have changed any of it.", "note": "Includes affectionate, true flaws, which makes the portrait recognisable and gives the room permission to laugh. Anchoring the humour in his widow's own words keeps it safe for the family."}, {"text": "There is a particular silence on a bandstand when a tune ends and nobody has yet decided what to play next. For twenty-two years I could look across that silence, and Peter would already know. || I don't know what to do with that silence now. | I don't think I am meant to know yet.", "note": "Names the loss through one precise image from their shared life rather than general statements about grief. Refusing false consolation is heard by mourners as honesty."}, {"text": "But I do know this. Every musician here who learned to comp from him, every student who learned that you play *for* the soloist and not at them, is carrying a piece of how he played. You will hear it on bandstands in this city for a long time. And when you do, | that will be Peter.", "note": "Locates what remains in the people present, a consolation everyone can hold regardless of belief. The stress on 'for' carries his whole musical ethic in one word."}, {"text": "Thank you, Peter, for the chords under my wrong notes. || Goodnight, old friend.", "note": "Speaks to him directly in very few words, recalling the central image of the speech. Short sentences are easier to speak through emotion, and stronger for it."}]}}, {"id": "type-toast", "name": "Toast", "occasion": "A short raised-glass tribute at a dinner, celebration or anniversary.", "minutes": [1, 3], "purpose": "Lift the room, honour the subject in a way everyone recognises, turn once to sincerity, and bring everyone to their feet with a clear call.", "parts": [{"part": "Opening hook", "what": "Catches the room with something specific and light.", "prompt": "What one detail will make everyone smile in recognition?", "share": 20}, {"part": "The tribute", "what": "Says why this person, group or occasion deserves the glass.", "prompt": "What is the single thing you most want to honour?", "share": 40}, {"part": "The turn", "what": "Moves from wit to feeling in one or two plain sentences.", "prompt": "What is the sincere sentence underneath the humour?", "share": 20}, {"part": "The call", "what": "Asks the room to stand and names exactly what they drink to.", "prompt": "What are the exact words of the toast?", "share": 20}], "pitfalls": ["Running past three minutes; a toast that needs notes has become a speech.", "Humour at the subject's expense that works for the speaker and costs the person honoured.", "No turn to sincerity, so the toast entertains and leaves nothing behind.", "An unclear call, so half the room raises a glass while the other half is still listening."], "model": {"title": "To the Founders", "setting": "A toast at the dinner marking the thirtieth anniversary of the schools' jazz festival association, with several founding committee members present.", "paragraphs": [{"text": "Thirty years ago, a group of teachers met in a staffroom after school and decided, with what I can only call magnificent optimism, that this state needed a jazz festival for students. The minutes of that meeting survive. They record the decision, a budget of four hundred dollars, and a note that refreshments were not provided. || Tonight, I am pleased to report, | refreshments have been provided.", "note": "Opens with a specific detail the founders will recognise and the room will enjoy. The held stop gives the punchline its timing."}, {"text": "In those thirty years the festival has grown from eleven bands in a borrowed school hall to more than a hundred ensembles across three days in a concert venue, and the budget has grown rather faster, | as your treasurer can confirm with some feeling. What has not changed is the idea in that first meeting: that young people should play this music for each other, and that teachers would give up their weekends to make it happen. Several of the people who had that idea are here tonight. || Some of them are still giving up their weekends.", "note": "Delivers the tribute in one movement, growth then continuity, with a self-deprecating aside about his own office. The last line lands lightly because the room knows it is true."}, {"text": "I will not embarrass anyone by name, but I am told that for the first four festivals the entire sound system travelled in the boot of one founder's station wagon, that the program was photocopied in another founder's school office after hours, | and that the first adjudicators were paid in sandwiches. || I am also told the sandwiches were very good.", "note": "One accumulating sentence of specific, affectionate detail honours the founders' labour without naming anyone. The second punchline, held back by a stop, keeps the register light just before the turn."}, {"text": "Every student who has walked onto that stage, and there are tens of thousands of them now, owes something to a decision made over cold tea in a staffroom. Some of them are teachers themselves now, standing in front of their own bands on festival weekend. Somewhere tonight, in a practice room or a bedroom in this state, a student is working on a solo for next year's festival, without the faintest idea that it exists because a handful of teachers once decided it should. Most of them will never know your names. | I suspect you would prefer it that way. || But this room knows them, and this room is grateful.", "note": "The turn to sincerity, stated plainly. The final line honours the founders' modesty rather than embarrassing them with praise."}, {"text": "So would you please stand, and raise your glasses. || To the founders, | and to the next thirty years.", "note": "A clear instruction to stand, a held stop while the room rises, then a two-part toast with a breath between. It is short enough for everyone to say back together."}]}}, {"id": "type-panel-remarks", "name": "Panel remarks (four-minute position statement)", "occasion": "An opening statement on a conference or public panel, where each panellist has a fixed short time before moderated discussion.", "minutes": [3, 5], "purpose": "Stake out a clear, defensible position distinct from the other panellists, give the moderator something to work with, and set up the discussion you want to have.", "parts": [{"part": "Position", "what": "States the answer to the panel's question, with its condition, in the first thirty seconds.", "prompt": "What is your answer to the question, in one sentence?", "share": 15}, {"part": "Ground", "what": "Gives the strongest reason, preferably as a counterfactual the room can test.", "prompt": "What is the strongest reason, and what happens if you are ignored?", "share": 20}, {"part": "Distinction", "what": "Separates your view from the predictable answer on your own side.", "prompt": "How does your view differ from the comfortable version of your own position?", "share": 20}, {"part": "Concession", "what": "Grants what is true in the opposing view, specifically.", "prompt": "What does the other side get right?", "share": 20}, {"part": "Proposal and handover", "what": "Ends with a concrete proposal and a question the discussion can take up.", "prompt": "What should actually change, and what do you want the panel to argue about next?", "share": 25}], "pitfalls": ["Using the four minutes to survey the issue, so the moderator cannot tell what you think.", "Over-running into colleagues' time; a panellist who goes over loses the room's goodwill before discussion begins.", "Repeating the previous speaker because the prepared text cannot adapt; keep one paragraph you can cut and one sentence you can add.", "Taking an extreme position for effect and then retreating under the first hard question.", "Ending on a summary instead of a provocation the discussion can pick up."], "model": {"title": "Selection Is a Debt", "setting": "A four-minute opening statement on a conference panel asked whether selective specialist music programs in public schools are defensible, alongside a policy academic, a principal and a parent advocate.", "paragraphs": [{"text": "My answer is yes, | but only on a condition that most selective programs, including at times my own, do not fully meet. A public school that selects students for a specialist program is taking something from the public: a place, a resource, a teacher's time, that could have gone elsewhere. *Selection is a debt.* || The program is defensible to exactly the extent that it repays it.", "note": "States the position at once, with its condition, and includes his own program in the critique, which buys credibility with a sceptical panel. The stressed four-word thesis gives the moderator and the room a handle."}, {"text": "Why yes at all? Because the alternative is not equity. Without selective public programs, serious ensemble training does not disappear; it goes to the families who can buy it privately. For a good number of the students I have taught, the specialist program was the only route into a full ensemble that their families could ever have reached. Take away selection and those students do not get a fairer system. || They get no system.", "note": "Grounds the position in a counterfactual, which persuades more than an abstract defence. The closing antithesis is short enough to be quoted back during discussion."}, {"text": "But I want to distinguish this from the comfortable version of the argument, which says selective programs are good because excellence is good. That is true, and it is not enough. A program that selects the strongest players, gives them the best rehearsal slots and sends them to festivals, and does nothing else, | is running a private music school on the public payroll.", "note": "Separates his view from the predictable answer on his own side, so his position is distinct on the panel. The breath sets up a sharp final phrase."}, {"text": "The critics are right about several things. Auditions favour children who have already had lessons, and those children are not evenly distributed across postcodes. Programs do concentrate staffing. And it is fair to ask why a twelve-year-old's musical potential should be judged on a single afternoon. I do not think any of that is answered by abolishing selection. | I think it is answered by changing what selection obliges.", "note": "Concedes three specific criticisms, which shows he has heard the other side at its strongest. The last two sentences turn concession into a pivot to his proposal."}, {"text": "Let me give you a picture of what that looks like when it works. Each week, a group of senior students from a selective program takes a bus to a primary school with no music specialist and runs a lunchtime band of thirty children. The seniors arrange the parts themselves, and they are, frankly, better teachers by the end of the year than some graduates. Three of those primary children auditioned for the program the following year. Two were offered places. Neither had ever had a private lesson. | That is the debt being repaid, || and it costs very little.", "note": "Gives one concrete, generic example of repayment, so the proposal that follows is heard as practical rather than aspirational. The small numbers are deliberately modest, which makes them more believable than a sweeping claim."}, {"text": "So, concretely: a selective program should have to show, every year, what it gives back. Its senior students teaching in feeder primary schools. Its ensembles playing in communities that have none. Auditions that test aptitude rather than prior tuition. And places held for students who have never had a lesson. Those things are measurable, | and they should be reported. || I would be interested to hear whether my fellow panellists see that as a burden, | or as a licence.", "note": "Ends with a concrete, checkable proposal and a question that sets up the discussion he wants. 'Burden or licence' frames the debate on his terms and hands it to the moderator."}]}}, {"id": "type-parent-address", "name": "Address to a parent body on a contested change", "occasion": "A meeting of parents called, or used, to explain a decision or proposal that some of them oppose.", "minutes": [8, 15], "purpose": "Explain the change and the reasoning honestly, show that objections have genuinely been heard, separate what is decided from what remains open, and leave parents trusting the process even if they still disagree.", "parts": [{"part": "Opening and frame", "what": "Names the change and the purpose of the meeting, and draws the line between decided and open.", "prompt": "What exactly is changing, and is this meeting to inform, to consult, or both?", "share": 12}, {"part": "The concerns acknowledged", "what": "States the parents' concerns accurately, in their terms, before answering any of them.", "prompt": "What are parents actually worried about, put as they would put it?", "share": 15}, {"part": "The reasons", "what": "Explains why the change is being made, with evidence the school holds.", "prompt": "What problem does this solve, and how do you know it exists?", "share": 18}, {"part": "The hardest objection", "what": "Takes the most pointed concern, often about motive, head on, conceding what is true in it.", "prompt": "What is the accusation behind the questions, and what part of it is fair?", "share": 12}, {"part": "What it means in practice", "what": "Shows concretely what will happen to their children.", "prompt": "What will a student's week and year actually look like?", "share": 15}, {"part": "What is open", "what": "Specifies what parents can still shape, how, and by when.", "prompt": "What can parents still change, through what mechanism, and when will they hear back?", "share": 15}, {"part": "Close", "what": "Restates commitment with candour and opens the floor.", "prompt": "What do you want them to believe about you and the program when they leave?", "share": 13}], "pitfalls": ["Defending the decision before acknowledging the concern, so parents hear that they have not been listened to.", "Pretending to consult on something already decided, which destroys more trust than the decision itself.", "Arguing the program's interests in the abstract when every parent is thinking about one child.", "Answering the angriest parent at the expense of the room; take the concern seriously, but speak to everyone.", "Over-explaining when anxious, so the key commitment is buried under reassurance."], "model": {"title": "Ensemble Placement by Audition", "setting": "A meeting of the music parents' association called to discuss the program's decision to place students in ensembles by audition rather than by year level from next year, with around one hundred and twenty parents present.", "paragraphs": [{"text": "Good evening, and thank you for coming out on a weeknight. I know some of you are here because you are worried, some because you are cross, and a few because the email mentioned supper. All three are reasonable. || Let me say at the start what tonight is. From next year, students in the program will be placed in ensembles by audition rather than by year level. That decision has been made. | What has not been decided, and what I want your help with tonight, is how the audition works and how we support students through it.", "note": "Names the room's feelings with a light touch, then states the change and draws the line between decided and open within the first minute. Parents can only engage constructively once they know which conversation they are in."}, {"text": "I want to start with your concerns, because I have read every email and I think I can state them fairly. You are worried that younger students will sit in lower ensembles for years and lose heart. You are worried that friendship groups will be split. You are worried that one stressful afternoon will decide a whole year. And some of you are worried that this is about the program's results at festivals rather than about your children. | If I have missed something, I would like to hear it tonight.", "note": "States the concerns in the parents' own terms before any defence, including the most pointed one, about motive. Inviting additions signals that the summary is a starting point, not a dismissal."}, {"text": "Here is why we are doing it. At present, a Year 8 clarinettist playing at the level of a Year 11 sits in a Year 8 band and is bored. A Year 10 student who started late sits in the Year 10 band and is frightened. We have both of those students in the program now, and some like them have left. I have the withdrawal records for the last three years, and the reason most often given is not workload. | It is 'the band is the wrong level for me'.", "note": "Explains the reasoning through two students every parent can picture, and points to evidence the program holds. The quoted reason, delivered after a breath, makes the case in the students' own words."}, {"text": "And to the hardest concern, about motive. Yes, our ensembles will probably sound better. I will not pretend otherwise, and I do not think that is bad for the students in them. But if this were about festival results, the simplest thing would be to put the strongest players in the top band and leave everyone else where they are. That is not the proposal. *Every* ensemble will be placed by level, | so that every student is in a band where they are stretched and not swamped.", "note": "Takes the most pointed objection head on and concedes what is true in it, which is the only credible way to answer a question about motive. The stress on 'Every' carries the difference between this change and its cynical version."}, {"text": "What will this mean for your child? Every student will still be in an ensemble every year; no one is dropped. Placement will be reviewed each semester, not once a year, so a student who improves moves up within months, not years. Section leaders will be drawn from every year level. And students will still work together by year level in their classroom music lessons, so the friendships formed there do not depend on the ensemble. || Nothing about the three-year commitment changes.", "note": "Translates the policy into concrete guarantees that answer each concern in turn, without labelling them as answers. The last line closes a likely question before it is asked."}, {"text": "Now, what is open. We have not settled the audition format. I would like it to be a short, relaxed play-through with the student's own instrumental teacher present, not a panel, and I would like students to be able to submit a recording if they are unwell or anxious on the day. We have not settled how students are told their placement, and I would value your views on that, particularly from those of you whose children have found transitions hard. There is a short form on your chairs. | I will read every one, and a working group that includes two parents will report back to this association before the end of Term 1.", "note": "Makes the open questions genuinely open, with proposals parents can react to and a concrete mechanism for their input. Parent membership of the working group and a reporting date give the consultation substance."}, {"text": "I would rather you hear this from me than from a newsletter, and I would rather you disagree with me in this room than in the car park. I believe this change is right for your children. I may well be wrong about some of the details, | which is exactly why the details are still open. || Thank you. I will take questions for as long as you have them.", "note": "Closes with candour about his fallibility on the details, which strengthens rather than weakens his authority on the decision. An open-ended offer of questions signals confidence and respect."}]}}], "great": [{"id": "great-lincoln-gettysburg", "speaker": "Abraham Lincoln", "occasion": "Dedication of the Soldiers' National Cemetery, Gettysburg, Pennsylvania, 19 November 1863", "year": "1863", "context": "Four and a half months after the battle, the cemetery for the Union dead was dedicated with a two-hour oration by Edward Everett, the principal speaker. Lincoln had been invited to offer a few closing remarks, and spoke for roughly two minutes, in about 270 words.", "excerpt": "Having been asked to dedicate the ground, Lincoln declares that the dead have already consecrated it beyond anything words can add, and transfers the act of dedication from the ground to the living, who must finish the work the dead began.", "quoted": false, "moves": [{"name": "Temporal arc (past, present, future)", "how": "The speech moves in three short panels: the founding of the nation four score and seven years earlier, the present war and this field, and the future the living must secure. The whole argument is carried by that movement in time, so the audience feels the occasion as one point on a long line."}, {"name": "Correctio (self-correction)", "how": "He sets out the formal purpose of the day, then corrects it: in a larger sense the living cannot dedicate or hallow this ground, because the soldiers already have. The ceremony is redefined in the act of being performed."}, {"name": "Tricolon with climax", "how": "The refusal is built as three near-synonymous verbs of consecration, each heavier than the last, and the closing sentence ends on a three-part phrase describing government by, of and for the people. Triads give both the middle and the end their cadence."}, {"name": "Antithesis", "how": "He contrasts what is said here, which the world will soon forget, with what was done here, which it never can. The modesty about words is itself a rhetorical claim about the weight of deeds."}, {"name": "Abstraction from particulars", "how": "He names no general, regiment, state or side, and does not mention slavery or the Confederacy by name. The omission lets the speech speak for every grave and for the nation as a proposition, rather than for one battle."}, {"name": "Transfer of obligation", "how": "The final, longest sentence turns honour for the dead into a task for the hearers. The audience leaves charged rather than consoled."}], "borrow": "At a ceremony or a valedictory he can do what Lincoln did: name the formal purpose, quietly correct it, and hand the obligation to the people in the room. Following a long principal address, the short speech that refuses to compete and instead reframes the occasion is often the one remembered."}, {"id": "great-pericles-funeral", "speaker": "Pericles, as reported by Thucydides", "occasion": "Public funeral for the Athenian dead of the first year of the Peloponnesian War, Athens", "year": "431 BC", "context": "Athenian custom required a chosen citizen to deliver an oration over the war dead each year; Pericles was chosen after the first campaigning season of the war with Sparta. The text survives in Book 2 of Thucydides' History, which is the historian's composition of what was fitting to the occasion, not a transcript.", "excerpt": "Instead of praising the fallen as individuals, Pericles praises the city and its way of life, so that the dead are honoured as people who judged Athens worth dying for, and the living are invited to judge the same.", "quoted": false, "moves": [{"name": "Exordium on the difficulty of the task", "how": "He opens by doubting the custom itself: deeds are better honoured by deeds, and a speaker risks either disappointing those who knew the dead or straining the belief of those who did not. This disarms the audience and establishes his judgement before he praises anything."}, {"name": "Praeteritio", "how": "He passes briefly over the ancestors and the military campaigns, saying his hearers know them already, and moves to the institutions and character of the city. Declining the expected material frees the speech for its real subject."}, {"name": "Encomium of the polity", "how": "The core is a portrait of Athenian government, law, openness, leisure and the habit of deliberating before acting. The dead are praised by being shown to be the expression of that order."}, {"name": "Synkrisis (implicit comparison)", "how": "Athenian openness and self-directed courage are set against a disciplined, closed rival without Sparta needing to be dwelt on. The contrast sharpens the portrait and flatters the audience's identity."}, {"name": "Consolatio addressed by audience segment", "how": "Near the end he turns to parents, sons and brothers, and widows in turn, giving each a distinct and brief word. The consolation is short and unsentimental, in keeping with the opening claim that words should not outrun deeds."}], "borrow": "For a valedictory, an anniversary or a memorial within a school community he can praise the institution and its practices as the thing the honoured person embodied, which avoids the inflation of personal eulogy. Opening by acknowledging the limits of what any speech can do earns the right to say something substantial."}, {"id": "great-churchill-june-1940", "speaker": "Winston Churchill", "occasion": "Statement to the House of Commons on the war situation after the Dunkirk evacuation, London, 4 June 1940", "year": "1940", "context": "Churchill had been Prime Minister for less than a month. The evacuation of the British Expeditionary Force and allied troops from Dunkirk had just ended, with more than 330,000 men brought off but almost all their equipment lost, and invasion of Britain was a real prospect.", "excerpt": "He gives Parliament an unsparing account of a military disaster, refuses to let the rescue be mistaken for a victory, and only then builds to a resolve to fight on, so that the defiance carries the authority of the candour that preceded it.", "quoted": false, "moves": [{"name": "Narratio of hard facts", "how": "Most of the speech is a sober, detailed military report: the collapse in Belgium and France, the losses of men and matériel, the work of the navy and air force. The peroration is persuasive because the House has first been treated as adults."}, {"name": "Correctio against false comfort", "how": "At the moment of relief he warns that wars are not won by evacuations and that the episode must not be read as a victory. Puncturing premature celebration is what gives his later confidence its credibility."}, {"name": "Anaphora in the peroration", "how": "The close repeats a single short clause of resolve across a sequence of places, from the seas to the beaches, fields, streets and hills. The repetition is rhythmic and cumulative, and the vocabulary is almost entirely plain, short, old English words."}, {"name": "Climax by widening scope", "how": "The peroration moves outward from the island to the empire and finally to the New World coming to the rescue. The last movement is aimed past the chamber, at the United States, without naming it as a request."}, {"name": "Ethos of shared ownership", "how": "He speaks of what the nation and House will do, not what he will do, and folds himself into the collective. The resolve is presented as the country's, which Parliament is invited to ratify."}], "borrow": "In a report to a council or board on a setback, he can give the full, unvarnished account first, explicitly refuse the comforting reading, and only then state the resolve. The order matters: candour first buys the authority for confidence second."}, {"id": "great-keating-redfern", "speaker": "Paul Keating", "occasion": "Launch of the Australian celebration of the International Year for the World's Indigenous People, Redfern Park, Sydney, 10 December 1992", "year": "1992", "context": "The High Court's Mabo decision of June 1992 had overturned the doctrine that Australia was land belonging to no one at settlement, and the Commonwealth was preparing its legislative response. Keating, Prime Minister, spoke to a largely Aboriginal audience in Redfern; the speech was drafted with his speechwriter Don Watson.", "excerpt": "Keating has non-Indigenous Australia accept that it was the agent of dispossession, naming the acts plainly in the first person plural, and frames that acknowledgement not as guilt but as the precondition for a test the nation can pass.", "quoted": false, "moves": [{"name": "Confessio in the active voice", "how": "He lists the wrongs as things we did: taking the land, destroying ways of life, bringing disease and alcohol, committing killings, removing children, practising discrimination. The grammar refuses the passive constructions that usually let a speaker avoid the agent."}, {"name": "Anaphora", "how": "The acknowledgement is a run of parallel clauses with the same opening pattern. The repetition gives an official, almost liturgical weight to what had rarely been said by a head of government."}, {"name": "Invitation to imaginative reversal", "how": "He asks the non-Indigenous listener to imagine those things having been done to them. This is a direct appeal to empathy that does the work of argument without any moralising tone."}, {"name": "Framing as a test", "how": "The issue is placed as a test of the nation's maturity and decency, a measure of what kind of country Australia is. This lets him address the whole nation, not only the audience in the park."}, {"name": "Turn to opportunity", "how": "After the acknowledgement he moves to Mabo, to practical reconciliation and to what can now be built. The speech ends forward-facing, so the confession reads as a beginning rather than a verdict."}], "borrow": "In an acknowledgement at a ceremony, or a submission about past inequity in access to a program, he can name what the institution itself did, in the active voice, and then frame the remedy as a test the institution can choose to pass. Owning agency plainly is more disarming to a sceptical room than any amount of qualification."}, {"id": "great-keating-unknown-soldier", "speaker": "Paul Keating", "occasion": "Funeral service for the Unknown Australian Soldier, Australian War Memorial, Canberra, 11 November 1993", "year": "1993", "context": "On the 75th anniversary of the Armistice, the remains of an unidentified Australian soldier of the First World War, exhumed from Adelaide Cemetery near Villers-Bretonneux in France, were interred in the Hall of Memory. Keating's eulogy, written with Don Watson, is a few minutes long.", "excerpt": "Keating builds the eulogy on everything that cannot be known about the man, and converts that ignorance into the reason he can stand for every Australian who served, and into a redefinition of the Anzac legend as something ordinary and democratic.", "quoted": false, "moves": [{"name": "Litany of the unknown", "how": "The opening is a list of what we do not know: his name, his rank, his age, where he came from, what he did, whether he was loved. Each absence is stated flatly, and together they make the anonymity itself the subject."}, {"name": "Synecdoche", "how": "Because he is no one in particular, he becomes all of them: the one stands for the sixty thousand Australians who died in that war and for all who served since. The logic of the speech depends on the part standing for the whole."}, {"name": "Correctio of the myth", "how": "He corrects the heroic legend: the soldiers were not supermen or born warriors but ordinary people, and the legend's real meaning lies in courage, ingenuity and mateship shown by ordinary people. The ceremony is used to say what the tradition should mean."}, {"name": "Restraint of register", "how": "The sentences are short, concrete and largely unadorned, with no martial grandeur. The plainness suits a grave for a man without a name and makes the few heightened sentences carry."}, {"name": "Closing affirmation", "how": "It ends by stating simply that he is one of us. The final move returns from abstraction to belonging."}], "borrow": "For a eulogy where he knew the person only in part, or a memorial for an anonymous or collective subject, he can build on what is not known and let that make the subject representative. At a ceremony steeped in tradition, the occasion can be used to say quietly what the tradition ought to mean."}, {"id": "great-rfk-indianapolis", "speaker": "Robert F. Kennedy", "occasion": "Campaign rally, Indianapolis, Indiana, on the evening Martin Luther King Jr was assassinated, 4 April 1968", "year": "1968", "context": "Kennedy, campaigning for the Democratic presidential nomination, learned on arrival that King had been shot dead in Memphis. Against the advice of police, he went to a largely Black neighbourhood rally and, standing on a flatbed truck, broke the news to a crowd most of whom had not heard it, speaking for a few minutes from brief notes.", "excerpt": "He tells the crowd the worst news at once, names the choice it faces between bitterness and understanding, and earns the right to ask for the second by speaking, for the only time in public, of his own brother's murder.", "quoted": false, "moves": [{"name": "Immediate disclosure", "how": "He delivers the news in the first sentences, without preamble. The audible shock of the crowd is allowed to happen, and he speaks into it rather than around it."}, {"name": "Dilemma posed as a choice", "how": "He sets out the two paths open to the listeners, hatred and revenge or an effort to understand and to replace violence with compassion. Putting the choice to them treats the crowd as moral agents, not a threat to be managed."}, {"name": "Ethos from shared grief", "how": "He says that he too had a member of his family killed, by a white man. The disclosure is brief and unelaborated; its power lies in being something he never otherwise said in public."}, {"name": "Citation of authority", "how": "He recites from memory a passage from Aeschylus, in Edith Hamilton's translation, about wisdom that comes through suffering. The unexpected classical voice lifts the moment out of politics into something older."}, {"name": "Brevity and close in a shared task", "how": "He ends by asking the crowd to dedicate themselves to an ancient aim of taming savagery and making life gentle, and to pray for King's family and the country. The speech stops as soon as the request is made."}], "borrow": "When he must announce bad news to a community, whether a death, a closure or a failed bid, he can say it first and plainly, then name the choice of response, and only then offer a word of his own experience. The authority comes from the brevity and the refusal to manage the room."}, {"id": "great-king-mountaintop", "speaker": "Martin Luther King Jr", "occasion": "Address at Mason Temple, Memphis, Tennessee, in support of the striking sanitation workers, 3 April 1968", "year": "1968", "context": "King had come to Memphis to support Black sanitation workers striking for recognition and safe conditions after two workers were crushed in a truck. He had not planned to speak that stormy night but was called to the temple; he was assassinated the next evening.", "excerpt": "King joins a practical program for the strike to a sweeping view of history and his own mortality, so that a local labour dispute becomes a moment in a long journey whose end he has seen but may not reach.", "quoted": false, "moves": [{"name": "Imagined survey of history", "how": "He opens by imagining being offered a vantage on any age of human history, from Egypt to Greece, the Reformation and the Emancipation, and choosing to live in the second half of the twentieth century. The device places the Memphis struggle in the long story and makes the present feel chosen."}, {"name": "Practical program", "how": "In the middle he gives concrete instructions: unity, economic withdrawal from named companies, moving deposits to Black-owned banks. The visionary frame is anchored by something the audience can do on Monday."}, {"name": "Parable reinterpreted", "how": "He retells the Good Samaritan and reverses the question that passers-by asked themselves: not what will happen to me if I stop, but what will happen to him if I do not. The scriptural story becomes an argument for supporting the strikers."}, {"name": "Personal narrative", "how": "He recounts being stabbed in New York years earlier and a letter from a schoolgirl glad he had not sneezed, because the blade was close to his aorta. The anecdote of near death prepares the close."}, {"name": "Peroration on the Moses figure", "how": "He ends by likening himself to Moses shown the promised land from the mountain, saying he may not get there with them but that they will. The audience hears both triumph and farewell, delivered with mounting cadence."}], "borrow": "In a keynote to music educators he can place a local, practical demand inside a long historical view, then give the room something concrete to do, then close on what the work is for beyond himself. Reversing the question a familiar story asks is a strong way to reframe a duty."}, {"id": "great-mandela-rivonia", "speaker": "Nelson Mandela", "occasion": "Statement from the dock at the opening of the defence case, Rivonia Trial, Supreme Court, Pretoria, 20 April 1964", "year": "1964", "context": "Mandela and his co-accused were charged with sabotage and conspiracy to overthrow the state, offences that carried the death penalty. Rather than give evidence and face cross-examination, he made an unsworn statement from the dock, reading for about three hours; he was sentenced to life imprisonment in June 1964.", "excerpt": "Mandela admits the core facts, that he helped found Umkhonto we Sizwe and planned sabotage, and turns the admission into a reasoned justification, so that the court becomes the setting for a statement of principle addressed to the world.", "quoted": false, "moves": [{"name": "Concessio and confession and avoidance", "how": "He freely admits the acts the state can prove, then explains why they were undertaken and denies what is false, such as being directed by communists or foreign powers. Conceding the provable strengthens everything he denies."}, {"name": "Narratio from personal history", "how": "He sets his choices in his biography and in the history of the African National Congress, showing decades of lawful protest met with repression. Violence is presented as a last resort, reached deliberately and with limits against loss of life."}, {"name": "Distinctio", "how": "He carefully distinguishes the ANC from the Communist Party, sabotage from terrorism, and his admiration for parliamentary institutions from the state that excluded him. Each distinction narrows the charge to what he can defend."}, {"name": "Appeal to the grievances as evidence", "how": "He describes in concrete terms poverty, pass laws, broken families and lack of education. The material shifts the question from what he did to what made it necessary."}, {"name": "Peroration at maximum stakes", "how": "He closes on the ideal of a democratic and free society for which he has lived and, if necessary, is prepared to die. Spoken by a man facing a possible death sentence, the conditional carries enormous weight; his counsel had urged him to temper the ending."}], "borrow": "Before a board or an inquiry where a decision of his is under challenge, he can concede the facts outright, explain the reasoning in historical order, draw the exact distinctions that matter, and close on the principle. Admitting what is provable is often the strongest defensive position."}, {"id": "great-elizabeth-tilbury", "speaker": "Elizabeth I", "occasion": "Address to the land forces assembled at Tilbury, Essex, against the threatened Spanish invasion, August 1588", "year": "1588", "context": "The Spanish Armada was in the Channel and an invasion force under the Duke of Parma was expected from the Netherlands; Elizabeth reviewed the army camped at Tilbury under the Earl of Leicester. The best-known text derives from a later letter by Leonel Sharp, a chaplain, printed in the seventeenth century, so the exact wording is uncertain.", "excerpt": "The queen turns the presumed weakness of a woman ruler into a pledge: she has come among her soldiers in person, in defiance of advice about her safety, and stakes her own life and honour alongside theirs.", "quoted": false, "moves": [{"name": "Ethos by presence", "how": "She begins by noting that she has been warned against trusting herself to armed multitudes for fear of treachery, and has come anyway. The act of being there becomes the first argument."}, {"name": "Concessio turned to strength", "how": "She concedes that she has the body of a weak and feeble woman and then claims the heart and stomach of a king, and a king of England. The concession is the setup for the claim, and the gendered objection is answered before anyone raises it."}, {"name": "Personal stake", "how": "She resolves to live or die among them and to lay down her honour and blood in the field. The ruler accepts the same risk she asks of the ranks."}, {"name": "Promise of reward", "how": "She tells them they have deserved rewards and crowns and that they will be paid. The heroic register is grounded in a practical assurance."}, {"name": "Delegation of authority", "how": "She names her lieutenant general and asks obedience to him as to herself. The speech ends by settling the chain of command."}], "borrow": "When he takes on a hard task or a contested room, he can name the objection others are thinking, concede it, and turn it into the basis of his commitment. Appearing in person where he was expected to delegate, and saying so, is itself persuasive."}, {"id": "great-cicero-catiline", "speaker": "Marcus Tullius Cicero", "occasion": "First Oration against Catiline, delivered to the Senate in the Temple of Jupiter Stator, Rome, 8 November 63 BC", "year": "63 BC", "context": "Cicero, consul, had evidence that Lucius Sergius Catilina was organising a conspiracy, including an attempt on Cicero's life the previous morning. Catiline himself attended the Senate meeting; Cicero's published version was revised for circulation some years later.", "excerpt": "Rather than prove the conspiracy to the Senate, Cicero addresses Catiline directly, shows that every detail of his plot is known, and so makes his continued presence in Rome impossible, driving him to leave the city that night.", "quoted": false, "moves": [{"name": "Apostrophe and interrogatio", "how": "He opens with a volley of rhetorical questions aimed straight at Catiline, asking how long his effrontery will go on and whether he thinks his plans are hidden. The Senate is made an audience to a confrontation, not a jury hearing argument."}, {"name": "Exclamatio", "how": "He breaks off to lament the times and their moral condition, that the Senate knows and the consul sees, yet the man lives. The exclamation turns individual outrage into a collective indictment of complacency."}, {"name": "Demonstration of knowledge", "how": "He recounts the meeting at the house of Laeca, the night, the participants, the allocation of tasks. Each detail tells Catiline that concealment has failed, which is more destabilising than a formal proof."}, {"name": "Prosopopoeia", "how": "He gives the fatherland a voice, first addressing Catiline and later addressing Cicero himself over why he does not act more severely. The personification lets him put arguments in a mouth more authoritative than his own and answer the charge of weakness."}, {"name": "Dilemma", "how": "He offers Catiline the choice to go into exile and take his followers, presenting departure as both a mercy and a confession. Either outcome serves Cicero's purpose."}], "borrow": "In a meeting where someone is acting in bad faith, he can, sparingly, set out precisely what is already known rather than argue a case, and offer a clean exit. Giving the institution itself a voice, such as what the program or the students would ask, lets him make the strongest point without appearing personal."}, {"id": "great-feynman-cargo-cult", "speaker": "Richard Feynman", "occasion": "Commencement address, California Institute of Technology, Pasadena", "year": "1974", "context": "Feynman, a Nobel laureate in physics on the Caltech faculty, was asked to give the address to graduating students. It was later published as the closing chapter of his memoir collection Surely You're Joking, Mr. Feynman!", "excerpt": "Using a comic image of people who imitate the outward forms of a practice without its substance, Feynman isolates the one quality that makes science science: an active, uncomfortable integrity about everything that might show you wrong, starting with yourself.", "quoted": false, "moves": [{"name": "Controlling analogy", "how": "He describes South Sea islanders after the war who built imitation runways and control huts in the hope that planes would land again. Pseudoscience is defined by that image: perfect form, missing substance."}, {"name": "Exempla from the laboratory", "how": "He tells stories of a psychologist who traced how rats were really navigating a maze, and of how later measurements of the electron's charge crept only gradually away from Millikan's value. Each case shows a specific failure or success of integrity, not an abstraction."}, {"name": "Distinctio", "how": "He distinguishes not lying from scientific integrity, which requires reporting everything that might undermine your result, including what you did not test. The distinction lifts the standard above ordinary honesty."}, {"name": "Maxim placed at the centre", "how": "He states as a first principle that one must not fool oneself, and that oneself is the easiest person to fool. Placing the maxim mid-speech, after the stories, lets it land as a conclusion rather than a sermon."}, {"name": "Application to the audience's future", "how": "He turns to what graduates will face: advertising, funding pressures, the temptation to omit inconvenient results. The close is a hope that they find a place where they are free to keep this kind of integrity."}], "borrow": "Addressing an assessment committee or a conference on evidence, he can open with one vivid image of form without substance, give two concrete cases, then state the principle as the conclusion. Distinguishing the ordinary standard from the higher one his field demands is a strong way to raise a room's expectations without lecturing it."}, {"id": "great-gehrig-farewell", "speaker": "Lou Gehrig", "occasion": "Lou Gehrig Appreciation Day, between games of a doubleheader, Yankee Stadium, New York, 4 July 1939", "year": "1939", "context": "Gehrig, the New York Yankees first baseman who had played 2,130 consecutive games, had been diagnosed weeks earlier with amyotrophic lateral sclerosis and had retired. Before a large crowd and his past and present teammates, he spoke briefly and without notes.", "excerpt": "Facing a fatal illness, Gehrig declares himself fortunate and spends almost the whole speech counting the people who made him so, so that a farewell becomes an act of gratitude rather than lament.", "quoted": false, "moves": [{"name": "Paradox in the exordium", "how": "He opens by saying people have read about his bad break and then claims to be the luckiest man alive. The gap between the circumstances and the claim seizes the audience at once."}, {"name": "Enumeratio of gratitude", "how": "He works through owners, managers, teammates, opponents, groundsmen, his parents, his mother-in-law and his wife. The catalogue is the argument: the evidence for his good fortune is the people in front of him."}, {"name": "Parallel conditionals", "how": "Several sentences share a shape: when such and such a person or group has done this for you, that is something. The repeated structure gives a plain speaker a rhythm and lets each item build on the last."}, {"name": "Litotes and understatement", "how": "His illness is mentioned only as a bad break, and its prognosis not at all. The restraint makes the gratitude more moving, because the audience supplies what he leaves unsaid."}, {"name": "Ring composition", "how": "He ends by returning to the opening idea, acknowledging that he may have had a tough break but has an awful lot to live for. The close completes the frame without adding new material."}], "borrow": "For an award acceptance or a valedictory he can open with one honest paradox and then let a specific catalogue of people carry the speech. Saying little about himself and much about others, in a repeated simple structure, suits a plain delivery and avoids self-regard."}, {"id": "great-heaney-nobel", "speaker": "Seamus Heaney", "occasion": "Nobel Lecture, Crediting Poetry, Swedish Academy, Stockholm, 7 December 1995", "year": "1995", "context": "Heaney received the Nobel Prize in Literature in 1995. His lecture was delivered while the Northern Ireland peace process was fragile, the 1994 ceasefires recent, and he had spent his life as a poet from a Catholic farming background in County Derry writing through the Troubles.", "excerpt": "Heaney moves from childhood memories of the radio in a rural farmhouse to the violence of Northern Ireland, and argues that poetry earns its credit by being both true to that violence and capable of a balancing affirmation that does not betray it.", "quoted": false, "moves": [{"name": "Autobiographical narratio", "how": "He begins with the family kitchen, the wireless, the names of foreign stations on the dial and the sense of a wider world reaching an enclosed childhood. The personal story becomes the frame for a theory of poetry."}, {"name": "Paronomasia on the title", "how": "The lecture plays on credit as belief, as acknowledgement and as something owed. The argument is gathered under that double sense: crediting poetry means both trusting it and giving it its due."}, {"name": "Testimony from the Troubles", "how": "He recounts the 1976 massacre of workmen at Kingsmill, in which a Protestant worker squeezed the hand of the one Catholic in the van as a sign of solidarity, moments before gunmen let the Catholic go and shot the rest. The anecdote is told with restraint and refuses to resolve into easy hope."}, {"name": "Exemplum from legend", "how": "He tells the story of St Kevin, who stayed motionless with his hand outstretched until a blackbird that had nested in it had hatched its young. The legend models a patient endurance he connects to the poet's task."}, {"name": "Literary authority", "how": "He reads closely from Yeats's poems written during the Irish Civil War, showing how a poet can register violence and still find an image of something living. The argument is made by demonstration through another poet's work."}], "borrow": "For a conference keynote on why music matters, he can begin with a concrete personal memory, choose a word with a double meaning as the spine, and test his claim against the hardest case he knows rather than an easy one. Arguing through close attention to one piece of art is more persuasive to scholars than generalisation."}, {"id": "great-obama-2004-keynote", "speaker": "Barack Obama", "occasion": "Keynote address, Democratic National Convention, Fleet Center, Boston, 27 July 2004", "year": "2004", "context": "Obama was an Illinois state senator and the Democratic candidate for the United States Senate, little known nationally. The keynote supported the nomination of John Kerry and made Obama a national figure.", "excerpt": "Obama offers his own improbable family history as proof of a national story, then uses it to argue against the habit of dividing the country into opposed camps, and ends by defining hope as a demanding stance rather than wishful thinking.", "quoted": false, "moves": [{"name": "Biography as argument", "how": "He recounts his father's origins in Kenya and his mother's family in Kansas, his grandfather's war service and the opportunities each generation found. His life is presented as evidence for a claim about the country, which is ethos and logos at once."}, {"name": "Enumeratio of ordinary citizens", "how": "He describes people he met in the campaign: workers losing jobs to offshoring, a father unable to afford medicine, a young man enlisting for Iraq. The particulars make policy concrete and set up the case for the nominee."}, {"name": "Anaphora and antithesis", "how": "The central passage repeats a pattern rejecting divisions: there is not a liberal or a conservative country but one country, not red states and blue states but one nation. The repetition builds toward applause while carrying the argument."}, {"name": "Distinctio", "how": "He separates hope from blind optimism or wilful ignorance, defining it as the conviction that drove slaves, immigrants and soldiers. Defining the key term protects it from the obvious objection."}, {"name": "Self-deprecating peroration", "how": "He ends with a skinny kid with a funny name who believes he has a place in the American story. The humour lightens the close and returns the abstract theme to his own figure."}], "borrow": "Introducing himself to a new body, such as a university committee or a funding panel, he can use one concise piece of his own history as evidence for the claim he wants to make. Defining his central word against its weaker cousin is an efficient way to anticipate the room's scepticism."}, {"id": "great-gillard-misogyny", "speaker": "Julia Gillard", "occasion": "Reply to the Leader of the Opposition's motion to remove the Speaker, House of Representatives, Canberra, 9 October 2012", "year": "2012", "context": "Tony Abbott moved to remove Speaker Peter Slipper over crude and sexist text messages that had become public, and argued that the government's defence of Slipper showed it tolerated sexism. Gillard, Prime Minister, replied for about fifteen minutes, largely from notes; the motion failed and Slipper resigned that evening.", "excerpt": "Gillard turns a motion accusing her government of tolerating sexism into a detailed prosecution of the accuser, using his own documented words and conduct as the evidence.", "quoted": false, "moves": [{"name": "Retorsio (turning the argument back)", "how": "The motion's premise, that sexism disqualifies someone from office, is accepted and applied to the mover. She does not defend the Speaker's messages; she changes the defendant."}, {"name": "Enumeratio of evidence", "how": "She lists specific remarks Abbott had made over years, about women in power, abortion, women and housework, and his conduct in the chamber, with dates and contexts. The particularity makes the charge hard to dismiss as rhetoric."}, {"name": "Anaphora", "how": "She repeats that she will not be lectured on sexism and misogyny by this man. The refrain gives the extemporised speech its structure and its quotable unit."}, {"name": "Direct address and personal stake", "how": "She speaks of being personally offended and refers to her father, whom Abbott's side had invoked. The personal register is controlled and bounded, used to give the evidence moral force."}, {"name": "Kairos", "how": "The speech seized a narrow procedural moment and reached an audience far beyond the chamber, particularly overseas. Domestically, much commentary at the time focused on the defence of Slipper, which shows the gap between the room and the wider audience."}], "borrow": "When a motion or complaint in a meeting rests on a principle, he can accept the principle and apply it with documented particulars, rather than defending the narrow point. A single refrain gives an extemporised reply a spine, and he should judge in advance which audience, the room or the wider community, the reply is really for."}, {"id": "great-burke-bristol", "speaker": "Edmund Burke", "occasion": "Speech to the electors of Bristol at the conclusion of the poll, Bristol, 3 November 1774", "year": "1774", "context": "Burke had just been elected one of Bristol's two members of Parliament. His fellow member had promised to follow the instructions of his constituents, and Burke used his thanks to the electors to set out a different view of a representative's duty.", "excerpt": "Burke tells the people who have just elected him that he owes them not obedience but his judgement, and that Parliament is a deliberative assembly of one nation, not a meeting of delegates from rival interests.", "quoted": false, "moves": [{"name": "Captatio benevolentiae", "how": "He opens with gratitude and warm acknowledgement of the electors and of his colleague. The goodwill is secured before he says something the audience may not wish to hear."}, {"name": "Concessio", "how": "He grants that a representative should live in close communication with constituents, give great weight to their wishes and attend to their business. The concession makes the disagreement narrow and precise."}, {"name": "Distinctio", "how": "He distinguishes opinion, which a representative should weigh, from authoritative instructions, which would bind him against his judgement and conscience. The line between them is the whole argument."}, {"name": "Definition by contrast", "how": "He defines Parliament by what it is not: not a congress of ambassadors from hostile interests each advocating for its own, but a deliberative assembly of one nation with one interest. The negative definition makes the positive clearer."}, {"name": "Ethos through candour at the moment of victory", "how": "He makes the argument at the moment he has least need to persuade and most to lose by offending. Saying it at once establishes the terms of the relationship from the start."}], "borrow": "As chair of a council or a member of a committee nominated by a constituency, he can say early, with warmth, what he owes the people who put him there and what he does not. Conceding their claim on his attention while reserving his judgement is a defensible position he can state once and then rely on."}], "openings": [{"id": "open-1", "device": "the scene", "occasion": "conference", "text": "At a quarter past eight on a Tuesday, | a Year 7 trombonist is holding a note she cannot yet hear in tune. || Everything I want to say this morning concerns the next ninety seconds of her life.", "note": "A single concrete moment makes the abstract policy argument that follows feel owed to a real person."}, {"id": "open-2", "device": "the paradox", "occasion": "conference", "text": "The most rigorous thing we ask of music students | is the thing we are least able to *measure*. || I want to keep that a problem a little longer than is comfortable.", "note": "A contradiction stated plainly creates tension the audience wants resolved."}, {"id": "open-3", "device": "the concession", "occasion": "council report", "text": "Let me begin with what has not gone well. || Enrolments held, but retention into Year 10 did *not*, | and the council is entitled to hear that first.", "note": "Leading with bad news buys credibility for every good figure that follows."}, {"id": "open-4", "device": "the question", "occasion": "panel", "text": "What would we lose | if music disappeared from the senior certificate tomorrow? || Less than we fear in the first year, I suspect, | and far more than we could recover in the tenth.", "note": "A question answered unexpectedly signals that the speaker has already thought past the obvious reply."}, {"id": "open-5", "device": "the stake", "occasion": "board submission", "text": "The decision before the board is small on paper | and large in consequence. || It concerns one budget line | and several hundred students who will never know it was made.", "note": "Naming the gap between scale and consequence makes a routine item feel weighty."}, {"id": "open-6", "device": "the plain ask", "occasion": "board submission", "text": "I will be brief, and I will ask for one thing. || The association needs the board to approve the venue contract *tonight*.", "note": "Stating the request first respects a board's time and frames everything after as support."}, {"id": "open-7", "device": "the welcome", "occasion": "festival opening", "text": "Welcome. || For the next three days this building belongs to young musicians, | and the rest of us are guests in it.", "note": "A gracious transfer of ownership sets the tone of the whole event in one sentence."}, {"id": "open-8", "device": "the image", "occasion": "concert", "text": "Before the first note, | look for a moment at the empty chairs on stage. || Each one has been fought for, | by a student, a parent, or a teacher who would not let go.", "note": "Directing the audience's eyes turns a familiar sight into a symbol."}, {"id": "open-9", "device": "self-deprecation", "occasion": "award", "text": "I have spent twenty-six years at the back of bandstands, | which is the proper place for a bass player. || Standing at the front is a new skill, | and I am still learning the changes.", "note": "Modesty grounded in a true detail disarms the room without false humility."}, {"id": "open-10", "device": "the portrait", "occasion": "valedictory", "text": "When she arrived, the department had one room, | a piano with opinions, | and a timetable written in pencil. || She leaves it with a building, a reputation, and a timetable written, regrettably, in pencil.", "note": "A before-and-after sketch with a comic repetition honours achievement without solemnity."}, {"id": "open-11", "device": "the particular detail", "occasion": "eulogy", "text": "He tuned every instrument in the room before anyone arrived, | and he never once mentioned it. || That is how most of us knew him: | by the things that were already done.", "note": "One exact habit conveys character more truthfully than any list of virtues."}, {"id": "open-12", "device": "the wry preamble", "occasion": "toast", "text": "I have been asked to keep this short, | which, as anyone who sits on a committee with me knows, | is an act of considerable faith.", "note": "Gentle self-mockery relaxes a social room and promises brevity."}, {"id": "open-13", "device": "the definition", "occasion": "conference", "text": "By *excellence* I do not mean the best performance in the room. || I mean the distance a student travels between February and November, | and whether anyone noticed.", "note": "Redefining a contested term at the outset controls the terms of the argument."}, {"id": "open-14", "device": "the turn", "occasion": "panel", "text": "We have been asked whether specialist programs are elitist. || I would like to turn the question around: | when did *access* to expertise come to be called elite?", "note": "Reframing the prompt shifts the burden of proof without refusing the question."}, {"id": "open-15", "device": "the shared ground", "occasion": "parent body", "text": "Every person in this room wants the same thing for these children. || We may disagree about the route, | so let us begin from the destination.", "note": "Establishing common purpose first lowers defences before any contested point."}, {"id": "open-16", "device": "the long view", "occasion": "conference", "text": "Music was taught in Australian schools long before many of the subjects that now outrank it on the timetable. || I want to ask how that ranking happened, | and whether anyone ever actually decided it.", "note": "Historical framing exposes an assumption as a choice that can be revisited."}, {"id": "open-17", "device": "the narrowing", "occasion": "council report", "text": "There is much I could report tonight. || I will report three things, | because three is what the council can act on before the end of term.", "note": "Announcing selection signals discipline and makes the agenda feel manageable."}, {"id": "open-18", "device": "the admission of difficulty", "occasion": "board submission", "text": "This is not a comfortable submission to make. || It asks the board to fund something whose benefits will arrive | long after most of us have left the table.", "note": "Candour about the hard part pre-empts the objection and invites trust."}, {"id": "open-19", "device": "the held silence", "occasion": "concert", "text": "Good evening. || That silence, just now, | is the last you will hear for an hour, | and the students have earned the right to fill it.", "note": "Using an actual pause as the subject turns stillness into anticipation."}, {"id": "open-20", "device": "the anecdote", "occasion": "conference", "text": "Last year a student asked why we still learn to read notation | when she could hear the whole score on her phone. || I gave her a poor answer that afternoon, | and this paper is my second attempt.", "note": "An honest failure makes the speaker a fellow inquirer rather than a lecturer."}, {"id": "open-21", "device": "the contrast", "occasion": "festival opening", "text": "In the rehearsal room, a mistake costs nothing. || On this stage it costs a little more, | and that small price is exactly why we built a festival.", "note": "Setting two spaces side by side states the event's purpose without abstraction."}, {"id": "open-22", "device": "the expectation reversed", "occasion": "award", "text": "I am told this award recognises a contribution to music education. || It does, | but it really recognises the colleagues who covered my classes while I was making it.", "note": "Redirecting credit mid-sentence is gracious and slightly surprising."}, {"id": "open-23", "device": "the apostrophe", "occasion": "valedictory", "text": "You asked us not to make a fuss. || The council has considered your request carefully, | and has decided, with respect, to ignore it.", "note": "Addressing the honoured person directly draws the room into a shared affection."}, {"id": "open-24", "device": "the stated purpose", "occasion": "council report", "text": "The purpose of this report is narrow: | to tell the council where the music budget went, | and why next year's must go somewhere else.", "note": "A tight remit tells governors precisely what they are being asked to judge."}, {"id": "open-25", "device": "the hypothetical", "occasion": "board submission", "text": "Imagine the festival without its junior divisions. || It would be shorter, cheaper and easier to run, | and within a few years it would have no senior divisions either.", "note": "A projected consequence makes an invisible dependency visible."}, {"id": "open-26", "device": "the precedent", "occasion": "panel", "text": "The last time this course was revised, | it followed years of complaint. || I would like us, this time, to revise it *before* the complaint.", "note": "Recalling how things were done invites the room to do better without accusing anyone."}, {"id": "open-27", "device": "the object", "occasion": "eulogy", "text": "On the music stand in his studio | there is still a chart with his pencil marks. || Bar thirty-two says, in his hand, *breathe here*.", "note": "A small surviving artefact carries grief more quietly and powerfully than description."}, {"id": "open-28", "device": "the thanks that moves on", "occasion": "award", "text": "Thank you. || I will keep the thanks brief, | because the people who deserve them most are in the second row | and would prefer I got on with it.", "note": "Acknowledging gratitude economically shows respect for both the honoured and the audience."}, {"id": "open-29", "device": "situational humour", "occasion": "toast", "text": "Twenty years ago this band could not agree on a key signature. || Tonight, I am told, it has agreed on a restaurant, | which is the greater achievement.", "note": "Affectionate exaggeration from shared history unites a celebratory room."}, {"id": "open-30", "device": "the show of hands", "occasion": "parent body", "text": "How many of you learned an instrument as a child | and stopped? || Keep your hand up a moment, | because that is the conversation I want to have.", "note": "Physical participation makes the audience's own experience the evidence."}, {"id": "open-31", "device": "the thesis first", "occasion": "conference", "text": "My argument is simple, | and I will spend twenty minutes making it difficult: | we assess *performance*, | and we have forgotten we meant to assess understanding.", "note": "Declaring the claim at once lets an expert audience follow the reasoning rather than hunt for it."}, {"id": "open-32", "device": "the wide shot", "occasion": "festival opening", "text": "Somewhere in this building, | a drummer is checking the same snare for the fourth time | and a parent is pretending not to be nervous. || Both are exactly where they should be.", "note": "Panning across unseen scenes captures the scale and feeling of an event at once."}, {"id": "open-33", "device": "the steelman", "occasion": "panel", "text": "The case against specialist selection is a serious one, | and it deserves to be put at its strongest | before I disagree with it.", "note": "Granting the opposing view its best form signals confidence and fairness to a sceptical panel."}, {"id": "open-34", "device": "the occasion named", "occasion": "concert", "text": "We gather at the end of a long year, | in a hall that has heard a great many endings. || Tonight it hears a few beginnings as well.", "note": "Marking the moment in time gives the evening a frame larger than the program."}, {"id": "open-35", "device": "the understatement", "occasion": "board submission", "text": "The financial position of the association is, | to put it with some restraint, | *interesting*. || I will explain why, | and then what we propose.", "note": "Dry restraint signals seriousness more effectively than alarm."}, {"id": "open-36", "device": "the tricolon", "occasion": "eulogy", "text": "She taught for most of her life, | played for all of it, | and listened, I think, | longer than either.", "note": "A rising three-part line compresses a life and lands on its quietest virtue."}, {"id": "open-37", "device": "the shared memory", "occasion": "valedictory", "text": "Most of us remember the first staff meeting he chaired. || It ran forty minutes over, | and nobody left, | which tells you most of what you need to know.", "note": "A remembered moment the room shares makes the tribute communal."}, {"id": "open-38", "device": "the correction of record", "occasion": "council report", "text": "Before the report proper, one correction. || The figure circulated last month was wrong, | the error was mine, | and the corrected figure is in front of you.", "note": "Owning an error immediately and briefly strengthens authority for everything after."}, {"id": "open-39", "device": "the invitation", "occasion": "parent body", "text": "I am not here to persuade you. || I am here to show you what your children do between three and five on a Thursday, | and to let you judge what it is worth.", "note": "Disclaiming persuasion lowers resistance and positions the evidence as decisive."}, {"id": "open-40", "device": "the sound foretold", "occasion": "concert", "text": "In a few minutes you will hear a chord | that took this ensemble six weeks to tune. || When it arrives, | I hope you will hear the six weeks as well.", "note": "Preparing the ear for one moment teaches the audience how to listen."}], "closings": [{"id": "close-1", "device": "the return to the opening image", "occasion": "conference", "text": "I began with a trombonist holding a note she could not yet hear. || By November she will hear it, | and the question is whether our systems will notice when she does.", "note": "Returning to the opening scene closes the frame and shows how far the argument has travelled."}, {"id": "close-2", "device": "the charge", "occasion": "conference", "text": "My request of this conference is modest and specific. || Go home, | find one assessment task you have set for years, | and ask whether it measures what you *believe*.", "note": "A concrete, doable instruction turns agreement into action."}, {"id": "close-3", "device": "the quiet ending", "occasion": "eulogy", "text": "He would not have wanted a long speech. || He would have wanted the room tuned, | and then he would have wanted us to play. || So let us.", "note": "A diminishing cadence lets grief resolve into action without rhetoric."}, {"id": "close-4", "device": "the tricolon", "occasion": "festival opening", "text": "Play *boldly*, | listen *generously*, | and leave this building better musicians than you entered it.", "note": "Three parallel imperatives give a youthful audience a memorable send-off."}, {"id": "close-5", "device": "the toast", "occasion": "toast", "text": "Please stand. || To the band that could not agree on a key, | and has agreed, at last, on almost everything else.", "note": "Calling back to the opening joke turns humour into warmth at the moment of the raised glass."}, {"id": "close-6", "device": "the ask restated", "occasion": "board submission", "text": "The board has the figures and the risks. || What it needs now is a decision, | and I would ask that it be made *tonight*.", "note": "Repeating the request in its simplest form leaves the board nothing to wonder about."}, {"id": "close-7", "device": "the summary in a line", "occasion": "council report", "text": "In short: | the program is healthy, | the budget is tight, | and the next twelve months will decide which of those matters more.", "note": "A compressed recapitulation gives governors the sentence they will repeat afterwards."}, {"id": "close-8", "device": "the turn", "occasion": "panel", "text": "We were asked whether specialist programs are elitist. || The better question is why we have let *excellence* become a word we apologise for.", "note": "Ending on a reframed question leaves the panel arguing on the speaker's ground."}, {"id": "close-9", "device": "the deflection of credit", "occasion": "award", "text": "I accept this on behalf of the people who never stand at the front. || Thank you.", "note": "Brevity and generosity at the close are what an award audience remembers."}, {"id": "close-10", "device": "the image left behind", "occasion": "concert", "text": "When the last chord fades, | sit with it for a moment before you applaud. || That silence belongs to the students too.", "note": "Giving the audience something to do with the final moment shapes how the night ends."}, {"id": "close-11", "device": "the open door", "occasion": "parent body", "text": "The rehearsal room is open on Thursday afternoons. || Come and listen. || Then tell me what it is worth.", "note": "An invitation in place of a demand returns judgement to the parents and builds trust."}, {"id": "close-12", "device": "the farewell", "occasion": "valedictory", "text": "You leave us a better department than you found, | and a harder act to follow than you know. || Go well.", "note": "A balanced pair followed by a plain blessing is affectionate without sentimentality."}, {"id": "close-13", "device": "the stake restated", "occasion": "board submission", "text": "If the board declines, | nothing will happen this year. || That is precisely the danger.", "note": "Making inaction the risk reverses the usual safety of saying no."}, {"id": "close-14", "device": "the concession", "occasion": "panel", "text": "I may be wrong about the mechanism. || I am not wrong about the students, | and I would rather we argue about the first than forget the second.", "note": "Yielding the minor point makes the major one harder to dislodge."}, {"id": "close-15", "device": "the question left open", "occasion": "conference", "text": "I do not have a tidy answer to the question I began with. || I have a better question, | and that may be the more useful thing to leave in the room.", "note": "Intellectual honesty at the close earns the respect of an expert audience."}, {"id": "close-16", "device": "the forward look", "occasion": "council report", "text": "Next term I will bring the council a proposal, | not a report. || Tonight was the diagnosis; | the treatment comes in March.", "note": "Signposting the next step converts information into a timeline the council can hold you to."}, {"id": "close-17", "device": "the understatement", "occasion": "award", "text": "It has been, | on balance, | a reasonable way to spend twenty-six years.", "note": "Dry restraint about a long career moves an audience more than any declaration."}, {"id": "close-18", "device": "the personal turn", "occasion": "eulogy", "text": "I learned more about time from him than from any metronome. || I am still keeping it, | badly, | and I think of him every time I rush.", "note": "A self-implicating confession makes the loss present and continuing."}, {"id": "close-19", "device": "the applause cue", "occasion": "concert", "text": "Please join me in thanking the musicians, | who have given you an evening, | and their families, | who have given them years.", "note": "A parallel structure scales gratitude up to its true size and cues the room."}, {"id": "close-20", "device": "the anaphora", "occasion": "festival opening", "text": "This festival is for the student who is ready. || It is for the student who is not. || It is, above all, for the student who comes back next year.", "note": "Repetition with variation builds to the value the event actually serves."}, {"id": "close-21", "device": "the plain close", "occasion": "council report", "text": "That concludes the report. || I am grateful to the council for its patience, | and happy to take questions.", "note": "A clean procedural ending signals command of the meeting and invites scrutiny."}, {"id": "close-22", "device": "the reversal", "occasion": "toast", "text": "We came to celebrate her retirement. || Knowing her, | I suspect we have actually come to her next rehearsal.", "note": "A gentle reversal honours a person's restlessness with affection."}, {"id": "close-23", "device": "the callback", "occasion": "eulogy", "text": "On the stand in his studio, | bar thirty-two still says *breathe here*. || So, for a moment, | let us.", "note": "Returning to the remembered object turns his instruction into the room's shared act."}, {"id": "close-24", "device": "the charge to the young", "occasion": "valedictory", "text": "You will not remember most of what we taught you. || Remember how it felt to play in tune with forty other people, | and go and find that again, wherever you end up.", "note": "A modest admission followed by one lasting instruction feels truthful to students leaving."}, {"id": "close-25", "device": "the principle named", "occasion": "board submission", "text": "This is, finally, a question of stewardship. || We hold this festival on trust | for students who have not yet enrolled anywhere.", "note": "Elevating a budget item to a governance principle reminds a board of its duty."}, {"id": "close-26", "device": "the full stop", "occasion": "panel", "text": "That is my position. || I am happy to be argued out of it, | but not tonight.", "note": "A short, firm close with a touch of wit projects settled confidence."}, {"id": "close-27", "device": "the shared resolve", "occasion": "parent body", "text": "We will not agree on everything. || We agree on the children, | and that is enough to begin with.", "note": "Ending on common ground leaves a divided room with somewhere to stand together."}, {"id": "close-28", "device": "the analogy from the art", "occasion": "conference", "text": "In a good rhythm section, | nobody leads and everybody listens. || I would settle for a sector that managed half of that.", "note": "An image from the speaker's own craft lands the argument with wit and authority."}, {"id": "close-29", "device": "the gentle humour", "occasion": "award", "text": "I am told the trophy is heavier than it looks. || So, I suspect, was the work, | and I share both with my colleagues.", "note": "A light physical joke opens into sincere acknowledgement."}, {"id": "close-30", "device": "the ascending tricolon", "occasion": "conference", "text": "We can measure it badly, | we can measure it well, | or we can stop pretending that what matters most in music | is the part we find *easiest* to count.", "note": "The lengthening third limb carries the thesis to its strongest form."}, {"id": "close-31", "device": "the dedication", "occasion": "concert", "text": "We dedicate the final piece | to the teachers who started these students, | in rooms none of us will ever see.", "note": "Honouring unseen contributors widens the evening's gratitude beyond the stage."}, {"id": "close-32", "device": "the release", "occasion": "festival opening", "text": "The stage is yours. || Begin.", "note": "Two short sentences hand over the event with ceremony and economy."}, {"id": "close-33", "device": "the cumulative toast", "occasion": "toast", "text": "So raise a glass | to the music, | to the people who made it, | and to the ones who carried the gear.", "note": "A comic final item in the list keeps the toast warm rather than solemn."}, {"id": "close-34", "device": "the undertaking", "occasion": "council report", "text": "I give the council this undertaking: | the next report will be shorter, | and the news, I hope, | better.", "note": "A personal commitment with a light touch leaves governors with goodwill."}, {"id": "close-35", "device": "the long view", "occasion": "eulogy", "text": "Years from now, | a student of a student of his | will tune a room before anyone arrives. || That is the legacy he would have preferred.", "note": "Projecting influence forward offers consolation without cliché."}, {"id": "close-36", "device": "the restatement of thesis", "occasion": "panel", "text": "Access and excellence are not opposites. || They are two halves of one promise, | and we break it whenever we choose only one.", "note": "A crisp final formulation gives the panel the line it will quote."}, {"id": "close-37", "device": "the hinge", "occasion": "board submission", "text": "The board can treat this as a cost. || I would ask it to treat it as a *decision*, | because that is how the students will experience it.", "note": "Shifting the category of the question changes what the board feels responsible for."}, {"id": "close-38", "device": "the confession", "occasion": "conference", "text": "I came to this work for the music. || I have stayed for the students, | and I suspect most of this room could say the same.", "note": "A candid personal note at the end binds speaker and audience in a shared vocation."}, {"id": "close-39", "device": "the final image", "occasion": "parent body", "text": "In five years your children will be somewhere else. || What they take with them | is being built on Thursday afternoons, | one rehearsal at a time.", "note": "A forward image makes present routine feel consequential to parents."}, {"id": "close-40", "device": "the last word", "occasion": "concert", "text": "Thank you for listening. || That, in the end, | is what music asks of all of us.", "note": "Turning a conventional thanks into the evening's meaning closes on an unexpected depth."}]};
